{"record_id":"D11-ALG-FND-000001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B01","topic":"Algorithm Foundations","subtopic":"Algorithm Definition","concept":"Algorithm as a finite computational procedure","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an algorithm?","answer":"An algorithm is a precisely specified procedure that transforms inputs into outputs through a finite or appropriately terminating sequence of computational steps.","explanation":"For software engineering purposes, an algorithm must define enough behavior to reason about correctness, termination, resource usage, and applicable input conditions.","principle":"Algorithm descriptions should be precise enough that independent implementations can be evaluated against the same intended behavior.","rule":"Do not treat an informal intention as an algorithm until inputs, outputs, operational behavior, and termination conditions are sufficiently specified.","correct_example":"A procedure that receives a finite array of numbers and returns the maximum element, with an explicit requirement that the input contain at least one element.","invalid_example":"Find the best result efficiently, without defining what best means, what input is provided, or when the procedure terminates.","problem":"Ambiguous algorithm specifications lead to incompatible implementations and unverifiable behavior.","failure_mode":"Different implementations satisfy different interpretations of the requirement.","cause":"Missing input domain, output contract, correctness condition, or termination condition.","solution":"Specify preconditions, inputs, outputs, postconditions, termination behavior, and relevant resource constraints.","alternative":"Use a formal specification when the behavior is safety-critical or mathematically complex.","tradeoff":"More precise specifications require more design effort but reduce implementation and verification ambiguity.","constraint":"The algorithm definition must match the actual problem domain.","edge_case":"Empty input may require an explicit error, sentinel, optional result, or other defined behavior.","security_implication":"Ambiguous algorithm contracts can create validation gaps at trust boundaries.","performance_implication":"Algorithm choice cannot be evaluated meaningfully until the computational objective and input domain are defined.","testing_guidance":"Create tests covering valid inputs, boundary inputs, invalid inputs, and termination behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that implementation behavior satisfies the stated preconditions and postconditions.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D11-ALG-FND-000002","D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm design, correctness, and complexity foundations"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-FND-000002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B01","topic":"Algorithm Foundations","subtopic":"Problem Specification","concept":"Input-output specification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must be specified before selecting an algorithm?","answer":"The problem must have a defined input domain, output contract, correctness condition, constraints, and relevant operational requirements.","explanation":"Algorithm selection depends on what must be computed and under which constraints. The same functional problem may require different algorithms for different input sizes, memory limits, ordering requirements, or latency requirements.","principle":"Specify the problem before optimizing its solution.","rule":"Do not select an algorithm solely from its name or asymptotic complexity without checking the actual problem constraints.","correct_example":"Selecting a sorting strategy after identifying whether data is in memory, whether stability is required, the expected input size, and whether worst-case guarantees matter.","invalid_example":"Choosing an algorithm because it is commonly considered fast without examining input constraints or required semantics.","problem":"Premature algorithm selection can produce unnecessary complexity or unacceptable runtime behavior.","failure_mode":"The implementation is functionally correct but violates practical constraints.","cause":"Incomplete problem specification.","solution":"Record functional requirements and non-functional constraints before algorithm selection.","alternative":"Prototype multiple candidates when constraints are uncertain.","tradeoff":"Upfront specification takes time but reduces downstream redesign.","constraint":"Constraints must be measurable or otherwise operationally testable where possible.","edge_case":"A small input domain can make a theoretically inferior algorithm preferable because constant factors dominate.","security_implication":"Security requirements may impose additional constraints such as bounded resource consumption.","performance_implication":"Input distribution and size can materially affect practical performance.","testing_guidance":"Convert important constraints into executable acceptance or performance tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify both functional correctness and required resource constraints.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-FND-000003","D11-ALG-FND-000004"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm problem solving and analysis"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-FND-000003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B01","topic":"Algorithm Foundations","subtopic":"Correctness","concept":"Precondition-postcondition reasoning","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an algorithm's correctness be specified?","answer":"Correctness should be expressed using preconditions describing valid input states and postconditions describing required output states and effects.","explanation":"Preconditions constrain when an algorithm is required to operate correctly. Postconditions define what must be true after successful completion.","principle":"Correctness is relative to an explicit specification.","rule":"Do not claim universal correctness when the implementation is only proven for a restricted input domain.","correct_example":"For binary search, require a sorted sequence and specify that a returned index contains the target value, while a failure result means no matching element exists.","invalid_example":"Claiming binary search is correct for arbitrary unsorted arrays.","problem":"Missing preconditions make correctness claims broader than the actual algorithm guarantees.","failure_mode":"The algorithm returns incorrect results for inputs outside its intended domain.","cause":"Input-domain assumptions are undocumented or unchecked.","solution":"Document and, where appropriate, enforce preconditions.","alternative":"Use an algorithm that does not require the restrictive precondition.","tradeoff":"Checking preconditions adds work but can convert hidden assumptions into explicit failures.","constraint":"The specification must define behavior for invalid inputs if they can reach the algorithm.","edge_case":"A sequence containing duplicate target values requires the specification to define whether any occurrence, first occurrence, or another occurrence is required.","security_implication":"Unchecked preconditions at externally controlled boundaries can become attack surfaces.","performance_implication":"Runtime precondition checks add cost but may prevent more expensive downstream failures.","testing_guidance":"Test both valid inputs satisfying preconditions and invalid inputs violating them.","verification_guidance":"Verify the implementation against the formalized precondition and postcondition.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000001","D11-ALG-FND-000002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-FND-000004","D11-ALG-FND-000005"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm correctness and proof foundations"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-FND-000004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B01","topic":"Algorithm Foundations","subtopic":"Correctness","concept":"Loop invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is a loop invariant?","answer":"A loop invariant is a property that remains true at designated points of each iteration and can be used to reason about algorithm correctness.","explanation":"A standard loop-invariant argument establishes initialization, maintenance, and termination conditions.","principle":"A maintained invariant provides a structured bridge between the algorithm's initial state and its required final state.","rule":"An invariant must be established before the relevant loop iterations and preserved by every iteration.","correct_example":"For an insertion-sort loop, an invariant can state that the prefix before the current index is sorted and contains exactly the elements originally present in that prefix.","invalid_example":"Calling a property an invariant without showing that it is true initially and remains true after each iteration.","problem":"Weak or incorrect invariants produce false correctness proofs.","failure_mode":"The proof appears plausible but does not establish the required postcondition.","cause":"The invariant is too weak, too strong, or not preserved.","solution":"Define an invariant that directly supports the postcondition and prove initialization and maintenance.","alternative":"Use another proof technique such as induction, structural reasoning, or formal verification.","tradeoff":"Strong invariants can simplify proof but may require more detailed reasoning.","constraint":"The invariant must refer to program state that is meaningful at the chosen loop point.","edge_case":"Loops with zero iterations require initialization and termination reasoning to remain valid.","security_implication":"Correctness proofs can expose state-validation gaps but do not automatically establish security.","performance_implication":"An invariant proof does not itself establish runtime complexity.","testing_guidance":"Use invariant-derived boundary cases to test intermediate and final states where observable.","verification_guidance":"Check initialization, maintenance, and termination separately.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-FND-000005"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Loop invariants and algorithm correctness"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}


{"record_id":"D11-ALG-FND-000005","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B01","topic":"Algorithm Foundations","subtopic":"Correctness","concept":"Termination","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must algorithm termination be considered separately from functional correctness?","answer":"An algorithm can produce correct results when it terminates but still fail its specification if it does not terminate for required inputs.","explanation":"Termination is a distinct property. Proof of output correctness does not by itself establish that the algorithm reaches the output state.","principle":"Correctness requires both appropriate termination and satisfaction of the output specification for the applicable input domain.","rule":"Analyze termination whenever loops, recursion, retries, or iterative convergence are involved.","correct_example":"A loop whose variant decreases over a well-founded domain until a defined boundary is reached.","invalid_example":"A retry loop that continues indefinitely without a bounded attempt count, timeout, cancellation condition, or proven convergence condition.","problem":"Non-terminating algorithms can consume unbounded resources and block dependent operations.","failure_mode":"Hang, resource exhaustion, unavailable service, or deadlocked workflow.","cause":"Missing termination condition or invalid progress assumption.","solution":"Define a terminating condition, prove progress where practical, and impose operational bounds such as timeout or cancellation where appropriate.","alternative":"Use a bounded iterative algorithm or a proven convergent formulation.","tradeoff":"Operational bounds improve availability but may cause incomplete results when the underlying computation legitimately requires longer execution.","constraint":"Termination requirements depend on the problem domain; some continuously running services intentionally have no natural termination.","edge_case":"An empty input may bypass the main loop and require separate handling.","security_implication":"Unbounded computation can create denial-of-service conditions.","performance_implication":"Termination guarantees do not imply acceptable runtime; both termination and complexity must be analyzed.","testing_guidance":"Include tests for minimum, maximum, invalid, and adversarial inputs that could prevent progress.","verification_guidance":"Establish a progress argument or bounded execution condition appropriate to the algorithm.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-FND-000004","D11-ALG-FND-000006"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm correctness and termination reasoning"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

