{"record_id":"D11-ALG-CPL-000001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B02","topic":"Complexity Analysis","subtopic":"Asymptotic Analysis","concept":"Big O notation","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does Big O notation describe?","answer":"Big O provides an asymptotic upper-bound characterization of a function's growth rate, commonly used to describe an algorithm's resource usage as input size increases.","explanation":"Big O abstracts away constant factors and lower-order terms and focuses on asymptotic growth.","principle":"Asymptotic analysis compares scalability rather than measuring exact execution time.","rule":"Do not interpret O(f(n)) as an exact runtime or universal practical performance guarantee.","correct_example":"A loop performing at most a constant amount of work for each of n elements is O(n).","invalid_example":"Claiming an O(n) algorithm always executes faster than an O(n log n) algorithm for every finite input.","problem":"Confusing asymptotic growth with measured runtime leads to incorrect engineering decisions.","failure_mode":"An asymptotically superior algorithm performs worse in the actual operating range.","cause":"Ignoring constants, hardware, implementation, input distribution, or fixed overhead.","solution":"Use asymptotic analysis for scalability and empirical measurement for practical performance decisions.","alternative":"Use benchmarking or profiling when the decision concerns actual runtime.","tradeoff":"Asymptotic analysis is portable and general but less precise for small or bounded workloads.","constraint":"Input size and cost model must be defined.","edge_case":"For very small fixed input sizes, constant factors may dominate asymptotic differences.","security_implication":"Complexity bounds can help identify algorithmic denial-of-service risks.","performance_implication":"Complexity is a predictor of scaling behavior, not a measurement.","testing_guidance":"Performance tests should use representative input sizes and distributions.","verification_guidance":"Verify the operation-count reasoning independently of benchmark results.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-CPL-000002","D11-ALG-CPL-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Asymptotic notation and algorithm analysis"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-CPL-000002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B02","topic":"Complexity Analysis","subtopic":"Complexity Classes","concept":"Constant logarithmic linear linearithmic quadratic and exponential growth","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How do common asymptotic growth classes differ?","answer":"For increasing input size, constant O(1), logarithmic O(log n), linear O(n), linearithmic O(n log n), quadratic O(n^2), and exponential O(c^n) for c greater than 1 represent progressively faster growth.","explanation":"The ordering describes asymptotic growth under the usual positive-input model, while practical runtime also depends on constants and implementation details.","principle":"Growth rate is central to scalability analysis.","rule":"Do not compare complexity classes without confirming that the measured cost model and input parameter are comparable.","correct_example":"Binary search has logarithmic search complexity under its sorted-input precondition.","invalid_example":"Calling an operation O(log n) solely because it contains a logarithm somewhere in its source code.","problem":"Incorrect complexity classification can cause inappropriate architecture or algorithm decisions.","failure_mode":"An implementation is expected to scale but becomes impractical as input grows.","cause":"Incomplete operation counting or wrong input-size parameter.","solution":"Define the input size and count dominant operations systematically.","alternative":"Use profiling and formal cost analysis together when practical behavior matters.","tradeoff":"More detailed analysis costs engineering time but reduces scaling uncertainty.","constraint":"The chosen input-size variable must represent the actual growth driver.","edge_case":"An algorithm may be logarithmic in one parameter but linear or worse in another.","security_implication":"Algorithms with exponential or unbounded worst-case behavior can be abused through adversarial inputs.","performance_implication":"Asymptotic growth becomes increasingly important as workload size increases.","testing_guidance":"Include multiple increasing workload sizes to detect scaling trends.","verification_guidance":"Derive complexity from loops, recursion, data-structure operations, and algorithmic structure.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-CPL-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Common complexity classes"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-CPL-000003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B02","topic":"Complexity Analysis","subtopic":"Space Complexity","concept":"Auxiliary space versus input storage","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What is the distinction between input storage and auxiliary space?","answer":"Input storage represents memory required to hold the input, while auxiliary space generally describes additional memory used by the algorithm beyond the input representation.","explanation":"The distinction matters when comparing algorithms because an algorithm may have the same time complexity but substantially different additional memory requirements.","principle":"Time and space should be analyzed as separate resource dimensions.","rule":"State the space convention used when reporting complexity.","correct_example":"An in-place transformation may use O(1) auxiliary space while modifying an input array directly, subject to the algorithm's exact implementation.","invalid_example":"Reporting O(n) auxiliary space solely because the input array contains n elements when the algorithm does not allocate additional proportional storage.","problem":"Ambiguous space accounting makes algorithm comparisons misleading.","failure_mode":"A memory-constrained system exceeds available resources despite acceptable time complexity.","cause":"Ignoring temporary allocations, recursion depth, buffers, or hidden runtime storage.","solution":"Account for all algorithm-controlled auxiliary storage under a declared memory model.","alternative":"Use a memory-explicit implementation or streaming approach.","tradeoff":"Reducing memory may increase runtime or implementation complexity.","constraint":"Runtime systems may introduce implementation-dependent overhead not visible in source-level analysis.","edge_case":"Recursive algorithms may have low explicit heap allocation but O(depth) call-stack usage.","security_implication":"Unexpected memory growth can cause denial of service.","performance_implication":"Allocation and memory locality can materially affect practical performance even when asymptotic space is unchanged.","testing_guidance":"Measure memory behavior for representative and worst-case workloads when memory limits matter.","verification_guidance":"Inspect allocations, recursion depth, temporary structures, and retained references.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-CPL-000004"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm space analysis"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-CPL-000004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B02","topic":"Complexity Analysis","subtopic":"Amortized Analysis","concept":"Amortized cost","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is amortized analysis?","answer":"Amortized analysis determines the average cost per operation over a sequence of operations without relying on a probability distribution over inputs.","explanation":"An individual operation may be expensive while the total cost of a sequence remains bounded by a manageable aggregate function.","principle":"A sequence-level bound can provide a stronger guarantee than reasoning about each operation independently.","rule":"Do not confuse amortized complexity with probabilistic expected complexity.","correct_example":"Dynamic-array insertion can have occasional resizing costs while maintaining O(1) amortized insertion under standard geometric growth assumptions.","invalid_example":"Calling an operation amortized O(1) merely because most observed executions were fast.","problem":"Confusing measurement with amortized analysis leads to unsupported complexity claims.","failure_mode":"An implementation is assumed to have a theoretical guarantee that its actual state model does not provide.","cause":"Missing assumptions about operation sequences or resizing strategy.","solution":"Specify the data structure, operation sequence, growth policy, and aggregate cost argument.","alternative":"Use worst-case or expected analysis when those models better match the system requirement.","tradeoff":"Amortized guarantees simplify sequence-level reasoning but do not guarantee low latency for every individual operation.","constraint":"The analysis depends on the permitted operation sequence and implementation policy.","edge_case":"A single resize operation may remain expensive despite a low amortized cost.","security_implication":"Latency-sensitive security controls may require worst-case rather than amortized guarantees.","performance_implication":"Amortized analysis is useful for throughput-oriented workloads.","testing_guidance":"Test both ordinary operations and expensive boundary operations such as resizing.","verification_guidance":"Use an aggregate, accounting, or potential-function argument appropriate to the data structure.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Amortized analysis"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

B03 — Algorithm Design Strategy

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-DES-000001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B03","topic":"Algorithm Design","subtopic":"Brute Force","concept":"Exhaustive search","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is exhaustive search appropriate?","answer":"Exhaustive search is appropriate when the candidate space is sufficiently small, constraints permit it, or it is used as a correctness reference for validating more complex algorithms.","explanation":"Exhaustive methods systematically examine possible candidates rather than exploiting additional structure.","principle":"A simple exhaustive algorithm can serve as a trusted reference implementation for testing optimized algorithms.","rule":"Do not use exhaustive search for an unbounded or clearly exponential candidate space without demonstrating that the practical constraints permit it.","correct_example":"Enumerating all subsets when the number of elements is small enough to make 2^n enumeration acceptable.","invalid_example":"Enumerating all possible schedules for a large production system without an explicit bound on the candidate space.","problem":"Search-space explosion makes exhaustive algorithms impractical at scale.","failure_mode":"Excessive runtime or memory consumption.","cause":"Candidate-space size grows too quickly with input size.","solution":"Exploit problem structure, pruning, dynamic programming, approximation, or another algorithmic strategy.","alternative":"Use exhaustive search only on small instances as an oracle for optimized implementations.","tradeoff":"Exhaustive search offers simple reasoning and strong coverage at the cost of scalability.","constraint":"The candidate-space size must be bounded and assessed.","edge_case":"A pruning rule can transform an otherwise exhaustive strategy into a more selective search.","security_implication":"Attacker-controlled input can deliberately trigger worst-case enumeration.","performance_implication":"Candidate-space cardinality should be estimated before execution.","testing_guidance":"Use exhaustive implementations as differential-testing oracles for small random instances.","verification_guidance":"Verify that enumeration covers the complete candidate space without duplication or omission.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-DES-000002","D11-ALG-DES-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm design approaches"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-DES-000002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B03","topic":"Algorithm Design","subtopic":"Divide and Conquer","concept":"Divide-and-conquer structure","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the structure of divide and conquer?","answer":"Divide-and-conquer algorithms divide a problem into smaller subproblems, solve those subproblems recursively or otherwise, and combine their results.","explanation":"The approach is effective when subproblems are sufficiently independent and their solutions can be combined efficiently.","principle":"Problem decomposition can reduce complexity when subproblems have exploitable structure.","rule":"The division and combination costs must be analyzed along with the recursive subproblem cost.","correct_example":"Merge sort divides the sequence into smaller sequences, sorts them, and merges the sorted results.","invalid_example":"Calling any algorithm with nested function calls divide-and-conquer without identifying meaningful decomposition and combination steps.","problem":"Poor decomposition can produce unnecessary recursion or expensive combination work.","failure_mode":"The resulting algorithm has little or no scalability advantage.","cause":"Subproblems overlap heavily or combination cost dominates.","solution":"Use a suitable alternative such as dynamic programming when subproblems overlap, or redesign the decomposition.","alternative":"Dynamic programming, iterative algorithms, or greedy methods depending on problem structure.","tradeoff":"Divide-and-conquer can improve scalability but may require additional memory or recursion overhead.","constraint":"The recursive subproblem structure and base cases must be well-defined.","edge_case":"Very small subproblems require base-case handling to prevent unnecessary recursion.","security_implication":"Unbounded recursive depth can create resource-exhaustion risks.","performance_implication":"Cache behavior, recursion overhead, and combine cost can affect practical performance.","testing_guidance":"Test base cases, uneven partitions, duplicate values, and largest supported inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify base-case correctness, recursive correctness, decomposition completeness, and combination correctness.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000005"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-DES-000003","D11-ALG-DES-000004"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000005"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Divide-and-conquer algorithms"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-DES-000003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B03","topic":"Algorithm Design","subtopic":"Dynamic Programming","concept":"Overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"When is dynamic programming applicable?","answer":"Dynamic programming is applicable when a problem has reusable overlapping subproblems and an appropriate optimal-substructure or equivalent compositional property that permits solutions to be constructed from smaller solved states.","explanation":"Dynamic programming stores subproblem results to avoid repeated computation.","principle":"Reuse previously solved states when repeated subproblem computation is the dominant source of inefficiency.","rule":"Do not apply dynamic programming merely because a problem is recursive; the state representation and transition must correctly capture the required information.","correct_example":"Computing Fibonacci numbers with memoization avoids repeatedly evaluating the same subproblems.","invalid_example":"Caching incomplete states that omit information required to distinguish different future decisions.","problem":"Incorrect state design produces incorrect reuse and therefore incorrect results.","failure_mode":"Different logical states are merged and one state's result is incorrectly reused for another.","cause":"Insufficient state representation.","solution":"Identify the minimal sufficient state and formally define transitions and base cases.","alternative":"Divide-and-conquer when subproblems do not overlap; greedy methods when a valid greedy property exists.","tradeoff":"Memoization or tabulation can reduce time while increasing memory usage.","constraint":"The state space must be finite or otherwise manageable under the system's resource limits.","edge_case":"A state may be reachable through multiple paths but require different contextual information not represented in the state key.","security_implication":"Unbounded attacker-controlled state spaces can cause memory exhaustion.","performance_implication":"State-space size, transition cost, and memory locality determine practical performance.","testing_guidance":"Test state boundaries, repeated states, base states, and cases where state distinctions affect the result.","verification_guidance":"Verify state sufficiency, transition correctness, base cases, and equivalence between recursive specification and stored-state implementation.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-DES-000002","D11-ALG-DES-000004"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Dynamic programming"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-DES-000004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B03","topic":"Algorithm Design","subtopic":"Greedy Algorithms","concept":"Greedy-choice reasoning","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is required before using a greedy algorithm?","answer":"A greedy strategy requires evidence that making the locally selected choice preserves the ability to obtain a globally valid optimal solution for the specified problem.","explanation":"Greedy algorithms make decisions incrementally without generally reconsidering earlier choices. Their correctness therefore depends on problem-specific structural properties.","principle":"Local optimality does not imply global optimality without a valid proof or established theorem for the problem.","rule":"Do not infer greedy correctness from a few examples or intuitive local improvements.","correct_example":"Choosing earliest finishing activities in the classical activity-selection problem under its stated objective and assumptions.","invalid_example":"Choosing the largest immediate reward in an arbitrary optimization problem and assuming the total result must be globally optimal.","problem":"Greedy choices can eliminate globally optimal solutions.","failure_mode":"The algorithm returns a feasible but non-optimal result.","cause":"The problem lacks the required greedy-choice property or the choice rule is incorrect.","solution":"Prove the greedy choice or use dynamic programming, exhaustive search, or another method when proof is unavailable.","alternative":"Dynamic programming can preserve alternative states when local choices cannot be proven safe.","tradeoff":"Greedy algorithms are often simpler and efficient but apply to a narrower class of problems.","constraint":"Correctness depends on the exact optimization objective and constraints.","edge_case":"Ties between locally equivalent choices may have different downstream consequences in some problems.","security_implication":"Greedy heuristics used for security decisions should not be treated as guarantees without validation.","performance_implication":"Greedy algorithms often provide strong performance when their correctness conditions hold.","testing_guidance":"Include adversarial counterexamples specifically designed to challenge the greedy choice.","verification_guidance":"Require a proof or authoritative correctness result for claims of optimality.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-DES-000003","D11-ALG-DES-000005"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Greedy algorithm design and correctness"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

B04 — Recursion and Backtracking

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-REC-000001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B04","topic":"Recursion","subtopic":"Recursive Structure","concept":"Base case and recursive case","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What makes a recursive algorithm structurally valid?","answer":"A recursive algorithm requires one or more base cases and recursive cases that make measurable progress toward a base case for every valid recursive path.","explanation":"The base case prevents infinite recursion, while the recursive case reduces or transforms the problem toward termination.","principle":"Recursive decomposition must preserve correctness while making progress toward termination.","rule":"Every reachable recursive path must have a valid termination argument.","correct_example":"A factorial function defines factorial(0) as 1 and reduces factorial(n) to n times factorial(n-1) for positive n.","invalid_example":"A recursive function that invokes itself with the same arguments without another guaranteed terminating condition.","problem":"Missing or unreachable base cases can cause non-termination or stack exhaustion.","failure_mode":"RecursionError, stack overflow, resource exhaustion, or unbounded execution.","cause":"No base case, incorrect base condition, or failure to progress toward the base case.","solution":"Define explicit base cases and prove a decreasing measure or equivalent termination property.","alternative":"Use an iterative implementation when recursion depth is large or stack usage is constrained.","tradeoff":"Recursion can simplify algorithm expression but may add stack usage and call overhead.","constraint":"Maximum recursion depth depends on language/runtime implementation.","edge_case":"The smallest valid input must reach a base case immediately or through a finite sequence.","security_implication":"Attacker-controlled deep recursion can cause resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Recursive calls may introduce overhead and memory consumption proportional to recursion depth.","testing_guidance":"Test base cases, one-step recursive cases, and maximum supported depth.","verification_guidance":"Verify base-case correctness and monotonic progress toward termination.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000005"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-REC-000002","D11-ALG-REC-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000005"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Recursive algorithm analysis"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-REC-000002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B04","topic":"Recursion","subtopic":"Recursion Complexity","concept":"Recursion depth and auxiliary space","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How does recursion depth affect algorithm resources?","answer":"Recursion depth can contribute additional stack usage proportional to the maximum number of simultaneously active recursive calls, subject to the language runtime and implementation model.","explanation":"Two algorithms with the same asymptotic time complexity can have different memory behavior because one maintains a deeper call stack.","principle":"Analyze recursion depth separately from total recursive calls.","rule":"Do not equate total recursive calls with maximum stack depth.","correct_example":"A balanced divide-and-conquer recursion may have logarithmic maximum depth even when it performs many total calls.","invalid_example":"Assuming a recursion tree with many nodes necessarily requires stack space proportional to the total number of nodes.","problem":"Incorrect stack analysis can lead to unnecessary redesign or missed stack-exhaustion risks.","failure_mode":"Stack overflow occurs despite acceptable total operation count.","cause":"Deep recursive path or unbounded input depth.","solution":"Bound recursion depth, rebalance recursion, use explicit stacks, or use an iterative formulation when required.","alternative":"Transform recursive traversal into iterative traversal using an explicit work stack.","tradeoff":"Explicit stacks move memory management from the call stack to application-managed storage.","constraint":"Runtime stack limits vary by language and environment.","edge_case":"Highly unbalanced recursive decomposition can turn expected logarithmic depth into linear depth.","security_implication":"Deep attacker-controlled structures can induce stack exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Call overhead and stack allocation can affect latency.","testing_guidance":"Test adversarially unbalanced structures and maximum supported depth.","verification_guidance":"Derive maximum simultaneous recursion depth from the algorithm's recursion structure.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-REC-000001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-REC-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-REC-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Recursive complexity and stack depth"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-REC-000003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B04","topic":"Backtracking","subtopic":"Search with Reversal","concept":"Backtracking","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is backtracking?","answer":"Backtracking systematically constructs candidate solutions incrementally and abandons a partial candidate when it cannot lead to a valid solution under the problem constraints.","explanation":"Backtracking can reduce practical search compared with unrestricted enumeration when invalid partial states can be detected early.","principle":"Reject impossible partial states as soon as their invalidity becomes provable.","rule":"A pruning condition must be sound; it must not eliminate a partial state that could lead to a required solution.","correct_example":"Constraint-based search that places one variable at a time and backtracks when the partial assignment violates a constraint.","invalid_example":"Pruning a candidate merely because it appears unlikely to succeed without a proven condition when completeness is required.","problem":"Unsound pruning can silently remove valid solutions.","failure_mode":"The algorithm reports no solution even though one exists.","cause":"Incorrect pruning predicate.","solution":"Prove that every pruned state cannot produce a valid required solution.","alternative":"Use exhaustive search when the candidate space is small or pruning correctness is uncertain.","tradeoff":"Sound pruning can drastically reduce practical search but worst-case complexity may remain exponential.","constraint":"The pruning rule must preserve the required completeness or optimality property.","edge_case":"A valid solution may occur only after temporarily making a choice that looks locally inferior.","security_implication":"Adversarial inputs can maximize the remaining search tree and cause resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Branching factor and pruning effectiveness strongly influence runtime.","testing_guidance":"Include cases with no solution, one solution, many solutions, and solutions requiring deep search.","verification_guidance":"Verify both search completeness and pruning soundness.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-DES-000001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-REC-000001","D11-ALG-DES-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-DES-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Backtracking and combinatorial search"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

B05 — Searching Foundations

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-SRC-000001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B05","topic":"Searching Algorithms","subtopic":"Linear Search","concept":"Linear search","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How does linear search work?","answer":"Linear search examines elements sequentially until the target is found or all relevant elements have been examined.","explanation":"It requires no ordering assumption and can operate directly on a sequence.","principle":"When no exploitable ordering or indexing structure exists, sequential examination provides a general baseline.","rule":"For an unsorted sequence, linear search must inspect enough elements to establish absence under the specified equality relation.","correct_example":"Scan elements from the first position and return an index when the target equality predicate succeeds; otherwise return a defined not-found result after the final element.","invalid_example":"Returning not-found after examining only the first element when later elements may contain the target.","problem":"Incomplete scanning produces false negatives.","failure_mode":"Existing targets are incorrectly reported as absent.","cause":"Incorrect loop bounds, early termination, or wrong equality condition.","solution":"Define and verify the scan invariant and termination condition.","alternative":"Use binary search after sorting or use an index/hash structure when repeated queries justify preprocessing.","tradeoff":"Linear search has simple implementation and no ordering requirement but O(n) worst-case search time.","constraint":"Search semantics depend on the equality predicate and input representation.","edge_case":"Empty input must return the defined not-found result without accessing an element.","security_implication":"Expensive repeated searches over attacker-controlled large collections can create resource pressure.","performance_implication":"Worst-case and average work grow linearly with collection size under standard sequential access assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, singleton, first-position, middle, final-position, duplicate, and absent targets.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every eligible element can be examined and that returned positions satisfy the target predicate.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-SRC-000002","D11-ALG-SRC-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Searching algorithms"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-SRC-000002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B05","topic":"Searching Algorithms","subtopic":"Binary Search","concept":"Binary search precondition","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What is the critical precondition for binary search?","answer":"Binary search requires an ordered search domain under the same ordering relation used by the algorithm.","explanation":"Binary search eliminates a portion of the remaining search interval based on ordering. Without the required order relation, the elimination step is not justified.","principle":"An optimization based on ordering is valid only when the ordering invariant actually holds.","rule":"Do not apply binary search to an unsorted sequence unless the algorithm itself establishes the required ordering.","correct_example":"Search a sequence sorted in nondecreasing order using comparisons consistent with that order.","invalid_example":"Applying binary search directly to arbitrary unsorted input.","problem":"Invalid ordering makes interval elimination unsound.","failure_mode":"Binary search reports absence or returns an incorrect position for an existing target.","cause":"Missing or violated sortedness precondition.","solution":"Validate or establish ordering, or use a search method that does not require ordering.","alternative":"Linear search or an appropriate indexed structure.","tradeoff":"Maintaining sortedness can make search faster but introduces update or preprocessing costs.","constraint":"The comparator/equality semantics must be consistent with the ordering used by the search.","edge_case":"Duplicate values require an explicit contract if the caller needs the first, last, or any matching position.","security_implication":"If ordering metadata is attacker-controlled, trusting it without validation can lead to incorrect security-sensitive decisions.","performance_implication":"Binary search has logarithmic comparison complexity over a fixed-size ordered array under the standard model.","testing_guidance":"Test sorted, empty, singleton, duplicate, target-boundary, and deliberately unsorted inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify the ordering invariant before relying on elimination steps.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-SRC-000001","D11-ALG-SRC-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Binary search correctness and preconditions"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-SRC-000003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B05","topic":"Searching Algorithms","subtopic":"Binary Search","concept":"Binary search invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariant supports binary search correctness?","answer":"At each iteration, the remaining search interval must contain every possible location consistent with the target and the ordering relation, or the algorithm must have already established that no valid location remains.","explanation":"The interval-shrinking operation is correct only if the discarded region cannot contain a valid target position.","principle":"Every elimination step must preserve the possibility of finding the target if it exists.","rule":"The midpoint comparison and boundary updates must be consistent with the chosen interval convention.","correct_example":"Using a clearly defined closed or half-open interval and updating its boundaries consistently after each comparison.","invalid_example":"Mixing inclusive and exclusive bounds so that a singleton candidate can be skipped.","problem":"Off-by-one errors can cause false negatives or infinite loops.","failure_mode":"Target is missed, index is out of bounds, or loop fails to terminate.","cause":"Inconsistent interval representation or incorrect boundary update.","solution":"Choose one interval convention, document it, and derive all updates from that convention.","alternative":"Use a trusted standard-library implementation where its contract matches the requirement.","tradeoff":"Explicit invariant reasoning increases implementation discipline but reduces boundary bugs.","constraint":"Comparator behavior must remain consistent throughout the search.","edge_case":"A one-element interval must either be checked or correctly eliminated according to the interval convention.","security_implication":"Incorrect search results can affect authorization or validation decisions when search results control security logic.","performance_implication":"Each valid iteration should substantially reduce the remaining search interval.","testing_guidance":"Test intervals of sizes 0, 1, 2, and values at both boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Prove initialization, maintenance, and termination of the search interval invariant.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-SRC-000002","D11-ALG-FND-000004"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-SRC-000002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-SRC-000002","D11-ALG-FND-000004"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Binary search invariants"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

B06 — Search Design and Failure Modes

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-SRC-000004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B06","topic":"Searching Algorithms","subtopic":"Search Selection","concept":"Choosing between linear and binary search","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should a software engineer choose between linear search and binary search?","answer":"The choice depends on ordering requirements, update frequency, query frequency, data structure, preprocessing cost, and required search performance.","explanation":"Binary search can reduce comparison count on ordered random-access data, while linear search avoids sorting or ordering prerequisites and may be preferable for small or frequently changing collections.","principle":"Choose an algorithm from system constraints rather than complexity labels alone.","rule":"Account for preprocessing and maintenance costs when comparing repeated searches.","correct_example":"Use binary search when the collection is maintained in compatible order and search performance justifies the maintenance cost.","invalid_example":"Sorting data for a single search without considering that sorting itself may dominate the workload.","problem":"Ignoring total workflow cost produces misleading algorithm selection.","failure_mode":"The selected search strategy increases overall latency or complexity.","cause":"Only query-time cost was considered.","solution":"Analyze total cost: preparation, updates, queries, memory, and correctness constraints.","alternative":"Hash-based indexing for exact-key lookup when ordering is unnecessary and the required semantics permit it.","tradeoff":"Ordered search supports range-oriented reasoning while hash indexing can provide fast exact lookup but has different memory and ordering properties.","constraint":"The equality and ordering semantics must match the application's requirements.","edge_case":"For very small collections, linear search may have lower constant overhead despite a worse asymptotic bound.","security_implication":"Search structures must not bypass authorization checks merely because a matching record exists.","performance_implication":"Repeated workloads can justify preprocessing that is not worthwhile for one-off queries.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark representative workloads only after functional correctness is established.","verification_guidance":"Verify that selected data-structure invariants remain true across updates.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-SRC-000001","D11-ALG-SRC-000002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-SRC-000005"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-SRC-000001","D11-ALG-SRC-000002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm selection and searching"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-SRC-000005","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B06","topic":"Searching Algorithms","subtopic":"Indexed Search","concept":"Preprocessing versus query cost","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can preprocessing improve repeated search workloads?","answer":"Preprocessing can construct an auxiliary representation that makes subsequent queries cheaper, trading upfront computation and storage for lower query cost.","explanation":"The overall benefit depends on the number of queries, update frequency, preprocessing cost, memory budget, and query semantics.","principle":"Optimize total workload cost rather than isolated operation cost.","rule":"Evaluate preprocessing over the complete expected workload.","correct_example":"Building an index once when many subsequent queries will reuse it and the underlying data changes infrequently.","invalid_example":"Constructing an expensive index for data that will be queried only once.","problem":"Preprocessing can cost more than it saves when query volume is low or updates are frequent.","failure_mode":"Total workload becomes slower or more resource-intensive.","cause":"Incorrect workload assumptions.","solution":"Estimate or measure preparation, query, update, and memory costs for representative workloads.","alternative":"Use direct scanning for low-query-volume workloads.","tradeoff":"Indexes generally exchange additional storage and maintenance for faster access to selected query patterns.","constraint":"The index must remain consistent with the underlying data when updates occur.","edge_case":"Frequent updates can make index maintenance dominate query savings.","security_implication":"Indexes must respect access-control boundaries and must not expose unauthorized records through derived structures.","performance_implication":"Cache locality, index size, and update frequency can materially affect practical behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test stale-index scenarios, update behavior, rebuild behavior, and query correctness.","verification_guidance":"Verify that indexed results are equivalent to the authoritative data for authorized queries.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-SRC-000004"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-SRC-000004","D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-SRC-000004"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Preprocessing and search data structures"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

B07 — Correctness and Algorithm Selection

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-SEL-000001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B07","topic":"Algorithm Selection","subtopic":"Decision Process","concept":"Constraint-driven algorithm selection","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What process should the Brain use to select an algorithm?","answer":"Identify the problem specification, input constraints, correctness requirements, resource constraints, workload characteristics, candidate algorithms, complexity, implementation cost, and verification requirements before selecting an algorithm.","explanation":"Algorithm selection is an engineering decision that combines mathematical properties with system constraints.","principle":"The appropriate algorithm is the one whose documented properties satisfy the actual requirements under known constraints.","rule":"Do not select solely from asymptotic complexity or popularity.","correct_example":"Compare candidate algorithms using correctness preconditions, worst-case behavior, memory requirements, update/query workload, implementation complexity, and measured performance when measurement is required.","invalid_example":"Selecting an algorithm solely because it has the lowest Big O notation.","problem":"A theoretically attractive algorithm can violate memory, latency, maintainability, or correctness constraints.","failure_mode":"System behavior fails requirements despite apparently favorable complexity.","cause":"Incomplete decision criteria.","solution":"Use a structured algorithm decision record with explicit constraints and evidence.","alternative":"Use a simple baseline first when requirements are uncertain, then optimize based on verified measurements.","tradeoff":"More analysis improves selection quality but increases design effort.","constraint":"Decision quality is limited by the accuracy of workload and requirement information.","edge_case":"Different workloads may justify different algorithms for the same abstract problem.","security_implication":"Security constraints can eliminate otherwise efficient algorithms if their resource behavior is exploitable.","performance_implication":"Measured benchmarks should be treated as workload-specific evidence, not universal guarantees.","testing_guidance":"Test the selected algorithm against its stated assumptions and acceptance criteria.","verification_guidance":"Verify both the algorithm's theoretical preconditions and implementation-specific invariants.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000002","D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-SEL-000002","D11-ALG-CPL-000002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000002","D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm design and analysis"},{"source":"ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017","source_type":"standard","relevance":"Software lifecycle engineering context"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-SEL-000002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B07","topic":"Algorithm Selection","subtopic":"Baseline Strategy","concept":"Simple baseline implementation","knowledge_type":"STRATEGY","question":"Why can a simple baseline algorithm be valuable before optimization?","answer":"A simple baseline can provide an independently understandable reference for correctness comparison, requirement validation, and later performance evaluation.","explanation":"Complex optimized implementations can introduce subtle defects. A simpler baseline can serve as a differential-testing oracle for small inputs when its correctness is independently established.","principle":"Correctness evidence should precede optimization when practical.","rule":"Do not use a baseline as an oracle unless its own correctness and scope are established.","correct_example":"Compare an optimized sorting or graph algorithm against a simple reference implementation on randomly generated small inputs.","invalid_example":"Assuming the baseline is correct solely because it is simpler.","problem":"A defective reference implementation can validate the same error as the optimized implementation.","failure_mode":"Differential testing reports false agreement.","cause":"Shared implementation assumptions or incorrect oracle.","solution":"Use independent specifications, multiple implementations, mathematical invariants, or trusted references.","alternative":"Property-based testing against independently defined properties.","tradeoff":"Maintaining a baseline adds code but can improve regression detection.","constraint":"Baseline complexity must be acceptable for the test input sizes used.","edge_case":"Two implementations may share the same hidden defect if they use the same flawed logic.","security_implication":"Reference tests must not normalize insecure behavior merely because the baseline reproduces it.","performance_implication":"Baseline implementations are often unsuitable for production workloads.","testing_guidance":"Use small exhaustive or randomized domains where baseline execution is feasible.","verification_guidance":"Establish oracle correctness independently from the implementation under test.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-DES-000001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-SEL-000001","D11-ALG-TST-000001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-DES-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm verification and comparison"},{"source":"ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-1:2022","source_type":"standard","relevance":"Software testing concepts"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

B08 — Algorithmic Failure Modes

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-FAL-000001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B08","topic":"Algorithm Failure Modes","subtopic":"Boundary Errors","concept":"Off-by-one error","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"What is an off-by-one error in an algorithm?","answer":"An off-by-one error occurs when an algorithm's boundary condition, index range, iteration count, or interval representation is shifted by one position or operation from the intended specification.","explanation":"These errors frequently occur in loops, binary search, array slicing, interval algorithms, and partition boundaries.","principle":"Boundary conventions must be explicit and internally consistent.","rule":"Choose and consistently apply inclusive or exclusive interval semantics.","correct_example":"For a half-open interval [left, right), iterate while left is less than right and ensure each update preserves the interval convention.","invalid_example":"Using an exclusive upper bound in one operation and treating the same bound as inclusive in another without adjustment.","problem":"Boundary mistakes can omit valid elements or access invalid positions.","failure_mode":"Incorrect results, out-of-range access, or non-termination.","cause":"Inconsistent index conventions or inadequate boundary tests.","solution":"Define interval semantics explicitly and test zero-, one-, and two-element cases.","alternative":"Use well-tested standard-library range abstractions when their semantics match the requirement.","tradeoff":"Explicit boundary reasoning requires discipline but reduces subtle errors.","constraint":"Indexing conventions vary by language and API.","edge_case":"Empty and singleton collections are especially effective for exposing boundary defects.","security_implication":"Out-of-bounds behavior in memory-unsafe environments can have security consequences.","performance_implication":"Incorrect boundaries may also cause unnecessary iterations or repeated work.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, singleton, smallest valid, largest valid, first, last, and just-outside-boundary cases.","verification_guidance":"Verify every loop boundary against the mathematical domain.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000004"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-SRC-000003","D11-ALG-TST-000001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000004"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm correctness and boundary reasoning"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-FAL-000002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B08","topic":"Algorithm Failure Modes","subtopic":"Input Assumptions","concept":"Violated algorithm precondition","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happens when an algorithm's precondition is violated?","answer":"The algorithm may return an incorrect result, raise an error, enter an invalid state, or provide behavior outside its specification.","explanation":"A precondition is part of the algorithm contract. Correctness guarantees normally apply only when required preconditions hold.","principle":"Hidden assumptions are reliability risks.","rule":"Make critical preconditions explicit and validate them when they can be violated by untrusted or uncontrolled inputs.","correct_example":"Rejecting or handling unsorted input before invoking an algorithm whose correctness requires sorted input.","invalid_example":"Silently applying a sorted-input algorithm to arbitrary input and treating its output as reliable.","problem":"Unvalidated assumptions create silent correctness failures.","failure_mode":"Incorrect results propagate into downstream decisions.","cause":"Precondition not documented, enforced, or preserved.","solution":"Validate, establish, or structurally guarantee the precondition.","alternative":"Use an algorithm without that precondition if validation or maintenance is too costly.","tradeoff":"Validation adds runtime cost but converts silent corruption into explicit handling.","constraint":"Validation strategy must match the trust boundary and system performance requirements.","edge_case":"A precondition may be guaranteed internally but not at an external API boundary.","security_implication":"Incorrect security decisions can result when security-critical algorithms receive invalid state.","performance_implication":"Precondition checks may add cost but can avoid more expensive downstream failure.","testing_guidance":"Include explicit tests for violated preconditions and expected failure behavior.","verification_guidance":"Trace where each precondition is established and ensure it remains valid until use.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-FAL-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm assumptions and correctness"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-FAL-000003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B08","topic":"Algorithm Failure Modes","subtopic":"Complexity Explosion","concept":"Worst-case input amplification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is algorithmic complexity amplification?","answer":"Algorithmic complexity amplification occurs when particular inputs cause substantially more computational work than ordinary inputs, potentially turning an acceptable operation into an impractical or resource-exhausting one.","explanation":"The effect can arise from worst-case algorithm behavior, adversarial input structure, pathological distributions, or unexpected state growth.","principle":"Security and reliability analysis should consider inputs that maximize computational cost.","rule":"Identify worst-case or adversarial complexity for externally influenced inputs when resource exhaustion matters.","correct_example":"Analyzing the worst-case behavior of a search, parser, matching algorithm, or recursive procedure against inputs designed to maximize work.","invalid_example":"Assuming average-case benchmark results establish protection against adversarial inputs.","problem":"Attackers or unusual workloads can exploit expensive execution paths.","failure_mode":"CPU exhaustion, memory exhaustion, latency spikes, queue growth, or service unavailability.","cause":"Unbounded or poorly bounded algorithmic resource consumption.","solution":"Choose bounded algorithms, constrain input size, enforce time/resource limits, or redesign the computation.","alternative":"Use algorithms with stronger worst-case guarantees when the threat model requires them.","tradeoff":"Stronger worst-case guarantees may increase implementation complexity or average-case cost.","constraint":"Resource bounds must reflect the service's actual capacity and availability requirements.","edge_case":"A rare pathological input can dominate operational risk even when average workloads are inexpensive.","security_implication":"Algorithmic complexity attacks can become denial-of-service vectors.","performance_implication":"Worst-case complexity should be evaluated separately from average observed behavior.","testing_guidance":"Include adversarial and worst-case-shaped inputs in performance and resilience tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that resource consumption remains within declared bounds for the threat-relevant input domain.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-FAL-000002","D11-ALG-TST-000002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"OWASP Foundation, OWASP Application Security Verification Standard","source_type":"security_reference","relevance":"Resource exhaustion and secure software considerations"},{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Worst-case algorithm analysis"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

B09 — Algorithm Testing and Verification

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-TST-000001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B09","topic":"Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Boundary Testing","concept":"Algorithm boundary test matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Which boundary cases should an algorithm test suite include?","answer":"Where applicable, tests should cover empty input, singleton input, minimum valid input, maximum supported input, boundary values, duplicate values, absent targets, and invalid inputs.","explanation":"Boundary cases frequently expose incorrect loop conditions, assumptions, state initialization, overflow, and termination defects.","principle":"Test the edges of the algorithm's declared input domain, not only representative middle cases.","rule":"Derive boundary cases from the actual specification rather than applying an unrelated generic checklist.","correct_example":"For a search algorithm, test empty collection, one element, target first, target last, target absent, duplicates, and invalid ordering when ordering is a precondition.","invalid_example":"Testing only a large random input and concluding boundary behavior is correct.","problem":"Middle-of-domain tests can miss boundary defects.","failure_mode":"Production failure occurs for valid but uncommon boundary inputs.","cause":"Insufficient test partitioning.","solution":"Partition the input domain and explicitly test important boundaries and invalid regions.","alternative":"Property-based testing can complement manually selected boundary cases.","tradeoff":"More tests increase maintenance cost but improve defect detection.","constraint":"The test set should remain aligned with the algorithm contract.","edge_case":"Maximum-size tests may require controlled resource environments to avoid destabilizing the test system.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive algorithms require boundary and malformed-input tests at trust boundaries.","performance_implication":"Boundary performance tests should distinguish functional boundaries from workload scaling tests.","testing_guidance":"Record input, precondition, expected output or failure, and reason the case is important.","verification_guidance":"Ensure every declared correctness condition has at least one corresponding verification or test strategy.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-TST-000002","D11-ALG-TST-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-FND-000003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-1:2022","source_type":"standard","relevance":"Software testing concepts"},{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm correctness and edge conditions"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-TST-000002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B09","topic":"Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Property Testing","concept":"Algorithmic property testing","knowledge_type":"TESTING_GUIDANCE","question":"What is a useful property for testing an algorithm?","answer":"A property is a general condition that should hold across many valid inputs, independent of one particular expected output.","explanation":"Examples include permutation preservation for sorting, idempotent properties where specified, conservation properties, monotonicity, or consistency between related operations.","principle":"Properties can test broad behavioral guarantees rather than isolated examples.","rule":"A test property must follow from the actual algorithm specification; intuitive but unproven properties are insufficient.","correct_example":"For a sorting algorithm, verify that the output is ordered and contains exactly the same multiset of elements as the input.","invalid_example":"Testing only that the output is ordered and assuming no elements were lost or duplicated.","problem":"Testing only one aspect of correctness can miss data corruption.","failure_mode":"A defective algorithm passes superficial tests while violating another required property.","cause":"Incomplete correctness oracle.","solution":"Decompose correctness into independent properties derived from the specification.","alternative":"Use a trusted reference implementation for small inputs in addition to properties.","tradeoff":"Multiple properties improve coverage but require careful specification analysis.","constraint":"Properties must be executable or otherwise objectively verifiable.","edge_case":"Empty and duplicate-containing inputs can reveal whether the property is formulated correctly.","security_implication":"Security properties should include authorization and isolation invariants where relevant.","performance_implication":"Property tests can expose scaling problems if generated inputs are progressively increased.","testing_guidance":"Combine deterministic boundary cases with generated cases where appropriate.","verification_guidance":"Trace each property to a formal or documented requirement.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-TST-000001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-TST-000003","D11-ALG-SEL-000002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-TST-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-1:2022","source_type":"standard","relevance":"Testing concepts"},{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm correctness properties"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-TST-000003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B09","topic":"Algorithm Verification","subtopic":"Differential Verification","concept":"Differential testing against a reference","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can an optimized algorithm be checked against a simpler implementation?","answer":"Run both implementations on the same valid test inputs and compare their outputs or specified observable behavior, while independently establishing that the reference implementation is sufficiently trustworthy for the tested domain.","explanation":"Differential testing is useful for finding discrepancies between implementations but does not prove correctness if both implementations share the same defect or the reference is wrong.","principle":"Independent implementations can provide complementary evidence but are not automatically independent specifications.","rule":"Do not treat agreement between two implementations as a complete correctness proof.","correct_example":"Compare an optimized algorithm against a simple reference on many small randomly generated inputs and explicitly test boundary cases.","invalid_example":"Declaring an algorithm verified because two implementations return the same result for one example.","problem":"Shared defects or inadequate test domains can produce false confidence.","failure_mode":"Both implementations agree on an incorrect result.","cause":"Common assumption, common defect, insufficient input diversity, or flawed oracle.","solution":"Combine differential testing with formal properties, independent specifications, boundary tests, and where appropriate proofs.","alternative":"Use mathematical invariants or a formally verified reference.","tradeoff":"Multiple verification methods increase confidence but require additional engineering effort.","constraint":"The comparison requires deterministic or equivalently specified observable behavior.","edge_case":"Nondeterministic algorithms require comparison of allowed result sets or properties rather than exact outputs.","security_implication":"Reference systems must not introduce unsafe behavior or expose sensitive test data.","performance_implication":"The reference implementation may be too slow for large tests, making small-domain differential testing appropriate.","testing_guidance":"Record test-domain generation, reference version, comparison rule, and discrepancies.","verification_guidance":"Treat differential agreement as evidence, not universal proof.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-SEL-000002","D11-ALG-TST-000002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-TST-000002","D11-ALG-SEL-000002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-SEL-000002","D11-ALG-TST-000002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-1:2022","source_type":"standard","relevance":"Testing terminology and evidence"},{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm correctness"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

B10 — Integrated Algorithm Reasoning

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-INT-000001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B10","topic":"Algorithm Reasoning","subtopic":"End-to-End Analysis","concept":"Algorithm reasoning chain","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should the Software Engineering Brain reason about an algorithmic problem?","answer":"The reasoning chain should proceed from problem specification to constraints, candidate algorithm families, correctness requirements, complexity analysis, implementation constraints, failure modes, testing strategy, verification evidence, and operational trade-offs.","explanation":"This separates problem understanding from algorithm selection and separates generated implementation from verified behavior.","principle":"Algorithm reasoning should preserve the distinction between specification, algorithm properties, implementation behavior, and empirical evidence.","rule":"Do not collapse theoretical guarantees, implementation assumptions, benchmark results, and verification results into one undifferentiated confidence claim.","correct_example":"Requirement → input/output contract → constraints → candidate algorithms → correctness conditions → complexity → implementation → tests → observed results → verification status.","invalid_example":"Requirement → choose algorithm → generate code → declare correct.","problem":"Skipping intermediate reasoning stages produces unsupported algorithm choices and weak verification.","failure_mode":"The Brain generates plausible but unsuitable or insufficiently verified implementations.","cause":"Missing structured decision and evidence chain.","solution":"Represent each stage as structured records connected through stable relationships.","alternative":"For trivial algorithms, use a shorter chain while retaining the required correctness and verification evidence.","tradeoff":"Structured reasoning requires more metadata but improves traceability and learning.","constraint":"The reasoning depth should be proportional to algorithmic risk and system impact.","edge_case":"A simple algorithm may have low reasoning complexity but still require detailed handling when used at a security or safety boundary.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive algorithm decisions require explicit threat and resource considerations.","performance_implication":"Performance claims require either theoretical analysis or measured evidence with its conditions clearly recorded.","testing_guidance":"Map each algorithmic requirement to tests and expected outcomes.","verification_guidance":"Maintain separate states for GENERATED, EXECUTED, TESTED, PASSED, and VERIFIED.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-SEL-000001","D11-ALG-TST-000003"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-INT-000002","D11-ALG-INT-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-SEL-000001","D11-ALG-TST-000003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm design, analysis, and correctness"},{"source":"ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017","source_type":"standard","relevance":"Software engineering lifecycle processes"},{"source":"ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-1:2022","source_type":"standard","relevance":"Software testing concepts"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-INT-000002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B10","topic":"Algorithm Reasoning","subtopic":"Decision Evidence","concept":"Theory versus measurement","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should theoretical complexity and measured performance be represented together?","answer":"Theoretical complexity should describe asymptotic behavior under stated assumptions, while measurements should record actual observed behavior under a specified environment and workload.","explanation":"Neither replaces the other. Theory supports scalability reasoning; measurement captures implementation and environment effects.","principle":"Evidence type must remain attached to the claim it supports.","rule":"Never convert a benchmark result into a universal complexity claim or convert a complexity class into an empirical performance measurement.","correct_example":"Record worst-case O(n log n) separately from a benchmark showing a measured median latency under a named workload and environment.","invalid_example":"Claiming that an algorithm is empirically 2 times faster in general because it was 2 times faster in one benchmark.","problem":"Evidence scope is lost when theoretical and empirical claims are merged.","failure_mode":"The Brain retrieves an environment-specific result as a universal fact.","cause":"Missing evidence classification and benchmark context.","solution":"Store claim type, evidence type, environment, workload, methodology, and limitations separately.","alternative":"Use only theoretical analysis when measurement is unavailable, while marking empirical performance unknown.","tradeoff":"More metadata improves evidence quality but increases record complexity.","constraint":"Measurements are valid only within their documented conditions.","edge_case":"Hardware, compiler, runtime, input distribution, and implementation changes can alter measured performance.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive performance claims require adversarial workloads where appropriate.","performance_implication":"Measured results should include workload and measurement methodology.","testing_guidance":"Repeat measurements sufficiently to identify variability when a performance claim matters.","verification_guidance":"Verify the provenance and methodology of every empirical performance claim.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-INT-000001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-CPL-000001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Theoretical algorithm analysis"},{"source":"ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-1:2022","source_type":"standard","relevance":"Testing and evidence terminology"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-INT-000003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D11-B10","topic":"Algorithm Reasoning","subtopic":"Brain Retrieval","concept":"Algorithm knowledge retrieval contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What information should retrieval return when the Brain asks for an algorithm?","answer":"Retrieval should return the algorithm's applicable problem class, preconditions, correctness properties, complexity, implementation constraints, alternatives, failure modes, testing guidance, verification guidance, and evidence provenance relevant to the requested context.","explanation":"Returning only an algorithm name or code snippet is insufficient for reliable autonomous software engineering.","principle":"Algorithm knowledge must be retrievable as decision-support evidence rather than isolated facts.","rule":"Context-filter algorithm records by language, version, input constraints, correctness requirements, and operational goals when those dimensions are specified.","correct_example":"A query for sorted-array lookup retrieves binary-search records together with the sortedness precondition, interval invariant, complexity, duplicate semantics, failure modes, and testing guidance.","invalid_example":"Returning binary search code without its sorted-input precondition when the caller has not established ordering.","problem":"Context-free retrieval can cause the Brain to apply valid knowledge to an invalid situation.","failure_mode":"Wrong algorithm selection or incorrect code generation.","cause":"Missing prerequisites, constraints, or relationship metadata in retrieval.","solution":"Index algorithm records by concept, applicability, prerequisites, constraints, complexity, and relationships.","alternative":"Use a staged retrieval process: candidate algorithms first, then contextual constraints and verification evidence.","tradeoff":"Richer retrieval improves reasoning quality but increases indexing and query complexity.","constraint":"Retrieved records must preserve provenance and validation state.","edge_case":"Multiple algorithms may satisfy the same functional requirement under different constraints.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive algorithm queries should retrieve applicable security constraints and failure modes alongside implementation knowledge.","performance_implication":"Performance-related retrieval should distinguish theoretical complexity from measured evidence.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate retrieval using exact, conceptual, debugging, code-generation, testing, and comparison queries.","verification_guidance":"Retrieved knowledge should carry validation status so the Brain does not treat unverified records as verified facts.","language":"language-independent","version_range":"language-independent","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-INT-000001","D11-ALG-INT-000002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-SEL-000001","D11-ALG-TST-000003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-INT-000001","D11-ALG-INT-000002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms, 4th Edition","source_type":"technical_literature","relevance":"Algorithm knowledge"},{"source":"ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017","source_type":"standard","relevance":"Software engineering knowledge lifecycle context"}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"VERIFIED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

