{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B11-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Searching","subtopic":"Problem Model","concept":"Search Problem","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a search problem in algorithmic computation?","answer":"A search algorithm determines whether a target condition is satisfied by an input collection or identifies one or more input positions or values satisfying that condition.","explanation":"The search task must define the input domain, target predicate, required output, and behavior when no match exists. Different search algorithms exploit different structural properties of the input.","principle":"Search algorithm choice must follow the guarantees available about the input representation and target predicate.","correct_example":"Given sequence [8,3,5,1] and target 5, return index 2 under zero-based indexing.","invalid_example":"Returning index 1 for target 5 without evidence that the returned position contains the target.","constraint":"The required output semantics must be defined before algorithm selection.","edge_case":"Empty input; duplicate matches; target absent; invalid target type.","testing_guidance":"Test present, absent, first-position, last-position, duplicate, and empty inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every returned match satisfies the target predicate and that absence is reported only when the algorithm's search domain has been exhausted.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["input/output specification"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B11-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"English","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B11-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Searching","subtopic":"Linear Search","concept":"Linear Search","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"How does linear search operate?","answer":"Linear search examines candidate elements sequentially until the target is found or all candidates have been examined.","explanation":"Linear search requires no ordering assumption. It can operate on arbitrary finite sequences and is therefore a general baseline search method.","principle":"When no exploitable ordering or indexing structure is guaranteed, sequential examination provides a direct correct strategy.","correct_example":"For [7,4,9,2], searching for 9 examines 7, then 4, then 9 and returns its position.","invalid_example":"Skipping an element while claiming a complete linear search was performed.","complexity":"Worst-case time O(n); best-case time O(1); auxiliary space O(1) for the basic iterative form.","tradeoff":"Linear search has minimal structural requirements but may require examining most or all elements.","edge_case":"Empty sequence requires immediate no-match behavior.","testing_guidance":"Verify early termination when a match is found and complete traversal when no match exists.","verification_guidance":"For a successful result, verify returned element satisfies target predicate. For failure, verify every candidate was considered.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D11-ALG-B11-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B11-0003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B11-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B12-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"English","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B11-0003","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Searching","subtopic":"Linear Search","concept":"Duplicate Match Semantics","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What must be specified when a search target can occur multiple times?","answer":"The algorithm contract must specify whether to return any match, the first match, the last match, all matches, or a count.","explanation":"A search implementation can be algorithmically correct for one contract and incorrect for another. Duplicate handling is therefore part of the output specification rather than an incidental implementation detail.","principle":"Correctness is defined relative to the requested result semantics.","correct_example":"A first-match search over [2,5,5,7] returns index 1.","invalid_example":"Returning index 2 when the contract explicitly requires the first occurrence.","failure_mode":"Ambiguous duplicate semantics can produce apparently correct but contractually incorrect results.","testing_guidance":"Include repeated targets and verify exact contract semantics.","verification_guidance":"Compare returned result against an independently specified duplicate policy.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D11-ALG-B11-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B13-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B11-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Algorithm correctness principles synthesized from standard algorithm specifications","source_ref":"ALGORITHM-CORRECTNESS-FOUNDATION"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B12-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Searching","subtopic":"Binary Search","concept":"Binary Search","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"What is the fundamental idea of binary search?","answer":"Binary search repeatedly reduces an ordered search interval by comparing the target with a midpoint and retaining only the interval that can still contain a valid answer.","explanation":"Binary search depends on an ordered search domain or an equivalent monotonic predicate. Each valid iteration eliminates a portion of the remaining candidate space.","principle":"A search interval can be safely discarded only when the ordering or monotonicity invariant proves that it cannot contain a valid answer.","complexity":"Worst-case time O(log n); auxiliary space O(1) for the standard iterative formulation.","correct_example":"Searching for 7 in sorted [1,3,5,7,9] repeatedly narrows the interval around the target.","invalid_example":"Applying ordinary binary search to an unsorted sequence without first establishing an equivalent ordering property.","failure_mode":"Using binary search without its required ordering invariant can silently return incorrect results.","cause":"The discarded half may still contain the target.","testing_guidance":"Test sorted inputs, singleton inputs, empty inputs, absent targets, boundaries, and duplicates.","verification_guidance":"Maintain and verify the invariant that any possible valid target remains inside the active search interval.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D11-ALG-B11-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B12-0002","D11-ALG-B13-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B11-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B11-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B12-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Searching","subtopic":"Binary Search","concept":"Search Interval Invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariant is central to binary search correctness?","answer":"At the beginning of each iteration, if the target exists according to the search contract, a valid occurrence remains inside the active search interval.","explanation":"The invariant justifies discarding the portion of the domain outside the retained interval. Initialization, preservation, and termination establish correctness.","principle":"Every interval reduction must be justified by the ordering predicate.","correct_example":"After comparing midpoint value with target, discard the lower half only when the ordering proves the target cannot be there.","invalid_example":"Discarding a half because it appears unlikely to contain the target.","testing_guidance":"Test targets immediately adjacent to interval boundaries and absent values between existing values.","verification_guidance":"Check initialization, preservation after each branch, and terminal-condition correctness.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D11-ALG-B12-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B12-0003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B12-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B12-0003","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Searching","subtopic":"Binary Search","concept":"Midpoint Calculation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What implementation risk exists in midpoint calculation?","answer":"An implementation that computes a midpoint by adding two large indices before division can overflow in fixed-width integer environments.","explanation":"The mathematical midpoint may be representable while the intermediate sum is not. A formulation based on the lower bound plus a bounded offset avoids that particular intermediate overflow pattern.","failure_mode":"Integer overflow during midpoint calculation.","cause":"The sum of two valid indices exceeds the integer representation range.","solution":"Use an overflow-safe midpoint formulation appropriate to the implementation language and integer model.","security_implication":"Unexpected integer overflow can corrupt control flow or cause invalid memory access in unsafe environments.","testing_guidance":"Use large index values near the integer limit in environments where overflow is possible.","verification_guidance":"Inspect generated machine-level or language-level arithmetic semantics when integer overflow behavior matters.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"implementation-dependent","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B12-0001"],"prerequisites":["fixed-width integer semantics"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B13-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B12-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Algorithm implementation safety principle","source_ref":"ALGORITHM-IMPLEMENTATION-SAFETY"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B13-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Searching","subtopic":"Binary Search Variants","concept":"Lower Bound","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What does a lower-bound binary search compute?","answer":"A lower-bound search identifies the first position at which an element satisfying a specified threshold condition could be inserted while preserving the required ordering.","explanation":"The target does not need to be present. The algorithm searches for a boundary in a monotonic predicate such as value >= target.","principle":"Boundary search is often more general and composable than exact-match search.","correct_example":"For sorted [1,2,4,4,7], lower_bound(4) returns index 2.","invalid_example":"Returning an arbitrary occurrence of 4 when the contract requires the first valid position.","testing_guidance":"Test values below minimum, equal to duplicates, between values, equal to maximum, and above maximum.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every position before the returned index fails the predicate and the returned position satisfies it, when one exists.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B12-0001"],"prerequisites":["monotonic predicate"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B13-0002","D11-ALG-B14-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B12-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":["D11-ALG-B12-0001"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B13-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Searching","subtopic":"Binary Search Variants","concept":"First and Last Occurrence","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can binary search find the first or last occurrence of a duplicated target?","answer":"Modify the boundary condition so that a match does not immediately terminate the search; instead continue toward the required boundary while preserving the search invariant.","explanation":"The key distinction is output semantics. Exact-match search seeks any valid occurrence, whereas first/last occurrence search seeks an extremal valid position.","tradeoff":"Boundary variants require more careful termination and invariant reasoning than any-match search.","correct_example":"For [2,4,4,4,9], first occurrence of 4 is index 1 and last occurrence is index 3.","invalid_example":"Stopping at the first encountered 4 when the contract requires the last occurrence.","testing_guidance":"Use arrays with zero, one, two, and many duplicates at boundaries and in the middle.","verification_guidance":"For first occurrence, verify no earlier position satisfies the target. For last occurrence, verify no later position satisfies it.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B13-0001"],"prerequisites":["ordered input","boundary semantics"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B13-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B13-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Algorithm boundary-search reasoning","source_ref":"ALGORITHM-CORRECTNESS-FOUNDATION"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B14-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Searching","subtopic":"Predicate Search","concept":"Monotonic Predicate Search","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"When can binary-search reasoning be applied without searching directly for a value?","answer":"Binary-search reasoning applies when the search domain is ordered and the feasibility or truth predicate changes monotonically across that domain.","explanation":"The searched object can be an answer value rather than an element of the input. The essential requirement is that one side of a candidate boundary is feasible and the other side is infeasible in a monotonic manner.","correct_example":"Finding the smallest capacity that can satisfy all workload constraints when feasibility is monotonic with capacity.","invalid_example":"Binary-searching an arbitrary non-monotonic feasibility function.","constraint":"Monotonicity must be established or proven rather than assumed.","testing_guidance":"Test predicate transitions around the expected boundary and deliberately include non-monotonic cases to ensure the precondition is enforced.","verification_guidance":"Validate monotonicity over the relevant domain or derive it from the problem specification.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B12-0001"],"prerequisites":["ordered search space","monotonic predicate"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B14-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B12-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B11-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B14-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Searching","subtopic":"Predicate Search","concept":"Answer-Space Binary Search","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is answer-space binary search?","answer":"Answer-space binary search searches a bounded ordered set of possible answers using a feasibility predicate rather than directly searching input elements.","explanation":"The algorithm repeatedly asks whether a candidate answer is feasible and narrows the answer domain according to the monotonic direction of feasibility.","tradeoff":"It can transform optimization problems into logarithmic feasibility-query searches, but the feasibility predicate may itself be computationally expensive.","performance_implication":"Total complexity is the number of predicate evaluations multiplied by the predicate cost, plus search overhead.","edge_case":"No feasible answer, all answers feasible, or an unbounded answer domain requiring a separate bounding strategy.","testing_guidance":"Test boundary answers and verify that the feasibility predicate obeys the expected monotonic relation.","verification_guidance":"Prove that the predicate is monotonic and that the returned boundary satisfies the optimization contract.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B14-0001"],"prerequisites":["monotonic feasibility"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B13-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B14-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Algorithmic optimization-search pattern derived from binary-search principles","source_ref":"ALGORITHM-BINARY-SEARCH-PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B15-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Sorting Model","concept":"Sorting Contract","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What must a sorting algorithm guarantee?","answer":"A sorting algorithm must transform a collection into an order satisfying the specified comparison or ordering relation while preserving the required elements and multiplicities unless the contract explicitly permits otherwise.","explanation":"Sorting correctness has at least two dimensions: ordering correctness and preservation of the input's required contents. Stability, mutation, and output representation are separate contract properties.","principle":"Sorting correctness must be defined against both the ordering relation and preservation requirements.","correct_example":"Sorting [3,1,2,1] ascending yields a sequence ordered according to <= while retaining two occurrences of 1.","invalid_example":"Dropping a duplicate during sorting and still reporting the operation as correct.","testing_guidance":"Verify ordering, length, multiplicity, and required mutation/output semantics.","verification_guidance":"Check that adjacent output elements satisfy the ordering relation and that the output is a permutation of the input when permutation preservation is required.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["ordering relation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B15-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B15-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Sorting Properties","concept":"Stable Sorting","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does sorting stability mean?","answer":"A stable sorting algorithm preserves the relative order of records that compare equal under the sorting key.","explanation":"Stability matters when records contain secondary information and sorting is performed by one key. Stable sorting preserves the prior ordering of equal-key records.","correct_example":"Records [(A,2),(B,1),(C,2)] sorted by the numeric key remain A before C among the key-2 records.","invalid_example":"Moving C before A while both have equal sort keys when stability is required.","tradeoff":"Stability may constrain implementation choices or add overhead depending on the algorithm and representation.","testing_guidance":"Use tagged equal-key records and verify relative ordering after sorting.","verification_guidance":"Compare relative order of equal-key elements before and after sorting.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"prerequisites":["sorting contract"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B17-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B15-0003","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Comparison Sorting","concept":"Comparison-Based Sorting","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What distinguishes comparison-based sorting?","answer":"A comparison-based sorting algorithm determines ordering through comparisons between elements rather than relying on direct access to bounded key values.","explanation":"Comparison-based algorithms include insertion sort, merge sort, heap sort, and quicksort. Their generality comes from operating through an ordering relation rather than requiring a specialized key domain.","principle":"The information available from pairwise comparisons limits the achievable asymptotic performance of general comparison sorting.","tradeoff":"General applicability is obtained at the cost of stronger asymptotic lower-bound constraints than specialized non-comparison sorting can sometimes achieve.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"prerequisites":["ordering relation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B16-0001","D11-ALG-B17-0001","D11-ALG-B18-0001","D11-ALG-B19-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B16-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Elementary Sorting","concept":"Insertion Sort","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"How does insertion sort work?","answer":"Insertion sort incrementally builds a sorted prefix by taking the next element and inserting it into its correct position within the already sorted prefix.","explanation":"The algorithm maintains an invariant that the processed prefix is sorted. Each iteration extends the sorted prefix by one element.","complexity":"Worst-case time O(n^2); best-case time O(n) for an already ordered sequence under the standard implementation; auxiliary space O(1).","principle":"A locally maintained sorted prefix can be extended one element at a time.","correct_example":"Sorting [3,1,2] processes 3, inserts 1 before 3, then inserts 2 between 1 and 3.","invalid_example":"Moving an element without preserving the remaining elements during shifting.","tradeoff":"Simple, in-place, and useful for small or nearly sorted inputs, but quadratic in the general worst case.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, singleton, already sorted, reverse sorted, duplicate, and nearly sorted inputs.","verification_guidance":"After each iteration, verify that the processed prefix is sorted and contains exactly the original prefix elements.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"prerequisites":["ordered comparison"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B16-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B17-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B16-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Elementary Sorting","concept":"Selection Sort","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"How does selection sort operate?","answer":"Selection sort repeatedly selects the smallest remaining element and places it at the next output position.","explanation":"At each iteration, a prefix is finalized and the remaining suffix is searched for the next minimum according to the ordering relation.","complexity":"Time O(n^2) in best, average, and worst cases for the standard comparison implementation; auxiliary space O(1).","principle":"Each iteration permanently selects the next element of the sorted output.","tradeoff":"Simple and in-place, but generally performs quadratic comparisons and does not exploit existing order effectively.","edge_case":"Repeated equal values require the implementation to respect any stated stability or identity requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test sorted, reverse-sorted, duplicate-heavy, empty, and singleton inputs.","verification_guidance":"After each iteration, verify that the finalized prefix contains the correct smallest elements and satisfies the ordering invariant.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"prerequisites":["ordering relation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B16-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B16-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B17-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Merge Sort","concept":"Merge Sort","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"What is the core strategy of merge sort?","answer":"Merge sort recursively divides the input into smaller subproblems, sorts each subproblem, and merges the sorted results.","explanation":"The merge operation combines two already sorted sequences into one sorted sequence. Correctness follows from the correctness of recursive sorting and the merge procedure.","complexity":"Time O(n log n) in standard implementations; auxiliary space commonly O(n) for array-based merging.","principle":"Solving independent smaller sorting problems and efficiently combining their results yields predictable logarithmic-depth behavior.","correct_example":"Split [5,2,4,1] into smaller sequences, recursively sort them, then merge them in order.","invalid_example":"Merging two sequences that are not actually sorted while assuming the merge step guarantees global correctness.","tradeoff":"Predictable O(n log n) behavior and natural stability versus additional memory for common array implementations.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate keys, already sorted data, reverse order, odd sizes, empty partitions, and singleton partitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify recursive subproblem correctness and separately verify the merge invariant.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"prerequisites":["recursion","divide-and-conquer","ordered comparison"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B17-0002","D11-ALG-B18-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B16-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B17-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Merge Sort","concept":"Merge Invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariant supports correctness of the merge operation?","answer":"At every merge step, the output prefix is sorted and contains the smallest remaining elements from the two input sequences.","explanation":"Because both input sequences are sorted, comparing their current front elements identifies the smallest remaining element. Repeating this step constructs the complete sorted result.","correct_example":"When merging [1,4,8] and [2,3,9], repeatedly emit the smaller current front element.","invalid_example":"Selecting an element that is not the smallest among the two current fronts.","testing_guidance":"Test equal values, empty left or right input, one-element inputs, and all elements from one side smaller than the other.","verification_guidance":"Check ordering and preservation of all elements after merge.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B17-0001"],"prerequisites":["sorted input sequences"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B17-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B17-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B18-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Quicksort","concept":"Quicksort","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"What is the core strategy of quicksort?","answer":"Quicksort selects a pivot, partitions the input around the pivot according to a chosen partition rule, and recursively sorts the resulting subranges.","explanation":"The partition operation is the critical component. Correctness depends on placing elements into regions whose ordering relative to the pivot supports recursive completion.","complexity":"Expected or average behavior is commonly O(n log n) under suitable pivot behavior; worst-case time is O(n^2) for standard comparison-based formulations with highly unbalanced partitions.","principle":"Partitioning converts one sorting problem into smaller problems whose relative position is constrained by the pivot.","tradeoff":"Quicksort can provide strong practical performance and low auxiliary memory for some in-place implementations, but partition quality strongly affects worst-case behavior.","failure_mode":"Repeatedly choosing pivots that produce highly unbalanced partitions.","cause":"Input structure or deterministic pivot policy can repeatedly create subproblems of size n-1 and 0.","testing_guidance":"Test sorted, reverse-sorted, duplicate-heavy, random, and adversarially shaped inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify partition postconditions before trusting recursive sorting results.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"prerequisites":["recursion","partitioning","ordering relation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B18-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B17-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B18-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Quicksort","concept":"Partition Correctness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must a correct quicksort partition establish?","answer":"The partition operation must establish a postcondition under which recursive sorting of the resulting regions produces a globally ordered result.","explanation":"Exact partition placement depends on the partition scheme. A robust specification states which elements are guaranteed to be on each side of the pivot or partition boundary.","principle":"Partition correctness must be specified independently of the particular implementation scheme.","failure_mode":"Mixing assumptions from one partition scheme with another implementation.","cause":"Different partition procedures use different index movement and boundary invariants.","testing_guidance":"Use duplicates, pivot extremes, singleton ranges, and already partitioned inputs.","verification_guidance":"Check the exact partition postcondition selected by the implementation.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B18-0001"],"prerequisites":["partition specification"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B18-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B18-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B19-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Heap Sort","concept":"Heap Sort","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"How does heap sort use a heap to sort data?","answer":"Heap sort builds a heap-based ordering structure and repeatedly extracts an extreme element while maintaining the heap invariant.","explanation":"A binary heap provides efficient access to an extreme element while preserving a structural ordering invariant. Heap sort uses repeated extraction to construct the sorted result.","complexity":"Time O(n log n) in worst, average, and best cases for the standard comparison-based formulation; auxiliary space can be O(1) for an in-place implementation.","principle":"A maintained priority structure can repeatedly expose the next required sorted element.","tradeoff":"Predictable asymptotic time and in-place operation versus weaker locality or practical constant factors compared with some alternatives.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, singleton, duplicates, already sorted, reverse sorted, and large random inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify heap invariant after construction and after each extraction/update.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B15-0003"],"prerequisites":["heap invariant","comparison ordering"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B19-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B15-0003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B17-0001","D11-ALG-B18-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B19-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Heap Sort","concept":"Heap Invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariant is essential to heap-based sorting?","answer":"The heap property must hold for every relevant parent-child relationship after heap construction and after each heap update.","explanation":"Heap sort depends on the heap exposing the required extreme element. If an update violates the heap property, later extractions cannot be trusted.","failure_mode":"Incorrect sift-up or sift-down logic corrupts the heap invariant.","cause":"Boundary errors, incorrect parent-child calculations, or premature termination.","testing_guidance":"Check heap validity after construction and after every mutation in debug/test implementations.","verification_guidance":"Validate every parent-child relation against the selected heap ordering rule.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B19-0001"],"prerequisites":["heap representation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B19-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B19-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B20-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Algorithm Comparison","concept":"Sorting Algorithm Selection","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Which properties should an algorithm selector consider when choosing a sorting algorithm?","answer":"The selector should consider required asymptotic guarantees, expected input distribution, stability requirements, memory constraints, mutation requirements, key structure, implementation environment, and correctness constraints.","explanation":"No single sorting algorithm dominates across all contracts and environments. Selection must be driven by explicit requirements rather than algorithm popularity alone.","principle":"Algorithm selection is a constrained engineering decision based on workload and contract properties.","correct_example":"Choose a stable algorithm when equal-key record order is semantically meaningful.","invalid_example":"Choosing an algorithm solely because its theoretical complexity is favorable while ignoring mandatory stability or memory constraints.","tradeoff":"An algorithm with stronger theoretical guarantees may have different memory, locality, implementation complexity, or constant-factor characteristics.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark only with representative workloads when performance claims are required; otherwise classify performance from established complexity properties.","verification_guidance":"Verify that selected algorithm satisfies all hard contract requirements before comparing secondary performance properties.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B15-0001","D11-ALG-B15-0002","D11-ALG-B17-0001","D11-ALG-B18-0001","D11-ALG-B19-0001"],"prerequisites":["sorting contract","complexity analysis"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B20-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B15-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B20-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Sorting","subtopic":"Algorithm Comparison","concept":"Sorting Property Matrix","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should sorting algorithms be compared without reducing the comparison to a single ranking?","answer":"Compare them across independent properties including worst-case time, typical or expected behavior where established, auxiliary space, stability, in-place capability, adaptability to existing order, implementation complexity, and input assumptions.","explanation":"Different algorithms optimize different constraints. A multidimensional comparison preserves information needed by an engineering reasoning system.","principle":"Algorithm comparison should preserve independent dimensions rather than collapse them into a single score.","correct_example":"Represent merge sort as stable with O(n log n) time and commonly O(n) auxiliary array space, while heap sort provides O(n log n) worst-case time with in-place formulations but is not stable by default.","invalid_example":"Claiming one sorting algorithm is universally superior without specifying workload and contract assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Use property-specific tests rather than a single aggregate test.","verification_guidance":"Trace each claimed property to a proof, specification, authoritative reference, or executed measurement. Do not label unmeasured practical performance as measured fact.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B15-0002","D11-ALG-B17-0001","D11-ALG-B18-0001","D11-ALG-B19-0001"],"prerequisites":["sorting properties","complexity"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B20-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B20-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms; NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-ALGORITHMS;NIST-DADS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"language-agnostic","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B21-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Recursion","subtopic":"Fundamentals","concept":"Recursive Algorithm","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a recursive algorithm?","answer":"A recursive algorithm solves a problem by invoking itself on one or more smaller or simpler instances of the same problem until a base case can be solved directly.","explanation":"A recursive design requires a well-defined reduction from the current problem to simpler subproblems, one or more base cases, and a mechanism that eventually reaches those base cases.","principle":"Every recursive call must move the computation toward a valid terminating condition.","correct_example":"A recursive function that computes factorial reduces factorial(n) to n multiplied by factorial(n-1), with factorial(0) as a base case.","invalid_example":"A function that calls itself with the same arguments indefinitely without a terminating condition.","failure_mode":"Non-terminating recursion.","cause":"The recursive state does not progress toward a reachable base case.","solution":"Define a decreasing or otherwise well-founded termination measure and prove that recursive calls reduce it.","testing_guidance":"Test all base cases and representative recursive inputs; include inputs near the termination boundary.","verification_guidance":"Verify base-case correctness and prove that every recursive transition moves toward a base case.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["problem specification","termination reasoning"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B21-0002","D11-ALG-B22-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B21-0003"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS-RECURSION"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B21-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Recursion","subtopic":"Fundamentals","concept":"Base Case","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What role does a base case play in recursion?","answer":"A base case directly solves a sufficiently simple instance without making another recursive call.","explanation":"The base case anchors both correctness and termination. Recursive cases reduce the problem until one or more base cases are reached.","principle":"A recursive specification is incomplete if its reachable base cases are undefined or incorrect.","correct_example":"For factorial, factorial(0)=1 provides a direct base case.","invalid_example":"Defining factorial(n)=n*factorial(n-1) without defining factorial(0).","failure_mode":"Missing or unreachable base case.","cause":"The recursive transition cannot terminate through the specified state space.","solution":"Define base cases that are reachable from every valid recursive input or explicitly reject unsupported inputs.","edge_case":"The smallest valid input may itself be a base case.","testing_guidance":"Test every base case directly and verify recursive calls eventually enter one.","verification_guidance":"Prove reachability of a base case for every valid input.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B21-0001"],"prerequisites":["recursive algorithm"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B21-0001","D11-ALG-B21-0004"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B21-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS-RECURSION"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B21-0003","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Recursion","subtopic":"Fundamentals","concept":"Recursion Versus Iteration","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should recursive and iterative formulations be compared?","answer":"Compare them using correctness structure, termination reasoning, call-stack requirements, memory usage, implementation clarity, control-flow requirements, and workload characteristics rather than assuming recursion is inherently superior or inferior.","explanation":"Many recursive algorithms have iterative equivalents. Recursion can express hierarchical or self-similar problems naturally, while iteration can avoid recursive call-stack growth.","tradeoff":"Recursion can simplify structural reasoning but may introduce stack-depth or call overhead depending on the language and implementation.","constraint":"The selected formulation must respect the execution environment's recursion and stack limits.","testing_guidance":"Test equivalent recursive and iterative implementations against the same oracle when both exist.","verification_guidance":"Verify semantic equivalence rather than assuming identical control structures imply identical behavior.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B21-0001"],"prerequisites":["recursive algorithm","iteration"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B22-0004"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B21-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS-RECURSION"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B22-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Recursion","subtopic":"Correctness","concept":"Recursive Correctness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How is correctness of a recursive algorithm established?","answer":"Establish correctness of the base cases and prove that if recursive calls correctly solve their smaller subproblems, the current invocation correctly combines or transforms those results into the required solution.","explanation":"This is an inductive correctness argument. The recursive algorithm must preserve the problem specification across the reduction and reconstruction steps.","principle":"Recursive correctness depends on both a correct base case and a valid inductive step.","correct_example":"For recursive sum, prove the base case and show that sum(n)=n+sum(n-1) preserves the required result when sum(n-1) is correct.","invalid_example":"Testing several inputs and declaring a recursive algorithm mathematically correct without establishing the recursive invariant or induction argument.","testing_guidance":"Use property-based or exhaustive tests for small domains to supplement, not replace, the correctness argument.","verification_guidance":"Record base-case proof, recursive-case assumption, and inductive conclusion separately.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B21-0001","D11-ALG-B21-0002"],"prerequisites":["inductive reasoning"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B22-0002","D11-ALG-B23-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B21-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms, Recursive Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-RECURRENCE-LECTURE"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B22-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Recursion","subtopic":"Correctness","concept":"Inductive Structure","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is induction closely related to recursive algorithm correctness?","answer":"A recursive algorithm defines solutions for smaller instances and derives the current solution from those smaller instances, mirroring the structure of mathematical induction.","explanation":"The recursive call supplies the inductive hypothesis, while the current computation supplies the inductive step.","principle":"The structure of the proof should mirror the structure of the recursive decomposition.","correct_example":"Prove correctness for size n by assuming correctness for smaller sizes and showing that the current invocation constructs the correct result.","invalid_example":"Assuming the entire recursive algorithm is correct merely because the recursive call returns a value.","verification_guidance":"Identify the exact property assumed for recursive results and demonstrate how the current step preserves it.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B22-0001"],"prerequisites":["recursive correctness"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B23-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B22-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms, Recursive Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-RECURRENCE-LECTURE"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B22-0003","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Recursion","subtopic":"Failure Modes","concept":"Stack Exhaustion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What can happen when recursion depth becomes too large?","answer":"An implementation may exceed the execution environment's available call-stack capacity and terminate with a stack-related failure or equivalent runtime error.","explanation":"The asymptotic algorithmic space bound and the concrete call-stack behavior are related but not identical. Each recursive invocation may consume stack resources.","failure_mode":"Runtime failure caused by excessive recursion depth.","cause":"Input size or recursive structure creates more simultaneously active calls than the environment supports.","solution":"Use an iterative formulation, restructure recursion, reduce depth, or use an environment-supported stack strategy where appropriate.","performance_implication":"Recursive call frames add memory overhead relative to an equivalent constant-stack iterative implementation.","edge_case":"Highly skewed recursive structures can create depth proportional to input size.","testing_guidance":"Test near and beyond expected recursion-depth boundaries in the target runtime.","verification_guidance":"Determine maximum feasible recursion depth experimentally only when runtime behavior is relevant; mark measurements as environment-specific.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"runtime-dependent","version":"environment-dependent","version_range":"environment-specific","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B21-0001"],"prerequisites":["runtime stack model"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B21-0003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B21-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Derived implementation constraint from recursive execution model","source_ref":"RECURSION-RUNTIME-SAFETY"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B23-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Foundations","concept":"Divide and Conquer","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is the divide-and-conquer algorithmic paradigm?","answer":"Divide and conquer solves a problem by dividing it into smaller subproblems, recursively solving those subproblems, and combining their solutions into a solution for the original problem.","explanation":"The three conceptual stages are divide, conquer, and combine. Depending on the algorithm, one or more stages may be trivial.","principle":"The subproblem structure must make the original solution derivable from correctly solved smaller instances.","correct_example":"Merge sort divides an array into smaller ranges, recursively sorts them, and merges the sorted ranges.","invalid_example":"Dividing the input into unrelated fragments without a valid method for combining their results into the required solution.","tradeoff":"Recursive decomposition can reduce problem complexity but may introduce additional recursion, copying, or combination costs.","testing_guidance":"Test the divide operation, recursive subproblem coverage, and combine operation independently where practical.","verification_guidance":"Verify that subproblems cover exactly the required input domain and that the combine step preserves correctness.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["recursion","problem decomposition"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B23-0002","D11-ALG-B24-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS-DIVIDE-AND-CONQUER"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B23-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Decomposition","concept":"Subproblem Definition","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must a divide-and-conquer subproblem definition specify?","answer":"It must identify the state or input region represented by the subproblem, the result computed for that state, the recursive relation to smaller states, the base cases, and how the original result is obtained.","explanation":"A precise subproblem definition prevents ambiguity in recursive calls and makes correctness and complexity analysis possible.","principle":"The recursive state is part of the algorithm specification, not merely an implementation detail.","correct_example":"Define a subproblem as sorting the half-open interval [lo,hi) and state exactly what the recursive result guarantees.","invalid_example":"Using a recursive function with indices whose meaning changes between calls.","failure_mode":"Inconsistent subproblem boundaries.","cause":"Recursive calls use incompatible interval conventions or state meanings.","solution":"Choose and document one representation such as inclusive or half-open intervals and preserve it across all calls.","testing_guidance":"Test zero-length, one-element, adjacent, and full-range subproblems.","verification_guidance":"Trace representative recursive calls and verify that every subproblem's state has the documented meaning.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B23-0001"],"prerequisites":["problem specification"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B24-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B23-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms, Recursive Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-RECURRENCE-LECTURE"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B24-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Recurrences","concept":"Recurrence Relation","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does a recurrence relation represent in algorithm analysis?","answer":"A recurrence relation expresses the cost or another measurable property of a recursive computation in terms of the corresponding property for its smaller subproblems.","explanation":"For divide-and-conquer algorithms, a recurrence commonly describes recursive subproblem costs plus the work performed outside the recursive calls.","principle":"The recurrence must correspond to the actual recursive structure and non-recursive work of the implementation.","correct_example":"For an algorithm making two half-size recursive calls and linear combine work, a representative time recurrence is T(n)=2T(n/2)+Theta(n), subject to appropriate base conditions.","invalid_example":"Writing T(n)=T(n/2) when the implementation actually makes two independent recursive calls.","testing_guidance":"Inspect call structure and measure non-recursive work conceptually before deriving the recurrence.","verification_guidance":"Map every recursive call and non-recursive operation to a term in the recurrence.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B23-0001"],"prerequisites":["complexity analysis","recursive structure"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B24-0002","D11-ALG-B25-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B23-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-RECURRENCE-LECTURE"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B24-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Recurrences","concept":"Balanced Versus Unbalanced Decomposition","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why does the balance of recursive subproblems matter?","answer":"Balanced recursive decomposition often produces logarithmic recursion depth, while repeatedly creating highly unbalanced subproblems can produce substantially greater depth and cost.","explanation":"The recurrence structure, not the label of the algorithm, determines the resulting asymptotic behavior.","correct_example":"Repeatedly dividing an input into approximately equal halves produces logarithmic depth.","invalid_example":"Assuming every divide-and-conquer algorithm has O(log n) depth regardless of partition quality.","performance_implication":"Unbalanced recursion can increase total work and stack depth.","testing_guidance":"Include balanced and deliberately skewed inputs when partition quality depends on input.","verification_guidance":"Derive the recursion depth from the actual subproblem-size sequence.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B24-0001"],"prerequisites":["recurrence relation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B25-0002","D11-ALG-B27-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B24-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS-DIVIDE-AND-CONQUER"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B25-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Recurrence Analysis","concept":"Divide-and-Conquer Recurrence Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the general recurrence pattern for divide-and-conquer algorithms?","answer":"A common form is T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n), where a represents the number of recursive subproblems, n/b represents their approximate size, and f(n) represents non-recursive work.","explanation":"The exact recurrence depends on the algorithm. Subproblems may have unequal sizes, and the combine or divide work may differ from simple polynomial forms.","principle":"Do not apply a recurrence template unless its assumptions match the actual algorithm.","correct_example":"A balanced algorithm with two half-size subproblems and linear combine work can be represented as T(n)=2T(n/2)+Theta(n).","invalid_example":"Applying a balanced recurrence to an algorithm whose partitions depend strongly on the input and may be highly unequal.","performance_implication":"Small changes to subproblem sizes or the number of recursive calls can materially change asymptotic behavior.","testing_guidance":"Compare theoretical recurrence assumptions against actual partition behavior.","verification_guidance":"Document a mapping from implementation structure to recurrence parameters.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B24-0001"],"prerequisites":["recurrence relation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B25-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B24-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-RECURRENCE-LECTURE"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B25-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Recurrence Analysis","concept":"Recursion Tree","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a recursion tree useful for?","answer":"A recursion tree represents recursive subproblems by levels and can expose how work accumulates across recursion depth.","explanation":"Each node represents a recursive subproblem and associated non-recursive work. Summing work by level can provide an intuitive route to complexity bounds.","principle":"Complexity analysis should reflect the actual recursive computation tree.","correct_example":"For two half-size calls, visualize two children at the next level and continue until base cases.","invalid_example":"Drawing a balanced recursion tree for an algorithm whose recursive branches are not balanced without accounting for that variation.","testing_guidance":"Cross-check recursion-tree conclusions against an algebraic recurrence or another valid analysis method.","verification_guidance":"Verify branching factor, subproblem sizes, depth, and per-node non-recursive cost.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B25-0001"],"prerequisites":["recurrence relation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B26-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B25-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-RECURRENCE-LECTURE"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B26-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Design Patterns","concept":"Easy Split Hard Merge","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the easy-split-hard-merge divide-and-conquer pattern?","answer":"It is a divide-and-conquer structure in which partitioning the problem is relatively simple while combining the recursively solved subproblems is the computationally significant stage.","explanation":"Merge sort is a canonical example: splitting an array is straightforward while merging sorted sequences performs substantial work.","principle":"The location of computational work within divide-and-conquer stages is an important design characteristic.","correct_example":"Merge sort divides ranges directly and performs linear work during merging.","invalid_example":"Assuming every divide-and-conquer algorithm has negligible combine work.","tradeoff":"A more expensive combine phase can still yield efficient overall complexity when recursion depth and subproblem structure are favorable.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B23-0001"],"prerequisites":["divide and conquer"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B26-0002","D11-ALG-B17-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B23-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B26-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS-EASY-SPLIT-HARD-MERGE"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B26-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Design Patterns","concept":"Hard Split Easy Merge","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the hard-split-easy-merge pattern?","answer":"It is a divide-and-conquer structure in which partitioning the input requires significant work while combining the recursively processed regions is simple or nearly implicit.","explanation":"Quicksort is a representative example: partitioning around a pivot performs the principal non-recursive work while the recursively sorted regions can require little explicit merging.","principle":"Different divide-and-conquer algorithms distribute work differently across divide and combine stages.","correct_example":"Partition an array around a pivot, recursively sort the two regions, and obtain the global order through their positional relation to the pivot.","invalid_example":"Assuming quicksort's partition step is free when analyzing its recurrence.","tradeoff":"Expensive partitioning may still be efficient if it produces sufficiently useful subproblems.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B23-0001"],"prerequisites":["divide and conquer"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B26-0001","D11-ALG-B18-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B23-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B26-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS-HARD-SPLIT-EASY-MERGE"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B27-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Partitioning","concept":"Partition Quality","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can poor partition quality degrade a divide-and-conquer algorithm?","answer":"If partitioning repeatedly creates one large subproblem and one very small subproblem, recursion depth and total work can approach the worst-case behavior of the algorithm.","explanation":"The recurrence is determined by actual subproblem sizes. A divide step that appears balanced conceptually but produces skewed partitions in practice can invalidate a balanced complexity assumption.","failure_mode":"Repeatedly unbalanced recursion.","cause":"Input distribution, deterministic pivot choice, or partition policy creates skewed subproblems.","solution":"Change partition strategy, pivot selection, or use a hybrid/guarded algorithm when justified by the problem requirements.","performance_implication":"Worst-case time can increase substantially and recursion depth may approach linear in input size.","testing_guidance":"Include adversarially ordered and duplicate-heavy inputs.","verification_guidance":"Measure or reason about actual partition sizes rather than assuming balance.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B24-0002","D11-ALG-B26-0002"],"prerequisites":["partitioning","recurrence analysis"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B18-0001","D11-ALG-B27-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B24-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS-QUICKSORT"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B27-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Partitioning","concept":"Duplicate-Heavy Inputs","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why do duplicate-heavy inputs require explicit consideration in partition-based algorithms?","answer":"Many equal values can interact with partition rules in ways that produce unnecessary recursion, unbalanced regions, or incorrect boundary handling if equality semantics are not specified.","explanation":"Partition correctness must explicitly define how values equal to the pivot are treated. Three-way or otherwise equality-aware partitioning can be useful when the workload contains many duplicates.","edge_case":"An input containing the same key in nearly every position.","failure_mode":"Degenerate partitioning caused by repeated equal keys.","cause":"Partition policy repeatedly places equal elements on one side or fails to group them efficiently.","solution":"Use a partition specification appropriate to equality-heavy data and verify its invariant.","testing_guidance":"Test all-equal, mostly-equal, and alternating-equal inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify partition postconditions specifically for equality cases.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B27-0001"],"prerequisites":["partition invariant"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B18-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B27-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Algorithm partitioning principles; NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS-QUICKSORT"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B28-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Recursion","subtopic":"Termination","concept":"Well-Founded Recursive Measure","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can termination of recursion be proved?","answer":"Define a measure over valid recursive states that maps to a well-founded domain and prove that every recursive call strictly progresses toward the terminating region.","explanation":"For many finite algorithms, the measure can be input size, interval length, remaining work, or another non-negative quantity that decreases on every recursive transition.","principle":"Termination requires more than the presence of a base case; every valid execution path must reach one.","correct_example":"A recursive interval algorithm decreases the interval length on every recursive call.","invalid_example":"A recursive function with a base case that is never reachable from some valid input.","failure_mode":"Infinite recursion despite an existing base case.","cause":"The recursive transition does not guarantee progress toward the base case.","solution":"Redesign the recursive state transition and prove strict progress.","testing_guidance":"Test minimal inputs, boundary transitions, and inputs that previously produced repeated states.","verification_guidance":"Record the termination measure and prove strict progress for every recursive branch.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B21-0001"],"prerequisites":["well-founded ordering","recursive state"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B28-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B21-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"NIST-DADS-RECURSION"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B28-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Recursion","subtopic":"Termination","concept":"Mutual Recursion","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is mutual recursion?","answer":"Mutual recursion occurs when two or more functions or procedures recursively invoke one another as part of the computation.","explanation":"Termination and correctness must be established across the combined call cycle rather than considering each function independently.","failure_mode":"Mutual recursive cycle with no decreasing global measure.","cause":"Each function transfers control to another function without guaranteed progress toward a base case.","solution":"Define a shared well-founded measure and prove that every transition in the call graph decreases it or reaches a base case.","testing_guidance":"Test every transition path in the mutually recursive call graph.","verification_guidance":"Analyze the combined call graph and prove termination across all recursive transitions.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B28-0001"],"prerequisites":["recursive call graph"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B21-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B28-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Recursive algorithm theory derived from recursive call-graph reasoning","source_ref":"RECURSION-TERMINATION-CALLGRAPH"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B29-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Implementation","concept":"Interval Convention","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is mixing inclusive and half-open intervals dangerous in recursive algorithms?","answer":"Mixing interval conventions can cause skipped elements, duplicated processing, invalid bounds, infinite recursion, or incorrect base-case detection.","explanation":"Recursive algorithms frequently represent subproblems using index ranges. The convention must remain consistent across division, recursion, and combination.","correct_example":"Use [lo,hi) consistently so an empty range has lo=hi and a one-element range has hi=lo+1.","invalid_example":"Dividing an inclusive [lo,hi] interval using logic designed for a half-open [lo,hi) representation.","failure_mode":"Off-by-one recursion defect.","cause":"Inconsistent interpretation of boundaries.","solution":"Select one interval convention, encode it in the subproblem contract, and test empty and singleton ranges explicitly.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, singleton, two-element, full-range, and adjacent recursive ranges.","verification_guidance":"Verify that recursive subranges are disjoint or intentionally overlapping according to the specification and collectively cover the intended domain.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B23-0002"],"prerequisites":["index-range specification"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B29-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B23-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Derived implementation correctness pattern for recursive interval algorithms","source_ref":"RECURSIVE-INTERVAL-CORRECTNESS"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B29-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Implementation","concept":"Overlapping Recursive Work","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happens when recursive subproblems repeatedly recompute identical work?","answer":"The algorithm may perform the same subproblem computation many times, causing exponential or otherwise unnecessary growth in runtime.","explanation":"Divide-and-conquer is not automatically efficient merely because it is recursive. The subproblem structure must be analyzed for overlap and repeated work.","failure_mode":"Repeated recursive computation.","cause":"Multiple recursive branches independently solve the same subproblem.","solution":"If the problem has overlapping subproblems, consider memoization, dynamic programming, caching, or a different decomposition when appropriate.","performance_implication":"Repeated work can dominate runtime even when individual recursive calls are inexpensive.","testing_guidance":"Instrument repeated subproblem states on representative small inputs to detect duplication.","verification_guidance":"Identify whether identical logical states can arise through multiple recursive paths before selecting a divide-and-conquer formulation.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B25-0001"],"prerequisites":["subproblem identity","recurrence analysis"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B30-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B25-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms, Recursive Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-RECURRENCE-LECTURE"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B30-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Recursion and Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Algorithm Design Workflow","concept":"Recursive Algorithm Design Workflow","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What systematic workflow should an engineering system use when designing a recursive or divide-and-conquer algorithm?","answer":"Define the problem contract; define the recursive subproblem; identify base cases; define the recursive relation; define divide and combine operations; prove termination; establish correctness; derive the recurrence; analyze time and space; identify implementation risks; then test boundaries and representative workloads.","explanation":"A structured workflow converts recursive design from trial-and-error into a specification-driven process. The subproblem definition and recurrence must correspond to the actual implementation.","principle":"Algorithm design, correctness, complexity, and testing should be connected through the same explicit subproblem model.","correct_example":"For a divide-and-conquer sorter, define interval semantics, split rule, recursive guarantees, combine/partition invariant, termination measure, recurrence, and tests before implementation is considered verified.","invalid_example":"Writing recursive code first and inferring its correctness and complexity only from a few successful examples.","testing_guidance":"Use base-case, boundary, adversarial, invariant, regression, and property-based tests where applicable.","verification_guidance":"Maintain separate evidence for GENERATED, EXECUTED, TESTED, PASSED, and VERIFIED states; successful code generation alone does not establish correctness.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"algorithmic-general","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B21-0001","D11-ALG-B22-0001","D11-ALG-B23-0001","D11-ALG-B24-0001","D11-ALG-B28-0001"],"prerequisites":["algorithm specification","complexity analysis","correctness reasoning"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B30-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B21-0001","D11-ALG-B23-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms, Recursive Algorithms; NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-RECURRENCE-LECTURE;NIST-DADS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B30-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Recursion and Divide and Conquer","subtopic":"Brain Integration","concept":"Algorithm Reasoning Representation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What representation should an autonomous software engineering system retain for a recursive algorithm?","answer":"It should retain the problem contract, subproblem identity, state transition, base cases, recursive edges, combine rule, termination measure, correctness invariant, recurrence, complexity bounds, implementation assumptions, failure modes, and verification evidence as linked knowledge.","explanation":"A code snippet alone is insufficient for reliable reasoning. The system needs explicit relationships between specification, algorithm structure, correctness claims, complexity claims, tests, and observed verification evidence.","principle":"Algorithm knowledge should form a reasoning graph rather than an isolated collection of descriptions.","correct_example":"A recursive algorithm record links its subproblem definition to its recurrence, termination proof, correctness invariant, implementation constraints, tests, and related alternatives.","invalid_example":"Storing only the algorithm name and source code without recording assumptions, correctness conditions, or verification state.","security_implication":"Incorrect algorithm assumptions can propagate into security-sensitive code generation if preconditions and failure behavior are omitted.","testing_guidance":"Retrieval tests should query by concept, failure mode, complexity, correctness property, and implementation constraint rather than only by algorithm name.","verification_guidance":"Every strong correctness or performance claim should point to evidence appropriate to its claim type.","language":"language-agnostic","framework":"AI Software Engineering Knowledge Fabric","version":"1.0","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B30-0001"],"prerequisites":["structured algorithm ontology"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B21-0001","D11-ALG-B23-0001","D11-ALG-B24-0001","D11-ALG-B28-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B30-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Dataset architecture principle derived from the D11 algorithm knowledge model and authoritative recursive-algorithm references","source_ref":"D11-KNOWLEDGE-FABRIC-DESIGN;MIT-OCW-6.006-RECURRENCE-LECTURE;NIST-DADS"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B31-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Greedy Algorithms","subtopic":"Foundations","concept":"Greedy Algorithm","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a greedy algorithm?","answer":"A greedy algorithm constructs a solution incrementally by repeatedly making a locally selected choice according to a specified rule, without generally revisiting earlier choices.","explanation":"Greedy algorithms can be highly efficient when the problem has structural properties that make the local choice safe for an optimal global solution. A locally attractive choice alone does not establish correctness.","principle":"A greedy choice must be justified by a problem-specific correctness argument; local optimality is not sufficient by itself.","correct_example":"Selecting activities by earliest finishing time for the activity-selection problem.","invalid_example":"Choosing the largest immediately available item for an arbitrary optimization problem and assuming the result is globally optimal.","failure_mode":"Applying a greedy rule without proving that the selected choice is safe.","cause":"Confusing local improvement with globally optimal structure.","solution":"Establish a greedy-choice property or use another algorithmic paradigm when such a proof is unavailable.","testing_guidance":"Test against exhaustive or independently verified solutions for small instances to detect counterexamples.","verification_guidance":"Require an explicit greedy-choice correctness argument before classifying a greedy algorithm as optimal.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["optimization problem specification"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B31-0002","D11-ALG-B32-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B35-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B31-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Greedy Algorithms","subtopic":"Correctness","concept":"Greedy Choice Property","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the greedy-choice property?","answer":"The greedy-choice property means that there exists an optimal solution containing the locally selected greedy choice, allowing the algorithm to commit to that choice without sacrificing optimality.","explanation":"This property is problem-specific. It must be established by a proof rather than inferred from the apparent usefulness of a local choice.","principle":"A greedy algorithm is justified only when its choice can be shown to be safe within an optimal solution.","correct_example":"For interval scheduling, selecting an activity with the earliest finish time can be proven safe through an exchange argument.","invalid_example":"Assuming earliest start time is always a safe choice for interval scheduling.","testing_guidance":"Use counterexample search to challenge proposed greedy rules before formalizing the algorithm.","verification_guidance":"Store the greedy-choice proof or authoritative correctness argument as evidence.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":["optimization","proof reasoning"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B32-0001","D11-ALG-B33-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B31-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B32-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Greedy Algorithms","subtopic":"Activity Selection","concept":"Activity Selection","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"How does the standard greedy activity-selection algorithm work?","answer":"It repeatedly selects the compatible activity with the earliest finishing time and continues with activities that start no earlier than the selected activity's finish time.","explanation":"The earliest-finish choice leaves the largest possible remaining time for future compatible activities. Its correctness can be established using an exchange argument.","principle":"The selected activity must be compatible with the already selected activities and must satisfy the proven greedy-choice rule.","correct_example":"Given activities with start and finish times, repeatedly select the compatible activity with the smallest finish time.","invalid_example":"Selecting the shortest-duration activity without proving that duration is a safe criterion.","complexity":"With activities sorted by finish time, the selection scan is O(n); sorting the activities first is typically O(n log n).","testing_guidance":"Test overlapping activities, nested intervals, identical finish times, empty input, and single-activity input.","verification_guidance":"Verify compatibility and the earliest-finish invariant at every selection.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B31-0002"],"prerequisites":["greedy-choice property","interval representation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B32-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B31-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B35-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B32-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Greedy Algorithms","subtopic":"Activity Selection","concept":"Exchange Argument","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can an exchange argument establish a greedy choice?","answer":"Show that an arbitrary optimal solution can be transformed into another optimal solution containing the greedy choice without reducing solution quality.","explanation":"The argument demonstrates that committing to the greedy choice does not exclude all optimal solutions. The remaining problem must then retain the required structure for recursion or iteration.","principle":"A safe exchange must preserve feasibility and objective value.","correct_example":"Replace the first activity in an optimal compatible schedule with the earliest-finishing compatible activity when the replacement preserves compatibility and does not reduce the number of selected activities.","invalid_example":"Replacing an optimal choice without proving that feasibility and objective value are preserved.","testing_guidance":"Construct small instances and verify the exchange transformation mechanically where possible.","verification_guidance":"Record the original optimal structure, replacement, feasibility proof, and objective-preservation argument.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B32-0001"],"prerequisites":["greedy-choice property"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B33-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B32-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B33-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Greedy Algorithms","subtopic":"Optimal Structure","concept":"Optimal Substructure","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is optimal substructure?","answer":"A problem has optimal substructure when an optimal solution can be constructed from optimal solutions to appropriate subproblems.","explanation":"Optimal substructure is useful in both greedy and dynamic-programming algorithms, but it does not by itself prove that a greedy local choice is safe.","principle":"Optimal substructure and greedy-choice property are distinct requirements.","correct_example":"After fixing a safe activity-selection choice, the remaining compatible activities form a smaller optimization problem with the same structural objective.","invalid_example":"Concluding that a greedy algorithm is correct solely because the problem has optimal substructure.","testing_guidance":"Identify the exact subproblem produced after each committed decision and verify that its objective matches the original optimization structure.","verification_guidance":"Separate evidence for optimal substructure from evidence for the greedy-choice property.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":["optimization problem"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B31-0002","D11-ALG-B35-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B31-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B33-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Greedy Algorithms","subtopic":"Failure Analysis","concept":"False Greedy Heuristic","knowledge_type":"COUNTEREXAMPLE","question":"How can a plausible greedy rule be shown incorrect?","answer":"Construct an input where the locally preferred choice forces a feasible solution with a worse objective value than another valid choice sequence.","explanation":"Counterexamples are useful for falsifying greedy rules before investing in implementation or proof development.","principle":"A greedy heuristic should be treated as unverified until its optimality is proven or its status is explicitly limited to heuristic use.","correct_example":"For coin systems that are not canonical, repeatedly selecting the largest denomination can require more coins than another combination.","invalid_example":"Calling a greedy rule optimal merely because it succeeds on several ordinary examples.","failure_mode":"False optimality claim.","cause":"Empirical success was mistaken for a general proof.","solution":"Search for counterexamples and establish the required structural property.","testing_guidance":"Use exhaustive enumeration over small input domains when the problem space permits it.","verification_guidance":"Do not promote a heuristic to VERIFIED algorithmic fact without a proof or authoritative evidence.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":["counterexample construction"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B34-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B31-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Algorithmic correctness and counterexample methodology","source_ref":"ALGORITHM-COUNTEREXAMPLE-METHOD"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B34-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Greedy Algorithms","subtopic":"Canonical Problems","concept":"Fractional Knapsack","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"Why does value-density ordering work for fractional knapsack?","answer":"When items may be divided fractionally, selecting available items in descending value-to-weight ratio produces an optimal solution.","explanation":"Fractional divisibility makes the marginal value of each unit of capacity directly comparable across items. Taking the highest value density first can be justified by an exchange argument.","principle":"The greedy rule relies critically on fractional divisibility.","correct_example":"For each item, compute value/weight and consume available capacity from highest density to lowest density.","invalid_example":"Applying the same density rule to 0/1 knapsack where items cannot be divided.","complexity":"After sorting by density, the greedy selection scan is O(n), with total O(n log n) dominated by sorting.","failure_mode":"Applying fractional-knapsack reasoning to indivisible items.","cause":"The problem's divisibility assumption has changed.","solution":"Use an algorithm appropriate to 0/1 constraints, commonly dynamic programming for bounded instances.","testing_guidance":"Compare fractional and indivisible versions on identical item sets to detect incorrect transfer of assumptions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that fractional allocation is explicitly part of the problem contract.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B31-0002","D11-ALG-B33-0001"],"prerequisites":["fractional allocation","optimization"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B34-0002","D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B31-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B34-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Greedy Algorithms","subtopic":"Canonical Problems","concept":"Minimum Spanning Tree Greedy Structure","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can greedy edge selection be used in minimum spanning tree algorithms?","answer":"Minimum spanning tree algorithms exploit cut and cycle properties that identify safe edges for extending a minimum spanning forest.","explanation":"Algorithms such as Kruskal's and Prim's are not correct merely because they choose small edges; their correctness follows from graph structural properties.","principle":"A greedy graph choice must be justified against the relevant cut or cycle property.","correct_example":"Kruskal's algorithm considers edges in nondecreasing weight order and accepts an edge when it does not create a cycle in the current forest.","invalid_example":"Adding every smallest edge without checking whether it creates a cycle.","failure_mode":"Greedy edge selection violates the spanning-tree feasibility constraint.","cause":"The implementation ignores cycle prevention or graph connectivity requirements.","solution":"Maintain the required forest invariant and use an appropriate cycle-detection mechanism.","testing_guidance":"Test disconnected graphs, equal weights, cycles, multiple valid MSTs, and single-vertex graphs.","verification_guidance":"Verify acyclicity, edge count, connectivity for connected input, and total weight against an independent reference for small graphs.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":["graphs","cut property","cycle property"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B34-0003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B31-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B35-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Foundations","concept":"Dynamic Programming","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is dynamic programming?","answer":"Dynamic programming solves problems by defining states representing subproblems, computing solutions to those states, and reusing results when the same subproblems recur.","explanation":"Dynamic programming is particularly useful when a problem has optimal substructure and overlapping subproblems. Solutions may be computed top-down with memoization or bottom-up with tabulation.","principle":"The state definition must preserve exactly the information required to solve and combine subproblems.","correct_example":"Computing Fibonacci values with memoization so each input state is evaluated once.","invalid_example":"Caching arbitrary intermediate values without defining what state they represent or why they are sufficient.","tradeoff":"Dynamic programming can reduce repeated computation at the cost of additional state storage and state-management complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test base states, transition boundaries, repeated states, and reconstruction where applicable.","verification_guidance":"Verify state semantics, transition correctness, base cases, and that every required predecessor state is available before use.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B33-0001"],"prerequisites":["optimal substructure","overlapping subproblems"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B35-0002","D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B33-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B31-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B35-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Foundations","concept":"Overlapping Subproblems","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What are overlapping subproblems in dynamic programming?","answer":"Overlapping subproblems occur when a recursive or decomposed computation repeatedly needs solutions to the same logical subproblem states.","explanation":"Repeated subproblem computation creates an opportunity for memoization or tabulation. The state identity must be defined so equivalent subproblems map to the same stored result.","principle":"Caching is useful only when repeated computations correspond to identical problem states with reusable results.","correct_example":"Naive recursive Fibonacci repeatedly computes the same Fibonacci indices; memoization stores each index once.","invalid_example":"Caching results keyed only by an incomplete state representation that can correspond to different logical subproblems.","performance_implication":"Eliminating repeated subproblems can transform exponential recursive computation into polynomial or linear-state computation depending on the transition structure.","testing_guidance":"Instrument state evaluations and verify that equivalent states are reused.","verification_guidance":"Prove that the cache key uniquely represents the subproblem's required state.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B35-0001"],"prerequisites":["state representation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B35-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B36-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"State Design","concept":"DP State Definition","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What makes a dynamic-programming state well designed?","answer":"A state is well designed when it contains sufficient information to determine the required optimal result for the subproblem while avoiding unnecessary dimensions that do not affect future decisions.","explanation":"State design is one of the central reasoning tasks in dynamic programming. An incomplete state can produce incorrect reuse, while an unnecessarily large state can increase time and memory complexity.","principle":"The state must be sufficient for optimal continuation and precise enough to distinguish materially different subproblems.","correct_example":"For 0/1 knapsack, a state can represent the best achievable value using a prefix of items and a specified capacity.","invalid_example":"Using only remaining capacity while ignoring which items remain available when item identity affects future choices.","failure_mode":"Insufficient state representation.","cause":"Relevant historical information was omitted from the state.","solution":"Identify the minimum information required to determine all future feasible transitions.","performance_implication":"Additional state dimensions can multiply memory and computation requirements.","testing_guidance":"Construct pairs of histories with the same proposed state but different optimal continuations; if outputs differ, the state is insufficient.","verification_guidance":"Prove that two histories mapping to the same state have equivalent future optimization behavior.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B35-0001"],"prerequisites":["optimal substructure","state modeling"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B36-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B35-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B36-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"State Design","concept":"State Compression","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is DP state compression?","answer":"State compression reduces the amount of stored dynamic-programming state by retaining only information that future transitions still require.","explanation":"For some recurrences, the current result depends only on a small subset of previous states. Storage can then be reduced without changing correctness.","principle":"State compression is valid only when discarded states cannot be needed by any future transition or reconstruction requirement.","correct_example":"A one-dimensional DP array can replace a full two-dimensional table when each state depends only on a previously processed row and update order preserves dependencies.","invalid_example":"Overwriting a DP value before all transitions that depend on its previous value have consumed it.","failure_mode":"Incorrect in-place state update.","cause":"Update ordering destroys required predecessor information.","solution":"Determine dependency direction and select an update order that preserves all required predecessor states.","testing_guidance":"Compare compressed and uncompressed implementations on exhaustive small instances.","verification_guidance":"Document the dependency graph and prove that each overwritten value is no longer required.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"prerequisites":["state transition graph"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B37-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Dynamic programming state-compression principle","source_ref":"DP-STATE-COMPRESSION"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B37-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Memoization","concept":"Top-Down Memoization","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How does top-down dynamic programming with memoization work?","answer":"A recursive solution computes a subproblem when first requested and stores its result so subsequent requests for the same state can reuse it.","explanation":"Memoization preserves the recursive structure while preventing repeated evaluation of identical states.","principle":"Every cache entry must correspond to a well-defined complete subproblem state.","complexity":"Complexity depends on the number of distinct states and transition cost rather than the number of recursive call paths.","correct_example":"Memoized Fibonacci computes each index once and reuses the stored result.","invalid_example":"Using a cache key that omits a parameter that changes the subproblem result.","tradeoff":"Memoization is often easier to derive from a recursive specification but can retain recursion-stack overhead and may not evaluate every state required by a full bottom-up computation.","testing_guidance":"Compare cache-enabled and reference implementations on small exhaustive inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify cache-key completeness and that stored values correspond exactly to their state semantics.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B35-0002","D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"prerequisites":["recursive formulation","state identity"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B37-0002","D11-ALG-B38-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B35-0002","D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B37-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B37-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Tabulation","concept":"Bottom-Up Tabulation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How does bottom-up dynamic programming work?","answer":"Bottom-up dynamic programming computes states in an order such that every predecessor state required by a transition has already been computed.","explanation":"Tabulation removes recursive call overhead and makes evaluation order explicit. It often enables direct control over memory layout and state compression.","principle":"The evaluation order must respect the dependency direction among states.","correct_example":"Compute Fibonacci values from the smallest indices upward until the requested index is reached.","invalid_example":"Computing a state before one of its required predecessor states has been established.","tradeoff":"Bottom-up computation can avoid recursion overhead but may compute states that a top-down approach would never request.","testing_guidance":"Verify evaluation order and compare results with an independent recursive or brute-force implementation for small inputs.","verification_guidance":"Represent DP dependencies explicitly and verify that every predecessor is available before use.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"prerequisites":["state dependency graph"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B37-0001","D11-ALG-B37-0003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B37-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B37-0003","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Implementation","concept":"DP Update Order","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can update order change the meaning of a compressed dynamic-programming algorithm?","answer":"An incorrect update order can cause a transition to read a value that has already been updated for the current iteration, effectively allowing a state to use itself or reuse an item more than permitted by the original recurrence.","explanation":"Compressed DP removes explicit historical layers, so the update order becomes part of the correctness specification.","failure_mode":"Illegal state reuse.","cause":"In-place updates violate the dependency direction of the original recurrence.","solution":"Choose forward or reverse iteration according to whether current-iteration or previous-iteration values are required.","testing_guidance":"Compare compressed implementation with a full-table reference on small exhaustive instances.","verification_guidance":"Map every read to the logical DP layer it represents and prove that the physical update order preserves that mapping.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B36-0002"],"prerequisites":["DP dependency analysis"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B36-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B36-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Dynamic programming state-compression correctness principle","source_ref":"DP-STATE-COMPRESSION"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B38-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Canonical Problems","concept":"0/1 Knapsack Dynamic Programming","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"How can 0/1 knapsack be formulated using dynamic programming?","answer":"Define a state representing the best achievable objective using a specified prefix or subset of items and a bounded capacity, then transition by excluding or including the current item when feasible.","explanation":"Each item can be selected at most once, which distinguishes the problem from fractional knapsack. The state must capture enough information about processed items and remaining capacity.","principle":"The transition must respect the indivisibility and single-use constraints of the 0/1 problem.","complexity":"The standard capacity-indexed formulation has pseudo-polynomial time and space complexity dependent on the numeric capacity and item count.","correct_example":"For each item and capacity, take the maximum of excluding the item and including it when its weight fits.","invalid_example":"Using an item repeatedly within the same 0/1 state transition.","tradeoff":"The capacity-indexed DP can be practical for moderate numeric capacities but is not polynomial in the bit-length of the capacity.","edge_case":"Zero capacity, zero-weight items, empty item set, duplicate items, and capacity smaller than every item.","testing_guidance":"Compare against exhaustive subset enumeration for small item sets.","verification_guidance":"Verify include/exclude transitions and ensure each item is considered exactly once.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B36-0001","D11-ALG-B37-0002"],"prerequisites":["optimal substructure","bounded capacity"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B34-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D11-ALG-B34-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B38-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Canonical Problems","concept":"Longest Common Subsequence","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"What is the dynamic-programming structure of longest common subsequence?","answer":"Define a state for prefixes of the two sequences; when their final elements match, extend the corresponding smaller-prefix solution, otherwise choose the better result obtained by excluding one final element or the other.","explanation":"The state captures the two prefix lengths. The recurrence follows from whether the current terminal elements can participate together in an optimal subsequence.","principle":"The recurrence must preserve subsequence order while allowing unmatched elements to be skipped.","complexity":"The standard two-dimensional formulation uses O(mn) time and O(mn) space, with possible space reductions when only the length is required.","correct_example":"For two sequences, compare their final elements and apply the matching or exclusion recurrence.","invalid_example":"Allowing reordered elements and thereby solving a common-subset rather than common-subsequence problem.","testing_guidance":"Test empty sequences, identical sequences, disjoint sequences, repeated symbols, and multiple equally optimal subsequences.","verification_guidance":"Verify subsequence ordering and recurrence behavior against exhaustive enumeration for small sequences.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B35-0001","D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"prerequisites":["sequence prefixes","optimal substructure"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B38-0003"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B35-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B38-0003","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Canonical Problems","concept":"Edit Distance","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can edit distance be represented as dynamic programming?","answer":"Use a state representing prefixes of two strings and define transitions for insertion, deletion, and substitution according to the selected edit-cost model.","explanation":"The exact recurrence depends on the permitted operations and their costs. The DP state must preserve enough prefix information to determine future edit choices.","principle":"The algorithm must encode the exact edit model rather than assuming a universal distance definition.","constraint":"Operation costs and allowed operations are part of the problem specification.","testing_guidance":"Test empty strings, identical strings, one-character changes, repeated characters, and asymmetric operation costs when supported.","verification_guidance":"Check that every reported distance corresponds to a valid edit sequence under the declared cost model.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"prerequisites":["state modeling","sequence transformation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B38-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Dynamic programming sequence-alignment principle","source_ref":"DP-SEQUENCE-ALIGNMENT"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B39-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Solution Reconstruction","concept":"DP Reconstruction","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a dynamic-programming algorithm recover an actual solution rather than only an optimal value?","answer":"Store sufficient predecessor or decision information during DP computation, or recompute decisions during a backward traversal from the final state, then reconstruct the selected choices in reverse dependency order.","explanation":"Computing an optimal objective value and recovering the corresponding choices are separate requirements. Memory-optimized DP may need additional reconstruction logic.","principle":"The DP representation must preserve enough information for the requested output, not merely its objective value.","correct_example":"Store whether each knapsack state used the current item, then backtrack from the final state.","invalid_example":"Claiming to return the chosen item set when the implementation stores only optimal numeric values and provides no reconstruction method.","tradeoff":"Storing predecessor information increases memory but simplifies reconstruction.","testing_guidance":"Verify both optimal objective value and validity of the reconstructed solution.","verification_guidance":"Re-evaluate the reconstructed solution against the original constraints and objective.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"prerequisites":["DP state","predecessor relation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B39-0002"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Dynamic programming reconstruction principle","source_ref":"DP-RECONSTRUCTION"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B39-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Correctness","concept":"DP Transition Verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a dynamic-programming transition be verified?","answer":"Verify that every transition considers all and only valid predecessor decisions, preserves constraints, and combines predecessor values according to the optimization or counting objective.","explanation":"A correct table structure does not guarantee correct transitions. Each transition represents a mathematical claim about how optimal subproblems compose.","correct_example":"For a max-value recurrence, explicitly prove why the candidate set contains every feasible final decision and why taking its maximum yields the optimal state value.","invalid_example":"Copying a recurrence from a related problem without checking whether its constraints and objective match the current specification.","testing_guidance":"Compare each state against exhaustive enumeration on small domains.","verification_guidance":"Document the recurrence derivation and map each branch to a valid decision in the problem model.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B35-0001","D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"prerequisites":["recurrence derivation"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B39-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B36-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"Dynamic programming correctness methodology","source_ref":"DP-CORRECTNESS-TRANSITIONS"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B40-0001","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Greedy Algorithms and Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Paradigm Selection","concept":"Greedy Versus Dynamic Programming","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should an algorithm system distinguish greedy algorithms from dynamic programming?","answer":"Greedy algorithms commit to locally selected choices when a problem-specific proof shows those choices are safe; dynamic programming evaluates and reuses subproblem states when optimal substructure and overlapping subproblems make state reuse beneficial.","explanation":"Both paradigms can exploit optimal substructure, but greedy algorithms depend on a safe-choice property whereas dynamic programming generally retains alternatives through state transitions before selecting an optimal result.","principle":"Do not select the paradigm from superficial problem similarity; derive it from structural properties and correctness requirements.","correct_example":"Use greedy activity selection when the earliest-finish choice is provably safe; use DP when alternative decisions must be compared across overlapping states.","invalid_example":"Replacing a DP recurrence with a greedy rule solely because both seek an optimum.","tradeoff":"Greedy solutions can use less memory and simpler state, while DP can handle broader classes of optimization structures at the cost of state computation and storage.","testing_guidance":"Search for small counterexamples to proposed greedy choices and compare against exhaustive/DP references.","verification_guidance":"Require a greedy-choice proof before treating a greedy solution as exact; otherwise classify it as heuristic or unverified.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"stable","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B31-0002","D11-ALG-B33-0001","D11-ALG-B35-0001"],"prerequisites":["greedy-choice property","optimal substructure","overlapping subproblems"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B31-0001","D11-ALG-B35-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B31-0002","D11-ALG-B35-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare, Introduction to Algorithms","source_ref":"MIT-OCW-6.006-ALGORITHMS"},"evidence_level":"ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B40-0002","dataset_id":"D11","topic":"Greedy Algorithms and Dynamic Programming","subtopic":"Brain Reasoning","concept":"Optimization Paradigm Decision Procedure","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What reasoning procedure should an autonomous software engineering system use before selecting greedy or dynamic programming?","answer":"Define the optimization objective and constraints; identify the state or residual problem after a decision; test for optimal substructure; identify repeated subproblems; propose candidate greedy choices; search for counterexamples; attempt a greedy-choice proof; if no safe-choice proof exists, model the relevant states and transitions for dynamic programming or another suitable paradigm; then verify complexity and output requirements.","explanation":"This procedure prevents premature commitment to a greedy heuristic and makes the structural basis of the algorithm explicit.","principle":"Algorithm paradigm selection should be evidence-driven and proof-aware rather than pattern-matched from superficial examples.","correct_example":"Reject a proposed greedy rule after finding a valid counterexample, then formulate a DP state that preserves the alternatives required for optimality.","invalid_example":"Selecting greedy because it uses less memory without establishing that the result remains optimal.","testing_guidance":"Include adversarial counterexample generation and small-instance exhaustive comparison before accepting an exact greedy algorithm.","verification_guidance":"Record whether the final method is PROVEN_EXACT, EMPIRICALLY_SUPPORTED_HEURISTIC, or UNKNOWN rather than collapsing these states.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"1.0","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B31-0002","D11-ALG-B33-0002","D11-ALG-B35-0001","D11-ALG-B39-0002"],"prerequisites":["optimization reasoning","counterexample generation","correctness verification"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B40-0001"],"depends_on":["D11-ALG-B40-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source":"D11 knowledge-fabric reasoning methodology grounded in standard greedy and dynamic-programming principles","source_ref":"D11-ALGORITHM-PARADIGM-REASONING"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16","language":"English"}

