{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B41-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Graph Model","concept":"Graph","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a graph in algorithmic problem solving?","answer":"A graph is a mathematical structure consisting of vertices and edges that represent entities and relationships between them. Edges may be directed or undirected and may optionally carry weights or other attributes.","explanation":"Graph algorithms operate on relationships rather than only linear sequences. The representation and edge semantics determine which algorithms are valid.","principle":"Model entities as vertices and relevant relationships as edges before selecting an algorithm.","correct_example":"A dependency system can represent software packages as vertices and dependency relations as directed edges.","invalid_example":"Treating an inherently directed dependency relation as undirected can introduce relationships that do not exist.","failure_mode":"Incorrect graph semantics","cause":"The model ignores direction, multiplicity, weights, or domain constraints.","solution":"Explicitly define vertex semantics, edge semantics, directionality, weighting, and allowed relationships before implementation.","edge_case":"Empty graph, isolated vertices, self-loops, parallel edges, disconnected components.","testing_guidance":"Test graphs containing isolated vertices, self-loops, parallel edges, and disconnected components where the representation permits them.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every modeled edge corresponds to an intended domain relationship and that edge direction/weight semantics are preserved.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B45-0001","D11-ALG-B47-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"Graph representations and applications; BFS; DFS","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B41-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Graph Representation","concept":"Adjacency List","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is an adjacency-list representation useful?","answer":"An adjacency list stores the neighbors of each vertex and is generally well suited to sparse graphs because storage is proportional to the vertices and represented edges.","explanation":"Traversal algorithms such as BFS and DFS naturally iterate through adjacency lists. The representation avoids storing absent edges explicitly.","principle":"Choose representation according to graph density and required operations.","correct_example":"A sparse directed graph with millions of vertices and relatively few edges can use one neighbor collection per vertex.","invalid_example":"Using a dense matrix solely because the graph has many vertices but very few edges wastes space.","tradeoff":"Adjacency lists support efficient neighbor iteration but do not provide constant-time edge-existence lookup in the same general way as a matrix.","edge_case":"Vertices with zero outgoing edges must still be representable.","testing_guidance":"Verify that every input edge appears in the correct adjacency collection and that isolated vertices remain present.","verification_guidance":"Check representation invariants: every represented edge has valid endpoints and directionality is preserved.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B41-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B42-0001","D11-ALG-B43-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"Graph search, representations, and applications","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B41-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Graph Representation","concept":"Adjacency Matrix","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is an adjacency matrix?","answer":"An adjacency matrix represents a graph using a matrix whose entries encode whether an edge exists between a pair of vertices and, in weighted variants, may encode the edge weight.","explanation":"It provides direct indexed access to a vertex pair but requires storage proportional to the square of the number of vertices.","tradeoff":"The matrix can make pairwise edge queries straightforward, while adjacency-list representations are often more space-efficient for sparse graphs.","edge_case":"The meaning of diagonal entries must be explicitly defined when self-loops are possible.","testing_guidance":"Test present edges, absent edges, directed asymmetry, and weighted entries.","verification_guidance":"Verify that matrix semantics match the graph's direction and weight model.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B41-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B41-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"Graph representations and applications","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B41-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Graph Representation","concept":"Directed versus Undirected Graph","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the algorithmic difference between directed and undirected graphs?","answer":"In a directed graph an edge has an ordered source and destination, while in an undirected graph an edge represents a symmetric relationship between its endpoints.","explanation":"Traversal, reachability, degree, cycle detection, and shortest-path behavior depend on edge direction.","counterexample":"Treating a directed dependency edge A→B as if B→A were also valid changes reachability and dependency semantics.","verification_guidance":"For directed graphs, verify that traversal follows only permitted edge directions.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B41-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B43-0001","D11-ALG-B45-0001","D11-ALG-B46-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"Graph search, representations, and applications","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B42-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Breadth-First Search","concept":"BFS","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"What is breadth-first search?","answer":"Breadth-first search explores a graph in layers of increasing number of edges from a chosen source, typically using a queue and a visited/discovery state.","explanation":"BFS discovers all vertices reachable by a path of k edges before vertices first reached at greater distance, assuming ordinary unweighted traversal semantics.","principle":"Use BFS when layer-by-layer reachability or shortest paths measured by number of edges are required.","correct_example":"Starting from source s, enqueue s, repeatedly remove the front vertex, and enqueue each undiscovered neighbor.","invalid_example":"Using a stack while calling the resulting traversal BFS changes the traversal discipline and can invalidate BFS distance guarantees.","failure_mode":"Incorrect frontier structure","cause":"A stack or unordered processing replaces the FIFO queue discipline.","solution":"Maintain FIFO processing of the discovered frontier.","testing_guidance":"Test line graphs, branching graphs, cycles, disconnected graphs, and graphs with multiple paths to the same vertex.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each discovered vertex receives a distance no greater than the distance of any later-discovered vertex and that parent edges decrease distance by one.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B41-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B42-0002","D11-ALG-B44-0001","D11-ALG-B45-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"Lecture 13: Breadth-first search","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B42-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Breadth-First Search","concept":"BFS Shortest Path in Unweighted Graph","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can BFS compute shortest path distance in an unweighted graph?","answer":"BFS processes vertices in nondecreasing number of edges from the source, so the first discovery of a vertex establishes a shortest path measured in edges.","explanation":"Every edge contributes equal unit cost. BFS's layer structure therefore corresponds to shortest-path distance in the unweighted graph.","constraint":"The guarantee is for shortest paths measured by equal edge count; arbitrary weighted edges require an appropriate weighted shortest-path algorithm.","counterexample":"Using BFS on a graph where one edge costs 100 and another costs 1 can return a path with fewer edges but greater total weight.","testing_guidance":"Construct graphs where a direct high-cost edge competes with a multi-edge low-cost path to ensure the implementation does not claim weighted optimality.","verification_guidance":"Compare returned edge-count distances against an independently computed shortest-path baseline on finite test graphs.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B42-0001","D11-ALG-B47-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B50-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"BFS and single-source shortest paths","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B42-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Breadth-First Search","concept":"BFS Complexity","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What is the standard asymptotic complexity of BFS with adjacency lists?","answer":"With adjacency-list representation, BFS runs in O(|V| + |E|) time when each vertex and edge is processed a constant number of times.","explanation":"The traversal examines reachable vertices and their represented incident edges, with additional initialization depending on the implementation.","constraint":"The bound assumes an appropriate adjacency-list representation and standard constant-time queue operations.","performance_implication":"For sparse graphs this is linear in the represented graph size.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B41-0002","D11-ALG-B42-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B43-0002","D11-ALG-B45-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"Graph reachability by BFS or DFS","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B43-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Depth-First Search","concept":"DFS","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"What is depth-first search?","answer":"Depth-first search explores a graph by following a path as far as possible before backtracking, using recursion or an explicit stack.","explanation":"DFS provides a systematic graph exploration order and supports applications such as cycle detection, topological sorting, and component analysis.","principle":"DFS prioritizes depth of exploration rather than shortest-distance layers.","correct_example":"Mark a vertex discovered, recursively or iteratively visit each undiscovered neighbor, and return after all reachable descendants have been explored.","invalid_example":"Using a FIFO queue and treating the result as DFS removes the characteristic depth-first processing discipline.","failure_mode":"Traversal-state error","cause":"A vertex is marked too late or multiple active paths are allowed to process it inconsistently.","solution":"Define and enforce explicit discovery/visited state semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test trees, cycles, disconnected graphs, self-loops, and graphs with cross-links.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every reachable vertex is discovered exactly according to the intended traversal state model.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B41-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B43-0002","D11-ALG-B44-0002","D11-ALG-B46-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"Lecture 14: Depth-first search, topological sort","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B43-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Depth-First Search","concept":"DFS Complexity","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What is the standard complexity of DFS with adjacency lists?","answer":"DFS runs in O(|V| + |E|) time with adjacency-list representation when each vertex and represented edge is processed a constant number of times.","explanation":"DFS visits reachable vertices and scans their adjacency lists. A full-graph traversal can invoke DFS from multiple unvisited vertices to cover disconnected components.","performance_implication":"The complexity is linear in the explicit graph representation.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B41-0002","D11-ALG-B43-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B42-0003","D11-ALG-B45-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"Graph reachability by BFS or DFS","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B43-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Depth-First Search","concept":"Recursive DFS Stack Risk","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What implementation risk exists when DFS is implemented recursively?","answer":"A very deep graph can create a recursion depth that exceeds the execution environment's supported call stack or recursion limit.","explanation":"The algorithmic DFS idea does not require recursion; recursion is only one implementation technique.","alternative":"Use an explicit stack when graph depth may exceed safe recursion depth or when iterative control is preferred.","constraint":"The safe recursion depth is environment-dependent and should not be assumed from algorithmic complexity alone.","testing_guidance":"Test very deep path-like graphs and environments with constrained recursion limits.","verification_guidance":"Verify behavior on depth-stress inputs and ensure failure is handled rather than silently truncating traversal.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"implementation-dependent","version_range":"environment-dependent","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B43-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B50-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived implementation guidance from standard DFS model","source_type":"derived_principle","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.94,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B44-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Traversal Correctness","concept":"BFS Distance Invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariant can verify BFS shortest-distance correctness?","answer":"For every discovered vertex v other than the source, its stored distance should equal the distance of its discovery parent plus one, and the traversal should discover vertices in nondecreasing distance layers.","principle":"The FIFO frontier maintains the layer ordering required by unweighted shortest-path correctness.","failure_mode":"A vertex receives a nonminimal distance.","cause":"Incorrect queue discipline, premature or incorrect distance assignment, or corrupted adjacency data.","solution":"Mark vertices at discovery, assign distance once under standard BFS semantics, and process the queue FIFO.","testing_guidance":"Use graphs with multiple competing paths and verify distances against an independent reference algorithm.","verification_guidance":"Check parent-distance consistency and global shortest-distance consistency.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B42-0001","D11-ALG-B42-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B50-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Breadth-first search","reference":"BFS graph exploration and shortest-path interpretation","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B44-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Traversal Correctness","concept":"DFS Discovery State","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must DFS maintain explicit discovery state?","answer":"Explicit discovery state prevents repeated traversal of already explored vertices and provides the basis for reasoning about DFS structure, cycles, and completion.","explanation":"In graphs, unlike trees, multiple paths can reach the same vertex. Without visited-state control, traversal can repeat work or fail to terminate on cycles.","counterexample":"A recursive traversal that follows every outgoing edge without marking vertices can recurse indefinitely on a directed cycle.","solution":"Mark a vertex when it enters the traversal's discovered state and define how completed state is represented when needed.","testing_guidance":"Test self-loops, two-vertex cycles, larger directed cycles, and converging paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every vertex has a well-defined discovery state and that no edge causes uncontrolled revisitation.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B43-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B46-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Depth-first search, topological sort","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B44-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Traversal Failure Modes","concept":"Visited Marking Too Late","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What can go wrong if graph vertices are marked visited only after full processing?","answer":"Multiple active traversal branches can enqueue or recurse into the same vertex before any branch records it as visited, causing redundant work and potentially incorrect traversal-state behavior.","explanation":"The exact failure depends on the traversal implementation, but the underlying issue is ambiguous ownership of discovery.","solution":"Define a clear discovery event and mark the vertex according to that event before other branches can treat it as undiscovered.","edge_case":"Parallel edges and converging paths make the issue especially visible.","testing_guidance":"Use two or more parents pointing to the same child and inspect discovery counts.","verification_guidance":"Assert that each vertex has one authoritative discovery event under the intended algorithm.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B42-0001","D11-ALG-B43-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B44-0001","D11-ALG-B44-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm implementation principle","source_type":"derived_principle","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B45-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Connectivity","concept":"Reachability","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is graph reachability?","answer":"A vertex v is reachable from source s if there exists a valid path from s to v following the graph's edge semantics.","explanation":"BFS and DFS can determine reachability in an explicitly represented graph by exploring the reachable region.","constraint":"For directed graphs, path direction must be respected.","testing_guidance":"Test reachable and unreachable targets, isolated vertices, cycles, and directed one-way paths.","verification_guidance":"For every reported reachable vertex, validate a path from the source; for every reported unreachable vertex, validate against an independent traversal or reference.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B42-0001","D11-ALG-B43-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B45-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"Graph reachability by BFS or DFS","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B45-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Connectivity","concept":"Connected Components","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How can connected components be found in an undirected graph?","answer":"Repeatedly start BFS or DFS from an unvisited vertex; all vertices reached by that traversal form one connected component, and continue until every vertex has been visited.","explanation":"Each traversal identifies one maximal region connected by undirected paths.","principle":"Full-graph traversal can partition an undirected graph into connected components.","edge_case":"An isolated vertex forms a component containing only itself.","testing_guidance":"Test empty graphs, one vertex, isolated vertices, multiple components, and a fully connected graph.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every vertex belongs to exactly one component and that every pair of vertices within a component has a path between them.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B45-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B42-0003","D11-ALG-B43-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"Graph exploration and connected components","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B45-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Connectivity","concept":"Directed Reachability versus Undirected Connectivity","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should directed reachability not be confused with undirected connectivity?","answer":"Undirected connectivity treats an edge as a symmetric connection, whereas directed reachability requires following edge directions. A directed graph can contain a path from A to B without a path from B to A.","explanation":"The same edge set can produce different connectivity semantics depending on direction.","counterexample":"A→B gives reachability from A to B but not necessarily from B to A.","solution":"Choose the graph property and traversal semantics explicitly before implementing connectivity analysis.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B41-0004","D11-ALG-B45-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B46-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived graph-model distinction","source_type":"derived_principle","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B46-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"DAGs","concept":"Directed Acyclic Graph","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a directed acyclic graph?","answer":"A directed acyclic graph, or DAG, is a directed graph containing no directed cycle.","explanation":"The absence of directed cycles permits a topological ordering in which every directed edge points from an earlier position to a later position.","principle":"Acyclic dependency structure enables ordering and one-pass dynamic processing.","correct_example":"A build dependency graph in which a package depends only on earlier build prerequisites can form a DAG.","invalid_example":"A dependency cycle A→B→A is not a DAG.","testing_guidance":"Test self-loops and directed cycles because either violates acyclicity.","verification_guidance":"Verify acyclicity before applying algorithms whose correctness requires a DAG.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B41-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B46-0002","D11-ALG-B47-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Topological sort","reference":"Directed acyclic graph relationship","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B46-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Topological Ordering","concept":"Topological Sort","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"What is a topological ordering?","answer":"A topological ordering arranges the vertices of a directed acyclic graph so that for every directed edge u→v, u appears before v.","explanation":"Topological ordering represents a partial-order dependency relation as a linear sequence consistent with all directed precedence constraints.","constraint":"A directed cycle prevents a valid topological ordering.","correct_example":"If A→B and B→C, an ordering A,B,C satisfies the dependency constraints.","invalid_example":"C,B,A violates both directed precedence relationships.","testing_guidance":"Validate every edge after producing the ordering by checking source position < destination position.","verification_guidance":"A valid result must contain every vertex exactly once and satisfy every directed edge constraint.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B46-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B46-0003","D11-ALG-B47-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Topological sort","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B46-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Topological Ordering","concept":"Cycle Detection through Topological Ordering","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can topological sorting reveal that a directed graph is cyclic?","answer":"If a topological-sort procedure cannot place every vertex while satisfying all precedence constraints, the graph contains a directed cycle under the procedure's assumptions.","explanation":"A directed cycle imposes mutually incompatible precedence requirements.","failure_mode":"Assuming every directed graph has a topological order","cause":"The acyclicity prerequisite was not checked.","solution":"Detect cycle conditions and return a failure or explicit cyclic status instead of fabricating an ordering.","testing_guidance":"Include self-loops, two-node cycles, longer cycles, and acyclic graphs.","verification_guidance":"Validate the produced ordering and confirm that all vertices are included exactly once.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B46-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B43-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Depth-first search, topological sort","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B46-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"DAG Processing","concept":"Topological Order as Processing Order","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is topological order useful beyond dependency display?","answer":"A topological order provides a valid sequence for processing DAG vertices so that each vertex's predecessors can be processed before the vertex itself.","explanation":"This allows one-pass dynamic computations on DAGs, including shortest-path relaxation when edge weights may be arbitrary.","correct_example":"Compute DAG shortest paths by processing vertices in topological order and relaxing outgoing edges.","constraint":"The graph must remain acyclic and the ordering must be valid.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B46-0002","D11-ALG-B47-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B47-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"DAG shortest paths","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B47-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Shortest Paths","concept":"Single-Source Shortest Paths","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is the single-source shortest-path problem?","answer":"Given a weighted graph and a source vertex, the single-source shortest-path problem seeks the minimum path cost from the source to every reachable vertex, subject to the graph's weight and cycle conditions.","explanation":"The correct algorithm depends on graph structure and edge-weight restrictions.","constraint":"Negative cycles reachable from the source can make finite shortest-path values undefined.","testing_guidance":"Include unreachable vertices, zero weights, positive weights, negative weights where supported, and negative cycles where relevant.","verification_guidance":"Verify path feasibility and compare path cost against the algorithm's distance output.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B41-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B47-0002","D11-ALG-B48-0001","D11-ALG-B49-0001","D11-ALG-B50-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Single-source shortest paths problem","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B47-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Shortest Paths","concept":"Edge Relaxation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is edge relaxation in shortest-path algorithms?","answer":"Relaxing an edge u→v checks whether the known distance to u plus the edge weight gives a smaller candidate distance for v; if so, v's distance and predecessor can be updated.","explanation":"Relaxation is the fundamental local operation used by several shortest-path algorithms.","correct_example":"If dist[u] is finite and dist[u]+w(u,v) < dist[v], update dist[v] and set its predecessor to u.","invalid_example":"Updating v without comparing the candidate against its current distance can overwrite a better path.","failure_mode":"Incorrect distance update","cause":"Relaxation condition is reversed, overflow is ignored, or unreachable vertices are treated as finite.","solution":"Define explicit infinity semantics and perform a safe candidate comparison before updating.","testing_guidance":"Test improving edges, non-improving edges, unreachable sources, zero weights, and negative edges where the selected algorithm supports them.","verification_guidance":"Check that every successful update strictly improves the represented distance under exact arithmetic or a correctly bounded numeric representation.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B47-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B48-0002","D11-ALG-B49-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Single-source shortest paths problem","reference":"Shortest-path relaxation","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B47-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"DAG Shortest Paths","concept":"DAG Shortest Paths","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"How can shortest paths be computed in a DAG?","answer":"Topologically order the DAG, initialize the source distance to zero and other distances to infinity, then process vertices in topological order and relax each outgoing edge.","explanation":"A topological order ensures that when a vertex is processed, all predecessor relationships relevant to the DAG's forward structure have been ordered before it.","constraint":"The graph must be a DAG; edge weights may be negative because the absence of directed cycles prevents repeated negative-cycle reduction.","testing_guidance":"Test positive, zero, and negative edge weights; unreachable vertices; multiple paths; and invalid cyclic inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify the topological ordering and then compare each returned distance with an independently enumerated or reference shortest path on small graphs.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B46-0002","D11-ALG-B47-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B50-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"DAG shortest paths","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B48-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Dijkstra","concept":"Dijkstra Algorithm","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"What is Dijkstra's algorithm used for?","answer":"Dijkstra's algorithm solves the single-source shortest-path problem for graphs whose edge weights are non-negative.","explanation":"It repeatedly selects the unsettled vertex with the smallest tentative distance and relaxes its outgoing edges. The non-negative-weight restriction is essential to its standard correctness guarantee.","constraint":"Negative edge weights invalidate the standard greedy correctness argument.","correct_example":"Use Dijkstra for a weighted road network when all modeled travel costs are non-negative.","invalid_example":"Using standard Dijkstra on a graph containing a reachable negative edge can produce an incorrect shortest-path result.","failure_mode":"Invalid weight assumption","cause":"The implementation does not enforce or document non-negative edge weights.","solution":"Reject unsupported negative weights or select an algorithm that supports them.","testing_guidance":"Include zero weights, positive weights, disconnected vertices, multiple equal shortest paths, and negative-weight rejection tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify all accepted edge weights satisfy the algorithm's precondition before execution.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B47-0001","D11-ALG-B47-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B48-0002","D11-ALG-B49-0001","D11-ALG-B50-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Lecture 16: Dijkstra","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B48-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Dijkstra","concept":"Priority Queue Dijkstra","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is a priority queue useful in Dijkstra's algorithm?","answer":"A priority queue efficiently identifies the vertex with the smallest current tentative distance, which is the next candidate for finalization under Dijkstra's non-negative-weight assumptions.","explanation":"Heap-based priority queues are a common implementation choice. The exact complexity depends on the priority-queue implementation and graph representation.","performance_implication":"A binary min-heap supports logarithmic insertion/extraction operations and is commonly used for practical Dijkstra implementations.","edge_case":"Duplicate priority-queue entries can occur in implementations that use insertion rather than decrease-key; stale entries must be detected safely.","testing_guidance":"Test graphs that cause repeated distance improvements and therefore stale queue entries.","verification_guidance":"When popping a queue entry, verify whether its distance still matches the current best-known distance before processing it as current.","language":"Python","framework":"Python standard library","version":"3.10+","version_range":"3.10-3.15","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B47-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B48-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Python Documentation","source_type":"official_documentation","title":"heapq — Heap queue algorithm","reference":"Min-heap invariant and priority queue API","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B48-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Dijkstra","concept":"Dijkstra Finalization Invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariant supports Dijkstra's correctness?","answer":"When a vertex with minimum tentative distance is selected under non-negative edge weights, that distance is final and equals the shortest-path distance from the source.","explanation":"A negative edge could later reduce a supposedly finalized distance, which is why the non-negative condition is fundamental.","failure_mode":"Premature finalization under unsupported weights","cause":"A reachable negative edge violates Dijkstra's assumptions.","solution":"Validate edge weights or use Bellman-Ford or an appropriate DAG-specific algorithm.","testing_guidance":"Use non-negative graphs for positive correctness tests and negative-edge cases as explicit precondition tests.","verification_guidance":"Check algorithm preconditions and compare finalized distances against a trusted reference on test graphs.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B48-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B49-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Dijkstra","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B49-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Bellman-Ford","concept":"Bellman-Ford Algorithm","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"What is Bellman-Ford used for?","answer":"Bellman-Ford solves the single-source shortest-path problem in weighted graphs that may contain negative edge weights and can detect reachable negative-weight cycles.","explanation":"It repeatedly relaxes all edges and then performs an additional check for further improvements to identify a reachable negative cycle.","constraint":"A reachable negative-weight cycle means finite shortest-path distances are not well-defined for vertices whose path can exploit that cycle indefinitely.","correct_example":"Use Bellman-Ford when negative edge weights are valid and negative-cycle detection is required.","invalid_example":"Assuming all shortest-path outputs are finite when a reachable negative cycle exists.","testing_guidance":"Test positive weights, negative edges without negative cycles, disconnected negative cycles, and reachable negative cycles.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the implementation distinguishes unreachable negative cycles from cycles reachable from the source.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B47-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B49-0002","D11-ALG-B50-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Bellman-Ford algorithm","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B49-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Bellman-Ford","concept":"Negative Cycle Detection","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How does Bellman-Ford detect a reachable negative-weight cycle?","answer":"After the standard relaxation passes, an additional pass checks whether any edge can still produce a strict distance improvement from a reachable source state. Such an improvement indicates a reachable negative-weight cycle.","explanation":"The extra improvement means a finite sequence of relaxations can continue reducing a distance rather than converging to a finite optimum.","failure_mode":"Incorrectly flagging unreachable negative cycles","cause":"The implementation relaxes edges from vertices whose distance is still effectively infinity.","solution":"Only consider relaxation candidates whose source distance is reachable.","testing_guidance":"Place a negative cycle in a disconnected component and verify that it does not affect the source's reachable shortest paths.","verification_guidance":"Check that cycle detection is conditioned on source reachability.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B49-0001","D11-ALG-B47-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B50-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Bellman-Ford algorithm","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B49-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Bellman-Ford","concept":"Bellman-Ford Complexity","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What is the standard time complexity of Bellman-Ford?","answer":"The standard Bellman-Ford implementation performs O(|V|) edge-relaxation passes over O(|E|) edges, giving O(|V||E|) time.","explanation":"The classic formulation uses |V|-1 passes followed by a final negative-cycle detection pass.","performance_implication":"Bellman-Ford is generally slower asymptotically than Dijkstra under non-negative edge-weight conditions, but it supports negative weights and negative-cycle detection.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B49-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B48-0001","D11-ALG-B50-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Bellman-Ford algorithm","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B50-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Algorithm Selection","concept":"Shortest Path Algorithm Selection","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should an algorithm be selected for a single-source shortest-path problem?","answer":"Use BFS for unweighted unit-edge graphs, DAG relaxation for DAGs, Dijkstra when accepted edge weights are non-negative, and Bellman-Ford when general edge weights including negative values must be supported.","explanation":"Algorithm selection should follow graph structure and weight restrictions rather than preference alone.","correct_example":"A DAG with negative edges can use topological-order relaxation without requiring Dijkstra's non-negative-weight restriction.","invalid_example":"Selecting Dijkstra solely because the graph is weighted while ignoring reachable negative edges.","tradeoff":"Bellman-Ford has broader weight support but higher standard asymptotic cost; Dijkstra is applicable under non-negative weights; DAG relaxation exploits acyclicity.","testing_guidance":"Build a decision-table test suite covering unweighted graphs, DAGs with negative edges, general non-negative graphs, and graphs with negative edges or negative cycles.","verification_guidance":"Before execution, verify the graph class and weight constraints against the algorithm's preconditions.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B42-0002","D11-ALG-B47-0003","D11-ALG-B48-0001","D11-ALG-B49-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B50-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Quiz 2 Review; Single Source Shortest Paths Review","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B50-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Shortest Path Failure Modes","concept":"Infinity Arithmetic","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"What implementation error occurs when unreachable distance values are treated as ordinary finite numbers?","answer":"Arithmetic involving an unreachable sentinel can create false candidate paths, overflow, or incorrectly mark unreachable vertices as reachable.","explanation":"Shortest-path implementations must define the semantics of infinity and guard relaxation from unreachable vertices.","invalid_example":"Computing infinity + negative_weight using a finite integer sentinel without checking whether the source vertex is reachable.","failure_mode":"False reachability or numeric corruption","cause":"The implementation performs relaxation without a reachability check or uses an unsafe sentinel representation.","solution":"Represent unreachable states explicitly or guard arithmetic before relaxation.","edge_case":"Extremely large positive or negative weights can expose sentinel collisions or overflow.","testing_guidance":"Test unreachable vertices, extreme weights, and disconnected components.","verification_guidance":"Assert that unreachable vertices remain unreachable unless a valid source-to-vertex path exists.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"implementation-dependent","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B47-0002","D11-ALG-B49-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B50-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived shortest-path implementation safety principle","source_type":"derived_principle","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B50-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Testing and Verification","concept":"Graph Algorithm Adversarial Test Suite","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What graph cases should be included in a robust algorithm test suite?","answer":"A robust suite should include empty graphs, single vertices, isolated vertices, disconnected components, self-loops where permitted, parallel edges where permitted, cycles, DAGs, deep paths, dense and sparse graphs, duplicate shortest paths, unreachable targets, zero weights, negative edges where supported, and negative cycles where relevant.","explanation":"Graph algorithms often fail at boundary conditions rather than on ordinary examples.","testing_guidance":"Partition tests by structural property and algorithm precondition instead of relying only on random examples.","verification_guidance":"Use independent invariants, reference solutions for small graphs, metamorphic relations, and explicit precondition checks.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B42-0001","D11-ALG-B43-0001","D11-ALG-B47-0001","D11-ALG-B48-0001","D11-ALG-B49-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B44-0001","D11-ALG-B44-0002","D11-ALG-B49-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm testing methodology","source_type":"derived_principle","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B50-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.14","topic":"Graph Algorithms","subtopic":"Integrated Graph Reasoning","concept":"Graph Algorithm Decision Procedure","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What decision procedure should an autonomous software-engineering system use when faced with a graph problem?","answer":"First identify vertex and edge semantics; then determine directedness, graph structure, density, weights, and required output; identify algorithm preconditions; select the simplest correct algorithm satisfying those constraints; define invariants; test adversarial cases; and verify the result independently where practical.","explanation":"Algorithm choice is a constraint-satisfaction problem. The same domain can require different algorithms after a change in graph direction, weights, acyclicity, or output requirements.","principle":"Model first, constrain second, select third, verify fourth.","failure_mode":"Algorithm selected from name recognition without checking assumptions.","cause":"The system maps surface wording directly to an algorithm without analyzing graph properties.","solution":"Use an explicit graph-property and algorithm-precondition decision layer.","testing_guidance":"Create paired problems with identical domain vocabulary but different graph constraints and verify that the selected algorithms differ when required.","verification_guidance":"Record graph properties, algorithm preconditions, selected algorithm, rejected alternatives, and correctness rationale.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B41-0001","D11-ALG-B42-0001","D11-ALG-B43-0001","D11-ALG-B46-0002","D11-ALG-B47-0001","D11-ALG-B48-0001","D11-ALG-B49-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B31-0001","D11-ALG-B40-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Synthesized from authoritative graph-algorithm principles","source_type":"derived_synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

