{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B61-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Asymptotic Analysis","concept":"Asymptotic Analysis","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is asymptotic analysis?","answer":"Asymptotic analysis studies how an algorithm's resource usage grows as the input size becomes large, abstracting away machine-specific constant factors and lower-order terms.","explanation":"The analysis commonly considers running time and space as functions of an input-size parameter n.","principle":"Analyze growth behavior rather than relying only on measurements from one machine or one input.","correct_example":"Comparing n log n and n^2 by their growth rates as n increases.","invalid_example":"Concluding that an algorithm is asymptotically faster solely because one benchmark run was faster.","constraint":"The input-size definition and computational model must be specified when they materially affect the result.","testing_guidance":"Check whether the stated complexity follows from the algorithm's actual operations and input-size definition.","verification_guidance":"Derive the dominant operation count or recurrence and independently simplify its asymptotic growth.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B61-0002","D11-ALG-B61-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Asymptotic time complexity","reference":"Asymptotic time complexity definition","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B61-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Asymptotic Notation","concept":"Big-O","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does f(n)=O(g(n)) mean?","answer":"f(n)=O(g(n)) means there exist positive constants c and n0 such that 0 <= f(n) <= c*g(n) for every n >= n0.","explanation":"Big-O is an asymptotic upper bound. It does not necessarily mean that f and g have equal growth rates.","invalid_example":"Treating O(n^2) as meaning that the function is exactly proportional to n^2 for every input.","constraint":"The constants in the definition must be independent of n.","testing_guidance":"When proving an O bound, explicitly identify suitable constants or derive the bound algebraically.","verification_guidance":"Check the inequality for all sufficiently large n rather than validating only a finite sample.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B61-0003","D11-ALG-B61-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Big-O notation","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B61-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Asymptotic Notation","concept":"Theta Notation","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does f(n)=Theta(g(n)) mean?","answer":"f(n)=Theta(g(n)) means that f(n) is bounded above and below by positive constant multiples of g(n) for all sufficiently large n.","explanation":"Theta expresses an asymptotically tight bound rather than only an upper bound.","correct_example":"If an algorithm performs exactly a constant number of operations for each input item, its running time can be Theta(n).","invalid_example":"Calling every O(n) algorithm Theta(n) without establishing a matching lower bound.","verification_guidance":"Prove both O(g(n)) and Omega(g(n)) when claiming Theta(g(n)).","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B61-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B61-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Asymptotic bounds","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B61-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Asymptotic Notation","concept":"Omega and Little-O","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How do Omega and little-o differ from Big-O?","answer":"Omega provides an asymptotic lower bound, while little-o expresses a strict asymptotic growth relationship in which f grows strictly slower than g under the formal definition.","explanation":"These notations describe different relationships and should not be substituted merely because they involve similar symbols.","constraint":"Formal definitions should be used when a proof depends on strict versus non-strict asymptotic relationships.","verification_guidance":"For lower-bound claims establish an appropriate Omega relationship; for little-o claims establish the required limiting or equivalent inequality condition.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B61-0002","D11-ALG-B61-0003"],"related_records":[],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Asymptotic lower bound and asymptotic notation","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B62-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Growth Rates","concept":"Asymptotic Growth Ordering","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What common asymptotic growth ordering should an algorithmic reasoning system recognize?","answer":"For standard positive-growth functions, logarithmic growth is asymptotically slower than polynomial growth, fixed-degree polynomials are slower than exponentials, and exponentials are slower than factorial growth under conventional integer-domain comparisons.","explanation":"The exact ordering depends on the functions being compared, but the general hierarchy helps eliminate obviously inferior asymptotic alternatives.","correct_example":"log n grows more slowly than n, n grows more slowly than n^2, and n^2 grows more slowly than 2^n.","invalid_example":"Assuming that a higher constant factor can change asymptotic ordering for sufficiently large n.","testing_guidance":"Use symbolic comparison rather than finite benchmark samples when establishing asymptotic ordering.","verification_guidance":"Prove the ordering using limits, inequalities, or known asymptotic relations.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B61-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B62-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Design and Analysis of Algorithms","reference":"Asymptotic performance and comparison of growth rates","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B62-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Dominant Terms","concept":"Dominant Term Simplification","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can lower-order terms and constant factors usually be omitted in asymptotic analysis?","answer":"Asymptotic notation focuses on growth as n becomes large, so fixed constant multipliers and lower-order terms do not change the leading growth class.","explanation":"For example, an expression such as 3n^2+5n+7 has quadratic asymptotic growth.","invalid_example":"Removing a term without checking whether it is actually lower order can produce an incorrect bound.","constraint":"The simplification is appropriate for asymptotic classification, not necessarily for practical performance prediction at small input sizes.","testing_guidance":"Compare the original function and proposed asymptotic bound symbolically.","verification_guidance":"Show that the omitted terms can be absorbed into a constant multiple of the dominant term for sufficiently large n.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B61-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B62-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Big-O notation","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B62-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Input Size","concept":"Input Size Modeling","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must input size be defined before deriving complexity?","answer":"Complexity is a function of an explicitly chosen input-size measure, and different dimensions of the same input can produce different complexity expressions.","explanation":"For graphs, for example, both the number of vertices and edges may matter; for strings, text length and pattern length may both matter.","correct_example":"Reporting BFS complexity as O(V+E) identifies both graph dimensions.","invalid_example":"Calling every graph operation O(n) without defining whether n represents vertices, edges, or total representation size.","verification_guidance":"State all independent size parameters that materially affect the operation count.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B61-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B63-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived complexity-modeling principle","source_type":"derived_principle","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B63-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Recurrences","concept":"Recurrence Relation","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a recurrence relation in algorithm analysis?","answer":"A recurrence relation defines the cost or another quantity for a problem of size n in terms of costs for smaller problem sizes.","explanation":"Divide-and-conquer algorithms frequently generate recurrences because a problem is decomposed into smaller subproblems plus combination work.","correct_example":"T(n)=2T(n/2)+Theta(n) represents two half-size recursive subproblems plus linear combination work.","invalid_example":"Writing a recurrence that ignores a non-recursive cost that grows with n can produce an incomplete complexity model.","testing_guidance":"Derive the recurrence directly from the algorithm's recursive calls and non-recursive work.","verification_guidance":"Check base cases, subproblem sizes, number of subproblems, and combination cost.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B62-0003"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B63-0002","D11-ALG-B63-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Introduction to Algorithms","reference":"Recurrences and divide-and-conquer analysis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B63-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Recurrences","concept":"Recursion Tree","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How can a recursion tree help analyze a recurrence?","answer":"A recursion tree expands recursive calls into levels, labels the non-recursive work at each node, sums the work per level, and then sums across levels until the base cases are reached.","explanation":"The tree provides a visual accounting of how work is distributed across recursive subproblems.","correct_example":"For a balanced divide-and-conquer recurrence, identify the number of nodes and total work at each depth.","invalid_example":"Summing only the root work while ignoring recursive levels underestimates the total cost.","testing_guidance":"Compare the resulting bound against substitution or another independent recurrence method.","verification_guidance":"Verify the number of nodes and subproblem sizes at each level.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B63-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B63-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Recurrences and divide-and-conquer","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B63-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Recurrences","concept":"Master Theorem Applicability","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"When can a standard Master Theorem form be applied?","answer":"A Master Theorem formulation applies only when the recurrence satisfies the theorem's structural assumptions, typically involving a fixed number of equal-size subproblems and a specified polynomially comparable non-recursive term.","explanation":"Not every recurrence fits the standard theorem, and forcing an incompatible recurrence into the theorem can yield an incorrect result.","invalid_example":"Applying a standard Master Theorem case to unequal or irregular recursive subproblem sizes without establishing an applicable generalized theorem.","solution":"First classify the recurrence structure; use substitution, recursion trees, or an appropriate generalized theorem when the standard form does not apply.","testing_guidance":"Create examples that differ only in recurrence structure and verify whether the theorem's prerequisites hold.","verification_guidance":"Record the theorem assumptions explicitly before invoking the theorem.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B63-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B64-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Recurrences and Master Method","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B64-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Correctness","subtopic":"Proof Methods","concept":"Correctness Proof","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a correctness proof for an algorithm?","answer":"A correctness proof establishes that an algorithm satisfies its specified input-output contract for every input within its stated domain.","explanation":"Correctness is stronger than observing that an implementation works on selected examples.","principle":"Specify the contract, prove termination when required, and prove that the returned result satisfies the contract.","invalid_example":"Claiming correctness because a test suite passes without establishing whether the tests cover the complete specification.","testing_guidance":"Use testing to find counterexamples and implementation defects, but do not treat finite tests as a universal proof unless exhaustive coverage is justified.","verification_guidance":"Separate empirical testing evidence from mathematical correctness arguments.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B64-0002","D11-ALG-B65-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Design and Analysis of Algorithms","reference":"Rigorous correctness proofs","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B64-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Correctness","subtopic":"Proof Methods","concept":"Loop Invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is a loop invariant?","answer":"A loop invariant is a property that is true before loop execution, remains true after each iteration under the loop's transition, and can be used at termination to establish the desired result.","explanation":"The standard proof structure is initialization, maintenance, and termination.","correct_example":"For insertion sort, an invariant can state that the sorted prefix contains exactly the elements originally present in that prefix and is sorted.","invalid_example":"Using a property that becomes true only after the loop finishes cannot serve as a maintained loop invariant.","testing_guidance":"Use the invariant to identify states that implementation tests should exercise.","verification_guidance":"Prove initialization, maintenance, and termination explicitly.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B64-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B64-0003","D11-ALG-B65-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Correctness of Algorithms","reference":"Loop invariants and inductive proofs","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B64-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Correctness","subtopic":"Proof Methods","concept":"Mathematical Induction for Algorithms","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can induction support an algorithm correctness proof?","answer":"An inductive proof establishes a base case and then proves that correctness for smaller instances implies correctness for the current larger instance according to the algorithm's recursive or iterative structure.","explanation":"Induction is particularly natural for recursive algorithms and recursively defined data.","failure_mode":"Incomplete induction hypothesis","cause":"The hypothesis is too weak to establish the required property for the next case.","solution":"State an induction hypothesis strong enough to cover every dependency used by the algorithm's next step.","verification_guidance":"Check base cases, induction assumptions, and the exact logical transition from smaller to larger instances.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B64-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B63-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Correctness of Algorithms","reference":"Inductive proofs","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B65-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","topic":"Algorithm Correctness","subtopic":"Termination","concept":"Termination Argument","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is a termination argument?","answer":"A termination argument establishes that an algorithm cannot continue indefinitely for inputs within its stated domain.","explanation":"Correct output is insufficient if an algorithm may fail to terminate.","principle":"Identify a quantity or well-founded measure that progresses toward a terminal condition and cannot decrease or increase indefinitely in the relevant direction.","correct_example":"A loop over a finite index range terminates because the index moves monotonically toward a fixed boundary.","invalid_example":"Assuming termination because the loop variable usually changes without proving that it reaches the stopping condition.","testing_guidance":"Include boundary values, zero-length inputs, repeated states, and adversarial transitions.","verification_guidance":"Identify a variant function or monotonic progress measure and prove that it reaches the termination condition.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B64-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B65-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm verification principle","source_type":"derived_principle","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B65-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Correctness","subtopic":"Termination","concept":"Well-Founded Progress Measure","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a useful way to prove termination of a recursive algorithm?","answer":"Define a well-founded measure of the problem state and show that every recursive call strictly progresses toward a base state under that measure.","explanation":"A well-founded measure prevents an infinite descending sequence in the relevant state space.","invalid_example":"A recursive call that does not reduce the stated problem measure can recurse indefinitely even if the code appears structurally recursive.","testing_guidance":"Test smallest valid inputs and cases near the base-case boundary.","verification_guidance":"For every recursive branch, prove that the measure decreases and eventually reaches a base-case region.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B65-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B63-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived recursive-verification principle","source_type":"derived_principle","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B65-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","topic":"Algorithm Correctness","subtopic":"Partial versus Total Correctness","concept":"Partial and Total Correctness","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between partial and total correctness?","answer":"Partial correctness establishes that if an algorithm terminates, its result satisfies the specification; total correctness additionally establishes that the algorithm terminates for all inputs in its specified domain.","explanation":"Termination therefore forms a separate logical component of a total-correctness argument.","constraint":"The exact proof obligations depend on the algorithmic model and specification.","verification_guidance":"For total correctness, maintain separate evidence for functional correctness and termination.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B64-0001","D11-ALG-B65-0001"],"related_records":[],"provenance":{"source":"Derived formal-verification principle","source_type":"derived_principle","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B66-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Amortized Analysis","concept":"Amortized Analysis","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is amortized analysis?","answer":"Amortized analysis bounds the average cost per operation over a sequence of operations without relying on a probability distribution over inputs.","explanation":"An individual operation may be expensive while the total cost of a long valid sequence remains bounded by a useful aggregate expression.","principle":"Analyze the total cost of a sequence rather than independently charging every operation its worst-case cost.","correct_example":"Dynamic table expansion has occasional expensive resizing operations but can have constant amortized insertion cost under standard doubling policies.","invalid_example":"Calling amortized analysis an average-case analysis based on randomly distributed inputs.","testing_guidance":"Construct operation sequences containing both cheap and expensive operations and compare total actual cost with total amortized charges.","verification_guidance":"Prove that total amortized charge over every valid sequence upper-bounds total actual cost.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B66-0002","D11-ALG-B66-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Amortized analysis","reference":"Lecture 11: Amortized Analysis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B66-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Amortized Analysis","concept":"Aggregate Method","knowledge_type":"METHOD","question":"What is the aggregate method of amortized analysis?","answer":"The aggregate method bounds the total actual cost of a sequence of n operations and divides that bound by n to obtain an amortized cost per operation.","explanation":"The method reasons about the sequence globally without assigning explicit credits or potentials to individual states.","correct_example":"Show that a sequence of n dynamic-array insertions performs only O(n) total element copies under geometric expansion.","invalid_example":"Dividing the worst-case cost of one operation by the number of operations without analyzing the sequence's total cost.","testing_guidance":"Count all expensive events over complete operation sequences rather than treating them as independently recurring at worst-case frequency.","verification_guidance":"Derive a total-cost bound first, then divide by sequence length.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B66-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B66-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Amortized analysis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B66-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Amortized Analysis","concept":"Potential Method","knowledge_type":"METHOD","question":"What is the potential method?","answer":"The potential method assigns a nonnegative potential to each data-structure state and defines amortized cost as actual cost plus the change in potential between states.","explanation":"Stored potential can represent prepaid work that is later used to pay for expensive operations.","constraint":"The potential function must be chosen and justified so that the resulting amortized bound holds over valid operation sequences.","invalid_example":"Choosing a potential that can become negative without accounting for its effect on the total bound.","verification_guidance":"Show that initial potential satisfies the required condition and that the sum of amortized costs upper-bounds total actual cost.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B66-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B66-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Amortized analysis","reference":"Potential method","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B66-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Amortized Analysis","concept":"Accounting Method","knowledge_type":"METHOD","question":"What is the accounting method in amortized analysis?","answer":"The accounting method assigns amortized charges to operations and interprets excess charge as stored credit that can pay for future expensive operations.","explanation":"The method requires a credit invariant ensuring that enough stored credit exists whenever a future operation needs it.","failure_mode":"Insufficient stored credit","cause":"The amortized charge assignment does not cover the expensive event sequence.","solution":"Construct charges and a credit invariant that guarantee total prepaid credit is sufficient.","verification_guidance":"Track credit after every operation and prove that it never becomes negative.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B66-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B66-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Amortized analysis","reference":"Accounting method","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B67-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Amortized Data Structures","concept":"Dynamic Array Expansion","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can dynamic-array insertion have constant amortized cost under geometric resizing?","answer":"When capacity is increased geometrically, expensive element-copy operations occur only at exponentially increasing capacities, so the total number of copied elements across many insertions is linear in the number of insertions.","explanation":"The expensive resize events are sparse enough that their aggregate cost can be distributed over the full operation sequence.","constraint":"The result depends on the resizing policy and the cost model for allocation and copying.","testing_guidance":"Simulate sequences that repeatedly cross capacity boundaries and count actual element copies.","verification_guidance":"Sum the copy costs across resize events and prove an O(n) aggregate bound for n insertions under the specified growth policy.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B66-0001","D11-ALG-B66-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B67-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Amortized analysis","reference":"Table doubling","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B67-0002","dataset_id":"D11-ALG-B67-0002","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Amortized Data Structures","concept":"Resizing Policy Tradeoff","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why does the dynamic-array growth factor matter?","answer":"A larger growth factor can reduce the frequency of resizing but may allocate more unused capacity, while a smaller factor can reduce unused capacity but may trigger resizing more often.","explanation":"The exact time-space tradeoff depends on the allocation model and growth policy.","constraint":"The asymptotic amortized result must be derived for the specific growth policy rather than assumed universally.","testing_guidance":"Measure allocation frequency, copied elements, and unused capacity under the chosen policy without conflating measurements with asymptotic proof.","verification_guidance":"Maintain separate asymptotic and empirical evidence for the resizing policy.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B67-0001"],"related_records":[],"provenance":{"source":"Derived data-structure tradeoff principle","source_type":"derived_principle","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B68-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Lower Bounds","concept":"Asymptotic Lower Bound","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an asymptotic lower bound for a problem?","answer":"An asymptotic lower bound describes a resource requirement that no algorithm can asymptotically beat under the specified problem definition and computational model.","explanation":"A lower bound is a statement about the problem or model, not merely the observed behavior of one implementation.","invalid_example":"Calling an algorithm's O(n log n) runtime a lower bound for the problem without proving that every valid algorithm requires that much work under the model.","constraint":"Lower bounds are model- and problem-specific.","verification_guidance":"State the computational model, input representation, and required output before interpreting a lower-bound result.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B61-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B68-0002","D11-ALG-B68-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Asymptotic lower bound","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B68-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Comparison Sorting","concept":"Comparison-Sorting Lower Bound","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why does comparison sorting have an Omega(n log n) worst-case lower bound in the standard comparison model?","answer":"A comparison sort can be modeled as a decision tree whose leaves distinguish possible input orderings. Distinguishing all n! permutations requires a tree with at least n! leaves, implying a worst-case depth of Omega(log(n!)), which is Omega(n log n).","explanation":"The lower bound applies to comparison-based sorting under the specified decision-tree model.","constraint":"The bound does not prohibit non-comparison sorting algorithms that exploit additional assumptions about the keys.","correct_example":"Merge sort and heapsort have O(n log n) worst-case comparison complexity, matching the standard lower-bound order.","invalid_example":"Claiming that no sorting algorithm can run in O(n) because counting or radix methods can exploit key-domain structure outside the comparison model.","testing_guidance":"Ensure lower-bound reasoning explicitly states the computational model.","verification_guidance":"Check that the algorithm class being compared is actually restricted to comparisons.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B68-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B68-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","source_type":"academic_course","title":"Design and Analysis of Algorithms","reference":"Lower bounds and linear-time sorting","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_ACADEMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B68-0003","dataset_id":"D11-ALG-B68-0003","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Lower-Bound Reasoning","concept":"Model Dependence","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must algorithm lower bounds specify a computational model?","answer":"A lower bound may hold for one class of operations or representations but not another, so the model determines which algorithms are included in the claim.","explanation":"For example, comparison-sorting lower bounds do not directly constrain algorithms that exploit bounded integer keys.","failure_mode":"Overgeneralized lower-bound claim","cause":"The computational model is omitted from the statement.","solution":"Include algorithm class, input representation, allowed operations, and required output in the lower-bound record.","verification_guidance":"Reject lower-bound claims whose model assumptions are absent or ambiguous.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B68-0001","D11-ALG-B68-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST complexity reference plus MIT algorithm-analysis curriculum","source_type":"derived_synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B69-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Worst Average Best Case","concept":"Case Analysis","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What are best-case, worst-case, and average-case complexity?","answer":"Best-case complexity describes the least costly inputs of a given size, worst-case complexity describes the most costly inputs of a given size, and average-case complexity describes expected cost under an explicitly specified input distribution or probability model.","explanation":"These are distinct claims and must not be interchanged.","invalid_example":"Calling an observed benchmark average-case complexity without specifying the input distribution.","constraint":"Average-case analysis requires a probability model or distribution assumption.","testing_guidance":"Construct separate input families that realize different costs and document any distribution used for expected analysis.","verification_guidance":"Record the case definition and assumptions alongside every complexity claim.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B61-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures","source_type":"government_reference","title":"Complexity and average-case concepts","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B69-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Performance Claims","concept":"Asymptotic Versus Empirical Performance","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should asymptotic complexity and empirical benchmark results be distinguished?","answer":"Asymptotic complexity predicts growth under a specified model, while empirical benchmarking measures observed behavior for selected implementations, environments, and inputs.","explanation":"Neither automatically replaces the other. Asymptotic analysis provides general growth reasoning; measurements provide environment-specific evidence.","invalid_example":"Using one benchmark to prove an asymptotic complexity class.","constraint":"Benchmark conclusions depend on hardware, runtime, compiler or interpreter, implementation, workload, and measurement methodology.","testing_guidance":"Record environment and workload when collecting empirical performance data.","verification_guidance":"Keep measured performance, theoretical complexity, and inferred practical conclusions in separate evidence fields.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B61-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST complexity definition and derived engineering methodology","source_type":"derived_synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B69-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Performance Claims","concept":"Complexity Measurement Honesty","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is a common failure when recording algorithm performance?","answer":"A common failure is presenting an estimated, theoretical, or unexecuted complexity claim as though it were an empirical measurement.","explanation":"The knowledge system must distinguish mathematical complexity from observed runtime and from unverified expectations.","solution":"Label theoretical complexity as theoretical, measurements as measured, estimates as estimated, and untested claims as not verified.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence metadata for every empirical performance claim.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B69-0002"],"related_records":[],"provenance":{"source":"Dataset integrity policy derived from evidence-driven engineering requirements","source_type":"derived_policy","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_POLICY","confidence":1.0,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}

{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B70-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Integrated Complexity Reasoning","concept":"Complexity Analysis Workflow","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What workflow should an autonomous software-engineering system use to analyze algorithm complexity?","answer":"Define the input-size parameters and computational model; identify primitive operations; derive operation counts or recurrences; classify best, worst, and average cases when meaningful; simplify asymptotically; state space usage separately; document assumptions; and independently verify the derivation.","explanation":"A structured workflow prevents unsupported complexity labels and makes analysis auditable.","principle":"Model → count → derive → simplify → qualify → verify.","failure_mode":"Complexity label without derivation","cause":"The system memorizes an algorithm name and attaches a standard complexity without checking the implementation or representation.","solution":"Require an explicit derivation trace tied to implementation structure and input model.","testing_guidance":"Compare the predicted operation structure against instrumented small cases when implementation measurement is available, without treating measurement as proof.","verification_guidance":"Require both a mathematical derivation and a machine-readable list of assumptions.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B61-0001","D11-ALG-B62-0003","D11-ALG-B63-0001","D11-ALG-B69-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B50-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"Synthesized from MIT algorithm-analysis curriculum and NIST complexity definitions","source_type":"derived_synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B70-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Correctness","subtopic":"Integrated Verification","concept":"Correctness Verification Workflow","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What workflow should an autonomous software-engineering system use to verify an algorithm?","answer":"Define the formal input-output contract; identify preconditions and postconditions; establish invariants; prove preservation; establish termination when total correctness is required; test boundary and adversarial cases; compare against an independent oracle when feasible; and record limitations explicitly.","explanation":"Mathematical reasoning and empirical testing provide complementary evidence and should not be conflated.","principle":"Contract → assumptions → invariant → preservation → termination → tests → independent verification → evidence record.","failure_mode":"Passing tests treated as universal correctness proof","cause":"Finite empirical evidence is mistaken for exhaustive proof.","solution":"Maintain separate proof, test, and execution evidence fields.","testing_guidance":"Include negative, boundary, randomized, regression, and property-based tests when appropriate.","verification_guidance":"A verification record must state what was proved, what was executed, what was tested, and what remains unknown.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B64-0001","D11-ALG-B64-0002","D11-ALG-B65-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B60-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare correctness curriculum plus derived verification methodology","source_type":"derived_synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B70-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Analysis","subtopic":"Complexity Failure Modes","concept":"Complexity Analysis Anti-Patterns","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Which complexity-analysis mistakes should an autonomous coding system detect?","answer":"Important anti-patterns include confusing O with Theta, ignoring input dimensions, omitting recursion costs, applying recurrence theorems outside their assumptions, counting only average observed behavior as worst-case, treating benchmark results as asymptotic proof, ignoring auxiliary space, and hiding implementation-dependent costs.","explanation":"These errors can produce code-generation and architecture decisions based on false performance assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Use adversarial review prompts that ask the system to identify missing parameters, assumptions, and unsupported bounds.","verification_guidance":"Require every nontrivial complexity claim to expose its model, derivation, case definition, and evidence level.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B61-0002","D11-ALG-B63-0003","D11-ALG-B69-0001","D11-ALG-B69-0002"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B70-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Synthesized algorithm-analysis failure taxonomy","source_type":"derived_synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B70-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.16","topic":"Algorithm Reasoning","subtopic":"Complexity and Correctness Integration","concept":"Algorithm Proof-and-Complexity Record","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What should a training-grade algorithm analysis record connect?","answer":"A complete analysis record should connect the problem specification, input model, algorithm variant, preconditions, correctness argument, termination argument, time complexity, space complexity, case assumptions, empirical evidence if available, tests, verification evidence, and known limitations.","explanation":"Separating these dimensions while preserving explicit relationships allows downstream systems to reason about correctness and performance without treating one type of evidence as another.","principle":"Correctness and complexity are separate claims that share an algorithmic model but require different evidence.","constraint":"A missing execution or benchmark must remain explicitly unverified rather than being inferred from theory.","testing_guidance":"Validate relationship completeness between implementation, complexity, correctness, tests, and verification records.","verification_guidance":"Reject records whose performance or correctness status is stronger than the evidence supports.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"algorithmic-general","version_range":"stable","dependencies":["D11-ALG-B70-0001","D11-ALG-B70-0002","D11-ALG-B70-0003"],"related_records":["D11-ALG-B61-0001","D11-ALG-B64-0001","D11-ALG-B69-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived knowledge-fabric integration principle","source_type":"derived_synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}

