{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B71-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Implementation Engineering","subtopic":"Cost-model awareness","concept":"Implementation cost model","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must an algorithm implementation be evaluated under an explicit cost model?","answer":"Theoretical complexity abstracts machine and implementation details, while a concrete implementation incurs costs from memory access, allocation, copying, branching, representation, synchronization, and runtime behavior. An implementation decision should therefore state which costs are represented by the analysis and which are outside its model.","explanation":"Two implementations with the same asymptotic complexity can have materially different practical behavior because their constant factors and lower-level operations differ. Algorithm engineering connects algorithmic analysis to implementation realities.","principle":"Do not treat asymptotic complexity as a complete description of implementation cost.","correct_example":"A linear scan and a hash-based lookup can both have useful asymptotic properties, but the implementation cost model must account for hashing, memory locality, allocation, and collision handling when comparing them.","invalid_example":"Assuming that every O(n) implementation is equally fast for every workload.","failure_mode":"Cost-model mismatch","cause":"Important implementation costs were omitted from the analysis.","solution":"State the computational model, implementation assumptions, and relevant cost dimensions before comparing implementations.","constraint":"The correct cost model depends on language, runtime, hardware, data representation, and workload.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B61-0001","D11-ALG-B69-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.5060 Algorithm Engineering","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"course scope on models of computation and performance engineering"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B71-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Implementation Engineering","subtopic":"Abstraction boundary","concept":"Algorithm versus implementation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should remain invariant when translating an algorithm into code?","answer":"The implementation must preserve the algorithm's required input assumptions, state transitions, output semantics, and correctness conditions even when representation, control structure, naming, or programming-language constructs change.","explanation":"An algorithm is an abstract computational procedure; implementation is a concrete realization. A valid implementation may use different language constructs while preserving the required behavior.","principle":"Separate semantic correctness from incidental implementation structure.","correct_example":"Implementing a recursive tree traversal iteratively with an explicit stack while preserving traversal order and required visit semantics.","invalid_example":"Replacing a required stable ordering operation with an unstable operation merely because both have the same asymptotic complexity.","failure_mode":"Semantic drift","cause":"Implementation optimization changed a behavior that was part of the algorithm's contract.","solution":"Define observable semantics before implementation and verify those semantics after transformation.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B64-0001","D11-ALG-B70-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT 6.01 Introduction to Electrical Engineering and Computer Science","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search37","evidence_basis":"distinction between algorithm and implementation"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B71-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Implementation Engineering","subtopic":"Representation cost","concept":"Representation-aware implementation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should data representation affect algorithm implementation decisions?","answer":"Choose representations that preserve required semantics while making dominant operations explicit and affordable under the target workload.","explanation":"The same abstract data structure can have different concrete representations with different access, update, memory, and copying behavior.","principle":"Optimize the representation for the operations the algorithm actually performs.","correct_example":"Using a contiguous representation when indexed traversal dominates and a node-based representation when structural insertion semantics dominate, subject to workload constraints.","invalid_example":"Selecting a representation solely because its abstract data structure has a favorable textbook complexity without checking the actual operations and memory behavior.","tradeoff":"Representation choices can improve locality or update flexibility while worsening other costs.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B71-0001","D11-ALG-B75-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.5060 Algorithm Engineering","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"algorithm engineering and implementation performance"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B71-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Implementation Engineering","subtopic":"Constant factors","concept":"Constant-factor awareness","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can constant factors matter despite identical asymptotic complexity?","answer":"Asymptotic notation suppresses multiplicative constants and lower-order terms. For finite workloads, these omitted factors can materially affect execution time, memory consumption, or resource usage.","explanation":"Asymptotic classification is useful for growth behavior but does not establish equivalence of finite implementations.","anti_pattern":"Treating equal Big-O classifications as proof of equal practical performance.","testing_guidance":"Measure implementations under representative workloads when performance decisions matter.","performance_implication":"Finite-size behavior can differ substantially even when asymptotic growth matches.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B61-0002","D11-ALG-B69-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures; MIT Algorithm Engineering","publisher":"NIST; MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"asymptotic analysis and implementation engineering distinction"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B72-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Recursion and Memory","subtopic":"Call-stack behavior","concept":"Recursive call-stack growth","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What implementation resource grows with active recursive depth?","answer":"Each active recursive invocation requires call-state storage, so the amount of stack storage generally grows with the maximum number of simultaneously active calls.","explanation":"Recursive implementations preserve state for callers until nested calls return. Consequently, recursion depth contributes to auxiliary stack usage.","correct_example":"A recursive traversal whose maximum depth is h requires stack space proportional to h, subject to language and implementation details.","invalid_example":"Assuming recursion uses constant auxiliary memory merely because the source code contains a constant number of local variables.","edge_case":"A tail-recursive source form does not guarantee constant stack usage in every language or runtime.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B65-0001","D11-ALG-B72-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT 6.031 Recursion","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search1","evidence_basis":"recursive calls consume stack space"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B72-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Recursion and Memory","subtopic":"Depth safety","concept":"Recursion-depth constraint","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should recursive depth be treated as an explicit implementation constraint?","answer":"A mathematically terminating recursive algorithm can still fail in a concrete runtime if its active call depth exceeds available stack capacity or interpreter limits.","explanation":"Termination and resource feasibility are different properties. The former concerns eventual completion; the latter concerns whether the implementation can complete within available runtime resources.","failure_mode":"Stack exhaustion or runtime recursion-depth failure","cause":"Maximum recursive depth exceeds implementation limits.","solution":"Bound the depth, redesign iteratively, use an explicit work stack, or use a runtime-supported mechanism appropriate to the language.","verification_guidance":"Test near the maximum expected depth and include adversarial deep inputs.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B65-0001","D11-ALG-B72-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Python Documentation, sys.setrecursionlimit; MIT 6.031 Recursion","publisher":"Python Software Foundation; MIT","source_ref":"turn0search12","evidence_basis":"documented recursion-depth safeguards and stack limitations"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","version":"Python 3.14 documentation","version_range":"Python 3.x documentation context","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B72-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Recursion and Memory","subtopic":"Recursive state","concept":"Per-call state preservation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why does each recursive invocation need independent state?","answer":"A caller must retain the information needed to resume after its child invocation returns. Overwriting caller-specific state without preservation can corrupt the computation.","explanation":"Recursive calls create nested activation contexts. The implementation must preserve values such as arguments, local state, and return information according to the language's calling convention.","invalid_example":"Using one shared mutable storage location for all recursive levels when each level requires its own value.","failure_mode":"Recursive state corruption","cause":"Nested invocations overwrite state required by outer invocations.","solution":"Use independent local state, explicit frames, or carefully designed immutable/shared state with defined ownership.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B72-0001","D11-ALG-B77-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT Computation Structures","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search6","evidence_basis":"recursive calls require preserved arguments and return state"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B72-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Recursion and Memory","subtopic":"Explicit stack transformation","concept":"Recursion-to-explicit-stack transformation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a recursive algorithm be transformed into an iterative implementation?","answer":"Represent each pending recursive invocation as an explicit stack frame containing the state needed to resume that invocation, then process frames iteratively.","explanation":"This transformation makes call-stack state explicit and can remove dependence on runtime recursion depth while preserving the algorithm's control semantics if the frame state is complete.","correct_example":"Replacing recursive depth-first traversal with an explicit stack containing vertices and required traversal state.","invalid_example":"Replacing recursion with a simple loop while omitting pending child or continuation state.","failure_mode":"Semantic loss during transformation","cause":"The explicit stack does not encode all state required by suspended recursive calls.","solution":"Model each recursive continuation explicitly and verify ordering and state transitions.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B73-0001","D11-ALG-B72-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT 6.031 Recursion","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search1","evidence_basis":"recursion and iterative alternative reasoning"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B73-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Implementation Strategy","subtopic":"Iteration versus recursion","concept":"Recursion-iteration tradeoff","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What factors should determine whether recursion or iteration is used?","answer":"Consider problem structure, readability, proof simplicity, maximum depth, stack/resource limits, state management, reentrancy, and language/runtime behavior.","explanation":"Recursion can naturally represent recursive problems and recursive data, while iteration can provide explicit control over state and resource usage. Neither form is universally superior.","alternative":"Use recursion for naturally recursive structures when depth and runtime constraints are acceptable; use iteration or explicit stacks when depth control is important.","tradeoff":"Recursion may simplify structural reasoning but can consume call-stack resources; iteration can make resource behavior explicit but may require more state-management code.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B72-0002","D11-ALG-B64-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT 6.031 Recursion","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search1","evidence_basis":"documented recursion-versus-iteration tradeoffs"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B73-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Implementation Strategy","subtopic":"Tail-position reasoning","concept":"Tail recursion limitation","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is replacing every loop with recursion not automatically a valid optimization?","answer":"Recursion can add stack usage and runtime call overhead, and the target language may not eliminate tail calls. A recursive formulation should therefore be justified by semantics or implementation needs rather than stylistic preference alone.","invalid_example":"Converting a potentially unbounded input loop into recursive calls without establishing a safe depth bound or runtime support.","failure_mode":"Resource exhaustion","cause":"Recursive depth grows with input while stack capacity remains bounded.","solution":"Prefer iteration or an explicit work stack when depth can become large and recursion provides no essential structural advantage.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B72-0002","D11-ALG-B73-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT 6.031 Recursion","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search1","evidence_basis":"stack limitations of recursion"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B73-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Implementation Strategy","subtopic":"Reentrancy","concept":"Reentrant algorithm implementation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What implementation property helps recursive or concurrently reused algorithms remain safe?","answer":"A reentrant implementation avoids hidden per-invocation mutable global or static state and keeps invocation-specific state in parameters, local state, or explicitly managed contexts.","explanation":"Reentrancy allows a computation to be entered again without corrupting the state of an existing invocation. This is especially relevant to recursion, callbacks, and concurrent execution.","invalid_example":"A recursive routine storing its current traversal index in a mutable global variable shared by all invocations.","failure_mode":"Cross-invocation state interference","cause":"Invocation-specific state is shared unexpectedly.","solution":"Move invocation state into local or explicitly scoped structures and define ownership of shared state.","security_implication":"Unexpected shared state can create integrity and isolation defects in security-sensitive algorithms.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B72-0003","D11-ALG-B78-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT 6.031 Recursion","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search1","evidence_basis":"reentrant-code guidance"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B73-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Implementation Strategy","subtopic":"Recursive helper design","concept":"Helper-state encapsulation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is a helper function useful in a recursive algorithm?","answer":"A helper is useful when recursive processing requires additional state that should remain internal to the public operation, such as bounds, accumulators, parent context, or traversal state.","explanation":"Separating public input contracts from internal recursive state can make the external API simpler while allowing the recursive implementation to carry the state it needs.","correct_example":"A public search function accepting a collection and target while an internal helper receives current bounds or a node context.","anti_pattern":"Exposing internal recursion state as public API parameters without a caller-facing need.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B72-0003","D11-ALG-B76-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT 6.031 Recursion","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search1","evidence_basis":"helper methods in recursive implementations"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B74-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Numeric Representation","subtopic":"Integer range","concept":"Integer overflow risk","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can integer overflow invalidate an otherwise correct algorithm?","answer":"If the implementation uses a fixed-width integer representation and an intermediate or final value exceeds its representable range, the computed value may no longer represent the mathematical result according to that language's integer semantics.","explanation":"Algorithm proofs often reason over mathematical integers, while implementations may use bounded machine representations. The representation model must therefore be part of correctness reasoning when arithmetic can exceed bounds.","invalid_example":"Computing a midpoint or product with bounded integers without considering whether intermediate arithmetic can exceed the representable range.","failure_mode":"Incorrect arithmetic result","cause":"Intermediate value exceeds the implementation's numeric range.","solution":"Use a safe formulation, wider representation where available, checked arithmetic, or explicit range validation.","constraint":"Overflow semantics differ by language and numeric type.","security_implication":"Integer overflow can contribute to incorrect bounds, allocation sizes, indexing, or validation logic.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B61-0004","D11-ALG-B74-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived implementation-safety principle","evidence_basis":"difference between mathematical and concrete numeric models"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B74-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Numeric Representation","subtopic":"Precision","concept":"Floating-point comparison","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should algorithms avoid assuming exact equality for arbitrary floating-point computations?","answer":"Floating-point representations generally approximate real-number arithmetic, so mathematically equal computations can produce different representable results because of rounding and operation ordering.","explanation":"A numerical algorithm must define an appropriate error model and comparison policy rather than assuming real-number exactness.","correct_example":"Using a documented tolerance or domain-specific numerical criterion when approximate equality is mathematically justified.","invalid_example":"Using exact equality as a universal test for independently computed floating-point values expected to be mathematically identical.","failure_mode":"False inequality or equality decision","cause":"The implementation treats approximate numeric representation as exact real arithmetic.","solution":"Define numerical tolerances or use exact representations when exactness is required and feasible.","constraint":"Tolerance selection must reflect the numerical problem and error scale; a universal epsilon is not generally valid.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B74-0001","D11-ALG-B70-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived numerical-algorithm engineering principle","evidence_basis":"finite-precision representation versus mathematical real numbers"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B74-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Numeric Representation","subtopic":"Division and midpoint","concept":"Safe arithmetic formulation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the purpose of algebraically equivalent arithmetic formulations in algorithm implementation?","answer":"A mathematically equivalent expression can have different intermediate ranges or rounding behavior in a concrete representation. Reformulating an expression can reduce avoidable overflow or numerical error.","explanation":"Implementation correctness depends on the operations actually performed, not only on symbolic equivalence.","correct_example":"Computing a midpoint using a formulation that avoids adding two potentially large bounded integers before division, when the language's numeric model makes that relevant.","invalid_example":"Assuming algebraic equivalence guarantees identical machine-level behavior.","tradeoff":"A safer formulation may require explicit conversions or additional operations.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B74-0001","D11-ALG-B74-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived implementation-safety principle","evidence_basis":"representation-aware arithmetic"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B74-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Numeric Representation","subtopic":"Sentinel values","concept":"Sentinel collision","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What can go wrong when a valid domain value is also used as an algorithmic sentinel?","answer":"A legitimate result can become indistinguishable from a failure, missing-value, or uninitialized-state marker.","explanation":"Sentinels compress multiple semantic states into one representation and are safe only when the sentinel cannot occur as a valid value or when an independent validity indicator exists.","invalid_example":"Using -1 to indicate search failure when -1 is a valid element in the problem domain.","failure_mode":"Ambiguous result state","cause":"Sentinel overlaps the valid value domain.","solution":"Use a disjoint representation, explicit status, tagged result, or domain guarantee excluding the sentinel.","security_implication":"Ambiguous validation states can produce authorization or bounds-checking defects.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B76-0001","D11-ALG-B77-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm implementation principle","evidence_basis":"state-representation correctness"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B75-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Indexing and Mutation","subtopic":"Bounds","concept":"Index-boundary discipline","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an implementation establish before indexing a sequence?","answer":"The implementation should establish that the index is valid under the sequence's indexing convention and that any arithmetic used to derive the index cannot produce an invalid value.","explanation":"Off-by-one defects frequently arise from mixing inclusive and exclusive bounds or from updating indices before versus after access.","correct_example":"Maintaining an invariant that a loop index remains within the documented valid range before each indexed access.","invalid_example":"Accessing an element and only afterward checking whether the index was valid.","failure_mode":"Out-of-bounds access or incorrect element selection","cause":"Index invariant is missing or incorrect.","solution":"Define bounds explicitly and preserve them through each state transition.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, singleton, first-index, last-index, and just-outside-range cases.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B64-0001","D11-ALG-B81-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived implementation correctness principle","evidence_basis":"loop invariants and boundary reasoning"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B75-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Indexing and Mutation","subtopic":"Off-by-one errors","concept":"Inclusive-exclusive interval convention","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are half-open intervals useful in algorithm implementations?","answer":"Representing a range as [start, end) makes its length equal to end minus start, composes naturally with adjacent ranges, and provides a clear empty-range representation when start equals end.","explanation":"Consistent interval conventions reduce ambiguity in slicing, partitioning, binary search, and divide-and-conquer implementations.","correct_example":"Representing a subarray as indices start through end-exclusive and recursively partitioning with non-overlapping half-open ranges.","invalid_example":"Mixing inclusive upper bounds in one helper with exclusive upper bounds in another without an explicit conversion rule.","failure_mode":"Boundary mismatch","cause":"Different components interpret the same range differently.","solution":"Choose one interval convention and document it at the API boundary.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B75-0001","D11-ALG-B64-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm implementation pattern","evidence_basis":"boundary invariant reasoning"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B75-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Indexing and Mutation","subtopic":"Aliasing","concept":"Mutable aliasing","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can aliases to the same mutable data structure break an algorithm?","answer":"Two references that appear independent can actually designate the same mutable object, so an update through one reference changes the state observed through the other.","explanation":"Algorithm reasoning may assume ownership or independence that the implementation does not provide.","invalid_example":"Modifying an input buffer in place while another supposedly independent structure still references the same storage.","failure_mode":"Unexpected state mutation","cause":"Shared mutable storage was not recognized or documented.","solution":"Define ownership and aliasing rules, copy when isolation is required, or explicitly design for shared mutation.","security_implication":"Unexpected aliases can violate isolation or integrity assumptions.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B77-0002","D11-ALG-B76-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived implementation correctness principle","evidence_basis":"state and ownership reasoning"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B75-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Indexing and Mutation","subtopic":"Mutation during traversal","concept":"Structural mutation during iteration","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can modifying a collection while traversing it invalidate an algorithm?","answer":"Structural mutation can change indices, iterators, links, or traversal state that the algorithm relies on, causing skipped elements, repeated elements, invalid references, or undefined behavior depending on the language and collection.","explanation":"Traversal correctness depends on a stable or explicitly managed relationship between the iterator state and collection structure.","solution":"Use a supported mutation protocol, collect changes for later application, or design the traversal to account explicitly for structural changes.","edge_case":"Some data structures and APIs intentionally support restricted forms of mutation during iteration; the exact contract is language/library dependent.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B75-0003","D11-ALG-B77-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm implementation principle","evidence_basis":"iterator and mutation contract reasoning"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B76-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Algorithm Contracts","subtopic":"Preconditions","concept":"Algorithm precondition contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an algorithm implementation do with assumptions that are required for correctness?","answer":"Required assumptions should be explicit as preconditions, validated when appropriate, or guaranteed by the surrounding system contract.","explanation":"An algorithm can only be proven correct over the domain for which its assumptions hold.","correct_example":"A binary search routine explicitly requiring a sorted sequence and documenting the ordering relation.","invalid_example":"Silently applying binary search to arbitrary unsorted input.","failure_mode":"Invalid-domain execution","cause":"Required precondition was omitted or violated.","solution":"Specify, enforce, or establish the precondition before algorithm execution.","verification_guidance":"Include tests for valid inputs and deliberate precondition violations.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B64-0001","D11-ALG-B75-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm specification principle","evidence_basis":"correctness depends on stated input assumptions"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B76-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Algorithm Contracts","subtopic":"Postconditions","concept":"Algorithm postcondition contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an implementation establish after successful completion?","answer":"It should satisfy the documented output and state postconditions, including ordering, membership, conservation, mutation, and error-state semantics that form part of the operation's contract.","explanation":"Returning a value without satisfying the semantic contract is not sufficient for algorithmic correctness.","correct_example":"A sorting routine returning a sequence that is ordered according to the specified comparator and contains exactly the required elements.","invalid_example":"Returning a sorted prefix while silently losing or duplicating elements.","verification_guidance":"Check output semantics independently from the implementation path when possible.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B76-0001","D11-ALG-B64-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm specification principle","evidence_basis":"postconditions and correctness proofs"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B76-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Algorithm Contracts","subtopic":"Error semantics","concept":"Explicit failure contract","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should algorithm failure behavior be part of the contract?","answer":"Callers need to distinguish valid results from invalid input, unavailable resources, cancellation, or algorithmic failure. Ambiguous failure behavior can make a correct internal algorithm unusable or unsafe.","explanation":"Failure is part of observable behavior and must be represented consistently.","correct_example":"An API explicitly documenting whether a missing target returns a status, option-like result, sentinel, or exception.","invalid_example":"Returning an ordinary domain value that can also mean failure without documentation.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B74-0004","D11-ALG-B76-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm API-contract principle","evidence_basis":"observable failure semantics"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B76-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Algorithm Contracts","subtopic":"Defensive implementation","concept":"Validate at the correct boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Where should algorithm preconditions be validated?","answer":"Validate assumptions at the boundary where responsibility for those assumptions is established, avoiding both unsafe silent acceptance and unnecessary repeated validation deep inside trusted internal layers.","explanation":"Boundary validation should be consistent with system architecture and ownership. Internal functions can rely on established invariants when their contracts explicitly state those assumptions.","tradeoff":"Repeated validation may improve isolation but can add cost; omitted validation can be unsafe when callers are not trusted to establish the contract.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive algorithms should not rely on undocumented or unenforced input assumptions.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B76-0001","D11-ALG-B77-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived defensive algorithm engineering principle","evidence_basis":"contract ownership and invariant establishment"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B77-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"State Management","subtopic":"Invariants","concept":"Executable state invariant","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should algorithm invariants influence implementation structure?","answer":"Represent state so that important invariants are easy to establish, preserve, and inspect at defined program points.","explanation":"A loop or recursive algorithm is easier to verify when its data structures and transitions correspond directly to the invariant used in the correctness argument.","correct_example":"Maintaining a queue invariant that every stored element represents exactly one pending unit of work.","invalid_example":"Allowing multiple undocumented representations of pending work and relying on callers to infer which one is active.","verification_guidance":"Assert internal invariants during development or testing when practical.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B64-0001","D11-ALG-B70-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT 6.006 Recursive Algorithms; MIT 6.031","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search38","evidence_basis":"invariants and recursive/algorithmic correctness reasoning"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B77-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"State Management","subtopic":"Ownership","concept":"State ownership discipline","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should mutable state have explicit ownership?","answer":"Explicit ownership identifies which component may modify state and under what conditions, reducing accidental interference between algorithmic steps and callers.","explanation":"Many implementation failures arise not from the algorithmic idea but from unclear mutation responsibility.","correct_example":"Documenting that an in-place partition operation owns and may mutate a supplied buffer.","invalid_example":"Mutating caller-owned data without documenting the mutation.","failure_mode":"Unexpected side effect","cause":"Ownership and mutation semantics were implicit.","solution":"Define ownership, mutation, borrowing, copying, or immutability semantics at the API boundary.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B75-0003","D11-ALG-B76-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived state-management principle","evidence_basis":"mutation and contract reasoning"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B77-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"State Management","subtopic":"Pure versus stateful algorithms","concept":"Purity as reasoning aid","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can minimizing side effects simplify algorithm reasoning?","answer":"A function with behavior determined by explicit inputs and return values is easier to reason about because hidden state transitions and external mutations do not need to be tracked across calls.","explanation":"Purity is not mandatory for efficient algorithms, but reducing unnecessary side effects can simplify correctness, testing, reuse, and concurrency reasoning.","tradeoff":"Pure designs may require additional data movement or allocation unless optimized by the language/runtime or implementation strategy.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B73-0003","D11-ALG-B77-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT 6.031 Recursion","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search1","evidence_basis":"discussion of immutable and pure recursive implementations"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B77-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"State Management","subtopic":"State-machine implementation","concept":"Explicit algorithm state machine","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is an explicit state machine useful in algorithm implementation?","answer":"It is useful when an algorithm has multiple phases, resumable work, asynchronous boundaries, complex control flow, or failure states that must be represented explicitly.","explanation":"Explicit state makes transitions, legal states, and recovery behavior visible rather than embedding them implicitly in nested control flow.","correct_example":"Representing a multi-stage graph-processing operation with explicit states for discovery, processing, completion, and failure.","invalid_example":"Using unrelated Boolean flags whose combinations implicitly encode undocumented states.","failure_mode":"Invalid state combination","cause":"State space is implicit and insufficiently constrained.","solution":"Model legal states and transitions explicitly and verify transition invariants.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B77-0001","D11-ALG-B79-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm-state engineering principle","evidence_basis":"explicit state transition reasoning"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B78-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Concurrency and Parallelism","subtopic":"Shared state","concept":"Concurrent algorithm state hazard","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can an algorithm that is correct sequentially fail under concurrent execution?","answer":"Concurrent interleavings can violate assumptions about atomicity, ordering, visibility, or exclusive ownership of mutable state.","explanation":"Sequential correctness does not automatically establish correctness under concurrent interleavings. The algorithm must specify synchronization or prove that shared state is safely accessed.","invalid_example":"Updating a shared counter through a read-modify-write sequence while assuming no other execution can interleave between the operations.","failure_mode":"Race condition","cause":"Shared state access lacks an adequate synchronization or atomicity guarantee.","solution":"Use appropriate synchronization, atomic operations, partitioned ownership, immutability, or an algorithm designed for the target concurrency model.","security_implication":"Races can produce integrity violations or authorization-state inconsistencies.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B73-0003","D11-ALG-B78-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT Multicore Programming course scope","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search4","evidence_basis":"concurrency and synchronization as algorithm-engineering concerns"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B78-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Concurrency and Parallelism","subtopic":"Atomicity","concept":"Compound-operation atomicity","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must multi-step state transitions be considered as units when required by an algorithm invariant?","answer":"If another execution can observe or modify state between steps that logically form one transition, the invariant may temporarily or permanently fail.","explanation":"An algorithm's logical atomic operation may require synchronization even when each individual machine or language operation is itself atomic.","correct_example":"Protecting a check-then-update transition when correctness requires the condition and update to observe a consistent state.","invalid_example":"Synchronizing only the final write while allowing another thread to change the checked state between the check and write.","failure_mode":"Check-then-act race","cause":"Logical atomicity was assumed from individual operation atomicity.","solution":"Synchronize the complete logical transition or use a primitive that provides the required atomic semantics.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B78-0001","D11-ALG-B77-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT Multicore Programming course scope","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search4","evidence_basis":"synchronization and concurrent data structures"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B78-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Concurrency and Parallelism","subtopic":"Determinism","concept":"Concurrent result determinism","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should deterministic output requirements be identified before parallelizing an algorithm?","answer":"Parallel execution can change operation order, exposing algorithms whose results depend on sequencing, floating-point accumulation order, tie-breaking, or shared-state timing.","explanation":"Parallelization preserves semantics only when the algorithm's operations and result specification tolerate the changed execution order or the implementation restores the required ordering semantics.","failure_mode":"Nondeterministic output","cause":"Result semantics depended on an unspecified execution order.","solution":"Define deterministic semantics, impose ordering where required, or explicitly document nondeterminism.","performance_implication":"Enforcing strict ordering can reduce parallelism.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B74-0002","D11-ALG-B78-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT Algorithm Engineering","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"parallel algorithm engineering and implementation"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B78-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Concurrency and Parallelism","subtopic":"Work partitioning","concept":"Parallel work ownership","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What must be defined when an algorithm partitions work across concurrent workers?","answer":"The implementation should define ownership of each work item, coordination rules for shared structures, completion conditions, failure propagation, and whether duplicate processing is permitted.","explanation":"Parallel decomposition changes the state model from a single execution path to multiple interacting execution contexts.","correct_example":"Partitioning disjoint ranges so each worker owns its range while a defined merge step combines results.","invalid_example":"Allowing multiple workers to mutate the same partition without a defined synchronization or ownership protocol.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B78-0001","D11-ALG-B78-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT Algorithm Engineering","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"parallel algorithm design and implementation"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B79-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Implementation Debugging","subtopic":"Failure localization","concept":"Invariant-first debugging","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an algorithm implementation be debugged when output is incorrect?","answer":"Identify the earliest point where an established invariant, precondition, or state transition becomes false rather than only inspecting the final incorrect output.","explanation":"The first invalid state often provides stronger causal information than the final symptom.","correct_example":"Instrumenting a sorting algorithm to check its partition invariant after each partition step and locating the first violated step.","invalid_example":"Adding arbitrary output transformations until the final expected result appears without identifying the underlying state defect.","solution":"Trace state transitions, establish the first divergence from the specification, then repair the responsible transition.","testing_guidance":"Use minimal failing inputs that isolate the first incorrect transition.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B77-0001","D11-ALG-B70-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived debugging methodology","evidence_basis":"invariant-based correctness reasoning"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B79-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Implementation Debugging","subtopic":"State-transition defects","concept":"Wrong-transition debugging","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a common root cause when an algorithm reaches a valid-looking but incorrect final state?","answer":"A state transition may have applied the wrong update, skipped a required transition, performed transitions in the wrong order, or accepted an invalid intermediate state.","explanation":"Complex algorithms often fail through a small local transition defect that only becomes visible in the final output.","failure_mode":"State-transition defect","cause":"Implementation transition differs from the specified algorithm transition.","solution":"Compare each transition against the formal or documented state relation and identify the earliest mismatch.","verification_guidance":"Test transition-level invariants and intermediate states where practical.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B77-0004","D11-ALG-B79-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm debugging principle","evidence_basis":"state-machine and invariant reasoning"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B79-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Implementation Debugging","subtopic":"Optimization defects","concept":"Optimization-induced semantic regression","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can an implementation optimization introduce an algorithmic bug?","answer":"An optimization can change evaluation order, mutation timing, numeric behavior, memory ownership, tie-breaking, or control flow in ways that violate the original specification.","explanation":"Performance optimization is valid only when the optimized implementation preserves required semantics under the target model.","invalid_example":"Removing a defensive copy because it appears unnecessary without proving that no alias can observe the mutation.","failure_mode":"Semantic regression","cause":"Optimization changed an observable behavior.","solution":"Treat optimization as a semantics-preserving transformation and verify the relevant invariants and contracts.","testing_guidance":"Run regression tests plus targeted tests for the changed invariant or ownership assumption.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B75-0003","D11-ALG-B76-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT Algorithm Engineering","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"engineering implementations while preserving algorithmic behavior"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B79-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Implementation Debugging","subtopic":"Premature optimization","concept":"Evidence-based optimization","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should algorithm implementations avoid optimization without evidence of a relevant bottleneck?","answer":"An optimization that targets a non-dominant cost can increase complexity or reduce correctness confidence without materially improving the actual workload.","explanation":"Algorithm engineering connects performance decisions to workload, cost model, and measurements or justified theoretical analysis.","invalid_example":"Replacing a clear implementation with a substantially more complex version solely because the new version appears theoretically faster without considering workload or measured bottlenecks.","tradeoff":"Optimization can improve resource use but may increase implementation complexity and verification burden.","solution":"Identify the dominant cost, establish the target metric, optimize the relevant component, and re-verify semantics.","performance_implication":"Performance claims must distinguish theoretical reasoning from actual measurement.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0002","D11-ALG-B71-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT Algorithm Engineering","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"performance engineering"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B80-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Algorithm Engineering Workflow","subtopic":"Implementation decision process","concept":"Algorithm-to-code engineering workflow","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What workflow should guide implementation of an algorithm?","answer":"Define the problem contract; identify assumptions; select the algorithm; choose data representations; define state and invariants; implement; analyze resource behavior; test normal and invalid cases; verify semantics; then optimize only where justified.","explanation":"A disciplined workflow keeps specification, algorithm choice, implementation, testing, and optimization connected rather than treating coding as an isolated step.","correct_example":"Documenting preconditions and postconditions before implementing a graph algorithm and then validating the chosen representation against expected operations.","invalid_example":"Writing code first and deciding the algorithm's actual contract only after failures appear.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B76-0001","D11-ALG-B79-0004","D11-ALG-B70-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT Algorithm Engineering; MIT 6.031","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"theory-to-practice algorithm engineering and software correctness"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B80-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Algorithm Engineering Workflow","subtopic":"Cross-language implementation","concept":"Language-semantic portability","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must be rechecked when porting an algorithm between programming languages?","answer":"Recheck numeric semantics, integer bounds, indexing conventions, mutation and aliasing, evaluation order, recursion behavior, exception/error semantics, collection guarantees, concurrency primitives, and library contracts.","explanation":"The abstract algorithm may remain unchanged while language and runtime semantics alter implementation correctness or resource behavior.","invalid_example":"Porting code line-for-line while assuming identical integer, indexing, recursion, or mutation semantics.","solution":"Preserve the abstract contract but redesign implementation details according to the target language's documented semantics.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B72-0002","D11-ALG-B74-0001","D11-ALG-B75-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived cross-language algorithm engineering principle","evidence_basis":"algorithm/implementation distinction and runtime-specific behavior"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"MULTI_LANGUAGE","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B80-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Algorithm Engineering Workflow","subtopic":"Verification matrix","concept":"Implementation verification matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What dimensions should be verified before considering an algorithm implementation trustworthy?","answer":"Verify semantic correctness, preconditions, postconditions, boundary behavior, resource constraints, failure behavior, representation assumptions, concurrency assumptions where applicable, and compatibility with the claimed version/environment.","explanation":"A correct result on ordinary inputs is insufficient evidence for all implementation claims.","verification_guidance":"Map each requirement to evidence such as proof, invariant reasoning, targeted tests, execution results, or authoritative specification references. Mark unsupported claims as unverified rather than inferred as verified.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B64-0001","D11-ALG-B69-0003","D11-ALG-B76-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived verification framework","evidence_basis":"algorithm correctness, complexity, contracts, and implementation engineering"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_VERIFICATION_POLICY","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B80-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Algorithm Engineering Workflow","subtopic":"Engineering tradeoffs","concept":"Implementation tradeoff record","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should an algorithm implementation tradeoff be recorded for machine reasoning?","answer":"Record the alternatives, workload assumptions, semantic requirements, theoretical costs, implementation costs, resource constraints, risks, evidence, and conditions under which each alternative is appropriate.","explanation":"A machine reasoning system should not infer a universal winner from an implementation comparison because suitability depends on context.","correct_example":"Recording recursive and iterative traversal as alternatives with depth, readability, stack, state-management, and runtime assumptions explicitly represented.","invalid_example":"Storing only a label such as 'iterative is better' without context or evidence.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B73-0001","D11-ALG-B71-0001","D11-ALG-B79-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT Algorithm Engineering","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"engineering tradeoffs between algorithmic implementations"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B80-0005","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.17","topic":"Algorithm Engineering Workflow","subtopic":"Final integration","concept":"Algorithm implementation readiness gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What conditions should be satisfied before an algorithm implementation is considered ready for downstream use?","answer":"The implementation should have an explicit contract, known assumptions, preserved invariants, identified resource constraints, defined failure semantics, appropriate tests, version-aware dependencies, and clearly separated verified evidence from unverified claims.","explanation":"Readiness is broader than source-code generation. The implementation must be explainable, testable, and auditable against its intended semantics.","constraint":"Actual execution, benchmark, or deployment claims require corresponding evidence and must not be inferred from source inspection alone.","verification_guidance":"Require structural review plus execution-based evidence where execution is available; otherwise mark execution-dependent claims NOT_EXECUTED or NOT_VERIFIED.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B80-0001","D11-ALG-B80-0003","D11-ALG-B79-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 dataset verification policy; MIT Algorithm Engineering","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"algorithm engineering plus explicit evidence discipline"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_VERIFICATION_POLICY","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}

