{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B81-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Test strategy","concept":"Algorithm test specification","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should be defined before testing an algorithm implementation?","answer":"Define the input domain, output contract, invalid-input behavior, important invariants, boundary conditions, resource constraints, and evidence required to establish correctness.","explanation":"Testing is meaningful only relative to an explicit expected behavior. A test suite should therefore derive from the algorithm contract rather than from arbitrary examples.","correct_example":"For a sorting algorithm, specify ordering semantics, element preservation, duplicate handling, empty-input behavior, comparator assumptions, and mutation behavior before constructing tests.","invalid_example":"Testing only several ordinary arrays and treating successful outputs as proof of general correctness.","testing_guidance":"Derive tests from the specification, invariants, boundaries, failure modes, and representative workloads.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B76-0001","D11-ALG-B76-0002","D11-ALG-B80-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST IR 8397","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"software verification should use multiple complementary testing techniques"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B81-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Oracle design","concept":"Test oracle","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a test oracle in algorithm verification?","answer":"A test oracle is a mechanism or criterion used to determine whether an observed algorithm result satisfies the expected behavior.","explanation":"The oracle may be an independently implemented reference algorithm, a mathematical property, an invariant, a known result, or another trusted specification-derived criterion.","correct_example":"Checking that a sorting implementation returns an ordered sequence containing exactly the original multiset of elements.","invalid_example":"Using the same potentially defective implementation as both system under test and oracle.","failure_mode":"Oracle contamination","cause":"The oracle shares the defect or assumption being tested.","solution":"Use an independent specification, reference implementation, invariant, metamorphic relation, or mathematically derived property.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B80-0003","D11-ALG-B81-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST algorithm testing literature; NIST IR 8397","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search24","evidence_basis":"comparison against reference algorithms and expected objectives"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B81-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Reference implementations","concept":"Independent reference implementation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is an independent reference implementation useful?","answer":"It is useful when the expected output is complex enough that direct expected-value construction is difficult and an independently designed implementation can provide a comparison oracle.","explanation":"The reference must be sufficiently independent to reduce the chance that both implementations reproduce the same defect.","correct_example":"Comparing an optimized sorting implementation against a simple independently implemented sorting algorithm on generated inputs.","invalid_example":"Copying the same implementation logic into a second function and calling it the reference.","failure_mode":"Common-mode oracle failure","cause":"The reference shares the same algorithmic defect.","solution":"Use different implementation logic or a mathematically independent oracle.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B81-0002","D11-ALG-B81-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Algorithm Testing and Evaluation Program","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search24","evidence_basis":"test software compared with reference algorithms"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B81-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Black-box testing","concept":"Black-box algorithm testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What does black-box testing evaluate?","answer":"Black-box testing evaluates observable behavior using inputs and expected outputs or externally observable properties without depending on internal implementation structure.","explanation":"It tests the contract from the outside and can detect errors regardless of how the implementation is internally organized.","correct_example":"Providing arrays to a sorting API and checking ordering and element preservation without inspecting its internal loops.","invalid_example":"A test that passes only because it relies on a private variable or specific internal loop structure.","testing_guidance":"Use representative, boundary, invalid, adversarial, and regression inputs derived from the external contract.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B81-0001","D11-ALG-B82-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST IR 8397","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search26","evidence_basis":"black-box test cases are among recommended verification techniques"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B82-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Boundary testing","concept":"Boundary-value testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why are boundary values disproportionately important in algorithm testing?","answer":"Many algorithm defects occur when control flow changes at domain boundaries, such as empty versus non-empty input, first versus last index, zero versus one, or minimum versus maximum representable values.","explanation":"Boundary cases directly exercise branch transitions and precondition limits.","correct_example":"Testing binary search on an empty sequence, singleton sequence, first element, last element, and absent values immediately before or after existing values.","invalid_example":"Testing only large random inputs while never testing empty or singleton inputs.","testing_guidance":"Include lower boundary, upper boundary, immediately-inside, immediately-outside, and transition cases.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B75-0001","D11-ALG-B75-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST software verification guidance","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"black-box, structural, and historical testing recommendations"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B82-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Degenerate inputs","concept":"Degenerate-case testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Which degenerate inputs should algorithm testing consider?","answer":"Common candidates include empty inputs, singleton inputs, all-equal values, already ordered values, reverse-ordered values, duplicate-heavy inputs, disconnected structures, zero-weight or zero-length cases where valid, and minimal legal structures.","explanation":"Degenerate inputs often collapse assumptions about iteration count, partitioning, connectivity, uniqueness, or progress.","testing_guidance":"Identify cases where one or more algorithmic dimensions collapse to their minimum meaningful size.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B82-0001","D11-ALG-B82-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm testing methodology","evidence_basis":"boundary and specification-based testing"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B82-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Adversarial inputs","concept":"Adversarial test construction","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What makes an input adversarial for an algorithm?","answer":"An adversarial input deliberately stresses a known assumption, worst-case structure, branch condition, resource limit, or implementation weakness.","explanation":"Adversarial testing is targeted rather than merely random. It attempts to expose specific failure modes predicted by analysis or implementation inspection.","correct_example":"Constructing an input that causes a pivot-selection strategy to repeatedly create highly unbalanced partitions.","invalid_example":"Calling a randomly generated input adversarial without identifying the property it is intended to stress.","testing_guidance":"Link each adversarial input to a concrete failure hypothesis.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0001","D11-ALG-B79-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST IR 8397; algorithm testing principles","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search26","evidence_basis":"historical, structural, black-box, and fuzz testing"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B82-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Metamorphic testing","concept":"Metamorphic relation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a metamorphic relation in algorithm testing?","answer":"A metamorphic relation specifies how outputs should change or remain invariant when a valid transformation is applied to an input.","explanation":"Metamorphic testing is useful when constructing exact expected outputs for every input is difficult.","correct_example":"For a sorting algorithm, permuting the input should not change the multiset of output elements, and the output should remain sorted.","invalid_example":"Changing an input and asserting an arbitrary output relationship without deriving the relationship from the algorithm specification.","verification_guidance":"Derive each metamorphic relation from mathematical or semantic properties of the problem.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B81-0002","D11-ALG-B83-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived verification methodology","evidence_basis":"property-based and specification-derived testing"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B83-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Property-Based Testing","subtopic":"Properties","concept":"Property-based algorithm testing","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should property-based testing be applied to algorithms?","answer":"Define general properties that must hold across broad input classes, generate or select diverse valid inputs, execute the implementation, and report any input that violates a property.","explanation":"Property-based testing complements example-based tests by exploring a wider input space while checking invariant-like semantic properties.","correct_example":"Testing that a sorting function always returns an ordered result containing the same multiset as its input across many generated arrays.","invalid_example":"Generating random inputs but checking only whether the function completes without failure.","testing_guidance":"Use domain-aware generators and shrinking/minimization mechanisms when available.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B82-0004","D11-ALG-B81-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived testing methodology","evidence_basis":"property-oriented validation"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B83-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Property-Based Testing","subtopic":"Invariant properties","concept":"Algorithmic property selection","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What makes a useful property for algorithm testing?","answer":"A useful property is necessary for correctness, broad enough to cover many inputs, precise enough to detect violations, and independent enough to avoid simply restating the implementation.","explanation":"A property should provide new evidence about behavior rather than duplicate implementation logic.","correct_example":"Checking conservation of elements in a sorting algorithm independently of the sorting procedure.","invalid_example":"Testing a sorting function by running the same sorting function again and comparing the two outputs.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B81-0002","D11-ALG-B83-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived property-based verification principle","evidence_basis":"independent semantic properties"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_VERIFICATION_POLICY","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B83-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Property-Based Testing","subtopic":"Input generation","concept":"Domain-aware input generation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should generated algorithm-test inputs be domain-aware?","answer":"Uniform random generation can overrepresent uninteresting cases and underrepresent structurally important cases. Domain-aware generators deliberately produce values and structures relevant to algorithm assumptions and failure modes.","explanation":"Generation should reflect the input grammar, constraints, distributions when relevant, and adversarial structures of the target problem.","correct_example":"Generating graphs with controlled numbers of vertices, disconnected components, cycles, sparse and dense edge sets, and valid weight constraints.","invalid_example":"Generating arbitrary bytes and treating every generated byte string as a valid graph instance.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B82-0003","D11-ALG-B84-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm testing principle","evidence_basis":"specification-based input generation"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B83-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Property-Based Testing","subtopic":"Failure minimization","concept":"Minimal counterexample","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should a failing generated input be minimized when possible?","answer":"A smaller counterexample often isolates the violated assumption or transition, making diagnosis and correction easier while preserving the failure.","explanation":"Minimization separates essential failure-causing structure from irrelevant input complexity.","correct_example":"Reducing a large failing graph to the smallest graph that still violates a shortest-path invariant.","invalid_example":"Keeping only the original huge failing input and attempting to infer the defect from thousands of unrelated elements.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B79-0001","D11-ALG-B83-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived debugging and property-testing principle","evidence_basis":"counterexample minimization"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B84-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Robustness","subtopic":"Fuzzing","concept":"Algorithm fuzz testing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What is fuzzing useful for in algorithm verification?","answer":"Fuzzing generates or mutates large numbers of inputs to expose crashes, invalid states, unexpected exceptions, invariant violations, resource failures, and other behaviors that may not appear in hand-written examples.","explanation":"Fuzzing is particularly useful when the input space is large or difficult to enumerate manually.","correct_example":"Fuzzing a parser-backed algorithm with structurally valid and malformed inputs while checking safety properties and failure contracts.","invalid_example":"Treating every fuzz-generated input as valid without distinguishing malformed input from valid-domain testing.","security_implication":"Fuzzing can reveal robustness and security defects at input boundaries.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B82-0003","D11-ALG-B83-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST IR 8397","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search26","evidence_basis":"fuzzing is a recommended software verification technique"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B84-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Robustness","subtopic":"Malformed inputs","concept":"Malformed-input classification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should malformed inputs be handled during algorithm testing?","answer":"Classify them according to the API contract as rejected, normalized, tolerated, or undefined rather than assuming every malformed input should produce an ordinary algorithmic result.","explanation":"A malformed input test is meaningful only when expected behavior is defined.","correct_example":"Testing that an API rejects a graph containing an invalid vertex identifier when its contract requires valid identifiers.","invalid_example":"Calling the result incorrect simply because malformed input does not produce a normal answer when the contract explicitly permits rejection.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B76-0003","D11-ALG-B84-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived defensive testing principle","evidence_basis":"precondition and failure contracts"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B84-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Robustness","subtopic":"Resource exhaustion","concept":"Resource-boundary testing","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why should algorithms be tested near relevant resource limits?","answer":"An algorithm can be semantically correct on ordinary inputs yet fail because of stack, memory, integer range, timeout, recursion-depth, or other resource constraints.","explanation":"Resource feasibility is a separate property from mathematical correctness.","testing_guidance":"Identify the resource bound, construct inputs approaching it, and distinguish graceful rejection from unintended failure.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B72-0002","D11-ALG-B74-0001","D11-ALG-B69-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST software verification guidance","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"verification should address resource and implementation risks"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B84-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Robustness","subtopic":"Security testing","concept":"Algorithm security properties","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Which security properties can be relevant to algorithm implementations?","answer":"Relevant properties can include input validation, bounds safety, resource exhaustion resistance, confidentiality of sensitive intermediate data, integrity of state, deterministic authorization decisions where required, and safe failure behavior.","explanation":"Security testing should be derived from the algorithm's role and threat model rather than attaching generic security claims to every algorithm.","correct_example":"Testing that an algorithm processing attacker-controlled sizes cannot allocate unbounded resources without an explicit limit.","invalid_example":"Claiming an algorithm is secure solely because its mathematical result is correct.","security_implication":"Algorithmic correctness and security assurance are distinct evidence categories.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B80-0003","D11-ALG-B84-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST IR 8397","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search26","evidence_basis":"threat modeling and security-oriented verification"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B85-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Performance Testing","subtopic":"Measurement discipline","concept":"Empirical performance measurement","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What is required before making an empirical performance claim about an algorithm implementation?","answer":"The implementation must actually be executed under a defined environment and workload, and the measurement procedure and observed results must be recorded.","explanation":"Theoretical complexity is not empirical evidence, and source inspection alone does not establish runtime or memory measurements.","invalid_example":"Claiming that an implementation is faster because it has a lower Big-O expression without executing or measuring it.","performance_implication":"Measured performance depends on workload, hardware, runtime, compiler, configuration, and measurement methodology.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0002","D11-ALG-B71-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST SP 500-93","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search25","evidence_basis":"distinction between a priori analysis and a posteriori testing"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B85-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Performance Testing","subtopic":"Workload design","concept":"Representative workload","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must performance tests use representative workloads?","answer":"An implementation can perform differently across input sizes, shapes, distributions, cache behavior, branch patterns, and operation mixes. A benchmark unrelated to actual workloads may not answer the intended engineering question.","explanation":"Performance evidence is conditional on the tested workload and environment.","correct_example":"Evaluating a graph algorithm using graph sizes and density ranges corresponding to the intended application domain.","invalid_example":"Benchmarking only one tiny synthetic input and generalizing the result to all workloads.","performance_implication":"Benchmark conclusions should state workload scope and environment.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0001","D11-ALG-B85-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST Algorithm Testing and Evaluation Program","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search24","evidence_basis":"test data should reflect expected range and variability"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B85-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Performance Testing","subtopic":"Complexity versus measurement","concept":"Theoretical-empirical separation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should theoretical complexity and empirical measurements be represented in the knowledge system?","answer":"Store them as separate evidence types and never use one as if it were the other. Complexity describes modeled growth; measurements describe observed behavior under specified conditions.","explanation":"The distinction prevents benchmark claims from being fabricated or theoretical claims from being mistaken for measurements.","correct_example":"Recording O(n log n) as a theoretical complexity fact and a separately dated benchmark result with hardware and workload metadata.","invalid_example":"Recording O(n log n) as proof that one implementation is faster than another in practice.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B61-0001","D11-ALG-B69-0002","D11-ALG-B85-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST SP 500-93","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search25","evidence_basis":"a priori analysis versus a posteriori testing"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B85-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Performance Testing","subtopic":"Regression performance","concept":"Performance regression test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What is a performance regression test?","answer":"A performance regression test checks whether a previously characterized implementation remains within defined performance expectations under a controlled or representative workload.","explanation":"Performance regressions should be evaluated against an explicit baseline and tolerance rather than an informal impression.","constraint":"Baseline measurements must be comparable across environments and methodology before differences are interpreted.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B85-0001","D11-ALG-B85-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived performance-testing principle","evidence_basis":"a posteriori performance testing"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B86-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Regression Testing","subtopic":"Regression suite","concept":"Algorithm regression test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should fixed failing cases become regression tests?","answer":"A previously observed failure represents a concrete defect condition. Retaining it as a regression test prevents later changes from silently reintroducing the same failure.","explanation":"Regression suites preserve institutional knowledge about discovered defects.","correct_example":"Adding the smallest known counterexample for an off-by-one defect to the permanent test suite.","invalid_example":"Fixing a bug and deleting the test because the current implementation passes it.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B83-0004","D11-ALG-B79-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST IR 8397","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"historical test cases and automated testing"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B86-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Regression Testing","subtopic":"Change impact","concept":"Algorithm change-impact analysis","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should be considered before changing an algorithm implementation?","answer":"Identify affected contracts, invariants, callers, data representations, complexity assumptions, numerical behavior, concurrency behavior, tests, and version-dependent dependencies.","explanation":"Algorithm changes can alter more than output values; they may change mutation, resource consumption, ordering, determinism, or failure semantics.","correct_example":"Before replacing a sorting implementation, checking whether callers rely on stability, in-place mutation, ordering of equal keys, or memory behavior.","invalid_example":"Replacing the implementation because its function signature is unchanged while ignoring semantic properties.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B76-0002","D11-ALG-B80-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm maintenance principle","evidence_basis":"contract and implementation semantics"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B86-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Regression Testing","subtopic":"Mutation testing","concept":"Mutation testing concept","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What does mutation testing attempt to reveal?","answer":"Mutation testing introduces controlled changes to an implementation and checks whether the existing test suite detects the altered behavior.","explanation":"If meaningful mutations survive, the test suite may lack sensitivity to important implementation changes.","correct_example":"Changing a comparison operator in a sorting partition and verifying that the test suite detects the semantic regression.","invalid_example":"Treating every surviving mutation as proof that the production implementation is incorrect.","limitation":"Mutation survival can result from equivalent mutations or tests that intentionally permit the changed behavior.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B86-0001","D11-ALG-B81-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived test-strength principle","evidence_basis":"fault-based testing"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B86-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Regression Testing","subtopic":"Historical failures","concept":"Failure knowledge preservation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should historical algorithm failures preserve their original context?","answer":"A failure without its triggering conditions, expected behavior, observed behavior, and root cause becomes difficult to interpret and may be incorrectly reused.","explanation":"Machine-readable failure knowledge should preserve the relationship between defect, trigger, diagnosis, fix, and verification evidence.","correct_example":"Recording the minimal failing input, violated invariant, defect cause, correction, and regression test relationship.","invalid_example":"Storing only a statement such as 'fixed binary search bug' without the failing condition.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B79-0001","D11-ALG-B83-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 knowledge-fabric policy","evidence_basis":"traceable failure and verification records"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B87-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Security and Defensive Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Input attacks","concept":"Algorithmic complexity attack surface","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What is an algorithmic complexity attack?","answer":"It is an input-driven condition in which an attacker causes an algorithm to consume disproportionately large computational resources relative to expected processing requirements.","explanation":"Security risk can arise from worst-case computational behavior even when the algorithm produces correct outputs.","correct_example":"An externally controlled input repeatedly triggering expensive worst-case processing in a request path.","invalid_example":"Calling any slow input an algorithmic complexity attack without establishing attacker control and resource impact.","security_implication":"Resource-exhaustion resistance may be a security requirement for exposed algorithms.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0001","D11-ALG-B84-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived security-analysis principle","evidence_basis":"worst-case algorithm behavior and resource-exhaustion reasoning"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_SECURITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B87-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Security and Defensive Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Resource controls","concept":"Algorithm resource budget","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can an exposed algorithm reduce resource-exhaustion risk?","answer":"Define resource budgets such as input-size limits, operation limits, recursion-depth limits, memory ceilings, timeouts, cancellation behavior, or bounded work where appropriate to the contract.","explanation":"The correct controls depend on the algorithm and execution environment; limits must not silently invalidate legitimate workloads.","tradeoff":"Strict limits improve predictability but may reject valid workloads.","security_implication":"Resource budgets can limit attacker-controlled computational amplification.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B84-0003","D11-ALG-B87-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived defensive algorithm engineering principle","evidence_basis":"resource-boundary testing and software security verification"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_SECURITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B87-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Security and Defensive Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Fail-safe behavior","concept":"Safe failure semantics","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should security-sensitive algorithms define failure behavior explicitly?","answer":"Ambiguous failures can cause callers to interpret incomplete, rejected, or unavailable results as valid results.","explanation":"A failure path is part of the security boundary when algorithm outputs influence authorization, validation, resource allocation, or state changes.","correct_example":"Returning an explicit failure state when a security decision cannot be computed within the permitted resource budget.","invalid_example":"Returning a default permissive result after timeout without a contract authorizing that behavior.","security_implication":"Fail-open versus fail-closed behavior must be explicit and context-specific.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B76-0003","D11-ALG-B84-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived security verification principle","evidence_basis":"explicit failure contracts and security assurance"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_SECURITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B87-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Security and Defensive Algorithm Testing","subtopic":"Data integrity","concept":"Algorithmic conservation property","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can conservation properties support integrity verification?","answer":"A conservation property asserts that required information is neither silently lost nor introduced during transformation.","explanation":"For many algorithms, integrity can be expressed as preservation of counts, multisets, graph connectivity constraints, or other domain-specific quantities.","correct_example":"Verifying that sorting preserves exactly the input multiset.","invalid_example":"Checking only that the output is ordered while ignoring whether elements were lost or duplicated.","security_implication":"Conservation checks can detect integrity failures when algorithm outputs influence security-sensitive state.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B76-0002","D11-ALG-B83-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived algorithm integrity principle","evidence_basis":"postcondition and property-based verification"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_SECURITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B88-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Verification","subtopic":"Static analysis","concept":"Static verification complement","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should static analysis complement dynamic algorithm testing?","answer":"Static techniques can identify certain structural, type, data-flow, control-flow, or coding defects without requiring execution, while dynamic tests reveal behavior on executed paths and inputs.","explanation":"Neither technique establishes all properties alone. Their evidence should be represented separately and combined where their scopes complement each other.","correct_example":"Using static analysis to identify suspicious operations and dynamic tests to validate behavior across boundary inputs.","invalid_example":"Treating a clean static-analysis result as proof that all runtime behaviors are correct.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B81-0001","D11-ALG-B80-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST IR 8397","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search26","evidence_basis":"static analysis and automated testing are distinct verification techniques"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B88-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Verification","subtopic":"Coverage interpretation","concept":"Coverage evidence limitation","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why does code coverage not prove algorithm correctness?","answer":"Coverage indicates which code regions or paths were exercised under a coverage definition; it does not establish that the exercised behavior was correct or that the input space and semantic properties were adequately tested.","explanation":"A test suite can execute every statement while still using incorrect expected results or missing important data relationships.","invalid_example":"Declaring an algorithm verified solely because all statements were executed by tests.","solution":"Combine coverage with specification-derived assertions, boundary tests, properties, regression tests, and other verification evidence.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B83-0002","D11-ALG-B88-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived verification principle","evidence_basis":"distinction between structural execution and semantic correctness"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_VERIFICATION_POLICY","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B88-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Verification","subtopic":"Formal reasoning","concept":"Proof versus testing","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How do formal reasoning and testing differ as algorithm verification evidence?","answer":"Formal reasoning attempts to establish properties over a defined model or domain, while testing evaluates selected concrete executions. Testing can find counterexamples; a proof can establish a property when its assumptions and proof are valid.","explanation":"The two forms of evidence answer different questions and can complement one another.","correct_example":"Using a loop invariant proof for general correctness and concrete tests for implementation-specific boundary and runtime behavior.","invalid_example":"Treating a finite test suite as mathematical proof over all possible inputs.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B64-0001","D11-ALG-B81-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST SP 500-93","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search25","evidence_basis":"algorithm correctness analysis versus testing"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B88-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Verification","subtopic":"Evidence levels","concept":"Verification evidence classification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should algorithm verification evidence be classified?","answer":"Classify evidence according to what was actually established: specification review, static inspection, mathematical proof, generated test, executed test, passed test, benchmark, formal verification, or other explicitly defined evidence.","explanation":"Different evidence types provide different guarantees and should not be collapsed into a single unqualified verified label.","correct_example":"Marking a test as EXECUTED and PASSED while separately recording that no formal proof was performed.","invalid_example":"Marking an implementation VERIFIED solely because source code was generated.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B80-0003","D11-ALG-B85-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST IR 8397; D11 verification policy","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search26","evidence_basis":"verification encompasses multiple techniques"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B89-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Failure Analysis","subtopic":"Fault classification","concept":"Algorithm fault taxonomy","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should algorithm implementation failures be classified for diagnosis?","answer":"Classify failures by semantic category such as precondition violation, boundary error, state-transition error, representation error, numerical error, resource exhaustion, concurrency error, API-contract violation, or performance regression.","explanation":"Consistent classification allows retrieval of related failures and prevents unrelated defects from being treated as the same problem.","correct_example":"Classifying a binary-search midpoint overflow as a numeric-representation failure rather than merely a search failure.","invalid_example":"Assigning every incorrect output to a generic 'algorithm bug' category.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B74-0001","D11-ALG-B79-0002","D11-ALG-B86-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 failure-analysis taxonomy","evidence_basis":"implementation failure modes developed across B71-B88"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B89-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Failure Analysis","subtopic":"Root cause","concept":"Root-cause versus symptom separation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should an algorithm knowledge base distinguish symptoms from root causes?","answer":"The same symptom can arise from different causes, while one root cause can generate multiple symptoms. Separating them improves diagnosis and retrieval.","explanation":"Machine reasoning should retrieve causal information rather than matching only surface-level error descriptions.","correct_example":"Treating an incorrect shortest-path result as a symptom and investigating stale state, invalid relaxation logic, numeric overflow, or violated input assumptions as possible causes.","invalid_example":"Recording 'wrong output' as both the symptom and the root cause.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B79-0001","D11-ALG-B89-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived diagnostic knowledge-model principle","evidence_basis":"causal failure analysis"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B89-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Failure Analysis","subtopic":"Reproduction","concept":"Deterministic failure reproduction","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What information should be retained to reproduce an algorithm failure?","answer":"Retain the exact input or generator seed where applicable, algorithm version, implementation version, relevant configuration, expected behavior, observed behavior, environment assumptions, and execution evidence available at the time.","explanation":"Reproduction requires enough information to distinguish the original defect from later environmental or implementation changes.","correct_example":"Storing a minimized input, software revision, generator seed, and expected property violation for a fuzz-discovered defect.","invalid_example":"Recording only a screenshot of the incorrect result without input or version information.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B83-0004","D11-ALG-B86-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived reproducibility principle","evidence_basis":"traceable testing and failure analysis"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_VERIFICATION_POLICY","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B89-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Failure Analysis","subtopic":"Fix verification","concept":"Defect-fix verification chain","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence chain should connect an algorithm defect to its correction?","answer":"The chain should identify the failure, minimal reproducer, root cause, correction, targeted regression test, broader regression results where available, and remaining limitations.","explanation":"A code change alone does not prove that the original defect was fixed or that no related regression was introduced.","correct_example":"Linking a failing boundary input to its violated invariant, correction, regression test, and subsequent executed result.","invalid_example":"Marking a defect resolved immediately after modifying the source code.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B86-0001","D11-ALG-B89-0003","D11-ALG-B88-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST IR 8397","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search26","evidence_basis":"verification and fixing critical bugs as separate activities"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B90-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Verification Integration","subtopic":"End-to-end validation","concept":"Algorithm verification pipeline","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is a comprehensive verification pipeline for an algorithm implementation?","answer":"Start with specification and assumptions; analyze complexity and resource limits; inspect representation and invariants; execute normal, boundary, invalid, adversarial, property-based, and regression tests; perform appropriate static and security analysis; measure performance when required; classify evidence; and record unresolved limitations.","explanation":"No single verification technique covers all algorithmic and implementation risks.","correct_example":"Combining proof-level reasoning with boundary tests, generated properties, historical regressions, static analysis, and controlled performance measurements.","invalid_example":"Using one successful example as the complete verification pipeline.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B80-0003","D11-ALG-B88-0001","D11-ALG-B89-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST IR 8397; NIST SP 500-93","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search26","evidence_basis":"multi-technique verification and a priori/a posteriori analysis"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B90-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Verification Integration","subtopic":"Evidence graph","concept":"Verification evidence graph","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should verification evidence be connected to algorithm knowledge records?","answer":"Link each claim to the tests, proofs, source specifications, measurements, failures, and version context that support or limit it.","explanation":"An evidence graph allows downstream systems to distinguish directly supported claims from inferred or unsupported ones.","correct_example":"Linking a complexity claim to its derivation and a performance claim to its specific executed benchmark record.","invalid_example":"Attaching one generic 'verified' flag to an entire algorithm without claim-level evidence.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B88-0004","D11-ALG-B85-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 provenance model","evidence_basis":"claim-level evidence tracking"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B90-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Verification Integration","subtopic":"Readiness criteria","concept":"Algorithm verification readiness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"When should an algorithm implementation be considered verification-ready rather than simply code-complete?","answer":"It is verification-ready when its contract, assumptions, relevant invariants, version context, failure semantics, test strategy, security/resource considerations, and required evidence have been explicitly identified.","explanation":"Code completeness means an implementation exists; verification readiness means the system knows what must be demonstrated and how that demonstration will be evaluated.","invalid_example":"Calling generated source code verified because it compiles or appears logically plausible.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B80-0005","D11-ALG-B90-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 verification policy; NIST IR 8397","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search26","evidence_basis":"verification planning and multiple verification techniques"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_VERIFICATION_POLICY","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B90-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Verification Integration","subtopic":"Knowledge-fabric integration","concept":"Testing-to-learning feedback loop","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should verified failures improve an algorithm knowledge fabric?","answer":"A verified failure should become linked knowledge containing its trigger, symptom, root cause, correction, regression test, relevant constraints, and evidence so future retrieval and reasoning can recognize similar conditions.","explanation":"The learning value is not merely the final fix; it is the causal relationship between conditions and failure.","correct_example":"Linking a discovered boundary defect to the relevant invariant, minimal counterexample, fix, and regression test.","invalid_example":"Storing only the corrected source code without the failure context.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B86-0004","D11-ALG-B89-0004","D11-ALG-B90-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 learning-system policy","evidence_basis":"failure analysis, regression, provenance, and verification relationships"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B90-0005","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"0.18","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Verification Integration","subtopic":"Final quality gate","concept":"Algorithm evidence completeness gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should prevent an algorithm record from being marked fully verified?","answer":"Missing contract assumptions, absent required evidence, unresolved contradictions, untested critical boundaries, unknown version semantics, unclassified failures, or unsupported empirical claims should prevent an unconditional verified status.","explanation":"Verification status must reflect available evidence rather than confidence based on source appearance.","correct_example":"Marking a performance claim NOT_VERIFIED when no execution measurement exists, while separately marking the theoretical complexity VERIFIED.","invalid_example":"Marking the complete record VERIFIED because its algorithm description is correct.","constraint":"Verification scope must be claim-specific.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B88-0004","D11-ALG-B90-0002","D11-ALG-B90-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 verification policy; NIST verification guidance","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search26","evidence_basis":"verification requires explicit evidence across techniques"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_VERIFICATION_POLICY","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

