{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B91-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Randomization","concept":"Randomized algorithm classification","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What distinguishes randomized algorithms from deterministic algorithms?","answer":"A randomized algorithm uses random choices during execution, so its behavior, running time, output, or error probability may depend on random outcomes.","explanation":"Randomization must be analyzed explicitly. A randomized algorithm is not the same as a deterministic algorithm receiving randomly distributed inputs.","correct_example":"A randomized pivot-selection algorithm whose pivot is selected using a random mechanism.","invalid_example":"Calling a deterministic algorithm randomized merely because its input is sampled randomly.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0001","D11-ALG-B91-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search7","evidence_basis":"randomized algorithm analysis and distinction from probabilistic inputs"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B91-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Randomization","concept":"Las Vegas versus Monte Carlo","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the distinction between Las Vegas and Monte Carlo randomized algorithms?","answer":"A Las Vegas algorithm preserves correctness while its running time may be random; a Monte Carlo algorithm typically provides a bounded or controlled running time while allowing a specified probability of error.","explanation":"Correctness probability and runtime guarantees must be represented separately.","correct_example":"Recording a randomized algorithm's error probability independently from its expected runtime.","invalid_example":"Describing every randomized algorithm simply as probabilistically correct without identifying its guarantee type.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B91-0001","D11-ALG-B91-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search7","evidence_basis":"course outcome explicitly distinguishes randomized algorithm classes"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B91-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Randomization","concept":"Expected running time","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should expected running time be represented for a randomized algorithm?","answer":"Expected running time should be stated relative to the probability distribution over the algorithm's random choices and, where relevant, the input distribution.","explanation":"Expected complexity is not identical to a worst-case deterministic bound and requires explicit assumptions about the source of randomness and input model.","constraint":"Do not convert expected complexity into a guaranteed per-execution bound.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0001","D11-ALG-B91-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search7","evidence_basis":"expected running time analysis of randomized algorithms"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B91-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Randomization","concept":"Randomization evidence discipline","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be recorded when making a claim about a randomized algorithm?","answer":"Record the randomization model, correctness guarantee, error probability where applicable, expected or worst-case complexity, input assumptions, and whether the claim is theoretical or empirically measured.","explanation":"Randomness introduces additional assumptions that must remain visible to downstream reasoning systems.","invalid_example":"Recording only an average runtime without specifying the randomization model or input assumptions.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0002","D11-ALG-B85-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J; D11 evidence policy","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search7","evidence_basis":"randomized analysis and evidence separation"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B92-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Computational Complexity","subtopic":"Complexity classes","concept":"P and NP distinction","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is the basic distinction between P and NP?","answer":"P contains decision problems solvable in polynomial time by a deterministic computational model, while NP contains decision problems whose proposed solutions can be verified in polynomial time under the standard formulation.","explanation":"Membership in NP does not mean that a problem is known to require exponential time.","invalid_example":"Claiming that every NP problem is computationally infeasible.","constraint":"Complexity-class statements depend on the computational model and formal problem definition.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B68-0003","D11-ALG-B92-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search34","evidence_basis":"formal discussion of P and NP"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B92-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Computational Complexity","subtopic":"Reductions","concept":"Polynomial-time reduction","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are reductions useful in algorithmic complexity?","answer":"A reduction transforms instances of one problem into instances of another while preserving the relevant answer relationship, allowing algorithmic difficulty or solvability to be transferred between problems.","explanation":"Reductions are central to proving hardness and relating apparently different problems.","correct_example":"Constructing a polynomial-time transformation from a known hard problem to a target problem to establish target hardness under the appropriate reduction definition.","invalid_example":"Calling two problems equivalent merely because both appear difficult.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B92-0001","D11-ALG-B92-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.890","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search13","evidence_basis":"reductions as a method for proving computational hardness"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B92-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Computational Complexity","subtopic":"NP-completeness","concept":"NP-complete classification","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does NP-complete mean under the standard polynomial-time reduction framework?","answer":"A decision problem is NP-complete when it belongs to NP and every problem in NP can be polynomial-time reduced to it, equivalently when it is in NP and is NP-hard under the specified reduction framework.","explanation":"NP-completeness is a formal classification, not a statement that no algorithm exists.","invalid_example":"Saying an NP-complete problem cannot ever be solved efficiently for any restricted input family.","constraint":"Hardness applies to the formally defined general problem unless additional assumptions or restrictions are stated.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B92-0001","D11-ALG-B92-0002","D11-ALG-B93-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.890","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search13","evidence_basis":"complexity and hardness proof curriculum"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B92-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Computational Complexity","subtopic":"Tractability","concept":"Complexity-aware problem reformulation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What should an algorithm designer do when an exact formulation appears computationally difficult?","answer":"Consider whether the problem can be restricted, approximated, parameterized, reformulated, solved heuristically, decomposed, randomized, or handled with domain-specific structure while preserving the required guarantees.","explanation":"Algorithm engineering can change the problem model or guarantee rather than merely searching for a faster exact implementation.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B93-0001","D11-ALG-B93-0002","D11-ALG-B93-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search10","evidence_basis":"approaches to intractability include approximation, heuristics, and fixed-parameter methods"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B93-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Approximation","concept":"Approximation algorithm","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an approximation algorithm?","answer":"An approximation algorithm produces a feasible solution to an optimization problem with a provable or otherwise explicitly characterized relationship to the optimum, rather than necessarily producing an exact optimum.","explanation":"Approximation trades exact optimality for computational tractability or other operational advantages.","constraint":"The approximation guarantee must be defined for the problem and objective direction.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B92-0004","D11-ALG-B93-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search7","evidence_basis":"approximation algorithms and approximation factors"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B93-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Approximation","concept":"Approximation factor","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What is an approximation factor?","answer":"An approximation factor bounds the relationship between the objective value produced by an approximation algorithm and the optimal objective value according to the formal definition for the optimization problem.","explanation":"For minimization and maximization problems the inequality direction differs, so the guarantee must be interpreted according to the problem definition.","invalid_example":"Reporting a numerical approximation factor without defining whether the problem is minimization or maximization.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B93-0001","D11-ALG-B85-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search7","evidence_basis":"analysis of approximation factors"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B93-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Approximation","concept":"PTAS and FPTAS distinction","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the conceptual distinction between PTAS and FPTAS?","answer":"A polynomial-time approximation scheme provides a polynomial-time algorithm for each fixed approximation parameter, while an FPTAS additionally requires polynomial running time in both input size and the appropriate representation of the approximation parameter.","explanation":"The distinction concerns the dependence of running time on the approximation parameter.","constraint":"The exact formal definition depends on the optimization problem and parameter representation.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B93-0001","D11-ALG-B92-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search7","evidence_basis":"PTAS and FPTAS included in course outcomes"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B93-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Parameterized algorithms","concept":"Fixed-parameter reasoning","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the central idea of fixed-parameter algorithm design?","answer":"Separate the effect of a selected parameter from the main input size so that computational cost can remain tractable for small parameter values even when the unrestricted problem is difficult.","explanation":"Parameterization changes the complexity question from input size alone to input size plus structurally meaningful parameters.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B92-0004","D11-ALG-B93-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search10","evidence_basis":"fixed-parameter algorithms as an approach to intractability"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B94-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Network optimization","concept":"Flow-network algorithm selection","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a flow-network problem be recognized?","answer":"Model entities as vertices and transferable capacity or directed relationships as edges when the problem asks about routing, throughput, cuts, matching, circulation, or related conservation constraints.","explanation":"Correct modeling is often more important than immediately selecting a specific flow algorithm.","correct_example":"Representing capacity-limited transportation between a source and destination as a directed flow network.","invalid_example":"Applying shortest path solely because the problem contains a graph when the objective is maximum throughput.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B45-0001","D11-ALG-B94-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"network flow included in advanced algorithms curriculum"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B94-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Flow optimization","concept":"Flow versus shortest path","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does a flow problem differ from a shortest-path problem?","answer":"Shortest-path algorithms optimize a path according to path costs or weights, whereas flow algorithms optimize aggregate movement subject to capacity and conservation constraints.","explanation":"The graph representation can look similar while the optimization objective and constraints are fundamentally different.","invalid_example":"Treating maximum throughput as the shortest path with the smallest edge weights.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B94-0001","D11-ALG-B47-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"distinct graph optimization topics"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B94-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Linear programming","concept":"Algorithmic optimization formulation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can linear programming be useful in algorithm design?","answer":"Linear programming expresses optimization objectives and linear constraints in a mathematical form that can model many resource-allocation and optimization problems and can sometimes provide bounds or formulations for discrete algorithms.","explanation":"The formulation must preserve the intended variables, constraints, objective, and domain semantics.","invalid_example":"Replacing an integer decision problem with unrestricted continuous variables without analyzing the resulting relaxation.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B92-0004","D11-ALG-B93-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search10","evidence_basis":"linear programming formulations and complexity"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B95-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Online algorithms","concept":"Online algorithm","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an online algorithm?","answer":"An online algorithm processes input incrementally without access to the complete future input sequence when decisions must be made.","explanation":"The algorithm must balance immediate decisions against uncertainty about future requests.","constraint":"The precise information available at each decision point must be defined.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B69-0001","D11-ALG-B95-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived advanced algorithmic model","evidence_basis":"online decision-making framework"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ALGORITHMIC_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B95-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Competitive analysis","concept":"Competitive ratio","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does competitive analysis measure for an online algorithm?","answer":"Competitive analysis compares the performance of an online algorithm against an appropriate offline optimum under the formal input model.","explanation":"The comparison provides a worst-case guarantee over input sequences rather than an average claim unless a distributional model is separately specified.","invalid_example":"Calling an online algorithm competitive merely because it performs well on a few observed workloads.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B95-0001","D11-ALG-B85-0003"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search7","evidence_basis":"competitive analysis as an algorithm-analysis technique"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B95-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Sublinear algorithms","concept":"Sublinear computation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the defining challenge of a sublinear algorithm?","answer":"A sublinear algorithm aims to answer a problem without examining all input elements, requiring an explicit access model and usually providing an exact or approximate answer under defined assumptions.","explanation":"Sublinear claims are meaningful only when the input-access model and query model are explicit.","invalid_example":"Calling an algorithm sublinear because its inner loop is short while it first scans the entire input.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B95-0004","D11-ALG-B61-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search34","evidence_basis":"sublinear algorithms included as advanced topic"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B95-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Algorithmic Paradigms","subtopic":"Access models","concept":"Computational-model awareness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must advanced algorithm records specify the computational model?","answer":"An algorithm's complexity and feasibility can change when the model changes, such as RAM, cache-aware, external-memory, parallel, distributed, streaming, or quantum models.","explanation":"A complexity result is conditional on the operations and cost assumptions of its model.","correct_example":"Distinguishing CPU-operation complexity from external-memory I/O complexity.","invalid_example":"Applying a RAM-model complexity result as an unconditional prediction of distributed execution cost.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B68-0003","D11-ALG-B67-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006 review and 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search35","evidence_basis":"alternative computational models explicitly discussed"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B96-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Parallel and Distributed Algorithms","subtopic":"Parallelism","concept":"Parallel algorithm decomposition","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What must be identified before parallelizing an algorithm?","answer":"Identify independent work, dependencies, synchronization points, shared-state hazards, communication costs, workload imbalance, and the target execution model.","explanation":"Parallelism changes the cost model and introduces coordination overhead and correctness hazards.","correct_example":"Partitioning independent data-processing tasks while preserving required ordering constraints and defining reduction synchronization.","invalid_example":"Adding multiple workers to a sequential algorithm without analyzing dependencies or shared mutable state.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B77-0002","D11-ALG-B95-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare 6.046J","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search10","evidence_basis":"parallel and distributed computation as advanced algorithmic topic"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B96-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Parallel and Distributed Algorithms","subtopic":"Parallel correctness","concept":"Parallel dependency preservation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must remain correct when an algorithm is parallelized?","answer":"The transformation must preserve required dependencies, synchronization semantics, data ownership, invariants, termination conditions, and output semantics under all permitted schedules.","explanation":"Sequential correctness does not automatically imply correctness under concurrent execution.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B64-0001","D11-ALG-B77-0002","D11-ALG-B96-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived parallel-algorithm verification principle","evidence_basis":"dependency and invariant preservation"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_VERIFICATION_POLICY","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B96-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Parallel and Distributed Algorithms","subtopic":"Scalability","concept":"Parallel speedup limits","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can adding processors fail to produce proportional speedup?","answer":"Sequential dependencies, synchronization, communication, scheduling overhead, memory contention, load imbalance, and finite problem size can limit speedup.","explanation":"Parallel performance must be evaluated against a defined workload and execution model rather than assuming linear scaling.","performance_implication":"Speedup claims require actual measurements or a formally stated analytical model.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B85-0001","D11-ALG-B96-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare advanced algorithms curriculum","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search10","evidence_basis":"parallel/distributed computation and algorithm analysis"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B97-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Cross-Language Algorithm Reasoning","subtopic":"Semantic transfer","concept":"Language-independent algorithm specification","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should an algorithm be represented for transfer across programming languages?","answer":"Represent the algorithm first through its mathematical problem definition, input/output contract, invariants, state transitions, complexity model, and failure semantics, then map those semantics into language-specific constructs.","explanation":"Separating algorithm semantics from syntax allows reasoning to survive language changes.","correct_example":"Representing binary search through its ordered-domain precondition and interval invariant before translating it into Python, Java, C++, or another language.","invalid_example":"Treating one language's implementation syntax as the algorithm itself.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B76-0001","D11-ALG-B88-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 cross-language reasoning policy","evidence_basis":"semantic specification and implementation separation"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"MULTI_LANGUAGE"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B97-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Cross-Language Algorithm Reasoning","subtopic":"Semantic equivalence","concept":"Cross-language behavioral equivalence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should equivalent implementations in different languages be verified?","answer":"Compare their externally defined contracts, outputs, invariants, error semantics, mutation behavior, ordering guarantees, numerical semantics, and relevant complexity properties rather than comparing syntax.","explanation":"Different languages may have different integer, memory, evaluation-order, exception, and concurrency semantics.","invalid_example":"Declaring two implementations equivalent because their control flow looks similar.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B74-0001","D11-ALG-B97-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived cross-language verification principle","evidence_basis":"semantic and representation differences"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_VERIFICATION_POLICY","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"MULTI_LANGUAGE"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B97-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Cross-Language Algorithm Reasoning","subtopic":"Portability","concept":"Portability constraint","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What can prevent an algorithm implementation from being semantically portable across languages?","answer":"Differences in integer ranges, floating-point behavior, overflow semantics, indexing conventions, memory models, evaluation order, character encoding, concurrency primitives, standard-library guarantees, and exception behavior can alter semantics.","explanation":"Porting requires explicit analysis of language and runtime assumptions.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B74-0001","D11-ALG-B75-0001","D11-ALG-B97-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived cross-language engineering principle","evidence_basis":"language/runtime semantic differences"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"MULTI_LANGUAGE"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B98-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Knowledge Retrieval","subtopic":"Concept retrieval","concept":"Concept-level retrieval","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should an algorithm knowledge system retrieve knowledge beyond exact keyword matches?","answer":"Retrieve using normalized concepts, aliases, problem structures, algorithm paradigms, invariants, failure symptoms, constraints, language/version metadata, and relationships between records.","explanation":"Algorithm questions are frequently expressed using different terminology from the stored knowledge.","correct_example":"Mapping 'find cheapest route' to shortest-path concepts while considering weights, negative edges, and graph assumptions.","invalid_example":"Returning only records containing the exact phrase used in the query.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B89-0001","D11-ALG-B90-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 retrieval architecture","evidence_basis":"conceptual and relationship-based retrieval"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B98-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Knowledge Retrieval","subtopic":"Debugging retrieval","concept":"Failure-symptom retrieval","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should algorithm debugging retrieval operate?","answer":"Use the observed symptom, input characteristics, violated properties, stack or state information when available, algorithm family, version context, and known failure relationships to retrieve candidate causes and corrections.","explanation":"Diagnosis should move from symptoms toward causally related records rather than relying only on textual similarity.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B89-0001","D11-ALG-B89-0002","D11-ALG-B89-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 debugging-retrieval policy","evidence_basis":"causal failure taxonomy and verification chain"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B98-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Knowledge Retrieval","subtopic":"Retrieval evaluation","concept":"Retrieval evaluation protocol","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should algorithm knowledge retrieval be evaluated?","answer":"Use representative exact, conceptual, debugging, code-generation, comparison, testing, security, and performance queries with known relevant records; measure retrieval coverage, relevance, false retrieval, and missed critical knowledge where measurable.","explanation":"A retrieval system cannot be considered effective merely because it returns documents.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B90-0002","D11-ALG-B98-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 retrieval validation policy","evidence_basis":"claim-level retrieval evaluation"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_VERIFICATION_POLICY","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B98-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Knowledge Retrieval","subtopic":"Relationship traversal","concept":"Relationship-aware retrieval","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should retrieval traverse algorithm relationships?","answer":"A useful answer may require connected records for prerequisites, variants, counterexamples, complexity, failure modes, proofs, tests, and version changes rather than one isolated concept.","explanation":"Relationship traversal allows the reasoning layer to construct evidence chains.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B90-0002","D11-ALG-B98-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 knowledge-graph policy","evidence_basis":"explicit record relationships"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B99-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Knowledge Quality","subtopic":"Contradiction handling","concept":"Algorithm knowledge contradiction resolution","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should conflicting algorithm records be resolved?","answer":"Compare definitions, input assumptions, computational models, algorithm variants, language/runtime versions, evidence authority, execution evidence, and publication or revision context before deciding whether the records truly conflict.","explanation":"Many apparent contradictions are actually context differences.","correct_example":"Separating two complexity claims because one uses worst-case input and the other uses expected randomized behavior.","invalid_example":"Deleting one record merely because two records use different complexity bounds.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B68-0003","D11-ALG-B69-0001","D11-ALG-B90-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 contradiction-resolution policy","evidence_basis":"context-aware evidence comparison"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B99-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Knowledge Quality","subtopic":"Deduplication","concept":"Semantic deduplication","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should duplicate algorithm knowledge be removed without losing useful distinctions?","answer":"Detect exact and semantic duplicates, merge equivalent claims while preserving provenance and relationships, and retain separate records when differences in version, assumptions, guarantees, implementation behavior, or evidence are meaningful.","explanation":"Aggressive deduplication can destroy important contextual distinctions.","invalid_example":"Merging two shortest-path records solely because they share the same topic when one supports negative weights and the other does not.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B90-0002","D11-ALG-B99-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 deduplication policy","evidence_basis":"context-preserving knowledge normalization"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B99-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Knowledge Quality","subtopic":"Versioning","concept":"Version-aware algorithm knowledge","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must algorithm knowledge retain version context?","answer":"Language, library, framework, hardware, and algorithm variants can change semantics, available operations, performance assumptions, or recommended implementations.","explanation":"A statement without version scope can become misleading when its environment changes.","correct_example":"Associating an implementation behavior with its language/runtime version range and marking deprecated APIs explicitly.","invalid_example":"Applying a current library API rule to an unknown historical version without evidence.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B97-0003","D11-ALG-B86-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"Derived D11 version-awareness policy","evidence_basis":"version-sensitive implementation semantics"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B99-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Algorithm Knowledge Quality","subtopic":"Provenance","concept":"Claim-level provenance","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What provenance is required for high-value algorithm knowledge?","answer":"Record the originating source or derivation, source type, relevant version or date, evidence scope, and limitations so that downstream reasoning can trace the claim.","explanation":"Provenance enables auditing and prevents unsupported claims from acquiring false authority through repetition.","invalid_example":"Assigning a generic citation to an entire dataset when individual records derive from different sources.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B90-0002","D11-ALG-B99-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"NIST DADS; D11 provenance policy","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search9","evidence_basis":"traceable algorithm definitions and source history"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B100-0001","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"D11 Final Integration","subtopic":"Algorithm selection","concept":"End-to-end algorithm selection workflow","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is the complete workflow for selecting an algorithm?","answer":"Define the problem and contract; identify input structure and constraints; choose an appropriate computational model; identify candidate paradigms; compare correctness guarantees, complexity, memory, numerical behavior, security, maintainability, and implementation constraints; test or prove the candidate; and record evidence and limitations.","explanation":"Algorithm selection is a constrained reasoning problem rather than a lookup of the theoretically fastest algorithm.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B92-0004","D11-ALG-B95-0004","D11-ALG-B90-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare algorithm-design outcomes; D11 integrated policy","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search3","evidence_basis":"algorithm synthesis, analysis, correctness, and engineering design"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B100-0002","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"D11 Final Integration","subtopic":"Problem-solving","concept":"Algorithm problem-solving loop","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What reasoning loop should an autonomous software-engineering system use for algorithm problems?","answer":"Understand the specification; model the problem; identify constraints; generate candidate approaches; derive correctness obligations; estimate complexity; identify risks and counterexamples; implement; test; verify; compare alternatives; preserve evidence; and learn from verified failures and outcomes.","explanation":"The loop connects algorithmic theory with implementation and verification rather than treating code generation as the endpoint.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B80-0003","D11-ALG-B90-0004","D11-ALG-B100-0001"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare algorithm design objectives; D11 autonomous reasoning policy","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search3","evidence_basis":"analysis, correctness, synthesis, and engineering application"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B100-0003","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"D11 Final Integration","subtopic":"Coverage","concept":"Algorithm knowledge coverage matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What dimensions must the final D11 coverage audit evaluate?","answer":"Coverage must include foundational concepts, data structures, searching, sorting, recursion, divide-and-conquer, greedy algorithms, dynamic programming, graph algorithms, strings, numerical algorithms, complexity, correctness, implementation behavior, testing, security, performance, advanced paradigms, cross-language semantics, provenance, versioning, failure analysis, retrieval, contradiction handling, deduplication, and verification.","explanation":"A topic list alone is insufficient; each major area should also have rules, examples, counterexamples, failure modes, testing guidance, and verification relationships where applicable.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B90-0005","D11-ALG-B99-0002"],"provenance":{"source":"MIT OpenCourseWare algorithm curricula; D11 scope policy","publisher":"MIT","source_ref":"turn0search0","evidence_basis":"broad algorithms curriculum coverage plus D11 knowledge requirements"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_EDUCATIONAL_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B100-0004","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"D11 Final Integration","subtopic":"Quality assurance","concept":"Final D11 quality gates","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Which quality gates must pass before D11 is locked?","answer":"Structural schema validation, stable IDs, relationship integrity, semantic deduplication, contradiction review, provenance review, version review, unsupported-claim review, example and counterexample review, testing and verification review, retrieval readiness, security and performance evidence discipline, and completeness assessment must pass.","explanation":"Locking is a quality-control decision, not merely completion of the planned batch count.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B99-0001","D11-ALG-B99-0002","D11-ALG-B99-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"D11 dataset governance policy; NIST verification guidance","publisher":"NIST","source_ref":"turn0search6","evidence_basis":"verification techniques and evidence discipline"},"evidence_level":"AUTHORITATIVE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DERIVED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL"}
{"record_id":"D11-ALG-B100-0005","dataset_id":"D11","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"D11 Final Integration","subtopic":"Lock status","concept":"D11 dataset lock","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What does LOCKED mean for D11?","answer":"LOCKED means the planned D11 scope has completed its final structural, semantic, provenance, relationship, contradiction, deduplication, retrieval-readiness, testing, security, performance-evidence, and verification-policy audit, and future changes must be represented through explicit correction or supersession records rather than silent mutation.","explanation":"A lock preserves stable knowledge identity and prevents uncontrolled changes from invalidating downstream relationships.","constraint":"A future authoritative correction may supersede a locked record; locking does not make every factual claim permanently true.","related_records":["D11-ALG-B100-0003","D11-ALG-B100-0004"],"provenance":{"source":"D11 dataset governance policy","evidence_basis":"stable versioned knowledge-fabric governance"},"evidence_level":"DERIVED_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","language":"GENERAL","created_at":"2026-09-16"}

