{"record_id":"D12-DS-B01-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Definition","concept":"Data Structure","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a data structure?","answer":"A data structure is a defined organization of data together with operations and behavioral properties that determine how data can be stored, accessed, modified, and related.","explanation":"A data structure is more than a container of values. Its representation and supported operations determine access behavior, update costs, ordering guarantees, uniqueness semantics, memory requirements, and applicable algorithms.","principle":"Choose a data structure according to required operations, invariants, constraints, and workload rather than according to its name alone.","rule":"The semantic requirements of the workload must be identified before selecting an implementation.","correct_example":"Representing a collection that requires unique membership tests with a set abstraction is appropriate when ordering is not a required semantic property.","invalid_example":"Selecting a linked list solely because insertion is described as constant-time without considering the cost of locating the insertion position.","problem":"Choosing a structure without modeling required operations.","failure_mode":"Poor asymptotic behavior, unnecessary memory use, or incorrect semantics.","cause":"Selection based on familiarity rather than workload requirements.","solution":"Identify required operations, ordering, uniqueness, mutation, access patterns, concurrency requirements, and resource constraints before selection.","tradeoff":"General-purpose structures reduce implementation effort but may not optimize specialized workloads.","constraint":"Complexity depends on the concrete representation and operation being performed.","edge_case":"A workload may have several equally valid structures with different constant factors and memory layouts.","performance_implication":"The selected representation can dominate runtime and memory behavior even when the algorithm is unchanged.","testing_guidance":"Test semantic invariants and representative operation patterns rather than only final output.","verification_guidance":"Verify both the abstract behavior and the concrete complexity assumptions used during design.","prerequisites":["D11-ALG-B61-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B01-0002","D12-DS-B01-0003"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Java Collections Framework","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search5","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Defines collections as a unified architecture of interfaces, implementations, and algorithms."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B01-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Abstract Data Types","concept":"Abstract Data Type","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an abstract data type in data-structure design?","answer":"An abstract data type specifies the logical behavior and operations of a data abstraction without requiring one particular physical representation.","explanation":"The same abstract behavior can be implemented by different representations. For example, a queue abstraction can have array-based, linked, or specialized concurrent implementations.","principle":"Separate required behavior from implementation representation.","rule":"Select an implementation only after the required abstract operations and guarantees are known.","correct_example":"A queue interface can expose enqueue, dequeue, and empty-state behavior while allowing different underlying implementations.","invalid_example":"Treating a specific implementation as if its incidental representation properties were part of every queue's contract.","tradeoff":"Abstraction improves substitutability but may hide implementation-specific performance or capability differences.","constraint":"An abstraction cannot guarantee properties that are not included in its contract.","edge_case":"Two implementations may satisfy the same abstract interface while having materially different complexity, memory, ordering, or concurrency behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test the abstract contract separately from implementation-specific performance tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every implementation satisfies the declared operations and invariants.","related_records":["D12-DS-B01-0001","D12-DS-B01-0003"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Java Collections Framework","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search5","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Documents separation between collection interfaces and concrete implementations."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B01-0003","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Interface versus Representation","concept":"Representation Independence","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should data-structure interfaces be separated from representations?","answer":"Separating interface from representation allows an implementation to change while preserving the externally required behavior when the contract remains satisfied.","explanation":"This separation enables implementation substitution for different performance, memory, or operational requirements.","principle":"Depend on required data-structure behavior rather than accidental implementation details.","tradeoff":"Greater abstraction can make implementation-specific optimization less visible to callers.","security_implication":"A stable abstraction can centralize validation and invariant enforcement instead of duplicating it across callers.","testing_guidance":"Use contract tests that can be applied to multiple implementations.","verification_guidance":"Verify behavioral equivalence at the abstraction boundary before replacing an implementation.","related_records":["D12-DS-B01-0002","D12-DS-B01-0005"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search8","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"States that collection interfaces allow manipulation independently of implementation details."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B02-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Operations","concept":"Operation Set","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Which operations should be modeled before choosing a data structure?","answer":"At minimum, model creation, access, search or membership, insertion, deletion, update, iteration, ordering requirements, and size or emptiness queries when relevant.","explanation":"Different structures optimize different operations. A correct selection requires a workload model rather than an isolated operation claim.","principle":"Optimize for the dominant required operations and their frequency.","correct_example":"A workload dominated by indexed reads should be evaluated differently from one dominated by arbitrary-key membership tests.","invalid_example":"Choosing a structure because one operation is fast while ignoring the operations that dominate the actual workload.","performance_implication":"Expected workload frequency should influence structure selection because average system cost depends on the distribution of operations.","testing_guidance":"Construct tests representing the expected operation mix, including boundary workloads.","verification_guidance":"Verify that claimed complexity corresponds to the actual operation and representation.","depends_on":["D12-DS-B01-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B02-0002","D12-DS-B03-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Summary of Implementations","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search1","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Compares general-purpose collection implementations and their intended usage."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B02-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Complexity","concept":"Operation Complexity","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should complexity claims for data structures be represented?","answer":"A complexity claim must identify the operation, representation, relevant assumptions, and whether the stated bound is worst-case, amortized, expected, or otherwise qualified.","explanation":"A statement such as constant-time is incomplete without specifying the operation and complexity model. Different operations on the same structure can have different bounds.","principle":"Never attach a single complexity label to an entire data structure without operation-level qualification.","invalid_example":"Claiming that a hash table is always O(1) for every operation under every input and implementation.","cause":"Failure to distinguish expected behavior, worst-case behavior, resizing, collisions, and implementation assumptions.","solution":"Record operation-specific complexity together with assumptions and evidence level.","performance_implication":"Asymptotic complexity is a model-based claim and does not establish actual latency or throughput.","testing_guidance":"Use workload-specific measurements when empirical performance matters.","verification_guidance":"Verify complexity against authoritative implementation documentation or a formal analysis appropriate to the representation.","depends_on":["D11-ALG-B61-0001","D11-ALG-B61-0007"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B02-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Summary of Implementations","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search7","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Explains that implementation performance is expressed in asymptotic terms and that nominal complexity does not always determine practical speed."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B03-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Memory Representation","concept":"Contiguous Storage","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is contiguous storage?","answer":"Contiguous storage places elements in adjacent memory locations according to the representation's layout rules.","explanation":"Contiguous storage supports efficient positional addressing and often favorable locality, but resizing or middle insertion may require moving elements.","principle":"Contiguous representation favors indexed access and locality when its movement and resizing costs are acceptable.","correct_example":"A fixed-size array stores elements in a contiguous region.","invalid_example":"Assuming that every resizable sequence remains at the same memory address after capacity growth.","edge_case":"A resize may allocate a new region and relocate existing elements.","performance_implication":"Contiguous layouts can provide efficient indexed access and locality, while resizing or middle insertion can require movement.","security_implication":"Bounds checking and correct index validation remain necessary regardless of physical layout.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, singleton, full-capacity, resize, boundary-index, and large-capacity cases.","verification_guidance":"Verify documented representation guarantees before relying on them in architecture decisions.","related_records":["D12-DS-B03-0002","D12-DS-B11-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"The Rust Standard Library","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search6","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Documents fixed arrays, slices, and Vec as contiguous-storage abstractions."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B03-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Memory Representation","concept":"Noncontiguous Storage","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is noncontiguous storage?","answer":"Noncontiguous storage represents logically related elements without requiring all elements to occupy adjacent memory locations.","explanation":"Linked structures commonly use individually allocated nodes connected by references or pointers. This can avoid bulk movement during some updates but introduces indirection and metadata overhead.","principle":"Use noncontiguous representation when its mutation or structural properties justify the additional indirection and memory overhead.","tradeoff":"Reduced movement for some updates can be exchanged for poorer locality, additional references, and allocation overhead.","edge_case":"A theoretically favorable linked update can still be expensive if locating the target position requires traversal.","performance_implication":"Pointer indirection and allocation behavior can materially affect practical performance beyond asymptotic complexity.","security_implication":"Pointer/reference integrity and lifetime management are critical in languages where memory safety is not automatically enforced.","testing_guidance":"Test insertion, deletion, traversal, empty-state transitions, and corrupted-link scenarios where applicable.","verification_guidance":"Verify ownership, lifetime, and link invariants for pointer-based implementations.","related_records":["D12-DS-B03-0001","D12-DS-B23-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"std::collections","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Documents linked-list collections and contrasts them with Vec and VecDeque."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B04-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Semantic Properties","concept":"Ordering","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does ordering mean for a data structure?","answer":"Ordering specifies whether and how elements have a defined sequence, such as insertion order, sorted order, priority order, or no guaranteed order.","explanation":"Ordering is a semantic property and must not be inferred from incidental implementation behavior unless documented as part of the contract.","principle":"Treat ordering guarantees as part of the data-structure contract.","invalid_example":"Depending on the observed iteration order of a structure when the API does not guarantee that order.","failure_mode":"Incorrect application behavior after an implementation, runtime, or version change.","solution":"Use a structure with an explicit ordering guarantee when ordering is semantically required.","testing_guidance":"Include ordering assertions only when the structure's documented contract guarantees the order.","verification_guidance":"Check the authoritative API contract rather than relying on empirical iteration order.","related_records":["D12-DS-B04-0002","D12-DS-B31-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search8","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Documents distinct collection interfaces and ordered/sorted variants."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B04-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Semantic Properties","concept":"Uniqueness","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is uniqueness as a data-structure property?","answer":"Uniqueness means the structure's logical contract prevents multiple equivalent members according to its equality or identity semantics.","explanation":"Set-like abstractions use uniqueness semantics, while list-like abstractions generally permit repeated values. The exact definition of equivalence is language- and implementation-dependent.","principle":"Use uniqueness as an explicit semantic requirement rather than removing duplicates as an incidental side effect.","correct_example":"A set abstraction is appropriate when membership should contain each logically equivalent element at most once.","invalid_example":"Using a list and assuming it enforces uniqueness without an explicit invariant.","edge_case":"Two values may be distinct objects but equivalent under the structure's equality or hashing semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test insertion of duplicate-equivalent values and verify the documented membership and size behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify equality/hash semantics and the collection contract together.","related_records":["D12-DS-B32-0001","D12-DS-B04-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Summary of Interfaces","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search2","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Documents Set as a specialized collection interface."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B05-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Interface Categories","concept":"Sequence","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a sequence data structure?","answer":"A sequence represents elements in a positional order where individual elements can be associated with positions in the sequence.","explanation":"Arrays, dynamic arrays, linked lists, and deques can provide sequence semantics while differing substantially in access and update costs.","principle":"Choose a sequence representation according to positional access, insertion, deletion, locality, and mutation requirements.","tradeoff":"Different sequence representations exchange indexing efficiency, insertion behavior, memory locality, and allocation overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test position boundaries, insertion/deletion behavior, ordering preservation, and empty-state transitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that positional semantics remain correct after every structural mutation.","related_records":["D12-DS-B11-0001","D12-DS-B21-0001","D12-DS-B23-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Summary of Interfaces","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search2","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Identifies List as a core collection interface."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B05-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Interface Categories","concept":"Set","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a set data structure?","answer":"A set represents a collection whose membership semantics normally treat equivalent elements as a single logical member.","explanation":"Set implementations can provide different ordering, complexity, memory, and concurrency properties.","principle":"Use a set when uniqueness and membership semantics are primary requirements.","alternative":"A list can be used when duplicates and positional order are semantically required.","tradeoff":"Hash-based sets generally prioritize membership operations, while ordered tree-based sets can provide ordering and range-oriented operations.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate insertion, membership, removal, empty state, and equality semantics.","verification_guidance":"Verify both uniqueness and any ordering guarantees claimed by the selected implementation.","related_records":["D12-DS-B04-0002","D12-DS-B32-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Summary of Interfaces","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search2","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Defines Set as a specialized collection interface."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B06-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Interface Categories","concept":"Map","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a map data structure?","answer":"A map associates keys with values and provides operations based on key identity or equality semantics.","explanation":"Maps are conceptually different from collection interfaces in some ecosystems. Java explicitly treats Map as a separate interface tree rather than a subtype of Collection.","principle":"Use a map when association by key is the primary semantic requirement.","invalid_example":"Using a list of key-value pairs as a map without considering lookup cost, duplicate-key semantics, or required ordering.","edge_case":"The handling of missing keys, duplicate keys, null keys, or equivalent keys is implementation-specific and must be checked in the relevant API contract.","testing_guidance":"Test missing keys, repeated keys, replacement behavior, key equivalence, and iteration semantics.","verification_guidance":"Verify key equality/hash or ordering requirements before selecting an implementation.","related_records":["D12-DS-B06-0002","D12-DS-B32-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Summary of Interfaces","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search9","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Explicitly distinguishes Map from the Collection hierarchy."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B06-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Interface Categories","concept":"Queue and Deque","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What are queue and deque abstractions?","answer":"A queue models ordered removal according to a queue policy, commonly FIFO, while a deque supports insertion and removal at both ends.","explanation":"The exact ordering policy can vary for specialized queues such as priority queues. A deque exposes two-ended operations and can support both queue-like and stack-like usage.","principle":"Select the abstraction according to the required removal policy, not merely because the data is sequential.","correct_example":"Use a queue abstraction for work that should normally be processed in arrival order.","invalid_example":"Using a priority queue when strict arrival-order processing is required.","tradeoff":"General queues provide predictable ordering semantics, while priority-oriented structures introduce ordering by priority rather than arrival time.","testing_guidance":"Test empty removal behavior, ordering, duplicate values, and transition between empty and non-empty states.","verification_guidance":"Verify the exact ordering contract of the chosen queue implementation.","depends_on":["D12-DS-B05-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B06-0003"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Summary of Implementations","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search7","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Distinguishes FIFO LinkedList queue semantics from priority queue ordering."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B07-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Language Ecosystems","concept":"Cross-Language Collection Mapping","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should equivalent data structures not be assumed identical across languages?","answer":"Languages expose different APIs, representation guarantees, equality models, memory models, ownership rules, ordering guarantees, concurrency properties, and complexity characteristics for similarly named structures.","explanation":"A list, map, set, or vector in one language may have different semantics or implementation constraints in another language.","principle":"Map abstract requirements across languages, then verify each language's concrete contract independently.","correct_example":"Rust's standard collections explicitly group Vec, VecDeque, LinkedList, HashMap, BTreeMap, HashSet, BTreeSet, and BinaryHeap with distinct intended use cases.","invalid_example":"Assuming that a structure called List has the same performance and mutation behavior in every language.","security_implication":"Incorrect assumptions about ownership, mutation, or concurrency can create security-relevant defects.","performance_implication":"Equivalent abstractions may use materially different representations and therefore have different practical costs.","testing_guidance":"Create language-specific contract tests for shared abstract behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify claims against each language's authoritative documentation.","related_records":["D12-DS-B07-0002","D12-DS-B01-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Collections","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"std::collections","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Shows explicit grouping and usage guidance for Rust collection types."},{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Java Collections Framework","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search8","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Shows Java's distinct collection interfaces and implementations."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B07-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Language Ecosystems","concept":"Rust Collection Selection","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Which common general-purpose collections does Rust document as standard choices?","answer":"Rust documents Vec, VecDeque, LinkedList, HashMap, BTreeMap, HashSet, BTreeSet, and BinaryHeap among its standard collection types, with Vec and HashMap described as covering many common use cases.","explanation":"The Rust standard library groups collections into sequences, maps, sets, and miscellaneous structures and provides selection guidance for them.","constraint":"This is Rust-specific API knowledge and must not be generalized to other languages.","performance_implication":"Collection selection should follow the documented operation and semantic requirements rather than type-name similarity across languages.","testing_guidance":"Verify the selected collection's documented behavior and test the intended workload.","verification_guidance":"Use the current Rust standard-library documentation for version-specific details.","version":"Rust std 1.98.1","version_range":"Rust 1.98.x documentation context","related_records":["D12-DS-B07-0001","D12-DS-B11-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Collections","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"std::collections","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Lists the standard collection categories and provides collection-selection guidance."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B08-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Selection","concept":"Data Structure Selection Workflow","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an autonomous software-engineering system select a data structure?","answer":"Identify required semantics, operations, workload frequencies, size bounds, mutation patterns, memory constraints, ordering requirements, concurrency requirements, version constraints, and security constraints; enumerate candidate structures; eliminate incompatible candidates; compare theoretical costs and practical constraints; then validate the selected choice against representative tests and measurements when performance matters.","explanation":"Selection is a multi-constraint engineering decision rather than a lookup from one operation to one structure.","principle":"Select the simplest structure that satisfies the required contract and operational constraints with adequate evidence.","correct_example":"Choose between a sequence, hash-based map, ordered map, or queue after explicitly modeling access, membership, ordering, and update requirements.","invalid_example":"Selecting a structure solely because its headline complexity is optimal for one operation.","tradeoff":"A more specialized structure may improve a dominant workload while increasing implementation, memory, or maintenance complexity.","performance_implication":"Theoretical complexity narrows candidates but does not replace workload-specific measurement.","testing_guidance":"Test semantic correctness first, then representative performance workloads if performance is a requirement.","verification_guidance":"Record the selection rationale, assumptions, evidence, and rejected alternatives.","depends_on":["D12-DS-B02-0001","D12-DS-B02-0002"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B08-0002","D12-DS-B41-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Summary of Implementations","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search7","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Documents choosing implementations according to behavioral and performance requirements and recommends measurement when uncertain."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B08-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Selection","concept":"Semantic Compatibility Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be checked before comparing data-structure performance?","answer":"First verify that each candidate satisfies the required semantics, including ordering, uniqueness, key equality, mutation behavior, error behavior, lifetime or ownership requirements, and concurrency guarantees.","explanation":"Performance comparisons among semantically incompatible structures are invalid for engineering selection.","rule":"Do not optimize among candidates that do not satisfy the required contract.","failure_mode":"Selecting a fast but semantically incorrect structure.","cause":"Treating complexity as the only selection criterion.","solution":"Apply a semantic compatibility gate before complexity and performance comparison.","testing_guidance":"Create contract tests that reject candidates violating required semantics.","verification_guidance":"Document which required properties each candidate satisfies or fails.","related_records":["D12-DS-B04-0001","D12-DS-B04-0002","D12-DS-B08-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search8","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Separates collection interfaces and implementation behavior, supporting semantic-first selection."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B09-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Failure Modes","concept":"Complexity-Only Selection Anti-Pattern","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is selecting a data structure using only one complexity number unsafe?","answer":"A single complexity number omits semantic requirements, operation distribution, constants, memory behavior, locality, allocation, concurrency, version-specific guarantees, and workload characteristics.","explanation":"A nominally superior asymptotic structure can be unsuitable when its semantics do not match the workload or when practical costs dominate.","invalid_example":"Selecting a linked structure because insertion is described as O(1) while ignoring that locating each insertion position requires traversal.","failure_mode":"Unexpected latency, excessive memory use, incorrect semantics, or unnecessary complexity.","cause":"Reducing multi-dimensional engineering selection to one asymptotic label.","solution":"Use semantic compatibility, operation-level complexity, workload characteristics, and empirical evidence where required.","performance_implication":"Theoretical bounds are necessary evidence but not sufficient empirical evidence.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark only after semantic compatibility is established and use representative workloads.","verification_guidance":"Require an explicit selection rationale and evidence classification.","depends_on":["D12-DS-B02-0002","D12-DS-B08-0002"],"contrasts_with":["D12-DS-B08-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Summary of Implementations","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search7","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Explicitly notes limitations of nominal complexity and recommends measurement when uncertain."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B09-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Failure Modes","concept":"Undocumented Behavior Dependency","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is relying on undocumented data-structure behavior dangerous?","answer":"Behavior not guaranteed by the authoritative contract can change across implementations, versions, optimization choices, or environments.","explanation":"Observed behavior during local testing is not equivalent to a documented API guarantee.","invalid_example":"Relying on an observed iteration order when the API does not guarantee that order.","failure_mode":"Latent correctness regressions after upgrades or implementation changes.","cause":"Confusing empirical observation with contractual guarantee.","solution":"Use documented guarantees and create explicit application-level ordering when ordering is required.","security_implication":"Incorrect assumptions can affect authorization, policy evaluation, or deterministic processing.","testing_guidance":"Test contractual behavior and include upgrade/regression tests for important guarantees.","verification_guidance":"Trace critical assumptions to authoritative documentation.","related_records":["D12-DS-B04-0001","D12-DS-B07-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search8","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Separates documented interface behavior from implementation-specific details."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B10-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Quality and Verification","concept":"Data Structure Contract Testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should contract tests verify for a data structure?","answer":"Contract tests should verify required operations, invariants, boundary behavior, error behavior, ordering or uniqueness guarantees, mutation semantics, and any documented concurrency or ownership constraints.","explanation":"A reusable contract suite allows multiple implementations to be tested against the same abstract requirements.","correct_example":"Apply the same queue contract to multiple queue implementations and verify FIFO behavior, empty-state behavior, and mutation semantics.","invalid_example":"Testing only one successful insertion while leaving empty, duplicate, boundary, and invalid-operation behavior unspecified.","edge_case":"The contract may intentionally allow implementation-specific error types or performance differences; those must not be falsely normalized.","testing_guidance":"Include positive, negative, boundary, invariant, mutation-sequence, and regression cases.","verification_guidance":"Separate behavioral verification from performance benchmarking and implementation-specific testing.","related_records":["D12-DS-B01-0002","D12-DS-B08-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search8","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Documents collection interfaces as reusable contracts across implementations."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B10-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.1","topic":"Data Structure Foundations","subtopic":"Quality and Verification","concept":"Data Structure Knowledge Verification Boundary","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must the Brain distinguish when storing data-structure knowledge?","answer":"The Brain must distinguish documented API facts, theoretical complexity claims, inferred engineering guidance, empirical measurements, executed examples, test results, and verified requirements.","explanation":"Data-structure knowledge can combine formal properties with implementation-specific documentation and empirical evidence. These evidence classes must not be collapsed.","principle":"Evidence type must remain attached to every important engineering claim.","rule":"A generated example is not evidence of execution, and a passing test does not prove universal correctness.","testing_guidance":"Store execution and test results separately from generated examples.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit evidence before promoting an inferred or generated claim to verified knowledge.","depends_on":["D11-ALG-B61-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B02-0002","D12-DS-B10-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Summary of Implementations","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search7","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Distinguishes implementation characteristics and theoretical complexity from practical measurement."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-16"}

