{"record_id":"D12-DS-B11-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Core Representation","concept":"Array","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an array?","answer":"An array is an indexed sequence representation in which elements are associated with positions and the representation provides direct positional access according to its contract.","explanation":"Array implementations differ across languages. Fixed-size arrays, dynamically resizable arrays, and JavaScript Array objects should not be treated as identical representations.","principle":"Use an array-like structure when positional access and sequence semantics are important.","correct_example":"Accessing an element by a valid index in a conventional array structure.","invalid_example":"Assuming that every language's Array provides identical type restrictions, resizing behavior, memory layout, or complexity guarantees.","performance_implication":"Direct indexing is typically efficient for conventional array representations, while insertion or deletion that shifts elements can require movement proportional to the affected region.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, singleton, first-index, last-index, out-of-range, repeated values, and large-size cases.","verification_guidance":"Verify the specific language and implementation contract before attaching complexity or memory-layout guarantees.","related_records":["D12-DS-B01-0001","D12-DS-B03-0001","D12-DS-B11-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"MDN","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Indexed collections","publisher":"MDN Web Docs","reference":"turn0search15","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents JavaScript arrays as indexed ordered collections."},{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search6","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents Java ArrayList as a resizable-array List implementation."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B11-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Resizable Arrays","concept":"Dynamic Array","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a dynamic array?","answer":"A dynamic array is a resizable sequence representation that maintains indexed elements while allowing its storage capacity to grow when necessary.","explanation":"Growth commonly involves allocating additional storage and moving elements. The exact growth policy is implementation-specific.","principle":"Use dynamic arrays when indexed sequence behavior is needed without fixing capacity in advance.","tradeoff":"Dynamic arrays provide convenient growth and efficient indexed access but may incur resizing costs and unused capacity.","performance_implication":"Individual growth operations can require relocation, while repeated append operations can have amortized favorable complexity under suitable growth policies.","edge_case":"A large capacity increase can temporarily require both old and new storage during relocation.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated append operations across capacity boundaries and verify element preservation.","verification_guidance":"Verify the implementation's documented growth and capacity semantics before relying on them.","related_records":["D12-DS-B03-0001","D12-DS-B11-0003"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"std::collections","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents Vec as a resizable array and discusses amortized costs."},{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search6","relevance":"Documents ArrayList as a resizable-array implementation."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B11-0003","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Capacity","concept":"Size versus Capacity","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"What is the difference between logical size and storage capacity in a dynamic array?","answer":"Logical size represents the number of elements currently contained, while capacity represents how many elements can be stored in the allocated representation before additional growth is required.","explanation":"Capacity is an implementation-level resource property and should not be confused with the number of logically present elements.","invalid_example":"Iterating through every capacity slot as if each slot contained a valid logical element.","failure_mode":"Reading uninitialized or non-logical storage and violating collection invariants.","solution":"Use logical size for valid-element boundaries and treat capacity as allocation metadata.","edge_case":"A collection can have nonzero capacity while its logical size is zero.","performance_implication":"Additional capacity can reduce the frequency of reallocations but may consume unused memory.","testing_guidance":"Test zero-size/nonzero-capacity states where the API exposes capacity.","verification_guidance":"Verify the collection invariant that valid indices are bounded by logical size rather than capacity.","related_records":["D12-DS-B11-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"std::vec::Vec","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Rust Vec documentation distinguishes length and capacity."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B12-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Indexing","concept":"Indexed Access","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When is array indexing appropriate?","answer":"Indexed access is appropriate when elements are naturally associated with positions and the implementation provides efficient positional access.","explanation":"Indexing is valuable for random access, but its cost and validity depend on the structure and index constraints.","principle":"Use index-based access when positional semantics are required or provide meaningful performance benefits.","invalid_example":"Using an array index without checking whether the index is within the valid logical range.","failure_mode":"Out-of-bounds access or incorrect element selection.","cause":"Confusing capacity with logical size or failing to validate external indices.","solution":"Validate indices according to the language's safety model and collection contract.","security_implication":"Unchecked externally controlled indices can cause memory-safety vulnerabilities in unsafe environments or runtime failures in bounds-checked environments.","testing_guidance":"Test negative, zero, last-valid, first-invalid, and excessively large indices where applicable.","verification_guidance":"Verify index bounds and language-specific indexing semantics.","depends_on":["D12-DS-B11-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B12-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"MDN","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Indexed collections","publisher":"MDN Web Docs","reference":"turn0search15","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents index-based access for JavaScript arrays."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B12-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Indexing","concept":"Bounds Discipline","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must array index bounds be treated as an explicit invariant?","answer":"Every valid array access must refer to an element within the collection's logical index domain.","explanation":"Bounds are part of the representation contract. The enforcement mechanism differs by language, but the invariant remains necessary.","rule":"Never infer index validity from allocated capacity, previous accesses, or caller intent.","edge_case":"Concurrent mutation or stale indices can invalidate an index that was previously valid.","security_implication":"In memory-unsafe environments, failed bounds discipline can become memory corruption; in memory-safe environments it may still cause controlled runtime failure or panic.","testing_guidance":"Include boundary and stale-index tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that all externally derived indices are validated or are statically constrained.","related_records":["D12-DS-B11-0003","D12-DS-B12-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"MDN","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Array","publisher":"MDN Web Docs","reference":"turn0search14","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents JavaScript Array indexing and length semantics."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B13-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Mutation","concept":"Append","knowledge_type":"OPERATION","question":"What does append mean for a dynamic array?","answer":"Append adds an element at the logical end of a sequence, increasing its logical size by one when successful.","explanation":"Append is often optimized in dynamic-array implementations because storage can be reserved beyond the current logical size.","principle":"Appending is a natural operation for dynamic arrays when growth occurs predominantly at the end.","edge_case":"An append may trigger allocation or relocation when capacity is exhausted.","performance_implication":"Append may be individually expensive during growth but can have amortized favorable complexity under suitable growth policies.","testing_guidance":"Test append to empty, partially full, full-capacity, and very large collections.","verification_guidance":"Verify that existing elements retain their required order and that the new element occupies the correct final position.","related_records":["D12-DS-B11-0002","D12-DS-B13-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"std::collections","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents Vec append/push use and amortized operation behavior."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B13-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Mutation","concept":"Amortized Append","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can repeated dynamic-array appends have amortized efficient cost?","answer":"When capacity grows geometrically or according to another suitable policy, expensive reallocations occur infrequently enough that their total cost can be distributed across many append operations.","explanation":"This is an amortized-analysis property, not a claim that every individual append is constant-time.","principle":"Distinguish per-operation worst-case cost from amortized cost across a sequence of operations.","invalid_example":"Claiming that every append is guaranteed to execute in constant time simply because the amortized cost is constant.","failure_mode":"Incorrect performance reasoning.","cause":"Confusing amortized analysis with worst-case per-operation analysis.","solution":"State the exact complexity model and growth assumptions.","performance_implication":"Amortized complexity does not provide a latency guarantee for every individual operation.","testing_guidance":"If latency bounds matter, test or design for individual-operation worst-case behavior rather than relying solely on amortized analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify the implementation's documented complexity and growth behavior.","depends_on":["D11-ALG-B61-0008"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B11-0002","D12-DS-B13-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"std::collections","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Explicitly discusses amortized collection operation costs."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B14-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Insertion","concept":"Middle Insertion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can inserting into the middle of an array-backed sequence be expensive?","answer":"Elements after the insertion position may need to be shifted to preserve sequence order and make room for the new element.","explanation":"The amount of movement depends on the insertion position and representation.","principle":"Use array-backed sequences for middle insertion only when movement costs are acceptable or workloads are appropriately bounded.","performance_implication":"Middle insertion can require linear movement in the affected suffix.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark representative insertion positions if insertion performance is a requirement.","verification_guidance":"Verify the implementation-specific complexity rather than generalizing from the abstract sequence interface.","related_records":["D12-DS-B11-0001","D12-DS-B14-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"std::collections","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents Vec insertion costs in relation to index position."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B14-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Deletion","concept":"Middle Deletion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can deleting from the middle of an array-backed sequence be expensive?","answer":"Removing an element from the middle generally requires subsequent elements to move so that the remaining sequence retains its required positional order.","explanation":"The movement cost depends on the number of affected elements and representation.","tradeoff":"Array-backed sequences trade efficient positional access for movement costs on structural edits away from efficient ends.","edge_case":"Deleting the last element can avoid shifting a suffix in conventional contiguous sequences.","performance_implication":"Deletion cost varies with the deletion position and representation.","testing_guidance":"Test deletion at the beginning, middle, end, empty state, and singleton state.","verification_guidance":"Verify both logical order and size after deletion.","related_records":["D12-DS-B14-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"std::collections","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Provides operation costs for Vec removal by index."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B15-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Memory and Locality","concept":"Array Locality","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can array-backed structures have favorable cache behavior?","answer":"When elements are stored contiguously or with compact locality, sequential traversal can access nearby memory locations efficiently and reduce pointer indirection.","explanation":"Actual processor-cache behavior depends on representation, element size, allocator, access pattern, runtime, and hardware. Locality is therefore a performance principle rather than a universal benchmark result.","principle":"Consider memory layout and access locality when selecting structures for traversal-heavy workloads.","performance_implication":"Contiguous or compact layouts can improve practical traversal performance, but the magnitude must be measured for the target workload when material.","testing_guidance":"Use representative profiling or benchmarks when locality is a performance requirement.","verification_guidance":"Distinguish documented representation facts from measured hardware performance.","related_records":["D12-DS-B03-0001","D12-DS-B15-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"LinkedList","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search9","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Explicitly contrasts array-based containers with linked lists regarding memory efficiency and CPU-cache usage."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B15-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Memory and Locality","concept":"Pointer Indirection Cost","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does pointer-based storage differ from array-backed storage for traversal?","answer":"Pointer-based structures can require following references between nodes, whereas array-backed structures can often traverse adjacent elements through a compact representation.","explanation":"This difference can affect locality and allocation behavior, although actual performance depends on workload and implementation.","tradeoff":"Pointer-based representations may offer structural flexibility at the cost of additional indirection and metadata.","performance_implication":"Do not infer a measured speed ratio from the representation alone.","testing_guidance":"Measure representative traversal workloads when performance decisions depend on this distinction.","verification_guidance":"Separate structural properties from empirical performance measurements.","related_records":["D12-DS-B03-0002","D12-DS-B15-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust LinkedList documentation","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"std::collections::linked_list","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search9","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"States that array-based containers generally use memory and CPU cache more efficiently than LinkedList."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B16-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Language Comparison","concept":"Java ArrayList","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What is Java ArrayList?","answer":"ArrayList is Java's general-purpose resizable-array implementation of the List interface.","explanation":"Java's Collections Framework distinguishes ArrayList from LinkedList and other collection implementations.","language":"Java","version":"Java SE 24 documentation context","version_range":"Java SE 24","framework":"Java Collections Framework","performance_implication":"Its representation is resizable-array based, so positional access and structural edits have characteristics associated with that representation.","testing_guidance":"Test list ordering, indexing, insertion, deletion, resizing, and concurrent-modification assumptions separately.","verification_guidance":"Use the applicable JDK documentation for exact API and implementation guarantees.","related_records":["D12-DS-B11-0002","D12-DS-B16-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"Oracle","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search6","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Lists ArrayList as the resizable-array general-purpose List implementation."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B16-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Language Comparison","concept":"Java ArrayList versus LinkedList","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the representation-level distinction between Java ArrayList and LinkedList?","answer":"ArrayList is a resizable-array implementation, while LinkedList is a linked-list implementation and also implements Queue and Deque interfaces.","explanation":"The two implementations provide different operational characteristics and should be selected according to workload and semantics.","language":"Java","version":"Java SE 24 documentation context","tradeoff":"ArrayList favors array-backed sequence behavior; LinkedList provides linked structure and deque capabilities but has different traversal and locality characteristics.","performance_implication":"Do not choose LinkedList merely because an abstract insertion operation can be constant-time; locating the position can dominate cost.","testing_guidance":"Compare representative workloads rather than isolated micro-operations.","verification_guidance":"Check the current JDK API documentation and benchmark only after semantic compatibility is established.","related_records":["D12-DS-B14-0001","D12-DS-B15-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"Oracle","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search6","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Lists ArrayList as resizable array and LinkedList as linked-list implementations."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B17-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Language Comparison","concept":"Python List","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What is Python list at the data-structure level?","answer":"Python's list is a mutable sequence type commonly used as a general-purpose sequence and supports indexed access and mutation operations.","explanation":"Python's list should be distinguished from linked-list structures and from the `collections.deque` type, which is designed for efficient operations at both ends.","language":"Python","version":"Python 3.14 documentation context","version_range":"Python 3.14.x","performance_implication":"End-oriented operations and arbitrary positional operations have different costs; workloads requiring efficient operations at both ends should consider deque.","testing_guidance":"Test indexing, append, insertion, deletion, slicing behavior, empty states, and large sequences.","verification_guidance":"Use current Python documentation for exact operation guarantees and version-specific behavior.","related_records":["D12-DS-B18-0001","D12-DS-B11-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Python Documentation","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Built-in Types: list","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","reference":"https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/stdtypes.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents Python's mutable sequence list type."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B17-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Language Comparison","concept":"JavaScript Array","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"How does JavaScript Array differ from a conventional fixed-type array?","answer":"JavaScript Array is an object-based indexed collection that is resizable and can contain values of different types.","explanation":"JavaScript arrays have language-specific semantics and should not be assumed to provide the same representation or type constraints as arrays in statically typed languages.","language":"JavaScript","version":"ECMAScript 2027 specification context","version_range":"Modern ECMAScript","performance_implication":"Array behavior and optimization can depend on engine implementation and array shape; theoretical sequence semantics should be separated from engine-specific performance assumptions.","security_implication":"Do not use ordinary arrays or object properties as a substitute for a key-value abstraction without considering key semantics and prototype-related behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test sparse arrays, mixed values, length changes, index boundaries, and mutation methods when relevant.","verification_guidance":"Use ECMAScript and MDN documentation for language-level semantics; treat engine performance claims as implementation-specific.","related_records":["D12-DS-B17-0003","D12-DS-B11-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"MDN","source_type":"OFFICIAL_TECHNICAL_REFERENCE","title":"Array","publisher":"MDN Web Docs","reference":"turn0search14","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents JavaScript Array as resizable and capable of mixed value types."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B17-0003","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Language Comparison","concept":"Typed Arrays","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What are typed arrays in JavaScript?","answer":"Typed arrays provide array-like indexed views over binary data buffers using fixed numeric element types such as Int8Array and Float32Array.","explanation":"Typed arrays are distinct from ordinary JavaScript Array objects and are intended for typed binary-data-oriented operations.","language":"JavaScript","version":"Modern ECMAScript","performance_implication":"Typed arrays can provide representation semantics appropriate for binary numeric data, but actual performance must be evaluated for the target engine and workload.","security_implication":"Binary parsing must still validate lengths, offsets, encodings, and trust boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Test byte offsets, lengths, numeric conversion, boundary values, and malformed external input.","verification_guidance":"Verify the typed-array and ArrayBuffer contract for the target runtime.","related_records":["D12-DS-B17-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"MDN","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"JavaScript data types and data structures","publisher":"MDN Web Docs","reference":"turn0search0","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents typed arrays as array-like views over binary buffers."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B18-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Sequences","subtopic":"Deque","concept":"Double-Ended Queue","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"Why use a deque instead of an array-backed list for frequent front operations?","answer":"A deque is designed to support efficient insertion and removal at both ends, avoiding the repeated front-shifting associated with conventional contiguous sequences.","explanation":"The concrete representation varies by language. Python deque and Rust VecDeque are examples of specialized double-ended sequence structures.","principle":"Use a deque when both-end operations are dominant requirements.","invalid_example":"Repeatedly removing the first element from a list-like structure when the workload requires high-frequency front removal.","failure_mode":"Repeated element movement creates unnecessary linear work per operation.","solution":"Use a deque implementation with documented efficient end operations.","performance_implication":"Python documentation states deque append and pop operations at either end have approximately O(1) performance, while list pop(0) and insert(0, v) require O(n) movement.","testing_guidance":"Test push/pop at both ends, empty transitions, alternating operations, and bounded-capacity behavior where supported.","verification_guidance":"Verify the target implementation's end-operation guarantees.","related_records":["D12-DS-B18-0002","D12-DS-B13-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Python Documentation","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"collections — Container datatypes","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","reference":"turn0search10","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents deque end operations and contrasts them with list front operations."},{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"VecDeque","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search13","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents VecDeque as a growable ring-buffer deque."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B18-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Sequences","subtopic":"Deque","concept":"Ring Buffer","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a ring buffer representation?","answer":"A ring buffer represents a sequence in a bounded or growable storage region while logically wrapping positions from the end back to the beginning.","explanation":"Ring buffers can support efficient operations at both logical ends without requiring every logical element to occupy one globally contiguous memory interval.","correct_example":"Rust VecDeque is documented as a double-ended queue implemented with a growable ring buffer.","edge_case":"The logical sequence can wrap across the physical end of the allocation, so its elements are not necessarily contiguous in memory.","performance_implication":"Ring-buffer representation can reduce element movement for end operations compared with a simple contiguous sequence.","testing_guidance":"Test wrap-around after repeated push/pop operations and verify logical ordering.","verification_guidance":"Verify logical order independently of physical storage layout.","related_records":["D12-DS-B18-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"VecDeque","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search13","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Explicitly documents VecDeque as a growable ring buffer and notes non-contiguous physical layout."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B19-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Sequences","subtopic":"Selection","concept":"Sequence Structure Selection","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should an array, dynamic array, deque, and linked list be distinguished at selection time?","answer":"Use the workload's semantic and operational requirements: indexed access favors array-backed sequences; dynamic growth favors dynamic arrays; frequent operations at both ends favor deques; linked lists should be considered only when their specific structural properties justify their indirection and memory costs.","explanation":"No sequence representation is universally optimal. Selection depends on operation distribution, size behavior, locality, mutation pattern, and language implementation.","tradeoff":"Array-backed structures generally favor locality and indexed access; deques optimize both ends; linked lists can provide structural operations without contiguous relocation but introduce traversal and allocation costs.","performance_implication":"Complexity tables must be tied to the concrete implementation and operation.","testing_guidance":"Build workload-specific tests covering dominant operations rather than testing only one operation.","verification_guidance":"Document semantic compatibility and complexity assumptions before selecting a structure.","depends_on":["D12-DS-B08-0001","D12-DS-B15-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B16-0002","D12-DS-B18-0001","D12-DS-B19-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"std::collections","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Provides explicit selection guidance and operation-cost comparisons for Vec, VecDeque, and LinkedList."},{"source":"Python Documentation","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"collections — Container datatypes","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","reference":"turn0search10","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Provides operation-level comparison between list and deque."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_SELECTION_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B19-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Sequences","subtopic":"Anti-Patterns","concept":"Linked List by Default","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should linked lists not be selected by default for sequence workloads?","answer":"A linked list introduces node allocation and pointer indirection and may have weaker locality and traversal performance than array-based structures, while its structural advantages apply only to particular workloads.","explanation":"The existence of constant-time endpoint or local-node operations does not make a linked list universally preferable.","invalid_example":"Replacing every dynamic array with a linked list because linked-list insertion is described as constant-time.","failure_mode":"Higher memory overhead, poorer locality, unnecessary traversal costs, and increased implementation complexity.","cause":"Focusing on one theoretical operation without modeling how the target position is reached.","solution":"Start with the simplest structure that satisfies the workload and select a linked list only when its specific properties are justified.","performance_implication":"Rust documentation explicitly advises that Vec or VecDeque are almost always preferable to LinkedList because array-based containers are generally faster and more memory efficient.","testing_guidance":"Compare realistic workloads when a linked list is being considered for performance reasons.","verification_guidance":"Record the specific workload evidence supporting the selection.","depends_on":["D12-DS-B15-0002","D12-DS-B19-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Rust Standard Library","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"LinkedList","publisher":"Rust Project","reference":"turn0search9","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Explicitly cautions that Vec or VecDeque are almost always preferable to LinkedList."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_DOCUMENTED_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B20-0001","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Testing","concept":"Array Boundary Test Matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What boundary cases should be tested for array-like structures?","answer":"At minimum test empty collections, singleton collections, first valid index, last valid index, first invalid index, deletion of the only element, insertion into empty and full states, capacity transitions for dynamic arrays, and very large valid indices where supported.","explanation":"Boundary testing targets the points where index and size invariants change state.","testing_guidance":"Each test should define precondition, operation, expected result, and failure interpretation.","security_implication":"Externally supplied indices and sizes must be treated as untrusted input when they cross trust boundaries.","verification_guidance":"A passing boundary suite provides evidence for tested cases only; it does not prove universal correctness.","related_records":["D12-DS-B12-0002","D12-DS-B11-0003"],"provenance":[{"source":"MDN","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Array","publisher":"MDN Web Docs","reference":"turn0search14","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Documents Array length, indexing, and mutation semantics relevant to boundary testing."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_TESTING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B20-0002","dataset_id":"D12","dataset_version":"0.2","topic":"Arrays","subtopic":"Testing","concept":"Mutation Invariant Testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariants should be checked after array structural mutations?","answer":"After insertion or deletion, verify logical size, element ordering, retained-element identity or equality as required, valid-index range, and the intended location of the inserted or removed element.","explanation":"Mutation testing should verify the complete state transition rather than only checking the newly modified element.","rule":"A successful mutation is not established merely because no exception occurred.","testing_guidance":"Use before-state and after-state assertions, including sequences of mutations rather than isolated operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify the abstract sequence contract independently from implementation-specific storage details.","related_records":["D12-DS-B13-0001","D12-DS-B14-0002","D12-DS-B20-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"Java Collections Framework","source_type":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","title":"Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","reference":"turn0search6","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Provides collection interfaces and implementations whose contracts can be validated through behavioral testing."}],"evidence_level":"DERIVED_VERIFICATION_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED"}

