{"record_id":"D12-DS-B21-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Stacks","subtopic":"LIFO semantics","concept":"Stack","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a stack data structure?","answer":"A stack is an abstract data type in which insertion and removal occur at the same logical end, following last-in-first-out ordering.","explanation":"The most recently inserted element is the next element eligible for removal. The principal operations are push, pop, and top or peek.","principle":"A stack exposes LIFO behavior independently of its underlying representation.","correct_example":"Push A, then B, then C; successive removals produce C, B, A.","invalid_example":"Treating a stack as a FIFO structure violates its defining ordering.","constraint":"The implementation must preserve LIFO ordering for all valid operations.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, singleton, repeated push/pop, and alternating push/pop sequences.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each pop returns the most recently pushed element that has not already been removed.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B01 through D12-DS-B20"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B21-0002","D12-DS-B21-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_standard_library_documentation","title":"C++ Containers Library","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B21-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Stacks","subtopic":"Core operations","concept":"Push, pop, top, empty","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What operations define the normal stack interface?","answer":"A conventional stack interface provides insertion at the top, removal from the top, inspection of the top element, and an emptiness query; exact names vary by language.","explanation":"Separating the abstract operations from implementation details allows a stack to be implemented with arrays, dynamic arrays, linked nodes, or other suitable representations.","principle":"Program logic should depend on stack semantics rather than a specific representation when representation is not part of the requirement.","correct_example":"An expression evaluator can push operands and pop operands without depending on whether the stack uses an array or linked nodes.","invalid_example":"Assuming that every stack implementation must provide indexed random access confuses implementation with abstraction.","testing_guidance":"Test operation preconditions and postconditions independently.","verification_guidance":"Check that each operation preserves the stack ordering invariant.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B21-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B22-0001","D12-DS-B24-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_standard_library_documentation","title":"C++ Containers Library","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B21-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Stacks","subtopic":"Complexity","concept":"Stack operation complexity","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What complexity should a stack normally provide?","answer":"A well-designed stack normally provides constant-time top insertion, top removal, and top inspection, although exact complexity depends on the representation and implementation.","explanation":"Array-backed and linked-node stacks can both provide constant-time operations at the active end when implemented appropriately.","principle":"Choose a representation that makes the required stack operations inexpensive rather than optimizing operations the abstraction does not require.","tradeoff":"Array-backed implementations may provide better locality, while linked implementations can avoid contiguous-capacity management but introduce node and pointer overhead.","performance_implication":"The theoretical operation complexity does not by itself determine real performance; memory locality and allocation behavior can matter.","testing_guidance":"Test operation complexity separately from functional correctness; do not claim empirical performance without measurement.","verification_guidance":"Inspect the implementation path to establish the stated asymptotic complexity.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B21-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B23-0001","D12-DS-B81-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_reference","title":"C++ Containers Library","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B22-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Stacks","subtopic":"Array-backed implementation","concept":"Array-backed stack","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is an array-backed stack appropriate?","answer":"An array-backed stack is appropriate when stack operations dominate, compact storage and locality are useful, and the implementation can manage capacity safely.","explanation":"The active stack region can occupy a contiguous sequence, with a size or top index identifying the next insertion position.","correct_example":"Maintain an array and an integer size; push writes at index size and increments size, while pop decrements size before reading the removed element.","invalid_example":"Using a fixed array without defining overflow behavior when the stack reaches capacity creates an incomplete implementation contract.","edge_case":"Empty stack, full fixed-capacity stack, capacity growth failure, and integer overflow of size metadata.","security_implication":"Bounds checks and capacity validation prevent memory-safety violations in unsafe implementations.","testing_guidance":"Test zero capacity, one element, capacity boundary, growth boundary, and repeated push/pop.","verification_guidance":"Verify that 0 <= size <= capacity and that active elements occupy exactly the documented range.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B21-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B22-0002","D12-DS-B22-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Stack representation analysis","publisher":"Derived from standard stack invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B22-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Stacks","subtopic":"Linked implementation","concept":"Linked-node stack","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a stack be implemented with linked nodes?","answer":"A linked-node stack can maintain the top element as a reference to the first node, with push adding a node at the front and pop removing that node.","explanation":"Both insertion and removal occur at one end, so no traversal is required for normal stack operations.","tradeoff":"Linked nodes avoid contiguous-capacity requirements but add per-node metadata and allocation overhead.","correct_example":"The stack top points to node C, whose next reference points to node B, followed by node A.","invalid_example":"Appending to the tail of a singly linked list and traversing to find the previous node for every pop destroys the expected constant-time stack removal.","performance_implication":"Allocation and pointer indirection can affect practical performance even when asymptotic operation complexity is constant.","testing_guidance":"Test node creation, top updates, final-node removal, and empty-state restoration.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every pop advances the top reference exactly one node and does not lose the remaining chain.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B21-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B22-0001","D12-DS-B23-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Stack representation analysis","publisher":"Derived from stack invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B22-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Stacks","subtopic":"Overflow and underflow","concept":"Stack boundary conditions","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Which boundary conditions must a stack implementation handle?","answer":"A stack must explicitly define behavior for removal or inspection when empty and for insertion when a fixed capacity is exhausted.","explanation":"These conditions are part of the operational contract and should not be left to accidental runtime behavior.","problem":"Calling pop on an empty stack without a defined contract can cause exceptions, sentinel ambiguity, undefined behavior, or data corruption depending on the environment.","solution":"Define explicit empty-state behavior such as an exception, optional result, error result, or documented precondition.","constraint":"Fixed-capacity stacks require a defined overflow policy.","testing_guidance":"Test empty pop, empty top, full push, and transitions between empty and non-empty states.","verification_guidance":"Verify that boundary operations follow the documented contract rather than relying on accidental implementation behavior.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B21-0002"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B22-0001","D12-DS-B22-0002","D12-DS-B90-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Stack boundary analysis","publisher":"Derived from stack operation contracts","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B23-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Stacks","subtopic":"Application patterns","concept":"Call-stack model","knowledge_type":"APPLICATION","question":"Why is a stack suitable for nested execution state?","answer":"Nested execution naturally follows LIFO behavior because the most recently entered unfinished context must normally be completed before returning to an earlier context.","explanation":"This makes stacks useful for modeling nested scopes, recursive activation records, parser state, and other nested workflows.","correct_example":"A recursive computation enters contexts A, B, and C; C must return before B can continue, and B before A.","alternative":"An explicit stack can replace recursion when execution state must be managed manually.","tradeoff":"Explicit stacks expose state and can avoid call-stack limitations, but the programmer must encode and maintain the required state correctly.","edge_case":"Very deep nesting can exhaust available stack or equivalent state storage.","testing_guidance":"Test shallow nesting, empty nesting, maximum supported depth, and abnormal termination of a nested operation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that contexts are resumed in exact reverse order of entry.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B21-0001"],"related_records":["D11-ALGO-B??"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"LIFO execution-state analysis","publisher":"Derived from stack semantics","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B23-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Stacks","subtopic":"Parsing","concept":"Operator and delimiter stack","knowledge_type":"APPLICATION","question":"Why are stacks useful in parsing nested delimiters?","answer":"A stack can retain unmatched opening delimiters so that each closing delimiter can be checked against the most recently opened construct.","explanation":"The LIFO property mirrors properly nested delimiter structure.","correct_example":"For input such as parentheses nested inside brackets, the most recent unmatched opening delimiter is checked first.","invalid_example":"Matching closing delimiters against the oldest opening delimiter implements FIFO behavior and fails for nested structures.","failure_mode":"Ignoring delimiter type can accept structurally invalid input such as mismatched bracket kinds.","testing_guidance":"Include empty input, balanced nesting, mismatched types, premature closing delimiters, and unclosed opening delimiters.","verification_guidance":"Verify both ordering and delimiter-type compatibility.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B21-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B23-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Nested delimiter parsing analysis","publisher":"Derived from LIFO semantics","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B24-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Queues","subtopic":"FIFO semantics","concept":"Queue","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a queue data structure?","answer":"A queue is an abstract data type in which elements are normally inserted at one end and removed from the other, producing first-in-first-out ordering.","explanation":"The oldest eligible element is processed before newer elements.","principle":"A queue models ordered waiting or processing where arrival order is preserved.","correct_example":"Enqueue A, B, C; successive removals produce A, B, C.","invalid_example":"Removing the newest element first changes the structure into stack-like behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, singleton, multiple elements, alternating enqueue/dequeue, and complete draining.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each removal returns the earliest element that remains in the queue.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B21-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B24-0002","D12-DS-B25-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_standard_library_documentation","title":"C++ Containers Library","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B24-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Queues","subtopic":"Core operations","concept":"Enqueue and dequeue","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Which operations are fundamental to a queue?","answer":"The fundamental queue operations are insertion at the rear, removal from the front, and usually inspection of the front element and an emptiness or size query.","explanation":"Exact API names differ among languages, but the abstract semantics remain FIFO.","correct_example":"A work scheduler inserts new jobs at the rear and takes the oldest eligible job from the front.","invalid_example":"Removing from the rear while claiming FIFO semantics is inconsistent with a conventional queue.","testing_guidance":"Verify rear insertion does not change the relative order of existing elements and front removal advances exactly one position.","verification_guidance":"Maintain and check the FIFO ordering invariant after every mutation.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B24-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B25-0001","D12-DS-B26-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_standard_library_documentation","title":"C++ Containers Library","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B25-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Queues","subtopic":"Array implementation","concept":"Circular queue","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can a circular array represent a queue efficiently?","answer":"A circular array reuses positions released at the front instead of requiring all remaining elements to be shifted after each dequeue.","explanation":"Front and rear positions advance modulo capacity, allowing the logical sequence to wrap around the physical array.","correct_example":"After dequeuing several elements, new elements can occupy the freed positions at the beginning of the array.","invalid_example":"Shifting every remaining element toward index zero after each dequeue adds unnecessary movement when a circular representation can preserve logical order.","tradeoff":"Circular queues require careful distinction between empty and full states, especially when front and rear positions can coincide.","edge_case":"Wraparound at the final physical index and transition between full, non-full, and empty states.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated wraparound cycles and full-to-empty-to-full transitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that logical FIFO order is preserved across physical wraparound.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B15-0001"],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B24-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B25-0002","D12-DS-B25-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Circular queue representation analysis","publisher":"Derived from queue invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B25-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Queues","subtopic":"Circular queue invariant","concept":"Front and rear state","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be verified in a circular queue implementation?","answer":"The implementation must preserve the relationship between front position, logical size, capacity, and rear position across every enqueue and dequeue operation.","explanation":"Incorrect index arithmetic can silently reorder, overwrite, duplicate, or lose elements.","rule":"For a bounded circular queue, logical size must remain within 0 and capacity and index transitions must use the documented wraparound rule.","failure_mode":"Using raw increment without wraparound eventually indexes outside the backing array.","cause":"Missing modulo or equivalent bounded-index logic.","solution":"Centralize index advancement and validate boundary transitions.","testing_guidance":"Exercise every index boundary repeatedly rather than testing only a single wraparound.","verification_guidance":"Use model-based testing against a simple reference FIFO model.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B25-0001"],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B24-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B25-0003","D12-DS-B90-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Circular queue invariant analysis","publisher":"Derived from bounded queue state","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B25-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Queues","subtopic":"Queue implementation anti-pattern","concept":"Front deletion by shifting","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can repeatedly deleting index zero from a contiguous sequence be a poor queue implementation?","answer":"If deletion requires shifting all remaining elements toward the front, repeated removals can require linear movement per operation.","explanation":"The queue abstraction does not require elements to remain physically adjacent at fixed indexes.","problem":"A workload with many dequeue operations can incur avoidable repeated movement.","solution":"Use a deque, circular buffer, linked queue, or another representation that supports efficient front removal.","tradeoff":"The appropriate alternative depends on locality, allocation, capacity, concurrency, and API requirements.","performance_implication":"Theoretical queue semantics alone do not guarantee efficient implementation.","testing_guidance":"Test workloads with large queues and repeated front removals, while avoiding unsupported empirical performance claims.","verification_guidance":"Inspect the mutation path and determine whether front removal requires proportional element movement.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B24-0001"],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B25-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B17-0001","D12-DS-B26-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Queue representation trade-off analysis","publisher":"Derived from sequence mutation semantics","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B26-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Queues","subtopic":"Linked queue","concept":"Two-ended linked queue","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a linked structure implement efficient FIFO operations?","answer":"A linked queue can maintain references to both the front and rear nodes so that insertion occurs at the rear and removal occurs at the front without traversal.","explanation":"Maintaining both endpoints avoids repeatedly traversing the list to find the insertion point.","correct_example":"The front reference identifies the next removable node while the rear reference identifies where a new node is attached.","edge_case":"After removing the final element, both front and rear references must represent the empty state consistently.","failure_mode":"Updating the front reference but not the rear reference when removing the last element leaves stale state.","solution":"Explicitly handle the singleton-to-empty transition.","testing_guidance":"Test empty-to-singleton, singleton-to-empty, and multi-element transitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify front and rear endpoint invariants after every mutation.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B24-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B26-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Linked queue endpoint analysis","publisher":"Derived from FIFO invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B26-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Queues","subtopic":"Queue state transitions","concept":"Singleton transition","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why is the singleton queue transition important?","answer":"Removing the only element must transition the queue from one-element state to a consistent empty state, including all endpoint metadata.","explanation":"This is a common source of stale rear references and incorrect subsequent insertion behavior.","failure_mode":"The queue reports empty through its front reference while its rear reference still points to the removed node.","cause":"Incomplete endpoint update during final removal.","solution":"When the final element is removed, reset all endpoint state required by the representation.","testing_guidance":"Perform enqueue, dequeue, enqueue repeatedly and inspect the resulting sequence.","verification_guidance":"Verify that an empty queue contains no reachable live queue node from either endpoint.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B26-0001"],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B24-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B90-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Queue state-transition analysis","publisher":"Derived from queue invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B27-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Priority Queues","subtopic":"Abstract semantics","concept":"Priority queue","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a priority queue?","answer":"A priority queue is an abstract data type in which removal selects an element according to priority rather than simply by insertion time.","explanation":"A priority queue can return the minimum, maximum, or another priority-defined element depending on its ordering policy.","principle":"Priority ordering is part of the abstraction; a heap is one common implementation rather than the definition itself.","correct_example":"A scheduler can select the highest-priority eligible task rather than the oldest task.","invalid_example":"Assuming that every priority queue is FIFO among all elements ignores the priority ordering rule.","tradeoff":"Priority queues provide prioritized access but do not generally provide sorted traversal of all elements as their primary operation.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B24-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B27-0002","D12-DS-B28-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_standard_library_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 java.util package summary","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/package-summary.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"standard_library_reference","title":"std::priority_queue","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/priority_queue","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B27-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Priority Queues","subtopic":"Ordering policy","concept":"Min-priority versus max-priority","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between a min-priority queue and a max-priority queue?","answer":"A min-priority queue exposes the smallest-priority value as the next removable element, while a max-priority queue exposes the largest-priority value under the selected ordering.","explanation":"The terminology depends on the ordering convention and comparator.","correct_example":"A min-priority queue can repeatedly expose the smallest numeric key.","invalid_example":"Calling a structure a min-priority queue while its documented comparator causes the largest key to be returned creates a semantic mismatch.","constraint":"The ordering relation must be explicitly defined, especially when custom comparators are used.","testing_guidance":"Test distinct priorities, equal priorities, negative values, and custom ordering.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every removal satisfies the documented priority ordering against all remaining elements.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B27-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B29-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standard_library_reference","title":"std::priority_queue","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/priority_queue","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B28-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Heaps","subtopic":"Heap definition","concept":"Binary heap","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a binary heap?","answer":"A binary heap is a complete-tree-based structure satisfying a heap-order property between each node and its children.","explanation":"In a max-heap each parent is ordered at least as high as its children; in a min-heap the ordering is reversed. The representation is commonly stored compactly in an array.","principle":"The heap-order invariant guarantees access to an extremal element at the root without requiring the entire structure to be sorted.","correct_example":"A max-heap has its maximum element at the root while every parent satisfies the max-heap ordering relation with its children.","invalid_example":"A heap is not required to have all elements globally sorted in array order.","edge_case":"Duplicate keys are valid when the heap ordering relation permits equality.","testing_guidance":"Check the heap-order relation for every parent-child pair after mutations.","verification_guidance":"Verify completeness representation and heap-order invariants independently.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B27-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B28-0002","D12-DS-B28-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standard_library_reference","title":"std::priority_queue","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container/priority_queue","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B28-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Heaps","subtopic":"Array representation","concept":"Implicit binary heap","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can a binary heap be stored in an array without explicit child pointers?","answer":"A complete binary tree can map parent and child positions systematically to array indexes, allowing structural relationships to be derived from indexes.","explanation":"This removes the need for explicit node pointers for the basic heap representation and supports compact storage.","tradeoff":"Index arithmetic is efficient but must be implemented carefully to avoid overflow or incorrect parent-child calculations.","edge_case":"The root has no parent, and the last internal nodes may have fewer than two children.","testing_guidance":"Test root, leaf, one-child, and last-index cases for child and parent calculations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that calculated indexes correspond to the intended complete-tree relationships.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B28-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B28-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Implicit heap representation analysis","publisher":"Derived from complete binary tree representation","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B28-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Heaps","subtopic":"Heap invariant","concept":"Parent-child ordering","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is the core invariant of a binary heap?","answer":"Every parent-child relationship must satisfy the selected heap-order relation.","explanation":"The invariant is local: it does not require every ancestor to be explicitly compared with every descendant because the transitive ordering implied by the invariant provides the required root extremum.","rule":"For a max-heap, each parent must be greater than or equal to its children; for a min-heap, each parent must be less than or equal to its children.","failure_mode":"A single violated parent-child relation means the structure is no longer a valid heap.","solution":"Restore heap order using the appropriate upward or downward adjustment procedure.","testing_guidance":"Check every parent-child pair after each mutation in test builds.","verification_guidance":"Perform a complete heap-order scan when validating a constructed heap.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B28-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B29-0002","D12-DS-B30-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Heap invariant analysis","publisher":"Derived from heap-order definition","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B29-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Heaps","subtopic":"Insertion","concept":"Heap insertion","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How is an element normally inserted into a binary heap?","answer":"Insert the element at the next available position preserving completeness, then restore heap order by moving the element upward as required.","explanation":"The new element begins at a leaf position and can violate the heap relation with its parent.","correct_example":"Insert a key at the end of the heap array and repeatedly exchange or otherwise reposition it with its parent while the ordering rule is violated.","invalid_example":"Inserting at an arbitrary index can break the complete-tree representation.","failure_mode":"Stopping before the parent-child invariant is restored leaves an invalid heap.","cause":"Incomplete upward restoration.","solution":"Continue until the root is reached or the parent-child ordering becomes valid.","performance_implication":"The number of upward adjustments is bounded by the heap height.","testing_guidance":"Test insertion of new extrema, duplicate keys, and already-valid keys.","verification_guidance":"Validate completeness and heap order after every insertion.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B28-0001","D12-DS-B28-0003"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B29-0002","D12-DS-B30-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standard_library_reference","title":"std::priority_queue::push","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/priority_queue/push","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B29-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Heaps","subtopic":"Removal","concept":"Heap extraction","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How is the root normally removed from a binary heap?","answer":"The root is removed, the last element is moved into the root position, and heap order is restored downward as required.","explanation":"Replacing the root with the final element preserves completeness but may violate the ordering relation with its children.","correct_example":"For a max-heap, compare the replacement root with its children and move it toward the larger child while the heap property is violated.","invalid_example":"Removing the root without restoring heap order leaves an invalid structure.","failure_mode":"Selecting the wrong child during downward restoration can preserve one local relation while violating another.","cause":"Incorrect child selection.","solution":"Select the child that should become the parent according to the heap ordering rule.","testing_guidance":"Test removal from empty, singleton, two-element, and multi-level heaps.","verification_guidance":"Check the root extremum and every parent-child relation after extraction.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B28-0001","D12-DS-B28-0003"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B29-0001","D12-DS-B30-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standard_library_reference","title":"std::priority_queue","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container/priority_queue","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B29-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Heaps","subtopic":"Complexity","concept":"Priority queue operation complexity","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What asymptotic operation costs are commonly associated with a binary-heap priority queue?","answer":"A binary-heap priority queue commonly provides constant-time access to the root extremum and logarithmic-time insertion and root extraction, subject to the underlying representation and operation definition.","explanation":"Heap insertion and extraction can traverse a path whose length is proportional to the heap height.","constraint":"The complexity statement assumes a conventional binary heap and appropriate implementation.","performance_implication":"Theoretical complexity does not establish real-world latency or throughput.","testing_guidance":"Do not replace asymptotic reasoning with unsupported benchmark claims.","verification_guidance":"Derive operation cost from the maximum height traversed by the mutation procedure.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B29-0001","D12-DS-B29-0002"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B81-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standard_library_reference","title":"std::priority_queue","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/priority_queue","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B30-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Heaps","subtopic":"Bulk construction","concept":"Heap construction from existing elements","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why can constructing a heap from an existing collection differ from repeated insertion?","answer":"A heap can be constructed by arranging an existing collection and restoring heap order systematically, rather than inserting each element through the full individual insertion path.","explanation":"The construction strategy affects total work and can exploit the structure of the entire input rather than treating every item independently.","correct_example":"A bottom-up heap construction processes internal positions from the lower portion of the tree toward the root.","alternative":"Repeated insertion is simpler and may be appropriate when elements arrive incrementally.","tradeoff":"Bulk construction is suited to an existing collection, while repeated insertion naturally supports streaming arrivals.","testing_guidance":"Compare functional results of bulk construction and repeated insertion using the same input set.","verification_guidance":"Verify that both methods produce structures satisfying the same heap invariant and root-order semantics.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B28-0003"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B30-0002","D12-DS-B30-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standard_library_reference","title":"std::priority_queue constructors","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container/priority_queue/priority_queue","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B30-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Heaps","subtopic":"Priority queue implementation","concept":"Heap versus fully sorted sequence","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why use a heap priority queue instead of maintaining a fully sorted sequence?","answer":"A heap is designed to maintain efficient access to an extremal element while avoiding the requirement that every element be globally ordered after each update.","explanation":"A fully sorted representation may support broader ordered traversal or search patterns, whereas a heap focuses on prioritized extraction.","alternative":"Use a sorted sequence when ordered iteration or operations requiring global order are central to the workload.","tradeoff":"Heap-based priority access trades global sorted order for efficient prioritized mutation and extraction.","constraint":"The correct choice depends on the complete operation workload, not on the priority queue operation alone.","testing_guidance":"Model the actual operation mix before selecting the representation.","verification_guidance":"Check that the selected structure directly supports all required operations with acceptable complexity and semantics.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B27-0001","D12-DS-B29-0003"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B30-0001","D12-DS-B82-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Priority structure selection analysis","publisher":"Derived from heap and ordered-sequence properties","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B30-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Priority Queues","subtopic":"Equal priorities","concept":"Tie handling","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"How should a priority queue handle elements with equal priority?","answer":"Equal-priority behavior must be defined explicitly when application semantics require deterministic or stable ordering; a priority queue abstraction alone does not imply FIFO behavior among ties.","explanation":"Applications sometimes require arrival-order preservation among equal-priority elements, while others only require priority correctness.","correct_example":"Represent each item with priority plus an explicit sequence number when stable tie-breaking is required.","invalid_example":"Assuming equal-priority elements will automatically emerge in insertion order without a documented stability guarantee.","failure_mode":"Application logic depends on an unstated tie-breaking behavior.","cause":"Confusing priority ordering with stability.","solution":"Encode the required secondary ordering explicitly or use a data structure with documented stability semantics.","testing_guidance":"Insert multiple equal-priority elements in known order and verify the required tie policy.","verification_guidance":"Test primary priority ordering separately from secondary tie-breaking behavior.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B27-0002"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B29-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standard_library_reference","title":"std::priority_queue","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/priority_queue","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B30-0004","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Priority Queues","subtopic":"Comparator correctness","concept":"Strict ordering contract","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"What can go wrong when a custom priority comparator is invalid?","answer":"An invalid or inconsistent comparator can make the priority structure's ordering assumptions unreliable and may prevent the implementation from maintaining its intended invariant.","explanation":"Priority structures depend on a coherent ordering relation. The exact comparator requirements depend on the language or library API.","problem":"A comparator with inconsistent results, mutable external state, or an ordering relation that violates the required contract can produce incorrect behavior.","cause":"Comparator semantics do not satisfy the required ordering contract.","solution":"Define a stable, documented ordering relation and test it against representative and adversarial values.","security_implication":"Incorrect ordering of security-critical work can cause priority inversions or incorrect scheduling decisions.","testing_guidance":"Test reflexive/equivalent cases, asymmetric values, duplicates, and transitivity-related cases where applicable.","verification_guidance":"Review comparator requirements against the target library's documented ordering contract.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B27-0002"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B30-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standard_library_reference","title":"std::priority_queue","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/priority_queue","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B30-0005","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Stacks, Queues and Priority Queues","subtopic":"Structure selection","concept":"Workload-driven selection","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should a developer choose among a stack, queue, and priority queue?","answer":"Choose according to the required removal policy: LIFO for stack behavior, FIFO for arrival-order processing, and priority-based selection for priority queues.","explanation":"The required semantic policy should be established before selecting a concrete implementation.","correct_example":"Nested-state processing maps naturally to a stack; arrival-order processing maps to a queue; deadline or severity ordering can map to a priority queue when that policy is appropriate.","invalid_example":"Choosing a priority queue merely because it sounds more advanced when the application requires strict FIFO behavior.","tradeoff":"More sophisticated ordering mechanisms can introduce unnecessary complexity when a simpler structure exactly matches the required semantics.","constraint":"Do not substitute one ordering policy for another without explicitly changing application semantics.","testing_guidance":"Write ordering-specific tests derived from the application's actual requirement.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the data structure's removal rule exactly matches the business or algorithmic invariant.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B21-0001","D12-DS-B24-0001","D12-DS-B27-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B82-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Data structure selection analysis","publisher":"Derived from abstract operation semantics","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

