{"record_id":"D12-DS-B31-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Core abstraction","concept":"Hash table","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a hash table?","answer":"A hash table is an associative data structure that uses a hash function to map keys into storage locations and supports key-based insertion, lookup, and removal.","explanation":"The structure separates key identity from physical storage location. A hash function maps a key to an initial bucket or slot, while collision resolution handles cases where multiple keys map to the same location.","principle":"Hash-based lookup depends on both hashing and equality semantics; a hash value alone does not establish key identity.","correct_example":"Store a user record under a stable user identifier and retrieve it using the same key.","invalid_example":"Treating two keys with the same hash value as automatically equal is incorrect.","constraint":"The implementation must define how collisions and key equality are handled.","security_implication":"Hashing behavior can affect denial-of-service resistance when attackers can influence keys.","testing_guidance":"Test unique keys, duplicate keys, collisions, missing keys, deletion, and resizing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every inserted key can be retrieved using the structure's equality semantics.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B01 through D12-DS-B30"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B31-0002","D12-DS-B32-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Python Data Model: Dictionaries","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/doc-files/coll-overview.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B31-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Associative lookup","concept":"Key-value association","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does a hash map solve?","answer":"A hash map associates each key with a value so that applications can retrieve, insert, update, or remove a value using its key rather than a positional index.","explanation":"The key acts as the logical identity of the entry. Physical placement is an implementation detail determined by hashing and collision management.","correct_example":"Map an account identifier to an account object and retrieve the object by identifier.","invalid_example":"Using a mutable field that changes hash or equality semantics as a key without preserving the required key contract.","tradeoff":"Hash maps optimize key-based access but do not inherently provide sorted-key traversal.","testing_guidance":"Test insert, update, lookup, missing-key lookup, deletion, and duplicate-key behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that updating an existing key changes the intended mapping rather than creating an unintended duplicate key.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B31-0003","D12-DS-B34-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Map Interface","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Map.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"JavaScript Map","publisher":"MDN","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B31-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Complexity model","concept":"Expected versus worst-case lookup","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why is hash-table lookup usually described as expected or average constant time rather than guaranteed constant time?","answer":"Hash-table lookup is commonly expected to be constant time under appropriate hashing and load assumptions, but collisions can cause multiple candidate entries to share storage locations and increase work.","explanation":"The asymptotic guarantee depends on the implementation, collision strategy, hash distribution, and workload assumptions.","principle":"Never convert an expected complexity claim into an unconditional worst-case guarantee.","performance_implication":"Actual performance can depend on hash computation, memory locality, resizing, collision behavior, and implementation details.","security_implication":"Adversarially selected keys can make collision behavior significantly more expensive when the hashing design is vulnerable.","testing_guidance":"Test ordinary distributions and explicitly test collision-heavy cases without claiming empirical performance unless measured.","verification_guidance":"State the computational assumptions behind every complexity claim.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B32-0002","D12-DS-B35-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"std::unordered_map","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/unordered_map","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B32-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hashing","subtopic":"Hash function","concept":"Hash function role","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does a hash function do in a hash table?","answer":"A hash function maps a key to a hash value that the hash-table implementation uses to determine an initial storage location or bucket.","explanation":"The hash value narrows where the structure should search, while equality semantics determine whether a candidate key is actually the requested key.","principle":"Hashing narrows the search space; equality establishes key identity.","correct_example":"Two equal keys must produce compatible hash values under the target map's key contract.","invalid_example":"Using only the hash value as proof that two keys are equal.","security_implication":"Predictable or attacker-manipulable collision patterns can degrade hash-table performance.","testing_guidance":"Test equal keys, unequal keys, repeated keys, and representative collision cases.","verification_guidance":"Verify the target library's documented requirements for hash/equality consistency.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B32-0002","D12-DS-B33-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"std::unordered_map","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/unordered_map","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Map Interface","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Map.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B32-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hashing","subtopic":"Distribution","concept":"Collision distribution","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why does hash distribution matter?","answer":"A hash function that distributes relevant keys effectively reduces concentration into the same buckets and supports the intended performance characteristics of the hash table.","explanation":"A poor distribution can increase collision work even when the table implementation itself is correct.","principle":"Hash-table performance depends on the interaction between the hash function, key distribution, table capacity, and collision strategy.","performance_implication":"Poor distribution can increase lookup and update work.","security_implication":"If users can deliberately choose keys, predictable collision behavior can become a resource-exhaustion vector.","testing_guidance":"Include adversarial and clustered key distributions where the application threat model justifies them.","verification_guidance":"Check the target library's documented hash requirements before replacing or customizing hashing.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B32-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B35-0002","D12-DS-B39-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"std::unordered_set","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/unordered_set","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B32-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hashing","subtopic":"Determinism","concept":"Hash value stability","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"When does hash-value stability matter?","answer":"Hash-value stability matters whenever a data structure or persistent representation depends on the hash value remaining compatible with its equality and storage assumptions.","explanation":"The required stability is context-dependent. In-memory tables may only require consistency during the object's lifetime, while persisted hash-based formats can impose stronger compatibility requirements.","invalid_example":"Assuming that a language's internal hash value is automatically a portable persistent identifier.","tradeoff":"Stable externally defined identifiers may require a deliberate canonicalization and hashing specification rather than reuse of a runtime's internal hash implementation.","security_implication":"Do not expose internal hash values as security-sensitive identifiers unless the design explicitly requires that behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test process boundaries, serialization boundaries, and version transitions when hash values are persisted.","verification_guidance":"Inspect the specification for the exact required stability scope.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B32-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B37-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Hash stability analysis","publisher":"Derived from hash-table key contracts","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B33-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Collision handling","concept":"Collision","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a hash collision?","answer":"A hash collision occurs when distinct keys produce the same hash result or map to the same storage bucket under the table's indexing scheme.","explanation":"Collisions are expected in finite hash spaces and must be handled by the data structure.","principle":"A correct hash table must remain functionally correct even when collisions occur.","correct_example":"Two unequal keys sharing a bucket are separately stored and distinguished using the table's collision and equality mechanism.","invalid_example":"Replacing an existing entry solely because its hash matches another key.","testing_guidance":"Use deliberately colliding test keys when the implementation permits controlled construction of such cases.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unequal colliding keys remain independently retrievable.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B32-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B33-0002","D12-DS-B33-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"std::unordered_map","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/unordered_map","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B33-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Collision handling","concept":"Separate chaining","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is separate chaining?","answer":"Separate chaining resolves collisions by allowing multiple entries associated with the same bucket to coexist in a secondary structure such as a linked or otherwise organized bucket collection.","explanation":"Lookup first identifies the bucket and then searches the candidates using key equality.","tradeoff":"Chaining simplifies collision handling and can tolerate multiple entries per bucket, but the secondary structure introduces additional indirection and memory overhead.","performance_implication":"The cost of a lookup depends on the number and organization of entries sharing a bucket.","security_implication":"An attacker who can force excessive bucket concentration can increase lookup work.","testing_guidance":"Test empty buckets, one-entry buckets, multiple-entry buckets, duplicate keys, and collision-heavy buckets.","verification_guidance":"Verify that equality checking is performed after bucket selection.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B33-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B33-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Hash collision resolution analysis","publisher":"Derived from hash-table architecture","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B33-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Collision handling","concept":"Open addressing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is open addressing?","answer":"Open addressing resolves collisions by storing entries directly within the table and probing alternative slots according to a defined probe sequence.","explanation":"A lookup follows the same probe rules used during insertion until it finds the key, an eligible empty state, or another terminal condition defined by the implementation.","tradeoff":"Open addressing can avoid separate per-entry bucket nodes but requires careful handling of capacity, probing, deletion, and clustering.","edge_case":"Deletion usually requires a representation that preserves the ability of later searches to continue through probe sequences.","testing_guidance":"Test collisions, probe wraparound, full-table behavior, deletion followed by lookup, and insertion after deletion.","verification_guidance":"Verify that insertion and lookup use compatible probe sequences.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B33-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B33-0002","D12-DS-B36-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Open-addressing analysis","publisher":"Derived from hash-table collision-resolution models","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B34-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Maps","subtopic":"Map semantics","concept":"Map uniqueness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What does it mean for a map to have unique keys?","answer":"A map with unique keys represents at most one current value for each key according to the map's key-equality relation.","explanation":"Inserting an equivalent key generally updates, replaces, or otherwise resolves the existing mapping according to the API contract rather than creating an independent mapping for the same logical key.","correct_example":"Calling set with the same key twice results in one current mapping for that key in APIs with update semantics.","invalid_example":"Treating two equal keys as separate logical keys in a unique-key map.","edge_case":"Different object instances may or may not represent the same key depending on the target language's key-equality semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated insertion of equal keys and distinguish equal keys from merely similar values.","verification_guidance":"Verify the target API's documented equality and duplicate-key behavior.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B31-0002"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B34-0002","D12-DS-B37-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Map Interface","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Map.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"JavaScript Map.prototype.set","publisher":"MDN","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map/set","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B34-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Maps","subtopic":"Lookup semantics","concept":"Missing-key behavior","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must missing-key behavior be defined explicitly?","answer":"A lookup for an absent key may return a sentinel, optional value, exception, default, or other result depending on the API contract.","explanation":"Ambiguous missing-key behavior can cause an absent value to be confused with a legitimately stored value.","problem":"Using a nullable or sentinel return without distinguishing absence from a stored sentinel value can create logical errors.","solution":"Use the target API's explicit presence check or a result representation that distinguishes missing from present.","testing_guidance":"Test absent keys, keys mapped to null-like values where permitted, and keys removed after insertion.","verification_guidance":"Verify that absence is distinguishable from every valid stored value.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B31-0002"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B34-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"JavaScript Map","publisher":"MDN","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Map Interface","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Map.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B35-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Capacity management","concept":"Load factor","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is the load factor of a hash table?","answer":"Load factor describes the relationship between the number of stored entries and the table's available bucket or slot capacity under the implementation's model.","explanation":"It is used by many hash-table implementations as part of capacity-management and resizing decisions.","principle":"Increasing occupancy can increase collision or probing work, while excessive capacity can increase memory consumption.","tradeoff":"Lower occupancy can reduce collision pressure at the cost of additional storage; higher occupancy can improve space efficiency but may increase lookup work.","performance_implication":"Load factor is a design parameter rather than a direct measurement of application latency.","testing_guidance":"Test behavior around documented resize thresholds.","verification_guidance":"Check the target implementation's definition of load factor before making implementation-specific assumptions.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B32-0002"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B35-0002","D12-DS-B35-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"std::unordered_set","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/unordered_set","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B35-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Resizing","concept":"Rehashing","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What happens when a hash table is rehashed?","answer":"Rehashing changes the table's bucket arrangement or capacity and redistributes existing entries according to the new table configuration.","explanation":"Because bucket selection depends on table state and hash mapping, existing entries may need to be placed into different buckets.","problem":"Assuming that references, iteration positions, or bucket locations remain unchanged across rehashing can violate an implementation's guarantees.","performance_implication":"A resize or rehash can perform substantial one-time work even when individual operations are usually inexpensive.","testing_guidance":"Test insertion across resize boundaries and verify that all existing entries remain retrievable.","verification_guidance":"After rehashing, compare the logical key-value set before and after the operation.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B35-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B35-0003","D12-DS-B37-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"std::unordered_set","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/unordered_set","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B35-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Capacity planning","concept":"Reserve before bulk insertion","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can reserving capacity before large hash-table insertion be useful?","answer":"Where the target implementation supports reservation, estimating required capacity before bulk insertion can reduce avoidable resizing and rehashing during construction.","explanation":"Reservation is an optimization and capacity-planning mechanism, not a guarantee of a particular runtime improvement.","tradeoff":"Over-reserving consumes more memory; under-reserving may still require growth.","performance_implication":"Any actual improvement must be measured for the target workload and implementation.","testing_guidance":"Test logical correctness independently of whether reservation is used.","verification_guidance":"Verify the target library's documented reserve semantics before relying on them.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B35-0002"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B38-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"std::unordered_set","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/unordered_set","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_PERFORMANCE_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B36-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Deletion","concept":"Deletion semantics","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"Why can deletion be more subtle than lookup in hash tables?","answer":"Deletion must remove the target mapping without preventing future lookups from correctly finding entries that were placed after or alongside the removed entry under the collision strategy.","explanation":"Open addressing in particular may require a special deleted-state representation or another mechanism that preserves probe-chain correctness.","failure_mode":"Replacing a deleted open-addressing slot with an ordinary empty marker can cause later searches to terminate prematurely.","cause":"Confusing permanent emptiness with prior occupancy.","solution":"Use the collision strategy's documented deletion mechanism, such as tombstone-like state or controlled reorganization.","testing_guidance":"Insert colliding keys, delete an earlier key, and verify later colliding keys remain searchable.","verification_guidance":"Run lookup tests across deletion boundaries and inspect the probe or bucket invariant.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B33-0003"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B36-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Open-addressing deletion analysis","publisher":"Derived from probe-chain invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B36-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Deletion","concept":"Logical versus physical deletion","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the distinction between logical and physical deletion in hash-based structures?","answer":"Logical deletion makes an entry unavailable through the public map or set semantics, while physical cleanup also reorganizes or removes its underlying storage representation when required by the implementation.","explanation":"A structure can logically remove an entry while retaining internal metadata temporarily to preserve search correctness.","tradeoff":"Deferred cleanup can simplify correctness but may increase internal occupancy until reorganization occurs.","performance_implication":"Accumulated deleted-state markers can affect probe behavior in implementations that use them.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated insert-delete cycles and verify that removed keys remain absent while unrelated keys remain accessible.","verification_guidance":"Verify both public semantics and the implementation's internal invariants.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B36-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B35-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Hash deletion-state analysis","publisher":"Derived from collision-resolution invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B37-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hashing","subtopic":"Equality contract","concept":"Hash-equality consistency","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What relationship must hold between hashing and equality for hash-based keys?","answer":"Whenever two keys are considered equal under the target map's key-equality relation, they must produce compatible hash results according to that implementation's contract.","explanation":"Otherwise equal keys can be placed into different search regions and become unreachable through normal lookup.","failure_mode":"A key mutates or its hash/equality implementation changes after insertion.","cause":"Violation of the key immutability or hash-equality contract.","solution":"Use stable key state while the key participates in the hash structure and follow the target language's documented contract.","security_implication":"Contract violations can cause security-sensitive authorization or identity lookups to fail or behave unexpectedly.","testing_guidance":"Test equal-key pairs, unequal-key pairs, mutation attempts, and custom key implementations.","verification_guidance":"Review hash and equality implementations together rather than validating either independently.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B32-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B37-0002","D12-DS-B37-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Map Interface","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Map.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Python Data Model","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B37-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hashing","subtopic":"Mutable keys","concept":"Mutable-key failure","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can mutating a key after insertion break a hash map?","answer":"If mutation changes the key's equality or hash behavior after insertion, the entry may remain physically stored according to its old location while future lookup uses the new key state.","explanation":"The entry can become logically unreachable even though it remains physically present.","problem":"The application can observe missing entries, failed removal, duplicate logical keys, or inconsistent behavior.","solution":"Use immutable or effectively immutable key state while the key participates in the hash structure.","security_implication":"Mutable identity keys can undermine access-control or cache correctness when identity fields participate in hashing.","testing_guidance":"Attempt key mutation only where the language permits it and verify the documented consequences.","verification_guidance":"Check every field contributing to equality or hashing for stability during membership.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B37-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B37-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Map Interface","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Map.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Python Data Model","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B37-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Identity and equality","concept":"Identity versus value equality","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why must a data structure distinguish identity semantics from value equality?","answer":"Different languages and structures define key equality differently, so two objects that represent equivalent values may or may not be treated as the same key depending on the API contract.","explanation":"JavaScript Map, Java Map implementations, Python dictionaries, and C++ unordered containers each have language-specific equality and hashing rules.","constraint":"Never assume that object identity, structural equality, and custom value equality are interchangeable.","testing_guidance":"Test distinct objects with equivalent-looking values and confirm the target language's documented behavior.","verification_guidance":"Use the target runtime's language and library specification rather than assumptions derived from another language.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B37-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B40-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"JavaScript Map","publisher":"MDN","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Map Interface","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Map.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B38-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Sets","subtopic":"Set abstraction","concept":"Set","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a set data structure?","answer":"A set is an abstract collection representing membership of distinct elements according to an equality or identity relation.","explanation":"Unlike a map, a conventional set associates no separate application value with each element; the element itself is the membership key.","principle":"Set semantics are about uniqueness and membership, not positional indexing.","correct_example":"A set of user identifiers can answer whether an identifier is present.","invalid_example":"Assuming that a set preserves arbitrary insertion order unless the specific implementation documents such behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test insertion of duplicates, membership, removal, empty state, and iteration where relevant.","verification_guidance":"Verify that equivalent elements cannot coexist when the set defines them as the same member.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B31-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B38-0002","D12-DS-B38-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"std::unordered_set","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/unordered_set","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"JavaScript Keyed Collections","publisher":"MDN","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Keyed_collections","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B38-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Sets","subtopic":"Hash-set operations","concept":"Membership and uniqueness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Which operations are central to a hash set?","answer":"Core hash-set operations include insertion, membership testing, removal, size or emptiness queries, and iteration according to the implementation's ordering guarantees.","explanation":"The set's defining property is that equivalent members are represented once according to the set's equality semantics.","correct_example":"Adding the same logical identifier twice leaves one member in a unique-key set.","invalid_example":"Counting repeated insertion of an equivalent member as two independent members.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate insertion and membership before and after removal.","verification_guidance":"Compare the logical member set before and after each mutation.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B38-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B39-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"std::unordered_set","publisher":"cppreference","url":"https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/unordered_set","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B38-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Sets","subtopic":"Map versus set","concept":"Map-set distinction","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the fundamental difference between a map and a set?","answer":"A map associates keys with values, whereas a set represents membership of distinct elements.","explanation":"A set can conceptually be viewed as a collection where each element itself serves as the membership key, while a map maintains a separate value associated with each key.","correct_example":"Use a set to answer whether a permission identifier exists; use a map to retrieve metadata associated with that identifier.","invalid_example":"Using a map with arbitrary placeholder values when only membership is required without considering whether a set better expresses the invariant.","tradeoff":"The choice should express application semantics and required operations rather than merely data representation preference.","testing_guidance":"Test membership-only and key-to-value workloads separately.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the selected abstraction directly represents the required invariant.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B38-0001"],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B31-0002"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B40-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/doc-files/coll-overview.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B39-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables and Sets","subtopic":"Iteration order","concept":"Ordering guarantees","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must iteration order be treated as an explicit contract?","answer":"A hash-based collection may or may not guarantee insertion, sorted, stable, or implementation-defined iteration order; the behavior must be taken from the target API contract.","explanation":"Different languages and collection implementations make different ordering guarantees. Python dictionaries preserve insertion order as a language guarantee, while other hash-based structures may not provide the same guarantee.","invalid_example":"Writing application logic that depends on hash-table iteration order without checking the target API specification.","problem":"An implementation or language-version change can invalidate an unstated ordering assumption.","solution":"Use an explicitly ordered structure or documented ordering guarantee when order is semantically required.","testing_guidance":"Test order only when the target API documents an order guarantee.","verification_guidance":"Record the target language/library/version when an ordering property is relied upon.","language":"language-neutral","version":"Python 3.7+ for the cited language guarantee; implementation-specific elsewhere","version_range":"Python >=3.7","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B38-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B39-0002","D12-DS-B40-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Python Data Model: Dictionaries","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Map Interface","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Map.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH