{"record_id":"D12-DS-B39-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables","subtopic":"Security","concept":"Hash collision denial of service","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can hash collisions become a denial-of-service concern?","answer":"If an attacker can cause unusually large numbers of relevant keys to concentrate into the same hash-table regions, lookup and update work can increase and consume disproportionate resources.","explanation":"The practical risk depends on the language runtime, hashing algorithm, attacker control over keys, collision strategy, workload, and available mitigations.","principle":"Hash-based data structures should be evaluated against adversarial input when untrusted keys can influence workload size or distribution.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive services should consider collision behavior as part of resource-exhaustion analysis.","constraint":"Do not claim that every hash table is vulnerable or that a specific attack is practical without evidence for the target implementation.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled collision-oriented tests in an isolated test environment and measure only when empirical evidence is required.","verification_guidance":"Review the target runtime's documented hash behavior and security guidance.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B32-0002","D12-DS-B33-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B35-0001","D12-DS-B40-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_security_reasoning","title":"Hash collision resource-exhaustion analysis","publisher":"Derived from hash-table collision behavior","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"SECURITY_REASONING","confidence":"MEDIUM_HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B40-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Selection","subtopic":"Map and set selection","concept":"Hash versus ordered associative structure","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should a hash-based map or set be considered instead of an ordered tree-based structure?","answer":"A hash-based structure is appropriate when efficient key-based membership or lookup is central and sorted-key operations are not fundamental requirements; an ordered structure is appropriate when ordering or range-oriented operations are first-class requirements.","explanation":"The choice follows the required operations, ordering semantics, complexity expectations, memory characteristics, and implementation constraints.","correct_example":"Use a hash set for fast membership checks when no sorted traversal is required.","invalid_example":"Choosing a hash set for a workload whose primary requirement is ordered range traversal.","tradeoff":"Hash structures generally emphasize key lookup and membership, while ordered structures provide ordering semantics at different complexity and memory trade-offs.","testing_guidance":"Enumerate required operations before selecting the structure and verify each against the candidate API.","verification_guidance":"Create an operation matrix containing lookup, insertion, deletion, ordering, range queries, iteration, and memory requirements.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B38-0003","D12-DS-B39-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B40-0002","D12-DS-B82-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/doc-files/coll-overview.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B40-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Selection","subtopic":"Cross-language semantics","concept":"Same abstraction different guarantees","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should a software engineering system avoid assuming that maps and sets behave identically across languages?","answer":"Map and set abstractions recur across languages, but equality, hashing, ordering, mutation, iteration, concurrency, and version guarantees differ between implementations.","explanation":"For example, Python dictionaries have a documented insertion-order guarantee, Java distinguishes HashMap, LinkedHashMap, and TreeMap, and JavaScript Map specifies insertion-order iteration.","constraint":"Cross-language code generation must bind an abstract data-structure requirement to the target language's actual API contract.","correct_example":"Represent the abstract requirement as key-value lookup, then select the target language's map implementation based on required ordering and equality semantics.","invalid_example":"Generating code for one language by mechanically translating the API assumptions of another.","testing_guidance":"Run language-specific contract tests for equality, missing keys, mutation, ordering, and duplicate-key behavior.","verification_guidance":"Attach language and library version metadata to generated implementation decisions.","language":"multi-language","version":"Python 3.7+; Java SE 24; ECMAScript current specification","version_range":"Target-version dependent","dependencies":["D12-DS-B37-0003","D12-DS-B39-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B40-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Python Data Model","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 Collections Framework Overview","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/doc-files/coll-overview.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"JavaScript Keyed Collections","publisher":"MDN","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Keyed_collections","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B40-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Hash Tables, Maps and Sets","subtopic":"Verification and testing","concept":"Reference-model testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How can a hash-based data structure be tested against a simpler reference model?","answer":"Maintain a simple specification-level model, such as a linear collection of key-value pairs or a mathematically defined member set, and compare observable behavior after generated operation sequences.","explanation":"Reference-model testing separates correctness of the optimized structure from correctness of the test oracle.","correct_example":"Generate insert, update, lookup, delete, and membership operations and compare the optimized structure's logical state with the reference model.","invalid_example":"Testing only final size while ignoring key presence, values, ordering guarantees, and deletion semantics.","edge_case":"Duplicate keys, missing keys, collisions, deletion after collisions, resizing, empty state, and large operation sequences.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic seeds for reproducible generated sequences and include negative cases.","verification_guidance":"Compare both return values and complete logical state after every operation where practical.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B31-0001","D12-DS-B36-0001","D12-DS-B39-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B90-0003","D12-DS-B91-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"verification_method","title":"Reference-model testing methodology","publisher":"Derived from data-structure verification principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

