{"record_id":"D12-DS-B41-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Core abstraction","concept":"Tree","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a tree data structure?","answer":"A tree is a hierarchical data structure composed of nodes connected by parent-child relationships and organized without cycles in the conventional rooted-tree model.","explanation":"A rooted tree has one distinguished root, and every non-root node has one parent. Nodes may have zero or more children.","principle":"Tree structure represents hierarchical relationships rather than a linear sequence.","correct_example":"A directory hierarchy can be represented as a rooted tree where directories and files are nodes.","invalid_example":"A conventional rooted tree cannot contain a cycle from a descendant back to an ancestor.","edge_case":"An empty tree and a single-node tree must have explicitly defined representation semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, singleton, shallow, deep, wide, and highly unbalanced trees.","verification_guidance":"Verify parent-child relationships and absence of cycles under the chosen tree model.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B01 through D12-DS-B40"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B41-0002","D12-DS-B42-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Tree data structure fundamentals","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B41-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Terminology","concept":"Root, parent, child, leaf, depth and height","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What are the fundamental terms used to describe a rooted tree?","answer":"The root is the distinguished starting node; a parent directly contains or points to a child; a child is directly descended from a parent; a leaf has no children; depth measures distance from the root under the chosen convention; and height measures the longest downward path under the chosen convention.","explanation":"Depth and height conventions can count edges or nodes, so the definition must be stated explicitly when numerical values matter.","constraint":"Do not mix edge-count and node-count conventions within one analysis.","testing_guidance":"Use a fixed reference tree and verify every reported depth and height against the declared convention.","verification_guidance":"Record the depth/height convention with complexity and structural claims.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B41-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B41-0003","D12-DS-B42-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Tree terminology","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B41-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Structural properties","concept":"Subtree","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a subtree?","answer":"A subtree is a node together with its descendants, interpreted as a tree rooted at that node under the applicable tree model.","explanation":"Subtrees allow recursive reasoning about tree algorithms because many operations can be defined in terms of a node and its child subtrees.","principle":"Recursive tree structure supports recursive definitions and algorithms.","correct_example":"The descendants of a node can be treated as a separate subtree for traversal or recursive search.","invalid_example":"Treating an arbitrary collection of unrelated nodes as a subtree without preserving parent-child structure.","testing_guidance":"Test recursive operations on the entire tree and on each child subtree.","verification_guidance":"Verify that subtree operations preserve the same structural invariants as the complete tree.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B41-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B43-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Tree recursion fundamentals","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B42-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Binary Trees","subtopic":"Definition","concept":"Binary tree","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a binary tree?","answer":"A binary tree is a tree in which each node has at most two child positions, conventionally called left and right.","explanation":"The two child positions are distinct, so left and right placement can carry semantic meaning.","correct_example":"A node may have only a left child, only a right child, both children, or no children.","invalid_example":"A node with three direct children is not a node in a conventional binary tree.","constraint":"At most two child positions exist per node.","testing_guidance":"Test zero-, one-, and two-child nodes independently.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no node contains more than the allowed number of child references.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B41-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B42-0002","D12-DS-B43-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Binary tree definition","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B42-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Binary Trees","subtopic":"Structural variants","concept":"Full, complete and perfect binary trees","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the distinction among full, complete, and perfect binary trees?","answer":"A full binary tree constrains each node to have either zero or two children; a complete binary tree fills levels from left to right with the final level potentially incomplete; and a perfect binary tree has every internal node with two children and all leaves at the same depth.","explanation":"These properties describe different structural constraints and should not be treated as synonyms.","invalid_example":"Calling every balanced-looking binary tree perfect is incorrect because perfectness requires all leaves to share the same depth and every internal node to have two children.","constraint":"Use the exact structural definition required by the algorithm or proof.","testing_guidance":"Construct positive and negative examples for each property.","verification_guidance":"Check node-child counts, level occupancy, and leaf depths separately.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B42-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Binary tree properties","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B42-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Binary Trees","subtopic":"Degeneracy","concept":"Skewed binary tree","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What is a skewed binary tree?","answer":"A skewed binary tree is a highly unbalanced binary tree in which nodes predominantly follow one child direction, causing the tree height to approach the number of nodes.","explanation":"A binary search tree can become skewed when insertion order interacts poorly with an unbalanced insertion policy.","performance_implication":"Operations whose cost depends on tree height can degrade from logarithmic-scale behavior to linear-scale behavior in a degenerate tree.","correct_example":"Repeatedly inserting keys in increasing order into a simple unbalanced binary search tree can create a right-skewed structure.","invalid_example":"Assuming that every binary search tree automatically has logarithmic height.","testing_guidance":"Test sorted, reverse-sorted, random, and duplicate-heavy insertion sequences.","verification_guidance":"Measure or calculate height from the actual structure rather than assuming balance.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B44-0002","D12-DS-B45-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Binary search tree analysis","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B43-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Traversal","concept":"Depth-first traversal","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is depth-first traversal of a tree?","answer":"Depth-first traversal explores a node and recursively or iteratively explores descendants before moving to the next sibling subtree.","explanation":"For binary trees, preorder, inorder, and postorder are common depth-first traversal orders.","principle":"Traversal order is a semantic choice and should match the operation being performed.","correct_example":"A postorder traversal can process children before processing their parent.","invalid_example":"Calling a level-by-level traversal depth-first traversal.","testing_guidance":"Use small trees where each traversal order produces a visibly different sequence.","verification_guidance":"Compare produced order with the formal traversal rule for every node.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B41-0003"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B43-0002","D12-DS-B43-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Tree traversal","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B43-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Binary Trees","subtopic":"Traversal","concept":"Preorder traversal","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is preorder traversal?","answer":"Preorder traversal processes the current node before recursively processing its left and right subtrees, in that order for a conventional binary tree.","explanation":"The sequence is root, left subtree, right subtree.","correct_example":"For a root A with left B and right C, preorder begins A, B, C.","invalid_example":"Processing both children before the root is not preorder traversal.","testing_guidance":"Test empty trees, singleton trees, missing-left-child trees, missing-right-child trees, and complete trees.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each node appears before all descendants in its subtrees.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B43-0001"],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B42-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B43-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Binary tree traversal","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B43-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Binary Trees","subtopic":"Traversal","concept":"Inorder and postorder traversal","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How do inorder and postorder traversal differ?","answer":"Inorder processes left subtree, current node, then right subtree; postorder processes left subtree, right subtree, then current node.","explanation":"Inorder has special significance for binary search trees because it visits keys in sorted order when the BST invariant is valid.","correct_example":"For a BST, inorder traversal produces nondecreasing key order under the tree's ordering relation.","invalid_example":"Assuming postorder traversal of a BST automatically produces sorted keys.","tradeoff":"Traversal choice depends on whether parent-before-child, sorted-key, or child-before-parent processing is required.","testing_guidance":"Use the same tree and independently verify each traversal against its formal ordering rule.","verification_guidance":"For BST inorder, verify both traversal order and BST invariant before interpreting the output as sorted.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B43-0001"],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B42-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B44-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Binary search tree traversal","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B44-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Binary Search Trees","subtopic":"Invariant","concept":"Binary search tree ordering","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is the defining ordering property of a binary search tree?","answer":"A binary search tree maintains an ordering relation in which keys in the left subtree precede the node key and keys in the right subtree follow the node key, subject to the implementation's duplicate-key policy.","explanation":"The exact strictness and duplicate handling must be defined by the implementation.","principle":"BST operations are correct only when the tree's ordering invariant is preserved.","correct_example":"For a strict-unique-key BST, every key in the left subtree is less than the node key and every key in the right subtree is greater.","invalid_example":"Placing an arbitrary smaller key into the right subtree violates the BST invariant.","edge_case":"Duplicate keys require an explicit policy such as rejection, counting, or consistent placement.","testing_guidance":"Validate every node against the permitted key range inherited from its ancestors.","verification_guidance":"Use range-based invariant checking rather than checking only immediate children.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B44-0002","D12-DS-B44-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Binary search tree invariant","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B44-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Binary Search Trees","subtopic":"Search","concept":"BST search","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How does search work in a binary search tree?","answer":"BST search compares the target key with the current node and recursively or iteratively follows the left or right subtree according to the ordering relation until the key is found or the search reaches an empty position.","explanation":"The ordering invariant allows the search to eliminate an entire subtree at each comparison.","correct_example":"If the target is smaller than the current key, search continues only in the left subtree under the standard ordering policy.","invalid_example":"Searching both subtrees at every node ignores the information supplied by the BST invariant.","failure_mode":"A corrupted ordering invariant can cause search to follow the wrong branch and miss an existing key.","cause":"Incorrect mutation or construction of the BST.","solution":"Validate and repair or rebuild the structure before relying on ordered search.","performance_implication":"Search cost depends on tree height; a degenerate tree can cause linear-scale traversal.","testing_guidance":"Test root, leaf, missing, minimum, maximum, duplicate, and skewed-tree searches.","verification_guidance":"Check the BST invariant before using complexity assumptions.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B44-0003","D12-DS-B45-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Binary search tree search","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B44-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Binary Search Trees","subtopic":"Insertion","concept":"BST insertion","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How is a key inserted into a conventional binary search tree?","answer":"The insertion follows the BST search path until an empty child position is reached, then creates or attaches the new node according to the duplicate-key policy.","explanation":"Insertion preserves the existing ordering invariant by following comparisons from the root.","correct_example":"A key smaller than the current node continues left until an available position is found.","invalid_example":"Attaching a new key without following the ordering path can make future searches incorrect.","edge_case":"Duplicate keys require explicit behavior rather than accidental placement.","testing_guidance":"Test insertion at root, leaf, internal positions, duplicate keys, and adversarial ordered sequences.","verification_guidance":"Run an invariant check after every insertion in development or verification builds.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B44-0002","D12-DS-B45-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Binary search tree insertion","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B45-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Binary Search Trees","subtopic":"Deletion","concept":"BST deletion cases","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What structural cases must BST deletion handle?","answer":"BST deletion must handle a leaf, a node with one child, and a node with two children; the root can additionally require special handling depending on representation.","explanation":"The two-child case requires selecting a replacement that preserves the ordering invariant, commonly an adjacent ordered predecessor or successor under the chosen policy.","correct_example":"A two-child node can be replaced by a suitable successor from its right subtree, followed by removal of that successor from its original position.","invalid_example":"Replacing a two-child node with an arbitrary descendant can violate the BST ordering invariant.","failure_mode":"Incorrect replacement selection or reconnection can lose a subtree or break ordering.","cause":"Incomplete handling of structural deletion cases.","solution":"Implement and test each case independently and then verify the complete BST invariant.","testing_guidance":"Construct separate tests for leaf, one-child, two-child, root, minimum, maximum, and repeated deletion cases.","verification_guidance":"Validate key membership and global ordering after each deletion.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B44-0003"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B45-0002","D12-DS-B45-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Binary search tree deletion","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B45-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Binary Search Trees","subtopic":"Balance","concept":"Height-dependent performance","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can an ordinary BST have poor worst-case performance?","answer":"An ordinary BST does not necessarily maintain a bounded height relative to the logarithm of its node count, so unfavorable insertion patterns can produce a height proportional to the number of nodes.","explanation":"Search, insertion, and deletion paths follow tree height. A skewed tree therefore loses the performance benefits associated with shallow balanced trees.","correct_example":"Increasing-key insertion into an unbalanced BST can create a chain-like tree.","invalid_example":"Claiming logarithmic worst-case search for every ordinary BST without a balance invariant.","performance_implication":"Worst-case path length can become linear in the number of nodes.","testing_guidance":"Include sorted and reverse-sorted insertion sequences in complexity regression tests.","verification_guidance":"Calculate actual tree height and identify whether a balancing invariant is present.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B42-0003","D12-DS-B44-0002"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B45-0003","D12-DS-B46-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Binary search tree complexity","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B45-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Binary Search Trees","subtopic":"Deletion verification","concept":"Membership preservation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified after BST deletion?","answer":"The deleted key should satisfy the documented absence semantics, all keys intended to remain should remain reachable, and the complete BST ordering invariant should still hold.","explanation":"Checking only that the deleted node is no longer present is insufficient because deletion can accidentally disconnect or reorder unrelated subtrees.","failure_mode":"A deletion implementation removes the target but silently loses a child subtree.","cause":"Incorrect pointer or reference reconnection.","solution":"Compare the expected remaining key set with an independent reference model and validate the global tree invariant.","testing_guidance":"Use randomized deletion sequences against a reference ordered set when practical.","verification_guidance":"Combine membership comparison with structural invariant checking.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B45-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B40-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"verification_method","title":"Tree invariant and reference-model verification","publisher":"Derived from BST correctness properties","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B46-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Balanced Trees","subtopic":"Need for balancing","concept":"Balance invariant","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why are balanced search trees useful?","answer":"Balanced search trees maintain a structural invariant that limits tree height, preserving efficient height-dependent operations across a wider range of insertion and deletion patterns.","explanation":"The exact height guarantee depends on the balancing scheme, such as AVL or red-black balancing.","principle":"A performance guarantee must be supported by an explicit structural invariant rather than assumed from the data type name.","tradeoff":"Balancing adds mutation complexity and metadata or restructuring work in exchange for stronger height guarantees.","testing_guidance":"Test operations that trigger every documented balancing case.","verification_guidance":"Validate both the search-tree ordering invariant and the balancing invariant.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B45-0002"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B46-0002","D12-DS-B47-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Balanced search tree fundamentals","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B46-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Balanced Trees","subtopic":"AVL trees","concept":"AVL tree","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an AVL tree?","answer":"An AVL tree is a self-balancing binary search tree that maintains a height-balance condition for each node, with rebalancing performed after relevant mutations.","explanation":"The balance condition limits subtree-height differences according to the AVL invariant.","tradeoff":"AVL trees provide a strong height-balance discipline but require rotations and balance metadata management during updates.","correct_example":"An insertion that violates the local AVL balance condition triggers the appropriate rotation or rotation sequence.","invalid_example":"Calling an arbitrary BST with a roughly balanced initial shape an AVL tree without maintaining the AVL invariant after mutations.","testing_guidance":"Test single and double rotation cases for insertion and deletion.","verification_guidance":"Check BST ordering and AVL balance conditions after every mutation.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B46-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B46-0003","D12-DS-B47-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"AVL tree fundamentals","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B46-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Balanced Trees","subtopic":"Rotations","concept":"Tree rotation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a tree rotation?","answer":"A tree rotation is a local restructuring operation that changes parent-child relationships while preserving the in-order ordering of a binary search tree.","explanation":"Rotations are fundamental to many self-balancing search trees.","principle":"A correct rotation changes shape without changing the ordered sequence of keys.","correct_example":"A left rotation can move a right child upward while retaining the BST in-order sequence.","invalid_example":"A restructuring that changes relative key order is not a valid BST-preserving rotation.","failure_mode":"Incorrect pointer reassignment can lose a subtree or create a cycle.","cause":"Rotation implementation does not preserve all affected child references.","solution":"Treat rotation as a formally specified local transformation and test every affected edge.","security_implication":"Corrupted tree topology can cause incorrect authorization or policy lookup results when a tree stores security-relevant mappings.","testing_guidance":"Test rotation with empty subtrees, non-empty transferred subtrees, root rotations, and repeated rotations.","verification_guidance":"Compare inorder output before and after rotation and validate absence of cycles.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B46-0002","D12-DS-B47-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Tree rotation fundamentals","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B47-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Balanced Trees","subtopic":"Red-black trees","concept":"Red-black tree","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a red-black tree?","answer":"A red-black tree is a self-balancing binary search tree that assigns a color state to nodes and maintains a set of structural invariants that bound tree height.","explanation":"Its balancing rules permit local recoloring and rotations after updates.","tradeoff":"The balancing rules are more complex than an unbalanced BST but support strong asymptotic guarantees for ordered operations.","invalid_example":"Assuming that node colors alone make an arbitrary BST balanced.","testing_guidance":"Test insertion and deletion cases involving recoloring and rotations.","verification_guidance":"Validate both BST ordering and all red-black invariants defined by the implementation.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B46-0001","D12-DS-B46-0003"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B47-0002","D12-DS-B47-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Red-black tree fundamentals","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B47-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Balanced Trees","subtopic":"Ordered operations","concept":"Ordered map and set","knowledge_type":"APPLICATION","question":"Why are balanced search trees useful for ordered maps and sets?","answer":"Balanced search trees maintain key ordering while supporting search, insertion, deletion, and ordered traversal with height-dependent complexity that remains bounded by the balancing invariant.","explanation":"They are useful when an application requires both key-based access and ordered operations such as finding neighboring keys or traversing keys in sorted order.","correct_example":"An ordered map can use a balanced search tree when range and predecessor/successor queries are first-class requirements.","invalid_example":"Replacing an ordered structure with an unordered hash structure when sorted traversal is semantically required.","tradeoff":"Balanced trees generally require more structural maintenance than simple hash-based structures but preserve ordering semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test exact lookup, predecessor, successor, minimum, maximum, range traversal, insertion, and deletion.","verification_guidance":"Verify sorted traversal and balancing invariants together.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B46-0001","D12-DS-B47-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B40-0001","D12-DS-B48-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Java SE 24 SortedMap","publisher":"Oracle","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/SortedMap.html","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_PLUS_ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B47-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Balanced Trees","subtopic":"Tree correctness","concept":"Ordered traversal as a consistency check","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How can ordered traversal help test a binary search tree?","answer":"For a BST whose duplicate policy is known, an inorder traversal can be checked against the expected ordering relation to detect structural ordering violations.","explanation":"The traversal provides an observable projection of the tree's key ordering, but it should not be the only invariant check because some structural defects may require additional checks.","correct_example":"Compare inorder output against a sorted reference sequence after a randomized mutation sequence.","invalid_example":"Checking only that the traversal is sorted while ignoring disconnected nodes, cycles, or metadata invariants.","edge_case":"Duplicate-key policies can require non-strict rather than strict ordering checks.","testing_guidance":"Combine inorder comparison with node-count, membership, reachability, and cycle checks.","verification_guidance":"Use an independent reference model for expected membership and ordering.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B43-0003","D12-DS-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B45-0003","D12-DS-B46-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"verification_method","title":"BST invariant testing","publisher":"Derived from BST ordering properties","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B48-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Ordered queries","concept":"Predecessor and successor","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What are predecessor and successor queries in an ordered tree?","answer":"For a key in an ordered tree, its predecessor is the greatest key that precedes it under the ordering relation, while its successor is the smallest key that follows it.","explanation":"These operations are useful for ordered navigation and can often be derived efficiently from a BST or balanced ordered tree.","correct_example":"The successor of a key is the next key encountered in inorder ordering when such a key exists.","invalid_example":"Selecting an arbitrary nearby node rather than the immediate ordered neighbor.","edge_case":"Minimum has no predecessor and maximum has no successor unless the API defines sentinel behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test minimum, maximum, root, internal nodes, absent keys where supported, and duplicate-key policies.","verification_guidance":"Compare results with an independently sorted reference collection.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B43-0003","D12-DS-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B48-0002","D12-DS-B49-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Ordered tree neighbor analysis","publisher":"Derived from BST ordering","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B48-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Range queries","concept":"Ordered range traversal","knowledge_type":"APPLICATION","question":"Why can an ordered tree support range-oriented queries naturally?","answer":"Because keys are maintained according to an ordering invariant, traversal can selectively explore portions of the tree that may contain keys within a requested interval.","explanation":"Subtree ordering allows entire regions outside the requested range to be skipped when the implementation supports such pruning.","correct_example":"A range query for keys between lower and upper bounds can avoid traversing subtrees whose keys are entirely outside the interval.","invalid_example":"Scanning every node when the goal is specifically to exploit ordered-tree pruning without considering the tree's ordering invariant.","tradeoff":"Range-query efficiency depends on tree height, output size, and the precise traversal algorithm.","testing_guidance":"Test empty ranges, singleton ranges, full ranges, open/closed boundary semantics, and ranges outside all stored keys.","verification_guidance":"Compare returned keys with an independently sorted reference collection and verify boundary inclusion rules.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B47-0002","D12-DS-B48-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Ordered tree range-query analysis","publisher":"Derived from ordered-tree invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B48-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Range-query edge cases","concept":"Boundary semantics","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must range-query boundary semantics be explicit?","answer":"A range can include or exclude its lower and upper endpoints, and implementations must define whether the interval is closed, open, or mixed.","explanation":"Off-by-one and comparator mistakes frequently occur when translating mathematical ranges into code.","correct_example":"A closed interval includes both endpoints when they exist; a half-open interval includes one boundary and excludes the other.","invalid_example":"Assuming all APIs use the same endpoint convention.","testing_guidance":"Test values exactly equal to both boundaries and values immediately inside and outside them.","verification_guidance":"Represent the range contract explicitly in tests and query APIs.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B48-0002"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B49-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Range boundary analysis","publisher":"Derived from ordered-query semantics","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B49-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Order statistics","concept":"Rank and selection","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can tree metadata support rank or selection queries?","answer":"A search tree can augment nodes with subtree-size or related metadata so that rank and selection operations can navigate toward the desired order position without traversing every key.","explanation":"The metadata changes the data structure's invariant and must be updated consistently after insertion, deletion, and rotations.","tradeoff":"Augmentation can provide additional queries but increases implementation complexity and mutation-maintenance requirements.","failure_mode":"Stale subtree-size metadata produces incorrect rank or selection results even when the BST ordering itself remains valid.","cause":"Metadata was not updated during structural mutation.","solution":"Treat augmentation metadata as a formal invariant and validate it recursively.","testing_guidance":"Test rank/select after every mutation and especially after rotations.","verification_guidance":"Recompute subtree metadata independently during verification and compare it with stored metadata.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B46-0003"],"prerequisites":["D12-DS-B44-0001"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B49-0002","D12-DS-B49-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Augmented search trees","publisher":"OpenDSA","url":"https://opendsa-server.cs.vt.edu/ODSA/Books/Everything/html/","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B49-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Augmented trees","concept":"Metadata invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should augmented tree metadata be verified?","answer":"For every node, stored metadata should equal the value derived independently from its children and local node state according to the augmentation definition.","explanation":"Local metadata checks can expose mutation bugs that ordinary membership tests do not detect.","correct_example":"For subtree-size metadata, verify size(node) equals one plus the sizes of its non-empty child subtrees under the chosen convention.","invalid_example":"Checking only the root metadata while ignoring stale values deeper in the tree.","testing_guidance":"Recursively recompute metadata after random mutation sequences.","verification_guidance":"Use an independent recomputation rather than the same update function used by the implementation.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B49-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B49-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"verification_method","title":"Augmented-tree invariant verification","publisher":"Derived from metadata invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B49-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Mutation complexity","concept":"Augmentation maintenance cost","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the cost of augmenting a search tree with additional metadata?","answer":"Augmentation requires every structural mutation that changes relevant subtrees to maintain the additional metadata, increasing implementation complexity and verification obligations.","explanation":"The benefit is support for additional queries that would otherwise require more traversal.","tradeoff":"Additional metadata can improve query efficiency while increasing mutation work, memory use, and correctness risk.","performance_implication":"Do not claim a query improvement without considering the cost of maintaining metadata.","testing_guidance":"Include metadata consistency checks in mutation regression tests.","verification_guidance":"Document every operation that can invalidate each metadata field.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B49-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B49-0002","D12-DS-B50-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Augmented tree trade-off analysis","publisher":"Derived from tree metadata invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B50-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Implementation safety","concept":"Pointer/reference integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is pointer or reference integrity important in mutable trees?","answer":"Incorrect child, parent, or root references can cause lost data, cycles, invalid traversal, memory-safety problems in unsafe languages, or denial-of-service behavior through non-terminating traversal.","explanation":"Tree mutations change multiple relationships simultaneously, so partial updates can corrupt topology.","failure_mode":"A rotation or deletion updates one reference but fails to update another required edge.","cause":"Non-atomic or incomplete structural mutation logic.","solution":"Define mutation preconditions and postconditions and verify topology after complex mutations.","security_implication":"Malformed externally controlled tree data should not be allowed to trigger unbounded traversal or unsafe memory access.","testing_guidance":"Test root changes, leaf changes, rotations, deletion, malformed structures, cycles, and deep nesting where relevant.","verification_guidance":"Check reachability, parent-child consistency, node count, cycle absence, and required ordering invariants.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B46-0003","D12-DS-B45-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B50-0002","D12-DS-B50-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_security_reasoning","title":"Mutable tree integrity analysis","publisher":"Derived from tree mutation invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"SECURITY_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B50-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Traversal robustness","concept":"Deep-tree traversal","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why can very deep trees require special traversal handling?","answer":"Recursive traversal of a sufficiently deep tree can exceed the available call-stack depth in environments with bounded call stacks, while iterative traversal can move the traversal state into explicit data structures.","explanation":"The exact recursion limit and behavior are runtime-specific.","alternative":"Use an explicit stack or another iterative traversal strategy when depth may exceed safe recursion limits.","tradeoff":"Iterative traversal requires explicit state management but can avoid dependence on call-stack depth.","testing_guidance":"Test depth boundaries appropriate to the target runtime and environment.","verification_guidance":"Ensure traversal visits each reachable node exactly as specified and terminates on valid acyclic input.","language":"language-neutral","version":"Target-runtime dependent","version_range":"Target-runtime dependent","dependencies":["D12-DS-B43-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B50-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Deep tree traversal analysis","publisher":"Derived from traversal and runtime stack constraints","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B50-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Trees","subtopic":"Structure selection","concept":"Unbalanced versus balanced search tree","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should a balanced search tree be considered instead of an ordinary BST?","answer":"A balanced search tree should be considered when predictable height-dependent performance is required across varied insertion and deletion patterns and ordered operations remain important.","explanation":"An ordinary BST may be simpler but has no inherent balance guarantee. A balanced variant adds a structural invariant that limits height.","correct_example":"Choose a balanced ordered structure when the workload requires ordered traversal and robust asymptotic bounds under adversarial update sequences.","invalid_example":"Choosing an ordinary BST while assuming input order will always remain favorable.","tradeoff":"Ordinary BSTs can be simpler to implement; balanced trees require more complex mutation logic and invariant maintenance.","constraint":"The selection must consider ordering requirements, mutation frequency, worst-case guarantees, memory, and available library implementations.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate sorted, reverse-sorted, duplicate-heavy, and randomized workloads at the semantic level.","verification_guidance":"Verify the target implementation's documented complexity and balancing guarantees rather than inferring them from its name.","language":"language-neutral","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B45-0002","D12-DS-B46-0001"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B47-0002","D12-DS-B82-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reasoning","title":"Search-tree selection analysis","publisher":"Derived from BST and balanced-tree invariants","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

