{"record_id":"D12-DS-B51-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Heap invariant","concept":"Heap-order invariant","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What invariant must an array-based binary heap maintain?","answer":"For a min-heap, every parent key must be less than or equal to the keys of its existing children; for a max-heap, the ordering is reversed.","explanation":"The heap invariant guarantees that the root contains the globally minimum or maximum element according to the heap ordering.","principle":"Local parent-child ordering is sufficient to expose the global extremum at the root.","correct_example":"A min-heap with root 2 and children 5 and 7 satisfies the parent-child ordering requirement.","invalid_example":"A heap with root 8 and child 3 violates the min-heap invariant.","failure_mode":"Returning the root as the extremum after an invariant violation produces incorrect results.","cause":"An insertion, deletion, or mutation changed an element without restoring heap order.","solution":"Restore the invariant using sift-up, sift-down, or an equivalent heap repair operation.","edge_case":"Duplicate keys are valid when the ordering relation permits equality.","testing_guidance":"After mutations, verify the parent-child invariant for every occupied node.","verification_guidance":"Verification requires checking every parent against its existing children rather than inspecting only the root.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B41-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"heapq — Heap queue algorithm","publisher":"Python Documentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B51-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Heap construction","concept":"Bottom-up heap construction","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why can a binary heap be constructed from an unordered array in linear time?","answer":"Bottom-up heap construction starts from the last internal node and repeatedly restores heap order toward the root; the total work across nodes is linear in the number of elements.","explanation":"Most nodes are near the leaves and require little or no downward movement, so summing the repair work gives linear total construction time.","principle":"Aggregate cost matters more than assigning the worst-case repair cost independently to every node.","correct_example":"Transforming an existing array into a heap using a bottom-up heapify operation.","invalid_example":"Assuming heap construction must always perform n separate logarithmic insertions.","problem":"Choosing repeated insertion when an entire unordered collection is already available.","solution":"Use bottom-up heap construction when the implementation provides it and the input can be mutated or copied appropriately.","performance_implication":"Bottom-up construction has O(n) theoretical time for a binary heap.","testing_guidance":"Compare the resulting structure against the heap invariant rather than assuming construction succeeded.","verification_guidance":"Check all parent-child relationships after construction.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Binary heap"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B51-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"heapq — Heap queue algorithm","publisher":"Python Documentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B51-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Heap representation","concept":"Zero-based heap indexing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a binary heap be represented compactly in an array?","answer":"With zero-based indexing, the children of index k are at 2k+1 and 2k+2, and the parent of a non-root node can be derived from its index.","explanation":"The complete-tree shape allows structural relationships to be encoded implicitly without storing explicit child pointers.","principle":"Use the complete-tree layout to eliminate per-node structural pointers.","correct_example":"For index 2, the children are indices 5 and 6.","invalid_example":"Using binary-tree pointer formulas against a zero-based array without adjusting the index convention.","tradeoff":"Array representation reduces structural overhead and improves locality but is less natural when arbitrary node references are required.","performance_implication":"Implicit representation avoids pointer allocations for tree links.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Binary heap"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B11-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"heapq — Heap queue algorithm","publisher":"Python Documentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B52-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Heap variants","concept":"D-ary heap","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a d-ary heap?","answer":"A d-ary heap generalizes a binary heap so that each internal node can have up to d children while preserving heap order.","explanation":"Increasing the branching factor decreases tree height but increases the number of children considered during downward repair.","principle":"Branching factor trades shallower height against more child comparisons per sift-down operation.","correct_example":"A 4-ary min-heap stores each node with up to four children.","invalid_example":"Treating a d-ary heap as binary while using binary child-index calculations.","tradeoff":"A larger branching factor can reduce levels traversed during some operations but can increase per-level selection work.","edge_case":"The final internal level may contain fewer than d children.","testing_guidance":"Test parent-child ordering and index calculations at boundaries where a node has fewer than d children.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Heap invariant"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B51-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Heap data structure literature","publisher":"General algorithmic knowledge","reference":"Conceptual reference; no empirical claim asserted"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B52-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Heap variants","concept":"d-ary heap operation trade-off","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How does increasing heap arity affect operations?","answer":"Increasing arity generally reduces heap height while increasing the number of children that may need inspection during downward repair.","explanation":"The optimal branching factor depends on workload, comparison cost, memory behavior, and implementation details.","principle":"There is no universally optimal heap arity independent of workload and machine characteristics.","performance_implication":"Theoretical operation costs should be derived for the selected arity rather than copied from binary heaps.","constraint":"Do not claim empirical superiority without measurements on the target workload and implementation.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark competing arities only when performance decisions matter, and record workload and environment.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["D-ary heap"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B52-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_reference","title":"Heap data structure literature","publisher":"General algorithmic knowledge"}],"evidence_level":"INFERENCE_FROM_ALGORITHMIC_STRUCTURE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B52-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Heap variants","concept":"Heap arity selection","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When should heap arity be treated as a tunable implementation parameter?","answer":"Heap arity should be treated as a tunable parameter when comparison cost, cache behavior, branching, workload distribution, or operation mix materially affects performance.","explanation":"The theoretical structure alone does not determine practical performance.","alternative":"Use a standard binary heap when there is no measured reason to introduce a specialized arity.","tradeoff":"Specialization can improve a workload while increasing implementation complexity and maintenance cost.","performance_implication":"Any claimed improvement must be measured rather than assumed.","testing_guidance":"Include representative workloads and regression tests before changing the production representation.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["D-ary heap"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B52-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B53-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Priority queues","concept":"Indexed priority queue","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is an indexed priority queue?","answer":"An indexed priority queue combines priority ordering with an external mapping from logical item identifiers to their current heap positions or entries.","explanation":"The additional index can make locating a particular item practical without scanning the entire heap.","principle":"Separate priority ordering from identity lookup when mutable keyed entries are required.","correct_example":"Maintain a mapping from task ID to heap position while the heap stores priority and task ID.","invalid_example":"Assuming a plain heap can locate an arbitrary task in logarithmic time without additional indexing.","tradeoff":"The index improves targeted updates but introduces synchronization and consistency requirements.","security_implication":"Incorrect index maintenance can cause stale or unauthorized entries to be selected.","testing_guidance":"Test insertion, deletion, priority update, duplicate identifiers, and heap-position changes together.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every indexed item corresponds to exactly one valid heap entry.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Priority queue","Hash table"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B31-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B53-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Priority queues","concept":"Decrease-key operation","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What does decrease-key mean in a min-priority queue?","answer":"Decrease-key changes an existing item's key to a smaller value and restores heap order so the item moves toward the root as necessary.","explanation":"The operation is important in algorithms and workloads where an item's priority improves after insertion.","principle":"A key mutation must be followed by the direction of repair implied by the new ordering.","correct_example":"Reducing a task's priority key from 20 to 5 and repairing its position toward the root.","invalid_example":"Changing the stored key without repairing the heap.","failure_mode":"The modified item can remain below a parent with a larger key, violating the min-heap invariant.","solution":"Locate the item, update its key, and perform the appropriate upward repair.","testing_guidance":"Test reductions to the root, reductions that require one swap, reductions that require multiple swaps, and equal-key updates.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Heap invariant","Indexed priority queue"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B53-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B53-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Priority queues","concept":"Increase-key in a min-heap","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What repair direction is normally required after increasing a key in a min-heap?","answer":"An increased key can violate the relationship with children, so downward repair is normally required.","explanation":"The direction follows from which neighboring elements can now violate the heap-order invariant.","principle":"Mutation direction determines the likely repair direction under a monotonic key update.","correct_example":"Increase a root key and sift it downward until both child relationships are valid.","invalid_example":"Always performing sift-up after every key mutation regardless of the mutation direction.","testing_guidance":"Test increasing a leaf, internal node, and root.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Heap invariant"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B53-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B54-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Mutable priority queues","concept":"Lazy deletion","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a priority queue handle deletion when direct arbitrary removal is expensive?","answer":"A lazy-deletion design can mark obsolete entries as inactive and discard them when they reach the root.","explanation":"The heap structure remains intact while a separate identity map can identify the current valid version of each logical task.","principle":"Logical deletion can be separated from physical removal when immediate structural deletion is costly or complex.","correct_example":"Push a new priority version and mark the old entry obsolete; ignore obsolete entries during pop.","invalid_example":"Returning an obsolete entry merely because it appears at the heap root.","tradeoff":"Lazy deletion simplifies updates but can accumulate stale entries and increase memory usage until cleanup occurs.","edge_case":"A workload that repeatedly updates the same item can create many stale entries.","security_implication":"Stale entries must not bypass current authorization or lifecycle state.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated updates, deletion before pop, deletion after reaching the root, and stale-entry accumulation.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Priority queue","Hash table"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B53-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"heapq — Priority Queue Implementation Notes","publisher":"Python Documentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B54-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Mutable priority queues","concept":"Stale-entry validation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a lazy priority queue verify that a popped entry is still current?","answer":"The queue should compare the popped entry against authoritative current-state metadata before treating it as executable or returning it.","explanation":"Heap membership alone does not prove that an entry is still logically valid.","principle":"Physical presence and logical validity are separate states.","correct_example":"Compare an entry version or identity token with the current mapping before accepting it.","invalid_example":"Executing every entry returned by the heap regardless of current state.","security_implication":"Failing to validate stale state can cause obsolete or revoked work to execute.","testing_guidance":"Inject stale, revoked, duplicated, and superseded entries and ensure only current entries are accepted.","verification_guidance":"The current-state authority and queue entry must agree on identity and version.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Lazy deletion"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B54-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B54-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Mutable priority queues","concept":"Priority update identity","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is updating a priority by matching only payload equality unsafe?","answer":"Different logical tasks may share equal payloads, so payload equality alone may update or remove the wrong entry.","explanation":"Priority queues should distinguish logical identity from payload content whenever multiple entries can represent separate work items.","principle":"Identity semantics must be explicit when mutable entries are managed.","correct_example":"Use a unique task identifier or immutable entry token for updates.","invalid_example":"Search for the first entry whose payload equals the requested object and mutate it without identity rules.","failure_mode":"The wrong queue entry is modified.","cause":"Payload equality was incorrectly treated as identity.","solution":"Introduce explicit unique identifiers or identity tokens.","testing_guidance":"Use duplicate payloads with different logical identifiers.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Priority queue"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B53-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B55-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Meldable heaps","concept":"Meld operation","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does it mean for a heap to support meld?","answer":"Meld combines two heaps into one heap while preserving the heap-order invariant.","explanation":"Meld is useful when independently maintained priority collections must be combined frequently.","principle":"A data structure's usefulness depends on its operation set, not only on its single-element insertion and removal costs.","correct_example":"Combine two compatible min-heaps into one priority structure.","invalid_example":"Concatenate two heap arrays and assume the result is automatically a valid heap.","failure_mode":"The combined structure violates heap order.","solution":"Use a heap implementation whose merge operation explicitly restores or preserves the invariant.","tradeoff":"Meld-friendly structures may have different constants, memory behavior, or implementation complexity than simple binary heaps.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Heap invariant"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B52-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B55-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Meldable heaps","concept":"Binomial heap","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a binomial heap?","answer":"A binomial heap is a collection of heap-ordered binomial trees designed to support efficient priority-queue operations including heap merging.","explanation":"Its structure represents a set of trees with controlled degrees and supports combining corresponding trees during operations.","principle":"A forest representation can make heap union a first-class operation.","tradeoff":"Binomial heaps have more structural complexity than ordinary array-based binary heaps.","constraint":"The appropriate complexity comparison depends on the exact operation and implementation model.","testing_guidance":"Test heap order after insertion, extraction, and repeated union operations.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Meld operation","Heap invariant"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B55-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B55-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Meldable heaps","concept":"Fibonacci heap","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What distinguishes a Fibonacci heap from a binary heap?","answer":"A Fibonacci heap uses a collection of heap-ordered trees and deferred structural consolidation to obtain strong amortized bounds for certain priority-queue operations.","explanation":"Its theoretical design is particularly relevant when decrease-key and meld operations dominate.","principle":"Amortized performance can justify deferred structural work when the workload supports it.","tradeoff":"Theoretical advantages come with greater implementation complexity and can differ from practical performance on real workloads.","constraint":"Do not convert asymptotic amortized bounds into guaranteed per-operation latency claims.","testing_guidance":"Test heap invariants and potential-state transitions across long sequences of operations.","performance_implication":"Practical performance must be measured for the target workload rather than inferred from asymptotic bounds alone.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Decrease-key","Meld operation"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B53-0002","D12-DS-B55-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B56-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Priority queue alternatives","concept":"Pairing heap","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is a pairing heap?","answer":"A pairing heap is a self-adjusting heap-ordered multiway tree designed to provide simple meld and priority-queue operations with useful practical behavior.","explanation":"It uses repeated linking of heap roots rather than the rigid array representation of a binary heap.","principle":"Simple self-adjusting structures can provide useful priority-queue behavior without implementing the full machinery of more theoretically elaborate heaps.","tradeoff":"Exact theoretical and practical behavior depends on the operation sequence and implementation.","constraint":"Do not state a universal performance advantage without workload-specific evidence.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated meld, insertion, minimum extraction, and key-update workloads.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Meld operation"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B55-0003"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_CONCEPT","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B56-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Priority queue alternatives","concept":"Binary heap versus meldable heap","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What determines whether a binary heap or meldable heap is appropriate?","answer":"The dominant operation mix determines the choice, especially the frequency and latency requirements of merge, insertion, extraction, and priority-update operations.","explanation":"A binary heap is structurally simple and broadly useful, while meldable structures explicitly optimize workloads where combining queues is important.","alternative":"Use a standard binary heap unless workload requirements justify a specialized structure.","tradeoff":"Specialized structures increase implementation and testing surface area.","performance_implication":"Theoretical operation bounds do not establish practical superiority for a particular application.","testing_guidance":"Use representative traces rather than synthetic single-operation tests when evaluating the choice.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Binary heap","Meldable heap"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B55-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B56-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Priority queue alternatives","concept":"Priority queue abstraction boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should application code depend on a priority-queue abstraction instead of a specific heap implementation when possible?","answer":"An abstraction allows the underlying priority structure to change without rewriting consumers that only require defined priority-queue operations.","explanation":"This separates application semantics from data-structure implementation.","principle":"Program to the required operations and invariants rather than incidental representation details.","correct_example":"Application code requests insert, peek, and pop through an interface while the implementation may use a binary heap.","invalid_example":"Application logic directly manipulates internal heap indices.","architecture_implication":"Encapsulation makes later representation changes safer.","testing_guidance":"Test the abstraction contract independently from each implementation.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Priority queue"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B52-0003"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"SOFTWARE_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B57-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Selection","concept":"Top-k selection with a heap","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a heap maintain the top k elements of a stream?","answer":"Maintain a heap containing at most k candidates and discard the candidate that falls outside the required top-k boundary as new items arrive.","explanation":"The heap bounds retained state by k instead of requiring the entire stream to be sorted or stored.","principle":"Maintain only the frontier required by the query.","performance_implication":"For suitable workloads, maintaining a heap of size k can require O(n log k) comparisons over n inputs.","constraint":"The ordering direction and heap type must match whether the goal is the largest or smallest k elements.","testing_guidance":"Test k=0, k=1, k greater than input size, duplicate values, sorted input, reverse-sorted input, and streaming input.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Heap","Streaming"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B51-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B57-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Selection","concept":"Heap-based selection versus full sorting","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When can heap-based top-k selection be preferable to sorting the complete input?","answer":"It can be appropriate when k is substantially smaller than the input size or when the input is streamed and retaining the complete dataset is undesirable.","explanation":"Heap selection focuses computation and memory on the requested boundary rather than fully ordering every element.","alternative":"Use full sorting when a complete ordering is required or when its implementation simplicity is more valuable.","performance_implication":"Actual performance depends on k, input size, comparator cost, memory behavior, and runtime implementation.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark only with representative workloads if this is a performance-sensitive design decision.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Top-k selection"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B57-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_TRADEOFF","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B57-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Selection","concept":"Tie handling in top-k selection","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must top-k algorithms define tie semantics?","answer":"When multiple elements share the boundary key, the algorithm must specify whether any tied element is acceptable or whether a deterministic secondary ordering is required.","explanation":"Without explicit tie semantics, two valid executions can return different members of an equal-key group.","principle":"Output determinism is a contract decision, not an incidental property.","correct_example":"Use (score, stable_id) as a comparison key when deterministic selection is required.","invalid_example":"Assuming equal scores imply a stable order without defining one.","testing_guidance":"Create datasets with many equal boundary values and verify the documented contract.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Top-k selection"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B57-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"heapq — Priority Queue Implementation Notes","publisher":"Python Documentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_DESIGN_CONSIDERATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B58-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"K-way merge","concept":"Heap-based k-way merge","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a heap merge multiple sorted streams efficiently?","answer":"Keep the smallest current item from each non-empty stream in a min-heap and repeatedly emit the smallest heap entry before advancing that stream.","explanation":"The heap tracks only the current frontier of each sorted input.","principle":"When multiple sources are individually sorted, merge their frontiers rather than repeatedly scanning all sources.","performance_implication":"For k sorted sources and N total items, heap-based merging can use O(N log k) comparisons.","edge_case":"A source becomes empty immediately after its current item is emitted.","testing_guidance":"Test empty streams, one stream, duplicate keys, unequal stream lengths, and large numbers of streams.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Min-heap","Sorted streams"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B51-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"heapq — Heap queue algorithm","publisher":"Python Documentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_ALGORITHMIC_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B58-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"K-way merge","concept":"Streaming merge memory bound","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why does heap-based k-way merge avoid loading all source items into memory?","answer":"The heap needs only the current frontier item from each active sorted source plus the state required to advance those sources.","explanation":"The merge exploits existing ordering rather than materializing every input item simultaneously.","tradeoff":"The implementation must retain access to each source and correctly handle source exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Auxiliary heap state is proportional to the number of active streams rather than total input size.","testing_guidance":"Verify that exhausted streams are removed and no source item is skipped or emitted twice.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["K-way merge"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B58-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"heapq — Heap queue algorithm","publisher":"Python Documentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B58-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"K-way merge","concept":"Heap merge comparator consistency","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must remain consistent during a heap-based merge?","answer":"The ordering used by heap entries must match the ordering promised by the merged output.","explanation":"A comparator mismatch can produce an output that is locally heap-ordered but globally violates the requested merge ordering.","failure_mode":"Merged output is not sorted according to the declared key.","cause":"Different streams or heap entries use inconsistent key extraction or comparison semantics.","solution":"Define one canonical merge key and apply it consistently to every stream entry.","testing_guidance":"Use different representations with equivalent logical keys and verify output ordering.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["K-way merge"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B58-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B59-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Running statistics","concept":"Two-heap median structure","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can two heaps support a running median?","answer":"Maintain a max-heap for the lower portion of values and a min-heap for the upper portion, keeping their sizes balanced so the median can be derived from their roots.","explanation":"The two roots represent the boundary between the lower and upper partitions.","principle":"Partition around an order statistic and maintain balanced frontier structures.","edge_case":"Even-sized input requires an explicit policy for combining the two middle values.","testing_guidance":"Test increasing, decreasing, duplicate, negative, floating-point, and alternating-extreme sequences.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Min-heap","Max-heap"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B51-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"heapq — Other Applications","publisher":"Python Documentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B59-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Running statistics","concept":"Two-heap balance invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariants must a two-heap running-median structure maintain?","answer":"All values in the lower heap must not exceed the values in the upper heap, and the heap sizes must satisfy the chosen balance policy.","explanation":"Correct roots alone are insufficient if the partitions overlap or become badly unbalanced.","principle":"Compound data structures require verification of every constituent and cross-structure invariant.","testing_guidance":"After every insertion, verify heap invariants, size balance, and cross-boundary ordering.","verification_guidance":"Compare results periodically with a trusted sorted reference implementation during testing.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Two-heap median structure"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B59-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B59-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Running statistics","concept":"Deletion in two-heap median structures","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is arbitrary deletion harder in a two-heap median structure?","answer":"The item may reside in either heap, and direct removal can violate that heap's structural invariant and the balance between both heaps.","explanation":"A running-median structure optimized for insertion and root access does not automatically provide efficient arbitrary deletion.","tradeoff":"Supporting arbitrary deletion may require identity indexes, lazy deletion, or additional bookkeeping.","invalid_example":"Searching only the heap roots and assuming that any requested value can be removed directly.","solution":"Use explicit identity tracking or lazy deletion when arbitrary deletion is a required operation.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate values and deletion of non-root elements from both partitions.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Two-heap median structure","Lazy deletion"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B54-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DESIGN_CONSIDERATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B60-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Priority scheduling","concept":"Heap-backed scheduler","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are heaps suitable for event scheduling?","answer":"A min-heap can keep the earliest scheduled event at the root, allowing the next event to be selected without scanning every pending event.","explanation":"Scheduling workloads naturally map an event timestamp or deadline to a priority key.","principle":"Choose a data structure whose root directly represents the next required decision.","correct_example":"Store (scheduled_time, sequence_number, event) in a min-priority queue.","invalid_example":"Scanning every pending event on every scheduler tick when events are maintained by time order.","edge_case":"Two events have identical scheduled times and require deterministic ordering.","testing_guidance":"Test equal timestamps, cancellation, rescheduling, clock anomalies, empty queues, and bursts of events.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Priority queue"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B57-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"heapq — Theory and Applications","publisher":"Python Documentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_APPLICATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B60-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Priority scheduling","concept":"Stable scheduler ordering","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How can a priority scheduler deterministically order equal-priority tasks?","answer":"Add a unique monotonic sequence number as a secondary ordering key after the primary priority.","explanation":"The sequence number prevents equal-priority records from depending on payload comparability or incidental implementation behavior.","principle":"Explicit secondary keys make deterministic ordering a defined contract.","correct_example":"Store entries as (priority, sequence_number, task).","invalid_example":"Store (priority, task) when task objects may be incomparable for equal priorities.","failure_mode":"Equal-priority insertion or extraction raises a comparison error or produces unintended ordering.","solution":"Use a deterministic secondary key or a comparison wrapper that excludes the payload from ordering.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Priority queue","Tie handling"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B57-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"heapq — Priority Queue Implementation Notes","publisher":"Python Documentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B60-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Advanced Heaps","subtopic":"Priority scheduling","concept":"Scheduler cancellation and rescheduling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What can go wrong when scheduled events are cancelled or rescheduled?","answer":"A stale heap entry may remain physically present after the logical event has been cancelled or replaced.","explanation":"Priority queues do not inherently know whether an application-level task is still valid.","failure_mode":"A cancelled or superseded event executes.","cause":"Physical queue state was treated as authoritative logical state.","solution":"Use cancellation markers, generation identifiers, lazy deletion, or an authoritative task registry and validate entries before execution.","security_implication":"Cancelled or revoked actions must not execute merely because an old queue entry remains.","testing_guidance":"Test cancellation before dequeue, cancellation at the root, repeated rescheduling, duplicate identifiers, and stale-entry accumulation.","verification_guidance":"Execution requires both priority selection and current logical-state validation.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Lazy deletion","Stale-entry validation"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B54-0002","D12-DS-B60-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"DESIGN_AND_FAILURE_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

