{"record_id":"D12-DS-B61-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph fundamentals","concept":"Graph data structure","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a graph data structure?","answer":"A graph is a collection of vertices, also called nodes, connected by edges that represent relationships between pairs of vertices.","explanation":"Graphs model relationships that are not necessarily hierarchical or sequential. Edges may be directed or undirected and may carry attributes such as weights or labels.","principle":"Represent entities as vertices and relationships as edges when relationships are central to the problem.","correct_example":"A dependency network can represent packages as nodes and dependency relationships as directed edges.","invalid_example":"Forcing an arbitrary many-to-many relationship into a tree representation solely because trees are simpler.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B41-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Introduction to NetworkX","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/introduction.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B61-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph fundamentals","concept":"Vertex and edge model","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What are the fundamental components of a graph?","answer":"The fundamental components are vertices representing entities and edges representing relationships between vertices.","explanation":"Additional metadata can be attached to either vertices or edges without changing the basic graph abstraction.","principle":"Separate topology from optional metadata.","correct_example":"A user is represented by a vertex and a friendship relationship by an edge.","invalid_example":"Encoding relationship metadata only in node names when multiple relationships exist between the same entities.","edge_case":"A graph may contain isolated vertices with no incident edges.","testing_guidance":"Test graphs containing zero vertices, isolated vertices, one vertex, and disconnected components.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph data structure"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B61-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Introduction to NetworkX","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/introduction.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B61-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph fundamentals","concept":"Graph order and size","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What do graph order and graph size commonly represent?","answer":"Graph order commonly denotes the number of vertices, while graph size commonly denotes the number of edges.","explanation":"Terminology can vary by source, so implementations and mathematical specifications should state their conventions explicitly.","principle":"Do not infer metric semantics from names when an API or specification provides an explicit definition.","edge_case":"For weighted graphs, some APIs expose weighted edge totals separately from edge count.","testing_guidance":"Verify node and edge counts after insertion, deletion, and duplicate-edge operations.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph data structure"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B61-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph class reference","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/graph.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B62-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph types","concept":"Undirected graph","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an undirected graph?","answer":"An undirected graph represents edges without an inherent direction, so an edge connects two vertices symmetrically.","explanation":"An undirected relationship can normally be traversed from either endpoint according to the graph model.","correct_example":"A mutual collaboration relationship can be modeled with an undirected edge.","invalid_example":"Treating an undirected edge as permission to perform a directed action when application semantics require authorization in a specific direction.","edge_case":"Self-loops may be permitted depending on the graph implementation.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph data structure"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B61-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph class reference","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/graph.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B62-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph types","concept":"Directed graph","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a directed graph?","answer":"A directed graph represents edges with an orientation from a source vertex to a target vertex.","explanation":"The relationship from u to v is distinct from the relationship from v to u unless the model explicitly contains both edges.","correct_example":"A dependency from package A to package B can be represented as A -> B.","invalid_example":"Automatically treating A -> B as equivalent to B -> A.","security_implication":"Direction can encode trust, authority, dependency, or permitted flow and must not be discarded casually.","testing_guidance":"Test asymmetric edge pairs and verify outgoing and incoming neighbor semantics separately.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph data structure"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B62-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph types","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/index.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B62-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph types","concept":"Directed versus undirected semantics","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the key structural difference between directed and undirected graphs?","answer":"Directed graphs distinguish source and destination, while undirected graphs treat the connection symmetrically.","explanation":"The distinction affects adjacency, degree definitions, traversal, path semantics, and many graph algorithms.","tradeoff":"Directed models express richer relationship semantics but require explicit handling of direction.","testing_guidance":"Construct paired graphs containing the same endpoints and verify that directional queries produce different results where expected.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Directed graph","Undirected graph"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B62-0001","D12-DS-B62-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph types","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/index.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B63-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph types","concept":"Multigraph","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a multigraph?","answer":"A multigraph permits multiple parallel edges between the same pair of vertices.","explanation":"Parallel edges allow multiple distinct relationships between the same endpoints, often with separate attributes or identities.","correct_example":"Two cities connected by several distinct transport routes can be represented using parallel edges.","invalid_example":"Collapsing all parallel relationships into one edge when their identities or attributes have independent meaning.","edge_case":"The graph implementation may require an edge key to distinguish parallel edges.","testing_guidance":"Test insertion, lookup, update, and deletion of one parallel edge without affecting the others.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph data structure"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B63-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph types","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/index.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B63-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph types","concept":"Self-loop","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a self-loop in a graph?","answer":"A self-loop is an edge whose source and destination are the same vertex.","explanation":"Self-loops are structurally valid in many graph models but may be prohibited by an application-level invariant.","correct_example":"An edge from node A to node A represents a self-loop.","invalid_example":"Silently removing self-loops when they carry meaningful domain information.","edge_case":"Some algorithms require special handling because a self-loop contributes differently to degree or matrix representations.","testing_guidance":"Explicitly test self-loop insertion and verify its effect on degree, adjacency, and edge count according to the chosen model.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph data structure"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B62-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph class reference","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/graph.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B63-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph types","concept":"Parallel edge identity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must parallel edges have explicit identity when they carry independent semantics?","answer":"If multiple edges connect the same endpoints, an explicit edge identifier or key is required when individual edges can be independently updated, removed, or referenced.","explanation":"Endpoint pairs alone cannot uniquely identify parallel relationships.","failure_mode":"An update intended for one relationship modifies or removes another relationship with the same endpoints.","solution":"Use stable edge identifiers or implementation-supported edge keys.","testing_guidance":"Create multiple parallel edges with different attributes and independently mutate each one.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Multigraph"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B63-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph types","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/index.html"}],"evidence_level":"DESIGN_AND_VERIFICATION_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B64-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph representation","concept":"Adjacency list","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is an adjacency-list representation?","answer":"An adjacency list stores, for each vertex, the vertices or edges directly connected to it.","explanation":"It represents local connectivity without allocating a matrix entry for every possible vertex pair.","principle":"Store existing relationships explicitly when the graph is sparse.","correct_example":"A dictionary maps each node to a collection of its neighboring nodes.","invalid_example":"Creating a full V-by-V matrix for a very sparse graph when constant-time pair lookup is not required.","performance_implication":"Neighbor iteration is naturally efficient for sparse graphs because only existing edges are represented.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph data structure"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B64-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Introduction to NetworkX","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/introduction.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B64-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph representation","concept":"Adjacency matrix","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is an adjacency-matrix representation?","answer":"An adjacency matrix stores a value for each ordered pair of vertices, with entries indicating whether an edge exists or what its edge weight represents.","explanation":"Matrix representation makes pairwise edge lookup direct at the cost of allocating space proportional to the square of the number of vertices.","correct_example":"A matrix entry A[i][j] represents the edge from vertex i to vertex j.","invalid_example":"Assuming a matrix row automatically represents undirected semantics when the matrix is actually directed.","performance_implication":"A dense matrix requires O(V^2) storage.","tradeoff":"It provides direct pair addressing but can waste substantial memory on sparse graphs.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph data structure"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B64-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"adjacency_matrix","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/latest/reference/generated/networkx.linalg.graphmatrix.adjacency_matrix.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B64-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph representation","concept":"Adjacency list versus matrix","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should adjacency lists and adjacency matrices be selected?","answer":"Use an adjacency list when efficient neighbor traversal and sparse storage are important; use a matrix when dense pairwise access or matrix-based algorithms justify the O(V^2) representation.","explanation":"The appropriate representation depends on graph density, operations, memory limits, and downstream algorithms.","tradeoff":"Adjacency lists favor sparse connectivity while matrices favor direct pair addressing and dense linear-algebra operations.","constraint":"Do not select a representation solely from the number of vertices without considering edge density and access patterns.","testing_guidance":"Validate representation-specific complexity assumptions against the actual workload.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Adjacency list","Adjacency matrix"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B64-0001","D12-DS-B64-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"NetworkX Introduction","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/introduction.html"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"adjacency_matrix","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/latest/reference/generated/networkx.linalg.graphmatrix.adjacency_matrix.html"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_TRADEOFF","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B65-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Sparse graphs","concept":"Sparse graph","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a sparse graph?","answer":"A sparse graph has relatively few edges compared with the maximum possible number of edges under its graph model.","explanation":"The exact threshold for calling a graph sparse depends on context; it is a structural characterization rather than a universal fixed percentage.","principle":"Storage and algorithm choices should reflect actual edge density.","correct_example":"A large dependency network where each package connects to a small subset of all packages is typically sparse.","invalid_example":"Calling every graph with fewer than an arbitrary fixed number of edges sparse regardless of vertex count.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph representation"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B64-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B65-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Sparse graphs","concept":"Sparse adjacency representation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why are adjacency-list structures commonly used for sparse graphs?","answer":"They store existing edges rather than all possible vertex pairs, avoiding the quadratic storage overhead of a dense matrix when the graph contains relatively few edges.","explanation":"NetworkX documents adjacency-list-based internal structures using dictionary-based mappings for graph objects.","performance_implication":"Neighbor iteration can scale with the number of incident edges rather than the total number of vertices.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Sparse graph","Adjacency list"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B64-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Introduction to NetworkX","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/introduction.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B65-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Dense graphs","concept":"Dense graph representation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can a dense graph justify matrix-style storage?","answer":"A dense graph can justify matrix-style storage when a large fraction of possible vertex pairs have edges and direct pair access or matrix algorithms are important.","explanation":"Dense connectivity reduces the relative waste of storing all pair positions.","tradeoff":"Matrix storage provides predictable pair indexing but incurs O(V^2) space.","performance_implication":"Memory capacity can become the limiting factor as V grows even when the representation is algorithmically convenient.","testing_guidance":"Estimate V^2 storage before choosing a dense matrix for large graphs.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Adjacency matrix","Dense graph"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B64-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B66-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Edge representation","concept":"Weighted graph","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a weighted graph?","answer":"A weighted graph associates a value with each edge to represent a quantity such as cost, distance, latency, capacity, or priority.","explanation":"The meaning and allowed domain of the weight must be defined by the application and algorithm.","correct_example":"An edge weight represents network latency between two endpoints.","invalid_example":"Using negative weights with an algorithm whose correctness requires non-negative edge weights.","security_implication":"Weights representing risk or authorization should not be treated as interchangeable with physical distance without explicit semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test zero, positive, negative where permitted, missing, extreme, and non-finite weights according to the contract.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph data structure"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B66-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Introduction to NetworkX","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/introduction.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B66-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Edge representation","concept":"Edge attributes","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should edge attributes be represented separately from topology?","answer":"Separating topology from attributes allows algorithms to operate on relationships while application-specific metadata remains explicitly associated with each edge.","explanation":"Graph implementations can store arbitrary edge attributes such as weights and labels.","principle":"Keep relationship existence, relationship identity, and relationship metadata conceptually distinct.","correct_example":"An edge connects A to B while attributes store latency, protocol, and creation metadata.","invalid_example":"Encoding all edge metadata into a serialized node name or edge label string.","architecture_implication":"Explicit attributes improve validation, filtering, and algorithm interoperability.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Weighted graph"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B63-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Introduction to NetworkX","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/introduction.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B66-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Edge representation","concept":"Weight domain validation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must graph edge weights be validated before algorithm execution?","answer":"Algorithms may require specific weight domains such as non-negative, finite, or numeric values; violating those assumptions can invalidate their results.","explanation":"The graph structure can remain valid while the algorithmic interpretation of its edge attributes becomes invalid.","failure_mode":"An algorithm returns an incorrect result or fails because input weights violate its assumptions.","solution":"Validate weight type, finiteness, allowed range, and semantic meaning before invoking the algorithm.","testing_guidance":"Include invalid types, missing weights, negative weights, NaN, infinity, and extreme values where relevant.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Weighted graph"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B66-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Shortest Paths","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/algorithms/shortest_paths.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_ALGORITHMIC_CONSTRAINT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B67-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Degree","concept":"Vertex degree","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is vertex degree in an undirected graph?","answer":"The degree of a vertex is the number of incident edges under the graph's degree convention.","explanation":"Self-loops and parallel edges can affect degree according to the specific graph model and API semantics.","correct_example":"A vertex connected by three ordinary edges has degree three in a simple undirected graph.","invalid_example":"Assuming a multigraph has the same degree semantics as a simple graph without checking its edge multiplicity rules.","testing_guidance":"Test isolated nodes, self-loops, and parallel edges explicitly.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph types"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B63-0001","D12-DS-B63-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph class reference","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/graph.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B67-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Degree","concept":"In-degree and out-degree","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What are in-degree and out-degree in directed graphs?","answer":"In-degree counts incoming incident edges while out-degree counts outgoing incident edges according to the graph's edge model.","explanation":"Directed graphs require separating incoming and outgoing relationships because edge orientation carries information.","correct_example":"For A -> B, A gains one outgoing edge and B gains one incoming edge.","invalid_example":"Treating A -> B as contributing an outgoing edge to B.","testing_guidance":"Construct asymmetric directed graphs and verify in-degree and out-degree independently.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Directed graph"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B62-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph types","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/index.html"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GRAPH_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B67-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Degree","concept":"Degree-based filtering","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can degree information support graph processing?","answer":"Degree can be used to identify isolated vertices, highly connected vertices, low-connectivity regions, or candidates for specialized processing.","explanation":"Degree is a structural property and should not automatically be interpreted as importance or risk without domain semantics.","principle":"Use structural metrics as evidence rather than as unqualified conclusions.","security_implication":"A high-degree security entity may be structurally significant but degree alone does not establish maliciousness or authorization.","testing_guidance":"Verify degree calculations after graph mutations before using them as downstream features.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Vertex degree"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B67-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B68-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph views","concept":"Graph projection","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a graph projection?","answer":"A graph projection creates a derived graph that preserves selected entities or relationships from an underlying graph according to explicit transformation rules.","explanation":"Projection is useful when algorithms need a simplified representation of a richer graph.","correct_example":"Project a heterogeneous graph onto only service-to-service relationships.","invalid_example":"Deleting graph attributes and relationships without documenting the transformation semantics.","tradeoff":"Projection can simplify computation while discarding information needed for other analyses.","testing_guidance":"Verify that every retained edge and node satisfies the projection predicate and that discarded information is intentional.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph data structure"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B66-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GRAPH_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B68-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph views","concept":"Subgraph","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a subgraph?","answer":"A subgraph is a graph derived from another graph by retaining a selected set of vertices and eligible edges.","explanation":"The exact definition may include induced-edge semantics or explicitly selected edges, so the construction rule should be stated.","correct_example":"An induced subgraph retains all source edges whose endpoints both belong to the selected vertex set.","invalid_example":"Calling a graph a subgraph without specifying whether omitted edges were intentionally filtered.","edge_case":"A selected vertex set may produce isolated vertices in the resulting subgraph.","testing_guidance":"Compare the resulting node and edge sets against the declared subgraph construction rule.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph projection"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B68-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph class reference","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/graph.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B68-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph views","concept":"Mutable versus snapshot graph views","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why must graph views and graph snapshots be distinguished?","answer":"A live view can reflect changes to an underlying graph, whereas a snapshot represents a point-in-time copy or immutable interpretation.","explanation":"Confusing the two can produce time-dependent analysis results or accidental mutation of shared state.","failure_mode":"An algorithm observes graph changes during iteration that were not part of its intended input.","solution":"Use an explicit snapshot when isolation is required and a view when synchronized visibility is intentional.","testing_guidance":"Mutate the underlying graph during tests and verify the documented visibility semantics.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Subgraph"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B68-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph class reference","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/graph.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_IMPLEMENTATION_CONSIDERATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B69-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph mutation","concept":"Atomic graph mutation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should related graph mutations be treated as one logical operation when invariants span multiple structures?","answer":"If a node, edge, index, and metadata registry must remain consistent, partially applying only some mutations can leave the graph in an invalid state.","explanation":"Compound graph implementations often maintain multiple correlated structures.","principle":"Update all structures required by an invariant as one logical transaction or provide explicit rollback/recovery semantics.","correct_example":"Adding an edge updates adjacency structures, edge identity indexes, and required metadata consistently.","invalid_example":"Updating adjacency first and allowing an exception to leave the edge registry inconsistent.","failure_mode":"Ghost edges, missing edges, stale indexes, or mismatched metadata.","solution":"Use transactional mutation, validation checkpoints, or compensating rollback logic.","security_implication":"Inconsistent graph state can produce incorrect authorization, dependency, or policy decisions.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures at each mutation step and verify invariant recovery.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph invariants"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B63-0003"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"SOFTWARE_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B69-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph mutation","concept":"Dangling edge prevention","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is a dangling edge?","answer":"A dangling edge is an edge record whose referenced endpoint is missing or invalid under the graph's integrity contract.","explanation":"Dangling references can arise from partial deletion, failed deserialization, or inconsistent external identifiers.","failure_mode":"Traversal or analysis encounters an edge whose endpoint cannot be resolved.","cause":"Node deletion or import occurred without updating all dependent edge structures.","solution":"Enforce referential integrity during mutation and validate imported graphs before analysis.","testing_guidance":"Delete nodes with incident edges and verify the documented cascade or rejection behavior.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Atomic graph mutation"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B69-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B69-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Graph mutation","concept":"Concurrent graph mutation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can mutating a graph while traversing it be unsafe?","answer":"Concurrent structural mutation can invalidate iteration assumptions, skip elements, duplicate observations, or produce inconsistent analysis results depending on the implementation.","explanation":"Graph traversal normally assumes a stable relationship structure unless the algorithm explicitly supports dynamic graphs.","failure_mode":"Traversal observes a state that never existed as a consistent graph snapshot.","solution":"Use synchronization, immutable snapshots, versioned traversal, or an algorithm designed for dynamic graphs.","testing_guidance":"Test insertion and deletion during traversal under the target concurrency model.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Graph views","Atomic graph mutation"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B68-0003"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph class reference","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/graph.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_IMPLEMENTATION_CONSIDERATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B70-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Traversal-ready representation","concept":"Neighbor iteration","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is neighbor iteration central to graph algorithms?","answer":"Many graph algorithms repeatedly examine the vertices directly adjacent to a current vertex, making efficient neighbor access a core representation requirement.","explanation":"Adjacency-list representations naturally expose neighbors without scanning unrelated vertex pairs.","principle":"Optimize the representation around the dominant graph operation.","performance_implication":"For sparse graphs, neighbor iteration can avoid scanning all V possible destinations for each vertex.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Adjacency list"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B64-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Graph class reference","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/classes/graph.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B70-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Traversal-ready representation","concept":"BFS and DFS representation requirements","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What graph operations should a representation support efficiently for BFS and DFS?","answer":"The representation should support identifying neighbors of a vertex and tracking visited state efficiently.","explanation":"BFS and DFS both repeatedly expand the frontier through graph adjacency; NetworkX exposes dedicated traversal algorithms over graph objects.","principle":"Data structure requirements should be derived from the algorithm's access pattern.","performance_implication":"For adjacency-list representations, standard BFS and DFS traversal can process vertices and edges in O(V+E) time.","testing_guidance":"Test disconnected graphs, cycles, self-loops, duplicate relationships, and isolated vertices.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Neighbor iteration","BFS","DFS"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B70-0001"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Traversal","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/algorithms/traversal.html"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Shortest Paths","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/algorithms/shortest_paths.html"}],"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_ALGORITHMIC_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B70-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Graphs","subtopic":"Traversal-ready representation","concept":"Graph traversal state isolation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should visited state normally be kept separate from graph topology?","answer":"Traversal state describes one algorithm execution and should not silently mutate the graph's persistent topology or become stale across independent traversals.","explanation":"Separating traversal metadata prevents previous searches from contaminating later analyses.","correct_example":"Maintain a local visited set keyed by node identity for each traversal.","invalid_example":"Store a permanent visited flag on every graph node and reuse it across unrelated searches without resetting it.","failure_mode":"A later traversal incorrectly treats previously visited nodes as already explored.","solution":"Scope traversal state to the execution or explicitly version and reset it.","testing_guidance":"Run multiple independent BFS and DFS operations on the same graph and verify identical results where the inputs are identical.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["BFS and DFS representation requirements"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B70-0002"],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Traversal","publisher":"NetworkX","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/algorithms/traversal.html"}],"evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED"}

