{"record_id":"D12-DS-B71-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Tries","concept":"Trie","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a trie?","answer":"A trie is a tree-based data structure in which paths represent sequences of symbols, commonly characters or tokens, and shared prefixes are represented by shared paths.","explanation":"Unlike a binary search tree that orders complete keys by comparison, a trie organizes keys according to their constituent symbols.","principle":"Represent shared prefixes explicitly when prefix-based operations are important.","correct_example":"Store the words 'cat', 'car', and 'cart' so their common 'ca' prefix shares nodes.","invalid_example":"Treating a trie as an ordered binary search tree and applying comparison-based traversal rules.","tradeoff":"Tries can provide prefix-oriented operations efficiently but may consume substantial memory depending on branching representation.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Trees"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B41-0001","D12-DS-B41-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B71-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Tries","concept":"Trie key termination","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why does a trie need explicit key termination information?","answer":"A trie needs to distinguish a complete stored key from a prefix that merely leads to longer keys.","explanation":"A node representing the sequence 'car' may also be a prefix of 'cart', so path existence alone does not indicate that 'car' is a stored key.","correct_example":"Mark the node representing 'car' as terminal while continuing the path to 'cart'.","invalid_example":"Assuming every trie node represents a complete key.","failure_mode":"Prefix-only paths are incorrectly returned as stored keys.","solution":"Store explicit terminal state or equivalent key-end metadata.","testing_guidance":"Insert a key and a longer key sharing its entire prefix, then query both independently.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Trie"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B71-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B71-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Tries","concept":"Trie deletion","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How can a key be deleted from a trie?","answer":"Clear the terminal marker for the key and remove nodes that no longer represent a prefix of another stored key, when safe to do so.","explanation":"Deletion must preserve every path required by remaining keys.","correct_example":"Deleting 'cart' while retaining shared nodes required by 'car'.","invalid_example":"Deleting the entire prefix path for 'cart' when 'car' remains stored.","failure_mode":"Deletion removes structural state required by another key.","cause":"Shared-prefix ownership was not considered.","solution":"Remove nodes only while they have no children, are not terminal for another key, and are no longer required.","testing_guidance":"Test deleting unique keys, prefixes of other keys, longer keys, duplicate insertions, and deleting absent keys.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Trie key termination"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B71-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B72-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Prefix operations","concept":"Prefix lookup","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How does a trie support prefix lookup?","answer":"Traverse the trie according to the prefix symbols, then enumerate terminal descendants beneath the resulting prefix node.","explanation":"The traversal cost depends primarily on prefix length, while enumeration cost depends on the number of matching descendants and their representation.","principle":"Separate locating the prefix from enumerating its matching keys.","correct_example":"Locate the node for 'app' and enumerate all stored words below it.","invalid_example":"Scan every stored key and compare its first three characters when the trie is intended to provide direct prefix access.","performance_implication":"Prefix location can be proportional to prefix length rather than the total number of stored keys.","testing_guidance":"Test empty prefixes, exact keys, prefixes with no matches, and prefixes matching many keys.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Trie"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B71-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B72-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Prefix operations","concept":"Autocomplete","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a trie support autocomplete?","answer":"Autocomplete locates the node representing a query prefix and then retrieves terminal keys from its descendant subtree, optionally applying ranking or result limits.","explanation":"The trie provides structural filtering by prefix while a separate ranking policy can determine which matches are returned first.","principle":"Separate candidate generation from candidate ranking.","correct_example":"Generate all words beginning with 'py' and rank them using application-specific frequency data.","invalid_example":"Assuming trie topology alone determines semantic relevance or popularity.","tradeoff":"Storing ranking metadata can speed result selection but increases mutation and consistency requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test result limits, empty results, duplicate keys, equal ranking values, and prefix normalization.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Prefix lookup"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B72-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_APPLICATION_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B72-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Prefix operations","concept":"Autocomplete ranking metadata","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must autocomplete ranking metadata be kept consistent with the stored keys?","answer":"If ranking information becomes stale or belongs to a deleted or replaced key, autocomplete can return incorrect results even though the trie topology remains valid.","explanation":"Structural correctness and ranking correctness are separate invariants.","failure_mode":"A deleted, stale, or unauthorized item appears in autocomplete results.","cause":"Ranking metadata was updated independently from key lifecycle state.","solution":"Tie ranking metadata to stable key identity and validate current state before returning results.","security_implication":"Autocomplete systems must not expose entries that the current caller is not authorized to discover.","testing_guidance":"Test deletion, authorization changes, ranking updates, and concurrent key replacement.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Autocomplete"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B72-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"DESIGN_AND_SECURITY_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B73-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Compressed tries","concept":"Radix tree","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a radix tree?","answer":"A radix tree is a compressed trie that combines chains of nodes with single children into edges labeled by sequences of symbols.","explanation":"Compression reduces structural nodes when many paths contain long non-branching segments.","principle":"Compress deterministic path segments without changing logical prefix semantics.","correct_example":"Represent the sequence 'inter' followed by 'national' as a compressed edge when no branching occurs within that segment.","invalid_example":"Compressing across a branching point and thereby losing distinct key prefixes.","tradeoff":"Compression can reduce memory overhead while making edge-splitting and update logic more complex.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Trie"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B71-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B73-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Compressed tries","concept":"Radix edge splitting","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What happens when a new key diverges inside a compressed radix edge?","answer":"The existing edge must be split at the common prefix so that the new branch and the existing suffix become separate children.","explanation":"Compression preserves common sequences, so inserting a key that diverges within an edge requires restoring an explicit branching point.","correct_example":"Split an edge labeled 'application' when inserting a key whose shared prefix ends after 'app'.","invalid_example":"Appending the new key after the entire existing edge even though their paths diverge earlier.","failure_mode":"The radix tree loses correct prefix structure.","solution":"Find the longest common prefix, split the edge, and attach both suffixes correctly.","testing_guidance":"Test divergence at the first symbol, middle symbol, final symbol, and exact edge boundary.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Radix tree"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B73-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B73-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Compressed tries","concept":"Trie versus radix tree","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the main trade-off between an ordinary trie and a radix tree?","answer":"An ordinary trie uses symbol-level paths and simpler branching semantics, while a radix tree compresses non-branching paths and can reduce structural overhead at the cost of more complex edge handling.","explanation":"Both preserve prefix-oriented organization but use different physical representations.","tradeoff":"The compressed representation can reduce memory usage for suitable key distributions but complicates insertion and deletion.","constraint":"Memory benefit depends on the actual prefix distribution and node representation.","testing_guidance":"Compare correctness under insertion and deletion before evaluating memory differences.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Trie","Radix tree"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B73-0001","D12-DS-B73-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_COMPARISON","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B74-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Ternary search trees","concept":"Ternary search tree","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a ternary search tree?","answer":"A ternary search tree stores one symbol per node and uses three child relationships to represent symbols less than, equal to, or greater than the node symbol.","explanation":"It combines aspects of trie-style character decomposition with binary-search-style comparisons.","principle":"Use character-level structure while avoiding a potentially large child collection per symbol node.","correct_example":"At a node containing character 'm', smaller characters go left, equal progression goes to the middle child, and larger characters go right.","invalid_example":"Treating the middle child as an arbitrary binary-tree child unrelated to the current character.","tradeoff":"Ternary search trees can reduce per-node branching storage compared with wide child arrays but add comparison-based traversal.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Trie","Binary search tree"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B71-0001","D12-DS-B41-0003"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B74-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Ternary search trees","concept":"Ternary search tree terminal state","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why does a ternary search tree still require key termination state?","answer":"The middle-character path can represent both a complete key and a prefix of longer keys, so explicit termination is needed to distinguish them.","explanation":"Character traversal alone does not identify whether the current sequence is a stored key.","correct_example":"Mark the final character of 'cat' as terminal while allowing the middle path to continue toward 'cater'.","invalid_example":"Returning every successfully traversed character sequence as a stored key.","testing_guidance":"Test prefix keys and longer keys sharing the same character path.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Ternary search tree"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B71-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B74-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Ternary search trees","concept":"Ternary search tree versus trie","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does a ternary search tree differ from a conventional trie?","answer":"A conventional trie typically branches among possible symbols at each prefix position, whereas a ternary search tree compares the current symbol and navigates among less-than, equal, and greater branches.","explanation":"Both support prefix-oriented string operations but expose different memory and comparison trade-offs.","tradeoff":"A ternary structure may avoid large sparse child arrays while requiring character comparisons during traversal.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Ternary search tree","Trie"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B74-0001","D12-DS-B71-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_COMPARISON","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B75-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Suffix structures","concept":"Suffix tree","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a suffix tree?","answer":"A suffix tree is a compressed trie representing the suffixes of a string, supporting specialized substring and pattern queries.","explanation":"Its structure captures suffix relationships so patterns can be located through path traversal.","principle":"Index all suffixes when repeated substring queries justify the preprocessing and memory cost.","correct_example":"Build a suffix structure for a document when many substring queries must be answered.","invalid_example":"Building a suffix tree for a single one-off substring search without considering simpler alternatives.","tradeoff":"Powerful substring queries require substantial construction and representation complexity.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Radix tree","Trie"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B73-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_STRING_DATA_STRUCTURE_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B75-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Suffix structures","concept":"Suffix array","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a suffix array?","answer":"A suffix array stores the starting positions of a string's suffixes in lexicographically sorted order.","explanation":"It represents suffix ordering compactly compared with an explicit suffix tree and is commonly paired with additional structures for advanced string queries.","principle":"Store suffix ordering separately from suffix content when compact indexing is valuable.","tradeoff":"Some queries require auxiliary structures or more computation than an explicit suffix tree.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["String indexing"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B75-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_STRING_DATA_STRUCTURE_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B75-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Suffix structures","concept":"Suffix tree versus suffix array","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should suffix trees and suffix arrays be distinguished?","answer":"Suffix trees provide an explicit hierarchical index over suffixes, while suffix arrays store sorted suffix starting positions and generally use a more compact representation.","explanation":"The choice depends on query requirements, memory constraints, implementation complexity, and available auxiliary structures.","tradeoff":"Suffix trees can offer rich structural navigation, while suffix arrays can provide better representation compactness.","constraint":"Do not infer practical memory or runtime superiority without specifying implementation and workload.","testing_guidance":"Validate substring and suffix-order queries against a trusted string-processing reference.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Suffix tree","Suffix array"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B75-0001","D12-DS-B75-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_COMPARISON","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B76-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Probabilistic structures","concept":"Bloom filter","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a Bloom filter?","answer":"A Bloom filter is a probabilistic membership structure that can report that an item is definitely absent or may be present, with false positives possible and false negatives excluded under correct operation.","explanation":"It stores compact hashed membership information rather than the original elements.","principle":"Use probabilistic filtering when compact approximate membership checks are acceptable.","correct_example":"Use a Bloom filter to avoid unnecessary database lookups for keys that are definitely absent.","invalid_example":"Using a Bloom filter as the sole authoritative store when false positives would be interpreted as proof of membership.","security_implication":"A false positive can cause extra work, but a correctly configured Bloom filter should not be treated as authorization evidence.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Hashing","Bitset"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B31-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PROBABILISTIC_DATA_STRUCTURE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B76-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Probabilistic structures","concept":"Bloom filter false positives","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What does a Bloom filter false positive mean?","answer":"A false positive means the filter reports that an item may be present even though the item was never inserted or is no longer represented by the authoritative source.","explanation":"False positives arise from hash collisions and filter saturation.","principle":"Approximate membership structures require explicit handling of uncertainty.","correct_example":"Use a positive Bloom-filter result as permission to perform an authoritative lookup.","invalid_example":"Treating a positive Bloom-filter result as definitive proof that the item exists.","testing_guidance":"Compare filter results against an authoritative set and measure false-positive behavior only through actual experiments.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Bloom filter"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B76-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PROBABILISTIC_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B76-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Probabilistic structures","concept":"Bloom filter saturation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happens when a Bloom filter becomes heavily saturated?","answer":"As more bits become set, the probability of false positives increases and the filter becomes less useful as a rejection mechanism.","explanation":"Saturation changes the filter's probabilistic behavior without necessarily corrupting its bit representation.","failure_mode":"Almost every membership query becomes a possible match.","cause":"The inserted population exceeds the design assumptions or the filter has been reused beyond its intended capacity.","solution":"Provision an appropriate filter, use scalable filters, rebuild, or choose a different structure according to workload requirements.","performance_implication":"Higher false-positive rates can increase downstream lookup and processing costs.","testing_guidance":"Measure false-positive rates against representative population sizes rather than assuming a fixed rate.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Bloom filter"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B76-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PROBABILISTIC_FAILURE_MODE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B77-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Bitsets","concept":"Bitset","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a bitset?","answer":"A bitset stores Boolean or bit-valued state compactly by packing many bits into machine words or another compact representation.","explanation":"Bitsets are useful when the domain is known or bounded and membership or Boolean operations dominate.","principle":"Exploit compact binary state when each element can be represented by a bit.","correct_example":"Represent a set of small integer IDs using one bit per possible ID.","invalid_example":"Using a bitset for arbitrary large object identifiers without an appropriate mapping layer.","performance_implication":"Bitwise operations can process multiple Boolean values together at the machine-word level.","tradeoff":"Direct indexing requires a manageable or mapped universe of possible values.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D12-DS-B76-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B77-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Bitsets","concept":"Bitset set operations","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are bitsets useful for set intersection and union?","answer":"Set operations can often be implemented as bitwise AND and OR across corresponding machine words.","explanation":"The representation converts set algebra into compact word-level operations.","principle":"Choose representations that map common logical operations directly to efficient primitive operations.","correct_example":"Intersect two equal-universe bitsets using bitwise AND.","invalid_example":"Assuming bitwise operations are valid when the two bitsets use different universe-to-bit mappings.","testing_guidance":"Verify identical universe mappings before comparing or combining bitsets.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Bitset"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B77-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B77-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Bitsets","concept":"Bitset indexing contract","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must a bitset define an explicit mapping between logical values and bit positions?","answer":"Without a stable mapping, the same bit position can represent different logical elements across structures or program versions.","explanation":"Correctness depends on consistent universe and indexing semantics.","failure_mode":"Set operations produce semantically invalid results despite bitwise correctness.","solution":"Version and validate the universe-to-bit mapping and reject incompatible bitsets.","testing_guidance":"Test version mismatches, changed universes, out-of-range values, and serialization/deserialization.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Bitset"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B77-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B78-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Disjoint sets","concept":"Disjoint-set union","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a disjoint-set union structure?","answer":"A disjoint-set union structure maintains a partition of elements into non-overlapping sets and supports operations that find the representative of a set and unite two sets.","explanation":"It is commonly used when connectivity components evolve through union operations.","principle":"Represent equivalence classes through representative-based forest structure.","correct_example":"Initially place each vertex in its own set and union endpoints of newly connected components.","invalid_example":"Using disjoint-set union when arbitrary edge deletion and dynamic connectivity queries are required without additional mechanisms.","tradeoff":"DSU is highly effective for incremental union operations but does not directly support general graph deletion semantics.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Trees"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B41-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B78-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Disjoint sets","concept":"Path compression","knowledge_type":"OPTIMIZATION","question":"What does path compression do in a disjoint-set structure?","answer":"Path compression changes parent links encountered during a find operation so that visited nodes point more directly toward the representative.","explanation":"Repeated find operations can therefore use flatter trees.","principle":"Use previous search paths to reduce future representative lookup work.","correct_example":"After finding the representative of a deep node, update the traversed nodes to reference the root representative.","invalid_example":"Changing parent links to arbitrary nodes without preserving the representative relationship.","performance_implication":"Path compression contributes to the very low amortized complexity of standard DSU operations.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Disjoint-set union"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B78-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_OPTIMIZATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B78-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Disjoint sets","concept":"Union by rank or size","knowledge_type":"OPTIMIZATION","question":"Why can union by rank or size improve disjoint-set performance?","answer":"It attaches the root of the smaller or lower-rank tree beneath the larger or higher-rank tree, limiting unnecessary tree growth.","explanation":"Combined with path compression, this keeps representative trees shallow over long operation sequences.","principle":"Control forest shape using structural metadata.","correct_example":"Attach the smaller component root under the larger component root.","invalid_example":"Always attaching one root under the other regardless of component sizes.","performance_implication":"Union by rank or size is part of the standard near-constant amortized DSU strategy.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Disjoint-set union","Path compression"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B78-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_OPTIMIZATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B79-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Disjoint sets","concept":"DSU representative invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariant must a disjoint-set representative structure preserve?","answer":"Following parent links from any element must eventually reach the representative of its current set, and parent relationships must not form cycles.","explanation":"A cyclic parent structure makes find operations fail to terminate or return a valid representative.","failure_mode":"Find loops indefinitely or returns an invalid representative.","cause":"Incorrect parent mutation during union or path compression.","solution":"Update only valid representatives and verify parent chains during testing.","testing_guidance":"Use randomized union sequences and periodically verify that every parent chain terminates at a root.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Disjoint-set union"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B78-0001","D12-DS-B78-0003"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B79-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Disjoint sets","concept":"DSU limitation under deletion","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why is ordinary disjoint-set union not a general-purpose dynamic connectivity structure?","answer":"Standard DSU efficiently supports merging components but does not directly undo arbitrary unions when an edge or relationship is deleted.","explanation":"The representation records merged equivalence classes rather than maintaining a fully dynamic graph topology.","invalid_example":"Using DSU alone to remove an edge and immediately recover all resulting connectivity components.","solution":"Use rollback techniques, offline processing, or a different dynamic connectivity structure when deletion is required.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Disjoint-set union"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B78-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONSTRAINT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B79-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Disjoint sets","concept":"DSU application to connected components","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can DSU identify connected components?","answer":"Initialize each vertex as its own set and union the endpoints of each undirected edge; vertices with the same representative belong to the same resulting component.","explanation":"The structure maintains connectivity equivalence under incremental edge additions.","correct_example":"Process every undirected edge (u,v) with union(u,v), then group vertices by find(v).","invalid_example":"Applying the same procedure to directed reachability and interpreting the result as directed connectivity.","constraint":"The semantics describe undirected connectivity unless a different equivalence relation is explicitly constructed.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Disjoint-set union"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B78-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_APPLICATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B80-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Structure selection","concept":"Prefix-query structure selection","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Which structure should be considered for heavy prefix-query workloads?","answer":"Tries, radix trees, or ternary search trees are candidates when prefix navigation is central; the choice depends on memory constraints, alphabet characteristics, update patterns, and implementation requirements.","explanation":"No single prefix structure dominates every workload.","alternative":"Use a sorted map or array when prefix queries are infrequent and implementation simplicity is more important.","tradeoff":"Specialized prefix structures add memory and maintenance complexity in exchange for prefix-oriented access.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate correctness first, then benchmark representative query distributions if performance is a requirement.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Trie","Radix tree","Ternary search tree"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B73-0003","D12-DS-B74-0003"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B80-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Structure selection","concept":"Approximate-membership structure selection","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When can a Bloom filter complement an authoritative set?","answer":"A Bloom filter can act as a compact preliminary rejection layer when false positives are acceptable and definitive membership can be checked by another authoritative structure.","explanation":"The filter should reduce unnecessary authoritative lookups rather than replace authoritative state.","correct_example":"Check the Bloom filter first and query the database only when the filter says the key may exist.","invalid_example":"Grant access because the Bloom filter returned positive membership.","security_implication":"Probabilistic membership must not become an authorization source unless the security model explicitly permits its uncertainty semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test both positive and negative paths against authoritative membership state.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Bloom filter","Hash table"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B76-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_APPLICATION_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B80-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Specialized Data Structures","subtopic":"Structure selection","concept":"Specialized structure selection matrix","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What factors should determine selection of a specialized data structure?","answer":"Selection should consider required operations, correctness guarantees, data distribution, memory limits, mutation frequency, concurrency model, security semantics, implementation complexity, version constraints, and measured workload performance when performance matters.","explanation":"Choosing solely from asymptotic complexity can miss representation overhead, operational constraints, and application semantics.","principle":"Select structures from explicit workload and contract requirements rather than popularity or isolated complexity figures.","correct_example":"Choose a radix tree because prefix queries dominate and memory measurements justify compression.","invalid_example":"Choose a Fibonacci heap solely because one operation has a stronger amortized bound without examining the actual workload.","tradeoff":"More specialized structures can provide stronger operation-specific behavior while increasing implementation and verification complexity.","testing_guidance":"Create a structure-selection test matrix covering required operations, edge cases, resource constraints, and correctness invariants.","verification_guidance":"Record the assumptions supporting the selection so that a future workload change can trigger reevaluation.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Structure selection"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B56-0002","D12-DS-B73-0003","D12-DS-B76-0001","D12-DS-B79-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

