{"record_id":"D12-DS-B81-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Performance","subtopic":"Complexity","concept":"Operation complexity versus workload","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should data-structure complexity be evaluated against the actual workload?","answer":"A data structure should be evaluated according to the frequency, distribution, and importance of its required operations rather than by considering one operation in isolation.","explanation":"A structure optimized for lookup may impose costly insertion, deletion, memory, or ordering behavior that matters more for another workload.","principle":"Optimize the workload that actually constrains the system.","correct_example":"Evaluate lookup, insertion, deletion, iteration, and memory requirements together before selecting a structure.","invalid_example":"Selecting a structure solely because its lookup operation has the smallest asymptotic complexity.","tradeoff":"A better bound for one operation can increase cost elsewhere.","testing_guidance":"Create a workload model before performance testing.","verification_guidance":"Record workload assumptions alongside the selected structure.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["D11"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B80-0003"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B81-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Performance","subtopic":"Complexity","concept":"Amortized complexity","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should amortized complexity be interpreted for data structures?","answer":"Amortized complexity describes the average cost per operation over a defined sequence of operations rather than guaranteeing that every individual operation has that cost.","explanation":"Some operations may be substantially more expensive while the aggregate sequence remains within the amortized bound.","correct_example":"A dynamic array resize may be expensive individually while append operations have a favorable amortized bound under the standard resizing model.","invalid_example":"Treating an amortized bound as a guarantee that every single operation has the same cost.","failure_mode":"Latency-sensitive applications incorrectly assume worst-case per-operation guarantees.","solution":"Distinguish worst-case, amortized, average-case, and empirical latency requirements.","performance_implication":"Amortized guarantees may be insufficient for strict tail-latency requirements.","testing_guidance":"Measure individual operation latency distributions when tail latency matters.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["D11"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B11-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B81-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Performance","subtopic":"Complexity","concept":"Worst-case versus expected performance","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why must worst-case and expected data-structure performance be distinguished?","answer":"Worst-case performance describes the most expensive permitted execution, while expected or average performance depends on assumptions about inputs, randomness, or workload distributions.","explanation":"A system requiring deterministic guarantees cannot substitute an expected bound without explicitly accepting its assumptions.","correct_example":"Document whether a hash-table lookup guarantee depends on hashing assumptions or a deterministic collision-management strategy.","invalid_example":"Reporting expected constant-time behavior as an unconditional worst-case guarantee.","constraint":"Expected bounds require their probabilistic or workload assumptions to remain valid.","testing_guidance":"Test adversarial inputs separately from representative workloads.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Hash tables","D11"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B31-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_COMPLEXITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B82-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Memory Representation","subtopic":"Object layout","concept":"Representation overhead","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why can the memory cost of a data structure exceed the logical data size?","answer":"Nodes, object headers, pointers or references, alignment, metadata, allocator overhead, capacity slack, and auxiliary indexes can consume additional memory beyond the payload.","explanation":"Asymptotic space complexity generally describes growth behavior rather than exact byte-level representation cost.","correct_example":"Account for node references and per-object metadata when estimating the memory footprint of a linked structure.","invalid_example":"Assuming that storing one integer necessarily consumes exactly the mathematical size of that integer.","tradeoff":"More metadata can provide faster operations while increasing memory usage.","testing_guidance":"Use target-runtime memory measurement when exact footprint matters.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Memory management"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B15-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B82-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Memory Representation","subtopic":"Contiguous storage","concept":"Contiguous representation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can contiguous storage have performance advantages?","answer":"Contiguous storage can improve spatial locality because nearby logical elements may occupy nearby memory locations, allowing hardware and runtime mechanisms to access them efficiently.","explanation":"The practical benefit depends on the language runtime, element representation, access pattern, and hardware.","correct_example":"Sequentially process elements in a contiguous numeric buffer.","invalid_example":"Assuming every contiguous structure automatically outperforms every linked structure regardless of workload.","performance_implication":"Sequential access may benefit from locality, but the exact improvement must be measured for a specific environment.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled benchmarks when claiming an empirical locality advantage.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Arrays","Memory representation"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B15-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B82-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Memory Representation","subtopic":"Indirection","concept":"Pointer indirection cost","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can pointer indirection affect data-structure performance?","answer":"Additional indirection can require extra memory accesses and may reduce locality, although the practical effect depends on hardware, runtime, object layout, and access pattern.","explanation":"The existence of a pointer does not establish a specific performance penalty without an execution environment and workload.","correct_example":"Treat pointer-heavy graph or linked-node structures as candidates for locality-sensitive evaluation.","invalid_example":"Assigning a fixed percentage slowdown to pointer indirection without measurement.","performance_implication":"Indirection can influence cache behavior and memory-access latency.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark representative access patterns on the target environment before making quantitative claims.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Pointer-based structures"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B82-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"SYSTEMS_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B83-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Memory and Cache","subtopic":"Locality","concept":"Spatial locality","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is spatial locality in data-structure access?","answer":"Spatial locality is the tendency for accesses to locations near a recently accessed location to occur relatively soon afterward.","explanation":"Data structures that place frequently traversed elements near one another can potentially benefit from this behavior.","correct_example":"Sequential iteration through contiguous elements exhibits strong spatial locality at the logical access level.","invalid_example":"Assuming that logically adjacent objects are physically adjacent in every runtime representation.","constraint":"Physical locality depends on the implementation and memory-management system.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Memory representation"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B82-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEMS_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B83-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Memory and Cache","subtopic":"Locality","concept":"Temporal locality","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is temporal locality?","answer":"Temporal locality is the tendency for recently accessed data or instructions to be accessed again within a relatively short period.","explanation":"Caching can benefit workloads that repeatedly reuse the same data.","correct_example":"Repeatedly querying a frequently accessed node or entry can exhibit temporal reuse.","invalid_example":"Assuming that every repeated logical access remains physically cached.","performance_implication":"Actual cache residency depends on the complete workload and hardware hierarchy.","testing_guidance":"Use hardware or runtime profiling when cache behavior is a material requirement.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Memory and Cache"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B83-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEMS_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B83-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Memory and Cache","subtopic":"Access patterns","concept":"Sequential versus random access","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why can sequential and random access behave differently for the same data structure?","answer":"Sequential access may expose stronger locality and predictable traversal, while random access can produce less predictable memory-access patterns and different cache behavior.","explanation":"This is a systems-level distinction and should not be converted into a universal performance ranking without measurements.","correct_example":"Benchmark sequential scanning separately from random indexed access when evaluating a large array.","invalid_example":"Using only sequential benchmarks to represent a workload dominated by random access.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark each representative access pattern independently.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Memory and Cache"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B82-0002","D12-DS-B83-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B84-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Memory Management","subtopic":"Capacity","concept":"Capacity slack","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why can capacity exceed logical size in dynamic structures?","answer":"Dynamic structures may reserve additional storage so that future insertions can occur without reallocating on every operation.","explanation":"Reserved capacity trades additional memory for fewer resizing events.","correct_example":"A dynamic array has logical size 100 and capacity 160, leaving room for additional elements.","invalid_example":"Treating unused capacity as initialized logical elements.","tradeoff":"More reserved capacity can reduce resize frequency while increasing memory consumption.","testing_guidance":"Test invariants separately for logical size and physical capacity.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Dynamic arrays"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B11-0002","D12-DS-B12-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_STRUCTURE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B84-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Memory Management","subtopic":"Resizing","concept":"Resize strategy","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What should be considered when choosing a dynamic-array resize strategy?","answer":"Consider reallocation frequency, memory overhead, copying or relocation cost, allocator behavior, workload growth patterns, and latency requirements.","explanation":"The exact growth policy is an implementation choice and should not be assumed to be identical across languages or libraries.","correct_example":"Document the runtime or library's actual growth semantics when relying on capacity behavior.","invalid_example":"Assuming that every dynamic array doubles capacity exactly on every resize.","version_awareness":"Growth policies may be implementation-specific and can change across runtime versions.","testing_guidance":"Inspect authoritative implementation documentation or source for version-specific behavior.","language":"General","version_range":"Implementation-dependent","dependencies":["Dynamic arrays"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B81-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"IMPLEMENTATION_DEPENDENT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B84-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Memory Management","subtopic":"Fragmentation","concept":"Memory fragmentation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can allocation patterns affect memory behavior?","answer":"Repeated allocation and release of differently sized objects can create fragmentation or allocator-specific overhead, potentially affecting memory efficiency and allocation behavior.","explanation":"The exact effect depends on the allocator, runtime, operating system, object lifetimes, and workload.","failure_mode":"Memory usage grows or allocation behavior degrades despite logical data volume remaining stable.","cause":"Object lifetime and allocation patterns interact poorly with the memory-management system.","solution":"Reduce unnecessary allocation, reuse storage where appropriate, choose suitable representations, and profile the target runtime.","performance_implication":"Allocation behavior can become a significant component of application performance.","testing_guidance":"Use target-environment memory profiling rather than assuming fragmentation from logical structure alone.","language":"General","version_range":"Implementation-dependent","dependencies":["Memory representation"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B82-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"SYSTEMS_PERFORMANCE_CASE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B85-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Concurrency","subtopic":"Shared data structures","concept":"Concurrent mutation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is concurrent mutation of a shared data structure dangerous?","answer":"Without an appropriate synchronization or ownership model, concurrent mutations can violate structural invariants, lose updates, expose inconsistent state, or produce data races where the execution model permits them.","explanation":"Thread safety is a property of the complete implementation and memory model, not merely the abstract data structure.","correct_example":"Use a documented thread-safe implementation or explicit synchronization around shared mutation.","invalid_example":"Assuming that a structure is thread-safe because individual read or write operations appear simple.","failure_mode":"Structural corruption or inconsistent observations under concurrent access.","solution":"Define ownership, synchronization, atomicity, and visibility requirements before exposing shared mutable state.","security_implication":"Corrupted shared state can become a security boundary failure when authorization or isolation data is stored in the structure.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic concurrency tests plus stress and race-detection tooling where supported.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Data structure invariants"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B50-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCURRENCY_SAFETY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B85-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Concurrency","subtopic":"Iteration","concept":"Concurrent modification during iteration","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What can happen when a collection is structurally modified during iteration?","answer":"Depending on the implementation, concurrent or reentrant structural modification may invalidate iteration state, raise an error, produce weakly consistent behavior, or yield implementation-specific results.","explanation":"Iterator invalidation rules are library- and language-specific.","correct_example":"Follow the target collection's documented safe-removal or concurrent-iteration mechanism.","invalid_example":"Assuming that removing elements through an unrelated mutation path is always safe during iteration.","solution":"Use documented iterator operations, snapshots, synchronization, or concurrent collection semantics as appropriate.","version_awareness":"Exact behavior must be verified against the target library and version.","testing_guidance":"Test mutation during iteration explicitly.","language":"General","version_range":"Implementation-dependent","dependencies":["Collections","Concurrency"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B85-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"IMPLEMENTATION_DEPENDENT_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B85-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Concurrency","subtopic":"Atomicity","concept":"Compound data-structure operations","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can individually atomic operations still fail to provide a safe compound operation?","answer":"A sequence of individually atomic operations may have an interleaving point that allows another actor to change the state between the operations.","explanation":"Correctness may require the entire logical transaction to be atomic rather than each primitive operation independently.","correct_example":"Atomically perform a check-and-update operation when the invariant requires the checked value to remain unchanged until the update.","invalid_example":"Performing check-then-act against shared mutable state without protecting the complete sequence.","failure_mode":"Time-of-check-to-time-of-use inconsistency.","cause":"The logical invariant spans multiple operations.","solution":"Use an atomic compound operation, transaction, lock, or suitable concurrent data structure.","security_implication":"Check-then-act races can become authorization or resource-accounting vulnerabilities.","testing_guidance":"Inject competing operations between the logical check and update.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Concurrency","Data structure invariants"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B85-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"SECURITY_AND_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B86-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Security","subtopic":"Input handling","concept":"Untrusted key handling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should data structures handling untrusted keys consider adversarial input?","answer":"Attackers may intentionally provide inputs that trigger expensive operations, excessive collisions, memory growth, or pathological structural behavior.","explanation":"A theoretically efficient structure can still become a denial-of-service vector when its input assumptions are controllable by an attacker.","correct_example":"Bound collection size and evaluate collision or depth behavior for externally supplied keys.","invalid_example":"Assuming normal random-looking input when an attacker controls the keys.","security_implication":"Data-structure complexity is part of the attack surface when input controls structure shape or resource consumption.","testing_guidance":"Include adversarial, oversized, duplicate, collision-heavy, and deeply nested inputs where relevant.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Hash tables","Trees","Tries"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B31-0001","D12-DS-B41-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"DEFENSIVE_SECURITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B86-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Security","subtopic":"Resource exhaustion","concept":"Unbounded data-structure growth","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is unbounded growth of a data structure a security concern?","answer":"An attacker or faulty workload can consume memory, CPU, storage, or other resources by causing the structure to grow without effective limits.","explanation":"Resource exhaustion can affect availability even when the structure remains logically correct.","correct_example":"Apply explicit limits, quotas, expiration, backpressure, or eviction policies where untrusted input can create state.","invalid_example":"Allowing an externally controlled cache or queue to grow indefinitely.","failure_mode":"Resource exhaustion denial of service.","cause":"Missing capacity or lifecycle policy.","solution":"Define and enforce resource budgets and failure behavior.","security_implication":"Resource limits should be treated as part of the security contract for externally influenced state.","testing_guidance":"Test maximum capacity, rejection behavior, eviction, and recovery after exhaustion.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Queues","Maps","Caches"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B86-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"DEFENSIVE_SECURITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B86-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Security","subtopic":"Information exposure","concept":"Sensitive data retention","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can data-structure lifetime matter for security?","answer":"A structure may retain sensitive data after it is logically no longer needed, increasing the period during which the information remains accessible to the process or subsystem.","explanation":"Logical deletion and physical memory behavior are distinct concerns.","correct_example":"Define explicit retention and disposal semantics for sensitive cached or queued information.","invalid_example":"Assuming that removing a reference immediately guarantees physical erasure from all memory representations.","constraint":"Actual memory clearing behavior depends on the language runtime, allocator, compiler, and representation.","security_implication":"Sensitive-state lifecycle should be analyzed separately from ordinary collection correctness.","testing_guidance":"Verify retention behavior according to the guarantees actually provided by the target environment.","language":"General","version_range":"Implementation-dependent","dependencies":["Memory representation"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B82-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"SECURITY_LIFECYCLE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B87-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Testing","subtopic":"Invariant testing","concept":"Structural invariant testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should a data structure's structural invariants be tested?","answer":"Tests should verify properties that must remain true after every valid mutation, not merely the final output of selected examples.","explanation":"Invariant testing detects corruption closer to the operation that introduced it.","correct_example":"After each randomized tree mutation, verify ordering, parent-child consistency, node reachability, and metadata constraints.","invalid_example":"Testing only a final lookup result after thousands of mutations without checking intermediate structure validity.","testing_guidance":"Run invariant checks after individual mutations in debug or property-based test modes.","verification_guidance":"Record each invariant explicitly and map it to one or more tests.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["D11","Data structure invariants"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B50-0002","D12-DS-B79-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B87-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Testing","subtopic":"Property-based testing","concept":"Model-based data-structure testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How can a simple reference model test a complex data structure?","answer":"Operations can be applied both to the implementation under test and to a simpler trusted model, with observable results compared after each operation.","explanation":"The model need not use the same internal algorithm or representation.","correct_example":"Compare a custom ordered structure against a sorted reference representation for equivalent insert, delete, and query sequences.","invalid_example":"Using the same implementation internally for both the system under test and the reference model.","testing_guidance":"Generate sequences containing valid, duplicate, absent, boundary, and randomized operations.","verification_guidance":"Shrink failing operation sequences to the smallest reproducible counterexample where the testing framework supports shrinking.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Property-based testing","Reference models"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B87-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B87-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Testing","subtopic":"Metamorphic testing","concept":"Data-structure metamorphic relations","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What is a metamorphic relation for a data structure?","answer":"A metamorphic relation specifies how outputs or invariants should change, or remain unchanged, when a valid transformation is applied to the input or operation sequence.","explanation":"This is useful when a complete expected output is difficult to enumerate.","correct_example":"Permuting independent insert operations should preserve set membership for a mathematical set.","invalid_example":"Assuming insertion-order independence for an ordered sequence structure where order is part of the contract.","testing_guidance":"Define metamorphic relations from the abstract data-type semantics, not from assumptions about implementation.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Data structure semantics"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B87-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B88-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Testing","subtopic":"Boundary testing","concept":"Empty-state testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why must empty-state behavior be tested for every data structure?","answer":"Empty state commonly exercises special branches for lookup, deletion, iteration, size reporting, and initialization.","explanation":"A structure that behaves correctly for populated data can still fail when no elements exist.","correct_example":"Test find, remove, iterate, peek, and size operations immediately after initialization.","invalid_example":"Assuming non-empty tests implicitly cover empty-state behavior.","testing_guidance":"Include empty initialization and repeated empty-state operations.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Data structure testing"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B87-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"TESTING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B88-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Testing","subtopic":"Boundary testing","concept":"Duplicate-element testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why must duplicate elements be tested explicitly?","answer":"Different abstract data types assign different semantics to duplicates, such as preserving them, rejecting them, replacing values, or collapsing them into one membership entry.","explanation":"Duplicate handling is part of the data-structure contract.","correct_example":"Verify whether a set remains size one after inserting the same logical value multiple times.","invalid_example":"Assuming all collections either preserve or reject duplicates uniformly.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate insertion, duplicate deletion, counting, iteration, and replacement behavior according to the contract.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Sets","Maps","Lists"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B31-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"TESTING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B88-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Testing","subtopic":"Boundary testing","concept":"Extreme-depth testing","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should deeply nested structures be tested?","answer":"Deep trees, recursive structures, or long chains can expose recursion limits, stack exhaustion, traversal bugs, and pathological performance.","explanation":"Average-depth tests may not exercise the resource boundaries of recursive or pointer-linked structures.","correct_example":"Construct a deliberately deep tree and verify traversal behavior using an implementation appropriate for the target depth.","invalid_example":"Assuming balanced-depth behavior when inputs can construct highly skewed structures.","security_implication":"Attacker-controlled depth can become a resource-exhaustion vector.","testing_guidance":"Test maximum supported depth and failure behavior beyond that limit.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Trees","Recursion","D11"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B41-0003"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"EDGE_CASE_AND_SECURITY_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B89-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Failure Analysis","subtopic":"Corruption","concept":"Structural corruption diagnosis","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should structural data-structure corruption be diagnosed?","answer":"Identify the first violated invariant, trace the mutation that introduced it, minimize the operation sequence, and compare the implementation against a reference model when possible.","explanation":"The final corrupted state may be far removed from the operation that caused the defect.","correct_example":"Capture an operation log and validate invariants after each mutation to locate the first divergence.","invalid_example":"Repairing the final pointer arrangement without determining which operation violated the invariant.","solution":"Use invariant checks, operation replay, minimized reproductions, and differential testing.","testing_guidance":"Persist enough deterministic operation information to reproduce failures.","verification_guidance":"Verify both the fix and the invariant that the fix is intended to preserve.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Invariant testing","Reference models"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B87-0001","D12-DS-B87-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"DEBUGGING_AND_VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B89-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Failure Analysis","subtopic":"Performance regression","concept":"Performance regression diagnosis","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a data-structure performance regression be investigated?","answer":"Compare the workload, implementation version, input distribution, operation mix, memory behavior, environment, and measurement methodology before attributing the regression to the data structure itself.","explanation":"Performance changes can originate from application workload, runtime, allocator, compiler, hardware, or measurement conditions.","correct_example":"Reproduce the same benchmark workload and environment across versions before comparing results.","invalid_example":"Concluding that a new data structure is slower from one uncontrolled timing measurement.","performance_implication":"Performance claims require reproducible measurement conditions.","testing_guidance":"Use repeated controlled measurements and report uncertainty or variance where appropriate.","verification_guidance":"Record environment, workload, version, and measurement method with benchmark results.","language":"General","version_range":"Implementation-dependent","dependencies":["Performance testing"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B81-0001","D12-DS-B83-0003"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"PERFORMANCE_ENGINEERING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B89-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Failure Analysis","subtopic":"Semantic bugs","concept":"Correct structure but wrong abstraction","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"How can a data structure be internally correct but still be wrong for the application?","answer":"A structure can preserve its own invariants while violating the application's required semantics, ordering, lifecycle, authorization, consistency, or concurrency contract.","explanation":"Structural correctness is necessary but not sufficient for system-level correctness.","correct_example":"A mathematically correct set is still inappropriate when the application requires duplicate-preserving event history.","invalid_example":"Declaring a data-structure implementation correct solely because all internal invariants pass.","failure_mode":"Application behavior violates domain requirements despite structurally valid state.","solution":"Verify both abstract data-type invariants and application-level contracts.","testing_guidance":"Map data-structure behavior to explicit domain requirements and contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Maintain separate structural and semantic verification records.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["D11","Data structure contracts"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B87-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURAL_ANTIPATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B90-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Selection","subtopic":"Decision framework","concept":"Operation-first selection","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is a practical process for selecting a data structure?","answer":"Define required operations, correctness semantics, expected workload, size bounds, mutation patterns, ordering requirements, concurrency model, resource limits, security constraints, and target runtime before comparing candidate structures.","explanation":"The selection process should start from requirements and eliminate structures that cannot satisfy mandatory constraints.","correct_example":"Define required prefix search, update frequency, memory budget, and authorization semantics before selecting among trie variants.","invalid_example":"Starting with a favorite data structure and adapting requirements to fit it.","tradeoff":"A requirement-driven process may select a less familiar structure when its contract better matches the workload.","verification_guidance":"Record selection assumptions so future changes can trigger reassessment.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["D11"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B80-0003"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURAL_SELECTION_PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B90-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Selection","subtopic":"Decision framework","concept":"Theoretical complexity is not empirical performance","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must theoretical complexity and empirical performance remain separate evidence types?","answer":"Asymptotic complexity describes growth under a specified computational model, while empirical performance measures actual execution under a particular implementation, environment, and workload.","explanation":"The two forms of evidence answer different questions and cannot be silently substituted for one another.","correct_example":"State that an operation is theoretically logarithmic while marking actual latency as UNKNOWN until measured.","invalid_example":"Claiming a measured speedup from an asymptotic complexity comparison alone.","performance_implication":"Quantitative performance claims require actual measurement or authoritative empirical evidence.","testing_guidance":"Separate complexity analysis from benchmark records.","verification_guidance":"Tag performance claims as THEORETICAL, EMPIRICAL, ESTIMATED, or UNKNOWN.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["D11"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B81-0002","D12-DS-B89-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"EVIDENCE_DISCIPLINE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B90-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data Structure Selection","subtopic":"Final validation","concept":"Structure-selection verification record","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence should support a production data-structure selection?","answer":"The decision should record requirements, candidate structures, rejected alternatives and reasons, complexity assumptions, workload assumptions, security constraints, resource limits, implementation/version constraints, tests, and empirical measurements when performance is material.","explanation":"A reproducible selection record prevents undocumented assumptions from becoming hidden architecture dependencies.","correct_example":"Record why a bounded hash map was selected, what collision and capacity assumptions apply, and which tests verify its contract.","invalid_example":"Recording only the final structure name without its requirements or evidence.","verification_guidance":"Treat the selection record as a reviewable engineering artifact and update it when assumptions materially change.","testing_guidance":"Link relevant correctness, boundary, security, concurrency, and performance tests.","language":"General","version_range":"Language-independent","dependencies":["Data structure selection","Verification"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B90-0001","D12-DS-B90-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_VERIFICATION_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

