{"record_id":"D12-DS-B91-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data-structure interoperability","subtopic":"composition","concept":"Composable data structures","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should data structures be composable rather than selected in isolation?","answer":"A complete system often combines multiple structures because different operations have different access requirements. Composition allows each structure to serve the workload for which its representation and operations are appropriate.","explanation":"Examples include a hash map whose values contain lists, a graph represented by adjacency maps and sets, or a priority queue backed by an array-based heap. Composition must preserve the invariants of every component.","principle":"Optimize the composed workload rather than treating each data structure as an isolated artifact.","correct_example":"Use a map for direct key lookup and a list when ordered iteration is also required, with an explicit consistency policy.","invalid_example":"Replacing every nested structure with one universal structure without analyzing the required operations.","problem":"A composite structure becomes inconsistent when one component is updated without updating dependent components.","failure_mode":"Cross-structure invariant violation.","cause":"Uncoordinated mutation or unclear ownership.","solution":"Define ownership, mutation boundaries, invariants, and update protocols for each component.","alternative":"Use a single structure when it can satisfy all required operations without disproportionate cost.","tradeoff":"Composition increases flexibility but also increases invariant and synchronization complexity.","constraint":"Component semantics must remain explicit.","edge_case":"Partial update after an exception can leave only some components changed.","security_implication":"Inconsistent authorization or indexing structures can expose stale or unauthorized data.","performance_implication":"Composition can add indirection and duplicate metadata.","testing_guidance":"Test component invariants and cross-component consistency after every mutating operation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that all documented composite invariants hold before and after successful and failed mutations.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B81-0001"],"prerequisites":["Data-structure invariants"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B88-0001","D12-DS-B90-0003"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B81-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_principle","source":"Established data-structure composition principle"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B91-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data-structure interoperability","subtopic":"adapters","concept":"Adapter-based interoperability","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should an adapter be used between data structures?","answer":"Use an adapter when an existing structure provides the required semantics through a different interface or representation and conversion can preserve the required invariants.","explanation":"Adapters can expose queue semantics over a deque or provide a set-like interface over a map when keys represent membership. The adapter must document whether ordering, identity, duplicate handling, and mutation semantics are preserved.","principle":"Adapt interfaces deliberately; do not conceal semantic differences.","correct_example":"Expose FIFO operations through a deque while explicitly defining how empty-state behavior is represented.","invalid_example":"Wrapping a structure and silently changing duplicate or ordering semantics.","problem":"Consumers assume semantics that the underlying structure does not provide.","failure_mode":"Semantic abstraction mismatch.","cause":"Interface compatibility mistaken for behavioral compatibility.","solution":"Specify and test semantic guarantees independently of the adapter interface.","alternative":"Implement a native structure when the adapter would require expensive translation or violate required guarantees.","tradeoff":"Adapters reduce implementation duplication but may obscure cost and semantics.","constraint":"The adapter must preserve or explicitly redefine documented behavior.","edge_case":"An empty underlying structure may map to different error or sentinel semantics.","security_implication":"Adapters must not bypass access-control or validation guarantees of the underlying structure.","performance_implication":"Translation layers can add allocation, copying, or lookup overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test behavioral equivalence for all supported operations and boundary conditions.","verification_guidance":"Verify both interface-level behavior and underlying invariant preservation.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Interface contracts"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B90-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_principle","source":"Adapter and abstraction contract principles"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B91-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Data-structure interoperability","subtopic":"ownership","concept":"Ownership and mutation boundaries","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should be defined when multiple components reference the same mutable structure?","answer":"The design should explicitly define ownership, permitted mutation, aliasing expectations, and lifecycle responsibility.","explanation":"Shared mutable references can create hidden coupling. Clear ownership prevents one component from invalidating assumptions made by another.","principle":"Shared state requires an explicit ownership and mutation contract.","correct_example":"Document that one component owns mutation while other components receive read-only access.","invalid_example":"Multiple components independently mutate the same structure without coordination.","problem":"Hidden aliasing causes unexpected structural changes.","failure_mode":"Aliasing-induced invariant failure.","cause":"Undocumented shared mutability.","solution":"Use ownership rules, encapsulation, immutability, copying, or synchronization as appropriate.","alternative":"Use immutable snapshots for consumers that do not need live mutation.","tradeoff":"Copying reduces aliasing risk but increases memory and update costs.","constraint":"The chosen ownership model must match the language and concurrency model.","edge_case":"A shallow copy may still share nested mutable structures.","security_implication":"Unauthorized mutation can corrupt policy or access-control state.","performance_implication":"Defensive copying can materially increase memory and CPU usage for large structures.","testing_guidance":"Test aliasing, mutation isolation, and lifecycle behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that documented owners are the only permitted mutation paths.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Mutation semantics"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B87-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_principle","source":"Ownership and aliasing principles"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B92-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Serialization and persistence","subtopic":"semantic preservation","concept":"Serialization is not automatically semantic preservation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must be preserved when serializing a data structure?","answer":"Serialization should preserve the state and semantics explicitly required by the persistence contract, including ordering, uniqueness, key relationships, nullability, and version information where applicable.","explanation":"A serialized representation can preserve values while losing ordering, object identity, type distinctions, or invariants. The persistence contract must identify which properties are authoritative.","principle":"Persist required semantics, not merely bytes representing visible values.","correct_example":"Serialize a priority structure with enough information to reconstruct its ordering invariant or document a deterministic rebuild step.","invalid_example":"Serializing only element values when priority metadata is required for correct reconstruction.","problem":"Deserialized data is structurally valid but behaviorally different.","failure_mode":"Semantic persistence loss.","cause":"Incomplete serialization contract.","solution":"Define serialized state, reconstruction rules, invariants, and compatibility requirements.","alternative":"Persist canonical logical records and rebuild derived indexes after loading.","tradeoff":"Rebuilding reduces persisted derived state but increases load-time computation.","constraint":"The reconstruction procedure must be deterministic where deterministic behavior is required.","edge_case":"Derived metadata can become stale while primary records remain correct.","security_implication":"Persisted structures must not restore unauthorized or obsolete security state.","performance_implication":"Persisting redundant indexes increases storage and serialization cost.","testing_guidance":"Round-trip serialize and deserialize representative and adversarial structures.","verification_guidance":"Compare documented semantic invariants before serialization and after reconstruction.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Data-structure invariants"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B88-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"systems_principle","source":"Persistence and invariant-preservation principles"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B92-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Serialization and persistence","subtopic":"Python","concept":"Python pickle security boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security constraint applies to Python pickle deserialization?","answer":"Python pickle is not safe for untrusted or tampered input because unpickling can execute arbitrary code through crafted serialized data.","explanation":"The Python documentation explicitly warns that only trusted pickle data should be unpickled. Safer interoperable formats such as JSON may be more appropriate for untrusted data.","principle":"Treat object deserialization as a security-sensitive operation and authenticate or reject untrusted serialized input.","correct_example":"Only unpickle data from a trusted, integrity-protected source with an explicit trust boundary.","invalid_example":"Unpickling arbitrary user-uploaded bytes.","problem":"Attacker-controlled serialized input reaches the deserializer.","failure_mode":"Deserialization-based code execution.","cause":"Treating serialization as passive data parsing.","solution":"Reject untrusted pickle input or replace it with a format and parser appropriate to the trust boundary.","alternative":"Use a constrained data format such as JSON when its type model is sufficient.","tradeoff":"Safer formats may represent fewer language-specific object types directly.","constraint":"Security properties depend on the parser and format, not merely the file extension.","edge_case":"A file can appear local or familiar while still being attacker-modified.","security_implication":"CRITICAL; deserialization is an executable trust boundary.","performance_implication":"Alternative formats may have different size and conversion costs.","testing_guidance":"Include malicious and tampered serialized-input tests in security testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that untrusted input cannot reach an unsafe object deserializer.","language":"Python","framework":"Python standard library","version":"3.14","version_range":"3.x","dependencies":["pickle"],"prerequisites":["Serialization security"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B88-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D12-DS-B92-0003"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Python pickle documentation","source_ref":"turn0search5"}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B92-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Serialization and persistence","subtopic":"format selection","concept":"Interoperable versus language-specific serialization","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should a serialization format be selected?","answer":"Select based on trust boundary, interoperability requirements, type fidelity, schema evolution, size, performance requirements, and ecosystem support.","explanation":"Python documentation distinguishes JSON as an interoperable text format from pickle as a Python-specific binary object format. Rust Serde similarly separates serialization frameworks from data formats.","principle":"Choose a serialization format from system requirements rather than convenience alone.","correct_example":"Use an interoperable schema-based representation for cross-language communication when the required data model is supported.","invalid_example":"Using a language-specific object serializer for a public cross-language protocol without a compatibility contract.","problem":"A serialized format becomes coupled to one implementation or runtime.","failure_mode":"Portability failure.","cause":"Serialization mechanism chosen without deployment-context analysis.","solution":"Define the protocol boundary and choose a format accordingly.","alternative":"Use a language-specific format for tightly controlled internal persistence where its constraints are acceptable.","tradeoff":"Interoperability can require explicit schema and conversion logic.","constraint":"Format support and guarantees vary by language and library version.","edge_case":"A format may be syntactically interoperable while semantic types remain incompatible.","security_implication":"Parser and deserializer behavior must be included in the threat model.","performance_implication":"Text, binary, schema-based, and object serializers have different overhead profiles; measure when performance matters.","testing_guidance":"Test cross-language round trips and schema evolution.","verification_guidance":"Verify compatibility against the documented protocol contract.","language":"multi-language","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B92-0002"],"prerequisites":["Serialization formats"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B92-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Python persistence documentation","source_ref":"turn0search1"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Rust Serde documentation","source_ref":"turn0search0"}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B93-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Version compatibility","subtopic":"serialized state","concept":"Versioned persistence contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should persisted data structures have explicit version metadata?","answer":"Version metadata allows loaders to distinguish representations, apply compatible migrations, reject unsupported versions, and avoid silently interpreting old state using new semantics.","explanation":"A data structure implementation can change while persisted state remains on disk. Explicit versioning makes compatibility decisions observable and testable.","principle":"Persisted representation and in-memory implementation version are separate compatibility concerns.","correct_example":"Store a schema version and migration path alongside persisted logical data.","invalid_example":"Assuming that the latest implementation can safely read every historical representation.","problem":"Old persisted state is interpreted using incompatible semantics.","failure_mode":"Silent state corruption.","cause":"Implicit version compatibility assumption.","solution":"Version serialized representations and define migration or rejection behavior.","alternative":"Use a stable external schema independent of internal structure representation.","tradeoff":"Stable schemas reduce coupling but may require conversion logic.","constraint":"Migration must preserve required invariants.","edge_case":"A newer reader may encounter a future version it cannot understand.","security_implication":"Unsupported or malicious version identifiers must not bypass validation.","performance_implication":"Migration can add startup or ingestion cost.","testing_guidance":"Test current-to-current, old-to-current, unsupported-version, and malformed-version cases.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each supported version maps to a documented representation and migration outcome.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Serialization and persistence"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B92-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"versioning_principle","source":"Schema evolution and compatibility engineering"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B93-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Version compatibility","subtopic":"migration","concept":"Migration versus reconstruction","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should persisted structures be migrated versus rebuilt?","answer":"Migration is appropriate when existing state contains authoritative information that would be expensive or impossible to reconstruct. Rebuilding is appropriate when the structure is derived from canonical data and can be deterministically regenerated.","explanation":"Indexes, caches, and auxiliary metadata are often rebuildable; authoritative records generally require preservation.","principle":"Classify persisted state as canonical or derived before designing migration.","correct_example":"Rebuild a derived index from canonical records after a representation change.","invalid_example":"Treating a unique historical ordering or authoritative relationship as disposable derived state.","problem":"A migration discards information that cannot be reconstructed.","failure_mode":"Irrecoverable data loss.","cause":"Incorrect canonical-versus-derived classification.","solution":"Document data authority and reconstruction guarantees.","alternative":"Persist both canonical state and versioned derived metadata when rebuild cost or availability requirements justify it.","tradeoff":"Persisting derived state improves recovery speed but increases consistency and migration complexity.","constraint":"Rebuild correctness depends on canonical data completeness.","edge_case":"A rebuild may produce equivalent values but different iteration order or identity semantics.","security_implication":"Rebuilt security indexes must preserve access-control semantics.","performance_implication":"Large rebuilds can increase startup or recovery time.","testing_guidance":"Test migration and rebuild equivalence against canonical fixtures.","verification_guidance":"Verify semantic equivalence, not merely byte equality.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B93-0001"],"prerequisites":["Canonical state classification"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B92-0001"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B93-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"systems_engineering_principle","source":"Persistence and migration engineering"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B93-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Version compatibility","subtopic":"runtime-specific behavior","concept":"Runtime documentation precedence","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should runtime-specific data-structure behavior be verified?","answer":"Use authoritative documentation for the exact target runtime or library version and validate implementation assumptions against that version.","explanation":"Collection behavior, serialization protocols, iteration guarantees, and performance characteristics can differ between language versions and implementations.","principle":"Version-specific claims require version-specific evidence.","correct_example":"Record the exact language/library version when relying on a documented collection guarantee.","invalid_example":"Applying behavior documented for one runtime release to an unrelated release without verification.","problem":"Version drift invalidates an implementation assumption.","failure_mode":"Compatibility regression.","cause":"Unversioned knowledge ingestion.","solution":"Attach version and version range to runtime-dependent records.","alternative":"Restrict the implementation to stable language-level guarantees.","tradeoff":"Stable guarantees may provide fewer optimization opportunities.","constraint":"Version metadata must remain synchronized with supported environments.","edge_case":"A minor release may change implementation details without changing the language-level contract.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive behavior must be verified against the deployed version.","performance_implication":"Implementation optimizations can change without changing asymptotic complexity.","testing_guidance":"Run compatibility tests across every supported runtime family/version.","verification_guidance":"Verify the exact deployed version against the documented guarantee.","language":"multi-language","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Version awareness"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B81-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Oracle Java SE specifications","source_ref":"turn0search4"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Python documentation","source_ref":"turn0search1"},{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Rust Serde documentation","source_ref":"turn0search0"}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B94-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Cross-language reasoning","subtopic":"semantic equivalence","concept":"Semantic equivalence of data structures","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can equivalent data-structure implementations in different languages be compared?","answer":"Compare observable semantics and required invariants rather than source syntax or class names.","explanation":"Different languages expose different APIs and memory models. Equivalent implementations should be evaluated by supported operations, ordering, duplicate rules, failure behavior, complexity expectations, mutation semantics, and invariants.","principle":"Cross-language equivalence is behavioral and contractual, not textual.","correct_example":"Verify that two priority queues return elements according to the same priority contract even if their implementations differ.","invalid_example":"Declaring equivalence because both implementations contain a class named PriorityQueue.","problem":"Ported code preserves syntax but changes behavior.","failure_mode":"Semantic portability failure.","cause":"Syntax-focused translation.","solution":"Create language-neutral behavioral specifications and cross-language tests.","alternative":"Use a shared protocol or canonical test corpus.","tradeoff":"Behavioral test suites require additional specification effort.","constraint":"Language-specific guarantees must remain separately documented.","edge_case":"Null, ownership, integer overflow, equality, and iteration semantics can differ.","security_implication":"Security invariants must be tested independently in each runtime.","performance_implication":"Equivalent asymptotic complexity does not imply equivalent runtime performance.","testing_guidance":"Use shared semantic test vectors where the languages support equivalent concepts.","verification_guidance":"Verify operation outcomes and invariants against a language-neutral specification.","language":"multi-language","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Semantic equivalence"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B93-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_principle","source":"Cross-language semantic equivalence"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B94-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Cross-language reasoning","subtopic":"API translation","concept":"Language-specific API mismatch","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is direct API-name translation insufficient for data-structure ports?","answer":"APIs with similar names can have different guarantees for ordering, mutation, equality, errors, concurrency, ownership, and complexity.","explanation":"A port must map semantics, not names. Language libraries may also expose different specialized structures and contracts.","principle":"Translate contracts before translating APIs.","correct_example":"Define the source queue's FIFO and empty-state behavior, then implement that contract using the target language's suitable structure.","invalid_example":"Replacing each source method with a similarly named target method without checking semantics.","problem":"The port compiles but violates application assumptions.","failure_mode":"Behavioral regression.","cause":"Name-based translation.","solution":"Create a semantic mapping table and tests before porting.","alternative":"Use a shared protocol-level abstraction.","tradeoff":"Semantic mapping takes more upfront effort but reduces hidden incompatibility.","constraint":"Target-language limitations must be represented explicitly.","edge_case":"Error types and iteration behavior may not have direct equivalents.","security_implication":"Validation and authorization semantics can be lost during API substitution.","performance_implication":"A direct-looking replacement may have substantially different complexity.","testing_guidance":"Use differential testing against a reference behavior where practical.","verification_guidance":"Verify contract-level equivalence rather than API-name equivalence.","language":"multi-language","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B94-0001"],"prerequisites":["API semantics"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B93-0003"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B94-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_principle","source":"Cross-language API compatibility"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B94-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Cross-language reasoning","subtopic":"complexity","concept":"Complexity portability","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must complexity claims be rechecked after porting a data-structure implementation?","answer":"The abstract data structure may retain the same asymptotic complexity while the target implementation introduces different constants, memory behavior, allocation patterns, or hidden operations.","explanation":"A theoretical operation bound describes the model, not every runtime cost. Library implementations can have different representation and optimization choices.","principle":"Ported complexity must distinguish abstract guarantees from implementation-specific behavior.","correct_example":"Record O(1) expected lookup as a structural property while separately validating allocation and runtime costs for the target library.","invalid_example":"Assuming identical latency because both structures are described as O(log n).","problem":"Performance regressions are hidden behind unchanged Big-O notation.","failure_mode":"Complexity interpretation error.","cause":"Confusing asymptotic and empirical performance.","solution":"Track theoretical complexity and empirical measurements separately.","alternative":"Use target-runtime benchmarks when performance requirements are strict.","tradeoff":"Benchmarking adds environment-specific evidence and maintenance cost.","constraint":"Benchmarks are valid only within their measured environment and workload.","edge_case":"Small datasets can be dominated by constant factors.","security_implication":"Resource-exhaustion risk can depend on implementation constants and allocation behavior.","performance_implication":"Implementation and workload can materially affect actual throughput and latency.","testing_guidance":"Use representative workload tests for performance-sensitive structures.","verification_guidance":"Verify both theoretical guarantees and target-specific measured claims separately.","language":"multi-language","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B81-0001"],"prerequisites":["Complexity analysis"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B90-0002"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B81-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"algorithmic_principle","source":"Asymptotic complexity and implementation analysis"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B95-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Knowledge provenance","subtopic":"claim classification","concept":"Evidence-level separation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should data-structure knowledge distinguish documented facts from recommendations?","answer":"Each record should identify whether a statement is an authoritative fact, derived inference, recommendation, heuristic, assumption, unknown, or conflicted claim.","explanation":"This prevents implementation advice from being mistaken for a language guarantee or empirical result.","principle":"Evidence status is part of the knowledge representation.","correct_example":"Mark an official API guarantee as VERIFIED_DOCUMENTATION and a structure-selection recommendation as RECOMMENDATION or INFERENCE.","invalid_example":"Marking an untested performance expectation as a verified benchmark.","problem":"Knowledge consumers cannot distinguish evidence strength.","failure_mode":"Evidence contamination.","cause":"Flattened truth representation.","solution":"Use explicit evidence level, confidence, provenance, and validation status.","alternative":"Maintain separate factual and recommendation collections while retaining cross-links.","tradeoff":"Richer metadata increases schema complexity but improves reasoning reliability.","constraint":"Confidence must not substitute for evidence.","edge_case":"Multiple authoritative sources can disagree because of version or scope differences.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive claims require stronger provenance and version specificity.","performance_implication":"Performance claims require explicit theoretical versus empirical classification.","testing_guidance":"Audit records for unsupported VERIFIED labels.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence appropriate to each claim type before marking it verified.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Evidence discipline"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B90-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"D12 evidence policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B95-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Knowledge provenance","subtopic":"contradictions","concept":"Context-aware contradiction resolution","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should contradictory data-structure claims be resolved?","answer":"Compare source authority, version, implementation, computational model, workload, assumptions, terminology, and execution evidence before declaring a contradiction.","explanation":"Two claims may both be correct under different conditions. For example, an expected hash-table lookup claim and a worst-case lookup claim are not contradictory when their assumptions differ.","principle":"Resolve contradictions by normalizing context before selecting or retaining claims.","correct_example":"Record both expected and worst-case lookup behavior with their explicit assumptions.","invalid_example":"Deleting one claim solely because it differs from another without checking scope.","problem":"Contextually distinct claims are incorrectly collapsed.","failure_mode":"Knowledge loss through false deduplication.","cause":"Ignoring assumptions and versions.","solution":"Attach context and retain unresolved claims as CONFLICTED when evidence cannot resolve them.","alternative":"Create a comparison record linking the competing claims.","tradeoff":"Retaining conflicts increases dataset size but preserves uncertainty.","constraint":"No contradiction may be silently discarded.","edge_case":"A newer version may supersede an older guarantee.","security_implication":"Conflicting security guarantees require conservative treatment until resolved.","performance_implication":"Conflicting performance claims must retain workload and environment context.","testing_guidance":"Create targeted tests when execution can resolve an implementation-specific conflict.","verification_guidance":"Record the evidence and resolution rationale explicitly.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Provenance","version awareness"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B93-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"D12 contradiction policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B95-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Knowledge provenance","subtopic":"source quality","concept":"Source hierarchy","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Which sources should receive stronger evidentiary weight for version-specific data-structure claims?","answer":"Prefer authoritative language, runtime, library, standard, or project documentation for claims about exact behavior, supplemented by reputable algorithmic or research sources for general principles.","explanation":"Primary documentation is especially important for API contracts and version-specific guarantees. Secondary explanations can provide context but should not silently override authoritative specifications.","principle":"Match source authority to claim type.","correct_example":"Use official Python documentation for pickle security behavior and official Java specifications for Java-specific semantics.","invalid_example":"Using an unsourced blog post as the sole authority for a version-specific API guarantee.","problem":"Low-quality or stale evidence enters the knowledge base as authoritative.","failure_mode":"Provenance degradation.","cause":"Source selection without claim-type matching.","solution":"Record source type, title, publisher, version, and retrieval date where available.","alternative":"Use multiple independent reputable sources for high-impact conceptual claims.","tradeoff":"Higher-quality provenance takes more acquisition effort.","constraint":"Source authority does not eliminate the need to check version and scope.","edge_case":"Official documentation can itself describe implementation-specific behavior rather than language guarantees.","security_implication":"Security claims should favor primary security documentation.","performance_implication":"Performance claims require implementation and workload evidence beyond generic documentation.","testing_guidance":"Audit high-impact records for primary-source provenance.","verification_guidance":"Verify source relevance to the exact claim and version.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Provenance"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B95-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"D12 provenance policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B96-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Retrieval readiness","subtopic":"query coverage","concept":"Multi-form retrieval representation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What query forms should a data-structure knowledge record support?","answer":"Records should support conceptual, operational, debugging, complexity, security, performance, testing, version, comparison, and code-generation-oriented queries.","explanation":"A single canonical question is insufficient for retrieval because engineers express the same need in many forms.","principle":"Represent knowledge around concepts and relationships while indexing multiple query formulations.","correct_example":"A hash-table record should be retrievable through queries about maps, key lookup, collisions, expected complexity, worst-case behavior, and collision-related security.","invalid_example":"Indexing only the phrase used in the original source.","problem":"Semantically relevant knowledge is missed by vocabulary mismatch.","failure_mode":"Retrieval false negative.","cause":"Insufficient query variation.","solution":"Add controlled query variants and relationship-aware retrieval metadata.","alternative":"Use semantic retrieval with lexical fallback.","tradeoff":"Additional query variants improve recall but can increase irrelevant retrieval.","constraint":"Variants must remain semantically faithful to the canonical record.","edge_case":"Synonyms can have different technical meanings in different languages or libraries.","security_implication":"Security queries should connect structures to relevant threat and mitigation records.","performance_implication":"Complexity and workload terminology should be indexed separately.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate exact, conceptual, debugging, comparison, and security retrieval queries.","verification_guidance":"Measure retrieval against labeled relevant records where a benchmark exists.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Retrieval metadata"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B90-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"retrieval_engineering","source":"Knowledge retrieval design principle"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B96-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Retrieval readiness","subtopic":"relationship retrieval","concept":"Relationship-aware retrieval","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should data-structure retrieval use relationships between records?","answer":"Relationships allow retrieval to expand from a directly matched concept to prerequisites, alternatives, failure modes, security implications, tests, and version-specific records.","explanation":"A developer debugging a tree implementation may need the invariant, corruption symptoms, repair strategy, and verification rule rather than only the definition of a tree.","principle":"Retrieve connected evidence, not isolated snippets.","correct_example":"Retrieve a structure-selection record together with its complexity, edge-case, testing, and security relationships.","invalid_example":"Returning only the first lexical match for a broad engineering question.","problem":"Context required for reasoning is absent.","failure_mode":"Context starvation.","cause":"Record-isolated retrieval.","solution":"Use explicit graph relationships and bounded neighborhood expansion.","alternative":"Store synthesized context records for high-frequency workflows.","tradeoff":"Graph expansion increases retrieval complexity and must be bounded to avoid noise.","constraint":"Relationship types must have defined semantics.","edge_case":"Cycles in the knowledge graph can cause repeated retrieval.","security_implication":"Relationship traversal must respect dataset and tenant authorization boundaries in deployed systems.","performance_implication":"Unbounded graph expansion can increase latency and irrelevant results.","testing_guidance":"Test relationship traversal with depth limits and expected neighborhoods.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each relationship points to an existing stable record.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B95-0001"],"prerequisites":["Relationship graph"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B96-0001"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B95-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"knowledge_graph_principle","source":"Relationship-aware retrieval design"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B96-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Retrieval readiness","subtopic":"evaluation","concept":"Retrieval evaluation discipline","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be measured when evaluating D12 retrieval quality?","answer":"Measure query coverage, relevant retrieval, missed relevant records, irrelevant retrieval, and where applicable precision, recall, coverage, and false-retrieval rates.","explanation":"Conceptual coverage does not prove retrieval quality. Evaluation requires representative labeled queries and explicit expected relevant records.","principle":"Retrieval readiness is a property to test, not assume.","correct_example":"Create labeled queries for structure selection, debugging, security, performance, versioning, and code generation and compare retrieved records with expected records.","invalid_example":"Declaring retrieval complete because every topic appears somewhere in the dataset.","problem":"Knowledge exists but cannot be reliably retrieved.","failure_mode":"Operational retrieval failure.","cause":"No retrieval evaluation.","solution":"Maintain a retrieval test set and run it against the indexed dataset.","alternative":"Use targeted smoke tests during incremental ingestion and full evaluation at release.","tradeoff":"Evaluation requires maintenance as the dataset evolves.","constraint":"Metrics are meaningful only against defined relevance labels and query populations.","edge_case":"Broad queries may legitimately have many relevant records.","security_implication":"Security-critical queries require high recall and careful irrelevant-result handling.","performance_implication":"Retrieval evaluation should include latency when the deployment has latency requirements.","testing_guidance":"Run exact, conceptual, debugging, comparison, security, and version-specific retrieval tests.","verification_guidance":"Do not label retrieval VERIFIED without measurable or explicitly scoped evidence.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B96-0001","D12-DS-B96-0002"],"prerequisites":["Retrieval test design"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_evaluation_policy","source":"Track B retrieval validation policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B97-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Deduplication","subtopic":"cross-batch","concept":"Cross-batch semantic deduplication","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should duplicate data-structure records be detected across D12 batches?","answer":"Compare normalized concepts, canonical questions, principles, answers, examples, failure modes, and relationships rather than relying only on identical text.","explanation":"Two records can describe the same concept using different wording. Exact duplicate detection therefore must be supplemented with near-semantic and relationship-aware review.","principle":"Deduplicate knowledge by meaning while preserving genuinely distinct scope or context.","correct_example":"Merge two records that both define the same invariant while retaining version-specific records when their guarantees differ.","invalid_example":"Deleting two records merely because they share a keyword such as heap or map.","problem":"Duplicate or near-duplicate knowledge creates retrieval noise and inconsistent answers.","failure_mode":"Semantic duplication.","cause":"Batch-local deduplication without final cross-batch review.","solution":"Run exact, normalized, semantic, and cross-dataset deduplication before lock.","alternative":"Retain distinct records and connect them with explicit relationships when context differs materially.","tradeoff":"Aggressive deduplication risks losing useful contextual distinctions.","constraint":"Locked stable IDs must not be silently reassigned.","edge_case":"Two records can be semantically similar but version-specific.","security_implication":"Duplicate security guidance can produce inconsistent mitigation retrieval.","performance_implication":"Redundant records increase index size and retrieval noise.","testing_guidance":"Use duplicate candidates and manually verified labels for deduplication evaluation.","verification_guidance":"Record duplicates found, merged, retained, or rejected with rationale.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Semantic deduplication"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B95-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"D12 deduplication policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B97-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Deduplication","subtopic":"preservation","concept":"Distinct-context preservation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When should apparently duplicate records remain separate?","answer":"Retain separate records when they differ materially by language, version, implementation, workload, security boundary, computational model, or other conditions that change the truth of the claim.","explanation":"Deduplication must not collapse conditional knowledge into an inaccurate universal statement.","principle":"Merge redundant wording; preserve meaningful contextual distinctions.","correct_example":"Keep separate records for expected and worst-case hash-table behavior when their assumptions are explicit.","invalid_example":"Merging all hash-table complexity records into one unconditional complexity claim.","problem":"Context is lost during deduplication.","failure_mode":"Over-normalization.","cause":"Textual similarity treated as semantic identity.","solution":"Compare assumptions and applicability before merging.","alternative":"Create a parent concept with context-specific child records.","tradeoff":"More records preserve precision but require stronger retrieval filtering.","constraint":"Context metadata must be complete enough to distinguish the records.","edge_case":"Two records may become duplicates after a version becomes obsolete; supersession should then be explicit.","security_implication":"Do not merge security guidance that applies to different trust models.","performance_implication":"Do not merge measurements from materially different environments.","testing_guidance":"Audit retained duplicate candidates for distinct applicability.","verification_guidance":"Require a documented distinction before retaining near-identical records.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12-DS-B97-0001"],"prerequisites":["Context-aware deduplication"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B95-0002"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B97-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"D12 semantic deduplication policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B97-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Deduplication","subtopic":"stable identifiers","concept":"Stable identity under correction","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should record identity behave when a locked knowledge record requires correction?","answer":"Do not silently mutate the historical identity or relationships. Use an explicit correction, supersession, or replacement record while preserving lineage.","explanation":"Stable IDs enable downstream systems to retain references and understand why knowledge changed.","principle":"Knowledge correction is a versioned event, not an invisible overwrite.","correct_example":"Mark an obsolete record SUPERSEDED and link it to a corrected record.","invalid_example":"Replacing the content under the same locked record ID without a change record.","problem":"Downstream references become impossible to audit.","failure_mode":"Lineage corruption.","cause":"Mutable locked records.","solution":"Use supersedes, contradicts, or correction relationships and retain change provenance.","alternative":"Maintain immutable snapshots and a current-view index.","tradeoff":"Immutable history increases storage and metadata but improves auditability.","constraint":"Stable identifiers must remain stable after lock.","edge_case":"A source may retract or correct its own documentation.","security_implication":"Security guidance changes require auditable lineage.","performance_implication":"Historical lineage can increase index size but generally supports safer retrieval.","testing_guidance":"Test reference resolution across superseded records.","verification_guidance":"Verify every supersession target exists and remains traceable.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Stable identifiers"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B95-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"Track B immutable-lock policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B98-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Completeness audit","subtopic":"scope verification","concept":"D12 scope closure","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What constitutes scope completion for the D12 Data Structures dataset?","answer":"D12 is complete only when its planned structures, operation semantics, complexity, memory behavior, use cases, trade-offs, invariants, edge cases, security, performance, testing, verification, interoperability, persistence, version awareness, provenance, retrieval, deduplication, and final quality gates have been reviewed.","explanation":"Topic presence alone does not establish dataset completeness. Each planned dimension requires a coverage status.","principle":"Completeness is multidimensional and must be explicitly audited.","correct_example":"Mark each planned D12 dimension as COVERED, PARTIALLY_COVERED, NOT_APPLICABLE, or UNKNOWN with evidence.","invalid_example":"Locking the dataset because every named structure appears once.","problem":"Important operational knowledge dimensions remain absent.","failure_mode":"False completeness.","cause":"Topic-only coverage audit.","solution":"Use a scope matrix and final quality gate.","alternative":"Use staged audits after every major group and a final global audit.","tradeoff":"Comprehensive auditing requires additional review time.","constraint":"Unknown coverage must not be silently converted to covered.","edge_case":"Some implementation-specific behavior may remain unknown without target-version execution.","security_implication":"Security coverage must be independently audited.","performance_implication":"Theoretical coverage does not imply empirical performance coverage.","testing_guidance":"Audit test cases across structure families and failure classes.","verification_guidance":"Only mark a dimension COVERED when its defined evidence requirement is satisfied.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D12 scope definition"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B96-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"D12 completion criteria"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B98-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Relationship integrity","subtopic":"graph validation","concept":"Relationship referential integrity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be checked before D12 is locked?","answer":"Every relationship reference should resolve to an existing stable record or an explicitly declared external dataset/entity, and relationship semantics should be valid for the referenced records.","explanation":"Broken references degrade retrieval, reasoning, provenance, and downstream learning.","principle":"Knowledge relationships require referential integrity just like database relationships.","correct_example":"Verify every depends_on and related_records identifier exists before lock.","invalid_example":"Allowing references to temporary or nonexistent IDs.","problem":"Downstream retrieval follows broken relationships.","failure_mode":"Knowledge graph inconsistency.","cause":"No referential integrity audit.","solution":"Run automated identifier and relationship validation before lock.","alternative":"Use a manifest registry to validate all references during ingestion.","tradeoff":"Validation adds build-time processing but prevents runtime graph errors.","constraint":"External references must be explicitly typed and distinguishable from internal record IDs.","edge_case":"A referenced record may be superseded but should remain resolvable historically.","security_implication":"Relationship traversal must not cross unauthorized dataset boundaries.","performance_implication":"Large relationship graphs require efficient validation and indexing.","testing_guidance":"Test dangling references, invalid relationship types, cycles, and superseded targets.","verification_guidance":"Lock only after referential integrity passes.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Stable identifiers","Relationship policy"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B97-0003"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B97-0003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"Track B relationship integrity policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B98-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Completeness audit","subtopic":"limitations","concept":"Explicit limitation recording","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must D12 limitations remain visible after lock?","answer":"Locked knowledge can still contain unexecuted examples, environment-dependent behavior, unknown empirical performance, or future version changes. Recording limitations prevents downstream systems from treating the dataset as universally verified.","explanation":"A locked dataset is a controlled release, not a claim that every record has empirical execution evidence.","principle":"Lock status means controlled completeness and validation, not universal empirical verification.","correct_example":"Record that implementation examples were documentation-validated but not executed.","invalid_example":"Changing NOT_EXECUTED to VERIFIED merely because the dataset was locked.","problem":"Downstream systems overestimate evidence strength.","failure_mode":"Verification inflation.","cause":"Confusing dataset status with record validation status.","solution":"Keep record-level validation and evidence metadata immutable and explicit.","alternative":"Run a later verified execution campaign and supersede affected records with new evidence.","tradeoff":"Visible uncertainty may reduce apparent completeness but increases factual reliability.","constraint":"No fabricated execution or benchmark claims.","edge_case":"A future runtime may invalidate a previously correct implementation detail.","security_implication":"Unverified security claims must not be treated as tested controls.","performance_implication":"No empirical performance claim should be inferred from theoretical complexity alone.","testing_guidance":"Track execution status separately from dataset lock status.","verification_guidance":"Audit evidence metadata during every dataset release.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Verification discipline"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B90-0002"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B90-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"Track B verification policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B99-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Final quality gates","subtopic":"structural validation","concept":"D12 structural quality gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What structural checks are required before D12 lock?","answer":"Validate JSONL structure, required identifiers, schema conformity, English-only content, stable record IDs, relationship syntax, valid enumerations, and manifest consistency.","explanation":"Structural validity is the first release gate because downstream retrieval and processing depend on predictable records.","principle":"No semantic quality assessment can compensate for malformed machine-readable structure.","correct_example":"Reject records with invalid IDs, malformed relationships, unsupported knowledge types, or broken JSONL syntax.","invalid_example":"Locking malformed records because their prose meaning appears correct.","problem":"Downstream ingestion fails or silently misinterprets records.","failure_mode":"Dataset ingestion failure.","cause":"Insufficient structural validation.","solution":"Run deterministic schema and manifest validation.","alternative":"Use a schema-aware ingestion pipeline that blocks invalid records.","tradeoff":"Strict validation can reject incomplete records that require explicit UNKNOWN fields.","constraint":"Schema rules must remain versioned.","edge_case":"Optional fields may legitimately be absent when not applicable.","security_implication":"Malformed security records can bypass automated controls.","performance_implication":"Schema validation should be performed before expensive semantic evaluation.","testing_guidance":"Include malformed-record fixtures and boundary field tests.","verification_guidance":"Require structural PASS before semantic lock gates.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"D12 schema 1.0","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Canonical schema"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B98-0002"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B98-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"D12 schema validation policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B99-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Final quality gates","subtopic":"semantic validation","concept":"D12 semantic quality gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What semantic checks are required before D12 lock?","answer":"Review scope coverage, duplicate concepts, contradictions, unsupported claims, version context, provenance, edge cases, security guidance, performance evidence discipline, testing guidance, verification guidance, and relationship integrity.","explanation":"Semantic validation ensures that structurally valid records are also useful, non-contradictory where resolvable, and honest about evidence.","principle":"Machine-readable structure and knowledge correctness are separate release gates.","correct_example":"Keep an unexecuted code example marked NOT_EXECUTED while still allowing it as a conceptual example.","invalid_example":"Treating every syntactically plausible code example as executed and verified.","problem":"False confidence enters the knowledge fabric.","failure_mode":"Semantic validation failure.","cause":"Insufficient evidence discipline.","solution":"Apply record-level validation and final cross-batch review.","alternative":"Separate generated knowledge from verified knowledge at retrieval time.","tradeoff":"More metadata is required but evidence boundaries become explicit.","constraint":"No unsupported claim may be upgraded solely for dataset completeness.","edge_case":"A claim may be correct but lack enough evidence for VERIFIED status.","security_implication":"Security claims require explicit defensive context and evidence.","performance_implication":"Performance claims must distinguish theory, inference, measurement, and unknown.","testing_guidance":"Audit all testing and verification records for evidence requirements.","verification_guidance":"Require all semantic quality gates to pass before lock.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"D12 schema 1.0","dependencies":["D12-DS-B99-0001"],"prerequisites":["Semantic audit"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B95-0001","D12-DS-B98-0001"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B99-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"D12 semantic quality policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B99-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Final quality gates","subtopic":"release status","concept":"LOCKED versus VERIFIED distinction","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What does LOCKED mean for D12?","answer":"LOCKED means the dataset has completed its defined scope and release-quality audits under the stated evidence policies; it does not mean every individual knowledge claim has been empirically executed or experimentally verified.","explanation":"Record-level evidence status remains authoritative after dataset lock.","principle":"Dataset release status and claim verification status are independent dimensions.","correct_example":"D12 status LOCKED while an individual example remains NOT_EXECUTED.","invalid_example":"Changing every record to VERIFIED because the dataset passed its lock audit.","problem":"Release status is misinterpreted as empirical proof.","failure_mode":"Verification-status conflation.","cause":"Single-status mental model.","solution":"Maintain dataset status and record validation_status separately.","alternative":"Expose both statuses in retrieval metadata.","tradeoff":"Two-dimensional status is more complex but materially more accurate.","constraint":"No record-level evidence may be fabricated during locking.","edge_case":"Later execution evidence may supersede an unexecuted record.","security_implication":"Locked security knowledge still requires target-environment validation before deployment.","performance_implication":"Locked theoretical complexity does not constitute benchmark evidence.","testing_guidance":"Test that lock tooling does not rewrite record evidence statuses.","verification_guidance":"Verify final manifest preserves record-level evidence distinctions.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"D12 schema 1.0","dependencies":["D12-DS-B99-0002"],"prerequisites":["Evidence discipline"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B98-0003"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B99-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"Track B lock policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D12-DS-B100-0001","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Final D12 audit","subtopic":"scope closure","concept":"Data Structures Knowledge Fabric final audit","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must the final D12 audit establish?","answer":"The final audit must establish that the planned D12 data-structure knowledge scope has been covered across structure families, operations, complexity, memory, use cases, trade-offs, invariants, edge cases, security, performance, testing, verification, interoperability, persistence, versioning, provenance, retrieval, deduplication, contradiction handling, and relationships.","explanation":"The audit is the release boundary between D12 construction and downstream dataset work.","principle":"A dataset moves forward only after its defined release gates are explicitly closed.","correct_example":"Produce a final manifest containing scope status, record count, relationship status, provenance quality, contradiction status, deduplication status, retrieval status, known limitations, and lock status.","invalid_example":"Moving to D13 while D12 remains silently incomplete or structurally unverified.","problem":"Downstream datasets inherit an unstable knowledge foundation.","failure_mode":"Pipeline contamination.","cause":"Premature dataset progression.","solution":"Lock D12 with a documented final manifest and preserve correction/supersession policy.","alternative":"Mark D12 PARTIALLY_COVERED and continue only when downstream dependencies do not require missing areas.","tradeoff":"Strict gating can delay progression but protects dataset integrity.","constraint":"No silent reopening of a locked dataset.","edge_case":"Future authoritative changes require superseding records rather than mutating locked records.","security_implication":"Security-related gaps must remain explicit in limitations.","performance_implication":"No benchmark coverage is implied unless measurements actually exist.","testing_guidance":"Run all defined structural, semantic, relationship, deduplication, retrieval, security, and evidence checks.","verification_guidance":"Lock only when every mandatory gate is PASS or explicitly documented as NOT_APPLICABLE/UNKNOWN with rationale.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"D12 schema 1.0","dependencies":["D12-DS-B99-0003"],"prerequisites":["All D12 batches"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B98-0001","D12-DS-B99-0002"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B99-0003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"D12 final audit specification"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B100-0002","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Final D12 audit","subtopic":"downstream readiness","concept":"D12 downstream integration contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should D12 expose to downstream datasets and the Autonomous Software Engineering Brain?","answer":"D12 should expose stable record IDs, structured concepts, relationships, evidence metadata, version context, limitations, testing guidance, verification guidance, complexity and trade-off knowledge, and machine-readable JSONL records suitable for retrieval and reasoning.","explanation":"Downstream systems should be able to retrieve data-structure knowledge without depending on informal batch text.","principle":"A locked dataset is an integration artifact, not merely a document collection.","correct_example":"Downstream retrieval can request a structure-selection concept and follow relationships to complexity, security, testing, and verification records.","invalid_example":"Requiring downstream systems to infer relationships from natural-language paragraphs.","problem":"Knowledge cannot be reliably composed by retrieval or reasoning systems.","failure_mode":"Integration opacity.","cause":"Missing machine-readable relationships and metadata.","solution":"Preserve canonical schema and relationship contracts in the locked release.","alternative":"Generate domain-specific indexes without altering canonical records.","tradeoff":"Multiple indexes increase maintenance but can improve retrieval specialization.","constraint":"Canonical IDs and relationships remain stable.","edge_case":"Future dataset versions may add relationships without changing historical record identity.","security_implication":"Downstream consumers must apply their own authorization and trust-boundary controls.","performance_implication":"Indexes can optimize retrieval without changing semantic content.","testing_guidance":"Run downstream contract tests against representative D12 queries.","verification_guidance":"Verify that all downstream-required fields and relationships resolve correctly.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"D12 schema 1.0","dependencies":["D12-DS-B100-0001"],"prerequisites":["Final D12 audit"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B96-0002"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B100-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_architecture","source":"Track B downstream integration contract"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D12-DS-B100-0003","dataset_id":"D12","topic":"Final D12 audit","subtopic":"lock policy","concept":"D12 immutable lock and supersession policy","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should D12 change after lock?","answer":"Locked D12 records must not be silently rewritten. Corrections, newly discovered contradictions, authoritative version changes, or newly verified execution evidence should be represented through explicit correction or supersession records while preserving lineage.","explanation":"This provides stable historical references while allowing the knowledge fabric to evolve.","principle":"Immutable history plus explicit supersession provides controlled knowledge evolution.","correct_example":"Create a new record with supersedes pointing to an obsolete record after authoritative documentation changes its behavior.","invalid_example":"Editing a locked record in place and leaving downstream references unaware of the change.","problem":"Historical reasoning and provenance become irreproducible.","failure_mode":"Knowledge lineage break.","cause":"Mutable locked artifacts.","solution":"Use immutable record identity, explicit supersession, provenance, and versioned dataset releases.","alternative":"Maintain a versioned current-view projection over immutable historical records.","tradeoff":"Historical retention increases metadata and storage but improves auditability.","constraint":"Record IDs and relationships remain stable across releases unless explicitly superseded.","edge_case":"A corrected source may require both contradiction and supersession relationships.","security_implication":"Security corrections must be propagated without erasing the historical evidence trail.","performance_implication":"Current-view indexing can hide superseded records from normal retrieval while retaining them for audit.","testing_guidance":"Test resolution of current and superseded records.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every supersession preserves traceability to the previous record.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"D12 schema 1.0","dependencies":["D12-DS-B100-0001","D12-DS-B100-0002"],"prerequisites":["Immutable dataset release"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B97-0003"],"depends_on":["D12-DS-B100-0001","D12-DS-B100-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"Track B immutable knowledge policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

