{"record_id":"D13-DP-B01-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design pattern foundations","subtopic":"definition","concept":"Design pattern as reusable design knowledge","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a software design pattern?","answer":"A software design pattern is a reusable description of a design solution to a recurring problem within a particular context and set of constraints. It is not a fixed code fragment and must be adapted to the system in which it is applied.","explanation":"A pattern captures design knowledge at a level above individual implementation code. Its value comes from identifying a recurring design problem, the forces affecting the decision, a solution structure, and the consequences of applying that structure.","principle":"Treat patterns as contextual design knowledge rather than templates to copy mechanically.","correct_example":"Choose a Strategy-like structure when a system must support interchangeable algorithms behind a stable policy boundary, then adapt the interfaces and lifecycle to the actual domain.","invalid_example":"Adding a Strategy hierarchy merely because the project has more than one algorithm.","problem":"The system needs reusable design knowledge but the problem has not yet been characterized.","failure_mode":"Pattern-first design.","cause":"Selecting a named pattern before identifying the actual design problem.","solution":"Describe the problem, context, forces, constraints, and desired consequences before selecting a pattern.","alternative":"Use a simpler direct implementation when variation and change do not justify an abstraction.","tradeoff":"Patterns can improve flexibility and communication while adding abstractions, indirection, and maintenance cost.","constraint":"Pattern applicability depends on context.","edge_case":"Two systems may use the same pattern name while requiring materially different implementations.","security_implication":"A pattern does not automatically provide security; security properties must be established from the actual design and trust boundaries.","performance_implication":"A pattern can introduce indirection or allocation; performance claims require target-specific analysis or measurement.","testing_guidance":"Test the behavioral contract created by the pattern rather than testing pattern names or class shapes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the selected pattern addresses an explicitly documented design problem and that its required invariants hold.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D11 Algorithms","D12 Data Structures"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B01-0002","D13-DP-B02-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_reference","source":"Refactoring.Guru Design Patterns","source_ref":"turn0search7"},{"source_type":"software_engineering_reference","source":"Martin Fowler, Writing Software Patterns","source_ref":"turn0search6"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B01-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design pattern foundations","subtopic":"abstraction level","concept":"Pattern versus algorithm","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does a design pattern differ from an algorithm?","answer":"An algorithm specifies a computational procedure for achieving a goal, while a design pattern describes a reusable design structure for addressing a recurring design problem. A pattern generally leaves implementation details and exact execution steps open to adaptation.","explanation":"Algorithms primarily describe computation. Patterns primarily organize responsibilities, collaboration, creation, structure, or variation points. A pattern can contain or employ algorithms, but the two concepts operate at different abstraction levels.","principle":"Do not treat a design pattern as a substitute for algorithm specification.","correct_example":"Use a Strategy structure to select among sorting algorithms while keeping each sorting algorithm separately specified.","invalid_example":"Calling a specific sorting algorithm a Strategy pattern merely because it can be selected dynamically.","problem":"The system needs both computational behavior and a maintainable structure for selecting or composing that behavior.","failure_mode":"Abstraction confusion.","cause":"Treating pattern and algorithm as interchangeable concepts.","solution":"Model the algorithm and the surrounding design structure separately.","alternative":"Directly invoke a fixed algorithm when no variation point is required.","tradeoff":"Separating algorithm selection can increase flexibility while adding abstraction.","constraint":"The pattern must not obscure required algorithmic guarantees.","edge_case":"A pattern may encapsulate an algorithm whose complexity remains relevant to the overall design.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive algorithms must retain their own security requirements after being encapsulated by a pattern.","performance_implication":"Pattern indirection does not change an algorithm's theoretical complexity but can affect implementation overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test both algorithm correctness and pattern-level collaboration.","verification_guidance":"Verify that algorithmic guarantees remain explicit after introducing the pattern.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B01-0001"],"prerequisites":["Algorithm abstraction"],"related_records":["D11"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B01-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_reference","source":"Refactoring.Guru pattern explanation","source_ref":"turn0search7"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B01-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design pattern foundations","subtopic":"classification","concept":"Pattern classification by intent","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What are the classical intent-based categories of design patterns?","answer":"The commonly presented classical categories are creational, structural, and behavioral patterns. Creational patterns address object creation mechanisms; structural patterns address composition of classes or objects; behavioral patterns address communication, responsibility assignment, or interaction.","explanation":"Classification provides a retrieval and reasoning aid rather than a requirement that every pattern fit only one conceptual dimension.","principle":"Use classification to narrow design search, not to replace contextual analysis.","correct_example":"Search creational patterns when object construction flexibility is the primary problem.","invalid_example":"Selecting any creational pattern simply because the system creates many objects.","problem":"A design problem must be mapped to an appropriate family of solutions.","failure_mode":"Category-only selection.","cause":"Confusing taxonomy with applicability.","solution":"Use the category as a first retrieval filter and then evaluate the pattern's context and consequences.","alternative":"Search by problem characteristics rather than category when the problem spans multiple dimensions.","tradeoff":"Classification improves discoverability but can bias selection if treated as prescriptive.","constraint":"Classification does not prove suitability.","edge_case":"A pattern can interact with patterns from other categories in one design.","security_implication":"Category membership provides no inherent security guarantee.","performance_implication":"Category membership provides no inherent performance guarantee.","testing_guidance":"Test the actual behavioral contract rather than category membership.","verification_guidance":"Verify pattern applicability independently of classification.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Design pattern definition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B01-0001","D13-DP-B02-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Design Patterns Catalog","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B02-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern selection","subtopic":"problem-first reasoning","concept":"Problem-first pattern selection","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an autonomous software engineering system select a design pattern?","answer":"It should first identify the design problem, context, constraints, forces, required variation, ownership boundaries, lifecycle requirements, and desired consequences; then retrieve candidate patterns and compare their applicability, trade-offs, alternatives, and failure modes.","explanation":"A pattern name is a retrieval key, not a design requirement. A reasoning system should infer candidate patterns from the problem and reject candidates whose assumptions do not hold.","principle":"Problem characterization precedes pattern selection.","correct_example":"Identify that object creation must vary independently from consumers, then evaluate Factory Method, Abstract Factory, Builder, or direct construction based on the actual variation.","invalid_example":"Selecting Factory Method whenever construction appears inside a conditional.","problem":"Pattern selection must be grounded in system requirements.","failure_mode":"Cargo-cult pattern application.","cause":"Pattern-name-driven reasoning.","solution":"Use a candidate-generation and elimination process based on applicability and consequences.","alternative":"Use no pattern when direct composition is simpler and satisfies the requirements.","tradeoff":"Reasoning before selection takes more design effort but reduces unnecessary abstraction.","constraint":"The selection process must preserve explicit requirements and constraints.","edge_case":"Several patterns may satisfy the problem with different complexity or evolution characteristics.","security_implication":"Trust boundaries and authorization responsibilities must be included in pattern selection.","performance_implication":"Candidate comparison should include allocation, indirection, synchronization, and data-flow implications where relevant.","testing_guidance":"Generate tests from the chosen pattern's behavioral contract and rejected alternatives' failure conditions.","verification_guidance":"Require an explicit rationale linking the selected pattern to the documented problem and forces.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B01-0001","D13-DP-B01-0003"],"prerequisites":["Requirement analysis"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B02-0002","D13-DP-B02-0003"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B01-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_reference","source":"Martin Fowler, Writing Software Patterns","source_ref":"turn0search6"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B02-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern selection","subtopic":"overengineering","concept":"Pattern overuse","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can applying design patterns unnecessarily harm software design?","answer":"Unnecessary patterns can add abstractions, indirection, coupling between framework-like components, and cognitive overhead without solving a real design problem.","explanation":"A pattern is useful when its consequences address forces that matter in the system. If the variation or complexity does not exist, the pattern can make a simple design harder to understand and change.","principle":"Do not introduce pattern machinery without a concrete design need.","correct_example":"Keep direct construction when construction is stable and no independent variation or substitution requirement exists.","invalid_example":"Creating factories, builders, interfaces, and registries around a simple immutable value object solely to demonstrate patterns.","problem":"A pattern increases design complexity without corresponding value.","failure_mode":"Overengineering.","cause":"Pattern-driven development or premature generalization.","solution":"Compare the pattern against the simplest design satisfying current requirements.","alternative":"Delay abstraction until a demonstrated variation point appears.","tradeoff":"Delayed abstraction can require later refactoring but avoids speculative complexity.","constraint":"Future extensibility should not be assumed without evidence.","edge_case":"A low-cost abstraction can still be justified when a stable architectural boundary is already required.","security_implication":"Additional indirection can obscure security-critical flows if poorly documented.","performance_implication":"Extra objects and dispatch layers can add overhead, though significance is workload-dependent.","testing_guidance":"Compare test complexity and behavior before and after pattern introduction.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every introduced abstraction has a documented responsibility or requirement.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B02-0001"],"prerequisites":["Design trade-off analysis"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B02-0003"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B02-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_reference","source":"Refactoring.Guru pattern criticism and guidance","source_ref":"turn0search0"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B02-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern selection","subtopic":"alternatives","concept":"Pattern versus simpler design","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What should be compared against a design pattern before adopting it?","answer":"At minimum compare the candidate pattern with a direct implementation, ordinary composition, language-native facilities, and other patterns that address the same forces.","explanation":"Patterns are alternatives within a larger design space. The best solution for a particular context may be a simple language construct rather than a named pattern.","principle":"Pattern adoption is a comparative design decision.","correct_example":"Compare Builder with a constructor plus named factory functions when constructing an immutable object with several optional values.","invalid_example":"Assuming Builder is superior because the constructor has many parameters.","problem":"The design needs a decision among multiple implementation structures.","failure_mode":"Single-option reasoning.","cause":"Failure to evaluate alternatives.","solution":"Build a decision matrix around complexity, flexibility, correctness, performance, testability, and expected change.","alternative":"Use the simplest solution that satisfies the requirements.","tradeoff":"A named pattern can improve shared vocabulary while a simpler solution may reduce code and indirection.","constraint":"Evaluation criteria must reflect actual system requirements.","edge_case":"The simplest current design may become inadequate if requirements later introduce independent variation.","security_implication":"Compare security responsibility and attack surface across alternatives.","performance_implication":"Compare theoretical and, when necessary, measured costs under representative workloads.","testing_guidance":"Compare test surface and failure observability for candidate designs.","verification_guidance":"Record why the selected alternative satisfies the relevant forces.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B02-0001"],"prerequisites":["Design alternatives"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B02-0002"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B02-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_reference","source":"Martin Fowler, Writing Software Patterns","source_ref":"turn0search6"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B03-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern description model","subtopic":"pattern anatomy","concept":"Pattern knowledge schema","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What information should a machine-readable design-pattern record contain?","answer":"A high-quality pattern record should capture intent, problem, context, forces, applicability, participants or responsibilities, collaboration, implementation considerations, consequences, alternatives, related patterns, failure modes, testing guidance, security implications, performance implications, version/language constraints, provenance, and verification status.","explanation":"A pattern name and short definition are insufficient for autonomous reasoning. The system needs the conditions under which a pattern applies and the consequences of choosing it.","principle":"Represent patterns as structured decision knowledge rather than static descriptions.","correct_example":"Store a Factory Method record with applicability conditions, creation responsibility, alternatives, consequences, and language-specific implementation notes.","invalid_example":"Store only pattern name, one-sentence definition, and code sample.","problem":"An autonomous system needs enough context to select, reject, implement, test, and verify a pattern.","failure_mode":"Context-poor pattern retrieval.","cause":"Documentation optimized only for human recognition.","solution":"Use a structured pattern schema with explicit relationships and evidence metadata.","alternative":"Maintain separate conceptual, implementation, and verification records connected by relationships.","tradeoff":"Richer schemas increase dataset complexity but improve reasoning and retrieval.","constraint":"Optional fields must not be fabricated when evidence is unavailable.","edge_case":"A pattern may have multiple valid implementations with different consequences.","security_implication":"Security implications must be represented separately from generic benefits.","performance_implication":"Performance consequences must distinguish theoretical properties from empirical measurements.","testing_guidance":"Include pattern-specific invariants and failure conditions.","verification_guidance":"Require applicability and consequence evidence before selecting a pattern automatically.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"D13 schema 1.0","version_range":"D13","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D13 pattern definition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B03-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_reference","source":"Refactoring.Guru pattern description model","source_ref":"turn0search7"},{"source_type":"dataset_architecture","source":"D13 machine-readable knowledge schema"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_DATASET_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B03-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern description model","subtopic":"forces","concept":"Design forces","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What are design forces in pattern reasoning?","answer":"Design forces are competing requirements, constraints, risks, or desired properties that influence a design decision and may prevent one solution from optimizing every dimension simultaneously.","explanation":"Forces can include changeability, coupling, testability, performance, memory usage, consistency, security boundaries, ownership, simplicity, and interoperability. A pattern is useful when its structure balances relevant forces better than alternatives for the context.","principle":"Pattern selection should explicitly reason about competing forces rather than optimizing a single attribute.","correct_example":"Choose an abstraction that allows implementation variation while maintaining a stable client contract when independent change is a primary force.","invalid_example":"Selecting a pattern solely because it maximizes extensibility while ignoring added complexity and performance constraints.","problem":"The design contains conflicting goals that require an explicit trade-off.","failure_mode":"Single-objective optimization.","cause":"Ignoring secondary design forces.","solution":"List important forces and compare candidate designs against them.","alternative":"Simplify requirements if the forces are not actually necessary.","tradeoff":"Balancing forces generally means accepting some cost in another dimension.","constraint":"Only material forces should influence pattern selection.","edge_case":"A force may become irrelevant after requirements or architecture change.","security_implication":"Security constraints can dominate other forces when trust boundaries are involved.","performance_implication":"Performance is one force among several and must be workload-specific.","testing_guidance":"Ensure tests reflect the forces claimed as important.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the chosen design actually satisfies the stated forces within documented limits.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Pattern selection"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B02-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B02-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_reference","source":"Martin Fowler, Writing Software Patterns","source_ref":"turn0search6"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B03-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern description model","subtopic":"applicability","concept":"Applicability boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must a design pattern record explicitly state when not to use the pattern?","answer":"Because a pattern describes a conditional solution, not a universally desirable structure. Explicit non-applicability conditions help an autonomous system reject attractive but inappropriate abstractions.","explanation":"The same pattern can be beneficial in one context and harmful in another. Applicability boundaries therefore form part of the pattern's decision logic.","principle":"A pattern is incomplete if its rejection conditions are unknown or ignored.","correct_example":"Document that a pattern should be reconsidered when its variation point is absent or when its additional indirection violates system constraints.","invalid_example":"Documenting only benefits and implementation steps.","problem":"The reasoning system must avoid false-positive pattern selection.","failure_mode":"Applicability overgeneralization.","cause":"Benefits recorded without boundaries.","solution":"Capture both positive applicability signals and rejection conditions.","alternative":"Use a direct implementation when pattern preconditions are absent.","tradeoff":"Explicit boundaries reduce misuse but increase documentation detail.","constraint":"Non-applicability must be tied to actual design forces or constraints.","edge_case":"A pattern may be inappropriate initially but become appropriate after a requirement change.","security_implication":"A pattern should be rejected if it obscures or weakens required security boundaries.","performance_implication":"Reject patterns when their overhead conflicts with demonstrated performance constraints.","testing_guidance":"Include negative selection cases where the pattern should not be applied.","verification_guidance":"Verify that pattern selection logic can produce a no-pattern outcome.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B03-0002"],"prerequisites":["Applicability reasoning"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B02-0002"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B03-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_reference","source":"Refactoring.Guru pattern guidance","source_ref":"turn0search7"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B04-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Factory Method","concept":"Factory Method","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does Factory Method address?","answer":"Factory Method provides a creation interface or operation whose concrete implementation can determine which product object is instantiated, allowing creation decisions to vary without requiring client code to know the concrete product class.","explanation":"The pattern is useful when a class or framework defines a creation point but subclasses or specialized implementations need to choose the concrete product.","principle":"Separate the decision about which concrete product to create from code that consumes the product when that variation is meaningful.","correct_example":"A framework defines an operation that creates a transport object while specialized implementations provide HTTP, file, or message transport products.","invalid_example":"Creating a factory method solely to wrap one fixed constructor when no creation variation is expected.","problem":"Concrete product selection must vary independently from client logic.","failure_mode":"Concrete creation coupling.","cause":"Clients directly instantiate variable concrete products.","solution":"Introduce a controlled creation point whose implementation can select the product.","alternative":"Direct construction, dependency injection, a standalone factory function, or Abstract Factory when multiple related products vary together.","tradeoff":"Reduces direct coupling to concrete products but can increase inheritance or abstraction complexity.","constraint":"The variation must justify the additional creation abstraction.","edge_case":"A factory method can accidentally become a large conditional registry if too many product decisions accumulate.","security_implication":"Creation logic can enforce construction-time validation and security invariants, but the pattern itself does not provide authorization.","performance_implication":"Additional dispatch or object creation layers may add overhead; significance is implementation-dependent.","testing_guidance":"Test each supported product selection and invalid or unsupported creation request.","verification_guidance":"Verify that clients depend on the intended product contract rather than concrete product details.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B03-0001"],"prerequisites":["Polymorphism or equivalent abstraction"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B04-0002","D13-DP-B04-0003","D13-DP-B05-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B03-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B05-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Factory Method","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B04-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Factory Method","concept":"Factory Method applicability","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When is Factory Method a reasonable design candidate?","answer":"It is a candidate when concrete product creation varies, clients should depend on a product abstraction, and the variation is naturally associated with a creation operation or implementation family.","explanation":"The pattern becomes less compelling when creation is stable, a simple factory function is sufficient, or a dependency-injection mechanism already provides the required decoupling.","principle":"Use Factory Method when creation variation is a first-class design concern.","correct_example":"A reusable framework delegates creation of framework-managed extension objects to specialized implementations.","invalid_example":"Adding subclasses solely to change which constant object is constructed.","problem":"Creation variation must be localized without spreading concrete-type dependencies.","failure_mode":"Creation logic duplication.","cause":"Each caller implements its own concrete-selection rules.","solution":"Centralize the creation variation at an explicit boundary.","alternative":"Dependency injection or a simple factory function.","tradeoff":"Factory Method can align creation with polymorphic behavior but may increase class count.","constraint":"The abstraction should remain cohesive.","edge_case":"Too many product types can turn a simple factory into a registry or configuration problem.","security_implication":"Validate product identifiers and construction parameters before creating privileged resources.","performance_implication":"Creation overhead depends on concrete products and runtime dispatch; no universal benchmark should be assumed.","testing_guidance":"Test concrete selection, product contract compliance, and unsupported-product behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that changing product selection does not require changing unaffected client logic.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B04-0001"],"prerequisites":["Factory Method concept"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B04-0003"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B04-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B05-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Factory Method","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B04-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Factory Method","concept":"Factory Method failure modes","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What common design failures can occur when Factory Method is misapplied?","answer":"Common failures include unnecessary subclass proliferation, creation logic becoming overly complex, hidden product coupling, and using inheritance solely to vary construction when composition would be simpler.","explanation":"Factory Method can solve creation coupling but may introduce a class hierarchy that is more complex than the original construction problem.","principle":"The creation abstraction should be smaller than the complexity it removes.","correct_example":"Use a factory method when specialized implementations already have meaningful behavioral differences and creation is part of that variation.","invalid_example":"Creating one subclass per product solely to override a trivial constructor call.","problem":"Factory Method introduces more structural complexity than creation variability warrants.","failure_mode":"Subclass explosion.","cause":"Using inheritance as a default factory mechanism.","solution":"Evaluate standalone factories, dependency injection, configuration, or composition.","alternative":"Simple factory function or injected creator.","tradeoff":"Composition often reduces class hierarchy size but can move configuration elsewhere.","constraint":"Do not introduce inheritance solely for naming a creation operation.","edge_case":"A product family may later require independent behavioral variation, making a subclass-based creation point reasonable.","security_implication":"Complex factory registries can accidentally permit unauthorized product construction if registration is not controlled.","performance_implication":"Large dynamic registries can add lookup and initialization overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test unsupported products, registration failures, invalid parameters, and product contract violations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the factory's responsibilities remain limited to legitimate creation concerns.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B04-0002"],"prerequisites":["Factory Method applicability"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B02-0002","D13-DP-B04-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B04-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B05-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_reference","source":"Refactoring.Guru Factory Method and pattern criticism","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B05-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Abstract Factory","concept":"Abstract Factory","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does Abstract Factory address?","answer":"Abstract Factory provides an interface for creating families of related products while allowing concrete product families to vary without requiring client code to depend directly on their concrete classes.","explanation":"The key concern is coordinated creation of related products whose combinations should remain compatible.","principle":"Abstract coordinated product creation when multiple related products vary together.","correct_example":"A UI abstraction creates a matching family of Button, Menu, and Dialog objects for one platform or theme.","invalid_example":"Using Abstract Factory when there is only one independent product type and no family-level variation.","problem":"Multiple related products must be created consistently without hard-coding one concrete family.","failure_mode":"Product-family coupling.","cause":"Clients directly instantiate concrete products from one family.","solution":"Define abstract product interfaces and an abstract family-creation interface.","alternative":"Factory Method for one creation point, dependency injection, configuration, or direct construction.","tradeoff":"Improves family consistency and substitution but introduces more interfaces and product types.","constraint":"Products within a family should satisfy compatible semantic contracts.","edge_case":"Adding a new product type can require changes across every concrete factory family.","security_implication":"Factory selection can become a policy boundary and must not allow unauthorized family selection.","performance_implication":"Factory indirection is usually secondary to product creation cost but should be measured when performance-sensitive.","testing_guidance":"Test that each concrete factory creates a compatible product family.","verification_guidance":"Verify cross-product compatibility and family substitution behavior.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B04-0001"],"prerequisites":["Factory abstraction","Product interfaces"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B05-0002","D13-DP-B05-0003","D13-DP-B04-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B04-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B04-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Abstract Factory","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B05-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Abstract Factory","concept":"Abstract Factory versus Factory Method","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the key distinction between Factory Method and Abstract Factory?","answer":"Factory Method typically focuses on delegating or varying creation of a product through a creation operation, whereas Abstract Factory focuses on creating a coordinated family of related products through a family-level abstraction.","explanation":"The distinction is about the scope of creation variation. A design may combine both patterns when a product family contains individual creation variation points.","principle":"Choose the abstraction level that matches the scope of creation variability.","correct_example":"Use Abstract Factory when Button, Menu, and Dialog must all come from the same compatible platform family.","invalid_example":"Using Abstract Factory solely because one object needs an alternative constructor.","problem":"The system must choose the correct granularity of creation abstraction.","failure_mode":"Wrong abstraction granularity.","cause":"Treating all factory mechanisms as interchangeable.","solution":"Identify whether variation concerns one product creation point or a coordinated family.","alternative":"Factory Method, simple factory, dependency injection, or direct construction.","tradeoff":"Family-level abstraction improves compatibility but increases interface surface.","constraint":"The family concept must be meaningful in the domain.","edge_case":"A system can begin with one product and later evolve into a product family.","security_implication":"Family selection should not accidentally mix components with incompatible trust or security assumptions.","performance_implication":"Additional family factories add indirection but usually do not determine overall performance alone.","testing_guidance":"Test both individual product contracts and cross-product family compatibility.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the selected abstraction matches the actual variation boundary.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B05-0001"],"prerequisites":["Factory Method","Abstract Factory"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B04-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B05-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B04-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru pattern catalog","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B05-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Abstract Factory","concept":"Abstract Factory failure modes","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What failure mode is particularly important when designing an Abstract Factory?","answer":"Adding a new product type can require modifying the abstract factory contract and every concrete factory, creating broad change impact across product families.","explanation":"Abstract Factory strongly models variation across product families but can make variation in the dimensions of the family itself expensive.","principle":"Choose Abstract Factory when family consistency matters more than unrestricted ease of adding new product dimensions.","correct_example":"Use a family abstraction when new platform families are expected but the product categories are relatively stable.","invalid_example":"Using Abstract Factory when new product categories are added frequently across many independent implementations.","problem":"The design must support evolution along multiple dimensions.","failure_mode":"Factory hierarchy rigidity.","cause":"Mismatch between factory abstraction and expected change direction.","solution":"Analyze the primary axis of variation before choosing the pattern.","alternative":"Dependency injection, registries, composition, or another creation architecture.","tradeoff":"Strong family consistency can reduce flexibility along other evolution dimensions.","constraint":"Expected change direction must be treated as an explicit design assumption.","edge_case":"Requirements can change and invalidate the original variation axis.","security_implication":"Adding product types should not bypass family-level security invariants.","performance_implication":"Factory hierarchy complexity can affect initialization and object-creation paths but requires target-specific evidence.","testing_guidance":"Test every factory against every product contract and family compatibility invariant.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the chosen factory abstraction matches the documented evolution model.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B05-0002"],"prerequisites":["Evolution analysis"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B02-0002"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B05-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_reference","source":"Refactoring.Guru Abstract Factory discussion","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B06-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Builder","concept":"Builder","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does Builder address?","answer":"Builder separates the construction of a complex object from the representation or construction process, allowing an object to be assembled through controlled steps and potentially supporting multiple representations or configurations.","explanation":"Builder is particularly useful when construction involves many optional components, ordered construction steps, validation, or multiple representations.","principle":"Separate complex construction from the final object's representation when construction complexity itself is a design concern.","correct_example":"Construct a complex immutable request through explicit builder operations followed by validation and finalization.","invalid_example":"Introducing a builder for an object with one required value and one simple constructor.","problem":"Direct constructors or construction logic become difficult to read, validate, or vary.","failure_mode":"Construction complexity leakage.","cause":"Clients directly manage complex assembly rules.","solution":"Encapsulate construction steps behind a builder contract.","alternative":"Constructors, named factory functions, parameter objects, or configuration objects.","tradeoff":"Builder can improve readability and validation while increasing API surface and object count.","constraint":"The builder should not become a second mutable domain object with unclear ownership.","edge_case":"A builder may be reused accidentally after finalization if lifecycle rules are unclear.","security_implication":"Builder validation must occur before constructing security-sensitive or privileged objects.","performance_implication":"Builder objects and intermediate state can add allocations; significance is implementation-dependent.","testing_guidance":"Test valid construction sequences, missing required fields, invalid combinations, reuse, and finalization behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that final objects satisfy all construction invariants regardless of build sequence where order is not intentionally significant.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B03-0001"],"prerequisites":["Object construction","Validation"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B06-0002","D13-DP-B06-0003"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B03-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B07-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Builder","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B06-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Builder","concept":"Builder applicability","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When is Builder a strong candidate?","answer":"Builder is a candidate when construction has enough optional parts, ordering, validation rules, configuration combinations, or representation concerns that direct construction becomes difficult to understand or safely control.","explanation":"The pattern is justified by construction complexity rather than by the mere existence of a constructor.","principle":"Use Builder when construction itself contains meaningful complexity.","correct_example":"Use a builder for an object requiring several optional configuration groups and cross-field validation before finalization.","invalid_example":"Using a builder for a two-field value object with no meaningful construction invariants.","problem":"Construction needs controlled assembly and validation.","failure_mode":"Constructor overload complexity.","cause":"Too many optional or interacting construction parameters.","solution":"Encapsulate construction state and validation in a builder or another explicit construction mechanism.","alternative":"Named factory methods or a parameter object when they remain clear.","tradeoff":"Builder improves construction readability but adds another lifecycle abstraction.","constraint":"The builder's API should represent valid construction states where practical.","edge_case":"Optional fields can create many invalid combinations if validation is delayed too long.","security_implication":"Do not allow partially validated builders to escape a trusted construction boundary.","performance_implication":"Intermediate builder state may consume memory temporarily.","testing_guidance":"Generate combinations of optional fields and verify invariant enforcement.","verification_guidance":"Verify that finalization cannot produce an invalid object through any supported builder path.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B06-0001"],"prerequisites":["Construction invariants"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B06-0003"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B06-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Builder","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B06-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Builder","concept":"Builder failure modes","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a Builder implementation become harmful?","answer":"A builder can become a parallel mutable representation of the final object, accumulate excessive configuration logic, allow invalid intermediate states, or duplicate validation and domain rules.","explanation":"Builder should simplify construction, not become a second domain model with conflicting semantics.","principle":"Keep construction responsibility separate from domain behavior and ensure validation has a clear authority.","correct_example":"Builder collects construction parameters and performs construction-time validation while the resulting domain object owns its enduring invariants.","invalid_example":"Duplicating every domain rule in both builder and final object with different behavior.","problem":"Construction and domain validation become inconsistent.","failure_mode":"Validation divergence.","cause":"Duplicated business rules.","solution":"Define authoritative invariant ownership and keep builder logic focused on construction concerns.","alternative":"Use validated value objects or named factories for smaller construction problems.","tradeoff":"Centralized validation can reduce duplication but may constrain construction flexibility.","constraint":"Final objects must remain valid independently of builder implementation.","edge_case":"Objects loaded from persistence may bypass normal builder paths and still require invariant enforcement.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive invariants must not exist only inside an optional builder path.","performance_implication":"Repeated validation can add cost; avoid redundant expensive checks when correctness permits.","testing_guidance":"Test direct construction or deserialization paths in addition to builder paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify that all supported construction paths enforce required invariants.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B06-0002"],"prerequisites":["Invariant ownership"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B06-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B06-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_principle","source":"Construction invariant ownership"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B07-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Prototype","concept":"Prototype","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does Prototype address?","answer":"Prototype creates new objects by copying an existing representative object rather than requiring direct knowledge of the concrete object's construction process.","explanation":"Prototype can be useful when object creation is expensive or complex, when concrete types should remain hidden from clients, or when copying a configured baseline is the desired construction model.","principle":"Use cloning of a known prototype when copying state is a better abstraction than reconstructing an object from concrete construction details.","correct_example":"Create configured document templates by cloning a validated prototype and then applying controlled modifications.","invalid_example":"Using Prototype merely to avoid writing a normal constructor for a simple object.","problem":"Clients need new instances without coupling to concrete construction details.","failure_mode":"Construction coupling or expensive reconstruction.","cause":"Complex object creation cannot be conveniently expressed through ordinary construction.","solution":"Define a controlled copy operation with explicit copy semantics.","alternative":"Factory, Builder, direct construction, serialization-based reconstruction, or dependency injection.","tradeoff":"Prototype can simplify creation but introduces difficult identity, aliasing, and deep-versus-shallow-copy semantics.","constraint":"Copy semantics must be explicitly defined.","edge_case":"Nested mutable objects can remain shared after a shallow copy.","security_implication":"Copied objects may unintentionally inherit credentials, permissions, secrets, or security-sensitive state.","performance_implication":"Deep copying can be expensive in time and memory; shallow copying can create aliasing risks.","testing_guidance":"Test identity, nested mutable state, copy depth, resource ownership, and post-copy mutation isolation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that copied instances contain exactly the state that the contract says is copied and do not share prohibited mutable state.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D12 Data Structures"],"prerequisites":["Object identity","Copy semantics"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B07-0002","D13-DP-B07-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B06-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Prototype","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B07-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Prototype","concept":"Copy semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must be specified before using Prototype?","answer":"The design must specify which state is copied, whether nested objects are shared or duplicated, how identity changes, how external resources are handled, and which fields must be reset or regenerated.","explanation":"Copying an object is not automatically equivalent to creating an independent object. Resource ownership and identity often require special handling.","principle":"Prototype requires an explicit copy contract.","correct_example":"Clone configuration state while generating a new identifier and excluding live network handles.","invalid_example":"Blindly copying every field including unique identifiers and open resource handles.","problem":"The copy operation produces invalid identity or shared-resource behavior.","failure_mode":"Unsafe cloning.","cause":"Implicit copy semantics.","solution":"Define copy categories and explicitly handle identity and resources.","alternative":"Use a factory or builder that reconstructs the required state.","tradeoff":"Explicit reconstruction can be safer but more verbose.","constraint":"External resources generally require lifecycle-aware handling rather than raw copying.","edge_case":"A nested object may be immutable and safely shareable even when other nested objects must be copied.","security_implication":"Secrets and authorization state must be deliberately included or excluded rather than inherited accidentally.","performance_implication":"Deep copying large graphs can have substantial memory and CPU cost.","testing_guidance":"Test mutation after copying and resource cleanup independently.","verification_guidance":"Verify copy invariants and prohibited shared references.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B07-0001"],"prerequisites":["Prototype"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B07-0003"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B07-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_principle","source":"Object copying and ownership semantics"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B07-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Prototype","concept":"Prototype security failure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security risk can arise from cloning an object?","answer":"A clone can unintentionally inherit sensitive credentials, authorization state, secrets, or privileged configuration from the prototype if copy semantics are not security-aware.","explanation":"Prototype copies state by design, so security-sensitive fields require explicit classification. A new object may need fresh credentials, identity, ownership, or authorization context.","principle":"Security-sensitive state must never be copied implicitly merely because it is stored on the prototype.","correct_example":"Clone non-sensitive configuration while generating fresh credentials and identity for the new instance.","invalid_example":"Copying an authenticated session object and reusing its authorization token for a new identity.","problem":"Copied state crosses an identity or trust boundary unintentionally.","failure_mode":"Privilege or credential inheritance.","cause":"Unclassified security-sensitive fields.","solution":"Classify fields and explicitly define copy behavior for identity, credentials, authorization, and secrets.","alternative":"Use a factory that constructs a new security context from approved configuration.","tradeoff":"Explicit security reconstruction adds complexity but reduces accidental privilege inheritance.","constraint":"Identity and authorization semantics must be defined separately from ordinary object state.","edge_case":"A prototype may contain references to mutable security policy objects that remain shared.","security_implication":"HIGH; cloning can duplicate security state across object identities.","performance_implication":"Security-safe deep copying can increase resource usage.","testing_guidance":"Use negative tests to confirm credentials, identities, and permissions are not unintentionally inherited.","verification_guidance":"Verify security-sensitive fields against an explicit clone policy.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B07-0002"],"prerequisites":["Security state classification"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B07-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B07-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"security_design_principle","source":"Object identity and security-state isolation"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B08-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Singleton","concept":"Singleton","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does Singleton attempt to solve?","answer":"Singleton restricts a type to one instance within a defined scope and provides a shared access mechanism to that instance.","explanation":"The pattern can represent a resource or coordinator for which exactly one instance is required within a clearly defined scope. The scope must be explicit because process-wide, application-wide, module-wide, and dependency-injection-container-wide uniqueness are different guarantees.","principle":"Use Singleton only when uniqueness is a genuine domain or infrastructure requirement and the scope of uniqueness is explicit.","correct_example":"Represent a process-wide registry only when process-wide uniqueness is itself a documented requirement and lifecycle is controlled.","invalid_example":"Making every service a Singleton simply to avoid dependency passing.","problem":"The system requires controlled uniqueness or shared coordination.","failure_mode":"Uncontrolled global state.","cause":"Singleton used as a convenience for access rather than a real uniqueness constraint.","solution":"Prefer explicit dependency ownership and dependency injection unless uniqueness is required.","alternative":"Dependency injection with a scoped lifetime, module-level state, or an explicitly owned shared resource.","tradeoff":"Singleton simplifies access but can introduce hidden dependencies, global mutable state, and testing difficulties.","constraint":"Uniqueness scope and lifecycle must be explicit.","edge_case":"Multiple processes, containers, workers, or test environments can each have their own instance despite an application-level Singleton implementation.","security_implication":"Global mutable state can become a privilege or tenant-isolation risk if scope is broader than intended.","performance_implication":"Shared synchronization or contention can become a bottleneck depending on the resource and implementation.","testing_guidance":"Test lifecycle, isolation, reset behavior, concurrency, and scope boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Verify the actual runtime scope of uniqueness rather than assuming the pattern provides system-wide uniqueness.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Object lifecycle","Dependency management"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B08-0002","D13-DP-B08-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B02-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Singleton","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B08-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Singleton","concept":"Singleton versus dependency injection","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why is dependency injection often an alternative to Singleton?","answer":"Dependency injection makes dependencies explicit and allows lifecycle and scope to be controlled externally, whereas Singleton commonly exposes shared state through a globally reachable access mechanism.","explanation":"The important distinction is dependency visibility and ownership. Dependency injection can still provide a single instance within a defined scope without making the dependency globally accessible.","principle":"Prefer explicit dependency ownership when global access is not itself a requirement.","correct_example":"Configure one service instance per application scope and inject it into consumers.","invalid_example":"Use a Singleton solely because many unrelated components need access to a service.","problem":"Consumers need a shared dependency without hidden global coupling.","failure_mode":"Global dependency coupling.","cause":"Access convenience mistaken for lifecycle requirement.","solution":"Use dependency injection with an explicitly defined scope.","alternative":"Singleton when a genuine globally unique resource is required and its scope is controlled.","tradeoff":"Injection increases wiring but improves testability and dependency visibility.","constraint":"The dependency container or composition root must define lifecycle correctly.","edge_case":"Multiple application instances or test suites can have separate scopes.","security_implication":"Explicit injection can make tenant and authorization boundaries easier to reason about than uncontrolled globals.","performance_implication":"Dependency injection itself does not establish a universal performance cost; lifecycle and implementation determine behavior.","testing_guidance":"Inject test doubles or isolated instances to verify consumers independently.","verification_guidance":"Verify lifecycle scope and dependency graph explicitly.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B08-0001"],"prerequisites":["Dependency injection"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B08-0003"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B08-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B08-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_principle","source":"Dependency visibility and lifecycle management"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B08-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"Singleton","concept":"Singleton testing failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can Singleton complicate testing?","answer":"A Singleton can retain mutable state across tests, hide dependencies, create ordering effects, and make isolation difficult when consumers access a shared global instance directly.","explanation":"Test isolation becomes harder when the lifecycle of shared state is controlled outside the test's dependency graph.","principle":"Shared state should have an explicit lifecycle and reset strategy if it must exist.","correct_example":"Inject a scoped shared component and create an isolated instance for each test context.","invalid_example":"Tests mutate a global Singleton and rely on execution order to restore its state.","problem":"Tests interfere through shared mutable state.","failure_mode":"Order-dependent tests.","cause":"Implicit global lifecycle.","solution":"Use dependency injection, controlled scopes, or explicit reset boundaries.","alternative":"Keep Singleton state immutable or encapsulate it behind a controlled interface.","tradeoff":"Isolation mechanisms can require more setup but improve deterministic testing.","constraint":"Reset operations must not compromise production invariants.","edge_case":"Parallel tests can expose race conditions in shared Singleton state.","security_implication":"Test leakage can accidentally expose credentials or tenant data between test contexts.","performance_implication":"Global synchronization can create contention under concurrent workloads.","testing_guidance":"Run tests in randomized and parallel order where supported to expose hidden shared-state coupling.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each test can establish and clean up its required state independently.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B08-0002"],"prerequisites":["Test isolation"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B08-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B08-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_principle","source":"Global-state testing risks"}],"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B09-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational pattern reasoning","subtopic":"construction decision tree","concept":"Construction pattern decision model","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an autonomous system reason about common creational pattern candidates?","answer":"First determine whether direct construction is sufficient. If creation of one product varies, consider Factory Method or a factory function. If related product families must vary together, consider Abstract Factory. If complex assembly or validation is the problem, consider Builder. If copying configured state is the desired construction mechanism, consider Prototype. If genuine scoped uniqueness is required, consider Singleton or an explicit scoped lifecycle.","explanation":"The decision tree maps problem characteristics to candidate patterns while retaining a no-pattern option.","principle":"Select the smallest creation abstraction that satisfies the actual creation forces.","correct_example":"Use Builder for complex assembly, not Abstract Factory, when the primary variation is construction sequence rather than product family.","invalid_example":"Selecting Singleton because an object is expensive to construct without establishing a uniqueness requirement.","problem":"The brain must distinguish superficially similar creational problems.","failure_mode":"Pattern misclassification.","cause":"Keyword matching without context.","solution":"Use explicit decision predicates and rejection conditions.","alternative":"Use direct construction or dependency injection when no pattern-specific problem exists.","tradeoff":"Decision reasoning adds analysis but reduces cargo-cult pattern use.","constraint":"Multiple candidates can remain valid until system-specific forces resolve the choice.","edge_case":"A real system may combine Builder with Factory or Prototype.","security_implication":"Creation decisions involving privileged resources require explicit authorization and validation regardless of pattern.","performance_implication":"Creation cost should be analyzed at the product/resource level rather than inferred from pattern names.","testing_guidance":"Test both selected-pattern behavior and negative cases where simpler alternatives should be selected.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every selected creational pattern has an explicit problem-to-pattern rationale.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"D13 schema 1.0","version_range":"D13","dependencies":["D13-DP-B04-0001","D13-DP-B05-0001","D13-DP-B06-0001","D13-DP-B07-0001","D13-DP-B08-0001"],"prerequisites":["Creational patterns"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B02-0001","D13-DP-B09-0002"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B04-0001","D13-DP-B05-0001","D13-DP-B06-0001","D13-DP-B07-0001","D13-DP-B08-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru pattern catalog","source_ref":"turn0search1"},{"source_type":"software_engineering_reference","source":"Martin Fowler pattern guidance","source_ref":"turn0search6"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B09-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational pattern reasoning","subtopic":"combined patterns","concept":"Pattern composition","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Can multiple design patterns be combined in one design?","answer":"Yes. Patterns can be composed when their responsibilities and consequences are compatible, but each pattern should solve a distinct design concern and the combined system must preserve all relevant invariants.","explanation":"A Builder can construct a product selected by a factory, or a prototype can be managed by another creation abstraction. Composition increases design power but also increases reasoning complexity.","principle":"Compose patterns by responsibility, not by pattern count.","correct_example":"Use a factory to select a product family and a builder to construct a complex member of that family when both variation dimensions are real.","invalid_example":"Combining multiple creational patterns around a simple object merely to maximize extensibility.","problem":"One design contains multiple independent creation forces.","failure_mode":"Pattern stacking without responsibility separation.","cause":"Treating pattern composition as inherently beneficial.","solution":"Define each pattern's responsibility, interaction contract, and incremental value.","alternative":"Remove patterns whose responsibilities overlap or are unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Composition can improve modularity but increases abstraction and interaction surface.","constraint":"Patterns must not introduce circular ownership or duplicated creation rules.","edge_case":"Two patterns may both attempt to own validation or lifecycle decisions.","security_implication":"Composed creation paths must not create a bypass around security validation.","performance_implication":"Multiple layers can add allocations and dispatch; significance must be evaluated in context.","testing_guidance":"Test each pattern independently and the integrated creation workflow.","verification_guidance":"Verify responsibility boundaries and invariants at every composition boundary.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B09-0001"],"prerequisites":["Pattern composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B06-0001","D13-DP-B04-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B09-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B02-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_engineering_reference","source":"Pattern composition principles","source_ref":"turn0search6"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B09-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational pattern reasoning","subtopic":"verification","concept":"Pattern-selection verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an autonomous software engineering brain verify a selected design pattern?","answer":"It should verify that the pattern's applicability conditions are satisfied, the intended responsibility boundaries exist, required invariants hold, stated consequences are acceptable, alternatives were considered where material, and implementation behavior matches the selected design contract.","explanation":"Successful implementation alone does not prove that a pattern was appropriate. Verification must cover both design intent and resulting behavior.","principle":"Verify pattern selection as a design decision, not merely pattern implementation as code.","correct_example":"Verify that Factory Method actually isolates variable creation and that clients remain independent of concrete products.","invalid_example":"Marking Factory Method verified because the factory class compiles and unit tests execute.","problem":"The system must establish that a design abstraction solves the intended problem.","failure_mode":"Implementation-only verification.","cause":"Ignoring design-level requirements.","solution":"Create explicit pattern-selection verification records linking problem, applicability, consequences, and evidence.","alternative":"Perform architecture/design review when automated verification cannot establish intent.","tradeoff":"Design verification requires richer evidence than compilation or unit tests.","constraint":"Some design qualities remain partially observable and require explicit uncertainty.","edge_case":"Two different patterns may both satisfy the same requirements.","security_implication":"Verify that the chosen pattern preserves security boundaries and does not create bypass paths.","performance_implication":"Verify only measured performance claims with actual measurements; theoretical consequences remain theoretical.","testing_guidance":"Combine behavioral tests, structural checks, and targeted negative cases.","verification_guidance":"Use the sequence GENERATED != EXECUTED != TESTED != PASSED != VERIFIED.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"D13 schema 1.0","version_range":"D13","dependencies":["D13-DP-B02-0001","D13-DP-B03-0001"],"prerequisites":["Design verification"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B10-0003"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B02-0001","D13-DP-B03-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"Track B verification policy"},{"source_type":"software_engineering_reference","source":"Pattern documentation principles","source_ref":"turn0search7"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE_PLUS_REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B10-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"pattern coverage","concept":"Classical creational pattern coverage","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Which classical creational patterns are included in the core D13 pattern catalog?","answer":"The classical catalog commonly presented by intent includes Factory Method, Abstract Factory, Builder, Prototype, and Singleton as creational patterns.","explanation":"These five patterns form the core classical creational group used in many design-pattern references. D13 will extend beyond memorization by representing applicability, alternatives, failure modes, testing, security, performance, version, and relationship metadata.","principle":"Pattern catalogs are foundations for knowledge organization, not exhaustive limits on modern design knowledge.","correct_example":"Represent each classical creational pattern as a separate machine-readable concept with explicit relationships and decision conditions.","invalid_example":"Treating the five classical creational patterns as the complete universe of software construction techniques.","problem":"The brain needs a stable baseline catalog without becoming limited to historical pattern names.","failure_mode":"Catalog absolutism.","cause":"Confusing a canonical catalog with the entire design space.","solution":"Use classical patterns as a foundational vocabulary and later include modern, language-specific, architectural, concurrency, distributed, and domain patterns when within D13 scope.","alternative":"Use problem-oriented pattern retrieval without relying on a fixed catalog.","tradeoff":"A stable catalog improves retrieval consistency while broader coverage improves practical applicability.","constraint":"Each later pattern must have its own provenance and applicability evidence.","edge_case":"Different pattern references can classify or name patterns differently.","security_implication":"Pattern category membership does not establish security properties.","performance_implication":"Pattern classification does not establish performance characteristics.","testing_guidance":"Ensure catalog records are distinct and cross-linked without duplicate semantic records.","verification_guidance":"Verify catalog membership against the cited source and preserve source/version metadata.","language":"language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Design pattern classification"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B01-0003","D13-DP-B04-0001","D13-DP-B05-0001","D13-DP-B06-0001","D13-DP-B07-0001","D13-DP-B08-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Design Patterns Catalog","source_ref":"turn0search1"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B10-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Creational patterns","subtopic":"language adaptation","concept":"Pattern implementation is language-dependent","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should D13 separate pattern concepts from language-specific implementations?","answer":"The same design pattern can be implemented differently across languages because languages provide different abstraction mechanisms, type systems, object models, module systems, concurrency primitives, and standard libraries.","explanation":"A language-neutral pattern concept should remain stable while implementation records capture language-specific idioms and constraints.","principle":"Separate invariant design intent from language-specific realization.","correct_example":"Store the conceptual Factory Method record separately from Java, Python, Rust, TypeScript, or C# implementation guidance.","invalid_example":"Treating a Java inheritance implementation as the definition of Factory Method itself.","problem":"The brain must transfer design knowledge across languages without copying incompatible implementation details.","failure_mode":"Language-bound pattern reasoning.","cause":"Conflating concept with one implementation.","solution":"Maintain language-neutral canonical records plus versioned language-specific implementation records.","alternative":"Use language-specific pattern records when the language changes the design semantics materially.","tradeoff":"Separate layers improve transferability but require additional relationship metadata.","constraint":"Language-specific guarantees must not be generalized across languages.","edge_case":"A language may make a classical pattern unnecessary through built-in language features.","security_implication":"Language-specific security semantics must be validated independently.","performance_implication":"Equivalent pattern implementations can have different runtime costs across languages.","testing_guidance":"Use language-specific tests against a shared language-neutral behavioral contract.","verification_guidance":"Verify semantic equivalence rather than syntactic similarity.","language":"multi-language","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B09-0003"],"prerequisites":["Cross-language reasoning"],"related_records":["D12-DS-B94-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B09-0003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reputable_pattern_reference","source":"Refactoring.Guru multilingual pattern examples","source_ref":"turn0search2"}],"evidence_level":"REPUTABLE_REFERENCE_PLUS_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_VALIDATED_NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B10-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"D13 quality foundation","subtopic":"batch quality gate","concept":"D13 B01-B10 release quality","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must pass before D13 B01-B10 can be considered complete?","answer":"The batch must satisfy schema integrity, stable identifiers, English-only content, provenance requirements, explicit evidence status, pattern applicability boundaries, alternatives, trade-offs, failure modes, security and performance discipline, testing guidance, verification guidance, relationship integrity, and no fabricated execution or benchmark claims.","explanation":"Early batches establish the semantic foundation for the remaining D13 pattern families. Weak foundation records would propagate poor pattern-selection behavior throughout the dataset.","principle":"Foundational pattern knowledge must be validated for reasoning quality before expanding the catalog.","correct_example":"Keep implementation examples marked NOT_EXECUTED unless actual execution evidence exists.","invalid_example":"Marking examples VERIFIED because they are plausible or syntactically conventional.","problem":"The dataset must remain reliable for autonomous reasoning.","failure_mode":"Foundation contamination.","cause":"Insufficient evidence and schema validation.","solution":"Run structural, semantic, provenance, relationship, contradiction, and deduplication gates before advancing.","alternative":"Mark unresolved records UNKNOWN or NOT_VERIFIED rather than fabricating evidence.","tradeoff":"Explicit uncertainty reduces apparent completeness but protects downstream reliability.","constraint":"No fabricated execution, benchmark, provenance, or operational experience.","edge_case":"Some design claims are inherently contextual and should remain conditional rather than universal.","security_implication":"Security implications must remain defensive and context-specific.","performance_implication":"Theoretical performance implications must not be represented as empirical measurements.","testing_guidance":"Validate both positive and negative pattern-selection examples.","verification_guidance":"Apply the full dataset verification model: GENERATED != EXECUTED != TESTED != PASSED != VERIFIED.","language":"English","version":"D13 schema 1.0","version_range":"D13","dependencies":["D13-DP-B09-0003"],"prerequisites":["D13 schema","Track B quality policy"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B03-0001","D13-DP-B09-0003"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B09-0003"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"dataset_governance","source":"Track B dataset quality policy"}],"evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

