{"record_id":"D13-SE-B11-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Requirement Analysis","concept":"Functional requirement","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a functional requirement?","answer":"A functional requirement specifies a behavior, capability, or service that a software system must provide under defined conditions.","explanation":"Functional requirements describe observable system behavior rather than implementation details. They can often be expressed as inputs, processing rules, outputs, state changes, or externally observable interactions.","principle":"Requirements should describe required system behavior in terms that can be verified.","correct_example":"The system shall reject an order when the requested quantity exceeds available inventory.","invalid_example":"The system shall use a highly optimized inventory algorithm.","problem":"Requirements may accidentally prescribe implementation rather than behavior.","failure_mode":"Implementation constraints are mistaken for business or system requirements.","cause":"Requirement wording focuses on technology instead of externally observable behavior.","solution":"Separate behavioral requirements from implementation constraints and record each with its appropriate type.","testing_guidance":"Create acceptance tests whose observable outcomes correspond directly to the functional requirement.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every functional requirement has an observable acceptance condition.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Basic requirements engineering"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B11-0002","D13-SE-B12-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Requirements engineering practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B11-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Requirement Analysis","concept":"Non-functional requirement","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a non-functional requirement?","answer":"A non-functional requirement specifies a quality attribute, constraint, or operational property governing how a system must behave or be operated.","explanation":"Examples include latency targets, availability requirements, security constraints, auditability, maintainability, portability, and resource limits. A non-functional requirement should still be measurable or objectively assessable when practical.","principle":"Quality requirements must be expressed in verifiable terms whenever possible.","correct_example":"95 percent of API requests shall complete within 200 milliseconds under the defined workload.","invalid_example":"The API shall be very fast.","problem":"Vague quality requirements cannot reliably drive engineering or verification.","failure_mode":"Different stakeholders interpret the same quality requirement differently.","cause":"Absence of measurable conditions, workload, scope, or acceptance criteria.","solution":"Specify metric, threshold, workload, scope, environment, and measurement method where applicable.","testing_guidance":"Use performance, security, reliability, or operational tests appropriate to the requirement.","verification_guidance":"Verify the requirement against an explicit measurement or objective acceptance rule.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Basic requirements engineering"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B11-0001","D13-SE-B12-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Software quality engineering practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B11-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Requirement Analysis","concept":"Requirement ambiguity","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is ambiguous requirement wording dangerous?","answer":"Ambiguous requirements permit multiple interpretations, which can produce inconsistent implementations, tests, and stakeholder expectations.","explanation":"Ambiguity can remain hidden until integration or acceptance. The Software Engineering Brain should detect ambiguous terms and convert them into explicit conditions whenever practical.","principle":"A requirement should have a sufficiently precise interpretation for implementation and verification.","correct_example":"The session shall expire after 30 minutes of inactivity.","invalid_example":"The session shall expire after a reasonable period.","problem":"The word reasonable does not define an objective boundary.","failure_mode":"Implementation and test teams choose different interpretations.","cause":"Missing measurable constraint.","solution":"Replace subjective wording with explicit timing, scope, exception, and measurement conditions.","testing_guidance":"Create boundary tests around the specified threshold.","verification_guidance":"Check that the requirement has one operationally testable interpretation.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D13-SE-B11-0001"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B11-0004","D13-SE-B13-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Requirements analysis practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B11-0004","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Requirement Analysis","concept":"Requirement traceability","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is requirement traceability?","answer":"Requirement traceability links requirements to their sources, design elements, implementation artifacts, tests, verification evidence, and resulting changes.","explanation":"Traceability enables impact analysis and evidence-based verification. It allows the Software Engineering Brain to determine which artifacts implement a requirement and which tests establish evidence for it.","principle":"Important requirements should have bidirectional traceability across the engineering lifecycle.","correct_example":"REQ-017 links to its design decision, implementation modules, acceptance tests, and verification result.","invalid_example":"A requirement exists only in an informal message with no artifact or test linkage.","problem":"Untraceable requirements make completeness and change impact difficult to determine.","failure_mode":"A requirement is implemented but not tested, or changed without updating dependent artifacts.","cause":"Missing stable identifiers and relationship tracking.","solution":"Assign stable requirement identifiers and maintain explicit artifact relationships.","testing_guidance":"Check that each test maps to one or more requirements and that important requirements map to verification evidence.","verification_guidance":"Perform bidirectional traceability analysis and identify orphaned requirements or tests.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B11-0001"],"prerequisites":["Requirement analysis"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B12-0003","D13-SE-B20-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Requirements traceability practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B12-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Specification","concept":"Acceptance criteria","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What makes acceptance criteria useful to a Software Engineering Brain?","answer":"Acceptance criteria translate a requirement into observable conditions that determine whether the requested behavior is satisfied.","explanation":"Acceptance criteria provide a bridge between requirements and executable or manually verifiable tests. They should define relevant inputs, conditions, expected outcomes, and important boundaries.","principle":"A requirement should be connected to explicit evidence conditions before implementation is considered complete.","correct_example":"Given an expired session, when the client requests a protected resource, the server returns the documented authentication error and does not expose protected data.","invalid_example":"The feature should work correctly.","problem":"Non-specific acceptance criteria provide no reliable completion boundary.","failure_mode":"Implementation is declared complete despite missing edge cases.","cause":"Acceptance conditions were never formalized.","solution":"Express acceptance criteria as observable scenarios with explicit expected outcomes.","testing_guidance":"Convert criteria into positive, negative, boundary, and regression tests as appropriate.","verification_guidance":"Verify each criterion independently and record evidence.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B11-0001"],"prerequisites":["Requirements analysis"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B12-0002","D13-SE-B13-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Acceptance testing practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B12-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Specification","concept":"Requirement decomposition","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should a complex requirement be decomposed?","answer":"A complex requirement should be decomposed into smaller, independently understandable and verifiable requirements while preserving the parent-child relationship.","explanation":"Decomposition reduces ambiguity and allows separate implementation and verification. The decomposition must not silently change the meaning of the parent requirement.","principle":"Decomposition should preserve semantic coverage of the parent requirement.","correct_example":"A secure file-upload requirement is decomposed into authentication, authorization, file-type validation, size limits, storage isolation, scanning, and audit requirements.","invalid_example":"Splitting a requirement into unrelated tasks without preserving the original behavioral objective.","problem":"Poor decomposition can lose constraints or create conflicting subrequirements.","failure_mode":"Subrequirements individually pass while the parent requirement remains unsatisfied.","cause":"No semantic coverage check between parent and children.","solution":"Maintain explicit parent-child relationships and perform parent-level acceptance verification.","testing_guidance":"Test both individual subrequirements and the integrated parent behavior.","verification_guidance":"Check that the union of child requirements covers the parent without introducing contradictions.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B12-0001"],"prerequisites":["Requirement decomposition"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B11-0004","D13-SE-B12-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_pattern","source":"Requirements decomposition practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B12-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Specification","concept":"Requirement dependency graph","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should dependencies between requirements be represented explicitly?","answer":"Explicit requirement dependencies allow impact analysis, implementation ordering, conflict detection, and verification planning.","explanation":"One requirement may constrain another. For example, authorization requirements may depend on identity establishment, while audit requirements may depend on successful identification of the acting principal.","principle":"Dependency relationships should be represented rather than inferred repeatedly from prose.","correct_example":"REQ-A requires authenticated identity before REQ-B can evaluate authorization.","invalid_example":"Both requirements are stored independently with no relationship despite one requiring the other.","problem":"Hidden dependencies cause incomplete implementations and incorrect change impact analysis.","failure_mode":"A dependent requirement is implemented without its prerequisite.","cause":"Relationship information was not modeled explicitly.","solution":"Represent dependency edges with stable identifiers and dependency semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test prerequisite and dependent requirements both independently and together.","verification_guidance":"Detect unresolved dependency nodes and verify dependency ordering.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B11-0004"],"prerequisites":["Requirement traceability"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B12-0002","D13-SE-B14-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_pattern","source":"Dependency analysis practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B13-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Requirements Validation","concept":"Requirement consistency","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can a Software Engineering Brain detect inconsistent requirements?","answer":"It can compare requirements for incompatible constraints, mutually exclusive behaviors, conflicting assumptions, and incompatible states or interfaces.","explanation":"Consistency analysis should consider scope, actors, conditions, versions, priority, and exceptions. Apparent conflicts may disappear when their contexts differ, so the Brain should preserve contextual evidence before declaring contradiction.","principle":"A contradiction must be evaluated in context rather than inferred from similar terminology alone.","correct_example":"One requirement explicitly permits anonymous access while another forbids access unless authentication succeeds for the same endpoint and condition.","invalid_example":"Two requirements use different words for the same concept but describe compatible behavior.","problem":"False contradiction detection can cause unnecessary requirement changes.","failure_mode":"Compatible requirements are incorrectly marked as contradictory.","cause":"Semantic matching ignores scope or conditions.","solution":"Compare preconditions, scope, actors, state, exceptions, and version before classifying a contradiction.","testing_guidance":"Use contradiction cases with both genuine and apparent conflicts.","verification_guidance":"Record evidence for each contradiction and preserve unresolved cases as CONFLICTED when necessary.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B11-0003"],"prerequisites":["Requirement analysis"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B13-0002","D13-SE-B19-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"reasoning_principle","source":"Requirements consistency analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B13-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Requirements Validation","concept":"Requirement completeness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should requirement completeness be evaluated?","answer":"Completeness should be evaluated against known actors, workflows, states, inputs, outputs, error conditions, constraints, dependencies, and acceptance evidence within the defined scope.","explanation":"Absolute completeness cannot generally be proven from an isolated requirement set because undiscovered needs may exist. The Brain should therefore distinguish verified coverage from unknown or unvalidated areas.","principle":"Completeness claims must be scoped to known evidence and defined system boundaries.","correct_example":"All identified business workflows have mapped requirements and acceptance criteria, while unconfirmed external workflows are explicitly marked unknown.","invalid_example":"The requirement set is declared complete simply because every document section contains text.","problem":"Structural completeness is confused with semantic completeness.","failure_mode":"Important scenarios remain unspecified.","cause":"No systematic coverage model exists.","solution":"Build a coverage matrix across actors, workflows, states, failures, constraints, and interfaces.","testing_guidance":"Generate tests for uncovered branches and requirement categories.","verification_guidance":"Report COVERED, PARTIALLY_COVERED, MISSING, NOT_APPLICABLE, or UNKNOWN rather than asserting unsupported completeness.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B11-0004"],"prerequisites":["Requirements validation"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B12-0002","D13-SE-B20-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Requirements validation practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B13-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Requirements Validation","concept":"Requirements volatility","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is requirements volatility?","answer":"Requirements volatility is the frequency and magnitude with which requirements change during the software lifecycle.","explanation":"Changes are not inherently defects. The engineering challenge is preserving traceability, understanding impact, updating dependent artifacts, and verifying the changed behavior.","principle":"A requirement change should trigger controlled impact analysis rather than silent artifact mutation.","correct_example":"Changing an authentication timeout triggers analysis of implementation, configuration, tests, documentation, monitoring, and security assumptions.","invalid_example":"Changing a requirement in a specification file without identifying affected code or tests.","problem":"Downstream artifacts become inconsistent with the current requirement.","failure_mode":"Old behavior remains implemented or verified after the requirement changes.","cause":"No traceability or change-impact mechanism.","solution":"Use stable requirement IDs and propagate changes through dependency relationships.","testing_guidance":"Run affected regression tests and add tests for newly introduced behavior.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that changed requirements have updated implementation and evidence.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B11-0004"],"prerequisites":["Requirement traceability"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B14-0002","D13-SE-B20-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Change-management practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B14-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Engineering Planning","concept":"Dependency-aware implementation ordering","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should implementation tasks be ordered when dependencies exist?","answer":"Prerequisite components should generally be established and verified before dependent components, unless the architecture deliberately supports parallel or contract-first development.","explanation":"Dependency-aware ordering reduces avoidable integration failures. The correct order depends on architectural contracts, team boundaries, and whether stubs or mocks can safely isolate dependencies.","principle":"Implementation order should reflect dependency constraints while exploiting safe parallelism.","correct_example":"A service contract is defined and validated before dependent components integrate against it.","invalid_example":"A dependent component is implemented against undocumented assumptions about a future dependency.","problem":"Hidden dependency assumptions create integration failures.","failure_mode":"Components pass isolated tests but fail when connected.","cause":"Implementation proceeded without an explicit dependency contract.","solution":"Represent dependencies and establish contracts, mocks, or verified prerequisites as appropriate.","testing_guidance":"Use contract tests and integration tests at dependency boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Check that every dependency has an established interface and validation evidence.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B12-0003"],"prerequisites":["Dependency analysis"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B14-0002","D13-SE-B15-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Software integration practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B14-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Change Management","concept":"Change impact analysis","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should a change impact analysis identify?","answer":"It should identify requirements, interfaces, components, data models, configurations, tests, documentation, security controls, operational dependencies, and other artifacts potentially affected by the change.","explanation":"Impact analysis follows explicit and inferred dependency relationships. It should distinguish confirmed impacts from potential impacts requiring investigation.","principle":"Change scope should be evidence-driven rather than limited to the file that was directly edited.","correct_example":"Changing an API field type triggers analysis of serializers, clients, database mappings, tests, contracts, and documentation.","invalid_example":"Only the API implementation file is reviewed because it is the directly modified artifact.","problem":"Indirect dependencies remain inconsistent.","failure_mode":"Downstream consumers fail after deployment.","cause":"Impact analysis stopped at the changed artifact.","solution":"Traverse the dependency and traceability graph and verify affected boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Run impacted unit, integration, contract, migration, and regression tests.","verification_guidance":"Record affected and unaffected artifacts with evidence for the classification.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B11-0004","D13-SE-B12-0003"],"prerequisites":["Dependency analysis","Traceability"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B13-0003","D13-SE-B20-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Software change-impact analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B14-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Engineering Planning","concept":"Risk-based prioritization","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should a Software Engineering Brain prioritize engineering risks?","answer":"It should consider potential impact, likelihood or uncertainty, exposure, detectability, dependencies, reversibility, and available evidence rather than relying on a single generic priority score.","explanation":"Risk prioritization is context-dependent. A high-impact but uncertain risk may require investigation before implementation, while a well-understood low-impact issue may be handled through normal engineering workflow.","principle":"Risk decisions should expose their assumptions and evidence.","correct_example":"A change affecting authentication boundaries receives explicit security review because its potential impact is high even when its probability is uncertain.","invalid_example":"All issues are ordered solely by estimated coding effort.","problem":"Effort-only prioritization can ignore system risk.","failure_mode":"Low-effort changes consume attention while high-impact risks remain unresolved.","cause":"Priority model excludes impact and uncertainty.","solution":"Use multidimensional risk reasoning and preserve the underlying evidence.","testing_guidance":"Prioritize verification depth according to identified risk.","verification_guidance":"Check that high-impact assumptions have explicit evidence or are marked unresolved.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Basic risk analysis"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B15-0002","D13-SE-B18-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Risk-based engineering practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B15-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Design Reasoning","concept":"Separation of concerns","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does separation of concerns mean in software engineering?","answer":"Separation of concerns means organizing a system so that distinct responsibilities can be understood, changed, tested, and governed with limited unnecessary coupling.","explanation":"The goal is not to maximize the number of modules. Boundaries should reflect meaningful responsibilities and dependency relationships.","principle":"A useful boundary isolates change-relevant responsibility without obscuring necessary interactions.","correct_example":"Authentication policy is separated from business-domain calculation while a defined interface connects them.","invalid_example":"Creating dozens of wrappers around a single operation solely to increase file count.","problem":"Artificial decomposition can increase complexity instead of reducing it.","failure_mode":"Engineers must traverse many layers to understand simple behavior.","cause":"Separation was optimized for structural fragmentation rather than responsibility.","solution":"Evaluate cohesion, coupling, change patterns, testability, and comprehension when defining boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Test each responsibility independently and verify integration contracts.","verification_guidance":"Check that boundaries correspond to meaningful responsibilities and do not hide required invariants.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Basic software architecture"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B15-0002","D13-SE-B16-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Software design principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B15-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Design Reasoning","concept":"Cohesion and coupling","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should cohesion and coupling influence component design?","answer":"High internal cohesion and appropriately limited coupling generally improve maintainability, but coupling should be evaluated according to the required architectural relationships rather than minimized indiscriminately.","explanation":"Some coupling is necessary. The objective is to make dependencies explicit, stable, and understandable while keeping unrelated responsibilities from becoming entangled.","principle":"Prefer meaningful cohesion and explicit, stable dependencies over either extreme isolation or uncontrolled coupling.","correct_example":"A persistence component owns persistence concerns and exposes a stable interface used by domain logic.","invalid_example":"A domain component directly manipulates unrelated UI state, database internals, and network transport details.","problem":"Mixed responsibilities increase change propagation.","failure_mode":"A change in one concern unexpectedly breaks unrelated behavior.","cause":"Low cohesion and broad implicit coupling.","solution":"Refine responsibility boundaries and make necessary dependencies explicit.","testing_guidance":"Use component tests and contract tests around stable boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Analyze dependency graphs for unnecessary cycles and responsibility leakage.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B15-0001"],"prerequisites":["Component design"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B15-0003","D13-SE-B16-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Software design principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B15-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Design Reasoning","concept":"Dependency direction","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why does dependency direction matter?","answer":"Dependency direction determines which components know about and rely upon which other components, strongly affecting change propagation, testability, and architectural boundaries.","explanation":"A dependency graph should reflect intentional architecture rather than accidental import or call relationships. Dependency direction is especially important when separating domain logic from infrastructure concerns.","principle":"Dependencies should point toward stable and intentionally owned abstractions where the architecture requires such a boundary.","correct_example":"Domain logic depends on an abstract persistence contract while infrastructure supplies its implementation.","invalid_example":"Core domain rules directly depend on a concrete database driver solely because it was convenient during initial development.","problem":"Infrastructure changes propagate into core logic.","failure_mode":"Changing infrastructure requires widespread domain modifications.","cause":"Dependency direction follows implementation convenience instead of architectural ownership.","solution":"Introduce an appropriate abstraction boundary when the architecture genuinely requires independence.","testing_guidance":"Test domain logic without requiring the concrete infrastructure implementation when the boundary is intended to isolate it.","verification_guidance":"Inspect dependency graphs and verify that prohibited dependency directions are absent.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B15-0002"],"prerequisites":["Architecture fundamentals"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B16-0001","D13-SE-B17-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Dependency architecture practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B16-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Interfaces","concept":"Contract boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a software contract boundary?","answer":"A contract boundary defines the expected inputs, outputs, errors, state assumptions, side effects, and compatibility expectations between components.","explanation":"Contracts reduce ambiguity between independently developed components. They may be represented through types, schemas, interface definitions, documentation, tests, or combinations of these mechanisms.","principle":"A component boundary should make externally relevant behavior explicit.","correct_example":"An API contract defines request schema, response schema, authentication requirement, error semantics, and compatibility rules.","invalid_example":"A consumer depends on undocumented fields and behavior discovered through trial and error.","problem":"Undocumented assumptions become hidden coupling.","failure_mode":"Consumers break when internal implementation details change.","cause":"The effective contract was never explicitly defined.","solution":"Document and verify the boundary behavior that consumers are allowed to depend upon.","testing_guidance":"Use contract tests to verify producer and consumer expectations.","verification_guidance":"Compare implementation behavior with the declared contract and record deviations.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B15-0003"],"prerequisites":["Component interfaces"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B16-0002","D13-SE-B17-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_pattern","source":"Interface and contract testing practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B16-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Interfaces","concept":"Contract violation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happens when a component violates its contract?","answer":"A contract violation occurs when an implementation fails to provide behavior that consumers are entitled to rely upon or provides behavior outside the declared contract in a way that breaks its assumptions.","explanation":"Violations may involve data shape, semantics, errors, timing, side effects, ordering, authorization, or compatibility. Detecting the specific violated contract is more useful than treating the resulting failure as a generic error.","principle":"Boundary failures should be diagnosed against the contract before modifying downstream consumers.","correct_example":"A service declares that a field is always an integer but begins returning null without a versioned contract change.","invalid_example":"A consumer changes its parser to accept undocumented behavior without determining whether the producer contract permits it.","problem":"Consumers may silently adapt to broken behavior and propagate inconsistency.","failure_mode":"Different consumers implement incompatible workarounds.","cause":"Contract ownership and compatibility rules are unclear.","solution":"Identify the authoritative contract and coordinate producer and consumer changes through explicit compatibility handling.","testing_guidance":"Add contract tests covering valid, invalid, boundary, and compatibility cases.","verification_guidance":"Verify both producer conformance and consumer assumptions.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B16-0001"],"prerequisites":["Contract boundaries"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B17-0001","D13-SE-B18-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Contract testing practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B17-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Integration Engineering","concept":"Integration boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why are integration boundaries important to software engineering reasoning?","answer":"Integration boundaries define where independently developed behavior, data, assumptions, or operational environments meet and therefore where compatibility failures can emerge.","explanation":"The Software Engineering Brain should treat boundaries as first-class reasoning points because failures often arise from mismatched assumptions rather than isolated component defects.","principle":"Analyze interfaces and boundary assumptions as explicitly as internal implementation logic.","correct_example":"An API integration is analyzed for schema, authentication, timeout, retry, idempotency, and error semantics.","invalid_example":"Integration is considered complete because both components pass their isolated unit tests.","problem":"Boundary behavior remains unverified.","failure_mode":"Components fail only after integration.","cause":"Tests did not model cross-component assumptions.","solution":"Define boundary contracts and test integration-specific behavior.","testing_guidance":"Use integration and contract tests with failure-path coverage.","verification_guidance":"Verify compatibility of both sides under expected and adverse boundary conditions.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B16-0001"],"prerequisites":["Integration fundamentals"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B17-0002","D13-SE-B18-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Integration engineering practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B17-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Integration Engineering","concept":"Hidden coupling","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is hidden coupling?","answer":"Hidden coupling exists when a component depends on another component's behavior, state, timing, configuration, or implementation details without that dependency being explicit in the architecture or contract.","explanation":"Hidden coupling makes impact analysis and independent testing unreliable because the true dependency graph differs from the documented one.","principle":"Dependencies that materially affect correctness should be explicit and observable.","correct_example":"A service silently relies on a database transaction being opened by an unrelated caller.","invalid_example":"A documented function explicitly accepts the transaction boundary it requires.","problem":"Hidden dependencies produce surprising failures during reuse or refactoring.","failure_mode":"A component works only within one accidental execution context.","cause":"Implicit environmental or state assumptions.","solution":"Expose required dependencies through explicit interfaces, parameters, or documented architectural contracts.","testing_guidance":"Run components in isolated and altered environments to detect implicit assumptions.","verification_guidance":"Compare observed dependencies with the declared dependency graph.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B15-0003"],"prerequisites":["Dependency analysis"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B16-0002","D13-SE-B18-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","source":"Software architecture failure analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B17-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Integration Engineering","concept":"Integration sequencing","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should integration be sequenced for a multi-component system?","answer":"Integration should proceed through explicit contracts and progressively broader verification, moving from isolated component validation to boundary validation and then system-level behavior.","explanation":"The exact sequence depends on architecture. Contract-first, incremental integration, or other strategies can be valid when their assumptions are explicit.","principle":"Integration should expose compatibility failures early and preserve evidence about their source.","correct_example":"A service validates its API contract before a dependent client is integrated, followed by end-to-end scenarios.","invalid_example":"All components are integrated simultaneously with no intermediate boundary verification.","problem":"A large integration failure has many possible causes.","failure_mode":"Debugging becomes combinatorially difficult.","cause":"Too many unverified boundaries were introduced at once.","solution":"Use incremental integration and boundary-level verification where practical.","testing_guidance":"Run component, contract, integration, and system tests in progressively broader scopes.","verification_guidance":"Record which boundary introduced each observed failure.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B17-0001"],"prerequisites":["Integration engineering"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B14-0001","D13-SE-B20-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_pattern","source":"Incremental integration practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B18-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Failure Analysis","concept":"Root cause versus symptom","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why must a Software Engineering Brain distinguish symptoms from root causes?","answer":"A symptom is an observed manifestation of failure, while a root cause is an underlying condition whose correction addresses the causal mechanism rather than merely suppressing the visible effect.","explanation":"One root cause can produce multiple symptoms, and one symptom can have multiple possible causes. Diagnosis therefore requires evidence rather than selecting the first plausible explanation.","principle":"Do not treat an observed failure as its cause without causal evidence.","correct_example":"A timeout is treated as a symptom while dependency saturation, deadlock, network loss, or incorrect timeout configuration are investigated as possible causes.","invalid_example":"Increasing the timeout immediately because the observed error says timeout.","problem":"Symptom suppression can conceal the actual defect.","failure_mode":"The same failure returns under different conditions.","cause":"Diagnosis stopped at the first observable manifestation.","solution":"Construct and test competing causal hypotheses using logs, traces, state, reproduction, and controlled experiments when available.","testing_guidance":"Create tests that distinguish candidate causes rather than only reproducing the symptom.","verification_guidance":"Record evidence supporting the identified cause and distinguish confirmed causes from hypotheses.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Debugging fundamentals"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B18-0002","D13-SE-B19-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"debugging_principle","source":"Failure analysis practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B18-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Failure Analysis","concept":"Fault error failure distinction","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is the distinction between fault, error, and failure?","answer":"A fault is a defect or incorrect condition in a system artifact; an error is an incorrect internal state or computation resulting from a fault or other cause; a failure is externally observable behavior that does not satisfy the required behavior.","explanation":"The terms provide a causal chain useful for debugging and verification. Exact terminology can vary across engineering standards, so the Brain should preserve the terminology source when precision matters.","principle":"Diagnosis should distinguish internal defect mechanisms from externally observed behavior.","correct_example":"An incorrect validation condition is a fault; an invalid internal state resulting from it is an error; returning unauthorized data is a failure.","invalid_example":"Using failure, error, and fault as interchangeable terms in a causal analysis.","problem":"Causal reasoning becomes imprecise.","failure_mode":"A visible failure is fixed without addressing the defect that produced it.","cause":"No explicit causal model.","solution":"Represent fault, internal error state, propagation, and external failure separately.","testing_guidance":"Test both the triggering condition and the externally visible failure.","verification_guidance":"Trace observed failures backward to supported causal evidence.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B18-0001"],"prerequisites":["Failure analysis"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B18-0003","D13-SE-B19-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_reliability_concept","source":"Software failure terminology","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCEPT"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B18-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Failure Analysis","concept":"Reproducibility","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why is reproducibility valuable during debugging?","answer":"Reproducibility allows engineers to repeatedly observe a failure under controlled conditions and evaluate whether a proposed correction changes the relevant behavior.","explanation":"Not every production failure is immediately reproducible. When reproduction is unavailable, evidence should be preserved and uncertainty explicitly recorded rather than fabricated into a deterministic explanation.","principle":"A debugging conclusion should reflect the strength and limitations of the available evidence.","correct_example":"A failure is reproduced with a minimal input and controlled environment before the fix is verified.","invalid_example":"A developer claims the fix is verified solely because the failure did not recur once during manual testing.","problem":"One-off observations provide weak evidence for causal claims.","failure_mode":"A non-deterministic defect is declared fixed prematurely.","cause":"Insufficient repetition or environmental control.","solution":"Build deterministic reproduction where possible and supplement unavailable reproduction with logs, traces, state snapshots, and targeted tests.","testing_guidance":"Repeat the triggering scenario and include regression coverage after correction.","verification_guidance":"Distinguish reproduced, not reproduced, and unable-to-reproduce states.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B18-0001"],"prerequisites":["Debugging"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B19-0001","D13-SE-B20-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"debugging_principle","source":"Reproducible debugging practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B19-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Debugging Reasoning","concept":"Hypothesis-driven debugging","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a Software Engineering Brain approach an unknown defect?","answer":"It should collect evidence, generate competing hypotheses, identify predictions that distinguish them, perform controlled checks, update confidence, and avoid treating an unverified hypothesis as fact.","explanation":"This converts debugging from random modification into evidence-based reasoning. Hypotheses may be ranked internally for investigation priority, but their evidentiary status must remain explicit.","principle":"A debugging hypothesis is not a confirmed cause until supporting evidence is obtained.","correct_example":"A suspected race condition leads to targeted concurrency instrumentation and a reproducible test designed to distinguish it from a serialization defect.","invalid_example":"Changing synchronization code immediately because a race condition seems likely.","problem":"Premature fixes can mask the actual cause.","failure_mode":"The original defect remains or a second defect is introduced.","cause":"Hypothesis was treated as established fact.","solution":"Record hypothesis, evidence, prediction, experiment or test, result, and confidence separately.","testing_guidance":"Design discriminating tests that distinguish plausible causes.","verification_guidance":"Update the hypothesis status only from observed evidence.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B18-0001","D13-SE-B18-0003"],"prerequisites":["Debugging and reasoning"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B19-0002","D13-SE-B20-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"debugging_method","source":"Scientific debugging and hypothesis testing practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_METHOD"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B19-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Debugging Reasoning","concept":"Contradictory evidence","knowledge_type":"CONTRADICTION","question":"How should contradictory debugging evidence be handled?","answer":"Contradictory evidence should be preserved, contextualized, and investigated by checking environment, version, timing, measurement method, scope, and assumptions before selecting an explanation.","explanation":"Two observations can both be valid under different environments. The Brain should not erase conflicting evidence merely to maintain a single narrative.","principle":"Contradiction is evidence about uncertainty or context and should not be silently discarded.","correct_example":"A defect occurs in one runtime version but not another; the records retain both observations and associate them with their versions.","invalid_example":"Deleting the observation that conflicts with the preferred explanation.","problem":"Loss of contradictory evidence causes false certainty.","failure_mode":"A diagnosis appears proven because disconfirming observations were discarded.","cause":"Evidence management favors narrative consistency over factual preservation.","solution":"Store each observation with context and classify the relationship as compatible, version-specific, unresolved, or contradicted.","testing_guidance":"Reproduce both conditions when practical.","verification_guidance":"Check whether environmental or version differences explain the contradiction before resolving it.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B13-0001","D13-SE-B19-0001"],"prerequisites":["Evidence-based debugging"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B20-0001","D13-SE-B20-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"evidence_reasoning_principle","source":"Evidence and contradiction analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B20-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Engineering Verification","concept":"Evidence-based engineering state","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should the Software Engineering Brain record about the state of an engineering claim?","answer":"It should distinguish at minimum between generated reasoning, implemented behavior, executed checks, test results, and verified requirements rather than collapsing them into a single completion state.","explanation":"A generated code patch is not evidence that the code works. A passing unit test is not necessarily proof of complete system behavior. Verification status must preserve the strength and scope of evidence.","principle":"Engineering claims must be accompanied by evidence appropriate to the claim.","correct_example":"A change is recorded as IMPLEMENTED but NOT_EXECUTED until the relevant test actually runs.","invalid_example":"Marking a generated implementation VERIFIED without execution or evidence.","problem":"False completion claims contaminate downstream reasoning.","failure_mode":"Later agents assume unverified behavior is reliable.","cause":"Implementation status and verification status are conflated.","solution":"Use separate lifecycle states such as GENERATED, IMPLEMENTED, EXECUTED, TESTED, PASSED, and VERIFIED.","testing_guidance":"Attach actual test evidence to claims that depend on execution.","verification_guidance":"Reject verification transitions that lack required evidence.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B18-0003","D13-SE-B19-0001"],"prerequisites":["Verification fundamentals"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B20-0002","D13-SE-B20-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"verification_principle","source":"Evidence-based software engineering","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B20-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Engineering Verification","concept":"Verification evidence scope","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must verification evidence include scope?","answer":"Evidence must identify what was tested or inspected, under which conditions, against which requirement or claim, and what limitations remain.","explanation":"A successful test proves only what its test conditions and assertions cover. Scope prevents a narrow result from being generalized to unrelated behavior.","principle":"Evidence strength is bounded by the claim, method, environment, and coverage actually observed.","correct_example":"A passing integration test is linked to the specific API contract scenario it exercised and does not automatically verify unrelated failure paths.","invalid_example":"A single successful smoke test is cited as proof that the entire system is correct.","problem":"Evidence is generalized beyond its demonstrated scope.","failure_mode":"Unsupported confidence propagates into release or design decisions.","cause":"No explicit evidence-to-claim mapping.","solution":"Store claim, evidence method, scope, observed result, environment, and limitations.","testing_guidance":"Map each important requirement to appropriate positive, negative, and boundary evidence.","verification_guidance":"Reject claims whose required evidence exceeds the observed scope.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B20-0001"],"prerequisites":["Verification evidence"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B11-0004","D13-SE-B20-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"verification_principle","source":"Evidence and verification practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-SE-B20-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Software Engineering Foundations","subtopic":"Engineering Verification","concept":"Change-to-verification traceability","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should an engineering system verify that a change was adequately tested?","answer":"The system should connect the change to affected requirements and dependencies, identify expected behavioral impacts, map relevant tests or inspections, and record their actual evidence and limitations.","explanation":"This creates a closed reasoning loop from change to expected effect to verification evidence. Missing links should be surfaced rather than assumed to be safe.","principle":"Every material change should have an evidence-based verification path proportional to its impact and risk.","correct_example":"A changed authorization rule is linked to affected requirements, implementation, positive and negative tests, and executed verification evidence.","invalid_example":"A change is merged because the modified file has no syntax errors.","problem":"Structural validity is mistaken for behavioral correctness.","failure_mode":"Regression reaches integration or production despite passing superficial checks.","cause":"Verification scope does not reflect change impact.","solution":"Use change-impact analysis to generate and verify an appropriate evidence set.","testing_guidance":"Include targeted regression, boundary, negative, and integration tests according to the affected behavior.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that the evidence covers the changed claim and record any unverified impact.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-SE-B14-0002","D13-SE-B20-0001"],"prerequisites":["Change impact analysis","Verification"],"related_records":["D13-SE-B11-0004","D13-SE-B20-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_pattern","source":"Change verification and traceability practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

