{"record_id":"D13-DP-B21-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Abstraction","concept":"Abstraction boundary","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an abstraction boundary in software design?","answer":"An abstraction boundary separates what a component guarantees from how that behavior is implemented.","explanation":"A useful abstraction exposes stable behavior while hiding implementation details that consumers do not need to depend upon.","principle":"Expose required semantics and hide unnecessary implementation details.","correct_example":"A storage interface exposes save and load semantics while allowing different storage implementations.","invalid_example":"An interface exposes database-specific transaction internals that every consumer must understand.","problem":"Leaky abstractions spread implementation details across consumers.","failure_mode":"Internal implementation changes require widespread consumer changes.","cause":"The abstraction boundary does not isolate the intended variation.","solution":"Redesign the boundary around stable consumer-visible behavior.","alternative":"Use a concrete component when no meaningful variation or isolation is required.","tradeoff":"Additional abstraction can increase indirection and complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test the abstraction contract independently from implementation-specific tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that consumers depend only on behavior explicitly promised by the abstraction.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Interfaces","Separation of concerns"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B21-0002","D13-DP-B22-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_principle","source":"General object-oriented design practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B21-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Abstraction","concept":"Leaky abstraction","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is a leaky abstraction?","answer":"A leaky abstraction is an abstraction whose consumers must understand or depend upon implementation details that the abstraction was intended to hide.","explanation":"Not every exposed detail is automatically a leak. The relevant question is whether consumers become coupled to details that should be independently changeable.","principle":"An abstraction should isolate the variations it was introduced to contain.","correct_example":"A repository interface requires callers to construct database-specific query objects.","invalid_example":"An interface exposes a domain-level query operation appropriate to all supported implementations.","problem":"Implementation-specific knowledge propagates across the system.","failure_mode":"Changing one implementation requires changes in unrelated consumers.","cause":"The abstraction boundary does not match the true variation point.","solution":"Identify stable semantics and move implementation-specific details behind the boundary.","alternative":"Remove the abstraction if the implementation is intentionally part of the public contract.","tradeoff":"Fixing a leaky abstraction may require migration effort and temporary compatibility layers.","testing_guidance":"Test consumers using only contract-level behavior and separately test implementation details.","verification_guidance":"Inspect consumer dependencies for references to supposedly hidden implementation details.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B21-0001"],"prerequisites":["Abstraction"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B22-0002","D13-DP-B24-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_failure_mode","source":"Abstraction design practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCEPT"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B21-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Abstraction","concept":"Premature abstraction","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can premature abstraction be harmful?","answer":"Premature abstraction can encode assumptions before the actual variation points are understood, creating unnecessary indirection and making later design changes harder.","explanation":"Abstraction is useful when there is a meaningful boundary or anticipated variation supported by evidence. Creating abstractions solely because future change is imaginable can increase complexity without providing current value.","principle":"Introduce abstraction when its boundary has a clear responsibility, variation point, or architectural purpose.","correct_example":"An interface is introduced after multiple implementations require a stable common contract.","invalid_example":"Creating ten interchangeable interfaces for components that currently have one behavior and no identified variation requirement.","problem":"Unnecessary abstractions increase cognitive and maintenance cost.","failure_mode":"Simple changes require navigation through meaningless layers.","cause":"Future flexibility was prioritized without evidence of useful variation.","solution":"Use the simplest design that preserves required boundaries and refactor when genuine variation emerges.","alternative":"Delay abstraction until a concrete second implementation or architectural boundary is established.","tradeoff":"Delayed abstraction can make later refactoring more expensive, while premature abstraction creates current complexity.","testing_guidance":"Verify behavior at the actual boundary rather than testing artificial abstraction layers.","verification_guidance":"Check whether each abstraction has an explicit responsibility or variation it isolates.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B21-0001"],"prerequisites":["Abstraction"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B23-0001","D13-DP-B29-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_principle","source":"Refactoring and abstraction practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B22-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Polymorphism","concept":"Programming to an abstraction","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does programming to an abstraction mean?","answer":"Programming to an abstraction means that client logic depends on a stable contract rather than unnecessary details of a particular implementation.","explanation":"The abstraction can be an interface, protocol, abstract type, or another explicit contract. The principle is about dependency semantics rather than a specific language feature.","principle":"Clients should depend on the behavior they require rather than incidental implementation details.","correct_example":"A notification service accepts a notifier contract rather than directly constructing one specific transport.","invalid_example":"Every business operation directly instantiates a concrete SMTP implementation.","problem":"Client logic becomes coupled to one implementation.","failure_mode":"Changing transport requires modifications throughout business logic.","cause":"Concrete dependency is embedded in high-level behavior.","solution":"Introduce an appropriate contract and inject or otherwise supply the implementation.","alternative":"Direct concrete dependency when the implementation is intentionally fixed and stable.","tradeoff":"Abstraction increases flexibility but also adds a boundary that must be maintained.","testing_guidance":"Test clients against contract-compatible implementations or test doubles where appropriate.","verification_guidance":"Inspect dependency direction and confirm consumers do not require forbidden implementation details.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B21-0001"],"prerequisites":["Abstraction","Dependency direction"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B22-0002","D13-DP-B25-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_principle","source":"Object-oriented design practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B22-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Polymorphism","concept":"Polymorphic variation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is polymorphism useful in design pattern reasoning?","answer":"Polymorphism is useful when multiple implementations can satisfy a common behavioral contract and client behavior should remain independent of the selected implementation.","explanation":"Polymorphism can be implemented through language-supported subtype polymorphism, protocols, interfaces, function objects, or other mechanisms. The pattern decision should depend on the required variation rather than terminology.","principle":"Use polymorphism when behavior varies behind a meaningful stable contract.","correct_example":"Multiple pricing strategies implement the same pricing operation while order processing remains unchanged.","invalid_example":"Creating separate polymorphic classes for behavior that can never vary and has no boundary value.","problem":"Unnecessary polymorphism adds indirection.","failure_mode":"The design becomes harder to understand without meaningful flexibility.","cause":"Polymorphism is introduced mechanically rather than to isolate variation.","solution":"Confirm the actual variation point and choose the simplest mechanism that supports it.","alternative":"A direct function or conditional may be sufficient for small stable variations.","tradeoff":"Polymorphism can improve extensibility but may increase navigation and runtime dispatch complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test the common contract and representative implementations, including invalid behavior where relevant.","verification_guidance":"Verify that implementations satisfy the same behavioral contract rather than merely sharing a method signature.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B22-0001"],"prerequisites":["Interfaces","Polymorphism"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B23-0002","D13-DP-B27-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_pattern","source":"Object-oriented design practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B22-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Polymorphism","concept":"Behavioral substitutability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why is matching an interface insufficient for substitutability?","answer":"A type can match the syntactic shape of an interface while violating the behavioral expectations encoded by the contract.","explanation":"Consumers rely on semantics such as valid input ranges, output guarantees, side effects, error behavior, ordering, and state transitions. Pattern-based design must preserve those expectations.","principle":"Substitutability requires behavioral compatibility, not merely structural compatibility.","correct_example":"Every implementation of a payment authorization contract preserves documented success, rejection, and error semantics.","invalid_example":"An implementation returns a success value while silently skipping a required authorization check.","problem":"Syntactically compatible implementations may produce semantic failures.","failure_mode":"A replacement implementation breaks consumers despite passing type checks.","cause":"Contract verification checks signatures but not behavior.","solution":"Specify and test behavioral invariants of the abstraction.","testing_guidance":"Use contract, property, negative, and compatibility tests where appropriate.","verification_guidance":"Verify implementation behavior against the complete contract, not just its method signatures.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B22-0002"],"prerequisites":["Contracts","Polymorphism"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B26-0002","D13-DP-B28-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_principle","source":"Behavioral substitutability principle","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B23-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Composition","concept":"Composition over inheritance","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does composition over inheritance mean?","answer":"Composition over inheritance means assembling behavior through contained collaborators rather than relying on subclassing as the primary mechanism for reuse or variation.","explanation":"Composition can reduce rigid class hierarchies and allow behavior to be changed through supplied collaborators. Inheritance remains appropriate when a genuine subtype relationship and substitutability exist.","principle":"Prefer composition when behavior varies independently of type identity or when inheritance would create unnecessary coupling.","correct_example":"An order object receives a pricing strategy instead of creating a subclass for every pricing rule.","invalid_example":"Creating a deep inheritance hierarchy solely to reuse small pieces of implementation code.","problem":"Inheritance can couple unrelated variation dimensions and make change propagation difficult.","failure_mode":"A change in a base class affects many subclasses unexpectedly.","cause":"Implementation reuse was treated as sufficient justification for inheritance.","solution":"Evaluate whether the relationship is a genuine subtype relationship; otherwise consider composition.","alternative":"Inheritance when subtype semantics, invariants, and substitutability are clear.","tradeoff":"Composition can increase object wiring and indirection while reducing hierarchy coupling.","testing_guidance":"Test collaborators independently and the composed behavior together.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each composition boundary has a clear responsibility and lifecycle.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B22-0003"],"prerequisites":["Polymorphism","Composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B23-0002","D13-DP-B25-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_principle","source":"Object-oriented design practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B23-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Composition","concept":"Composition boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should be considered before introducing a collaborator into a composed design?","answer":"The design should establish the collaborator's responsibility, contract, lifecycle, ownership, failure behavior, and dependency direction.","explanation":"Composition is not automatically simpler. Poorly defined collaborators can merely move complexity from inheritance into object wiring.","principle":"A collaborator should represent a meaningful responsibility rather than an arbitrary fragment of implementation.","correct_example":"A retry policy object owns retry decisions and exposes a defined policy contract.","invalid_example":"A collaborator exists only to forward a single call without isolating any responsibility or variation.","problem":"Excessive composition can create unnecessary indirection.","failure_mode":"Simple behavior requires tracing through many trivial collaborators.","cause":"Composition boundaries were created without meaningful responsibilities.","solution":"Combine trivial collaborators when no independent responsibility or variation exists.","testing_guidance":"Test collaborator contracts and composed behavior at appropriate levels.","verification_guidance":"Review whether each collaborator can be explained by responsibility, variation, lifecycle, or boundary.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B23-0001"],"prerequisites":["Composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B21-0003","D13-DP-B29-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_principle","source":"Composition design practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B24-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Variation","concept":"Encapsulating variation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What does encapsulating variation mean?","answer":"Encapsulating variation means isolating a behavior or implementation detail that is expected to change from the parts of the system that should remain stable.","explanation":"This is a recurring design principle behind many patterns. The exact mechanism may be polymorphism, composition, factories, configuration, modules, or another boundary.","principle":"Separate stable behavior from independently varying behavior.","correct_example":"A report generator isolates output formatting so business calculations do not change when the output format changes.","invalid_example":"Formatting decisions are scattered throughout business logic branches.","problem":"A single variation causes changes across unrelated code.","failure_mode":"New variants require repeated modifications to stable behavior.","cause":"Variation was not isolated at a coherent boundary.","solution":"Identify the variation axis and encapsulate it behind a stable contract.","alternative":"Conditional logic may be simpler when the variation is small and stable.","tradeoff":"Encapsulation improves local changeability but introduces an abstraction boundary.","testing_guidance":"Test each variation independently and verify stable behavior remains unchanged.","verification_guidance":"Trace a variation change and confirm that unrelated stable components remain unaffected.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B21-0001","D13-DP-B22-0002"],"prerequisites":["Abstraction","Polymorphism"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B24-0002","D13-DP-B25-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_principle","source":"Object-oriented design practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B24-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Variation","concept":"Variation axis","knowledge_type":"REASONING_RULE","question":"How should the Brain identify a variation axis?","answer":"It should identify behavior or implementation decisions that change independently while surrounding behavior remains substantially stable.","explanation":"A variation axis can be discovered from repeated conditionals, recurring subclasses, configuration branches, independent requirements, or observed change history. These signals are evidence, not automatic proof that a pattern is required.","principle":"Pattern selection should follow observed or justified variation rather than pattern-first design.","correct_example":"Multiple independent delivery mechanisms change while order validation remains unchanged.","invalid_example":"A pattern is introduced because a catalog contains a pattern whose name resembles the feature.","problem":"Pattern choice is driven by terminology instead of design forces.","failure_mode":"The selected pattern does not address the actual variation.","cause":"No explicit context or variation analysis.","solution":"Identify the stable behavior, varying behavior, constraints, and change forces before selecting a pattern.","testing_guidance":"Create tests that preserve stable behavior while exercising each supported variation.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that the abstraction corresponds to an actual independent variation axis.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B24-0001"],"prerequisites":["Design reasoning"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B29-0001","D13-DP-B30-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_selection_method","source":"Design pattern reasoning practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_METHOD"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B25-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Dependency Management","concept":"Dependency injection","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does dependency injection address?","answer":"Dependency injection separates the construction or selection of a dependency from the component that uses it, allowing the dependency to be supplied through an explicit mechanism.","explanation":"Dependency injection can improve substitutability, testing, configuration, and dependency direction when used at meaningful boundaries. It is not synonymous with any particular framework.","principle":"Dependencies that need to vary or be independently controlled should be supplied through explicit boundaries.","correct_example":"A service receives a payment processor through its constructor rather than constructing a specific processor internally.","invalid_example":"A service creates a new database client internally every time it performs an operation despite requiring configurable or testable infrastructure.","problem":"Dependency construction is tightly coupled to behavior.","failure_mode":"Replacing or testing the dependency requires modifying the consumer.","cause":"Consumer owns dependency selection unnecessarily.","solution":"Move dependency selection to an appropriate composition boundary.","alternative":"Direct construction when the dependency is stable, local, and intentionally owned by the component.","tradeoff":"Injection can improve flexibility but may increase wiring complexity.","testing_guidance":"Supply controlled implementations or test doubles at the injection boundary where appropriate.","verification_guidance":"Verify that dependency ownership and lifecycle are explicit.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B22-0001","D13-DP-B23-0001"],"prerequisites":["Dependency direction"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B25-0002","D13-DP-B27-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_pattern","source":"Dependency injection design practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B25-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Dependency Management","concept":"Dependency injection overuse","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"When can dependency injection become counterproductive?","answer":"Dependency injection can become counterproductive when nearly every object is configurable without a meaningful requirement for variation, producing excessive wiring and indirection.","explanation":"The useful question is whether dependency ownership, replacement, isolation, or configuration provides concrete value. A framework container does not automatically make a design better.","principle":"Inject dependencies that require independent control; avoid abstraction solely for mechanical consistency.","correct_example":"Infrastructure services are injected because different runtime environments require different implementations.","invalid_example":"Every small value object is registered in a dependency container despite having no independent lifecycle or variation.","problem":"Configuration becomes harder to understand than direct construction.","failure_mode":"Developers cannot easily determine where objects originate.","cause":"Dependency injection is applied universally rather than selectively.","solution":"Keep simple local dependencies local and inject meaningful architectural dependencies.","alternative":"Direct construction, factory functions, or module-level composition for stable dependencies.","tradeoff":"Less injection reduces wiring but may increase coupling where future variation is actually required.","testing_guidance":"Ensure tests remain easy to construct without requiring unnecessary infrastructure.","verification_guidance":"Trace dependency ownership and confirm every injected dependency has a reason for external control.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B25-0001"],"prerequisites":["Dependency injection"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B21-0003","D13-DP-B29-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_guidance","source":"Dependency management practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B26-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"State Modeling","concept":"Explicit state representation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should state be represented explicitly in a design?","answer":"State should be represented explicitly when behavior depends materially on current state and implicit flags or scattered conditionals make valid transitions difficult to reason about.","explanation":"Explicit state can use state objects, enums, state machines, transition tables, or other representations. The appropriate mechanism depends on complexity and verification needs.","principle":"Make important state transitions explicit when they are otherwise difficult to understand or verify.","correct_example":"A workflow explicitly represents Pending, Approved, Rejected, and Completed states with defined transitions.","invalid_example":"Multiple unrelated boolean flags implicitly encode dozens of workflow states with no defined transition rules.","problem":"Implicit state combinations can create invalid or unreachable states.","failure_mode":"Unexpected behavior occurs because flags form combinations never considered by the design.","cause":"State model is distributed and undocumented.","solution":"Define explicit states, legal transitions, guards, and resulting actions.","alternative":"Simple conditional logic when there are very few states and transitions.","tradeoff":"Explicit state modeling improves reasoning but can add structural complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test every important valid transition and representative invalid transition.","verification_guidance":"Verify that transitions preserve state invariants and that forbidden transitions are rejected.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["State modeling"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B26-0002","D13-DP-B27-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_pattern","source":"State modeling practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B26-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"State Modeling","concept":"State transition invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is a state transition invariant?","answer":"A state transition invariant is a condition that must remain true before, during, or after allowed state transitions.","explanation":"Invariants provide stronger verification than checking only individual outputs. They can describe authorization, ownership, data consistency, lifecycle, or other properties that must persist across transitions.","principle":"State transitions should preserve all invariants that define valid system state.","correct_example":"An approved transaction must retain an immutable association with the authenticated actor that approved it.","invalid_example":"A transition changes the transaction owner without validating the ownership invariant.","problem":"State changes can silently violate system-wide assumptions.","failure_mode":"Later operations operate on corrupted or unauthorized state.","cause":"Transitions were tested only for local output.","solution":"Define invariants and verify them across relevant transitions.","testing_guidance":"Use transition tests, property-based tests, and negative tests where appropriate.","verification_guidance":"Record each invariant and the evidence demonstrating its preservation.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B26-0001"],"prerequisites":["State machines","Invariants"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B22-0003","D13-DP-B28-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_verification_principle","source":"State machine verification practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B27-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Behavior Selection","concept":"Strategy selection","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does the Strategy pattern address?","answer":"The Strategy pattern encapsulates interchangeable algorithms or behaviors behind a common interface so that a context can use different strategies without embedding their implementations.","explanation":"Strategy is useful when a behavior varies independently and multiple implementations are meaningful. A small stable conditional may be simpler when the variation does not justify separate objects.","principle":"Encapsulate interchangeable behavior behind a stable contract when the variation is meaningful.","correct_example":"A shipping calculator selects among different shipping policies implementing the same calculation contract.","invalid_example":"Creating a Strategy hierarchy for two branches that will never vary independently and are simpler as direct conditional logic.","problem":"Algorithm selection is scattered throughout the context.","failure_mode":"Adding a new algorithm requires modifying unrelated context logic.","cause":"Variation was not isolated.","solution":"Move the varying algorithm behind a common contract when justified.","alternative":"A conditional, function parameter, or mapping can be simpler for small stable variations.","tradeoff":"Strategy improves extensibility and test isolation but adds objects and indirection.","testing_guidance":"Test each strategy against the common contract and test context behavior with representative strategies.","verification_guidance":"Verify that strategy selection does not bypass required context invariants.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B22-0002","D13-DP-B24-0001"],"prerequisites":["Polymorphism","Encapsulation of variation"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B27-0002","D13-DP-B29-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern","source":"Refactoring.Guru Design Patterns catalog","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B27-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Behavior Selection","concept":"Strategy versus conditional","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should Strategy be preferred over a conditional?","answer":"Strategy becomes more useful when algorithms are independently evolving, require separate testing, are selected dynamically, or make the context difficult to understand; a conditional may remain preferable when variation is small and stable.","explanation":"There is no universal threshold. The Brain should evaluate change frequency, number of variants, complexity, independent testing needs, and architectural boundaries.","principle":"Choose the simplest design that handles the actual variation and change forces.","correct_example":"Independent pricing algorithms have separate ownership and evolve without changing the order-processing context.","invalid_example":"A Strategy object is introduced for two one-line branches that are tightly coupled to the same local operation.","problem":"Pattern structure may exceed the complexity of the problem.","failure_mode":"Simple behavior becomes unnecessarily fragmented.","cause":"Pattern selection is based on pattern availability rather than design forces.","solution":"Compare direct conditional logic with Strategy against explicit context-specific criteria.","alternative":"Conditional logic, function objects, lookup tables, or configuration-driven dispatch.","tradeoff":"Strategy provides extensibility and isolation at the cost of additional abstraction and wiring.","testing_guidance":"Compare test complexity and coverage before and after the design change.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that the pattern actually isolates a meaningful variation axis.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B27-0001"],"prerequisites":["Pattern selection"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B24-0002","D13-DP-B29-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_selection_reasoning","source":"Design pattern tradeoff analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B28-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Delegation","concept":"Delegation","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is delegation in object-oriented design?","answer":"Delegation is the practice of having one component request behavior from another component rather than implementing or inheriting that behavior itself.","explanation":"Delegation is a fundamental mechanism used by many compositional designs and patterns. It can reduce inheritance coupling while making collaboration explicit.","principle":"Delegate responsibilities to components that own the relevant behavior or variation.","correct_example":"A report service delegates formatting to a formatter object implementing a formatting contract.","invalid_example":"A service duplicates formatting logic instead of using an existing responsible component.","problem":"Responsibility becomes duplicated across components.","failure_mode":"Different implementations diverge over time.","cause":"Behavior ownership is unclear.","solution":"Assign responsibility to a coherent component and delegate to it through a clear contract.","alternative":"Inheritance where subtype semantics genuinely represent the relationship.","tradeoff":"Delegation adds an interaction boundary but can improve responsibility separation.","testing_guidance":"Test delegated behavior independently and verify integration at the delegation boundary.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that the delegating component does not duplicate or bypass the delegated responsibility.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B23-0001"],"prerequisites":["Composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B28-0002","D13-DP-B27-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_principle","source":"Delegation and composition practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B28-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Delegation","concept":"Delegation cycle","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What is a delegation cycle?","answer":"A delegation cycle occurs when components directly or indirectly delegate responsibility back to one another without reaching a terminating owner of the operation.","explanation":"Cycles may cause recursion, deadlock, repeated work, or unclear responsibility. Not every dependency cycle is a runtime delegation loop, so the actual execution path must be analyzed.","principle":"Delegation graphs should have an understandable ownership and termination model.","correct_example":"A service delegates validation to a validator, which delegates only to independent rules and returns a result.","invalid_example":"Service A delegates to Service B while Service B delegates the same operation back to Service A without a termination condition.","problem":"Responsibility has no clear terminal owner.","failure_mode":"Infinite recursion, repeated processing, or circular dependency.","cause":"Responsibilities were distributed without a clear termination boundary.","solution":"Identify ownership, break the cycle, or introduce an explicit coordinator where architecturally justified.","testing_guidance":"Test cyclic dependency and repeated invocation scenarios with bounded execution.","verification_guidance":"Analyze call and dependency graphs for cycles and verify intentional termination.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B28-0001"],"prerequisites":["Delegation","Dependency graphs"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B26-0002","D13-DP-B29-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_failure_mode","source":"Delegation and dependency analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_FAILURE_MODE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_FAILURE_MODE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B29-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern Selection","concept":"Pattern selection by forces","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should the Software Engineering Brain select a design pattern?","answer":"It should identify the problem, context, forces, constraints, variation points, required consequences, alternatives, and evidence before selecting a pattern.","explanation":"A pattern name should be the result of reasoning rather than the starting assumption. The same problem may have multiple valid solutions depending on constraints.","principle":"Select patterns because their tradeoffs address the current design forces, not because their structure is familiar.","correct_example":"The Brain identifies interchangeable algorithms, independent evolution, and a need for isolated testing before considering Strategy.","invalid_example":"The Brain sees several conditionals and automatically selects Strategy.","problem":"Pattern-first reasoning can introduce unnecessary structure.","failure_mode":"The implementation reflects the catalog instead of the actual system forces.","cause":"Pattern name replaced problem analysis.","solution":"Require explicit problem, context, forces, alternatives, and consequence analysis before pattern selection.","testing_guidance":"Include design-level tests or architecture checks that validate the selected boundary and behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the selected pattern addresses the stated forces and that rejected alternatives have recorded reasons.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B24-0002","D13-DP-B27-0002"],"prerequisites":["Pattern reasoning"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B29-0002","D13-DP-B30-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_method","source":"Pattern selection and catalog methodology","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_METHOD"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B29-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern Selection","concept":"Overengineering through patterns","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is pattern overengineering?","answer":"Pattern overengineering occurs when design-pattern structure is introduced without sufficient problem complexity, variation, or architectural justification, making the solution harder to understand or maintain.","explanation":"Patterns are reusable design knowledge, not mandatory implementation templates. A simple design may remain preferable when it satisfies the requirements and expected change forces.","principle":"Complexity introduced by a pattern must be justified by a corresponding design benefit.","correct_example":"A simple conditional remains in place because the behavior is small, local, and stable.","invalid_example":"A small conditional is transformed into multiple factories, interfaces, strategies, and registries without a demonstrated need.","problem":"Pattern machinery becomes larger than the behavior it manages.","failure_mode":"Maintenance cost increases while functional value remains unchanged.","cause":"Pattern structure was treated as inherently superior to simple code.","solution":"Compare the patterned and unpatterned alternatives against explicit forces and maintenance consequences.","alternative":"Use direct implementation until evidence supports a more structured boundary.","tradeoff":"A simpler design may require later refactoring if genuine variation emerges.","testing_guidance":"Compare behavioral coverage and maintenance complexity rather than pattern presence.","verification_guidance":"Require an explicit design rationale for non-trivial pattern introduction.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B21-0003","D13-DP-B29-0001"],"prerequisites":["Pattern selection"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B23-0002","D13-DP-B30-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_failure_mode","source":"Design pattern misuse analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B30-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern Decision Model","concept":"Pattern decision record","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What should a machine-readable pattern decision record contain?","answer":"A pattern decision record should capture the problem, context, forces, constraints, candidate solutions, selected solution, rejected alternatives, expected consequences, risks, implementation boundary, and verification evidence.","explanation":"This transforms pattern knowledge from a static catalog entry into reusable engineering reasoning. The record can later support retrieval, code generation, architecture review, debugging, and learning.","principle":"Pattern selection should be explainable and traceable from problem context to verified consequence.","correct_example":"A Strategy decision records the varying algorithm, stable context, rejected conditional implementation, expected extensibility benefit, and tests validating the contract.","invalid_example":"The architecture record states only 'Strategy pattern used' with no reason or evidence.","problem":"Future engineers cannot determine why the pattern was selected.","failure_mode":"Patterns are copied into unrelated contexts without understanding their forces.","cause":"Design rationale was discarded after implementation.","solution":"Persist structured pattern decision metadata and connect it to implementation and verification artifacts.","testing_guidance":"Link design decisions to tests that validate the intended behavioral consequences.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the selected pattern and its expected consequences match the actual implementation.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B29-0001"],"prerequisites":["Pattern selection","Traceability"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B30-0002","D13-DP-B20-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"knowledge_representation_pattern","source":"Software engineering decision-record practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B30-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern Decision Model","concept":"Pattern consequence validation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should the Brain verify that a selected design pattern produced its intended consequence?","answer":"It should identify the consequence claimed by the design decision and verify it with appropriate structural, behavioral, testing, performance, security, or maintenance evidence.","explanation":"Using a pattern does not guarantee its intended benefit. For example, an abstraction intended to reduce coupling should be evaluated against actual dependency relationships.","principle":"Pattern usage and pattern benefit are separate claims requiring separate evidence.","correct_example":"A design claims that a strategy boundary isolates algorithm changes; dependency and regression evidence shows the context remains unchanged when strategies vary.","invalid_example":"The design is declared successful solely because classes named Strategy and Context exist.","problem":"Pattern structure is confused with pattern effectiveness.","failure_mode":"Architectural complexity is retained without verifying its intended benefit.","cause":"No consequence-level verification.","solution":"Map each important claimed consequence to measurable or inspectable evidence.","testing_guidance":"Use appropriate architecture tests, regression tests, benchmarks, or inspections without fabricating measurements.","verification_guidance":"Mark consequences as VERIFIED only when supporting evidence exists; otherwise retain UNKNOWN or NOT_VERIFIED.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B30-0001"],"prerequisites":["Verification","Pattern reasoning"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B29-0002","D13-DP-B20-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"verification_principle","source":"Design decision verification practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B30-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern Retrieval","concept":"Natural-language pattern retrieval","knowledge_type":"RETRIEVAL_RULE","question":"How should a Software Engineering Brain retrieve a design pattern from a natural-language problem?","answer":"It should map the problem statement to context, forces, variation points, desired consequences, constraints, and anti-pattern signals before matching candidate patterns.","explanation":"Pattern retrieval should not depend only on exact pattern names. Different users may describe the same design problem using different terminology.","principle":"Retrieve patterns by problem and forces as well as by canonical names.","correct_example":"A request to switch interchangeable algorithms without modifying the calling workflow retrieves Strategy-related knowledge even when the user never names the pattern.","invalid_example":"The Brain fails to retrieve Strategy because the query does not contain the word Strategy.","problem":"Name-dependent retrieval misses semantically relevant design knowledge.","failure_mode":"The Brain proposes unrelated or incomplete solutions.","cause":"Retrieval indexes only pattern labels.","solution":"Index intent, problem, context, forces, consequences, alternatives, failure modes, and terminology variants.","testing_guidance":"Test exact-name, conceptual, debugging, and natural-language retrieval queries.","verification_guidance":"Evaluate retrieval against expected relevant records and record misses and false positives.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B29-0001","D13-DP-B30-0001"],"prerequisites":["Semantic retrieval","Pattern ontology"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B24-0002","D13-DP-B27-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"retrieval_design_principle","source":"Knowledge retrieval engineering","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_METHOD"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

