{"record_id":"D13-DP-B31-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Creational Patterns","concept":"Creational pattern purpose","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What problem do creational design patterns address?","answer":"Creational design patterns address concerns around object creation, construction, initialization, and selection while reducing inappropriate coupling between clients and concrete creation mechanisms.","explanation":"Creational patterns can separate construction from use, encapsulate creation decisions, or control how complex objects are assembled. They do not imply that every object should be created through a pattern.","principle":"Introduce creation abstractions when object construction itself creates meaningful coupling, variation, complexity, or lifecycle concerns.","correct_example":"A client requests a product through a creation contract instead of directly depending on multiple concrete product classes.","invalid_example":"Adding a factory layer around a trivial object whose construction has no variation or complexity.","problem":"Direct construction may couple clients to concrete types or complex construction rules.","failure_mode":"Creation changes require modifications across many clients.","cause":"Creation responsibility is distributed unnecessarily.","solution":"Encapsulate creation only when the resulting boundary addresses an actual design force.","alternative":"Direct construction for simple stable objects.","tradeoff":"Creation abstraction can improve changeability but adds indirection.","testing_guidance":"Test creation behavior separately from product behavior where the creation boundary has meaningful logic.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the creation abstraction actually isolates the intended variation or construction complexity.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Design pattern fundamentals"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B31-0002","D13-DP-B32-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Design Patterns","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN_CLASSIFICATION"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B31-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Creational Patterns","concept":"Creation responsibility","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When should object creation be separated from object use?","answer":"Creation should be separated from use when creation varies independently, requires complex assembly, depends on runtime context, or would otherwise couple clients to concrete implementations.","explanation":"The separation should be proportional to the actual complexity and variation. Simple construction is often clearer when kept local.","principle":"Separate creation when creation is itself a meaningful source of change or coupling.","correct_example":"A client selects among multiple compatible product implementations without containing the concrete construction logic.","invalid_example":"Introducing a factory solely because a class has a constructor.","problem":"Creation abstraction is introduced without a meaningful variation point.","failure_mode":"The code becomes more indirect without reducing coupling.","cause":"Pattern structure is treated as inherently beneficial.","solution":"Evaluate concrete construction complexity and change forces before introducing a creational pattern.","alternative":"Direct constructor invocation.","tradeoff":"Centralized creation improves consistency but can create a large creation dependency.","testing_guidance":"Test creation paths and invalid creation requests separately.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that creation logic has an identifiable responsibility and boundary.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":["Creational design"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B29-0002","D13-DP-B32-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_principle","source":"Creational design practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B32-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Factory Method","concept":"Factory Method","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Factory Method pattern?","answer":"Factory Method defines a method for creating an object while allowing a subclass or specialized implementation to determine which concrete product is created.","explanation":"The essential idea is to defer a creation decision behind a method contract. Different implementations can produce compatible products while client logic works with the product abstraction.","principle":"Move a varying product-creation decision behind a stable creation operation.","correct_example":"A document-processing framework defines a creation operation for documents while specialized document handlers create concrete document types.","invalid_example":"A factory method always creates the same concrete type and has no meaningful variation or extension point.","problem":"Client code is coupled directly to concrete product construction.","failure_mode":"Adding another product type requires modifying existing creation logic or clients.","cause":"Creation variation is not isolated.","solution":"Use a Factory Method when concrete product selection should vary through an extension point.","alternative":"Simple factory function, registry, dependency injection, or direct construction depending on context.","tradeoff":"Factory Method can improve extensibility but may introduce subclasses or additional types.","testing_guidance":"Test each concrete creator and verify that the returned product satisfies the product contract.","verification_guidance":"Verify that creator implementations produce valid products and that clients do not depend on concrete product details unnecessarily.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B31-0001","D13-DP-B22-0002"],"prerequisites":["Polymorphism","Creational patterns"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B32-0002","D13-DP-B33-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Factory Method","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B32-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Factory Method","concept":"Factory Method applicability","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When is Factory Method applicable?","answer":"Factory Method is applicable when a framework or base component needs to use products through an abstraction while allowing specialized creators to determine the concrete product.","explanation":"It is particularly useful when product creation is part of an extension mechanism. It is less compelling when creation does not vary or when a simpler factory function adequately isolates the decision.","principle":"Use Factory Method when creation varies through a polymorphic creator boundary.","correct_example":"A base workflow calls a creation method and subclasses provide different compatible product implementations.","invalid_example":"Using Factory Method merely to hide a constructor in a system with one immutable product type.","problem":"The pattern can create unnecessary inheritance.","failure_mode":"The class hierarchy grows without providing a useful extension mechanism.","cause":"Factory Method is selected without a subclass variation requirement.","solution":"Compare Factory Method with a function, dependency injection, or composition-based creation mechanism.","alternative":"Factory function or injected creator.","tradeoff":"Factory Method supports polymorphic extension but can increase hierarchy complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test base workflow behavior independently from each creator implementation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that subclass-specific creation is actually required by the design.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B32-0001"],"prerequisites":["Factory Method"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B32-0003","D13-DP-B35-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Factory Method","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B32-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Factory Method","concept":"Factory Method failure mode","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can Factory Method be misused?","answer":"Factory Method can be misused when subclasses exist primarily to select products even though composition or a simple creator abstraction would provide the same variation with less coupling.","explanation":"The pattern's extension mechanism should justify the additional creator hierarchy. Otherwise the hierarchy can become difficult to navigate and maintain.","principle":"A polymorphic creation hierarchy should have a genuine polymorphic responsibility.","correct_example":"Different creator subclasses represent independently extensible product families within a framework.","invalid_example":"One subclass exists solely to change one constructor call and will never be extended independently.","problem":"Subclassing is used for a trivial creation difference.","failure_mode":"The hierarchy becomes unnecessarily deep or fragmented.","cause":"Creation variation was coupled to inheritance without sufficient reason.","solution":"Move creation variation into composition, dependency injection, or a factory function when appropriate.","alternative":"Injected creator object or callable.","tradeoff":"Composition may require explicit wiring but can reduce inheritance coupling.","testing_guidance":"Test whether creator substitution is actually required by consumer behavior.","verification_guidance":"Review creator hierarchy depth and responsibility before approving the pattern.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B32-0001","D13-DP-B23-0001"],"prerequisites":["Factory Method","Composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B25-0002","D13-DP-B29-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_failure_analysis","source":"Factory Method tradeoff analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B33-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Abstract Factory","concept":"Abstract Factory","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Abstract Factory pattern?","answer":"Abstract Factory provides an interface for creating families of related or compatible objects without requiring client code to depend directly on their concrete classes.","explanation":"The pattern is useful when products form coherent families and compatibility between products matters. A concrete factory supplies one consistent family.","principle":"Encapsulate creation of related product families behind a common factory contract.","correct_example":"A UI toolkit provides a factory that creates mutually compatible button, dialog, and menu implementations for a selected platform family.","invalid_example":"Using Abstract Factory when there is only one unrelated product type with no family compatibility concern.","problem":"Clients need multiple related objects whose concrete implementations must remain compatible.","failure_mode":"Clients accidentally combine incompatible product variants.","cause":"Product-family consistency is not centrally controlled.","solution":"Represent the family through an Abstract Factory when the family relationship is meaningful.","alternative":"Separate factories or direct construction when products are independent.","tradeoff":"Abstract Factory improves family consistency but can make adding a new product type expensive across all factories.","testing_guidance":"Test each concrete factory for family compatibility and each product contract.","verification_guidance":"Verify that products returned by a concrete factory belong to the intended compatible family.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B32-0001"],"prerequisites":["Factory Method","Product abstractions"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B33-0002","D13-DP-B34-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Abstract Factory","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B33-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Abstract Factory","concept":"Product family compatibility","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an Abstract Factory verify product compatibility?","answer":"Each concrete factory should produce products whose contracts and compatibility assumptions belong to the same intended product family.","explanation":"Type compatibility alone may not guarantee semantic compatibility. Family-level invariants should be represented and tested where they matter.","principle":"A factory family should preserve the compatibility guarantees promised to clients.","correct_example":"A platform factory produces all UI controls from the same platform implementation family.","invalid_example":"A factory returns a button from one platform and a dialog from an incompatible platform while exposing them as one family.","problem":"Products can satisfy individual interfaces while violating family-level assumptions.","failure_mode":"Integration failures appear only when products interact.","cause":"Compatibility is tested per product rather than across the family.","solution":"Add family-level contract and integration tests.","testing_guidance":"Test representative combinations of products produced by each concrete factory.","verification_guidance":"Verify family invariants rather than only individual product interfaces.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B33-0001"],"prerequisites":["Abstract Factory","Contract testing"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B33-0003","D13-DP-B34-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_verification","source":"Abstract Factory compatibility reasoning","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B33-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Abstract Factory","concept":"Abstract Factory tradeoff","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is a major structural tradeoff of Abstract Factory?","answer":"Abstract Factory makes adding a new product family relatively localized but can make adding a new product type across all families require changes to multiple factory interfaces and implementations.","explanation":"This tradeoff results from the pattern's representation of a fixed set of related product roles. The design should reflect which dimension is expected to vary more frequently.","principle":"Pattern structure should match the expected axis of variation.","correct_example":"Multiple platform families are expected while the set of control roles is relatively stable.","invalid_example":"The system frequently adds new product roles but rarely adds new product families, while the factory interface fixes every product role.","problem":"The pattern makes the common change direction expensive.","failure_mode":"Every new product role requires widespread factory changes.","cause":"The abstraction aligns with the wrong variation axis.","solution":"Re-evaluate the product model and consider alternative creation mechanisms.","alternative":"Separate factories, registration mechanisms, composition, or another architecture depending on requirements.","tradeoff":"Changing the factory architecture may improve one variation axis while weakening family-level guarantees.","testing_guidance":"Test both new-family and new-product-type scenarios before finalizing the design.","verification_guidance":"Compare expected change frequency against the structural change cost imposed by the pattern.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B33-0001","D13-DP-B24-0002"],"prerequisites":["Abstract Factory","Variation analysis"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B34-0001","D13-DP-B39-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_tradeoff","source":"Abstract Factory design analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DESIGN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DESIGN_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B34-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Abstract Factory","concept":"Abstract Factory versus Factory Method","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the conceptual difference between Abstract Factory and Factory Method?","answer":"Factory Method focuses on deferring creation of a product through a creation method, while Abstract Factory focuses on creating a family of related products through a coordinated factory interface.","explanation":"The patterns can coexist. An Abstract Factory may internally use Factory Methods or other creation mechanisms.","principle":"Choose based on whether the central design force is one varying product-creation operation or coordinated creation of compatible product families.","correct_example":"Use Factory Method when one product type varies through an extension point; use Abstract Factory when multiple related products must vary together.","invalid_example":"Choosing Abstract Factory merely because multiple constructors exist.","problem":"Pattern choice does not match the actual creation structure.","failure_mode":"The architecture introduces unnecessary family-level machinery.","cause":"Pattern names are used without analyzing product relationships.","solution":"Identify product roles, family compatibility, and expected variation before selection.","alternative":"Direct construction or simpler factory functions.","tradeoff":"Abstract Factory provides stronger family consistency but introduces broader creation abstractions.","testing_guidance":"Test product creation independently and family compatibility when using Abstract Factory.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that the chosen pattern corresponds to the actual variation dimensions.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B32-0001","D13-DP-B33-0001"],"prerequisites":["Creational pattern comparison"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B33-0003","D13-DP-B39-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_comparison","source":"Classical creational pattern analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B34-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Abstract Factory","concept":"Factory family security boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security concern can arise when an Abstract Factory selects implementation families?","answer":"An incorrect or attacker-influenced family selection can instantiate components with weaker security properties or incompatible trust assumptions.","explanation":"The pattern itself does not provide security. Factory selection must respect authorization, trust boundaries, configuration integrity, and allowed implementation policies.","principle":"Creation mechanisms must not become an uncontrolled path around security policy.","correct_example":"A factory selects only implementations permitted for the authenticated tenant and deployment environment.","invalid_example":"A request parameter directly selects an arbitrary implementation class without allowlisting or authorization checks.","problem":"Dynamic creation can become a policy bypass.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized or unsafe components are instantiated.","cause":"Creation selection is treated as neutral data rather than a security-sensitive decision.","solution":"Validate and constrain implementation selection through explicit policy and trusted configuration.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized selections, malformed selectors, tenant isolation, and default behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every dynamically selectable implementation is authorized and intended for the execution context.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B33-0001"],"prerequisites":["Security boundaries","Factory selection"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B38-0002","D13-DP-B40-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"security_design_reasoning","source":"Secure dynamic component selection principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B35-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Builder","concept":"Builder","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Builder pattern?","answer":"Builder separates the construction of a complex object from its representation so that the same construction process can create different representations or configurations.","explanation":"Builder is particularly useful when an object has many optional or ordered construction steps, complex validation, or multiple representations. It should not automatically replace a straightforward constructor.","principle":"Separate complex construction sequencing from the final product representation when that separation provides meaningful value.","correct_example":"A report builder incrementally configures sections, metadata, formatting, and output representation before producing the final report.","invalid_example":"Using Builder for a class with two required fields and no construction complexity.","problem":"Complex constructors can become difficult to read and validate.","failure_mode":"Callers pass many parameters incorrectly or construction rules become scattered.","cause":"Object construction has significant configuration or sequencing complexity.","solution":"Centralize construction steps and validation through a Builder when justified.","alternative":"Constructor, named constructor, factory function, or configuration object.","tradeoff":"Builder improves construction clarity but introduces another abstraction and lifecycle.","testing_guidance":"Test required fields, optional combinations, invalid sequences, defaults, and final object invariants.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the built product satisfies all construction invariants before becoming observable.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":["Object construction","Validation"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B35-0002","D13-DP-B36-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Builder","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B35-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Builder","concept":"Builder validation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Where should Builder validate object invariants?","answer":"Validation should occur at appropriate construction boundaries so that a completed product cannot be exposed as valid when required invariants are unsatisfied.","explanation":"Validation may be distributed across individual setters and final build validation. Cross-field invariants generally require validation when enough information is available to evaluate them.","principle":"A builder should prevent invalid completed products from being treated as valid.","correct_example":"The final build operation rejects a configuration where an end date precedes a start date.","invalid_example":"Each field is individually valid, but the builder never checks relationships between fields.","problem":"Cross-field invalid states pass local validation.","failure_mode":"Invalid objects reach downstream components.","cause":"Validation is performed only at individual input boundaries.","solution":"Add construction-level invariant validation before finalization.","testing_guidance":"Test individual invalid values and combinations of individually valid but mutually incompatible values.","verification_guidance":"Verify that successful build completion implies all documented construction invariants.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B35-0001","D13-DP-B26-0002"],"prerequisites":["Builder","Invariant validation"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B35-0003","D13-DP-B36-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_verification","source":"Builder validation practice","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B35-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Builder","concept":"Builder misuse","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"When is Builder unnecessary?","answer":"Builder is unnecessary when object construction is simple, stable, and clearly represented by a constructor or small factory function.","explanation":"Builder should solve construction complexity rather than provide ceremony around ordinary object creation.","principle":"Construction complexity should justify construction abstraction.","correct_example":"A configuration object has numerous optional fields, validation dependencies, and multiple construction representations.","invalid_example":"A two-field immutable value object requires a Builder with several additional classes.","problem":"Simple construction becomes unnecessarily verbose.","failure_mode":"Developers must navigate extra types and lifecycle steps for trivial objects.","cause":"Builder was introduced as a stylistic default rather than a complexity response.","solution":"Use direct construction or a small factory where it is clearer.","alternative":"Constructor, named constructor, factory function, configuration object.","tradeoff":"Removing Builder reduces indirection but may require another mechanism if construction complexity later grows.","testing_guidance":"Compare construction readability and validation coverage rather than pattern presence.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that the Builder isolates real construction complexity.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B35-0001"],"prerequisites":["Builder"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B29-0002","D13-DP-B36-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_failure_analysis","source":"Builder tradeoff analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B36-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Builder","concept":"Builder versus factory","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the main difference between Builder and Factory patterns?","answer":"A Factory primarily encapsulates which object or family of objects should be created, while Builder primarily encapsulates how a complex object is incrementally constructed.","explanation":"The distinction is about the dominant creation concern. A Builder can use a Factory internally, and a Factory can return objects produced through complex construction.","principle":"Choose based on whether the primary variation is product selection or construction process.","correct_example":"Use Factory when selecting one implementation from several; use Builder when assembling one complex product from many configuration steps.","invalid_example":"Using Builder solely because multiple concrete product classes exist.","problem":"Selection and construction concerns are conflated.","failure_mode":"The resulting creation abstraction does not address the actual complexity.","cause":"No distinction between what is created and how it is assembled.","solution":"Analyze product-selection and construction complexity independently.","alternative":"Combined Factory and Builder when both concerns are genuinely present.","tradeoff":"Combining patterns can isolate separate concerns but increases structural complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test selection separately from construction steps when both mechanisms exist.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each abstraction has one identifiable creation responsibility.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B32-0001","D13-DP-B35-0001"],"prerequisites":["Creational patterns"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B36-0002","D13-DP-B39-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_comparison","source":"Creational pattern analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B36-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Builder","concept":"Builder lifecycle","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What lifecycle risks exist when a Builder is reusable?","answer":"A reusable Builder can retain state from a previous construction operation and accidentally transfer configuration into a subsequent product unless its lifecycle and reset semantics are explicit.","explanation":"Builders are often stateful during construction. Reuse should therefore be intentional and safe rather than assumed.","principle":"Mutable construction state must have explicit ownership and lifecycle semantics.","correct_example":"A builder creates one product and is discarded, or it provides a verified reset operation before reuse.","invalid_example":"A shared mutable builder is reused concurrently by unrelated requests.","problem":"Construction state can leak between operations or threads.","failure_mode":"Products contain configuration belonging to another construction operation.","cause":"Builder lifecycle and ownership are unclear.","solution":"Prefer isolated builder instances or explicitly synchronize and reset state when reuse is required.","testing_guidance":"Test reuse, reset, concurrent access, and partial construction scenarios where applicable.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each product contains only configuration belonging to its own construction lifecycle.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B35-0001"],"prerequisites":["Builder lifecycle","Concurrency"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B40-0001","D13-DP-B40-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_failure_mode","source":"Mutable builder lifecycle analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_FAILURE_MODE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B37-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Prototype","concept":"Prototype","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Prototype pattern?","answer":"Prototype creates new objects by copying an existing prototype object rather than constructing each object from scratch through a separate concrete creation path.","explanation":"Prototype can be useful when object initialization is expensive, complex, configuration-heavy, or when callers need copies of an existing configured state.","principle":"Reuse an existing object configuration through controlled copying when copying is simpler or more appropriate than reconstruction.","correct_example":"A configured document template is cloned to create independent documents with the same initial configuration.","invalid_example":"Cloning an object whose identity, resources, or ownership semantics cannot safely be copied.","problem":"Repeated reconstruction may duplicate complex initialization logic.","failure_mode":"Copies accidentally share mutable or identity-sensitive state.","cause":"Copy semantics were not explicitly defined.","solution":"Define shallow versus deep copy behavior and ownership of referenced resources.","alternative":"Builder, Factory, serialization-based reconstruction, or explicit copy constructor.","tradeoff":"Prototype can simplify complex initialization but makes copy semantics and object ownership important.","testing_guidance":"Test nested mutable state, identity fields, resource ownership, and modifications after cloning.","verification_guidance":"Verify that cloned objects satisfy the intended independence and shared-state semantics.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":["Object copying","Object identity"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B37-0002","D13-DP-B38-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Prototype","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B37-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Prototype","concept":"Shallow versus deep copy","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between shallow and deep copying in Prototype design?","answer":"A shallow copy duplicates the outer object while retaining references to nested objects, whereas a deep copy recursively creates independent copies of relevant nested mutable state.","explanation":"Neither strategy is universally correct. The correct semantics depend on ownership, mutability, identity, resource sharing, and the intended lifecycle of referenced objects.","principle":"Copy semantics must match the ownership and independence requirements of the product.","correct_example":"A prototype with immutable shared configuration may safely share those values while creating independent mutable session state.","invalid_example":"A cloned security context shares mutable authorization state with the original object unintentionally.","problem":"Shared references create hidden coupling between supposedly independent objects.","failure_mode":"Changing one clone changes another clone's behavior or security state.","cause":"Copy depth does not match ownership semantics.","solution":"Explicitly classify fields as shared immutable state, shared controlled resources, or independently owned mutable state.","testing_guidance":"Modify nested fields in one clone and verify expected effects on other clones.","verification_guidance":"Inspect and test reference relationships after cloning.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B37-0001"],"prerequisites":["Prototype","Object ownership"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B37-0003","D13-DP-B38-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_copy_semantics","source":"Object copying principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCEPT"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B37-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Prototype","concept":"Prototype identity hazard","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can cloning identity-bearing objects be dangerous?","answer":"A clone may accidentally retain identifiers, ownership markers, credentials, resource handles, or audit identity that should belong only to the original object.","explanation":"Prototype is primarily about copying structure and state; identity semantics must be deliberately defined. Security-sensitive and externally unique identifiers often require regeneration rather than copying.","principle":"Structural similarity does not imply identity equivalence.","correct_example":"A cloned order template receives a new order identifier while copying permitted configuration fields.","invalid_example":"A cloned security credential retains the original credential identifier and secret.","problem":"Identity-bearing fields may be duplicated unintentionally.","failure_mode":"Two objects are treated as the same entity or gain unintended authority.","cause":"Copy operation includes identity-sensitive state.","solution":"Classify and regenerate identity-sensitive fields during cloning.","testing_guidance":"Test identity uniqueness, authorization state, ownership, audit records, and secret handling after cloning.","verification_guidance":"Verify that cloned objects cannot inherit unintended identity or authority.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B37-0002"],"prerequisites":["Prototype","Security identity"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B38-0002","D13-DP-B40-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"security_design_failure_mode","source":"Prototype identity analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B38-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Prototype","concept":"Prototype versus Builder","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When is Prototype preferable to Builder?","answer":"Prototype can be preferable when a valid configured instance already exists and copying its state is simpler than reconstructing that state through construction steps; Builder is preferable when construction itself is the primary complexity.","explanation":"The choice depends on copy semantics, initialization cost, configuration reuse, and lifecycle requirements.","principle":"Choose copying when an existing valid configuration is the useful source of creation; choose Builder when construction steps are the useful abstraction.","correct_example":"Cloning a validated document template creates variants with the same established configuration.","invalid_example":"Using Prototype when every clone requires a substantially different configuration and copying provides little value.","problem":"Copying can preserve unwanted state when reconstruction would be safer.","failure_mode":"Products inherit configuration that should have been explicitly selected.","cause":"Prototype is used despite weak state-reuse requirements.","solution":"Compare copy semantics with explicit construction and select the mechanism with clearer invariants.","alternative":"Builder or Factory.","tradeoff":"Prototype can reduce repeated initialization but requires precise copy semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test copied state and ensure excluded state is not inherited.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the cloned object's invariants are equivalent to intended construction semantics.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B35-0001","D13-DP-B37-0001"],"prerequisites":["Builder","Prototype"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B36-0001","D13-DP-B39-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_comparison","source":"Creational pattern analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B38-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Prototype","concept":"Prototype security boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security controls should be considered when cloning security-sensitive objects?","answer":"Cloning should explicitly control secrets, credentials, authorization state, ownership, identity, capability references, and audit metadata so that copies do not inherit unintended authority or sensitive material.","explanation":"Prototype does not provide security guarantees by itself. Copy operations can become security boundaries when objects contain sensitive state.","principle":"Sensitive state must have explicit copy semantics and should not be duplicated merely because it is present in the source object.","correct_example":"A cloned session-like object receives new identity and authorization state rather than copying security credentials.","invalid_example":"A clone automatically inherits authentication tokens from the source object.","problem":"Sensitive state can cross an intended isolation boundary.","failure_mode":"A newly created object gains unintended access or exposes copied secrets.","cause":"Copy semantics treat security state as ordinary data.","solution":"Classify security-sensitive fields and explicitly exclude, regenerate, or securely transform them.","testing_guidance":"Test cloning with expired, privileged, and malformed security state.","verification_guidance":"Inspect resulting authority and sensitive references after cloning.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B37-0003"],"prerequisites":["Prototype","Security"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B34-0002","D13-DP-B40-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"security_design_reasoning","source":"Secure object-copy principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B39-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Singleton","concept":"Singleton","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Singleton pattern?","answer":"Singleton restricts a class or component to a single instance within a defined scope and provides a controlled access mechanism to that instance.","explanation":"The important qualification is scope: process-wide, module-wide, application-context-wide, container-wide, or another explicitly defined scope may differ. Singleton is often used to control unique resources or shared coordination state.","principle":"Use Singleton only when uniqueness within a defined scope is a genuine invariant or resource requirement.","correct_example":"A process-local component owns one shared coordination resource whose uniqueness is explicitly required.","invalid_example":"Making every service a Singleton simply to avoid passing dependencies.","problem":"Global uniqueness can create hidden coupling and shared mutable state.","failure_mode":"Tests interfere with each other or unrelated components depend on global state.","cause":"Singleton is used as a convenience mechanism rather than an actual uniqueness requirement.","solution":"Prefer explicit ownership and dependency injection when global uniqueness is not required.","alternative":"Module-level state, dependency-injected scoped instance, resource manager, or ordinary object depending on requirements.","tradeoff":"Singleton can enforce controlled uniqueness but reduces isolation and can complicate testing.","testing_guidance":"Test initialization, lifecycle, concurrency, reset behavior, and cross-test state isolation.","verification_guidance":"Verify the intended uniqueness scope and ensure the implementation actually enforces it.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B25-0001"],"prerequisites":["Object lifecycle","Shared state"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B39-0002","D13-DP-B40-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Singleton","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B39-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Singleton","concept":"Singleton global-state risk","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can Singleton create problems in software design?","answer":"A Singleton can behave like globally accessible shared state, creating hidden dependencies, making test isolation harder, and increasing coupling between otherwise independent components.","explanation":"These risks are consequences of global accessibility and shared mutable state rather than a universal property of every singleton implementation.","principle":"Global accessibility should not be introduced merely to simplify dependency access.","correct_example":"A uniquely scoped resource is exposed through an explicit lifecycle-managed dependency while consumers receive the instance through composition.","invalid_example":"Unrelated modules access mutable singleton state directly because dependency passing is considered inconvenient.","problem":"Consumers become coupled to shared state and lifecycle.","failure_mode":"Behavior depends on previous operations performed by unrelated tests or components.","cause":"Global mutable state is uncontrolled.","solution":"Use explicit dependency injection and controlled lifecycle scopes when uniqueness is not itself the requirement.","alternative":"Scoped dependency container or explicit resource owner.","tradeoff":"Removing global access can require more explicit wiring but improves dependency visibility.","testing_guidance":"Run tests in different orders and isolated environments to detect shared-state contamination.","verification_guidance":"Inspect all access paths to shared state and verify lifecycle ownership.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B39-0001","D13-DP-B25-0001"],"prerequisites":["Singleton","Dependency management"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B25-0002","D13-DP-B40-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_failure_analysis","source":"Singleton design tradeoff analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B39-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Singleton","concept":"Singleton versus dependency injection","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does an explicitly injected shared instance differ from a Singleton?","answer":"An injected shared instance can provide one object within a chosen scope while keeping ownership and dependency relationships explicit; a Singleton usually embeds or centralizes access to the uniqueness mechanism.","explanation":"Dependency injection can therefore preserve uniqueness without requiring consumers to locate global state themselves.","principle":"Uniqueness and global accessibility are separate concerns.","correct_example":"An application composition root creates one resource manager and injects that same instance into all components requiring it.","invalid_example":"Every component independently retrieves a globally accessible singleton because explicit ownership is absent.","problem":"Global access is mistaken for required uniqueness.","failure_mode":"Dependencies become hidden even though only one instance is needed.","cause":"Instance scope and access mechanism are coupled unnecessarily.","solution":"Separate lifecycle ownership from access by injecting the shared instance where practical.","alternative":"Singleton when global access and uniqueness are both deliberate requirements.","tradeoff":"Injection increases explicit wiring but makes dependencies visible.","testing_guidance":"Test multiple consumers using the same injected instance and verify lifecycle behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the intended scope is maintained without introducing unintended global access.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B39-0001","D13-DP-B25-0001"],"prerequisites":["Singleton","Dependency injection"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B39-0002","D13-DP-B40-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_comparison","source":"Singleton and dependency management analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B40-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Singleton","concept":"Singleton concurrency","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What concurrency issue can arise during Singleton initialization?","answer":"Concurrent initialization can result in multiple instances or partially initialized shared state if the implementation does not provide an appropriate synchronization or language-level initialization guarantee.","explanation":"The correct mechanism depends on the programming language and runtime memory model. The Brain must not assume that an apparently simple lazy initialization implementation is automatically safe under concurrency.","principle":"Singleton initialization must satisfy the language and runtime's actual concurrency guarantees.","correct_example":"A runtime-supported safe initialization mechanism guarantees one fully initialized instance within the documented scope.","invalid_example":"Multiple threads lazily initialize shared state without synchronization or a language guarantee.","problem":"Concurrent access can race during initialization.","failure_mode":"Multiple instances, inconsistent state, or visibility problems occur.","cause":"Initialization protocol is not concurrency-safe.","solution":"Use a language/runtime-supported safe initialization mechanism or explicit synchronization appropriate to the environment.","testing_guidance":"Run concurrent initialization tests and inspect instance identity and initialization state.","verification_guidance":"Verify the guarantee using language/runtime documentation and targeted concurrency tests.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B39-0001"],"prerequisites":["Singleton","Concurrency"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B36-0002","D13-DP-B40-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"concurrency_failure_mode","source":"Singleton concurrency analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B40-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Singleton","concept":"Singleton security boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security risk can arise from a globally accessible Singleton?","answer":"A globally accessible Singleton can become a privileged shared capability or repository for sensitive state, allowing unrelated code to access or modify information beyond its intended authority.","explanation":"The risk depends on what the Singleton exposes and who can reach it. Singleton itself is not a security vulnerability, but global access can weaken authority boundaries.","principle":"Object lifetime uniqueness must not be confused with authorization to use the object.","correct_example":"A uniquely scoped resource is exposed through an authorized service boundary that checks caller permissions.","invalid_example":"Any module can directly modify a globally accessible privileged security manager.","problem":"Global access bypasses intended authorization boundaries.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized code can invoke privileged operations or alter security state.","cause":"Access control is delegated to object visibility rather than explicit policy.","solution":"Place authorization at the operation boundary and restrict access to privileged capabilities.","alternative":"Dependency-injected capability with explicit authorization wrapper.","tradeoff":"Explicit access control adds boundary checks but makes authority more visible.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized callers, privilege boundaries, tenant isolation, and mutation attempts.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every privileged operation has an explicit authorization path independent of Singleton access.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B39-0001","D13-DP-B39-0002"],"prerequisites":["Singleton","Authorization"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B34-0002","D13-DP-B38-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"security_design_reasoning","source":"Global-state and capability security principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B40-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Creational Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Creational pattern selection matrix","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should a Software Engineering Brain distinguish common creational patterns?","answer":"Factory Method addresses polymorphic creation through a creator extension point; Abstract Factory coordinates compatible product families; Builder separates complex construction steps from representation; Prototype creates objects by copying existing instances; Singleton constrains instance count within a defined scope.","explanation":"The distinctions describe dominant design forces rather than rigid implementation rules. Patterns can be combined when their responsibilities remain distinct.","principle":"Select the smallest creational abstraction that directly addresses the dominant creation problem.","correct_example":"A complex configurable product uses Builder, while platform-specific related products use Abstract Factory.","invalid_example":"Selecting Singleton because an object has many configuration options.","problem":"Pattern names are substituted for problem analysis.","failure_mode":"The chosen creation mechanism solves a different problem than the system actually has.","cause":"No explicit mapping between forces and pattern intent.","solution":"Identify creation selection, family compatibility, construction complexity, copying semantics, and uniqueness requirements before selecting.","alternative":"Direct construction or small factory functions for simple cases.","tradeoff":"More structure can isolate change but increases indirection and maintenance cost.","testing_guidance":"Test the specific creation invariant associated with each selected pattern.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every selected pattern has an explicit intent-to-problem mapping and documented alternatives.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B32-0001","D13-DP-B33-0001","D13-DP-B35-0001","D13-DP-B37-0001","D13-DP-B39-0001"],"prerequisites":["Creational patterns"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B29-0001","D13-DP-B30-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_synthesis","source":"Classical creational pattern catalog and comparative reasoning","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B41-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Creational Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Creation versus lifecycle ownership","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should object creation and object lifecycle ownership be treated as separate design concerns?","answer":"Creating an object does not automatically determine who owns, scopes, disposes, caches, shares, or replaces it.","explanation":"A factory can create an object while another component owns its lifecycle. Confusing these responsibilities can produce resource leaks, premature disposal, hidden sharing, or inappropriate global state.","principle":"Creation responsibility and lifecycle responsibility should be modeled independently unless they are intentionally coupled.","correct_example":"A factory creates a database client while an application-scoped resource manager owns its lifecycle.","invalid_example":"A factory silently caches every created resource forever without an explicit ownership policy.","problem":"Creation and ownership semantics become implicit.","failure_mode":"Resources remain alive beyond their intended scope or are disposed while still in use.","cause":"Factory responsibility was expanded into unexamined lifecycle management.","solution":"Document creation, ownership, scope, disposal, and sharing semantics separately.","alternative":"Dependency-injection scope management or explicit resource owner.","tradeoff":"Explicit lifecycle management adds design information but reduces hidden resource behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test creation, reuse, disposal, and scope boundaries independently.","verification_guidance":"Verify ownership and disposal responsibilities from creation through termination.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B40-0003"],"prerequisites":["Creational patterns","Resource lifecycle"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B39-0001","D13-DP-B42-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_principle","source":"Object lifecycle and creational pattern reasoning","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B41-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Creational Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Pattern selection by dominant variation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should a Software Engineering Brain select among creational patterns?","answer":"First identify the dominant variation: product implementation, product family, construction process, copied state, or instance scope. Then select the smallest abstraction that directly addresses that variation.","explanation":"Pattern selection should begin with design forces rather than pattern names. A pattern is justified when its structure addresses a recurring change or constraint.","principle":"Select by design force and expected change, not by familiarity with pattern terminology.","correct_example":"Use Builder when construction sequencing and optional configuration dominate the problem.","invalid_example":"Use Abstract Factory simply because several constructors exist.","problem":"Pattern selection becomes name-driven rather than evidence-driven.","failure_mode":"The implementation carries unnecessary abstraction and mismatched responsibilities.","cause":"The problem was not modeled before selecting a pattern.","solution":"Record context, forces, variation axis, alternatives, and consequences before pattern selection.","alternative":"Direct construction when no meaningful variation exists.","tradeoff":"More deliberate selection takes analysis time but reduces pattern misuse.","testing_guidance":"Test the behavior that motivated the selected pattern rather than testing pattern shape itself.","verification_guidance":"Require an explicit problem-to-pattern rationale.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B40-0003"],"prerequisites":["Pattern selection"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B24-0001","D13-DP-B29-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_selection_method","source":"Design-pattern reasoning","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B41-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Creational Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Pattern versus abstraction layer","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should a design pattern not automatically become a permanent abstraction layer?","answer":"A pattern is a reusable design solution, not a requirement to preserve every structural element forever. If the original design force disappears, the abstraction may become unnecessary complexity.","explanation":"Refactoring can remove a pattern when direct design becomes clearer and preserves required behavior.","principle":"Patterns should remain justified by current design forces.","correct_example":"A factory abstraction is removed after the system becomes stable around one concrete implementation and no extension requirement remains.","invalid_example":"Keeping multiple unused factory interfaces solely because the project once used Factory Method.","problem":"Historical pattern structure becomes accidental architecture.","failure_mode":"Maintenance requires understanding abstractions that no longer provide value.","cause":"Pattern removal is treated as architectural regression.","solution":"Periodically evaluate whether the original design force still exists.","alternative":"Direct construction or simpler abstraction.","tradeoff":"Removing abstraction reduces complexity but may require reintroducing it if variation returns.","testing_guidance":"Use regression tests to prove behavior remains equivalent after simplification.","verification_guidance":"Verify that removed abstractions do not contain remaining contractual responsibilities.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B41-0002"],"prerequisites":["Refactoring","Pattern lifecycle"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B29-0002","D13-DP-B42-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"refactoring_principle","source":"Pattern evolution and refactoring guidance","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B42-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern Implementation","concept":"Pattern implementation versus pattern intent","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can a Software Engineering Brain verify that code actually implements a claimed pattern?","answer":"Verify the pattern's intent, participants, collaboration, and required design forces rather than matching class or method names.","explanation":"Different languages and implementations can express the same pattern differently. Structural name matching is insufficient for reliable pattern recognition.","principle":"Pattern recognition should be semantic and behavioral, not merely lexical.","correct_example":"Recognizing Factory Method from polymorphic creator behavior even when classes do not use names such as Factory or Creator.","invalid_example":"Classifying any class named Factory as a Factory Method implementation.","problem":"Lexical similarity produces false pattern matches.","failure_mode":"The Brain retrieves incorrect pattern knowledge during code reasoning.","cause":"Pattern representation is reduced to naming conventions.","solution":"Model participants, dependencies, creation flow, variation points, and collaboration semantics.","alternative":"Name-based heuristics may be used only as retrieval hints.","tradeoff":"Semantic analysis requires more reasoning but improves pattern identification.","testing_guidance":"Test pattern recognition against renamed and structurally equivalent implementations.","verification_guidance":"Require intent and collaboration evidence before assigning a pattern classification.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B41-0002"],"prerequisites":["Pattern recognition","Code structure"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B42-0002","D13-DP-B44-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_analysis","source":"Design pattern recognition principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B42-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern Implementation","concept":"Pattern recognition uncertainty","knowledge_type":"UNKNOWN","question":"What should the Software Engineering Brain do when code partially resembles a known design pattern?","answer":"It should classify the evidence as uncertain or partial rather than asserting that the pattern is implemented.","explanation":"Real systems frequently combine pattern ideas, local idioms, framework mechanisms, and ad hoc designs. Similarity alone does not establish pattern identity.","principle":"Pattern classification must preserve uncertainty when intent or collaboration cannot be established.","correct_example":"Classify an implementation as `POSSIBLE_FACTORY_METHOD` when creation is polymorphic but the required extension semantics cannot yet be verified.","invalid_example":"Declare a Factory Method solely because a method returns an interface type.","problem":"Incomplete evidence is converted into an unsupported fact.","failure_mode":"Subsequent reasoning assumes a pattern contract that the code does not actually satisfy.","cause":"Recognition confidence is not represented.","solution":"Store pattern classification, evidence, confidence, and unresolved questions separately.","alternative":"UNKNOWN or PARTIALLY_MATCHING classification.","tradeoff":"Explicit uncertainty reduces false certainty but may require additional analysis.","testing_guidance":"Use targeted code inspection or tests to resolve pattern evidence.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence for each essential pattern participant and collaboration.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":["Pattern recognition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B43-0001","D13-DP-B50-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"reasoning_policy","source":"Evidence-driven software pattern recognition","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_REASONING_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B42-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern Implementation","concept":"Pattern documentation contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What information should a pattern record preserve for machine reasoning?","answer":"A machine-usable pattern record should preserve intent, context, problem, forces, participants, collaboration, applicability, consequences, implementation considerations, alternatives, failure modes, testing, security, performance considerations, version context, and provenance.","explanation":"Pattern names alone are insufficient for automated selection and reasoning.","principle":"Represent a pattern as a structured decision model rather than as a name and code template.","correct_example":"A record links Factory Method to its creation variation force, alternatives, failure modes, and verification rules.","invalid_example":"A record contains only `pattern_name = Factory Method`.","problem":"Insufficient semantic information prevents reliable retrieval and selection.","failure_mode":"The Brain retrieves a pattern without understanding when or why it applies.","cause":"Knowledge representation is too shallow.","solution":"Store pattern knowledge as structured, contextual, and evidence-backed records.","alternative":"Short textual summaries can be used as retrieval views but should not replace structured knowledge.","tradeoff":"Richer representation increases storage and maintenance requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test retrieval using intent, problem, symptom, and misuse queries.","verification_guidance":"Verify required semantic fields before dataset lock.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":["Knowledge representation"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B49-0001","D13-DP-B50-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_schema_policy","source":"D13 structured pattern knowledge model","evidence_level":"PROJECT_SPECIFICATION"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B43-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern Composition","concept":"Pattern composition","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does it mean to compose design patterns?","answer":"Pattern composition means using multiple patterns together so that each addresses a distinct but interacting design concern.","explanation":"Composition should preserve clear responsibilities and avoid introducing unnecessary layers. Patterns can complement each other when their forces differ.","principle":"Compose patterns only when each pattern solves an identifiable concern and their interactions remain understandable.","correct_example":"An Abstract Factory provides product families while Builder handles complex construction of one selected product.","invalid_example":"Stacking multiple creational patterns around a trivial object because each pattern appears reusable.","problem":"Pattern composition can multiply complexity.","failure_mode":"Developers cannot determine which abstraction owns a decision.","cause":"Pattern boundaries were selected without responsibility analysis.","solution":"Document responsibility and data/control flow for every participating pattern.","alternative":"Use a simpler single abstraction if it satisfies the requirements.","tradeoff":"Composition can increase flexibility but also increases cognitive and maintenance cost.","testing_guidance":"Test each pattern boundary and their integration separately.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every pattern contributes an independent design responsibility.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B40-0003"],"prerequisites":["Pattern composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B43-0002","D13-DP-B44-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_composition","source":"Design pattern composition principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B43-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern Composition","concept":"Pattern interaction ambiguity","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happens when multiple patterns appear to own the same design decision?","answer":"Overlapping pattern responsibilities can create ambiguity, duplicated logic, circular dependencies, and inconsistent behavior.","explanation":"The Brain should identify ownership of decisions such as creation, caching, lifecycle, validation, authorization, and transformation.","principle":"Every significant design decision should have an identifiable owner.","correct_example":"Builder owns construction sequencing while a factory owns implementation selection.","invalid_example":"Both a factory and builder independently decide product implementation and initialization defaults.","problem":"Creation responsibilities overlap.","failure_mode":"Configuration is applied inconsistently depending on entry path.","cause":"Pattern boundaries were not defined.","solution":"Assign each decision to one abstraction and document handoff points.","alternative":"Collapse patterns into one simpler abstraction if boundaries cannot be made coherent.","tradeoff":"Explicit ownership may require additional interfaces but reduces hidden behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test all creation entry points for consistent outcomes.","verification_guidance":"Trace each significant decision from input to final product and identify its owner.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B43-0001"],"prerequisites":["Pattern composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B41-0001","D13-DP-B44-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"architecture_failure_mode","source":"Pattern composition analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B43-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern Composition","concept":"Pattern composition verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should composed patterns be verified?","answer":"Verify each pattern's local intent and then verify the interaction contracts between patterns, including ownership, ordering, state flow, failure propagation, and lifecycle.","explanation":"Local correctness does not guarantee composition correctness.","principle":"Pattern composition requires both component-level and interaction-level verification.","correct_example":"Factory selection, Builder construction, and final product validation are tested independently and as one integrated path.","invalid_example":"Each pattern has unit tests but no test verifies their combined behavior.","problem":"Interactions remain unverified.","failure_mode":"Individually correct components produce incorrect system behavior when composed.","cause":"Verification stops at pattern boundaries.","solution":"Add integration and contract tests across pattern boundaries.","alternative":"Formal interface contracts where applicable.","tradeoff":"Integration testing adds effort but catches interaction failures.","testing_guidance":"Use integration, contract, negative, and lifecycle tests for composed patterns.","verification_guidance":"Trace every inter-pattern dependency and verify expected state transitions.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B43-0001","D13-DP-B43-0002"],"prerequisites":["Integration testing","Pattern composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B47-0001","D13-DP-B50-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"verification_method","source":"Composed design verification principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B44-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Structural Patterns","concept":"Structural pattern purpose","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What problem do structural design patterns address?","answer":"Structural design patterns address how classes and objects are composed to form larger structures while managing coupling, compatibility, abstraction boundaries, or representation.","explanation":"Structural patterns operate primarily on composition and relationships rather than object creation or algorithmic behavior.","principle":"Use structural patterns when relationships between components create a recurring design problem.","correct_example":"An Adapter converts one interface into another expected by a client without modifying the incompatible component.","invalid_example":"Adding a structural pattern when direct composition already expresses the relationship clearly.","problem":"Component relationships can become tightly coupled or incompatible.","failure_mode":"Changes in one component propagate unnecessarily through clients.","cause":"Structural dependency boundaries are poorly designed.","solution":"Introduce an appropriate structural abstraction only where it reduces meaningful coupling or compatibility problems.","alternative":"Direct composition or interface redesign.","tradeoff":"Structural abstractions improve separation but introduce additional indirection.","testing_guidance":"Test both component contracts and composition behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify the structural boundary preserves required contracts and failure semantics.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B43-0001"],"prerequisites":["Object composition","Design patterns"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B44-0002","D13-DP-B45-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru structural patterns classification","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN_CLASSIFICATION"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B44-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Structural Patterns","concept":"Structural pattern versus architecture","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How do structural design patterns differ from architectural patterns?","answer":"Structural design patterns generally address reusable relationships among classes or objects, while architectural patterns address broader system organization, boundaries, responsibilities, and deployment or interaction structure.","explanation":"The distinction is about scale and scope rather than an absolute technical boundary. A pattern can be expressed at different levels depending on its context.","principle":"Pattern selection should match the scale of the problem being solved.","correct_example":"Adapter addresses interface compatibility between components; an architectural style addresses organization of an entire application.","invalid_example":"Treating a small object composition technique as a complete system architecture.","problem":"A local solution is mistaken for a system-level design.","failure_mode":"Important architectural concerns remain unmodeled.","cause":"Pattern scale is ignored.","solution":"Classify the design problem by scope before selecting a pattern.","alternative":"Architecture pattern, component pattern, or object-level pattern depending on scope.","tradeoff":"Higher-level patterns constrain more of the system and therefore require broader evidence.","testing_guidance":"Test structural patterns locally and architecture patterns through system-level constraints.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the abstraction level matches the requirement scope.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":["Architecture concepts"],"related_records":["D14-AP-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_classification","source":"Refactoring.Guru pattern classification","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN_CLASSIFICATION"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B45-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Adapter","concept":"Adapter","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Adapter pattern?","answer":"Adapter allows an object with one interface to be used where another interface is expected by converting calls between the incompatible interfaces.","explanation":"Adapter is useful when an existing component is valuable but its interface does not match the contract required by the client. It can isolate compatibility logic from the client.","principle":"Translate one interface contract into another without requiring the incompatible component to change.","correct_example":"An application adapter translates a legacy payment API into the interface expected by the current payment service.","invalid_example":"Creating an Adapter when both sides already share a compatible contract.","problem":"Client and existing component have incompatible interfaces.","failure_mode":"Compatibility logic spreads throughout clients.","cause":"Interface mismatch is handled at every call site.","solution":"Centralize translation in an Adapter.","alternative":"Modify the existing interface, use a shared abstraction, or create a facade depending on intent.","tradeoff":"Adapter preserves an existing component but adds a translation boundary.","testing_guidance":"Test contract mapping, error translation, data conversion, and edge cases at the boundary.","verification_guidance":"Verify that all required client operations map correctly and that failure semantics are preserved.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":["Interfaces","Composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B45-0002","D13-DP-B46-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Adapter","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B45-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Adapter","concept":"Adapter contract translation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an Adapter translate besides method names?","answer":"An Adapter may need to translate data formats, units, error semantics, lifecycle expectations, nullability, ordering, timing assumptions, and other contract differences between interfaces.","explanation":"An adapter that only renames methods can still be incorrect if semantic contracts differ.","principle":"Interface compatibility includes behavioral and semantic contracts, not only method signatures.","correct_example":"An Adapter converts a legacy error code into the exception or result model expected by the client while preserving meaningful failure information.","invalid_example":"Mapping a legacy timeout failure to successful completion because the method signatures match.","problem":"Semantic differences remain hidden behind a syntactically compatible interface.","failure_mode":"Client logic receives incorrect assumptions about success or failure.","cause":"Adapter validation focuses only on signatures.","solution":"Document and test semantic mappings explicitly.","alternative":"A richer integration boundary or replacement component if semantics cannot be safely translated.","tradeoff":"Complete adaptation can require significant translation logic.","testing_guidance":"Test successful, failed, partial, timeout, malformed, and boundary inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify semantic equivalence or explicitly documented semantic transformation.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B45-0001"],"prerequisites":["Adapter","Contract semantics"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B45-0003","D13-DP-B47-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"integration_design","source":"Interface adaptation principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B45-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Adapter","concept":"Adapter anti-pattern","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"When is Adapter a poor solution?","answer":"Adapter is a poor solution when the underlying component fundamentally violates required semantics, security guarantees, or lifecycle assumptions that cannot be safely translated.","explanation":"Adapter should not hide incompatible behavior that clients cannot safely reason about.","principle":"Do not use interface translation to conceal fundamental semantic incompatibility.","correct_example":"Replace a legacy component when its authentication model cannot satisfy the application's required security boundary.","invalid_example":"Wrapping an unsafe component and exposing it as though it fully satisfies a stronger security contract.","problem":"Surface compatibility hides deeper incompatibility.","failure_mode":"Consumers trust guarantees that the adapted component cannot provide.","cause":"Structural compatibility is mistaken for semantic compatibility.","solution":"Reject or replace the incompatible component when required guarantees cannot be preserved.","alternative":"Component replacement, redesign, or stronger isolation boundary.","tradeoff":"Replacement may cost more initially but avoids false compatibility.","testing_guidance":"Test security, failure, lifecycle, and consistency properties in addition to interface calls.","verification_guidance":"Verify every advertised guarantee independently of method-signature compatibility.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B45-0002"],"prerequisites":["Adapter","Security contracts"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B46-0002","D13-DP-B47-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"integration_failure_analysis","source":"Adapter semantic compatibility analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B46-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Adapter","concept":"Object Adapter versus class Adapter","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between object and class forms of Adapter?","answer":"An object Adapter uses composition to hold an adaptee instance, while a class Adapter uses inheritance mechanisms where the language supports the required multiple-interface or inheritance structure.","explanation":"The exact implementation options depend on language capabilities and type systems. Composition often allows adapting objects at runtime without requiring inheritance relationships.","principle":"Choose the adaptation mechanism according to language constraints, substitution needs, and lifecycle ownership.","correct_example":"A composed Adapter receives an adaptee through dependency injection and translates its interface.","invalid_example":"Using inheritance solely because it resembles a classic pattern diagram when composition better expresses ownership.","problem":"Implementation technique is selected without considering language semantics.","failure_mode":"Adapter becomes rigidly coupled to a concrete implementation hierarchy.","cause":"Classical diagrams are copied literally across languages.","solution":"Map pattern intent to language-appropriate mechanisms.","alternative":"Composition-based Adapter.","tradeoff":"Inheritance may simplify some integrations while composition generally allows more flexible object substitution.","testing_guidance":"Test substitution, lifecycle, and contract behavior for the selected implementation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the chosen mechanism preserves intended adaptation semantics in the target language.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B45-0001"],"prerequisites":["Adapter","Inheritance","Composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B46-0002","D13-DP-B49-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_implementation","source":"Adapter implementation variants","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B46-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Adapter","concept":"Adapter security translation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security concerns should an Adapter preserve?","answer":"An Adapter should preserve or explicitly transform authorization, identity, confidentiality, integrity, validation, and error-handling semantics rather than silently weakening them.","explanation":"An integration boundary can unintentionally change trust assumptions when translating protocols or interfaces.","principle":"Security semantics are part of the contract being adapted.","correct_example":"An adapter maps an authenticated principal into a downstream authorization model while preserving tenant boundaries.","invalid_example":"An adapter drops tenant identity because the legacy API does not support it and silently uses a shared privileged account.","problem":"Security context is lost during translation.","failure_mode":"Requests execute with broader authority than intended.","cause":"Only functional parameters were adapted.","solution":"Model security context explicitly and fail closed when required security semantics cannot be preserved.","alternative":"Gateway or dedicated security boundary.","tradeoff":"Explicit security translation can require additional integration logic.","testing_guidance":"Test identity propagation, authorization failures, tenant isolation, and privilege boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Verify security invariants across both sides of the adapter boundary.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B45-0002"],"prerequisites":["Adapter","Authorization"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B45-0003","D13-DP-B47-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"security_integration","source":"Adapter security boundary principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B47-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Adapter","concept":"Adapter testing strategy","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should be tested in an Adapter implementation?","answer":"Adapter tests should cover contract mapping, successful translation, invalid input, boundary values, error translation, resource failures, timeout behavior, security context, and any documented semantic differences.","explanation":"Adapter correctness exists at the boundary between two contracts, so happy-path testing alone is insufficient.","principle":"Test the translation contract, including failure and security semantics.","correct_example":"A test verifies that a downstream timeout is translated into the client's documented timeout result rather than a false success.","invalid_example":"Testing only that the adapter method can be called without exceptions.","problem":"Translation failures remain undetected.","failure_mode":"Production behavior diverges from either interface contract.","cause":"Tests verify invocation rather than semantics.","solution":"Define explicit mapping assertions for inputs, outputs, errors, and security context.","alternative":"Contract testing between adapter and each side.","tradeoff":"More boundary tests increase effort but improve integration reliability.","testing_guidance":"Include positive, negative, boundary, failure-injection, and security cases.","verification_guidance":"Map each adapted contract requirement to at least one verification method.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B45-0002","D13-DP-B46-0002"],"prerequisites":["Adapter testing","Contract testing"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B43-0003","D13-DP-B47-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"testing_guidance","source":"Adapter contract testing principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B47-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Adapter","concept":"Adapter failure propagation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should an Adapter preserve meaningful downstream failures?","answer":"Suppressing or incorrectly translating downstream failures can cause clients to make unsafe or incorrect decisions because the adapter presents a false representation of system state.","explanation":"An adapter should translate errors according to documented client semantics while retaining enough information for correct handling and observability.","principle":"Failure translation must preserve decision-relevant meaning.","correct_example":"A downstream authorization failure remains distinguishable from a transient transport failure after adaptation.","invalid_example":"All downstream errors are converted to a generic success or empty result.","problem":"Different failure classes become indistinguishable.","failure_mode":"Clients retry, authorize, or continue incorrectly.","cause":"Adapter treats errors as implementation details rather than contract information.","solution":"Define explicit error mappings and preserve diagnostic context where appropriate.","alternative":"Expose a richer result type or use a dedicated integration boundary.","tradeoff":"Richer failure models can increase client complexity but improve correctness.","testing_guidance":"Test each relevant downstream failure category and verify client-visible mapping.","verification_guidance":"Verify that safety-critical distinctions survive the adapter boundary.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B45-0002"],"prerequisites":["Adapter","Error semantics"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B47-0001","D13-DP-B48-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"failure_semantics","source":"Integration error propagation principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B48-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Bridge","concept":"Bridge","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Bridge pattern?","answer":"Bridge separates an abstraction from its implementation so that the two can vary independently.","explanation":"Bridge is useful when two dimensions of variation would otherwise produce a growing inheritance combination. Composition connects the abstraction to an implementation interface.","principle":"Separate independent axes of variation rather than encoding their combinations in one inheritance hierarchy.","correct_example":"A rendering abstraction varies independently from rendering implementations for different platforms.","invalid_example":"Introducing Bridge when there is only one stable abstraction and one implementation with no expected independent variation.","problem":"Independent dimensions of change become tightly coupled.","failure_mode":"Adding one variation requires creating many combination classes.","cause":"Variation axes are encoded directly into inheritance.","solution":"Separate abstraction and implementation through composition when independent variation is real.","alternative":"Simple inheritance or direct composition for small stable cases.","tradeoff":"Bridge reduces combinatorial inheritance growth but introduces additional indirection.","testing_guidance":"Test each abstraction implementation combination relevant to supported configurations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that abstraction and implementation can change independently without violating contracts.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":["Composition","Polymorphism"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B48-0002","D13-DP-B49-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Bridge","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B48-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Bridge","concept":"Bridge variation axes","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How does Bridge reduce combinatorial class growth?","answer":"Bridge separates two independently varying dimensions so that implementations for one dimension can be combined with abstractions from the other without creating a concrete subclass for every combination.","explanation":"The value comes from independent variation. If the dimensions do not actually vary independently, the abstraction may be unnecessary.","principle":"Model independent variation as independent abstractions connected through composition.","correct_example":"Multiple notification abstractions use multiple transport implementations without creating a class for every notification-transport combination.","invalid_example":"Creating a Bridge where only one dimension changes and the other is fixed.","problem":"Inheritance encodes combinations rather than dimensions.","failure_mode":"Class count grows approximately with combinations of variation choices.","cause":"Variation axes are coupled structurally.","solution":"Separate the axes when independent evolution is expected.","alternative":"Direct composition or simple inheritance.","tradeoff":"Bridge reduces combination classes but increases conceptual indirection.","testing_guidance":"Test representative combinations and independently changing implementations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that both dimensions can vary without requiring modifications to the other abstraction.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B48-0001"],"prerequisites":["Bridge","Variation analysis"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B48-0003","D13-DP-B50-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_reasoning","source":"Bridge variation-axis analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B48-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Bridge","concept":"Bridge misuse","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"When can Bridge be unnecessary?","answer":"Bridge can be unnecessary when the implementation and abstraction do not have independent variation, when there are few combinations, or when direct composition already expresses the relationship clearly.","explanation":"Bridge adds a deliberate abstraction boundary. Its value should be assessed against the complexity it introduces.","principle":"Do not introduce independent variation abstractions when independent variation is not a meaningful requirement.","correct_example":"Use direct composition when there is one implementation and no credible second variation axis.","invalid_example":"Creating abstraction and implementation hierarchies for two classes that will always evolve together.","problem":"The design separates dimensions that are not actually independent.","failure_mode":"Additional interfaces and indirection obscure a simple relationship.","cause":"Pattern structure was applied mechanically.","solution":"Evaluate expected variation and change coupling before adopting Bridge.","alternative":"Direct composition or inheritance.","tradeoff":"Simplification reduces abstraction cost but may require restructuring if independent variation later emerges.","testing_guidance":"Test current behavior and document expected variation assumptions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that independent change is a real requirement rather than a hypothetical possibility.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B48-0001"],"prerequisites":["Bridge"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B41-0003","D13-DP-B49-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_failure_analysis","source":"Bridge tradeoff analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B49-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Composite","concept":"Composite","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Composite pattern?","answer":"Composite composes objects into tree structures so that clients can treat individual objects and compositions uniformly through a common component abstraction.","explanation":"Composite is useful when the domain naturally contains part-whole hierarchies and operations should often apply recursively across those hierarchies.","principle":"Represent individual and grouped elements through a common abstraction when uniform recursive treatment is valuable.","correct_example":"A file-system model represents both files and directories through a common component interface, with directories containing components.","invalid_example":"Using Composite for unrelated objects merely because they can share a method name.","problem":"Part-whole hierarchies require separate client logic for leaves and groups.","failure_mode":"Clients repeatedly inspect object types to decide whether to recurse.","cause":"The hierarchy lacks a uniform component contract.","solution":"Use Composite when leaf and composite operations can meaningfully share a common abstraction.","alternative":"Explicit tree model with separate node types when uniform treatment is not appropriate.","tradeoff":"Composite simplifies recursive clients but can make some operations difficult to constrain uniformly.","testing_guidance":"Test empty composites, deep trees, repeated references if allowed, leaf operations, and recursive failure behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify tree invariants and the contract shared by leaf and composite components.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":["Tree structures","Composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B49-0002","D13-DP-B50-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Composite","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B49-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Composite","concept":"Composite invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariants should a Composite structure maintain?","answer":"A Composite should define and enforce structural rules such as valid child types, ownership, cycle constraints, parent relationships, and allowed mutation operations where those rules apply.","explanation":"Recursive structures can become invalid if arbitrary objects or cyclic references are admitted without explicit semantics.","principle":"Tree-like abstractions require explicit structural invariants.","correct_example":"A directory node rejects a child that would introduce a cycle into a hierarchy that is required to be acyclic.","invalid_example":"A Composite permits arbitrary parent-child references even though algorithms assume a tree.","problem":"The data structure can violate assumptions required by traversal logic.","failure_mode":"Infinite recursion, duplicated traversal, or incorrect aggregation occurs.","cause":"Structural invariants are implicit.","solution":"Validate mutations and define ownership/reference semantics explicitly.","alternative":"Immutable tree construction when mutation is not required.","tradeoff":"Validation adds cost but protects downstream traversal assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, deep, cyclic, duplicate, invalid-child, and mutation scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify all documented structural invariants after every permitted mutation.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B49-0001"],"prerequisites":["Composite","Tree invariants"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B49-0003","D13-DP-B50-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"data_structure_verification","source":"Composite structural invariant reasoning","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B49-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Composite","concept":"Composite performance risk","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What performance risk can arise from recursive Composite operations?","answer":"Operations that recursively traverse a large Composite structure can become expensive in proportion to the number of visited nodes and may consume substantial call-stack or memory resources depending on implementation.","explanation":"The exact complexity depends on the operation, structure, caching, traversal strategy, and language runtime. Performance must therefore be analyzed from the actual operation rather than from the pattern name.","principle":"Treat recursive structural traversal as an explicit performance concern when hierarchy size can grow significantly.","correct_example":"A large hierarchy uses an iterative traversal or bounded operation where recursion depth could exceed safe limits.","invalid_example":"Assuming Composite automatically provides constant-time aggregate operations.","problem":"Recursive work is hidden behind a uniform component interface.","failure_mode":"Large structures cause latency, memory pressure, or recursion failures.","cause":"Traversal cost is not modeled.","solution":"Analyze traversal complexity and use iterative traversal, caching, indexing, or bounded evaluation when justified.","alternative":"Flat representation or indexed structure for workloads dominated by aggregate queries.","tradeoff":"Optimization can increase memory or implementation complexity.","testing_guidance":"Measure traversal behavior on representative and worst-case hierarchy sizes when performance matters.","verification_guidance":"Record actual measured performance separately from theoretical complexity and assumptions.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B49-0001"],"prerequisites":["Composite","Algorithmic complexity"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B50-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"performance_reasoning","source":"Recursive composite traversal analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B50-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Structural Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Adapter versus Bridge","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the conceptual difference between Adapter and Bridge?","answer":"Adapter primarily addresses an existing interface incompatibility, while Bridge is designed to separate abstraction and implementation so that both can evolve independently.","explanation":"Adapter usually connects pre-existing interfaces that do not match. Bridge establishes a deliberate separation between two variation dimensions in the design.","principle":"Use Adapter for compatibility translation and Bridge for independent variation axes.","correct_example":"Wrap a legacy API with Adapter; design a rendering abstraction and implementation hierarchy independently with Bridge.","invalid_example":"Calling any composition-based wrapper a Bridge without an independent abstraction-implementation variation problem.","problem":"Pattern intent is confused because both may use composition.","failure_mode":"The wrong design rationale is documented and future changes are misdirected.","cause":"Implementation structure is mistaken for pattern intent.","solution":"Classify the design by original problem and expected change forces.","alternative":"Direct composition if neither compatibility translation nor independent variation requires a named pattern.","tradeoff":"Both patterns add boundaries, but they optimize different design concerns.","testing_guidance":"Test interface translation for Adapter and independent variation for Bridge.","verification_guidance":"Verify the original design force rather than relying on structural resemblance.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B45-0001","D13-DP-B48-0001"],"prerequisites":["Adapter","Bridge"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B50-0002","D13-DP-B42-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_comparison","source":"Structural pattern analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B50-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Structural Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Structural pattern decision process","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What decision process should the Software Engineering Brain use when considering Adapter, Bridge, or Composite?","answer":"Identify the dominant structural problem: incompatible interfaces suggest Adapter; independently varying abstraction and implementation suggest Bridge; recursive part-whole hierarchy suggests Composite. Then evaluate simpler alternatives, contracts, failure behavior, security, performance, and testing requirements.","explanation":"The same implementation technique can appear in different patterns, so semantic problem identification must precede pattern classification.","principle":"Choose structural patterns from domain structure and change forces rather than implementation appearance.","correct_example":"A legacy interface mismatch is classified as Adapter even though the adapter uses composition internally.","invalid_example":"A wrapper is classified as Bridge simply because it contains another object.","problem":"Pattern selection is driven by implementation shape.","failure_mode":"The Brain applies incorrect pattern-specific reasoning.","cause":"Intent is ignored.","solution":"Use problem-force-intent analysis followed by structural evidence.","alternative":"No named pattern when direct composition is sufficient.","tradeoff":"Explicit analysis increases reasoning effort but reduces pattern misuse.","testing_guidance":"Test the invariant that motivated the structural abstraction.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence for the selected pattern's defining intent and collaboration.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B45-0001","D13-DP-B48-0001","D13-DP-B49-0001"],"prerequisites":["Structural design patterns"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B42-0001","D13-DP-B50-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"reasoning_procedure","source":"Structural pattern selection methodology","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B50-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Structural Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Pattern knowledge retrieval queries","knowledge_type":"RETRIEVAL_CASE","question":"What query variations should retrieve Adapter, Bridge, and Composite knowledge?","answer":"Retrieval should recognize intent-based queries such as interface mismatch, legacy API wrapping, independent implementation variation, avoiding inheritance combinations, tree hierarchy, part-whole composition, recursive traversal, and pattern misuse.","explanation":"A Software Engineering Brain should retrieve patterns from problem descriptions and symptoms, not only exact pattern names.","principle":"Pattern retrieval should be semantic, synonym-aware, symptom-aware, and implementation-aware.","correct_example":"The query 'I need to wrap an incompatible legacy API without changing clients' retrieves Adapter records.","invalid_example":"Only the exact query 'Adapter pattern' retrieves Adapter knowledge.","problem":"Exact-name retrieval misses natural engineering questions.","failure_mode":"Relevant design knowledge is not retrieved during coding or debugging.","cause":"Retrieval index is name-centric.","solution":"Index pattern intent, problems, symptoms, alternatives, failure modes, and implementation characteristics.","alternative":"Hybrid lexical and semantic retrieval.","tradeoff":"Broader retrieval improves recall but can increase irrelevant matches and requires ranking/verification.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate exact-name, conceptual, natural-language, debugging, and design-decision queries.","verification_guidance":"Track expected relevant records, retrieved records, missed records, and false retrievals.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B42-0003"],"prerequisites":["Retrieval systems","Design pattern knowledge"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B49-0001","D13-DP-B50-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"retrieval_design","source":"D13 retrieval requirements","evidence_level":"PROJECT_SPECIFICATION"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B51-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Decorator","concept":"Decorator","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Decorator pattern?","answer":"Decorator attaches additional responsibilities to an object dynamically by wrapping it with objects that implement a compatible interface.","explanation":"Decorator supports incremental behavior composition without requiring a separate subclass for every combination of responsibilities.","principle":"Add behavior through composition while preserving a compatible client-facing abstraction.","correct_example":"Wrap a data stream with buffering, compression, and encryption decorators where each layer preserves the stream contract.","invalid_example":"Creating decorators that expose unrelated interfaces and therefore cannot be composed uniformly.","problem":"Behavior combinations would otherwise require many subclasses or modifications to the original class.","failure_mode":"Subclass combinations grow rapidly or the original class accumulates unrelated responsibilities.","cause":"Behavior variation is encoded directly into the base implementation hierarchy.","solution":"Use compatible wrappers when dynamic behavior composition is a genuine requirement.","alternative":"Inheritance, middleware, composition through explicit services, or direct implementation when variation is small.","tradeoff":"Decorator improves composability but can create deep wrapper chains and harder debugging.","testing_guidance":"Test each decorator independently and representative combinations of decorators.","verification_guidance":"Verify interface compatibility, ordering semantics, failure propagation, and preservation of required invariants.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B43-0001"],"prerequisites":["Composition","Polymorphism"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B51-0002","D13-DP-B52-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Decorator","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B51-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Decorator","concept":"Decorator versus inheritance","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why can Decorator be preferable to inheritance for combinable behavior?","answer":"Decorator can compose behavior dynamically and independently, whereas inheritance generally fixes behavior through a class hierarchy and can require a separate subclass for combinations.","explanation":"Decorator is particularly useful when multiple optional responsibilities can be combined in different orders or configurations.","principle":"Use composition when behavior combinations vary independently and runtime composition is valuable.","correct_example":"A request processor is wrapped with logging, metrics, retry, and tracing layers selected by configuration.","invalid_example":"Creating dozens of subclasses for every combination of optional processing behaviors.","problem":"Inheritance encodes combinations as class types.","failure_mode":"Subclass count and coupling increase as behavior combinations grow.","cause":"Independent behavior dimensions are represented by inheritance.","solution":"Use composable wrappers when the contract permits it.","alternative":"Strategy or explicit pipeline composition when behavior replacement rather than layering is required.","tradeoff":"Decorator adds runtime indirection and ordering concerns.","testing_guidance":"Test individual decorators, order-sensitive combinations, and repeated decoration where permitted.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each wrapper preserves the required base contract.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B51-0001"],"prerequisites":["Inheritance","Composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B51-0003","D13-DP-B58-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_comparison","source":"Decorator composition principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B51-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Decorator","concept":"Decorator ordering","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why does decorator ordering matter?","answer":"Decorator behavior can be order-dependent because each wrapper receives the output, failures, timing, or side effects of the inner component.","explanation":"Logging, retry, caching, compression, authorization, validation, and transformation layers can produce materially different behavior depending on their order.","principle":"Treat decorator ordering as part of the design contract whenever wrappers are not commutative.","correct_example":"Authorization executes before an operation is retried or its result is cached when that ordering is required by the security model.","invalid_example":"Randomly reordering decorators because every wrapper is assumed to be behaviorally independent.","problem":"Wrapper order changes system semantics.","failure_mode":"Security, correctness, latency, or caching behavior changes unexpectedly.","cause":"Ordering constraints are undocumented.","solution":"Declare ordering requirements and test valid compositions.","alternative":"Explicit pipeline with named stages and ordering rules.","tradeoff":"Explicit ordering improves predictability but reduces unrestricted composition.","testing_guidance":"Test valid and invalid ordering combinations.","verification_guidance":"Verify ordering invariants for security-sensitive and stateful decorators.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B51-0001"],"prerequisites":["Decorator","Composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B52-0002","D13-DP-B53-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"composition_reasoning","source":"Decorator ordering analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B52-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Decorator","concept":"Decorator contract preservation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must a Decorator preserve?","answer":"A Decorator should preserve the required client-facing contract while adding its intended responsibility, including relevant return behavior, error semantics, lifecycle expectations, and invariants.","explanation":"A wrapper that changes the contract unintentionally is no longer a transparent decorator for clients expecting the original abstraction.","principle":"Additional behavior must not silently invalidate the contract being decorated.","correct_example":"A logging decorator records operation metadata while preserving the wrapped component's result and documented failures.","invalid_example":"A logging decorator catches every exception and returns success to simplify logging.","problem":"Decorator behavior changes the underlying contract.","failure_mode":"Clients receive incorrect results or failure semantics.","cause":"The decorator treats cross-cutting behavior as permission to rewrite functional semantics.","solution":"Define which behavior may change and which contract properties must remain invariant.","alternative":"Facade or Adapter if the intended contract is intentionally different.","tradeoff":"Strict contract preservation limits decorator transformations but improves substitutability.","testing_guidance":"Compare decorated and undecorated behavior for contract invariants.","verification_guidance":"Verify all preserved contract properties explicitly.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B51-0001"],"prerequisites":["Decorator","Contracts"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B47-0002","D13-DP-B52-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"verification_principle","source":"Decorator contract analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B52-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Decorator","concept":"Decorator failure propagation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should failures propagate through Decorators?","answer":"Failures should follow the documented contract of the decorated abstraction unless a decorator explicitly owns a defined transformation such as retry, fallback, translation, or recovery.","explanation":"Silently swallowing failures can make downstream state appear valid when the underlying operation failed.","principle":"Failure transformation must be intentional, documented, and testable.","correct_example":"A retry decorator retries only documented transient failures and ultimately propagates the failure when retry policy is exhausted.","invalid_example":"A decorator catches every exception and returns a default value regardless of the operation's contract.","problem":"Failure semantics become ambiguous.","failure_mode":"Callers cannot distinguish success from partial or failed execution.","cause":"Decorators modify errors without explicit policy.","solution":"Define failure classes and allowed transformations for each decorator.","alternative":"Dedicated error-handling boundary when failure policy is too complex for a simple decorator.","tradeoff":"Explicit failure policies increase implementation complexity but improve reliability.","testing_guidance":"Test transient, permanent, cancellation, timeout, and unexpected failures.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every transformed failure has a documented reason and observable outcome.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B51-0003"],"prerequisites":["Error handling","Decorator"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B47-0002","D13-DP-B52-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"failure_semantics","source":"Decorator failure-handling principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B52-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Decorator","concept":"Decorator performance overhead","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What performance overhead can Decorator introduce?","answer":"Each wrapper can add call indirection, processing, allocation, synchronization, logging, transformation, or other work. A long decorator chain can therefore increase latency and resource usage.","explanation":"The actual cost depends on implementation and workload and should not be assumed from the pattern alone.","principle":"Measure or estimate wrapper costs according to the real execution path when performance is material.","correct_example":"A high-throughput service measures the overhead of tracing, metrics, retry, compression, and encryption layers independently and in combination.","invalid_example":"Assuming decorators are performance-neutral because each wrapper only delegates one call.","problem":"Layered behavior adds hidden execution cost.","failure_mode":"Latency or resource consumption exceeds requirements.","cause":"Composed wrapper cost was not analyzed.","solution":"Profile relevant compositions and remove unnecessary layers or combine compatible operations where justified.","alternative":"Explicit optimized pipeline.","tradeoff":"Optimization may reduce composability or observability.","testing_guidance":"Use representative workload measurements; distinguish measured results from estimates.","verification_guidance":"Record workload, environment, measurement method, and observed results before making performance claims.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B51-0001"],"prerequisites":["Performance analysis"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B58-0002","D13-DP-B60-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"performance_reasoning","source":"Decorator execution-cost analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B53-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Facade","concept":"Facade","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Facade pattern?","answer":"Facade provides a simplified interface to a larger subsystem or group of components.","explanation":"Facade reduces the amount of subsystem knowledge required by clients and can establish a controlled entry point into complex functionality.","principle":"Expose the operations clients actually need while hiding unnecessary subsystem coordination details.","correct_example":"A deployment facade coordinates configuration validation, artifact preparation, rollout, and health checks behind a single application-level operation.","invalid_example":"A facade that merely renames one method without simplifying subsystem interaction.","problem":"Clients must understand many subsystem components and their coordination order.","failure_mode":"Subsystem knowledge spreads throughout the application.","cause":"No stable high-level boundary exists.","solution":"Provide a facade that owns or coordinates the intended high-level workflow.","alternative":"Direct subsystem access when clients genuinely need fine-grained control.","tradeoff":"Facade simplifies usage but can become an overly broad abstraction if responsibilities are not controlled.","testing_guidance":"Test facade workflows and important subsystem failure paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify that facade operations preserve required subsystem invariants and failure semantics.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":["Subsystem design","Composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B53-0002","D13-DP-B54-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Facade","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B53-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Facade","concept":"Facade versus Adapter","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does Facade differ from Adapter?","answer":"Adapter changes one interface into another compatible interface, whereas Facade provides a simpler interface to a subsystem without necessarily translating an incompatible client contract.","explanation":"Both can wrap other components, but their primary design forces differ.","principle":"Use Adapter for interface compatibility and Facade for subsystem simplification and coordination.","correct_example":"Wrap a legacy API with Adapter; expose a simplified multi-service workflow through Facade.","invalid_example":"Calling a multi-component orchestration facade an Adapter solely because it delegates calls.","problem":"Pattern intent is confused by superficial wrapper similarity.","failure_mode":"Design reasoning and maintenance expectations become inaccurate.","cause":"Pattern identity is inferred from delegation rather than purpose.","solution":"Classify by client problem and contract transformation.","alternative":"Direct composition if no meaningful boundary is required.","tradeoff":"Facade may reduce client coupling while Adapter specifically isolates interface incompatibility.","testing_guidance":"Test translated contracts for Adapter and subsystem workflow contracts for Facade.","verification_guidance":"Verify the defining intent rather than the presence of wrapper code.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B45-0001","D13-DP-B53-0001"],"prerequisites":["Adapter","Facade"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B50-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_comparison","source":"Adapter and Facade distinction","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B53-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Facade","concept":"Facade scope control","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"How can a Facade become an architectural bottleneck?","answer":"A Facade can become problematic when it accumulates unrelated operations, business rules, state management, authorization, and orchestration responsibilities without clear boundaries.","explanation":"A facade should simplify subsystem access, not become an unbounded application-wide god object.","principle":"Keep facade responsibilities aligned with a coherent subsystem or client-facing workflow boundary.","correct_example":"Separate deployment, billing, and identity facades when their responsibilities and change drivers differ significantly.","invalid_example":"One global facade exposes every operation across every subsystem.","problem":"The facade becomes a centralized dependency hub.","failure_mode":"Small changes create broad coupling and regression risk.","cause":"Facade scope expands without responsibility boundaries.","solution":"Split facades by coherent subsystem or workflow and delegate domain responsibilities appropriately.","alternative":"Application services or bounded subsystem interfaces.","tradeoff":"More facade boundaries require additional interfaces but reduce coupling concentration.","testing_guidance":"Test facade boundaries and dependency direction.","verification_guidance":"Review facade responsibilities for cohesion and unnecessary cross-subsystem dependencies.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B53-0001"],"prerequisites":["Facade","Cohesion","Coupling"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B54-0002","D13-DP-B59-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"architecture_failure_mode","source":"Facade scope analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B54-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Facade","concept":"Facade security boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Can a Facade act as a security boundary?","answer":"A Facade can centralize security checks and establish a controlled entry point, but it should not automatically be assumed to provide a complete security boundary unless all relevant access paths are controlled and security invariants are enforced.","explanation":"Subsystem components may remain directly accessible through other paths. Security depends on actual reachable interfaces and enforcement mechanisms.","principle":"A facade is a structural simplification mechanism; security-boundary status requires explicit architectural verification.","correct_example":"A subsystem is inaccessible externally and all supported operations pass through a facade that enforces authorization and tenant context.","invalid_example":"Assuming the facade provides authorization while internal APIs remain directly reachable and unauthenticated.","problem":"Structural encapsulation is mistaken for security enforcement.","failure_mode":"Attackers or unauthorized callers bypass facade checks.","cause":"Reachability and enforcement were not verified.","solution":"Control all relevant access paths and independently verify authorization invariants.","alternative":"Dedicated gateway or policy enforcement point where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Centralized enforcement simplifies policy but can become a critical dependency.","testing_guidance":"Test direct bypass attempts, authorization failures, tenant isolation, and privilege boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual network/process/module reachability rather than relying on intended usage.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B53-0001"],"prerequisites":["Facade","Authorization"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B46-0002","D13-DP-B54-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"security_architecture","source":"Facade security-boundary analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B54-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Facade","concept":"Facade failure coordination","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What failure problem occurs when a Facade coordinates multiple subsystem operations?","answer":"Partial failure can leave some subsystem operations completed while later operations fail, requiring explicit consistency, rollback, compensation, or recovery semantics.","explanation":"A facade that orchestrates multiple operations must not imply atomicity unless the underlying system actually provides it.","principle":"Facade workflows must define partial-failure semantics explicitly.","correct_example":"A deployment facade records completed stages and invokes documented compensation or recovery when a later stage fails.","invalid_example":"Returning a single failure result as if all previous side effects were automatically rolled back.","problem":"Multi-step orchestration is mistaken for atomic execution.","failure_mode":"System state becomes partially changed while clients assume full failure or full rollback.","cause":"Transactional semantics are unspecified.","solution":"Model transaction, compensation, idempotency, and recovery behavior explicitly.","alternative":"Transactional workflow engine where required.","tradeoff":"Compensation and recovery add state and implementation complexity.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures at each workflow stage and verify resulting state.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual post-failure state rather than only return values.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B53-0001"],"prerequisites":["Distributed workflows","Failure handling"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B54-0003","D13-DP-B60-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"failure_analysis","source":"Facade orchestration failure semantics","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B54-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Facade","concept":"Facade verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a Facade workflow be verified?","answer":"Verify input validation, operation ordering, subsystem contracts, failure propagation, partial-state behavior, authorization, idempotency where required, and final externally visible state.","explanation":"Facade correctness depends on coordination across subsystem boundaries.","principle":"Verify the workflow's observable outcomes and intermediate failure semantics, not merely facade method invocation.","correct_example":"A deployment facade test injects failure during health verification and confirms documented rollback or degraded-state behavior.","invalid_example":"Testing only that the facade returns a success object for a successful mock path.","problem":"Coordination errors remain hidden.","failure_mode":"Production workflow diverges from the facade's advertised contract.","cause":"Tests ignore intermediate subsystem behavior.","solution":"Use integration, contract, failure-injection, and state verification tests.","alternative":"Workflow-level formal specification where applicable.","tradeoff":"More realistic tests require greater setup but provide stronger evidence.","testing_guidance":"Test happy path, every meaningful failure stage, authorization, timeout, retry, and recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual state transitions and side effects.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B54-0002"],"prerequisites":["Facade","Integration testing"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B43-0003","D13-DP-B60-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"verification_method","source":"Facade workflow verification","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B55-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Flyweight","concept":"Flyweight","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Flyweight pattern?","answer":"Flyweight shares common intrinsic object state across multiple logical instances to reduce repeated memory or object representation costs.","explanation":"The pattern separates shareable intrinsic state from context-specific extrinsic state supplied by clients or callers.","principle":"Share immutable or safely shareable intrinsic state while keeping context-dependent state external.","correct_example":"Many rendered text glyphs share immutable glyph metadata while position and color remain external per occurrence.","invalid_example":"Sharing mutable context-specific state between unrelated logical objects.","problem":"Large numbers of similar objects duplicate expensive shared state.","failure_mode":"Memory usage or initialization cost grows unnecessarily.","cause":"Intrinsic and extrinsic state are not separated.","solution":"Extract genuinely shareable state and manage contextual state externally.","alternative":"Compact data structures, interning, caching, or specialized representations.","tradeoff":"Flyweight can reduce memory but adds indirection and state-management complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test identity independence, state isolation, cache behavior, and concurrent access where applicable.","verification_guidance":"Verify that shared state is immutable or safely synchronized and that extrinsic state never leaks between logical instances.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B43-0001"],"prerequisites":["Object sharing","State separation"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B55-0002","D13-DP-B56-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Flyweight","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B55-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Flyweight","concept":"Intrinsic versus extrinsic state","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should intrinsic and extrinsic state be distinguished in Flyweight?","answer":"Intrinsic state is stable and shareable across logical instances; extrinsic state varies by usage context and should be supplied externally rather than stored in the shared Flyweight.","explanation":"The separation is what makes sharing safe. The exact boundary depends on domain semantics.","principle":"Share only state whose semantics remain valid for every consumer of the shared instance.","correct_example":"A glyph's immutable shape is intrinsic while screen position is extrinsic.","invalid_example":"Storing the current user's authorization context inside a globally shared Flyweight.","problem":"Context-dependent state becomes globally shared.","failure_mode":"One logical instance observes another instance's state.","cause":"Intrinsic and extrinsic state were incorrectly classified.","solution":"Move contextual state to the caller or a context object.","alternative":"Independent instances when state cannot be safely separated.","tradeoff":"External state passing increases call complexity but preserves isolation.","testing_guidance":"Test multiple consumers with different extrinsic state simultaneously.","verification_guidance":"Verify that shared objects contain no mutable context-specific state unless explicitly synchronized and semantically shared.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B55-0001"],"prerequisites":["Flyweight","State modeling"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B56-0002","D13-DP-B58-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_semantics","source":"Flyweight state-separation principles","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B55-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Flyweight","concept":"Flyweight misuse","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"When should Flyweight not be used?","answer":"Flyweight should not be introduced when objects are few, state is mostly context-specific, sharing provides negligible savings, or synchronization and indirection cost outweigh the benefit.","explanation":"The pattern is an optimization-oriented structural technique and should be justified by actual object-volume or representation constraints.","principle":"Do not trade straightforward ownership for shared-state complexity without a meaningful resource problem.","correct_example":"Avoid Flyweight for a small number of independently mutable domain objects.","invalid_example":"Introducing a global Flyweight cache for ten lightweight objects solely to demonstrate the pattern.","problem":"Optimization complexity is introduced without a material resource constraint.","failure_mode":"Code becomes harder to reason about with no meaningful benefit.","cause":"Pattern use is driven by catalog completeness rather than requirements.","solution":"Measure or establish a credible resource problem before adopting Flyweight.","alternative":"Ordinary object instances or compact value representations.","tradeoff":"Direct instances use more memory in some cases but simplify state ownership.","testing_guidance":"Test resource usage only when the optimization is justified by workload requirements.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence for the memory or allocation problem being addressed.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B55-0002"],"prerequisites":["Flyweight","Performance reasoning"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B52-0003","D13-DP-B56-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_misuse_analysis","source":"Flyweight tradeoff analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B56-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Flyweight","concept":"Flyweight cache lifecycle","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What lifecycle concerns arise when Flyweights are stored in a cache?","answer":"The cache requires explicit policies for creation, retention, eviction, synchronization, memory limits, and stale-state handling when the shared objects are not permanently valid.","explanation":"A Flyweight factory or cache can become a long-lived resource owner even though the conceptual pattern focuses on sharing state.","principle":"Treat Flyweight cache lifecycle as an independent resource-management concern.","correct_example":"A cache has bounded retention and deterministic behavior for invalidating obsolete shared state.","invalid_example":"An unbounded global cache stores every encountered key indefinitely.","problem":"Shared-state cache growth becomes uncontrolled.","failure_mode":"Memory consumption grows with workload diversity.","cause":"Cache lifecycle was left implicit.","solution":"Define capacity, eviction, ownership, and invalidation policies appropriate to the workload.","alternative":"Weak-reference or bounded cache mechanisms where supported and appropriate.","tradeoff":"Eviction may increase reconstruction cost while bounded memory improves predictability.","testing_guidance":"Test cache growth, eviction, invalidation, repeated creation, and concurrent access.","verification_guidance":"Verify memory and lifecycle invariants under representative workloads.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B55-0001"],"prerequisites":["Flyweight","Caching"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B56-0002","D13-DP-B60-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"cache_lifecycle","source":"Flyweight cache analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B56-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Flyweight","concept":"Flyweight concurrency","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can shared Flyweights create concurrency risks?","answer":"Shared Flyweights may be accessed by many consumers simultaneously. Mutable shared state or unsynchronized initialization can therefore create races and cross-consumer state leakage.","explanation":"The pattern's safety depends on what state is shared and how the implementation manages concurrent access.","principle":"Shared state must have explicit immutability or synchronization semantics.","correct_example":"Immutable Flyweight instances are safely shared among concurrent consumers while contextual state remains per request.","invalid_example":"A shared Flyweight stores the current request identity in a mutable field.","problem":"Per-consumer state is accidentally shared.","failure_mode":"Requests observe or overwrite each other's state.","cause":"Thread-safety and state ownership were not modeled.","solution":"Make shared state immutable where possible and externalize request-specific state.","alternative":"Independent instances for mutable context.","tradeoff":"Immutability may require additional allocations for changed state but simplifies concurrency.","testing_guidance":"Use concurrent tests and race detection where available.","verification_guidance":"Verify that shared mutable state is either prohibited or protected by a correct synchronization model.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B55-0002"],"prerequisites":["Concurrency","Flyweight"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B58-0003","D13-DP-B59-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"concurrency_security","source":"Shared-state safety analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B57-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Proxy","concept":"Proxy","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Proxy pattern?","answer":"Proxy provides a substitute or surrogate for another object and controls or mediates access to that object.","explanation":"A proxy can be used for access control, lazy initialization, remote access, caching, logging, or other access-related concerns depending on the design.","principle":"Insert an explicit access intermediary when controlling access to the target object is a meaningful requirement.","correct_example":"A proxy checks authorization before forwarding a request to a protected service object.","invalid_example":"Adding a proxy solely to delegate a method when no access-control, lifecycle, locality, or representation concern exists.","problem":"Direct access has a recurring control or indirection requirement.","failure_mode":"Access policy, expensive initialization, or remote behavior is duplicated across clients.","cause":"The target object is accessed without a consistent mediation point.","solution":"Use a proxy with a clearly defined responsibility and compatible contract where appropriate.","alternative":"Facade, Decorator, Adapter, direct dependency, or framework-level middleware depending on intent.","tradeoff":"Proxy adds an indirection boundary and can affect latency and failure semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test proxy behavior separately from target behavior and test bypass paths when security matters.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every intended access path passes through the proxy when the proxy is part of the security model.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":["Composition","Access control"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B57-0002","D13-DP-B58-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_catalog","source":"Refactoring.Guru Proxy","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN"},"evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B57-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Proxy","concept":"Proxy variants","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What are common purposes of Proxy implementations?","answer":"Common proxy purposes include access control, lazy initialization, remote access mediation, caching, logging or monitoring, and other controlled access behaviors.","explanation":"The shared concept is mediation of access to another object; the exact behavior depends on the specific proxy use case.","principle":"Classify a proxy by the access problem it solves rather than treating all proxies as equivalent.","correct_example":"A virtual proxy delays construction of an expensive object until first use; a protection proxy checks authorization before access.","invalid_example":"Assuming every proxy provides authorization merely because it mediates access.","problem":"Proxy behavior varies significantly by purpose.","failure_mode":"Consumers assume guarantees that a particular proxy does not provide.","cause":"Proxy classification is too generic.","solution":"Record proxy purpose and guarantees explicitly.","alternative":"Dedicated abstraction for the required concern.","tradeoff":"General proxy interfaces can simplify substitution but may hide important behavior differences.","testing_guidance":"Test purpose-specific guarantees independently.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual proxy guarantees rather than inferring them from the pattern name.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B57-0001"],"prerequisites":["Proxy"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B57-0003","D13-DP-B58-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_classification","source":"Proxy pattern variants","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B57-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Proxy","concept":"Proxy versus Decorator","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does Proxy differ from Decorator?","answer":"Decorator primarily adds or composes responsibilities while preserving the underlying abstraction, whereas Proxy primarily controls or mediates access to the target object.","explanation":"Both commonly wrap another object and may look structurally similar. Their distinguishing factor is intent.","principle":"Classify wrappers by the problem they solve rather than by their delegation structure.","correct_example":"A caching layer that intentionally adds behavior to an operation may be a Decorator; an access-control intermediary is a Proxy.","invalid_example":"Calling every wrapper a Decorator because it delegates to another object.","problem":"Wrapper patterns are confused.","failure_mode":"Incorrect assumptions about lifecycle, access, or behavioral composition.","cause":"Structural similarity is mistaken for intent equivalence.","solution":"Use intent, responsibility, and access semantics for classification.","alternative":"Direct composition when no pattern-specific force exists.","tradeoff":"Intent-based classification improves reasoning but requires contextual analysis.","testing_guidance":"Test added behavior for Decorator and access mediation for Proxy.","verification_guidance":"Verify the actual responsibility assigned to the wrapper.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B51-0001","D13-DP-B57-0001"],"prerequisites":["Decorator","Proxy"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B50-0002","D13-DP-B58-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"design_pattern_comparison","source":"Decorator and Proxy distinction","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B58-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Proxy","concept":"Protection Proxy","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should a protection Proxy enforce authorization?","answer":"A protection Proxy should evaluate the caller's authenticated identity and authorization context against an explicit policy before allowing the target operation, and it should fail closed when required authorization information is missing or invalid.","explanation":"A protection proxy can centralize access checks, but its effectiveness depends on controlling all relevant access paths and correctly establishing caller identity.","principle":"Authorization enforcement must be explicit, policy-driven, and complete across reachable access paths.","correct_example":"The proxy checks tenant and operation permissions before forwarding a request to a protected object.","invalid_example":"The proxy checks authorization only for some methods while clients can directly obtain the underlying target.","problem":"Authorization enforcement is incomplete.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized operations bypass the proxy.","cause":"Proxy was treated as a convention rather than an enforced boundary.","solution":"Control target exposure and verify every supported access path.","alternative":"Dedicated policy enforcement point or capability-based access design.","tradeoff":"Centralized authorization simplifies policy but can create a critical dependency and requires reliable identity context.","testing_guidance":"Test allowed, denied, missing-identity, cross-tenant, privilege-escalation, and bypass scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify both authorization decisions and architectural reachability constraints.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B57-0001"],"prerequisites":["Proxy","Authorization","Identity"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B54-0001","D13-DP-B58-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"security_pattern","source":"Protection Proxy security reasoning","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B58-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Proxy","concept":"Virtual Proxy","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What problem does a Virtual Proxy address?","answer":"A Virtual Proxy can defer creation or loading of an expensive target object until the object is actually needed.","explanation":"The proxy can reduce startup work or avoid constructing resources that are never used, but first-use latency and lifecycle complexity must be considered.","principle":"Defer expensive work when demand is uncertain and the added first-use boundary is acceptable.","correct_example":"A proxy delays loading a large resource until a client invokes an operation requiring it.","invalid_example":"Adding lazy initialization to a resource that is always used immediately and whose first-use latency is critical.","problem":"Expensive initialization occurs unnecessarily or too early.","failure_mode":"Startup cost increases without corresponding benefit, or first-use latency becomes unacceptable.","cause":"Initialization timing is not aligned with actual demand.","solution":"Measure usage and choose eager or lazy initialization according to requirements.","alternative":"Eager initialization, asynchronous preloading, or explicit resource lifecycle.","tradeoff":"Lazy initialization can improve startup/resource usage but complicates concurrency and failure timing.","testing_guidance":"Test first access, repeated access, initialization failure, concurrent first access, and disposal.","verification_guidance":"Verify initialization occurs at the documented lifecycle point and that failure is observable.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B57-0002"],"prerequisites":["Proxy","Lazy initialization"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B56-0001","D13-DP-B58-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"performance_pattern","source":"Virtual Proxy lifecycle analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B58-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Proxy","concept":"Proxy transparency limits","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"When can a Proxy fail to be behaviorally transparent?","answer":"A Proxy may change timing, exception types, identity semantics, resource ownership, concurrency behavior, network failure modes, or side effects even when it exposes the same nominal interface.","explanation":"Interface compatibility does not imply behavioral equivalence.","principle":"Treat proxy-induced semantic differences as part of the contract.","correct_example":"A remote proxy documents network timeouts and partial failures that do not exist for an in-process target.","invalid_example":"Assuming a remote proxy is equivalent to a local object because method signatures are identical.","problem":"Location or mediation changes behavior.","failure_mode":"Clients mishandle latency, failures, retries, or identity.","cause":"Only syntactic substitutability was considered.","solution":"Document and test behavioral differences explicitly.","alternative":"Explicit remote service abstraction when local transparency would be misleading.","tradeoff":"Transparency simplifies clients but can hide important operational semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test timeout, cancellation, retry, partial failure, identity, and concurrency behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify documented semantic guarantees at the proxy boundary.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B57-0002"],"prerequisites":["Proxy","Distributed systems"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B47-0002","D13-DP-B54-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_systems_reasoning","source":"Proxy transparency analysis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B59-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Structural Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Decorator, Facade, Flyweight, Proxy selection","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should the Brain distinguish Decorator, Facade, Flyweight, and Proxy?","answer":"Decorator adds composable responsibilities; Facade simplifies access to a subsystem; Flyweight shares intrinsic state across logical instances; Proxy mediates access to a target object.","explanation":"All four can involve object composition, but their design forces are different.","principle":"Select the pattern from the primary problem: behavior layering, subsystem simplification, state sharing, or access mediation.","correct_example":"Use Decorator for optional behavior layers, Facade for simplified subsystem workflow, Flyweight for safely shareable intrinsic state, and Proxy for controlled target access.","invalid_example":"Selecting among them solely because all can be implemented with wrapper-like objects.","problem":"Structural patterns overlap syntactically.","failure_mode":"The Brain retrieves inappropriate implementation and testing guidance.","cause":"Pattern intent is not represented in retrieval features.","solution":"Index problem, intent, forces, consequences, and distinguishing characteristics.","alternative":"Direct composition when none of the pattern-specific forces are present.","tradeoff":"Intent-based classification requires richer contextual reasoning.","testing_guidance":"Create discriminative retrieval and classification tests using natural-language problem descriptions.","verification_guidance":"Require a documented dominant design force before pattern selection.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B51-0001","D13-DP-B53-0001","D13-DP-B55-0001","D13-DP-B57-0001"],"prerequisites":["Structural patterns"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B50-0002","D13-DP-B60-0001"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_selection_comparison","source":"Structural pattern synthesis","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN_KNOWLEDGE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B59-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Structural Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Shared-state and wrapper interaction","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What should be analyzed when Flyweight state is combined with Decorator or Proxy wrappers?","answer":"The design must distinguish shared intrinsic state from per-wrapper and per-request state, and must define ownership, caching, synchronization, lifecycle, and identity semantics.","explanation":"Combining sharing with wrappers can accidentally cause contextual state to become shared or cause caches to retain wrapper-specific resources.","principle":"Pattern composition must preserve state ownership across every layer.","correct_example":"Immutable Flyweight metadata is shared while each request-specific Decorator or Proxy stores its own contextual state.","invalid_example":"A shared Flyweight contains mutable request-specific state used by all wrappers.","problem":"State boundaries become ambiguous across composed patterns.","failure_mode":"Cross-request leakage, stale data, or race conditions occur.","cause":"Composition was analyzed structurally but not semantically.","solution":"Document state ownership and mutability for every layer.","alternative":"Separate instances when ownership cannot be safely shared.","tradeoff":"More explicit state management may increase memory use but improves isolation.","testing_guidance":"Use concurrent multi-context tests and state-isolation checks.","verification_guidance":"Verify no context-specific mutable state crosses logical instance boundaries.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B55-0002","D13-DP-B51-0001","D13-DP-B57-0001"],"prerequisites":["Pattern composition","State ownership"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B56-0002","D13-DP-B58-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"pattern_composition_analysis","source":"Shared-state and wrapper interaction","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B60-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Structural Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Structural pattern selection algorithm","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What selection algorithm should an SE Brain use for common structural patterns?","answer":"Identify the primary problem, determine the affected relationship or state dimension, identify required invariants, evaluate the simplest non-pattern solution, compare candidate patterns by intent and consequences, then record the selected pattern with alternatives and confidence.","explanation":"Pattern selection is a constrained reasoning problem rather than a lookup by name.","principle":"Problem-first selection should precede structural implementation.","correct_example":"An interface mismatch is analyzed as compatibility translation before Adapter is selected.","invalid_example":"Selecting Decorator because the implementation happens to contain a wrapper class.","problem":"Pattern selection becomes implementation-driven.","failure_mode":"The generated architecture solves a different problem from the stated requirement.","cause":"No explicit selection procedure exists.","solution":"Use a structured pattern-selection record containing problem, forces, constraints, alternatives, decision, and confidence.","alternative":"Rule-based pattern classifier supplemented by semantic retrieval.","tradeoff":"Structured reasoning is slower than direct lookup but produces stronger justification.","testing_guidance":"Test the classifier against positive, negative, and ambiguous pattern scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the selected pattern's defining intent is supported by evidence from the requirement or code.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B59-0001"],"prerequisites":["Pattern selection","Structural patterns"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B42-0002","D13-DP-B60-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"reasoning_procedure","source":"D13 pattern-selection methodology","evidence_level":"PROJECT_SPECIFICATION"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B60-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Structural Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Structural pattern performance verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should structural pattern performance claims be verified?","answer":"Separate theoretical complexity from measured runtime behavior and verify performance under a defined workload, environment, data size, concurrency level, and measurement method.","explanation":"Patterns such as Decorator, Proxy, Facade, and Flyweight can affect indirection, allocation, caching, synchronization, and traversal, but their actual impact depends on implementation and workload.","principle":"Never convert a pattern's expected structural effect into an unverified performance claim.","correct_example":"A benchmark records workload and environment before comparing decorated and undecorated execution paths.","invalid_example":"Claiming that Proxy is slow or Flyweight is faster without workload-specific evidence.","problem":"Pattern-level assumptions are mistaken for measurements.","failure_mode":"Architecture decisions are based on unsupported performance claims.","cause":"Theoretical reasoning and empirical evidence are conflated.","solution":"Label performance statements as measured, estimated, inferred, or unknown.","alternative":"Analytical complexity reasoning when measurement is unavailable.","tradeoff":"Benchmarking costs time but provides stronger evidence for workload-specific decisions.","testing_guidance":"Use repeatable measurements and representative workloads when performance is material.","verification_guidance":"Store measurement methodology, environment, workload, observed result, and limitations.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B52-0003","D13-DP-B56-0001"],"prerequisites":["Performance testing","Evidence classification"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B60-0003"],"provenance":{"source_type":"verification_policy","source":"Evidence-driven performance verification","evidence_level":"PROJECT_SPECIFICATION"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"SCHEMA_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B60-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Patterns","subtopic":"Structural Pattern Synthesis","concept":"Structural pattern failure-injection matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Which failure scenarios should structural pattern implementations consider?","answer":"Relevant scenarios include invalid inputs, target unavailable, target timeout, partial subsystem failure, authorization denial, malformed translation data, cache miss, cache invalidation, concurrent access, lifecycle failure, and resource exhaustion where applicable.","explanation":"Structural patterns often create boundaries where failures can be transformed, delayed, shared, or hidden. Failure testing verifies that the boundary does not create false success or unsafe state.","principle":"Test boundary failures according to the actual semantics introduced by the pattern.","correct_example":"A Proxy test verifies timeout and cancellation behavior; an Adapter test verifies malformed legacy responses; a Facade test verifies partial workflow failure.","invalid_example":"Testing only successful calls through structural wrappers.","problem":"Boundary-specific failures remain unverified.","failure_mode":"Production systems exhibit unexpected behavior during degraded conditions.","cause":"Pattern testing focuses on nominal delegation.","solution":"Build pattern-specific failure matrices and map each case to expected observable behavior.","alternative":"Generic integration failure suite supplemented with pattern-specific cases.","tradeoff":"Failure testing increases test effort but improves confidence in boundary behavior.","testing_guidance":"Include negative, boundary, concurrency, security, timeout, cancellation, and partial-failure cases relevant to the pattern.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual resulting state and externally observable behavior, not only exception presence.","language":"English","version":"1.0","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D13-DP-B47-0001","D13-DP-B54-0003","D13-DP-B58-0003"],"prerequisites":["Failure injection","Structural patterns"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B43-0003","D13-DP-B60-0002"],"provenance":{"source_type":"testing_methodology","source":"Structural pattern failure testing","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_GUIDANCE"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONCEPTUALLY_VALIDATED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

