{"record_id":"D13-DP-B71-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"State","subtopic":"Intent","concept":"State pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the State pattern?","answer":"State allows an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes by delegating state-dependent behavior to state representations.","explanation":"It makes state-specific behavior explicit rather than concentrating all transitions and conditional behavior in one large conditional structure.","principle":"Represent meaningful behavioral states explicitly when state-dependent behavior is substantial enough to justify separate representations.","correct_example":"A workflow object delegates behavior to Draft, Approved, and Rejected state objects.","invalid_example":"Create separate state objects for a class with only one trivial conditional.","alternative":"A simple enum plus conditional logic can be sufficient for small and stable state-dependent behavior.","tradeoff":"State can improve cohesion and extensibility but introduces additional objects and transition complexity.","language":"language-neutral","version":"classic","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B71-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"State","subtopic":"Transitions","concept":"Explicit state transition model","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should a State-based design define?","answer":"It should define valid states, permitted transitions, transition triggers, entry and exit behavior when relevant, and behavior for invalid transitions.","explanation":"Separating state behavior without defining transition semantics can create an incomplete state model.","correct_example":"A payment lifecycle explicitly defines which operations can move an object from pending to authorized, failed, or completed.","invalid_example":"Allow every state object to arbitrarily assign another state without transition constraints.","failure_mode":"Invalid or impossible state combinations.","cause":"Transitions are implicit and unrestricted.","solution":"Define an explicit transition model and enforce valid transitions.","testing_guidance":"Test every valid transition and representative invalid transitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that reachable states and transitions conform to the documented state machine.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B71-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"State","subtopic":"State machine relationship","concept":"State pattern versus finite-state model","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does State relate to a finite-state machine?","answer":"A State implementation can represent the behavioral aspects of a state machine, while a formal state-machine model focuses explicitly on states, transitions, events, and transition rules.","explanation":"State is a design approach; a formal state-machine representation can be used to specify or verify the underlying behavior.","tradeoff":"Object-oriented State representations can improve localization of behavior while formal state models can make transition completeness and reachability easier to analyze.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":0.94,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B72-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"State","subtopic":"State invariants","concept":"State-specific invariants","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why are invariants important in the State pattern?","answer":"Each state may impose different valid operations and data constraints, so state-specific invariants should be explicit and checked at appropriate boundaries.","explanation":"A state object should not merely change behavior; it should preserve the domain invariants associated with that state.","correct_example":"An Approved state prevents modifications that are permitted in Draft state.","invalid_example":"Expose all operations identically regardless of state while assuming callers will enforce restrictions themselves.","security_implication":"Authorization or safety constraints must not be weakened merely because behavior is delegated through state objects.","testing_guidance":"Test state-specific permitted and forbidden operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify invariants before and after every state transition.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B72-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"State","subtopic":"Transition failures","concept":"Failed state transitions","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should failed State transitions be handled?","answer":"The design should define whether an invalid transition is rejected, represented as a domain failure, retried, or transformed into another valid state.","problem":"A transition partially modifies state before failing.","cause":"State mutation and transition validation are not coordinated.","solution":"Validate transition preconditions and define atomic state-update semantics.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures before, during, and after transition processing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that failed transitions cannot leave prohibited partial states.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B72-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"State","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Concurrent state transitions","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What risk exists when multiple actors change State concurrently?","answer":"Concurrent transitions can produce lost updates, invalid transitions, stale decisions, or inconsistent state unless synchronization or concurrency semantics are explicitly defined.","solution":"Use appropriate synchronization, optimistic concurrency controls, serialized transitions, or other domain-specific consistency mechanisms.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrent transition attempts and stale-state operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that concurrent executions preserve the documented state invariants.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B73-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Strategy","subtopic":"Intent","concept":"Strategy pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Strategy pattern?","answer":"Strategy defines a family of interchangeable algorithms, encapsulates each algorithm, and allows the algorithm to vary independently from the client that uses it.","explanation":"The client depends on an algorithm contract rather than embedding every algorithm directly in its own control flow.","principle":"Encapsulate algorithmic variation behind a stable abstraction when multiple algorithms are legitimate alternatives.","correct_example":"A pricing service selects different pricing strategies according to a business policy.","invalid_example":"Create separate Strategy classes when there is only one algorithm and no meaningful variation requirement.","alternative":"A direct function, conditional expression, or language-level function parameter may be simpler for small variations.","tradeoff":"Strategy improves substitution and testability but introduces abstraction and selection complexity.","language":"language-neutral","version":"classic","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B73-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Strategy","subtopic":"Strategy selection","concept":"Runtime algorithm selection","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When is Strategy particularly appropriate?","answer":"It is appropriate when multiple algorithms implement the same conceptual operation and the choice may vary by configuration, runtime context, policy, or caller.","correct_example":"A compression service selects a compression implementation according to the target format.","invalid_example":"Use Strategy only to move one unchanged method into another class.","failure_mode":"Strategy proliferation.","cause":"Every minor implementation variation becomes a separate strategy without a meaningful substitution boundary.","solution":"Create strategies around stable behavioral variation rather than arbitrary code fragmentation.","testing_guidance":"Test each strategy against the common contract and representative selection conditions.","verification_guidance":"Verify substitutability across all supported strategies.","language":"language-neutral","version":"HEURISTIC","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.95,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B73-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Strategy","subtopic":"Contract","concept":"Strategy contract compatibility","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must interchangeable Strategies share?","answer":"They must satisfy the semantic contract expected by the client, including input assumptions, output guarantees, error behavior, side effects, and relevant performance constraints.","explanation":"Matching method signatures alone does not establish behavioral substitutability.","invalid_example":"A strategy returns an apparently valid result but silently changes an externally observable side effect required by the contract.","testing_guidance":"Use contract tests across all strategy implementations.","verification_guidance":"Verify semantic equivalence of required guarantees rather than only interface compatibility.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B74-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Strategy","subtopic":"Strategy versus State","concept":"Strategy versus State","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does Strategy differ from State?","answer":"Strategy primarily selects among interchangeable algorithms, while State represents behavior that changes according to an object's current state and often includes state transitions.","explanation":"The same structural mechanism can sometimes implement both, but their design intent differs.","correct_example":"Use Strategy for selecting a sorting algorithm; use State for an order whose allowed operations change through its lifecycle.","tradeoff":"Confusing the two can lead to unclear ownership of transitions and algorithm selection.","related_records":["D13-DP-B71-0001","D13-DP-B73-0001"],"language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B74-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Strategy","subtopic":"Security","concept":"Strategy security boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security issue can arise from dynamically selected Strategies?","answer":"An attacker-controlled or insufficiently validated strategy selector could cause execution of an unintended algorithm or privileged behavior.","explanation":"Dynamic selection expands the set of executable behaviors and therefore requires explicit validation of selection inputs.","security_implication":"Use allowlisted strategy identifiers and enforce authorization independently of strategy selection.","invalid_example":"Map arbitrary external class names directly to executable implementations.","testing_guidance":"Test invalid, unauthorized, deprecated, and malicious strategy identifiers.","verification_guidance":"Verify that only explicitly permitted strategies can be selected.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B74-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Strategy","subtopic":"Performance","concept":"Strategy dispatch overhead","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What performance consideration applies to Strategy?","answer":"Strategy can introduce an additional dispatch or object boundary, but the practical cost depends on the language runtime, implementation, and algorithmic work performed.","explanation":"The pattern itself does not establish a fixed performance penalty.","constraint":"Do not claim a specific overhead without measurements for the target environment.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark representative workloads when dispatch cost is relevant to requirements.","verification_guidance":"Record measured results with environment and workload details.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.91,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B75-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Observer","subtopic":"Intent","concept":"Observer pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Observer pattern?","answer":"Observer defines a one-to-many dependency so that when one subject changes state or produces an event, registered observers can be notified.","explanation":"It provides a mechanism for propagating changes without requiring the subject to know the concrete implementation details of each observer.","principle":"Separate event production from interested consumers when loose notification coupling is valuable.","correct_example":"A domain event source notifies multiple subscribers about a completed operation.","invalid_example":"Use Observer when there is one tightly coupled consumer and direct invocation is clearer.","alternative":"Direct callbacks, event buses, message brokers, or reactive mechanisms may provide similar capabilities depending on scale and requirements.","tradeoff":"Observer can reduce direct coupling but introduces lifecycle, ordering, failure, and delivery semantics.","language":"language-neutral","version":"classic","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B75-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Observer","subtopic":"Subscription lifecycle","concept":"Observer registration lifecycle","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must Observer subscription lifecycle be explicit?","answer":"Observers that remain registered after they are no longer needed can receive unwanted notifications and retain resources or references longer than intended.","failure_mode":"Stale subscription.","cause":"Missing unsubscribe, ownership, lifecycle, or weak-reference policy.","solution":"Define registration ownership and removal semantics explicitly.","edge_case":"An observer is destroyed logically but remains registered in the subject.","testing_guidance":"Test registration, repeated registration, unregistration, object disposal, and subject shutdown.","verification_guidance":"Verify that obsolete observers cannot continue receiving notifications.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B75-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Observer","subtopic":"Notification semantics","concept":"Observer delivery semantics","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What notification semantics should an Observer design specify?","answer":"It should specify whether notifications are synchronous or asynchronous, ordered or unordered, best-effort or guaranteed, duplicated or deduplicated, and how observer failures affect delivery.","explanation":"The Observer pattern itself does not establish these delivery guarantees.","constraint":"Delivery semantics must be part of the system contract when observers perform important work.","testing_guidance":"Test ordering, duplicate delivery, observer failure, slow observers, and subject failure.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual delivery behavior against documented guarantees.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B76-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Observer","subtopic":"Failure isolation","concept":"Observer failure isolation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should observer failures be handled?","answer":"The system should explicitly define whether one observer failure stops notification, is isolated and recorded, is retried, or is propagated to the caller.","explanation":"A notification mechanism should not accidentally allow an unrelated observer to corrupt the producer's operation.","failure_mode":"One observer failure prevents other observers from receiving a notification.","cause":"Sequential notification without failure isolation.","solution":"Define failure isolation and delivery semantics appropriate to the application.","testing_guidance":"Inject observer failures at different positions in the observer set.","verification_guidance":"Verify the documented behavior for partial observer failure.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B76-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Observer","subtopic":"Security","concept":"Observer data exposure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security concern exists when notifications contain sensitive data?","answer":"Every subscribed observer may receive the notification payload, so subscription scope and payload minimization must be controlled.","security_implication":"Do not publish sensitive fields merely because observers might need the event.","correct_example":"Publish an event identifier and authorized metadata while requiring separately controlled access to sensitive details.","invalid_example":"Broadcast complete confidential records to every subscriber.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized subscribers, tenant boundaries, payload filtering, and subscription changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each observer receives only data it is authorized to access.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B76-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Observer","subtopic":"Performance","concept":"Observer fan-out","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can Observer affect performance?","answer":"Notification cost can increase with the number of observers and the work performed by each observer, particularly for synchronous delivery.","explanation":"Asynchronous delivery can decouple producer latency from observer work but introduces queueing and delivery semantics.","performance_implication":"High fan-out can create latency, queue growth, or resource pressure.","testing_guidance":"Measure producer latency, observer processing time, queue depth, and failure behavior under representative fan-out.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.95,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B77-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Template Method","subtopic":"Intent","concept":"Template Method pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Template Method pattern?","answer":"Template Method defines the skeleton of an algorithm in a base abstraction while allowing selected steps to vary through overriding or delegated operations.","explanation":"The invariant workflow remains centralized while variation points are explicitly defined.","principle":"Fix algorithm structure while permitting controlled variation in individual steps.","correct_example":"A processing workflow defines validation, processing, and finalization order while subclasses customize the processing step.","invalid_example":"Make every algorithm step overridable when the ordering itself must remain invariant.","alternative":"Strategy can be preferable when the complete algorithm should be interchangeable rather than only selected steps.","tradeoff":"Template Method provides strong workflow control but can create inheritance coupling.","language":"language-neutral","version":"classic","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B77-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Template Method","subtopic":"Invariants","concept":"Template algorithm invariants","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should remain stable in Template Method?","answer":"The template should preserve the ordering and invariants that define the algorithm while exposing only intended variation points.","failure_mode":"Subclass overrides violate required workflow assumptions.","cause":"Variation points are insufficiently constrained.","solution":"Make invariant steps non-overridable where the language permits and clearly document extension hooks.","testing_guidance":"Test subclasses against shared workflow invariants.","verification_guidance":"Verify that all implementations preserve required ordering and postconditions.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B77-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Template Method","subtopic":"Inheritance coupling","concept":"Template Method inheritance cost","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is a major tradeoff of Template Method?","answer":"It can reduce duplicated workflow logic but couples subclasses to the base class's algorithm structure and extension points.","alternative":"Composition with Strategy or explicit pipeline components can reduce inheritance coupling.","constraint":"Use inheritance only where the subclass relationship represents a stable abstraction boundary.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B78-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Visitor","subtopic":"Intent","concept":"Visitor pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Visitor pattern?","answer":"Visitor separates operations from the object structures on which those operations operate, allowing new operations to be added without changing each visited element's core representation.","explanation":"Visitor is particularly useful when the object structure is relatively stable but many independent operations need to be performed over it.","principle":"Separate frequently changing operations from a relatively stable object structure when the resulting double-dispatch or visitor protocol is justified.","correct_example":"An abstract syntax tree supports multiple visitors for analysis, formatting, or transformation operations.","invalid_example":"Use Visitor for a tiny object hierarchy with one stable operation.","alternative":"Polymorphic methods, pattern matching, external functions, or data-oriented traversal may be simpler depending on language and design.","tradeoff":"Visitor makes new operations easier but can make adding new element types harder and introduces protocol complexity.","language":"language-neutral","version":"classic","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B78-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Visitor","subtopic":"Double dispatch","concept":"Visitor dispatch protocol","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why does Visitor commonly require a structured dispatch protocol?","answer":"The visitor must select behavior based on the concrete element type while preserving the abstraction boundary between the visitor and the visited structure.","explanation":"The implementation technique varies by language, but the essential design concern is dispatching the appropriate operation for each element type.","failure_mode":"Visitor methods become inconsistent with supported element types.","cause":"Visitor and element hierarchies evolve independently without contract validation.","solution":"Treat the visitor-element protocol as a versioned contract and test every supported element type.","testing_guidance":"Maintain coverage for every visitor-element combination required by the contract.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each supported element dispatches to exactly the intended visitor operation.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B78-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Visitor","subtopic":"Change tradeoff","concept":"Visitor change asymmetry","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What change pattern does Visitor favor?","answer":"Visitor generally favors adding new operations over adding new element types because new element types may require changes across visitor implementations.","explanation":"This asymmetry is central to deciding whether Visitor is appropriate.","correct_example":"A stable syntax tree receives many new analysis visitors over time.","invalid_example":"Use Visitor when new node types are expected constantly and visitors are numerous.","alternative":"Use polymorphism, pattern matching, or a different representation when element-type evolution dominates.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B79-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Visitor","subtopic":"Security","concept":"Visitor operation authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security issue can arise with Visitor operations?","answer":"A visitor may perform powerful operations over every supported element type, so exposing a visitor requires clear authorization and operation boundaries.","security_implication":"Do not assume that structural traversal automatically authorizes every operation available through a visitor.","correct_example":"A read-only visitor is exposed to an untrusted context while mutation visitors require stronger authorization.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized visitor types, restricted elements, and privileged operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify authorization independently from dispatch correctness.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B79-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Visitor","subtopic":"Performance","concept":"Visitor traversal cost","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What performance factors should be considered with Visitor?","answer":"Traversal cost depends on the size of the visited structure, visitor operation complexity, dispatch overhead, allocation behavior, and whether repeated traversals can be avoided.","constraint":"Do not attribute a fixed performance cost to Visitor without measurements.","testing_guidance":"Measure representative structure sizes and visitor workloads.","verification_guidance":"Record actual measurements separately from architectural expectations.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.91,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B79-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Visitor","subtopic":"Failure handling","concept":"Visitor failure propagation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What should happen if a Visitor operation fails halfway through traversal?","answer":"The design must define whether traversal stops, continues, accumulates errors, rolls back mutations, or records partial results.","problem":"A visitor performs mutations and fails after modifying only part of the structure.","cause":"No explicit atomicity or partial-failure semantics.","solution":"Separate analysis from mutation when possible or define transaction and compensation semantics explicitly.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures at different traversal positions.","verification_guidance":"Verify the resulting structure and reported outcome for partial failures.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B80-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Selection synthesis","concept":"Behavioral pattern selection matrix","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should common behavioral patterns from D13 B61-B80 be distinguished?","answer":"Use Chain of Responsibility for sequential request handling, Command for explicit request representation, Iterator for controlled traversal, Mediator for centralized participant coordination, State for state-dependent behavior, Strategy for interchangeable algorithms, Observer for change notification, Template Method for fixed algorithm structure with variable steps, and Visitor for adding operations over a relatively stable object structure.","explanation":"The distinction is based on primary design intent rather than class structure alone.","related_records":["D13-DP-B62-0001","D13-DP-B64-0001","D13-DP-B66-0001","D13-DP-B68-0001","D13-DP-B71-0001","D13-DP-B73-0001","D13-DP-B75-0001","D13-DP-B77-0001","D13-DP-B78-0001"],"language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B80-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern composition","concept":"Behavioral pattern composition","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Can behavioral design patterns be combined?","answer":"Yes. Patterns can be composed when their responsibilities are distinct and their interaction contracts are explicit.","correct_example":"A Command can represent a request, a Chain of Responsibility can validate or route it, and an Observer can publish its resulting event.","invalid_example":"Combine patterns merely to maximize abstraction or class count.","tradeoff":"Composition can increase flexibility while also increasing control-flow complexity, lifecycle interactions, and testing requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test each pattern boundary independently and then test the composed workflow.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each pattern retains a single clear responsibility and that composition does not create contradictory lifecycle or failure semantics.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B80-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Final verification","concept":"Behavioral pattern verification protocol","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should behavioral pattern implementations be verified before acceptance?","answer":"Verify problem-pattern fit, participant contracts, lifecycle behavior, failure semantics, concurrency behavior where applicable, security boundaries, performance assumptions, tests, version compatibility, and rejected alternatives.","explanation":"A pattern implementation is not verified merely because its class names resemble a catalog description.","testing_guidance":"Use unit, integration, negative, boundary, regression, concurrency, security, and performance testing according to the pattern's risk profile.","verification_guidance":"Separate design verification from executed implementation verification and measured performance verification.","constraint":"Unexecuted tests, unmeasured benchmarks, and unsupported claims must remain explicitly marked as NOT_EXECUTED, NOT_MEASURED, or UNKNOWN.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

