{"record_id":"D13-DP-B81-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Command and Observer composition","concept":"Explicit command event lifecycle","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can Command and Observer be combined?","answer":"Command can represent an executable request while Observer can publish lifecycle events such as accepted, started, completed, or failed execution.","explanation":"The command owns request semantics while observers consume published execution events without becoming part of command execution logic.","correct_example":"A queued command emits a completion event after execution while independent consumers update audit or monitoring state.","invalid_example":"Make observers responsible for determining whether the command itself should execute when authorization belongs to the command execution boundary.","tradeoff":"The combination separates execution from notification but requires explicit event ordering and failure semantics.","related_records":["D13-DP-B64-0001","D13-DP-B75-0001"],"language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B81-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"State and Strategy composition","concept":"State with state-specific Strategy","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Can State and Strategy be combined?","answer":"Yes. A State can select or own a Strategy when behavior depends both on lifecycle state and on an independently variable algorithm.","explanation":"State answers which behavioral mode is active while Strategy can encapsulate an algorithm selected within that mode.","correct_example":"A workflow state selects an algorithm appropriate to the current lifecycle phase.","tradeoff":"The combination can represent complex variation cleanly but can produce a large state-by-strategy configuration space.","failure_mode":"Combinatorial complexity.","cause":"Too many independent state and strategy combinations.","solution":"Identify whether the variations are truly independent and consolidate or compose only the dimensions that require independent change.","testing_guidance":"Test the cross-product of supported state and strategy combinations or use model-based/property-based coverage where appropriate.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.95,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B81-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Composition risk","concept":"Pattern composition complexity","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"When does behavioral pattern composition become harmful?","answer":"It becomes harmful when multiple abstractions obscure the actual control flow, create overlapping responsibilities, or make lifecycle and failure semantics difficult to establish.","explanation":"Combining valid patterns does not automatically produce a valid architecture.","invalid_example":"Use State, Strategy, Command, Observer, and Mediator for a small workflow that could be expressed clearly with direct calls.","problem":"Excessive indirection makes debugging and verification harder.","solution":"Remove patterns whose independent variation or coordination value cannot be demonstrated.","verification_guidance":"Compare the composed design against a simpler baseline and document why each abstraction is required.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B82-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Chain and Command composition","concept":"Command routing pipeline","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can Chain of Responsibility and Command complement each other?","answer":"A Command can represent the request while a handler chain performs validation, authorization, routing, transformation, or execution selection before the command reaches an appropriate executor.","explanation":"The command provides request identity and lifecycle; the chain provides ordered processing responsibility.","correct_example":"A command passes through validation and authorization handlers before an execution handler processes it.","security_implication":"Authorization handlers must have explicit fail-closed semantics when they protect privileged commands.","testing_guidance":"Test command construction, every handler decision, terminal execution, and denied execution.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no chain path reaches execution without mandatory policy checks.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B82-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Mediator and Observer","concept":"Mediator versus event notification","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does Mediator differ from Observer when coordinating components?","answer":"Observer primarily distributes notifications to interested subscribers, while Mediator coordinates interactions and decisions among participants.","explanation":"An event notification can inform observers without requiring the notification mechanism to determine the complete collaboration workflow.","correct_example":"Use Observer for publishing that an operation completed; use Mediator when several participants must be coordinated to complete a workflow.","tradeoff":"Replacing a simple notification mechanism with a mediator can unnecessarily centralize behavior.","related_records":["D13-DP-B68-0001","D13-DP-B75-0001"],"language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":0.95,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B82-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Iterator and Visitor","concept":"Traversal-operation separation","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How can Iterator and Visitor complement each other?","answer":"Iterator can control traversal while Visitor encapsulates operations applied to elements encountered during traversal.","explanation":"This separation can be useful when traversal policy and operation policy vary independently.","correct_example":"An iterator traverses a graph while a visitor performs analysis on each eligible node.","tradeoff":"Combining them introduces two abstraction layers and should be justified by independent variation requirements.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.94,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B83-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Template Method and Strategy","concept":"Inheritance versus composition for algorithm variation","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should Template Method and Strategy be distinguished?","answer":"Template Method keeps the overall algorithm structure in a base abstraction and varies selected steps, while Strategy replaces or selects an algorithm implementation through composition.","explanation":"The key distinction is whether the invariant workflow is owned by a common base abstraction or the complete algorithm is interchangeable.","alternative":"Use composition when runtime substitution and reduced inheritance coupling are more important than centralized template control.","related_records":["D13-DP-B73-0001","D13-DP-B77-0001"],"language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B83-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"State and Observer","concept":"State transition notification","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can State and Observer be combined safely?","answer":"State can own lifecycle behavior while Observer publishes state-transition events to external consumers that should not directly control the state machine.","explanation":"This preserves separation between internal state management and external notification.","correct_example":"A workflow changes state internally and emits a transition event after a successful transition.","invalid_example":"Allow an arbitrary observer to directly mutate the state machine during notification without explicit reentrancy semantics.","failure_mode":"Reentrant state corruption.","cause":"Observers perform synchronous state mutations while a transition is incomplete.","solution":"Define post-transition notification boundaries or explicit reentrant transition rules.","testing_guidance":"Test observers that trigger additional operations during notifications.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B83-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern selection","concept":"Behavioral pattern elimination test","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What question should be asked before introducing a behavioral pattern?","answer":"Can the design problem, required variation, or collaboration complexity be expressed clearly with simpler language constructs or direct collaboration?","explanation":"Patterns should solve demonstrated design forces rather than serve as mandatory architectural decoration.","correct_example":"Introduce Strategy because multiple algorithms are independently selected and tested.","invalid_example":"Introduce Strategy because the code contains a conditional even though the alternatives are stable and trivial.","verification_guidance":"Record the simpler design considered and the concrete force that justified the pattern.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B84-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Concurrent behavioral coordination","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What additional concerns arise when behavioral patterns operate concurrently?","answer":"Concurrency introduces ordering, synchronization, visibility, cancellation, duplicate execution, race conditions, and lifecycle concerns that are not solved merely by selecting a behavioral pattern.","explanation":"Patterns describe design responsibilities but do not automatically establish thread safety or distributed consistency.","failure_mode":"Assuming a pattern provides concurrency safety.","cause":"Pattern semantics are confused with synchronization guarantees.","solution":"Specify concurrency contracts independently and test them under realistic interleavings.","testing_guidance":"Use concurrency, stress, race-detection, timeout, cancellation, and failure-injection testing where appropriate.","verification_guidance":"Verify documented happens-before, ordering, ownership, and cancellation guarantees.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B84-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Cancellation","concept":"Cancellation-aware behavioral patterns","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"How should cancellation be represented in behavioral pattern designs?","answer":"Cancellation should be an explicit part of the operation or coordination contract when work can outlive its caller or consume significant resources.","explanation":"Commands, strategies, iterators, observers, and mediators may all require defined cancellation behavior in long-running workflows.","failure_mode":"Cancellation request is accepted but underlying work continues indefinitely.","cause":"No propagation or cooperative cancellation mechanism.","solution":"Define cancellation ownership, propagation, cleanup, and final outcome semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test cancellation before start, during execution, after partial progress, and during cleanup.","verification_guidance":"Verify resources are released and the final state is explicitly represented.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B84-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Timeouts","concept":"Timeout semantics","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should timeouts be explicit in behavioral coordination?","answer":"A timeout is not necessarily proof that an operation stopped; the operation may still be running or may have completed remotely without the caller receiving the result.","explanation":"This distinction is important for Commands, Mediators, observers, and asynchronous workflows.","problem":"A caller retries after timeout and unintentionally creates duplicate work.","solution":"Define timeout, cancellation, idempotency, and reconciliation semantics separately.","testing_guidance":"Inject delayed responses and completion-after-timeout scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify the final outcome independently when timeout does not prove termination.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B85-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Distributed systems","concept":"Behavioral patterns across process boundaries","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What changes when a behavioral pattern crosses process or network boundaries?","answer":"Serialization, transport failures, retries, partial failure, identity, version compatibility, latency, duplicate delivery, and distributed consistency become part of the behavioral contract.","explanation":"An in-process pattern abstraction cannot be assumed to provide distributed-systems guarantees.","security_implication":"Identity and authorization must be preserved across the boundary rather than inferred from local object relationships.","performance_implication":"Network latency and serialization can dominate local dispatch costs.","testing_guidance":"Test transport failure, timeout, duplication, reordering, malformed messages, and version mismatch.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B85-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Command","subtopic":"Distributed execution","concept":"Distributed Command identity","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should a distributed Command identify?","answer":"A distributed command should have sufficient identity and metadata to support authorization, tracing, deduplication, lifecycle tracking, and outcome reconciliation where those capabilities are required.","explanation":"A method call's in-memory identity is insufficient once the request crosses process boundaries.","security_implication":"Command identity and authorization context must not be trusted solely because they originated in a transport payload.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate command delivery, replay, stale commands, and malformed identity metadata.","verification_guidance":"Verify that command identity remains stable across serialization and transport.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B85-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Observer","subtopic":"Distributed notification","concept":"Distributed Observer semantics","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What must be specified for distributed Observer-style notifications?","answer":"The design should specify delivery guarantees, ordering, duplication, persistence, subscriber recovery, replay, and failure behavior.","explanation":"Remote notification cannot safely inherit assumptions from in-process callbacks.","alternative":"A durable message or event system may be more appropriate when delivery guarantees are part of the business contract.","testing_guidance":"Test subscriber restart, duplicate events, out-of-order events, producer failure, and replay.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B86-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Observability","concept":"Pattern-aware observability","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What should be observable in a production implementation of behavioral patterns?","answer":"Important behavioral transitions, request identifiers, handler or strategy selection, command lifecycle, subscription changes, state transitions, failures, retries, and terminal outcomes should be observable when operationally relevant.","explanation":"Observability should expose meaningful behavior without coupling production logic unnecessarily to diagnostic concerns.","security_implication":"Logs and traces must avoid exposing sensitive command payloads or protected observer data.","testing_guidance":"Verify that important success and failure paths emit the required telemetry.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B86-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Debugging","concept":"Behavioral control-flow debugging","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an unexpected behavioral-pattern failure be debugged?","answer":"Identify the actual runtime participants, trace the request or event lifecycle, inspect pattern contracts and transition decisions, locate the first divergence from the expected protocol, then test the smallest failing interaction.","explanation":"Debugging should follow observable behavior rather than assumptions based on class names.","testing_guidance":"Create a minimal reproducer that isolates the failing collaboration.","verification_guidance":"Confirm the fix with regression tests covering both the original failure and neighboring paths.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B86-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Diagnostics","concept":"First-divergence principle","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why is finding the first behavioral divergence useful?","answer":"Later failures may be consequences of an earlier incorrect transition, dispatch, notification, or state mutation, so locating the first divergence narrows causal analysis.","correct_example":"Trace a Command through validation, authorization, execution, and notification to find where the actual path first differs from the expected path.","invalid_example":"Patch the final exception without determining which earlier interaction produced the invalid state.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B87-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Testing architecture","concept":"Contract testing for behavioral patterns","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why are contract tests valuable for behavioral patterns?","answer":"They verify that interchangeable participants preserve the behavioral contract expected by the surrounding design.","explanation":"This is especially useful for Strategy implementations, State implementations, handlers, commands, observers, and visitor implementations.","correct_example":"Run the same contract suite against every Strategy implementation.","testing_guidance":"Include valid inputs, invalid inputs, boundary conditions, failure behavior, side effects, and required invariants.","verification_guidance":"Record which contract clauses are tested and which remain unverified.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B87-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Property-based testing","concept":"Property testing for stateful behavior","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"When can property-based testing help behavioral patterns?","answer":"It can explore many sequences of events, state transitions, commands, or collection operations and test general invariants rather than only predefined examples.","explanation":"Stateful behavioral systems often have large interaction spaces that are difficult to cover manually.","correct_example":"Generate valid and invalid state-transition sequences and assert that forbidden states are never reached.","constraint":"Generated tests still require a correct property specification; property-based testing does not prove the property itself.","verification_guidance":"Record the generated input strategy, properties, observed failures, and reproducibility information.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B87-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Regression testing","concept":"Pattern regression boundary","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should behavioral-pattern regression tests protect?","answer":"They should protect public contracts, important collaboration sequences, failure semantics, security boundaries, state invariants, and previously corrected defects.","explanation":"A refactoring can preserve interfaces while silently changing collaboration behavior.","testing_guidance":"Retain regression tests for every verified defect and important protocol guarantee.","verification_guidance":"Run regression tests after pattern substitutions, refactoring, dependency changes, and version migrations.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B88-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Refactoring","concept":"Refactoring toward behavioral patterns","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"When should existing code be refactored toward a behavioral pattern?","answer":"Refactor when repeated or unstable behavioral variation, excessive conditional logic, tangled collaboration, or independently evolving algorithms creates a demonstrated maintenance problem.","explanation":"The existing design should first be understood so the pattern addresses a real force rather than merely changing code shape.","correct_example":"Repeated algorithm branches with independently changing policies are candidates for Strategy extraction.","invalid_example":"Convert every conditional statement into a pattern regardless of complexity.","testing_guidance":"Establish regression coverage before structural refactoring where practical.","verification_guidance":"Compare observable behavior before and after the refactoring.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B88-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Refactoring safety","concept":"Behavior-preserving pattern refactoring","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a behavioral pattern refactoring be verified?","answer":"Verify that externally observable behavior, error semantics, security properties, side effects, ordering requirements, and performance requirements remain within their documented contracts.","explanation":"A structurally cleaner design can still introduce behavioral regressions.","testing_guidance":"Use characterization tests, contract tests, regression tests, and targeted negative tests.","verification_guidance":"Separate verified behavior from assumptions about internal equivalence.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B88-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Pattern removal","concept":"Pattern simplification","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"When should a behavioral pattern be removed?","answer":"Consider removing it when its original design force no longer exists, its variation has collapsed, its indirection dominates its value, or simpler constructs now express the behavior clearly.","explanation":"Patterns are design tools rather than permanent obligations.","correct_example":"Replace a Strategy hierarchy with a direct function when only one stable algorithm remains and no independent variation is required.","verification_guidance":"Verify behavior equivalence and document the reason for pattern removal.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B89-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Version evolution","concept":"Behavioral pattern compatibility","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What should be considered when evolving a behavioral pattern implementation across software versions?","answer":"Preserve or explicitly version public contracts, serialized commands or events, state representations, visitor protocols, strategy identifiers, handler interfaces, and observable failure semantics where compatibility is required.","explanation":"Pattern implementations may expose more compatibility surface than their class interfaces suggest.","edge_case":"Persisted commands or events may outlive the software version that produced them.","solution":"Version durable representations and provide migration or compatibility handling where required.","verification_guidance":"Test old and new representations against the documented compatibility contract.","language":"language-neutral","version":"version-dependent","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B89-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Deprecation","concept":"Pattern participant deprecation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should a behavioral participant be deprecated?","answer":"Deprecate the contract explicitly, identify replacement behavior, preserve compatibility for the supported period, and test mixed-version interactions when applicable.","explanation":"Deprecating a strategy, command type, event, visitor operation, or handler can affect persisted or remotely exchanged behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test both deprecated and replacement paths during the supported compatibility period.","verification_guidance":"Verify that deprecation does not silently alter security or failure semantics.","language":"language-neutral","version":"version-dependent","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.95,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B89-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Compatibility failure","concept":"Behavioral protocol version mismatch","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What can happen when behavioral participants use incompatible protocol versions?","answer":"Participants can reject valid-looking requests, interpret events incorrectly, fail to recognize new states or commands, or produce incompatible results.","cause":"Contract evolution without negotiated or explicitly supported compatibility.","solution":"Version contracts, validate capabilities, and fail explicitly when compatibility is not supported.","security_implication":"Do not silently downgrade security-sensitive behavior to an older protocol without an explicit policy.","testing_guidance":"Test mixed-version participants and unsupported protocol versions.","verification_guidance":"Verify explicit rejection or documented compatibility behavior.","language":"language-neutral","version":"version-dependent","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B90-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Integrated synthesis","concept":"Behavioral pattern decision framework","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What decision sequence can guide behavioral pattern selection?","answer":"Identify the changing behavior or collaboration; define its forces and invariants; determine whether the variation concerns routing, request representation, traversal, coordination, state, algorithm choice, notification, workflow structure, or operations over a stable structure; compare simpler alternatives; select the smallest pattern set that resolves the actual problem; define failure, security, concurrency, and performance semantics; then verify through tests and evidence.","explanation":"Pattern selection should be driven by the problem and required variation rather than by catalog familiarity.","correct_example":"A design explicitly maps each required variation to one behavioral abstraction and rejects unnecessary patterns.","invalid_example":"Select several patterns first and retrofit the problem description afterward.","testing_guidance":"Test the chosen design against normal, boundary, failure, concurrency, and security cases.","verification_guidance":"Record rationale, alternatives, evidence, implementation status, and unresolved assumptions.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B90-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Integrated failure analysis","concept":"Behavioral pattern failure taxonomy","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What broad failure classes should D13 behavioral-pattern implementations consider?","answer":"Contract violations, incorrect routing, invalid state transitions, duplicate execution, stale subscriptions, reentrancy, ordering errors, partial completion, cancellation failures, timeout ambiguity, concurrency races, authorization bypass, data exposure, version mismatch, excessive indirection, and performance bottlenecks should be considered according to applicability.","explanation":"Not every pattern has every failure class; applicability must be determined from the actual architecture.","testing_guidance":"Map applicable failure classes to explicit negative and failure-injection tests.","verification_guidance":"Mark unsupported or untested failure classes as UNKNOWN or NOT_TESTED rather than assuming absence.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D13-DP-B90-0003","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Behavioral Design Patterns","subtopic":"Batch closure","concept":"D13 behavioral pattern quality gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What quality gate should behavioral-pattern knowledge pass before D13 is locked?","answer":"Each covered pattern should have problem/context, intent, applicability, structure, implementation guidance, alternatives, tradeoffs, anti-patterns, failure modes, security implications, performance considerations, testing guidance, verification guidance, version awareness, provenance, stable relationships, and retrieval-oriented terminology.","explanation":"Coverage by pattern name alone is insufficient for a training-grade design-pattern dataset.","constraint":"Claims of execution, benchmark results, or verification require corresponding evidence.","verification_guidance":"Run structural, semantic, deduplication, contradiction, provenance, version, retrieval, and completeness audits before locking D13.","language":"language-neutral","version":"conceptual","evidence_level":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

