{"record_id":"D13-DP-B91-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Pattern Selection","subtopic":"Requirement-to-pattern mapping","concept":"Pattern selection should begin from the concrete design problem and forces rather than from a desire to use a named pattern.","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should a design pattern be selected?","answer":"Identify the problem, context, constraints, forces, required variability, ownership boundaries, lifecycle, and quality attributes before selecting a pattern.","principle":"Patterns are means for resolving recurring design forces, not goals in themselves.","correct_example":"A system needs interchangeable pricing policies selected at runtime; Strategy may be considered after confirming that the variability is algorithmic and the policies share a suitable abstraction.","invalid_example":"Choosing Strategy solely because the code contains several classes.","problem":"Pattern-first design can impose unnecessary abstractions.","failure_mode":"Increased indirection without solving a real design problem.","cause":"Selection based on pattern names instead of design forces.","solution":"Document the problem, forces, alternatives, and consequences before adopting the pattern.","alternative":"Use a direct function, conditional, or simpler abstraction when variability is small and stable.","tradeoff":"Explicit pattern structure can improve extensibility but increases conceptual and maintenance overhead.","constraint":"The selected pattern must fit the actual language and architectural context.","edge_case":"A pattern may solve today's problem but become unnecessary after requirements stabilize.","testing_guidance":"Test externally observable behavior and variation points rather than pattern structure alone.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each introduced participant has a justified responsibility.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D13 pattern fundamentals"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B02-0001","D13-DP-B90-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B02-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B90-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_pattern_literature","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","source":"Pattern selection principles synthesized from established design-pattern literature"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B91-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Design Pattern Selection","subtopic":"Minimum sufficient abstraction","concept":"A pattern should not be introduced when a simpler design satisfies the requirements.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is the minimum sufficient abstraction principle?","answer":"Use the simplest design that satisfies current requirements while preserving explicitly justified future variation points.","principle":"Complexity should be justified by concrete design forces.","correct_example":"Use a direct dependency when there is one stable implementation and no meaningful substitution requirement.","invalid_example":"Introducing Abstract Factory, Factory Method, Strategy, and Dependency Injection for a single stable implementation.","problem":"Premature abstraction increases cognitive and maintenance cost.","failure_mode":"Over-engineered design with unnecessary participants.","cause":"Speculative extensibility.","solution":"Remove abstractions that have no current design justification.","alternative":"Introduce a narrow interface only where an actual boundary or substitution need exists.","tradeoff":"Simpler designs may require later refactoring if requirements genuinely change.","constraint":"Simplicity must not eliminate a requirement that is already known.","edge_case":"Externally imposed extension contracts can justify an abstraction before multiple implementations exist.","testing_guidance":"Compare behavioral requirements before and after simplification.","verification_guidance":"Verify that removing the pattern does not violate explicit extension, isolation, or lifecycle requirements.","security_implication":"Unnecessary abstraction can obscure security-critical control flow.","performance_implication":"Additional dispatch and object creation may introduce overhead, although significance is context-dependent.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D13-DP-B04-0003","D13-DP-B88-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B07-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B92-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Composition","subtopic":"Composition boundaries","concept":"Multiple patterns should be composed only when their responsibilities remain distinguishable and their combined lifecycle is understandable.","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When is pattern composition justified?","answer":"Composition is justified when each pattern addresses a distinct design force and the resulting interactions remain understandable, testable, and maintainable.","principle":"Composed patterns should reduce total design complexity rather than merely increase abstraction.","correct_example":"Strategy selects an algorithm while Observer communicates the resulting state change to independent subscribers.","invalid_example":"Using several overlapping behavioral patterns to route the same event through multiple opaque layers.","problem":"Pattern composition can create emergent control-flow complexity.","failure_mode":"Difficult-to-trace behavior and unclear ownership.","cause":"Overlapping responsibilities and hidden coupling.","solution":"Define ownership, event flow, lifecycle, and failure semantics for every composed pattern.","alternative":"Collapse the composition into a simpler abstraction when responsibilities overlap.","tradeoff":"Composition can improve separation of concerns but increases interaction surfaces.","constraint":"Every composition should have explicit interaction semantics.","edge_case":"Asynchronous composition may make ordering and failure behavior non-obvious.","security_implication":"Composed authorization or event mechanisms must preserve security boundaries and fail safely.","performance_implication":"Each additional dispatch, allocation, subscription, or mediation layer may add overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test each pattern independently and the composed interaction paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify ordering, ownership, failure propagation, cancellation, and lifecycle behavior.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Pattern composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B81-0001","D13-DP-B83-0002"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B81-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D13-DP-B81-0003"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_pattern_literature","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B92-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Composition","subtopic":"Pattern interaction contract","concept":"Pattern composition requires explicit contracts for ownership, invocation direction, lifecycle, error propagation, and state transitions.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified when patterns interact?","answer":"Verify participant responsibilities, invocation direction, lifecycle ownership, state visibility, error propagation, cancellation behavior, and externally observable outcomes.","correct_example":"A Command invokes a receiver operation while an Observer receives a documented post-operation event.","invalid_example":"Subscribers implicitly mutate command state without a defined ownership contract.","problem":"Undocumented interactions produce hidden coupling.","failure_mode":"Behavior depends on accidental ordering or implementation details.","cause":"Missing interaction contracts.","solution":"Define explicit interaction contracts and test them at the composition boundary.","alternative":"Use direct calls when no independent interaction boundary is required.","tradeoff":"Explicit contracts add documentation and test work but improve predictability.","constraint":"Contracts must match actual runtime semantics.","edge_case":"Asynchronous observers may execute after the originating command has returned.","testing_guidance":"Use integration and contract tests around interaction boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Record expected event/order/lifecycle behavior and compare it with observed behavior.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Pattern composition"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B87-0001","D13-DP-B86-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_verification_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B93-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Cross-Language Patterns","subtopic":"Semantic portability","concept":"A design pattern can transfer across languages only when its intent and required semantics are preserved rather than its original class structure being copied literally.","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should patterns be transferred between programming languages?","answer":"Translate the pattern's intent, responsibilities, collaboration, lifecycle, and constraints into idioms supported by the target language.","principle":"Pattern intent is more portable than implementation structure.","correct_example":"Represent Strategy with functions or callable objects in a language where first-class functions naturally express the required substitution.","invalid_example":"Creating unnecessary interface and class hierarchies solely to reproduce a Java-style pattern in a language with native function values.","problem":"Literal pattern translation can create artificial complexity.","failure_mode":"Non-idiomatic architecture and unnecessary boilerplate.","cause":"Treating a pattern diagram as mandatory implementation structure.","solution":"Preserve semantics while using target-language idioms.","alternative":"Use language-native constructs when they directly satisfy the same design force.","tradeoff":"Native constructs may reduce structural similarity to conventional pattern diagrams while improving clarity.","constraint":"Target-language semantics must support required lifecycle, ownership, and substitution guarantees.","edge_case":"Different languages may have materially different concurrency or type-system semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test behavior and contracts rather than structural resemblance to the source-language pattern.","verification_guidance":"Verify semantic equivalence of the intended responsibility and collaboration.","language":"Cross-language","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Cross-language reasoning"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B10-0003","D13-DP-B89-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_design_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B93-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Cross-Language Patterns","subtopic":"Type-system effects","concept":"Static typing, dynamic typing, generics, traits, protocols, interfaces, functions, and metaprogramming can change the implementation shape of a pattern.","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why does language type-system design affect pattern implementation?","answer":"Pattern implementations depend on the abstraction, dispatch, composition, and type mechanisms provided by the language.","principle":"The same design intent may require different implementation mechanisms in different languages.","correct_example":"An interface-based Strategy implementation and a function-valued Strategy implementation can express the same substitution intent.","invalid_example":"Assuming identical class hierarchies are required in every language.","problem":"Ignoring language capabilities causes unnecessary structural complexity.","failure_mode":"Boilerplate-heavy or semantically distorted implementations.","cause":"Pattern catalog treated as syntax rather than design guidance.","solution":"Map participants to the strongest appropriate language construct.","alternative":"Retain explicit classes where lifecycle, state, identity, or dependency boundaries require them.","tradeoff":"Language-native representations can improve brevity while making pattern recognition less visually obvious.","constraint":"The implementation must preserve required behavior and contracts.","edge_case":"Dynamic dispatch can shift some errors from compile time to runtime.","testing_guidance":"Add tests for substitution and contract behavior where static guarantees differ.","verification_guidance":"Verify required invariants independently of the representation.","language":"Cross-language","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D13-DP-B93-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"language_design_and_software_engineering_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B94-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Security","subtopic":"Security boundary analysis","concept":"A design pattern does not inherently make a system secure; security depends on how responsibilities and trust boundaries are implemented.","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should security be evaluated in design-pattern usage?","answer":"Analyze trust boundaries, authority, data flow, validation, failure behavior, lifecycle, and attack surface introduced or obscured by the pattern.","principle":"Security properties must be verified at the system boundary and cannot be inferred from pattern names.","correct_example":"A Proxy enforcing authorization validates identity, authority, target, and failure behavior before delegating access.","invalid_example":"Assuming a Proxy is secure merely because all calls pass through it.","problem":"Pattern labels can create false confidence.","failure_mode":"Security boundary exists conceptually but is bypassable or incorrectly enforced.","cause":"Security reasoning separated from actual control flow.","solution":"Trace every security-sensitive path and verify enforcement points.","alternative":"Use direct explicit authorization checks when they are clearer and sufficient.","tradeoff":"Centralized enforcement can improve consistency but creates a critical boundary requiring strong availability and verification.","constraint":"Security controls must remain effective under errors, concurrency, retries, and partial failures.","edge_case":"A caller may obtain a reference to the underlying implementation and bypass the intended proxy.","testing_guidance":"Use negative authorization tests, boundary tests, bypass tests, and failure-path tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unauthorized operations cannot reach protected operations through alternate paths.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Security fundamentals"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B49-0001","D13-DP-B90-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"secure_software_engineering_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B94-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Security","subtopic":"Observer and event security","concept":"Observer and event-based patterns can create unintended information-flow and authorization risks when subscribers receive data without appropriate isolation.","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security concerns apply to Observer-style event systems?","answer":"Control subscriber authorization, event payload exposure, tenant boundaries, subscription ownership, lifecycle, and event replay or retention behavior.","principle":"Event publication is an information-flow boundary.","correct_example":"Sensitive event payloads are delivered only to authorized subscribers with tenant-aware filtering.","invalid_example":"Publishing complete internal objects to every registered observer.","problem":"Observers can become unintended data recipients.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized information disclosure or cross-boundary data exposure.","cause":"Treating subscription as purely technical rather than an authorization boundary.","solution":"Apply explicit subscription and payload authorization policies.","alternative":"Publish minimal event data and require authorized consumers to retrieve additional data through controlled interfaces.","tradeoff":"Minimal events reduce exposure but may increase downstream lookups.","constraint":"Security policy must apply consistently to synchronous and asynchronous delivery.","edge_case":"Previously authorized subscribers may retain events after their authorization is revoked.","security_implication":"Event systems require lifecycle-aware access control and data minimization.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized subscriptions, cross-tenant delivery, revoked access, replay, and malformed events.","verification_guidance":"Verify recipient authorization and payload classification at publication and delivery boundaries.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Observer pattern","Security boundaries"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B83-0002","D13-DP-B85-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"secure_event_system_design","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B95-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Performance","subtopic":"Indirection cost","concept":"Patterns may introduce additional calls, allocations, dispatch, synchronization, subscriptions, or traversal compared with direct implementations.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How should pattern performance overhead be evaluated?","answer":"Measure or otherwise analyze the actual workload and identify the operations added by the pattern; do not assume a universal performance penalty.","principle":"Performance impact is workload- and implementation-dependent.","correct_example":"Benchmark a hot-path Strategy dispatch when profiling identifies that dispatch frequency may materially affect runtime.","invalid_example":"Declaring all Strategy implementations slow without workload evidence.","problem":"Unmeasured assumptions can cause unnecessary optimization or rejection of useful abstractions.","failure_mode":"Incorrect performance conclusions.","cause":"Confusing structural indirection with measured bottleneck impact.","solution":"Profile and benchmark representative workloads when performance matters.","alternative":"Use simpler dispatch or specialized implementations only when evidence justifies it.","tradeoff":"Lower abstraction overhead may reduce flexibility or maintainability.","constraint":"Benchmarks must reflect realistic workload, runtime, compiler/interpreter, and configuration conditions.","edge_case":"Optimization effects can differ substantially across language runtimes and versions.","testing_guidance":"Separate correctness tests from performance tests.","verification_guidance":"Record workload, environment, metric, method, and observed result for any performance claim.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Performance measurement"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B50-0002","D13-DP-B84-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","performance_implication":"No numeric performance claim is asserted without measurement."}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B95-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Performance","subtopic":"Allocation and lifecycle","concept":"Creational and structural patterns can alter allocation frequency, object lifetime, caching behavior, and memory retention.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What lifecycle performance effects should be considered?","answer":"Analyze object creation frequency, retention, cache behavior, synchronization, cleanup, and memory ownership where these factors affect workload performance.","principle":"Object lifecycle is part of pattern performance analysis.","correct_example":"Evaluating Flyweight usage by measuring whether shared state actually reduces memory pressure without creating excessive synchronization or lookup overhead.","invalid_example":"Assuming Flyweight always improves performance because it shares objects.","problem":"Sharing can introduce lookup, synchronization, or lifecycle complexity.","failure_mode":"Memory or latency behavior worsens despite reduced object count.","cause":"Optimizing one resource dimension without measuring total cost.","solution":"Measure memory, allocation, latency, contention, and lifecycle behavior under representative workloads.","alternative":"Use ordinary objects when sharing provides no meaningful benefit.","tradeoff":"Sharing may reduce memory use while increasing coordination complexity.","constraint":"Correctness and ownership semantics must remain explicit.","edge_case":"Weak references, caches, and garbage collection behavior vary by language/runtime.","testing_guidance":"Use workload-specific performance and memory tests where relevant.","verification_guidance":"Verify that lifecycle assumptions match the actual runtime behavior.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Object lifecycle"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B50-0002","D13-DP-B95-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B96-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Testing","subtopic":"Pattern-oriented test design","concept":"Tests should validate the behavior and contracts that motivated the pattern rather than requiring a specific internal pattern structure.","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should design patterns be tested?","answer":"Test observable behavior, variation points, invariants, failure semantics, lifecycle, security boundaries, and interaction contracts introduced by the design.","principle":"Tests should protect requirements rather than implementation labels.","correct_example":"A Strategy test verifies that equivalent strategy contracts produce valid outcomes and invalid strategies fail according to the defined contract.","invalid_example":"A test that only asserts that a class named Strategy exists.","problem":"Structure-only tests can pass while behavior is broken.","failure_mode":"False confidence in pattern correctness.","cause":"Testing implementation shape instead of system behavior.","solution":"Build tests from requirements, contracts, and failure modes.","alternative":"Use structural checks only when architecture explicitly requires structural constraints.","tradeoff":"Behavior-focused tests tolerate refactoring better but may require more scenario design.","constraint":"Tests must reflect actual requirements and contracts.","edge_case":"Pattern internals may legitimately change during optimization or refactoring.","testing_guidance":"Cover positive, negative, boundary, lifecycle, concurrency, and interaction scenarios as applicable.","verification_guidance":"Map each critical requirement to observable evidence.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Software testing","Design contracts"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B87-0001","D13-DP-B87-0002","D13-DP-B87-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_testing_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B96-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Testing","subtopic":"Failure injection","concept":"Pattern interactions should be tested under failure conditions when the pattern influences propagation, recovery, retries, cancellation, or state transitions.","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"When should failure injection be used for pattern-based designs?","answer":"Use controlled failure injection when pattern behavior depends on downstream failure, timeout, cancellation, partial completion, unavailable participants, or recovery semantics.","principle":"Failure paths are part of the pattern's effective behavior.","correct_example":"Injecting receiver failure into a Command workflow and verifying rollback or failure propagation according to the defined contract.","invalid_example":"Testing only successful command execution.","problem":"Happy-path-only tests leave interaction failures unverified.","failure_mode":"Production failures reveal undefined propagation or inconsistent state.","cause":"Incomplete behavioral model.","solution":"Define and test failure semantics explicitly.","alternative":"Use deterministic mocks or fault-injection harnesses appropriate to the environment.","tradeoff":"Failure testing increases test complexity but exposes hidden coupling and lifecycle defects.","constraint":"Injected failures must be controlled and reproducible.","edge_case":"Concurrent failures can expose ordering-sensitive defects.","testing_guidance":"Include timeout, cancellation, partial failure, retry, duplicate invocation, and recovery cases where applicable.","verification_guidance":"Compare observed failure behavior against the documented contract.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure analysis"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B84-0002","D13-DP-B84-0003","D13-DP-B90-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_testing_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B97-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Refactoring","subtopic":"Pattern-to-pattern migration","concept":"A pattern can be replaced by another pattern or a simpler design when requirements and forces change.","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a pattern be migrated when it no longer fits?","answer":"Characterize current behavior, identify changed forces, define the target design, establish compatibility requirements, migrate incrementally where necessary, and verify behavior before removing the old structure.","principle":"Patterns are replaceable design mechanisms rather than permanent commitments.","correct_example":"Replacing an Observer implementation with an explicit event interface when delivery semantics and ownership requirements become stricter.","invalid_example":"Deleting the pattern structure without preserving required behavior.","problem":"Pattern persistence can become technical debt when its original forces disappear.","failure_mode":"Complexity remains after the underlying problem has changed.","cause":"Failure to reassess design assumptions.","solution":"Perform evidence-based refactoring and verify behavior after migration.","alternative":"Retain the pattern if its original forces remain valid.","tradeoff":"Migration has short-term cost but may reduce long-term complexity.","constraint":"Compatibility requirements must be identified before migration.","edge_case":"External consumers may depend on behavior not documented by the pattern implementation.","testing_guidance":"Use characterization, regression, contract, and integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify both intended behavior and compatibility obligations.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Refactoring","Regression testing"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B88-0001","D13-DP-B88-0002","D13-DP-B88-0003"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B88-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"refactoring_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B97-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Refactoring","subtopic":"Pattern debt","concept":"Pattern debt occurs when an existing pattern creates more ongoing complexity than the design value it provides.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is pattern debt?","answer":"Pattern debt is accumulated complexity caused by retaining or composing abstractions whose design benefits no longer justify their cost.","principle":"Pattern value must be reassessed as requirements, implementations, and system boundaries evolve.","correct_example":"Removing an obsolete mediator after the participating components become stable and direct collaboration is clearer.","invalid_example":"Keeping every historical abstraction solely because it was once justified.","problem":"Historical design decisions can become unnecessary constraints.","failure_mode":"Excessive indirection and difficult maintenance.","cause":"No periodic architectural reassessment.","solution":"Review pattern participants, forces, coupling, tests, and maintenance cost.","alternative":"Refactor incrementally when evidence indicates declining value.","tradeoff":"Removing pattern debt creates migration risk that must be controlled with tests and compatibility analysis.","constraint":"Do not remove abstractions that still enforce required contracts or boundaries.","edge_case":"A seemingly unused pattern may preserve an external extension or compatibility contract.","testing_guidance":"Use characterization and regression tests before removal.","verification_guidance":"Verify that required behavior and contracts remain intact after simplification.","language":"Language-agnostic","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Pattern lifecycle","Refactoring"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B04-0003","D13-DP-B88-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"software_maintenance_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"ANTI_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B98-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Knowledge Representation","subtopic":"Pattern record completeness","concept":"A training-grade pattern record should represent intent, context, forces, structure, behavior, consequences, alternatives, failure modes, testing, security, performance, and version context when applicable.","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What information makes a design-pattern record useful for autonomous software engineering?","answer":"A useful record connects the design problem to context, forces, solution structure, consequences, alternatives, implementation constraints, failure modes, verification, and applicable language or version semantics.","principle":"Pattern knowledge must support reasoning, not merely pattern-name retrieval.","correct_example":"A record links a runtime substitution problem to Strategy, explains tradeoffs, compares alternatives, and defines verification conditions.","invalid_example":"A record containing only the pattern name and a class diagram.","problem":"Shallow pattern descriptions cannot support reliable design reasoning.","failure_mode":"Pattern retrieval without correct applicability judgment.","cause":"Insufficient context and consequence information.","solution":"Represent pattern knowledge as structured, relational evidence.","alternative":"Use smaller records when the domain is narrow, while preserving required relationships.","tradeoff":"Richer records increase dataset complexity but support more reasoning tasks.","constraint":"Records must avoid fabricated evidence and execution claims.","edge_case":"Some patterns have multiple valid implementations depending on language and runtime.","testing_guidance":"Validate records for schema completeness and contradiction consistency.","verification_guidance":"Check whether each major claim has appropriate evidence level and provenance.","language":"English","version":"Dataset-level","version_range":"D13 v1.0","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Dataset schema","Pattern fundamentals"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B01-0001","D13-DP-B01-0002","D13-DP-B02-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B01-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":["D13-DP-B01-0002"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_ontology","evidence_level":"STRUCTURAL"},"evidence_level":"RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B98-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"Pattern Knowledge Representation","subtopic":"Retrieval variants","concept":"The same pattern concept should be retrievable through names, intents, problems, symptoms, alternatives, implementation forms, and failure descriptions.","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should pattern knowledge support retrieval?","answer":"Index pattern records using canonical concepts and multiple semantically equivalent query forms, including problem statements, design symptoms, intent descriptions, tradeoffs, alternatives, and implementation terminology.","principle":"Retrieval should map natural engineering questions to design concepts.","correct_example":"A query describing interchangeable runtime algorithms retrieves Strategy records even when the query does not contain the word Strategy.","invalid_example":"Requiring the exact pattern name for retrieval.","problem":"Name-only retrieval misses intent-driven engineering questions.","failure_mode":"Low conceptual recall.","cause":"Lexical indexing without semantic problem representation.","solution":"Store canonical concepts, synonyms, problem formulations, and relationships.","alternative":"Use embedding or semantic retrieval where available, combined with exact identifiers.","tradeoff":"Broader retrieval improves recall but can increase irrelevant results.","constraint":"Retrieval evaluation must distinguish relevant from merely similar records.","edge_case":"Different patterns can legitimately solve closely related problems.","testing_guidance":"Test exact, conceptual, natural-language, comparison, debugging, and design queries.","verification_guidance":"Record expected relevant records and measure retrieval outcomes when the evaluation system is available.","language":"English","version":"Dataset-level","version_range":"D13 v1.0","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Retrieval testing"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B10-0003","D13-DP-B98-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B98-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"retrieval_engineering_principle","evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL"},"evidence_level":"RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B99-0001","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"D13 Integrated Validation","subtopic":"Contradiction analysis","concept":"Pattern knowledge contradictions should be resolved by checking intent, context, language, version, scope, evidence level, and execution evidence before declaring conflict.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should contradictions in pattern knowledge be resolved?","answer":"Compare the competing claims against their context, applicability conditions, language semantics, version, source authority, and evidence; preserve a conflict when no justified resolution exists.","principle":"Apparently contradictory pattern guidance may be context-dependent rather than genuinely inconsistent.","correct_example":"Treating two different performance observations as context-specific when their workloads and runtime environments differ.","invalid_example":"Deleting one claim merely because the other was encountered later.","problem":"Unqualified contradiction resolution can destroy valid contextual knowledge.","failure_mode":"Incorrect canonicalization.","cause":"Ignoring scope and evidence metadata.","solution":"Perform structured contradiction analysis and preserve unresolved conflicts explicitly.","alternative":"Mark the record CONFLICTED when evidence is insufficient.","tradeoff":"Conflict preservation reduces false certainty but requires downstream reasoning support.","constraint":"No contradiction may be silently resolved without evidence.","edge_case":"Older guidance may remain valid for legacy versions while newer guidance applies to current versions.","testing_guidance":"Run contradiction-detection checks across related records.","verification_guidance":"Require provenance and contextual evidence for resolved contradictions.","language":"English","version":"Dataset-level","version_range":"D13 v1.0","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Provenance","Version awareness"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B89-0001","D13-DP-B90-0002"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B89-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_quality_method","evidence_level":"STRUCTURAL"},"evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D13-DP-B99-0002","dataset_id":"D13","topic":"D13 Integrated Validation","subtopic":"Deduplication","concept":"Deduplication must preserve distinct context, version, language, and evidence rather than collapsing records merely because they share a concept.","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should duplicate design-pattern knowledge be handled?","answer":"Remove exact and semantically redundant records while retaining records whose context, version, language, failure mode, or evidence materially differs.","principle":"Deduplication should remove redundancy without destroying contextual knowledge.","correct_example":"Merge two identical Strategy definitions while retaining separate records for Strategy-specific concurrency or language semantics.","invalid_example":"Merging every record mentioning Strategy into one record.","problem":"Aggressive deduplication can erase useful context.","failure_mode":"Loss of specialized knowledge.","cause":"Concept-only duplicate detection.","solution":"Use exact, semantic, contextual, and version-aware deduplication.","alternative":"Link related records instead of merging when their purposes differ.","tradeoff":"Conservative deduplication retains more records but preserves information density and traceability.","constraint":"Stable record IDs and relationships must remain valid after deduplication.","edge_case":"Two records may use different wording but encode the same rule.","testing_guidance":"Run exact, near-duplicate, and semantic duplicate checks.","verification_guidance":"Verify retained records remain distinguishable by context or evidence.","language":"English","version":"Dataset-level","version_range":"D13 v1.0","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Dataset identity model"],"related_records":["D13-DP-B10-0003","D13-DP-B98-0001"],"depends_on":["D13-DP-B98-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_quality_method","evidence_level":"STRUCTURAL"},"evidence_level":"RULE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

