{"record_id":"D14-AP-B51-0001","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Database Architecture","subtopic":"Shared database","concept":"Shared Database pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Shared Database architecture pattern?","answer":"Multiple application components or services use a common database as a shared persistence boundary.","explanation":"A shared database can simplify transactions and reporting but creates coupling through shared schemas, ownership, and operational dependencies.","principle":"Shared persistence should have explicit ownership and access boundaries.","correct_example":"Several tightly coupled modules use a common database with clearly owned tables and controlled interfaces.","invalid_example":"Independent services freely modify each other's tables without ownership rules.","failure_mode":"Schema changes in one component unexpectedly break others.","cause":"Database structure becomes an implicit shared API.","solution":"Define ownership, access contracts, migration governance, and dependency boundaries.","alternative":"Database-per-service or separately owned persistence.","tradeoff":"Shared persistence can simplify consistency but reduces independent evolution.","security_implication":"Database-level permissions should restrict components to authorized data domains.","performance_implication":"Shared workloads can compete for the same database resources.","testing_guidance":"Test schema migrations, concurrent access, ownership boundaries, and cross-module queries.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every table and mutation path has an explicit owner.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D14-AP-B51-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D14-AP-B52-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"data_architecture_pattern","title":"Shared Database pattern","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Shared persistence boundaries"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B51-0002","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Database Architecture","subtopic":"Schema ownership","concept":"Database schema ownership","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why is schema ownership important in a shared database architecture?","answer":"Explicit schema ownership identifies which component is responsible for defining, changing, validating, and maintaining each persistence structure.","explanation":"Without ownership, database tables become implicit integration contracts that are difficult to evolve safely.","principle":"Every persistent structure should have a clear authoritative owner.","correct_example":"The billing module owns billing tables while other modules consume approved interfaces rather than modifying them directly.","invalid_example":"Any service can add columns or alter constraints in another service's tables.","failure_mode":"Uncoordinated schema evolution.","cause":"No authoritative schema owner.","solution":"Establish ownership and controlled migration processes.","security_implication":"Ownership boundaries should align with least-privilege database permissions.","performance_implication":"Cross-owner queries may create hidden coupling and resource contention.","testing_guidance":"Test migration compatibility and unauthorized schema mutation paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify that schema changes are traceable to an explicit owner.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":["D14-AP-B51-0001"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"data_architecture_principle","title":"Persistence ownership","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Database governance"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B52-0001","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Database Architecture","subtopic":"Service persistence","concept":"Database per Service","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Database per Service pattern?","answer":"Each independently owned service controls its own persistence boundary rather than allowing other services to directly access its database.","explanation":"The pattern strengthens service autonomy and prevents database schemas from becoming direct inter-service contracts.","principle":"A service should own its persistent state and expose behavior through explicit interfaces.","correct_example":"Order Service owns order storage while Inventory Service accesses inventory through an API or event contract.","invalid_example":"Inventory Service directly queries Order Service's private database tables.","failure_mode":"Independent services become coupled through persistence implementation details.","cause":"Direct cross-service database access.","solution":"Expose explicit service contracts and transfer only required information.","alternative":"Shared database for intentionally cohesive modules.","tradeoff":"Database isolation improves autonomy but can make distributed queries and transactions more complex.","security_implication":"Independent persistence boundaries reduce unauthorized cross-domain data access.","performance_implication":"Service APIs or events may add network and serialization overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test service contracts, independent migrations, failure isolation, and data ownership.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no unauthorized service directly accesses another service's persistence boundary.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D14-AP-B52-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D14-AP-B51-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"service_data_architecture","title":"Database per Service pattern","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Service data ownership"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B52-0002","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Database Architecture","subtopic":"Cross-service data","concept":"Data ownership boundary","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What constraint follows from database-per-service architecture?","answer":"A service must not depend on another service's internal database schema as its primary integration contract.","explanation":"Direct persistence coupling defeats the intended autonomy of separate data owners.","principle":"Integrate through explicit contracts rather than another component's implementation details.","correct_example":"A reporting component consumes an approved event or query API rather than joining another service's private tables.","invalid_example":"A service performs direct SQL joins against another service's private database.","failure_mode":"Independent deployments become unsafe or impossible.","cause":"Persistence implementation becomes an external dependency.","solution":"Use APIs, events, replication pipelines, or purpose-built read models according to requirements.","security_implication":"Data exposure can be controlled through explicit interfaces.","performance_implication":"Cross-boundary data retrieval may require additional infrastructure.","testing_guidance":"Test contract compatibility and behavior when the producer changes its internal schema.","verification_guidance":"Inspect dependency graphs for unauthorized persistence-level coupling.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":["D14-AP-B52-0001"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"service_boundary_principle","title":"Data ownership boundaries","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Independent service evolution"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B53-0001","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Polyglot Persistence","subtopic":"Persistence technology selection","concept":"Polyglot Persistence","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is polyglot persistence?","answer":"Polyglot persistence uses different persistence technologies for different workloads or data characteristics within an architecture.","explanation":"Different workloads may have materially different access, consistency, query, scale, or storage requirements.","principle":"Select persistence technology according to explicit workload and data requirements.","correct_example":"Transactional data uses a relational store while a specialized search workload uses a search-oriented index.","invalid_example":"Different databases are introduced without distinct requirements or operational ownership.","failure_mode":"Technology sprawl increases operational complexity without corresponding value.","cause":"Persistence selection driven by novelty rather than workload requirements.","solution":"Document the requirement and operational justification for each persistence technology.","alternative":"Single general-purpose persistence technology.","tradeoff":"Specialized stores can improve workload fit but increase operational and integration complexity.","security_implication":"Each persistence system requires independent access control, encryption, backup, and audit considerations.","performance_implication":"Specialized stores may improve specific workloads but add synchronization and operational costs.","testing_guidance":"Test each store and all synchronization or translation paths between them.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each persistence technology has a measurable or documented architectural purpose.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D14-AP-B53-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"data_architecture_pattern","title":"Polyglot Persistence","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Workload-specific persistence"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B53-0002","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Polyglot Persistence","subtopic":"Technology sprawl","concept":"Unjustified persistence diversity","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"When does polyglot persistence become an architectural anti-pattern?","answer":"It becomes problematic when multiple persistence technologies are introduced without materially different requirements, resulting in unnecessary operational, security, migration, and consistency complexity.","explanation":"Technology diversity is not inherently beneficial; every additional persistence system creates another operational boundary.","principle":"Persistence diversity should be requirement-driven and operationally supportable.","correct_example":"A specialized persistence technology is introduced only after its workload requirements cannot be adequately served by the existing platform.","invalid_example":"Each team selects a different database for similar workloads solely for local preference.","failure_mode":"Excessive operational fragmentation.","cause":"Local optimization without system-level governance.","solution":"Require explicit workload justification and lifecycle ownership for each persistence technology.","security_implication":"Security controls may become inconsistent across stores.","performance_implication":"Operational overhead can offset workload-specific performance gains.","testing_guidance":"Test backup, restore, failover, migration, access control, and data consistency across every store.","verification_guidance":"Review each persistence technology against documented architectural requirements.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":["D14-AP-B53-0001"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"architecture_anti_pattern","title":"Unjustified persistence diversity","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Operational complexity control"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B54-0001","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Read Replica Architecture","subtopic":"Read scaling","concept":"Read Replica","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does a read replica address?","answer":"A read replica provides additional copies of persisted data that can serve eligible read workloads, reducing pressure on the primary persistence system.","explanation":"Read replicas can separate some read traffic from write processing but introduce replication lag and additional operational complexity.","principle":"Scale read workloads only when the consistency model permits replicated reads.","correct_example":"A reporting workload reads from a replica with documented replication lag tolerance.","invalid_example":"A security-sensitive authorization decision reads from a lagging replica without considering revocation freshness.","failure_mode":"Stale data is used where current data is required.","cause":"Replica consistency semantics are ignored.","solution":"Classify workloads by freshness requirements and route them accordingly.","alternative":"Read scaling through caching, partitioning, or query optimization.","tradeoff":"Replicas can improve read capacity but complicate consistency and failover.","security_implication":"Sensitive reads must account for replication lag and authorization state freshness.","performance_implication":"Read throughput can increase, while replication consumes resources and network bandwidth.","testing_guidance":"Test lag, replica failure, promotion, stale reads, and routing behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify each replica-backed workload against its allowed staleness budget.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D14-AP-B54-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"data_scaling_pattern","title":"Read Replica pattern","publisher":"Conceptual data architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Read scaling"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B54-0002","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Read Replica Architecture","subtopic":"Replication lag","concept":"Replica lag budget","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What is a replica lag budget?","answer":"A replica lag budget defines the maximum acceptable staleness for a workload that reads from a replicated data source.","explanation":"Different workloads tolerate different levels of replication delay.","principle":"Make data freshness an explicit architectural requirement.","correct_example":"An analytics dashboard accepts a documented delay while transactional authorization reads require current authoritative state.","invalid_example":"All reads are routed to replicas without considering freshness requirements.","failure_mode":"Incorrect decisions based on stale state.","cause":"No workload-specific freshness classification.","solution":"Define and enforce freshness requirements per operation.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive state often requires stricter freshness guarantees.","performance_implication":"Lower lag requirements can require additional replication capacity or stronger coordination.","testing_guidance":"Inject replication delay and verify behavior at and beyond the allowed threshold.","verification_guidance":"Measure or observe actual lag where the architecture claims a bounded lag guarantee.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":["D14-AP-B54-0001"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_consistency_principle","title":"Replica lag budgets","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Freshness requirements"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B55-0001","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Caching Architecture","subtopic":"Cache-aside","concept":"Cache-Aside","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Cache-Aside pattern?","answer":"In Cache-Aside, the application checks the cache first, retrieves data from the authoritative source on a miss, and then populates the cache.","explanation":"The application explicitly controls cache population and treats the underlying source as authoritative.","principle":"Cache derived data while preserving a clear authoritative source.","correct_example":"A service checks the cache, reads the database on a miss, and stores the retrieved representation with a defined expiration policy.","invalid_example":"The cache is treated as the only authoritative copy without a recovery strategy.","failure_mode":"Stale or missing cached data causes incorrect application behavior.","cause":"Cache semantics are not explicitly defined.","solution":"Define authority, invalidation, expiration, consistency, and failure behavior.","alternative":"Write-through or write-behind caching where requirements justify them.","tradeoff":"Cache-aside is flexible but requires careful invalidation and miss handling.","security_implication":"Cached data must enforce tenant and authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Caching can reduce repeated backend reads but adds memory, serialization, and invalidation overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test hits, misses, expiration, invalidation, cache outage, stale entries, and concurrent population.","verification_guidance":"Verify that cache failures do not silently change the authoritative data semantics.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D14-AP-B55-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"caching_architecture_pattern","title":"Cache-Aside pattern","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Derived data caching"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B55-0002","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Caching Architecture","subtopic":"Invalidation","concept":"Cache invalidation correctness","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is cache invalidation a major architectural concern?","answer":"A cache can continue serving a previously valid representation after the authoritative state changes unless expiration, invalidation, versioning, or another freshness mechanism removes or supersedes it.","explanation":"Caching introduces a second representation of data that can diverge from its source.","principle":"Every cached representation requires an explicit freshness or invalidation model.","correct_example":"A cached resource includes a defined expiration and is invalidated when authoritative changes require immediate freshness.","invalid_example":"Cached authorization or pricing data remains indefinitely valid after the underlying policy changes.","failure_mode":"Stale data is served beyond its permitted lifetime.","cause":"Undefined invalidation or freshness semantics.","solution":"Define TTL, event-based invalidation, version keys, or authoritative bypass rules according to requirements.","security_implication":"Stale authorization, identity, or tenant data can create security violations.","performance_implication":"Aggressive invalidation reduces cache effectiveness; long TTLs increase staleness risk.","testing_guidance":"Change authoritative data and verify cache behavior under every defined freshness path.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual freshness behavior against documented guarantees.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":["D14-AP-B55-0001"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"cache_consistency_analysis","title":"Cache invalidation correctness","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Cache consistency"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B56-0001","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Caching Architecture","subtopic":"Write-through caching","concept":"Write-Through Cache","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the Write-Through caching pattern?","answer":"A write-through cache updates the cache as part of a write operation while coordinating persistence to the underlying authoritative store according to defined semantics.","explanation":"The pattern can reduce subsequent read misses but requires clear ordering and failure behavior between cache and persistence.","principle":"Write paths must define which storage layer is authoritative and how failures are reconciled.","correct_example":"A successful write updates the authoritative store and maintains the corresponding cache representation according to a defined protocol.","invalid_example":"The system considers a cache write successful even though authoritative persistence failed, without reconciliation semantics.","failure_mode":"Cache and authoritative state diverge.","cause":"Unclear write ordering or failure handling.","solution":"Define transactional or compensating behavior and authoritative-state semantics.","alternative":"Cache-aside.","tradeoff":"Write-through can improve cache coherence but increases write-path complexity.","security_implication":"Cache writes must preserve tenant and authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Write latency may increase because cache and persistence must be coordinated.","testing_guidance":"Test failures at each write stage and recovery after partial success.","verification_guidance":"Verify convergence between cache and authoritative persistence after failures.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D14-AP-B56-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D14-AP-B55-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"caching_architecture_pattern","title":"Write-Through caching","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Write-path cache consistency"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B56-0002","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Caching Architecture","subtopic":"Write-behind","concept":"Write-Behind Cache","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What architectural tradeoff does Write-Behind caching introduce?","answer":"Write-Behind caching can acknowledge or retain writes before the authoritative persistence operation completes, potentially improving write throughput while introducing durability, ordering, and failure-recovery complexity.","explanation":"The architecture must explicitly define what a successful write acknowledgment means.","principle":"Never imply durable authority before the required durability boundary has actually been satisfied.","correct_example":"The system documents that an accepted write is durably queued but not yet committed to the authoritative store.","invalid_example":"The system reports durable success when the only copy exists in volatile cache memory.","failure_mode":"Acknowledged data is lost after cache failure.","cause":"Durability semantics are ambiguous.","solution":"Define durable queueing, persistence guarantees, replay, and recovery behavior.","security_implication":"Sensitive writes require appropriate durability and audit guarantees.","performance_implication":"Write-behind can improve throughput but adds asynchronous persistence latency.","testing_guidance":"Test cache failure, process crash, queue loss, ordering, retries, and recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify that reported write status matches actual durability guarantees.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":["D14-AP-B56-0001"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"caching_architecture_pattern","title":"Write-Behind caching","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Asynchronous persistence"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B57-0001","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Materialized View Architecture","subtopic":"Precomputed reads","concept":"Materialized View","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a Materialized View architecture pattern?","answer":"A materialized view stores a precomputed representation of data optimized for specific queries or consumers rather than calculating the complete result for every request.","explanation":"The view is derived from authoritative source data and must have explicit refresh and consistency semantics.","principle":"Precompute expensive or repeated queries when the resulting freshness and maintenance costs are acceptable.","correct_example":"A reporting view stores pre-aggregated daily metrics generated from authoritative transactional records.","invalid_example":"A materialized view is treated as authoritative despite lacking a reliable update path.","failure_mode":"Derived data becomes permanently stale or inconsistent.","cause":"No refresh, reconciliation, or authority model.","solution":"Define refresh mechanisms, lag guarantees, rebuild procedures, and source ownership.","alternative":"On-demand queries or caching.","tradeoff":"Materialized views improve read performance but require storage and refresh processing.","security_implication":"Derived views must enforce appropriate data access and tenant boundaries.","performance_implication":"Complex read queries can become cheaper at request time while refresh consumes resources.","testing_guidance":"Test refresh, rebuild, stale data, partial updates, and source correction.","verification_guidance":"Verify view contents against authoritative source data according to the defined consistency window.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D14-AP-B57-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"data_read_model_pattern","title":"Materialized View pattern","publisher":"Conceptual data architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Precomputed query architecture"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B57-0002","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Materialized View Architecture","subtopic":"Rebuildability","concept":"Derived view rebuildability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should important materialized views be rebuildable?","answer":"A rebuild path allows derived state to be reconstructed from authoritative sources after corruption, logic changes, migration, or processing failure.","explanation":"Derived data should not become an irreplaceable source of truth unless explicitly designed as such.","principle":"Critical derived state should have a deterministic recovery path from authoritative inputs.","correct_example":"A reporting view can be regenerated from retained source records using a versioned transformation.","invalid_example":"A corrupted derived table cannot be reconstructed because the original inputs were discarded.","failure_mode":"Permanent loss of derived information or inability to correct transformation errors.","cause":"Derived state is treated as an undocumented authority.","solution":"Retain sufficient authoritative inputs and define rebuild procedures.","security_implication":"Rebuild processes require controlled access to source data.","performance_implication":"Large rebuilds may require significant compute and storage capacity.","testing_guidance":"Perform controlled rebuild tests and compare results with expected invariants.","verification_guidance":"Verify that rebuild output converges to the expected representation from authoritative inputs.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":["D14-AP-B57-0001"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"data_recovery_architecture","title":"Derived view rebuildability","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Derived-state recovery"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B58-0001","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Change Data Capture","subtopic":"Data change propagation","concept":"Change Data Capture (CDC)","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does Change Data Capture address?","answer":"Change Data Capture propagates changes from a source data store into downstream consumers without requiring every consumer to directly query the source for every change.","explanation":"CDC can support integration, replication, analytics, search indexing, and derived-state maintenance when its semantics are correctly defined.","principle":"Treat captured changes as an integration stream with explicit ordering, completeness, and delivery semantics.","correct_example":"Database changes are captured and transformed into downstream events for a reporting pipeline.","invalid_example":"Consumers assume CDC events are always complete, ordered, and immediately available without verifying the source mechanism's guarantees.","failure_mode":"Downstream state diverges from the source.","cause":"CDC semantics are assumed rather than defined.","solution":"Document capture scope, ordering, duplication, transaction boundaries, deletion handling, and recovery.","security_implication":"Captured changes may expose sensitive source data to additional systems.","performance_implication":"CDC adds capture, transport, storage, and downstream processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test inserts, updates, deletes, transactions, schema changes, duplicates, gaps, and recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify downstream state against source data using reconciliation where correctness requires it.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D14-AP-B58-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"data_integration_pattern","title":"Change Data Capture architecture","publisher":"Conceptual data architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Data change propagation"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B58-0002","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Change Data Capture","subtopic":"Change completeness","concept":"CDC reconciliation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should CDC pipelines verify that downstream state remains complete?","answer":"Where correctness requires it, downstream consumers should have a reconciliation mechanism that can compare derived state or captured changes against an authoritative source or independently verifiable checkpoint.","explanation":"A CDC stream can experience gaps, duplicates, ordering issues, or transformation errors depending on the implementation.","principle":"Streaming propagation should have an independent mechanism for detecting divergence when divergence matters.","correct_example":"A consumer periodically compares aggregate counts or version checkpoints against the authoritative source.","invalid_example":"The system assumes downstream state is correct solely because the CDC process reports that it is running.","failure_mode":"Silent divergence persists undetected.","cause":"No independent correctness verification.","solution":"Introduce reconciliation, checkpoints, sequence tracking, or source comparison appropriate to the system.","security_implication":"Reconciliation must preserve access controls and avoid exposing source data unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"Reconciliation adds periodic workload and may require efficient sampling or checkpoint strategies.","testing_guidance":"Inject missing and duplicate changes and verify detection and recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify reconciliation detects intentionally introduced divergence.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":["D14-AP-B58-0001"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"data_consistency_verification","title":"CDC reconciliation","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Change propagation verification"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B59-0001","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Mesh Architecture","subtopic":"Domain-owned data","concept":"Data Mesh","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What architectural idea is central to Data Mesh?","answer":"Data Mesh treats data products as domain-owned assets and distributes responsibility for producing and maintaining usable data across domain teams while applying shared governance and platform capabilities.","explanation":"The approach changes organizational and architectural ownership of analytical data rather than merely changing database technology.","principle":"Data ownership should align with domain knowledge while interoperability and governance remain explicit.","correct_example":"A domain team owns a documented data product with quality, access, schema, and lifecycle responsibilities.","invalid_example":"Data is distributed across teams but no team owns quality, semantics, or consumer contracts.","failure_mode":"Distributed data becomes fragmented and inconsistent.","cause":"Decentralization without product ownership and governance.","solution":"Define data-product ownership, contracts, discoverability, interoperability, and shared governance.","alternative":"Centralized enterprise data platform or warehouse architecture.","tradeoff":"Domain ownership can improve semantic alignment but requires organizational maturity and platform support.","security_implication":"Domain-level ownership must operate within centralized security, privacy, and governance constraints.","performance_implication":"Distributed data products can reduce some central bottlenecks but introduce additional integration paths.","testing_guidance":"Test data contracts, quality rules, lineage, access policies, and interoperability.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each data product has explicit ownership, contract, quality expectations, and lifecycle.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D14-AP-B59-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"data_mesh_architecture","title":"Data Mesh architecture concept","publisher":"Conceptual data architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Domain-oriented data ownership"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B59-0002","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Mesh Architecture","subtopic":"Data products","concept":"Data product contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should a domain-owned data product contract define?","answer":"A data product contract should define semantic meaning, schema, ownership, quality expectations, access conditions, compatibility rules, freshness expectations, lineage, and lifecycle responsibilities.","explanation":"Treating data as a product requires consumers to understand what they can rely on and producers to own the resulting obligations.","principle":"A reusable data product requires an explicit consumer-facing contract.","correct_example":"A data product documents field semantics, freshness expectations, quality constraints, access policy, and version compatibility.","invalid_example":"A dataset is published without ownership, semantics, freshness, or compatibility information.","failure_mode":"Consumers build incompatible assumptions around undocumented data.","cause":"Data is exposed as raw storage rather than a governed product.","solution":"Create explicit contracts and automated validation where feasible.","security_implication":"Contracts must define permitted consumers and sensitive-data handling requirements.","performance_implication":"Quality and freshness guarantees may require additional processing and infrastructure.","testing_guidance":"Validate schema, semantic constraints, freshness, quality, and backward compatibility.","verification_guidance":"Verify published data against the declared contract.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":["D14-AP-B59-0001"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"data_product_architecture","title":"Data product contract","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Data product interoperability"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B60-0001","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Architecture","subtopic":"Schema evolution","concept":"Backward-compatible schema evolution","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the goal of backward-compatible schema evolution?","answer":"Backward-compatible schema evolution allows existing consumers to continue operating while producers introduce additive or otherwise compatible changes according to the contract.","explanation":"Distributed systems often cannot update every producer and consumer simultaneously.","principle":"Evolve shared data contracts in ways that tolerate independently deployed participants.","correct_example":"A producer adds an optional field while preserving existing fields and semantics required by older consumers.","invalid_example":"A producer renames or removes a required field without a compatibility strategy.","failure_mode":"Older consumers fail after producer deployment.","cause":"Schema change violates the existing contract.","solution":"Use additive evolution, versioning, compatibility checks, or coordinated migration where necessary.","alternative":"Breaking schema version with explicit consumer migration.","tradeoff":"Backward compatibility reduces coordination requirements but can temporarily increase schema complexity.","security_implication":"New fields may introduce sensitive data that existing consumers are not authorized to access.","performance_implication":"Compatibility layers or dual representations can add processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test old and new consumers against old and new schemas.","verification_guidance":"Run compatibility validation against the declared consumer contract before deployment.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D14-AP-B60-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"schema_evolution_architecture","title":"Backward-compatible schema evolution","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Independent contract evolution"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D14-AP-B60-0002","dataset_id":"D14","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Architecture","subtopic":"Schema migration","concept":"Expand-and-contract migration","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How does expand-and-contract support safe schema migration?","answer":"Expand-and-contract introduces a compatible schema state first, migrates producers and consumers, and removes obsolete structures only after dependencies have transitioned.","explanation":"The pattern separates compatibility introduction from removal of legacy structures.","principle":"Separate additive compatibility changes from destructive cleanup.","correct_example":"A new column is added, writers begin populating both representations, readers migrate, and the old column is removed only after verification.","invalid_example":"A required column is renamed and the old column immediately deleted while older consumers remain active.","failure_mode":"Deployment order causes runtime incompatibility.","cause":"Destructive migration occurs before all consumers have migrated.","solution":"Use staged compatibility, migration verification, and explicit retirement criteria.","edge_case":"A rollback may require the old representation to remain available longer than expected.","security_implication":"Dual-write or dual-read periods must preserve consistent authorization and data-protection rules.","performance_implication":"Temporary duplicate storage or transformation increases resource usage.","testing_guidance":"Test mixed-version deployments, rollback, dual-write consistency, and final cleanup.","verification_guidance":"Verify consumer migration before destructive schema removal.","language":"Architecture","framework":"Architecture-neutral","version_range":"Architecture-neutral","dependencies":["D14-AP-B60-0001"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"schema_migration_pattern","title":"Expand-and-contract migration","publisher":"Conceptual architecture knowledge","version":"1.0","relevance":"Safe distributed schema migration"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

