{"record_id":"D14-AP-B61-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Load Balancing","concept":"Load Balancer","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does the Load Balancer architecture pattern solve?","answer":"A load balancer distributes requests or workloads across multiple eligible instances so that capacity, availability, and failure isolation can be improved.","explanation":"The load balancer acts as an architectural distribution point between clients or upstream components and a pool of equivalent or compatible backend instances. Distribution may be based on algorithms such as round robin, weighted selection, least connections, or latency-aware policies. Correctness requires that all selected instances support the required protocol, contract, and state assumptions.","principle":"Distribute independently serviceable workload across interchangeable capacity while preserving routing correctness.","correct_example":"Route requests to multiple stateless application instances while keeping shared durable state outside individual instances.","invalid_example":"Route requests across instances that maintain incompatible local session state without session affinity or externalized state.","problem":"Uneven workload distribution, single-instance overload, or reduced availability.","failure_mode":"A backend instance becomes overloaded or unhealthy while traffic continues to reach it.","cause":"Incorrect health checks, stale membership, unsuitable balancing policy, or hidden stateful dependencies.","solution":"Use meaningful health checks, controlled membership, appropriate balancing policies, connection draining, and explicit state-management strategy.","alternative":"Client-side load balancing or service-discovery-driven request selection.","tradeoff":"Centralized balancing simplifies clients but introduces infrastructure dependency and potential bottleneck; client-side balancing distributes responsibility but increases client complexity.","constraint":"Backend instances must be compatible with the traffic they receive.","edge_case":"Long-lived connections can create uneven distribution even when request counts appear balanced.","security_implication":"The load balancer is a security boundary that must validate routing configuration, protect administrative interfaces, and avoid exposing unintended backends.","performance_implication":"Balancing improves utilization when routing overhead and policy evaluation remain small relative to workload processing.","testing_guidance":"Test healthy routing, unhealthy-instance removal, recovery, connection draining, uneven workloads, and membership changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that traffic reaches only eligible instances and that unhealthy instances stop receiving new work within the defined detection window.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["service discovery or configured backend membership"],"prerequisites":["multiple compatible service instances"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B61-0002","D14-AP-B64-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Load Balancing architecture pattern","publisher":"General software architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B61-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Load Balancing","concept":"Load Balancer Health-Based Routing","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should a load balancer determine whether a backend should receive new traffic?","answer":"Backend eligibility should be based on explicit health and readiness semantics rather than process existence alone.","explanation":"A running process can be alive while unable to serve requests correctly. Health architecture should distinguish liveness from readiness when the platform requires it. Health checks should represent the minimum conditions necessary for safe traffic acceptance without creating excessive dependency or cascading load.","principle":"Traffic eligibility should represent service readiness, not merely process liveness.","rule":"Do not route new traffic to an instance that has failed the conditions required to safely serve the relevant request class.","correct_example":"Remove an instance from normal traffic when its readiness check reports that required dependencies or application state are unavailable.","invalid_example":"Continue routing production traffic solely because the operating-system process remains alive.","problem":"Traffic is sent to instances that cannot successfully process requests.","failure_mode":"Health checks pass despite functional unavailability.","cause":"Health endpoint checks only process existence or an unrelated dependency.","solution":"Define health semantics according to the service's actual traffic-acceptance requirements.","alternative":"Passive health monitoring based on observed request failures.","tradeoff":"More meaningful checks improve routing decisions but can themselves add dependency and monitoring complexity.","constraint":"Health checks must not create disproportionate load or trigger cascading failures.","edge_case":"A dependency outage may affect only one endpoint or request class, making a single binary health state insufficient.","security_implication":"Health endpoints should not expose sensitive diagnostic information.","performance_implication":"Health checks should be lightweight and bounded.","testing_guidance":"Inject dependency failures and verify that routing changes match defined readiness semantics.","verification_guidance":"Compare health state transitions with observed traffic acceptance and rejection behavior.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["load balancing"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B61-0001","D14-AP-B69-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B61-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Health-aware traffic routing","publisher":"General software architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B62-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Bulkhead","concept":"Bulkhead Isolation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does the Bulkhead pattern solve?","answer":"Bulkhead isolation limits the impact of resource exhaustion or failure in one workload, tenant, dependency, or component so that unrelated workloads retain usable capacity.","explanation":"The pattern creates explicit resource or concurrency partitions. A failure or saturation event in one partition is prevented from consuming all shared capacity. Isolation can apply to worker pools, connection pools, queues, tenants, or dependency-specific concurrency.","principle":"Failure containment requires bounded resource sharing.","correct_example":"Give an expensive external dependency its own bounded worker pool so its slowdown cannot consume all application workers.","invalid_example":"Allow every dependency to consume an unlimited shared worker pool.","problem":"One overloaded workload causes unrelated workloads to become unavailable.","failure_mode":"Resource exhaustion propagates across logical boundaries.","cause":"Unbounded concurrency or unrestricted sharing of finite resources.","solution":"Partition resources and establish explicit concurrency or capacity limits.","alternative":"Separate deployment units or process-level isolation.","tradeoff":"Isolation improves resilience but may reduce aggregate utilization when partitions are idle.","constraint":"Partition sizes and admission policies must reflect workload requirements.","edge_case":"A partition can become underutilized while another partition is saturated.","security_implication":"Tenant or trust-boundary isolation can reduce cross-tenant resource interference.","performance_implication":"Partitioning can increase predictable latency under contention but may lower peak utilization.","testing_guidance":"Exhaust one partition and verify that unrelated partitions remain within their availability objectives.","verification_guidance":"Measure resource consumption per partition during induced saturation.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["explicit resource model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B62-0002","D14-AP-B67-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Bulkhead isolation pattern","publisher":"General software architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B62-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Bulkhead","concept":"Bulkhead Capacity Partitioning","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should bulkhead capacity be partitioned?","answer":"Partitions should correspond to meaningful failure or resource-consumption boundaries and have explicit admission limits.","explanation":"A useful partition prevents a known class of workload from exhausting resources required by another class. Partitioning only by arbitrary component names does not necessarily provide meaningful isolation.","principle":"Partition around failure domains and resource contention rather than organizational naming alone.","rule":"Every bulkhead should have a defined protected resource, admission rule, capacity limit, and behavior when capacity is exhausted.","correct_example":"Separate premium and background workloads into bounded queues with explicit maximum concurrency.","invalid_example":"Create separate queues without limits while all queues still share one unlimited worker pool.","problem":"Nominal isolation does not prevent resource exhaustion.","failure_mode":"A supposedly isolated workload still consumes shared capacity.","cause":"Isolation boundary does not align with the actual constrained resource.","solution":"Trace resource ownership and enforce isolation at the true contention point.","alternative":"Deployment-level isolation.","tradeoff":"Finer partitions provide stronger isolation but increase configuration and capacity-management complexity.","constraint":"The total partition capacity cannot exceed available system resources.","edge_case":"Dynamic workload changes can make static partition limits inappropriate.","security_implication":"Partitioning can reduce denial-of-service propagation between tenants or trust zones.","performance_implication":"Excessive partitioning can create stranded capacity.","testing_guidance":"Test both saturation and underutilization scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify that exhaustion of one partition does not consume protected capacity from another.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["resource capacity model"],"prerequisites":["bulkhead isolation"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B62-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B62-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Bulkhead capacity partitioning","publisher":"General software architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B63-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Circuit Breaker","concept":"Circuit Breaker","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does the Circuit Breaker pattern solve?","answer":"A circuit breaker prevents repeated calls to a failing dependency from consuming local resources and amplifying an existing failure.","explanation":"The pattern tracks dependency failures and changes request behavior after a defined threshold or policy. A closed state permits calls, an open state rejects or redirects calls without invoking the dependency, and a half-open state may permit controlled probes to determine recovery. Exact states and policies depend on implementation.","principle":"Stop repeatedly invoking a known-failing dependency when continued calls cannot improve the immediate outcome.","correct_example":"Open the circuit after defined dependency failures and return a bounded fallback or explicit failure until controlled recovery probes succeed.","invalid_example":"Open the circuit permanently after the first transient timeout with no recovery mechanism.","problem":"Cascading failures caused by repeated calls to an unhealthy dependency.","failure_mode":"Dependency failure consumes threads, connections, queues, or CPU in callers.","cause":"No failure-state transition or uncontrolled retries.","solution":"Define failure classification, opening thresholds, recovery probes, and fallback behavior.","alternative":"Timeout plus admission control without a circuit state.","tradeoff":"Circuit breaking protects callers but can temporarily reject requests that might have succeeded after transient recovery.","constraint":"Circuit thresholds must account for workload volume and dependency behavior.","edge_case":"Low-traffic services may not accumulate enough observations to detect recovery confidently.","security_implication":"Circuit behavior must not bypass authorization or return sensitive fallback data.","performance_implication":"An open circuit can reduce wasted dependency calls and resource consumption.","testing_guidance":"Test transient failures, sustained failures, recovery, probe concurrency, and fallback behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that open-state requests do not invoke the protected dependency except for permitted recovery probes.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["timeout or failure detection"],"prerequisites":["bounded dependency calls"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B63-0002","D14-AP-B66-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Circuit Breaker pattern","publisher":"General software architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B63-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Circuit Breaker","concept":"Circuit Breaker State Semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must be defined for a circuit breaker's state transitions?","answer":"The architecture should define failure signals, transition thresholds, open duration or recovery policy, probe behavior, and the handling of requests in each state.","explanation":"A circuit breaker is only predictable when its state machine has explicit semantics. Failure classification should distinguish failures that indicate dependency health from failures caused by invalid client input or local defects.","principle":"A resilience state machine requires explicit transition semantics and failure classification.","correct_example":"Count dependency timeouts and server failures toward the circuit threshold while excluding deterministic client validation failures.","invalid_example":"Count every application exception as evidence that the external dependency is unhealthy.","problem":"Incorrect circuit state transitions cause either insufficient protection or excessive rejection.","failure_mode":"The circuit opens for local defects or remains closed during dependency failure.","cause":"Poor failure classification or undefined thresholds.","solution":"Define observable signals and state transitions based on dependency-specific semantics.","alternative":"Adaptive failure detection using rolling error rates.","tradeoff":"Simple thresholds are easier to reason about; adaptive policies can better match changing workloads but are more complex.","constraint":"The circuit state must not become the sole source of truth for dependency health.","edge_case":"A dependency may partially fail by endpoint, tenant, region, or request type.","security_implication":"Fallback behavior must preserve security and authorization requirements.","performance_implication":"State evaluation adds small local overhead while potentially preventing expensive remote calls.","testing_guidance":"Exercise every state transition and boundary condition.","verification_guidance":"Compare observed transitions with the documented state machine.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["circuit breaker"],"prerequisites":["failure classification"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B63-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B63-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Circuit breaker state semantics","publisher":"General software architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B64-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Retry and Backoff","concept":"Retry with Exponential Backoff","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should retry with backoff be used?","answer":"Retries are appropriate when an operation may succeed after a transient failure and repeating it is safe or explicitly made idempotent.","explanation":"A retry policy should specify retryable failures, maximum attempts or elapsed time, delay strategy, and termination behavior. Exponential backoff increases delays between attempts and can be combined with jitter to reduce synchronized retry bursts.","principle":"Retry only when failure is plausibly transient and repeated execution is safe.","correct_example":"Retry a transient network failure using bounded exponential backoff and an idempotency mechanism for operations that may have been partially processed.","invalid_example":"Retry every application error indefinitely with no delay or attempt limit.","problem":"Transient failures cause avoidable request loss, while uncontrolled retries can amplify failures.","failure_mode":"Retry storms overwhelm an already degraded dependency.","cause":"Unbounded retries, synchronized delays, or retrying non-transient failures.","solution":"Bound retries, classify failures, use backoff and jitter, and enforce operation safety.","alternative":"Fail fast and delegate recovery to an asynchronous queue.","tradeoff":"Retries can improve transient-failure success rates but consume additional latency and resources.","constraint":"Total retry work must remain within system and dependency capacity.","edge_case":"A timeout may occur after the remote operation succeeded, creating an ambiguous outcome.","security_implication":"Retries must not bypass authorization or multiply security-sensitive side effects.","performance_implication":"Retries increase tail latency and resource consumption during failure periods.","testing_guidance":"Test transient errors, permanent errors, timeout ambiguity, retry exhaustion, and concurrent retry behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that retry counts, delays, and termination conditions match policy.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["failure classification"],"prerequisites":["bounded request lifecycle"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B64-0002","D14-AP-B65-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Retry and backoff pattern","publisher":"General software architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B64-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Retry and Backoff","concept":"Retry Storm Prevention","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What makes retry behavior unsafe at architecture scale?","answer":"Unbounded, synchronized, or nested retries can multiply load precisely when a dependency has reduced capacity.","explanation":"If multiple layers independently retry the same operation, a single failure can generate substantially more work than the original request. Jitter and centralized retry ownership can reduce synchronization and multiplicative retry behavior.","principle":"Retry amplification must be bounded across the complete call graph, not only within one component.","correct_example":"Define retry ownership at one architectural layer and cap the total number or duration of attempts.","invalid_example":"A client, API gateway, service, and database driver each independently retry the same failed operation without coordination.","problem":"Failure increases workload instead of reducing it.","failure_mode":"A transient outage becomes a sustained overload.","cause":"Nested retries and lack of global retry budgets.","solution":"Define retry boundaries, budgets, jitter, and failure propagation semantics.","alternative":"Asynchronous durable queues for recoverable background work.","tradeoff":"Centralized retry policy improves predictability but may require coordination across components.","constraint":"Retry policy must account for downstream capacity and operation semantics.","edge_case":"Different protocols may already have implicit retry behavior.","security_implication":"Repeated authentication or authorization operations can create unintended load or account-locking effects.","performance_implication":"Retry storms can dramatically increase tail latency and resource usage.","testing_guidance":"Inject dependency failures across multiple call layers and measure aggregate attempts.","verification_guidance":"Verify that total downstream attempts remain within the architecture's retry budget.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["retry policy"],"prerequisites":["distributed call graph"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B64-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B64-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Retry amplification and failure containment","publisher":"General software architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B65-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Timeouts","concept":"Timeout Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What architectural problem does an explicit timeout solve?","answer":"An explicit timeout bounds how long a component waits for an operation so unavailable or excessively slow dependencies cannot consume resources indefinitely.","explanation":"Timeouts establish temporal boundaries around remote calls, queue operations, lock acquisition, or other potentially blocking work. A timeout is not proof that the remote operation failed; the operation may continue or may have completed remotely.","principle":"Every potentially blocking dependency interaction should have an intentional time budget.","correct_example":"Set a bounded deadline for a remote request and propagate the remaining request budget to downstream calls where supported.","invalid_example":"Use an unlimited network wait for a dependency whose response is required before completing the request.","problem":"Hung operations consume scarce resources.","failure_mode":"Connection, thread, task, or worker exhaustion caused by stalled operations.","cause":"Missing or inconsistent timeout boundaries.","solution":"Define operation-specific deadlines and enforce them at appropriate layers.","alternative":"Asynchronous decoupling through durable messaging.","tradeoff":"Shorter timeouts protect resources but may reject legitimate slow operations.","constraint":"Timeouts must align with end-to-end latency objectives and downstream behavior.","edge_case":"Timeout expiry can race with successful remote completion.","security_implication":"Timeouts can limit certain resource-exhaustion attacks but do not replace request-size or concurrency controls.","performance_implication":"Bounded waits improve resource predictability while potentially increasing visible failure rates.","testing_guidance":"Test normal latency, delayed responses, connection hangs, cancellation, and timeout races.","verification_guidance":"Verify that blocked resources are released after timeout and that downstream work is handled according to policy.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["defined latency budget"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B65-0002","D14-AP-B66-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Timeout architecture pattern","publisher":"General software architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B65-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Timeouts","concept":"Deadline Propagation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should distributed systems propagate request deadlines?","answer":"Deadline propagation prevents downstream components from continuing expensive work after the upstream request can no longer use the result.","explanation":"A caller can provide a remaining time budget to downstream operations. Each component consumes part of the budget and may reject work when insufficient time remains. Exact propagation mechanisms depend on the protocol and framework.","principle":"Distributed work should respect the end-to-end time budget of the operation that requires its result.","correct_example":"Propagate a request deadline through service calls and stop downstream work when the remaining budget is exhausted.","invalid_example":"Each service starts a fresh independent timeout that may collectively exceed the original request's useful lifetime.","problem":"Downstream work continues after upstream cancellation or timeout.","failure_mode":"Resources are consumed for results that no caller can use.","cause":"Independent timeout configuration without end-to-end budget awareness.","solution":"Model and propagate deadlines where protocol support exists.","alternative":"Centralized request orchestration with explicit time budgets.","tradeoff":"Deadline propagation improves efficiency but requires consistent semantics across service boundaries.","constraint":"Clock differences and protocol limitations must be considered.","edge_case":"A component may receive a deadline that is already expired or too short for safe processing.","security_implication":"Deadline metadata must not be trusted as an authorization signal.","performance_implication":"Early cancellation reduces wasted downstream work.","testing_guidance":"Test near-expiry, expired, missing, and malformed deadline values.","verification_guidance":"Verify that downstream operations respect propagated deadlines within documented tolerance.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["timeout boundary"],"prerequisites":["request lifecycle model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B65-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B65-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Deadline propagation","publisher":"General distributed-systems architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B66-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Rate Limiting","concept":"Rate Limiter","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does rate limiting solve?","answer":"Rate limiting bounds the rate of admitted operations so that clients, tenants, services, or resources cannot consume capacity without an explicit limit.","explanation":"Rate limits can be expressed per identity, tenant, endpoint, resource, or system. Common models include fixed windows, sliding windows, token buckets, and leaky buckets. The correct model depends on whether bursts, sustained throughput, fairness, or strict temporal limits matter.","principle":"Admission rate should be bounded according to available capacity and policy.","correct_example":"Apply a tenant-specific request budget with defined burst capacity and a documented response when the budget is exceeded.","invalid_example":"Apply an arbitrary global request limit without accounting for legitimate tenant capacity differences.","problem":"Traffic bursts or abusive clients exhaust shared capacity.","failure_mode":"Resource saturation causes elevated latency or service unavailability.","cause":"Unbounded admission.","solution":"Define limits, scope, burst behavior, and rejection semantics.","alternative":"Concurrency limiting or queue-based load leveling.","tradeoff":"Strict limits protect capacity but may reject legitimate bursts.","constraint":"Rate-limit state must be reliable enough for the required enforcement semantics.","edge_case":"Distributed instances can disagree about counters without coordinated state or an intentionally approximate algorithm.","security_implication":"Rate limiting can reduce abuse and denial-of-service impact but must not be treated as a complete security control.","performance_implication":"Rate limiting adds admission-check overhead while protecting downstream capacity.","testing_guidance":"Test sustained traffic, bursts, multiple identities, distributed enforcement, and boundary conditions.","verification_guidance":"Verify admitted traffic against the documented limit and burst semantics.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["capacity model"],"prerequisites":["defined traffic unit"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B66-0002","D14-AP-B67-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Rate limiting pattern","publisher":"General software architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B66-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Rate Limiting","concept":"Rate Limit Scope","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should rate limits be scoped?","answer":"Rate limits should be attached to the resource or policy boundary they are intended to protect, such as tenant, identity, endpoint, dependency, or system capacity.","explanation":"A global limit can protect the entire service but may fail to provide fairness. Per-tenant or per-identity limits can improve isolation but require reliable identity and distributed state semantics.","principle":"The enforcement scope should correspond to the resource or policy being protected.","correct_example":"Apply separate tenant limits when tenant isolation is a requirement, with an additional global safety limit for total capacity.","invalid_example":"Use only a global limit when one high-volume tenant can legitimately consume the entire allowance.","problem":"Rate limiting fails to provide intended fairness or protection.","failure_mode":"One traffic source consumes capacity intended for other sources.","cause":"Limit scope does not match the protected resource.","solution":"Layer policy-specific limits with global capacity protection where appropriate.","alternative":"Bulkhead isolation.","tradeoff":"Multiple limits improve control but increase configuration and state-management complexity.","constraint":"Identity used for enforcement must be trustworthy and stable for the intended scope.","edge_case":"Clients behind shared network addresses may be incorrectly grouped if network identity is used alone.","security_implication":"Rate-limit keys should avoid allowing trivial identity spoofing.","performance_implication":"More granular limits require more state lookups and policy evaluation.","testing_guidance":"Test fairness across tenants and identities and verify limit isolation.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that one scope's traffic cannot bypass or unintentionally consume another scope's allowance.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["rate limiter"],"prerequisites":["rate-limit policy"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B66-0001","D14-AP-B62-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B66-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Rate-limit scope and fairness","publisher":"General software architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B67-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"Backpressure","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does backpressure solve?","answer":"Backpressure prevents producers from continuously overwhelming consumers when production capacity exceeds downstream processing capacity.","explanation":"A system with bounded consumers must communicate or enforce reduced admission, buffering, batching, or production when downstream capacity is constrained. Backpressure can be explicit through protocol signals or implicit through bounded queues and admission control.","principle":"Production rate must respond to downstream capacity when buffering alone cannot absorb the difference.","correct_example":"Pause or slow producers when a bounded queue reaches its configured capacity.","invalid_example":"Allow an unbounded producer queue to grow indefinitely during sustained consumer degradation.","problem":"Producer throughput exceeds consumer capacity.","failure_mode":"Queue growth, memory exhaustion, latency explosion, or downstream overload.","cause":"No capacity feedback or unbounded buffering.","solution":"Use bounded queues, admission control, flow-control signals, or load shedding.","alternative":"Queue-based load leveling for workloads that can tolerate asynchronous delay.","tradeoff":"Backpressure preserves stability but may reduce immediate throughput or reject work.","constraint":"The system must define what happens when downstream capacity remains unavailable.","edge_case":"A producer may not support pausing, requiring buffering or explicit rejection.","security_implication":"Backpressure can reduce resource-exhaustion attack impact when limits are correctly enforced.","performance_implication":"It stabilizes resource use under overload but may lower offered throughput.","testing_guidance":"Saturate consumers and observe producer behavior, queue growth, memory use, and recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify that queue/resource usage remains within configured bounds during sustained overload.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["bounded resource model"],"prerequisites":["producer-consumer relationship"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B67-0002","D14-AP-B68-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Backpressure pattern","publisher":"General distributed-systems architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B67-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"Load Shedding","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is load shedding appropriate?","answer":"Load shedding is appropriate when preserving system stability requires intentionally rejecting or deprioritizing work that cannot be safely processed within available capacity.","explanation":"Load shedding can reject excess requests, drop optional work, reduce quality, or prioritize critical traffic. It should be policy-driven rather than an accidental consequence of resource exhaustion.","principle":"Under overload, controlled rejection is preferable to uncontrolled failure propagation.","correct_example":"Reject low-priority requests with an explicit overload response while preserving capacity for critical operations.","invalid_example":"Allow all traffic to compete until the entire service becomes unavailable.","problem":"Demand exceeds sustainable capacity.","failure_mode":"Global degradation caused by attempting to process all offered work.","cause":"No overload policy.","solution":"Define priority classes, admission limits, and explicit overload behavior.","alternative":"Horizontal scaling where capacity can be added within the required time.","tradeoff":"Load shedding protects availability for selected workloads but intentionally denies some work.","constraint":"Priority classification must be explicit and resistant to trivial manipulation.","edge_case":"A supposedly low-priority request may be a prerequisite for a high-priority workflow.","security_implication":"Attackers must not be able to cheaply obtain protected priority classes.","performance_implication":"Shedding reduces resource contention and can preserve latency for admitted work.","testing_guidance":"Generate overload and verify priority-specific admission behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that overload behavior follows documented priority and capacity rules.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["backpressure or admission control"],"prerequisites":["workload prioritization"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B67-0001","D14-AP-B70-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B67-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Load shedding under overload","publisher":"General resilience architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B68-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Queue-Based Load Leveling","concept":"Queue-Based Load Leveling","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does queue-based load leveling solve?","answer":"Queue-based load leveling absorbs temporary differences between workload arrival rate and processing rate so consumers can process work at a controlled rate.","explanation":"Producers submit work to a queue instead of requiring immediate synchronous processing. Consumers remove work according to available capacity. The pattern is appropriate when asynchronous processing is acceptable and the queue's durability and ordering semantics match the workload.","principle":"Decouple arrival bursts from processing capacity when the business operation tolerates asynchronous execution.","correct_example":"Place background report-generation jobs on a durable queue and process them using bounded workers.","invalid_example":"Use a queue for a user operation that requires an immediate authoritative response without defining asynchronous semantics.","problem":"Traffic bursts overwhelm synchronous consumers.","failure_mode":"Queue backlog grows beyond acceptable latency or storage capacity.","cause":"Arrival rate persistently exceeds service rate.","solution":"Scale consumers, apply backpressure, prioritize work, or reject work when backlog limits are reached.","alternative":"Direct synchronous processing with load balancing.","tradeoff":"Queues improve burst tolerance and decoupling but introduce delay, operational complexity, and eventual processing semantics.","constraint":"Maximum acceptable queue delay and backlog must be defined.","edge_case":"A queued job may become obsolete before processing.","security_implication":"Queue contents require access control, integrity protection, and appropriate data handling.","performance_implication":"Queues smooth bursts but add enqueue/dequeue and persistence overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test bursts, consumer failures, duplicate delivery, backlog growth, expiration, and recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify queue durability, processing semantics, backlog limits, and eventual outcome handling.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["queue or messaging infrastructure"],"prerequisites":["asynchronous workflow acceptance"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B67-0001","D14-AP-B68-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Queue-based load leveling","publisher":"General distributed-systems architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B68-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Queue-Based Load Leveling","concept":"Queue Backlog Budget","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an architecture define for a load-leveling queue?","answer":"The architecture should define maximum acceptable backlog, age or latency budget, capacity behavior, retry policy, poison-message handling, and recovery semantics.","explanation":"A queue is not unlimited capacity. A backlog that grows indefinitely converts overload into delayed failure. Queue policy must therefore define how the system behaves when backlog age or size exceeds acceptable limits.","principle":"Queue capacity is a finite resource and must have explicit operational limits.","correct_example":"Reject or deprioritize new low-value work when queue age exceeds the defined service objective.","invalid_example":"Assume that every queued item will eventually be processed regardless of backlog growth.","problem":"Queue accumulation hides sustained overload.","failure_mode":"Users experience extreme delays or storage exhaustion.","cause":"No backlog budget or overload policy.","solution":"Define backlog limits, expiration, priority, scaling, and load-shedding behavior.","alternative":"Direct admission control.","tradeoff":"Strict backlog limits protect stability but may reject work sooner.","constraint":"Backlog policy must match business tolerance for delay and loss.","edge_case":"A long-running task may legitimately exceed average processing time and require separate queue classes.","security_implication":"Queue exhaustion can be an attack vector if admission is unrestricted.","performance_implication":"Bounded backlog improves predictability but may reduce accepted throughput during overload.","testing_guidance":"Inject sustained overload and verify backlog and expiration policies.","verification_guidance":"Verify backlog age and size remain within defined limits under expected and failure conditions.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["queue-based load leveling"],"prerequisites":["defined latency objective"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B68-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B68-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Queue backlog budgets","publisher":"General distributed-systems architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B69-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Leader Election","concept":"Leader Election","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does leader election solve in a distributed system?","answer":"Leader election selects one currently eligible member to coordinate operations that require a single active coordinator, while providing a mechanism to replace it after failure.","explanation":"Leader election requires coordination and a definition of membership, leadership validity, and failure detection. Correctness depends on preventing stale leaders from continuing to perform operations after losing authority.","principle":"Single-coordinator semantics require explicit authority validity and safe leadership transitions.","correct_example":"Use a coordination mechanism that grants time-bounded leadership and prevents an expired leader from safely performing protected operations.","invalid_example":"Assume that a process remains leader forever because it started first.","problem":"Multiple instances may act as coordinator or a failed leader may continue acting.","failure_mode":"Split-brain coordination causes conflicting writes or actions.","cause":"Unclear leadership authority, stale membership, or inadequate fencing.","solution":"Use leases, epochs, fencing tokens, or another mechanism appropriate to the coordination system.","alternative":"Active-active coordination using commutative operations or consensus-based state management.","tradeoff":"Leader-based coordination simplifies some workflows but creates leadership-transition complexity.","constraint":"The system must define what happens during network partitions and ambiguous membership.","edge_case":"A leader can be alive but unable to communicate with the coordination system.","security_implication":"Leadership credentials or fencing authority must not be forgeable or reusable after expiry.","performance_implication":"Leader coordination may add synchronization overhead and can concentrate workload.","testing_guidance":"Test leader failure, delayed communication, partition scenarios, simultaneous candidates, and recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify that only currently authorized leadership can perform protected coordinator operations.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["distributed coordination mechanism"],"prerequisites":["defined coordinator responsibility"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B69-0002","D14-AP-B70-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Leader election pattern","publisher":"General distributed-systems architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B69-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Leader Election","concept":"Fencing Against Stale Leaders","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can an architecture prevent a stale leader from continuing protected operations?","answer":"Use a fencing mechanism that makes authority generation explicit and rejects operations carrying an obsolete leadership epoch or token.","explanation":"Failure detection can be uncertain in distributed systems. A former leader may remain running after losing coordination authority. Fencing moves correctness enforcement to the protected resource or operation boundary.","principle":"Loss of coordination authority must become enforceable at the operation boundary, not merely observable by the coordinator.","correct_example":"Attach a monotonically increasing fencing epoch to leader-issued writes and reject writes from older epochs.","invalid_example":"Rely only on the old leader eventually noticing that another node has become leader.","problem":"Stale coordinators continue issuing valid-looking operations.","failure_mode":"Conflicting operations are accepted after leadership has changed.","cause":"No enforceable authority generation.","solution":"Use fencing tokens, epochs, leases with enforced expiry, or equivalent protection supported by the resource.","alternative":"Centralized serialization through a strongly coordinated service.","tradeoff":"Fencing improves correctness but requires the protected resource to understand or enforce authority state.","constraint":"The fencing mechanism must remain monotonic and resistant to replay.","edge_case":"Clock-based leases can fail under clock uncertainty if the resource does not independently enforce authority.","security_implication":"Fencing tokens should not be forgeable and should have clear scope.","performance_implication":"Additional authorization checks may add small per-operation overhead.","testing_guidance":"Simulate delayed old leaders and verify that their operations are rejected.","verification_guidance":"Attempt protected operations with obsolete epochs and confirm deterministic rejection.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["leader election"],"prerequisites":["authority epoch model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B69-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B69-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Fencing and stale leader prevention","publisher":"General distributed-systems architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B70-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Integrated Resilience","concept":"Resilience Control Loop","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should scalability and resilience patterns work together rather than independently?","answer":"Architecture should combine capacity distribution, isolation, failure detection, bounded retries, deadlines, admission control, backpressure, load leveling, and coordination according to the actual failure modes of the system.","explanation":"Resilience controls interact. For example, retries without rate limits can amplify overload, queues without backlog budgets can hide failure, and circuit breakers without timeouts may react too slowly. A coherent control loop observes demand and health, limits admission, protects resources, and verifies recovery.","principle":"Resilience is a system-level control problem rather than a collection of isolated middleware features.","correct_example":"Use load balancing for distribution, bulkheads for isolation, deadlines for bounded waits, controlled retries, rate limits for admission, and queues for workloads that tolerate asynchronous processing.","invalid_example":"Enable retries, queues, and autoscaling independently without defining their interaction during dependency failure.","problem":"Individually useful patterns interact to create unintended feedback loops.","failure_mode":"Retry amplification, queue explosion, resource starvation, or oscillating capacity.","cause":"Patterns selected without end-to-end failure modeling.","solution":"Model demand, capacity, dependencies, state transitions, and overload behavior as one resilience architecture.","alternative":"Simpler architecture with fewer explicit resilience controls when system scale and failure modes are limited.","tradeoff":"Integrated resilience provides stronger control but increases architecture and operational complexity.","constraint":"Every resilience mechanism must have defined scope, failure semantics, and ownership.","edge_case":"A mitigation can hide an underlying failure and delay detection.","security_implication":"Resilience mechanisms must preserve authorization, isolation, and audit semantics during degraded operation.","performance_implication":"Protection mechanisms consume resources and may add latency; their cost must be included in capacity planning.","testing_guidance":"Perform combined failure-injection scenarios rather than testing each mechanism only in isolation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that overload remains bounded, protected workloads retain capacity, and recovery transitions are deterministic.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["load balancing","bulkhead","circuit breaker","retry","timeout","rate limiting","backpressure"],"prerequisites":["failure-mode analysis"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B61-0001","D14-AP-B62-0001","D14-AP-B63-0001","D14-AP-B64-0001","D14-AP-B65-0001","D14-AP-B66-0001","D14-AP-B67-0001","D14-AP-B68-0001","D14-AP-B69-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Integrated scalability and resilience architecture","publisher":"Derived from architecture pattern relationships","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B70-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Scalability and Resilience Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Integrated Resilience","concept":"Resilience Architecture Decision Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified before accepting a scalability or resilience architecture?","answer":"Verify that capacity limits, failure boundaries, timeout semantics, retry budgets, admission controls, overload behavior, recovery mechanisms, and critical-state correctness are explicitly defined and testable.","explanation":"A resilience pattern is not architecturally complete merely because a component or library is present. The architecture must define what failure it addresses, what resource it protects, what happens during saturation, and how recovery is verified.","principle":"Every resilience mechanism must map to a documented failure mode and an observable verification condition.","correct_example":"For each dependency, document timeout, retry policy, circuit behavior, capacity boundary, fallback, and recovery verification.","invalid_example":"Declare the system resilient because it contains a circuit-breaker library without specifying thresholds, fallback, or verification.","problem":"Resilience claims lack explicit evidence criteria.","failure_mode":"Controls exist but do not provide the intended protection.","cause":"Pattern adoption without architectural verification.","solution":"Create a resilience decision record linking each mechanism to failure modes, limits, tests, and observed evidence.","alternative":"Formal resilience model or reliability review.","tradeoff":"Explicit verification requires more design and test effort but improves traceability.","constraint":"Verification must distinguish conceptual design from executed evidence.","edge_case":"Some resilience properties depend on production-scale conditions that cannot be fully established in a local test.","security_implication":"Verification must include abuse, isolation, and degraded-security scenarios where applicable.","performance_implication":"Capacity and latency claims require measured evidence rather than architectural assumption.","testing_guidance":"Combine unit, integration, failure-injection, load, recovery, and regression tests according to risk.","verification_guidance":"Mark claims as NOT_EXECUTED or NOT_VERIFIED when supporting evidence has not actually been produced.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["resilience control loop"],"prerequisites":["defined failure modes","test strategy"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B70-0001","D14-AP-B32-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B70-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Resilience architecture verification gate","publisher":"Derived from D14 architecture-pattern verification principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

