{"record_id":"D14-AP-B71-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Twelve-Factor Architecture","concept":"Configuration Externalization","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should deploy-specific configuration be externalized from application code?","answer":"Deploy-specific configuration should be supplied through an explicit configuration mechanism so the same application artifact can operate across environments without source changes.","explanation":"Configuration includes values such as service endpoints, credentials references, feature settings, and environment-specific limits. Externalization separates deploy-time concerns from application logic and reduces environment-specific code variants.","principle":"Build artifacts should not require source modification merely because deployment configuration changes.","correct_example":"Use environment or platform configuration to provide a service endpoint while keeping the application artifact unchanged.","invalid_example":"Hard-code production credentials and production-only service addresses directly into application source.","problem":"Environment-specific behavior becomes coupled to source code.","failure_mode":"Incorrect configuration is deployed or source changes are required for routine environment changes.","cause":"Configuration is embedded in application logic or artifacts.","solution":"Define typed, validated configuration inputs with explicit precedence and lifecycle semantics.","alternative":"Environment-specific build artifacts.","tradeoff":"Externalized configuration improves deployment portability but introduces configuration-management requirements.","constraint":"Sensitive configuration values must use appropriate secret-management controls rather than unrestricted environment variables.","edge_case":"A missing or malformed configuration value may prevent safe startup.","security_implication":"Configuration can contain secrets, authorization settings, and security policies; access must be restricted.","performance_implication":"Configuration should generally be loaded efficiently and should not require repeated remote lookups unless explicitly designed.","testing_guidance":"Test missing, malformed, conflicting, and environment-specific configuration values.","verification_guidance":"Verify that identical application artifacts can be deployed with different validated configurations.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["configuration schema"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B71-0002","D14-AP-B74-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Configuration externalization in cloud-native applications","publisher":"General cloud-native architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B71-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Twelve-Factor Architecture","concept":"Stateless Process Design","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is stateless process design useful for horizontally scaled services?","answer":"Keeping durable business state outside replaceable service instances allows instances to be added, removed, restarted, or rescheduled without requiring local state continuity.","explanation":"A process may maintain temporary in-memory state for performance or request handling, but authoritative durable state should have an explicit persistence boundary. This improves replaceability and simplifies scaling and recovery.","principle":"Replaceable compute instances should not be the sole authoritative store for durable business state.","correct_example":"Store durable session or business state in an appropriate shared persistence mechanism while keeping application instances replaceable.","invalid_example":"Store the only copy of critical customer state in one process's local memory.","problem":"Instance replacement causes state loss or inconsistent behavior.","failure_mode":"Scaling, restart, or rescheduling destroys required state.","cause":"Durable state is coupled to ephemeral compute.","solution":"Separate durable state from replaceable compute and define ownership and consistency semantics.","alternative":"Sticky sessions with replicated local state.","tradeoff":"Externalized state improves replaceability but introduces network and persistence dependencies.","constraint":"External state must meet required consistency, availability, and latency characteristics.","edge_case":"Temporary caches and connection-local state may legitimately remain local when loss is safe.","security_implication":"Externalized state requires explicit access control and tenant isolation.","performance_implication":"Remote state access adds latency and network overhead; caching may be appropriate where correctness permits.","testing_guidance":"Restart and replace instances while preserving required state.","verification_guidance":"Verify that instance replacement does not lose authoritative state.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["appropriate persistence mechanism"],"prerequisites":["state ownership model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B51-0001","D14-AP-B72-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Stateless process design","publisher":"General cloud-native architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B72-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Containerization","concept":"Containerized Application Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What architectural purpose does containerization provide?","answer":"Containerization packages an application and its runtime dependencies into a deployable unit with a defined execution boundary, while relying on the underlying platform for kernel and infrastructure services.","explanation":"Containers improve packaging consistency and deployment repeatability but do not automatically provide application correctness, security isolation, resilience, or portability across every environment.","principle":"Package application runtime dependencies consistently while keeping infrastructure responsibilities explicit.","correct_example":"Build an immutable application image containing the application and required runtime dependencies, then supply deployment configuration separately.","invalid_example":"Assume that placing an application in a container automatically makes its internal dependencies, secrets, and network exposure secure.","problem":"Deployment environments differ in dependencies and runtime assumptions.","failure_mode":"Works in one environment but fails after deployment due to missing or incompatible runtime assumptions.","cause":"Incomplete packaging or undocumented platform dependencies.","solution":"Define image contents, runtime contract, configuration, resource limits, and platform assumptions.","alternative":"Virtual-machine-based deployment or direct process deployment.","tradeoff":"Containers can improve packaging consistency and density but add image, orchestration, and lifecycle complexity.","constraint":"Container images and runtimes require security maintenance and resource controls.","edge_case":"Native dependencies can behave differently across CPU architecture or base-image versions.","security_implication":"Image provenance, dependency vulnerabilities, privileges, filesystem access, and network permissions require explicit controls.","performance_implication":"Container overhead is generally dependent on workload and platform rather than being universally zero.","testing_guidance":"Test image startup, dependency availability, architecture compatibility, resource limits, and graceful termination.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the built image contains the declared runtime requirements and runs according to its deployment contract.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["container runtime"],"prerequisites":["reproducible build process"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B72-0002","D14-AP-B76-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Containerized application architecture","publisher":"General cloud-native architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B72-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Containerization","concept":"Immutable Application Image","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should production container images be treated as immutable deployment artifacts?","answer":"An immutable image provides a stable versioned artifact whose contents do not change after publication, improving reproducibility and rollback reasoning.","explanation":"Runtime modification of deployed images makes it difficult to determine which software was actually executed. Configuration and mutable application state should have separate lifecycle semantics.","principle":"Deployment artifacts should be identifiable and reproducible across environments.","correct_example":"Publish a versioned image digest and deploy that exact artifact across test and production environments.","invalid_example":"Modify application binaries manually inside a running production container and consider the container equivalent to the original image.","problem":"The deployed software diverges from the known artifact.","failure_mode":"Rollback, debugging, and provenance become ambiguous.","cause":"Mutable runtime artifacts.","solution":"Redeploy a newly built immutable artifact for software changes.","alternative":"Managed package deployment with equivalent immutable release semantics.","tradeoff":"Immutable artifacts improve traceability but require disciplined build and release workflows.","constraint":"External mutable state must be managed independently.","edge_case":"Temporary diagnostic files may exist in runtime storage but should not redefine the application artifact.","security_implication":"Artifact integrity and provenance can be verified through cryptographic identifiers.","performance_implication":"Immutable images can improve deployment repeatability but image distribution introduces storage and transfer costs.","testing_guidance":"Compare deployed artifact identity against the approved release artifact.","verification_guidance":"Verify image digest or equivalent immutable identity before and during deployment.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["container image registry or equivalent artifact store"],"prerequisites":["reproducible build"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B72-0001","D14-AP-B76-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B72-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Immutable deployment artifacts","publisher":"General cloud-native architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B73-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Serverless","concept":"Serverless Compute","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What architectural problem does serverless compute address?","answer":"Serverless compute abstracts much of the server provisioning and lifecycle management so applications can execute in response to events or requests using provider-managed compute capacity.","explanation":"Serverless shifts infrastructure management responsibilities to a platform while retaining application-level responsibilities such as correctness, authorization, dependency management, and observability. It is particularly compatible with event-driven or request-driven workloads, subject to platform constraints.","principle":"Delegate infrastructure lifecycle management when the workload and platform contract justify the abstraction.","correct_example":"Use a managed function execution model for independently triggered background transformations with bounded execution requirements.","invalid_example":"Assume serverless execution has unlimited runtime, storage, concurrency, or network behavior.","problem":"Infrastructure management overhead is disproportionate to workload requirements.","failure_mode":"Architecture violates platform execution limits or incurs unexpected operational behavior.","cause":"Platform constraints were ignored during design.","solution":"Model runtime limits, concurrency, cold-start behavior, state, networking, observability, and cost semantics before adoption.","alternative":"Containerized service or managed application platform.","tradeoff":"Serverless reduces infrastructure management but increases platform coupling and introduces execution constraints.","constraint":"The workload must fit the platform's runtime, resource, networking, concurrency, and state model.","edge_case":"Cold starts or scaling behavior can affect latency-sensitive workloads.","security_implication":"Function identity, permissions, event sources, and dependency access require least-privilege controls.","performance_implication":"Scaling behavior and startup latency depend on workload and platform characteristics; these should be measured rather than assumed.","testing_guidance":"Test cold and warm execution, concurrency, timeouts, retries, partial failures, and event duplication.","verification_guidance":"Verify that workload behavior stays within documented platform constraints under expected concurrency and failure conditions.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["serverless execution platform"],"prerequisites":["event or request execution model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B73-0002","D14-AP-B23-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D14-AP-B72-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Serverless compute architecture","publisher":"General cloud-native architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B73-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Serverless","concept":"Serverless State Separation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should durable state be handled in serverless architectures?","answer":"Durable business state should be stored in explicitly managed persistence systems rather than relying on an individual function invocation's local execution environment.","explanation":"Serverless execution instances can be created, reused, suspended, or terminated according to platform behavior. Local memory and temporary storage should therefore be treated according to the platform's documented lifecycle rather than as guaranteed durable state.","principle":"Invocation-local state must not be confused with durable application state.","correct_example":"Persist authoritative workflow state in a durable store and treat function-local memory as temporary optimization state.","invalid_example":"Store the only copy of a business transaction in invocation-local memory and assume it will survive a later invocation.","problem":"State disappears between invocations or execution instances.","failure_mode":"Incomplete workflows or inconsistent business state.","cause":"Incorrect lifecycle assumptions.","solution":"Define durable state ownership independently of function lifecycle.","alternative":"Stateful containerized service.","tradeoff":"Externalized state improves correctness but adds persistence latency and operational dependency.","constraint":"The chosen persistence mechanism must satisfy the workflow's consistency and durability requirements.","edge_case":"Warm execution may retain memory temporarily, but this must not be treated as a durable contract.","security_implication":"State stores require explicit authorization and tenant isolation.","performance_implication":"External state access can dominate execution latency for small functions.","testing_guidance":"Force invocation replacement and verify workflow continuity.","verification_guidance":"Verify that workflow state survives independent invocation lifecycles.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["durable persistence"],"prerequisites":["serverless execution model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B73-0001","D14-AP-B71-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B73-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Serverless state separation","publisher":"General cloud-native architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B74-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Platform Architecture","concept":"Platform Engineering Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What architectural purpose does an internal platform provide?","answer":"An internal platform provides standardized reusable capabilities and interfaces so application teams can consume infrastructure and operational functions without independently implementing every underlying mechanism.","explanation":"A platform may provide deployment, identity integration, observability, configuration, networking, storage, policy, and software supply-chain capabilities. The platform should expose clear contracts rather than hide operational constraints.","principle":"Centralize reusable platform capabilities while keeping application ownership and service contracts explicit.","correct_example":"Provide a standardized deployment interface that automatically applies approved security, observability, and resource policies.","invalid_example":"Create a platform that requires every application to depend directly on undocumented internal implementation details.","problem":"Repeated infrastructure implementation creates inconsistent operational and security behavior.","failure_mode":"Teams implement divergent solutions with incompatible operational characteristics.","cause":"Missing reusable platform capabilities or unclear ownership.","solution":"Define platform capabilities as versioned, documented products with explicit application interfaces.","alternative":"Direct infrastructure consumption by each application team.","tradeoff":"Platforms reduce repeated work and variance but can become bottlenecks if overly centralized or inflexible.","constraint":"Platform interfaces must support application requirements without exposing unnecessary implementation coupling.","edge_case":"Exceptional workloads may require controlled escape paths from standard platform capabilities.","security_implication":"Platform controls can enforce baseline security but create concentrated privilege and supply-chain risk.","performance_implication":"Shared platform services can improve efficiency but may introduce centralized bottlenecks.","testing_guidance":"Test platform contracts with representative application workloads.","verification_guidance":"Verify that applications receive the promised platform capabilities and policy enforcement.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["platform capabilities"],"prerequisites":["application-platform contract"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B74-0002","D14-AP-B79-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Platform engineering architecture","publisher":"General cloud-native architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B74-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Platform Architecture","concept":"Platform Contract Versioning","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should internal platform interfaces be versioned?","answer":"Versioning allows platform capabilities to evolve while giving consuming applications explicit compatibility expectations and migration paths.","explanation":"A platform is effectively a product consumed by other software. Its APIs, templates, policies, deployment interfaces, and behavioral guarantees can become dependencies that require compatibility management.","principle":"Shared platform interfaces require the same compatibility discipline as external APIs.","correct_example":"Publish a platform interface version with documented supported consumers and migration guidance.","invalid_example":"Change deployment semantics globally without identifying affected applications or providing a compatibility path.","problem":"Platform changes unexpectedly break consuming applications.","failure_mode":"Large numbers of services fail after an infrastructure or policy update.","cause":"Unversioned platform contracts and uncontrolled coupling.","solution":"Define interface versions, compatibility guarantees, deprecation periods, and migration procedures.","alternative":"Fully centralized deployment where consumers have no independent interface contract.","tradeoff":"Versioning increases lifecycle management but reduces uncontrolled blast radius.","constraint":"Old versions eventually require retirement and migration governance.","edge_case":"Security fixes may require accelerated deprecation compared with normal compatibility timelines.","security_implication":"Deprecated insecure platform interfaces should not remain indefinitely for compatibility convenience.","performance_implication":"Supporting multiple versions can increase platform maintenance and resource overhead.","testing_guidance":"Run contract tests against supported platform versions and representative consumers.","verification_guidance":"Verify that documented compatibility guarantees match actual platform behavior.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["platform contract"],"prerequisites":["platform engineering boundary"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B74-0001","D14-AP-B60-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B74-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Platform interface versioning","publisher":"General platform architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B75-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Deployment Patterns","concept":"Blue-Green Deployment","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does blue-green deployment solve?","answer":"Blue-green deployment maintains two deployment environments or release slots so traffic can be shifted from one version to another with a controlled transition and rollback path.","explanation":"One environment serves the current release while the other contains the candidate release. After validation, traffic is switched according to a defined mechanism. The pattern requires compatible state and rollback assumptions.","principle":"Separate release validation from traffic transition when operational conditions permit two concurrently available environments.","correct_example":"Deploy a candidate version to an inactive environment, validate it, then switch eligible traffic to that version while retaining rollback capability.","invalid_example":"Switch traffic between versions that use incompatible database schemas with no migration compatibility strategy.","problem":"A release needs a controlled traffic transition and rapid rollback mechanism.","failure_mode":"Rollback is unsafe because shared state has become incompatible.","cause":"Application and persistence changes were not backward-compatible.","solution":"Design state transitions using expand-and-contract or another compatibility strategy.","alternative":"Canary deployment.","tradeoff":"Blue-green can simplify traffic switching but may require substantial duplicate capacity.","constraint":"Both environments must be supportable during the transition period.","edge_case":"External caches, sessions, background jobs, and asynchronous consumers may continue interacting with both versions.","security_implication":"Both release environments must receive equivalent security controls and secrets governance.","performance_implication":"Running two environments can increase temporary resource consumption.","testing_guidance":"Validate candidate behavior, traffic switching, rollback, session handling, and background processing.","verification_guidance":"Verify traffic routing and rollback behavior before declaring the new release active.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["deployment routing mechanism"],"prerequisites":["compatible release contract"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B75-0002","D14-AP-B76-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D14-AP-B75-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Blue-green deployment pattern","publisher":"General deployment architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B75-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Deployment Patterns","concept":"Canary Deployment","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does canary deployment solve?","answer":"Canary deployment exposes a new release to a controlled subset of traffic before broader rollout so behavior can be evaluated under real operating conditions.","explanation":"Traffic is progressively shifted according to explicit rollout stages and verification criteria. The subset can be defined by percentage, tenant, region, request type, or another controlled dimension.","principle":"Increase release exposure gradually when incremental evidence can reduce deployment risk.","correct_example":"Route a defined small traffic segment to the new release, evaluate predefined health and correctness signals, then expand exposure if criteria remain satisfied.","invalid_example":"Send an arbitrary small percentage of traffic to the new version without defining what observations determine continuation or rollback.","problem":"A release may behave differently under real production conditions than in pre-production tests.","failure_mode":"A defective release reaches an uncontrolled portion of traffic.","cause":"No exposure boundary or automated/manual rollout criteria.","solution":"Define cohort selection, observation windows, success criteria, rollback conditions, and maximum exposure at each stage.","alternative":"Blue-green deployment.","tradeoff":"Canary deployment reduces blast radius but requires reliable traffic segmentation and meaningful production signals.","constraint":"Signals must distinguish release-specific effects from unrelated environmental changes.","edge_case":"Low-volume cohorts may not produce sufficient evidence for rare failures.","security_implication":"Cohort selection must not accidentally expose sensitive tenants or traffic classes to an unapproved release.","performance_implication":"Gradual rollout can reveal resource or latency regressions before full exposure.","testing_guidance":"Test cohort routing, rollback, mixed-version compatibility, and signal evaluation.","verification_guidance":"Verify each rollout stage against predefined objective criteria before expanding exposure.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["traffic segmentation","observability"],"prerequisites":["release compatibility"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B75-0001","D14-AP-B75-0003"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D14-AP-B75-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Canary deployment pattern","publisher":"General deployment architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B76-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Deployment Patterns","concept":"Rolling Deployment","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a rolling deployment?","answer":"A rolling deployment gradually replaces instances of an existing release with instances of a new release while maintaining service capacity during the transition.","explanation":"The rollout controls how many instances are updated at each stage. Correctness depends on mixed-version compatibility, capacity margins, readiness checks, and rollback semantics.","principle":"Replace capacity incrementally while maintaining a valid service state throughout the transition.","correct_example":"Update a bounded number of instances at a time while ensuring remaining capacity can serve traffic and both versions can safely coexist.","invalid_example":"Deploy a new version whose API contract is incompatible with still-running old instances.","problem":"A full replacement may create excessive disruption or require duplicate environments.","failure_mode":"Mixed-version operation causes incompatible requests or insufficient capacity.","cause":"No compatibility or capacity planning.","solution":"Define rollout batch size, readiness conditions, compatibility rules, and rollback behavior.","alternative":"Blue-green or canary deployment.","tradeoff":"Rolling deployment can reduce extra capacity requirements but requires mixed-version compatibility.","constraint":"The system must tolerate old and new versions operating simultaneously.","edge_case":"Long-running connections may remain attached to old instances.","security_implication":"Both versions must receive appropriate security controls throughout the transition.","performance_implication":"Temporary capacity reduction or duplicated capacity may occur depending on rollout strategy.","testing_guidance":"Test mixed-version traffic, readiness failure, partial rollout, rollback, and capacity constraints.","verification_guidance":"Verify service capacity and compatibility after each rollout step.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["deployment controller","health/readiness mechanism"],"prerequisites":["backward-compatible release"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B75-0001","D14-AP-B76-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D14-AP-B75-0001","D14-AP-B75-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Rolling deployment pattern","publisher":"General deployment architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B76-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Deployment Patterns","concept":"Deployment Rollback Contract","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be true before a deployment strategy can claim rollback capability?","answer":"The previous release must remain deployable and compatible with the state and dependencies that exist after the candidate release has operated.","explanation":"Software rollback is not equivalent to reversing a binary deployment. Database schemas, messages, external APIs, caches, and persisted data may have changed. Safe rollback therefore depends on the entire state transition.","principle":"Rollback safety is a property of the complete release state, not merely the application artifact.","correct_example":"Use backward-compatible database migrations so both old and new application versions can operate during the rollback window.","invalid_example":"Deploy a destructive schema migration and assume restoring the previous application binary guarantees rollback.","problem":"Application rollback fails because persistent state is incompatible.","failure_mode":"Old software cannot read or safely process state produced by the new release.","cause":"Release changes were not designed for reversibility or compatibility.","solution":"Use compatible state transitions and explicitly define rollback boundaries.","alternative":"Forward-fix deployment where reverting the application is intentionally unsupported.","tradeoff":"Designing for rollback increases release discipline and migration complexity.","constraint":"Some irreversible changes may require forward remediation rather than binary rollback.","edge_case":"External side effects cannot always be undone after a release has executed.","security_implication":"Rollback must not restore insecure configuration or vulnerable software without an explicit risk decision.","performance_implication":"Rollback mechanisms may require maintaining duplicate capacity or migration paths.","testing_guidance":"Execute rollback scenarios against realistic persistent and asynchronous state.","verification_guidance":"Verify rollback from each supported release stage rather than assuming theoretical reversibility.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["release versioning","state compatibility"],"prerequisites":["deployment strategy"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B76-0001","D14-AP-B60-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Deployment rollback contract","publisher":"Derived from deployment and schema-evolution architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B77-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Runtime Sidecars","concept":"Sidecar Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does the Sidecar pattern solve?","answer":"The Sidecar pattern deploys a supporting capability alongside an application component so that the capability can be reused or separated from application business logic.","explanation":"A sidecar may provide networking, telemetry, proxying, policy enforcement, or another supporting function. It shares a lifecycle or deployment boundary with the primary workload but remains a separate process or component.","principle":"Separate cross-cutting runtime capabilities from business logic when their lifecycle and resource requirements justify the boundary.","correct_example":"Run a local telemetry or protocol proxy alongside an application while keeping application code independent of its implementation.","invalid_example":"Add a sidecar for a capability that requires no isolation and could be implemented more simply inside the application without meaningful operational benefit.","problem":"Cross-cutting infrastructure logic is duplicated across applications.","failure_mode":"Application code becomes tightly coupled to infrastructure-specific mechanisms.","cause":"No reusable runtime boundary or unsuitable abstraction.","solution":"Use a sidecar where deployment-local communication and lifecycle coupling are beneficial.","alternative":"Shared platform service or application library.","tradeoff":"Sidecars improve separation but increase process count, resource usage, configuration, and debugging complexity.","constraint":"The primary workload and sidecar must have compatible lifecycle and communication semantics.","edge_case":"A sidecar failure may affect application startup or traffic if the dependency is mandatory.","security_implication":"Sidecars may receive privileged network, identity, or data access and therefore expand the local trust boundary.","performance_implication":"Inter-process communication adds overhead and consumes additional resources.","testing_guidance":"Test independent startup, shutdown, failure, upgrade, and communication behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the sidecar provides its declared capability without creating unintended application coupling.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["process or container co-location"],"prerequisites":["clear cross-cutting capability"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B77-0002","D14-AP-B78-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D14-AP-B78-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Sidecar pattern","publisher":"General cloud-native architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B77-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Runtime Sidecars","concept":"Sidecar Failure Boundary","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What failure considerations arise when a sidecar is introduced?","answer":"A sidecar can become an additional failure, latency, resource, and security dependency for the primary application.","explanation":"If application traffic or startup depends on the sidecar, its availability and resource consumption become part of the application failure model. Optional sidecars may have different semantics from mandatory ones.","principle":"Every sidecar creates an additional runtime dependency that must be included in failure analysis.","correct_example":"Define whether the application can continue operating when telemetry or proxy sidecar functionality is unavailable.","invalid_example":"Assume sidecar failure is irrelevant because the sidecar is not part of the application source code.","problem":"A supporting component becomes an unrecognized single point of failure or resource consumer.","failure_mode":"Application availability or performance degrades when the sidecar fails or saturates.","cause":"Sidecar dependency was omitted from architecture analysis.","solution":"Define mandatory versus optional behavior, resource limits, health semantics, and degraded-mode behavior.","alternative":"In-process library or shared infrastructure service.","tradeoff":"Explicit sidecar dependency analysis increases design effort but makes failure behavior visible.","constraint":"Sidecar resource limits must leave sufficient capacity for the primary workload.","edge_case":"A sidecar can be healthy while its downstream dependency is unavailable.","security_implication":"Sidecar compromise may provide access to local application traffic or credentials.","performance_implication":"CPU, memory, network, and serialization overhead must be included in capacity planning.","testing_guidance":"Kill, throttle, restart, and isolate the sidecar while exercising the primary workload.","verification_guidance":"Verify documented application behavior under sidecar failure and recovery.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["sidecar pattern"],"prerequisites":["sidecar dependency analysis"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B77-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B77-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Sidecar failure boundaries","publisher":"Derived from runtime isolation principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B78-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Runtime Networking","concept":"Ambassador Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does the Ambassador pattern solve?","answer":"The Ambassador pattern places a proxy or helper component alongside an application to handle communication concerns on the application's behalf.","explanation":"The ambassador can centralize connection management, protocol translation, retries, observability, or access to an external service while presenting a simpler local interface to the application. Its responsibilities should remain explicit.","principle":"Move suitable communication concerns into a local intermediary when the resulting boundary provides meaningful reuse or isolation.","correct_example":"Expose a local endpoint to an application while an adjacent proxy manages connections and protocol-specific communication with an external service.","invalid_example":"Insert an ambassador merely to add another network hop without reducing complexity or providing a required capability.","problem":"Application communication logic is repeated or tightly coupled to external protocol details.","failure_mode":"Network behavior becomes duplicated across many applications.","cause":"No reusable communication abstraction.","solution":"Use a local intermediary with a documented contract and bounded responsibility.","alternative":"Shared remote gateway or application library.","tradeoff":"The ambassador can simplify application code but adds a local dependency and network hop.","constraint":"The intermediary must preserve required semantics and security properties.","edge_case":"Proxy behavior may alter retries, timeouts, connection pooling, or error semantics.","security_implication":"The ambassador may terminate or originate connections and therefore becomes part of the trust boundary.","performance_implication":"An extra process and hop can add latency and consume resources.","testing_guidance":"Test protocol translation, failure propagation, timeout behavior, and intermediary restart.","verification_guidance":"Verify that communication semantics remain within the documented contract.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["local proxy or intermediary"],"prerequisites":["communication abstraction requirement"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B77-0001","D14-AP-B78-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D14-AP-B77-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Ambassador pattern","publisher":"General cloud-native architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B78-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Runtime Networking","concept":"Proxy Responsibility Boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should determine whether a networking concern belongs in an ambassador or in the application?","answer":"The boundary should be based on whether the concern is infrastructure-level and reusable without changing application business semantics.","explanation":"Protocol mechanics, connection pooling, transport policy, and common telemetry may fit a proxy. Business-specific retries, workflow decisions, or domain semantics generally remain application responsibilities.","principle":"Infrastructure communication concerns may be delegated; business semantics should remain owned by the business component.","correct_example":"Delegate connection pooling to a proxy while keeping business-level retry decisions in the service when they depend on domain semantics.","invalid_example":"Move business transaction decisions into a generic proxy that cannot understand domain invariants.","problem":"Infrastructure and business responsibilities become mixed.","failure_mode":"Proxy behavior changes application semantics unexpectedly.","cause":"Incorrect responsibility boundary.","solution":"Define an explicit contract for proxy behavior and keep domain decisions within the owning application.","alternative":"Application library with shared communication code.","tradeoff":"A proxy provides centralized behavior but may hide important semantics from application developers.","constraint":"Proxy policy must be observable and configurable according to the required governance model.","edge_case":"A supposedly generic concern may contain domain-specific exceptions.","security_implication":"Security policy delegation must preserve end-to-end identity and authorization semantics.","performance_implication":"Centralized proxy behavior can optimize connections but may become a local bottleneck.","testing_guidance":"Test both normal communication and semantic edge cases where proxy policy interacts with application behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the proxy changes only the behaviors included in its documented contract.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["ambassador pattern"],"prerequisites":["responsibility classification"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B78-0001","D14-AP-B77-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B78-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Proxy responsibility boundary","publisher":"Derived from architecture responsibility-separation principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B79-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Infrastructure Automation","concept":"Operator Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does the Operator pattern solve?","answer":"The Operator pattern encodes operational knowledge for managing a particular application or system through automated reconciliation against a declared desired state.","explanation":"An operator observes current state, compares it with desired state, and performs permitted actions to move the system toward convergence. It is useful when operational procedures are repeatable and can be expressed through explicit control logic.","principle":"Automate repeatable operational control through an explicit desired-state and reconciliation model.","correct_example":"An operator observes application resources and creates or updates dependent resources until declared desired configuration is satisfied.","invalid_example":"Implement an operator that continuously mutates resources without an explicit desired state or bounded action policy.","problem":"Complex application operations are performed manually and inconsistently.","failure_mode":"Operational state drifts from intended state or recovery procedures vary by operator.","cause":"Operational knowledge is undocumented or not automated.","solution":"Represent desired state, observed state, reconciliation rules, permissions, and failure behavior explicitly.","alternative":"Manual runbooks or centralized deployment automation.","tradeoff":"Operators automate complex lifecycle management but introduce control-loop complexity and elevated privileges.","constraint":"Reconciliation must be idempotent and safe under repeated observation and partial failure.","edge_case":"External systems may change state independently of the operator.","security_implication":"Operators often require high privileges; least privilege, action boundaries, auditability, and safe handling of untrusted state are essential.","performance_implication":"Frequent reconciliation consumes API and compute resources; polling intervals and event triggers should be bounded.","testing_guidance":"Test convergence, repeated reconciliation, partial failures, resource deletion, external drift, and operator restart.","verification_guidance":"Verify that reconciliation converges toward desired state without unauthorized or uncontrolled actions.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["control-plane API or equivalent resource interface"],"prerequisites":["desired-state model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B79-0002","D14-AP-B69-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCEPTUAL_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Operator pattern","publisher":"General cloud-native architecture literature","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B79-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Infrastructure Automation","concept":"Reconciliation Loop","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What properties should a safe reconciliation loop have?","answer":"A reconciliation loop should observe current state, compute the difference from desired state, perform bounded permitted actions, and repeat until convergence or an explicit failure condition is reached.","explanation":"Reconciliation is not equivalent to blindly executing a sequence of commands. It must tolerate repeated execution, partial failures, stale observations, and external changes.","principle":"Control loops should be state-aware, idempotent where possible, bounded, and observable.","correct_example":"Re-read relevant state after an update and reconcile again rather than assuming the requested change succeeded.","invalid_example":"Assume an API call succeeded because it returned without locally checking the resulting state.","problem":"Control logic acts on stale or incorrect assumptions about system state.","failure_mode":"Repeated actions create drift or amplify an existing failure.","cause":"No state verification or idempotent action design.","solution":"Use explicit desired/observed state, action preconditions, post-action verification, and retry limits.","alternative":"Transactional workflow engine.","tradeoff":"Reconciliation tolerates drift well but may be more complex than a one-time imperative workflow.","constraint":"The state model must identify authoritative sources and permitted transitions.","edge_case":"Two controllers may attempt to reconcile the same resource concurrently.","security_implication":"Every reconciliation action must be authorized and auditable.","performance_implication":"Continuous reconciliation can create API load and should use appropriate event-driven or bounded polling strategies.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate events, stale state, partial updates, conflicting actors, and controller restarts.","verification_guidance":"Verify eventual convergence and rejection of unauthorized state transitions.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["operator or reconciliation controller"],"prerequisites":["desired-state model","state authority definition"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B79-0001","D14-AP-B70-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B79-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Reconciliation loop principles","publisher":"Derived from control-loop and cloud-native architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B80-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Cloud-Native Synthesis","concept":"Cloud-Native Pattern Selection","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should cloud-native architecture patterns be selected?","answer":"Select patterns according to workload characteristics, operational requirements, failure modes, state semantics, platform constraints, security boundaries, and organizational capabilities rather than adopting them as a default technology bundle.","explanation":"Containers, serverless, sidecars, platform engineering, deployment strategies, and operators solve different problems. Combining them without a specific architectural need can increase complexity without improving required system properties.","principle":"Adopt the minimum architecture necessary to satisfy explicit system requirements and failure constraints.","correct_example":"Choose serverless for a bounded event-driven workload when platform limits and operational requirements fit, while using containers for a workload requiring longer-lived process control.","invalid_example":"Require every service to use containers, sidecars, operators, and serverless functions regardless of workload characteristics.","problem":"Technology adoption becomes pattern-driven rather than requirement-driven.","failure_mode":"Operational complexity increases without a corresponding requirement benefit.","cause":"Pattern cargo culting and missing architecture decision criteria.","solution":"Document problem, quality attributes, constraints, alternatives, and verification criteria for each selected pattern.","alternative":"Simpler deployment architecture with fewer platform abstractions.","tradeoff":"More sophisticated patterns can provide stronger automation or isolation but increase cognitive and operational load.","constraint":"Pattern selection must remain compatible with team skills, platform capabilities, lifecycle requirements, and budget constraints.","edge_case":"A pattern may solve one quality attribute while degrading another.","security_implication":"Every added platform component expands the trust, privilege, and supply-chain surface.","performance_implication":"Performance impact must be evaluated for the actual workload rather than assumed from the pattern name.","testing_guidance":"Test the composed architecture, including failure interactions between selected patterns.","verification_guidance":"Map each selected pattern to an explicit requirement and observable verification condition.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture requirements","failure-mode analysis"],"prerequisites":["quality attributes","architecture decision process"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B70-0001","D14-AP-B80-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Cloud-native architecture pattern selection","publisher":"Derived from D14 cloud-native pattern relationships","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B80-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Cloud-Native Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Cloud-Native Synthesis","concept":"Cloud-Native Architecture Quality Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified before declaring a cloud-native architecture pattern set complete?","answer":"Verify deployment reproducibility, state ownership, failure boundaries, resource limits, security controls, observability, compatibility, rollback or recovery behavior, and the operational contract of every selected platform pattern.","explanation":"Cloud-native architecture is a composition of independently evolving runtime and platform components. The quality gate must therefore evaluate the interactions between patterns rather than only the individual patterns.","principle":"Architecture completeness requires evidence that the composed system satisfies its stated contracts under normal and failure conditions.","correct_example":"Trace each selected pattern to a requirement, dependency, failure mode, test, and verification condition, while marking unexecuted claims explicitly.","invalid_example":"Declare architecture production-ready solely because it uses containers, orchestration, or automated deployment.","problem":"Architectural maturity is inferred from technology adoption rather than evidence.","failure_mode":"Production behavior violates undocumented assumptions about state, scaling, recovery, or security.","cause":"Missing architecture-level verification.","solution":"Use an architecture quality gate covering requirements, contracts, dependencies, security, resilience, testing, and evidence status.","alternative":"Formal architecture review with independent verification.","tradeoff":"Comprehensive review requires additional effort but improves traceability and reduces assumption-driven design.","constraint":"Conceptual validation must remain distinct from executed verification.","edge_case":"Some production-scale properties cannot be established without representative environments and workloads.","security_implication":"Verification must include identity, privilege, secrets, supply-chain, network, and tenant-isolation boundaries where applicable.","performance_implication":"Performance claims require measured evidence under representative conditions; architecture alone cannot establish numerical performance.","testing_guidance":"Include contract, integration, failure-injection, deployment, rollback, security, and load tests according to risk.","verification_guidance":"Use explicit statuses such as NOT_EXECUTED, NOT_VERIFIED, TESTED, and VERIFIED rather than inferring evidence.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["cloud-native pattern selection"],"prerequisites":["architecture requirements","verification strategy"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B80-0001","D14-AP-B70-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B80-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Cloud-native architecture quality gate","publisher":"Derived from D14 architecture verification principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

