{"record_id":"D14-AP-B81-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Zero Trust","concept":"Zero Trust Architecture","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What architectural problem does Zero Trust address?","answer":"Zero Trust architecture treats access decisions as explicit policy decisions rather than assuming that network location or prior access automatically establishes trust.","explanation":"A zero-trust design evaluates identity, device or workload context, requested resource, action, policy, and relevant risk signals according to the system's requirements. The exact signals and enforcement mechanisms vary by environment.","principle":"Access should be explicitly authorized for the requested resource and action rather than granted solely because a requester is inside a trusted network boundary.","correct_example":"Require authenticated workload identity and resource-specific authorization before allowing a service to access another service's protected operation.","invalid_example":"Allow unrestricted access to all internal services because traffic originates from an internal network.","problem":"Network location is treated as a sufficient security boundary.","failure_mode":"Compromise of one internal component enables unauthorized lateral access.","cause":"Implicit trust based on network placement or prior authentication.","solution":"Apply explicit identity, policy, authorization, and enforcement boundaries to protected resources.","alternative":"Traditional network-perimeter security with internal segmentation.","tradeoff":"Zero-trust controls can improve access granularity but increase identity, policy, telemetry, and enforcement complexity.","constraint":"Reliable identity and policy-enforcement mechanisms are required.","edge_case":"Emergency or degraded operation may require explicitly governed fallback access.","security_implication":"The architecture should minimize implicit trust and constrain lateral movement.","performance_implication":"Additional identity and policy checks add processing and network overhead that should be measured.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized lateral access, expired credentials, policy changes, and degraded identity-provider conditions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that access decisions depend on defined authorization conditions rather than network location alone.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity","authorization","policy enforcement"],"prerequisites":["resource classification","identity model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B81-0002","D14-AP-B82-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D14-AP-B81-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Zero Trust architecture","publisher":"General security architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B81-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Zero Trust","concept":"Explicit Trust Boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should define a trust boundary in a zero-trust architecture?","answer":"A trust boundary should correspond to an explicit authorization and resource-protection boundary rather than simply a network segment.","explanation":"Network segmentation can support enforcement but does not by itself define authorization. A service, data store, tenant, privileged operation, or administrative interface may each require separate authorization semantics.","principle":"Trust boundaries should be derived from protected resources and allowed actions.","correct_example":"Treat an administrative operation as a distinct authorization boundary even when it is exposed through the same network path as ordinary operations.","invalid_example":"Assume every service inside one private network belongs to one unrestricted trust domain.","problem":"Different sensitivity levels share implicit authorization.","failure_mode":"A compromised low-privilege component gains access to higher-value resources.","cause":"Coarse trust boundaries.","solution":"Define resource-level and action-level authorization boundaries.","alternative":"Network-only segmentation.","tradeoff":"Fine-grained boundaries improve containment but increase policy complexity.","constraint":"Every protected boundary requires an enforceable policy decision point or equivalent mechanism.","edge_case":"A single request may cross multiple trust boundaries.","security_implication":"Explicit boundaries reduce lateral-movement opportunities.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained authorization can increase request-path overhead.","testing_guidance":"Attempt access across every defined boundary using identities with different privileges.","verification_guidance":"Verify that crossing a boundary requires the expected authorization decision.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["authorization policy"],"prerequisites":["zero-trust model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B81-0001","D14-AP-B83-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B81-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Explicit trust boundaries","publisher":"General zero-trust architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B82-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Identity-Centric Architecture","concept":"Identity Perimeter","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is an identity perimeter?","answer":"An identity perimeter makes authenticated and attributable identities a primary architectural boundary for controlling access to resources and operations.","explanation":"Instead of relying primarily on network location, the architecture associates requests with identities such as users, services, workloads, devices, or tenants. Authorization then evaluates the identity against resource and action policy.","principle":"Access control should be tied to an identifiable principal and its authorized scope.","correct_example":"Authorize a service request using a verifiable workload identity and a policy describing the permitted resource and operation.","invalid_example":"Permit access based only on source IP address when identity-level authorization is required.","problem":"Network identifiers do not adequately express who or what is requesting an operation.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized principals obtain access through shared network paths or compromised hosts.","cause":"Identity is not part of the authorization decision.","solution":"Establish verifiable identities and map them to explicit permissions.","alternative":"Network-based access control.","tradeoff":"Identity-centric controls provide finer granularity but require identity lifecycle and credential management.","constraint":"Identity must be reliably authenticated and appropriately scoped.","edge_case":"Identity-provider unavailability can affect access decisions.","security_implication":"Compromised identity credentials can become a direct authorization path, requiring credential protection and revocation mechanisms.","performance_implication":"Identity validation and policy lookup introduce request overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test identity issuance, expiration, revocation, impersonation resistance, and authorization mapping.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unauthorized identities cannot obtain permissions through network placement alone.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity provider","authorization system"],"prerequisites":["principal model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B82-0002","D14-AP-B81-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B81-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Identity-centric security architecture","publisher":"General security architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B82-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Identity-Centric Architecture","concept":"Workload Identity","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should service-to-service access use workload identity?","answer":"Workload identity provides a distinct, attributable identity for an application or service instance so authorization can be based on the workload rather than only on network location or shared credentials.","explanation":"Workload identities can support service-level authorization, credential rotation, audit attribution, and reduced dependence on shared static secrets. Implementation details depend on the platform and identity system.","principle":"Each security-relevant workload should have an identity appropriate to its authorization scope.","correct_example":"Authorize Service A to invoke a specific operation on Service B using a verifiable workload identity.","invalid_example":"Give every internal service the same shared credential with broad permissions.","problem":"Shared credentials prevent precise attribution and increase blast radius.","failure_mode":"Compromise of one service exposes access intended for many services.","cause":"Shared identity or overbroad service credentials.","solution":"Use distinct workload identities with least-privilege permissions and lifecycle management.","alternative":"Shared service account with tightly restricted scope.","tradeoff":"Distinct identities improve isolation and attribution but increase identity lifecycle complexity.","constraint":"Identity issuance and rotation must be operationally reliable.","edge_case":"Ephemeral workloads require automated identity provisioning and revocation.","security_implication":"Workload identity reduces shared-secret exposure when implemented correctly.","performance_implication":"Identity verification adds overhead but can be cached or optimized according to security requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test identity issuance, rotation, revocation, impersonation, and cross-service authorization.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each workload receives only the permissions associated with its declared identity.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity infrastructure"],"prerequisites":["service inventory","authorization model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B82-0001","D14-AP-B84-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B82-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Workload identity","publisher":"General cloud-native security architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B83-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Policy Enforcement Point","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a Policy Enforcement Point?","answer":"A Policy Enforcement Point is an architectural component that intercepts or controls access to a protected resource and enforces the result of an authorization policy decision.","explanation":"The enforcement point may be located at an API gateway, service boundary, data access layer, operating-system boundary, or another resource-specific control point. A policy decision mechanism and enforcement mechanism should have clearly defined responsibilities.","principle":"Authorization policy is effective only when its decision is reliably enforced at the protected resource boundary.","correct_example":"Require an authorization decision before a request reaches a protected administrative operation.","invalid_example":"Evaluate authorization in a user interface while allowing the backend resource to be accessed directly without enforcement.","problem":"Policy exists conceptually but can be bypassed through alternate access paths.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized requests reach the protected resource.","cause":"Missing or incorrectly placed enforcement point.","solution":"Place enforcement at every relevant resource boundary and protect alternate paths.","alternative":"Embedded authorization inside each resource implementation.","tradeoff":"Centralized enforcement improves consistency while distributed enforcement can provide resource-specific control.","constraint":"Every bypass path must be considered.","edge_case":"Internal service calls may bypass an external gateway unless equivalent enforcement exists internally.","security_implication":"A PEP is a security-critical component and should fail according to explicit policy when its decision service is unavailable.","performance_implication":"Enforcement adds request processing and potentially policy-lookup latency.","testing_guidance":"Test direct, indirect, malformed, and alternate-path access attempts.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every protected resource has an enforceable authorization boundary.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["policy decision mechanism"],"prerequisites":["resource authorization policy"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B83-0002","D14-AP-B81-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B81-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Policy Enforcement Point","publisher":"General access-control architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B83-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Fail-Safe Authorization Boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should a policy enforcement point behave when authorization cannot be determined?","answer":"The behavior should be explicitly defined according to the sensitivity of the protected operation; for security-sensitive access, the architecture commonly requires denying access when required authorization evidence is unavailable.","explanation":"The correct degraded behavior depends on resource criticality and business requirements. The important architectural property is that authorization failure is not silently converted into unrestricted access.","principle":"Loss of authorization evidence must not accidentally create broader authority.","correct_example":"Reject a privileged operation when the required authorization service or credential validation result is unavailable.","invalid_example":"Allow unrestricted administrative access whenever the policy service is unreachable.","problem":"Dependency failure creates an unintended authorization bypass.","failure_mode":"Protected operations become available during control-plane failure.","cause":"Undefined fail-open behavior.","solution":"Define explicit fail-open or fail-closed semantics per resource and risk class, with controlled emergency procedures where necessary.","alternative":"Cached authorization decisions with bounded validity.","tradeoff":"Fail-closed behavior protects security but can reduce availability during identity or policy-service outages.","constraint":"Emergency access must be separately controlled, auditable, and time-bounded.","edge_case":"Read-only low-risk operations may have different degraded-mode requirements than destructive administrative actions.","security_implication":"Authorization degradation must never silently expand privileges.","performance_implication":"Synchronous policy validation can affect latency and availability.","testing_guidance":"Simulate policy-service failure, stale decisions, expired credentials, and network partitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each protected operation follows its documented degraded authorization behavior.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["policy enforcement point","authorization service"],"prerequisites":["resource risk classification"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B83-0001","D14-AP-B89-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B83-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Authorization failure semantics","publisher":"General secure-access architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B84-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Token Exchange","concept":"Token Exchange","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does token exchange solve in distributed authorization?","answer":"Token exchange allows a caller to obtain a credential representing a narrower or different authorized context for a downstream resource instead of forwarding an original credential indiscriminately.","explanation":"A token exchange mechanism can reduce privilege propagation by issuing a token scoped to the intended audience, resource, action, or delegation context. Exact semantics depend on the identity protocol and authorization architecture.","principle":"Credentials should be scoped to the resource and authority required for the next operation.","correct_example":"Exchange an incoming user or service credential for a downstream token intended only for Service B.","invalid_example":"Forward a broad administrative credential through every downstream service regardless of its required permissions.","problem":"Broad credentials propagate unnecessarily across service boundaries.","failure_mode":"Compromise of an intermediate service exposes authority intended for unrelated resources.","cause":"Credential forwarding without audience or scope restriction.","solution":"Use scoped downstream credentials and explicit delegation semantics.","alternative":"End-to-end original-token propagation with strict audience validation.","tradeoff":"Token exchange improves privilege containment but introduces identity infrastructure and token lifecycle complexity.","constraint":"Delegation and subject identity semantics must remain auditable.","edge_case":"A downstream service may need both user identity and service identity for authorization.","security_implication":"Audience, scope, expiry, issuer, and delegation claims must be validated according to the trust model.","performance_implication":"Token exchange adds authorization infrastructure calls or cryptographic processing.","testing_guidance":"Test wrong audience, excessive scope, expired tokens, replay, delegation chains, and revocation behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that exchanged credentials cannot authorize operations outside their declared scope.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity provider","token validation"],"prerequisites":["delegation model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B84-0002","D14-AP-B82-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B82-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Token exchange and delegated authorization","publisher":"General identity architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B84-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Token Exchange","concept":"Audience and Scope Restriction","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be verified for a scoped downstream token?","answer":"The receiving resource should verify that the token was issued by an accepted authority, is intended for the receiving audience, remains valid, and contains sufficient but not excessive authority for the requested operation.","explanation":"Authentication of a token does not automatically establish authorization for a particular resource or operation. Audience and scope restrictions limit authority propagation.","principle":"Valid credentials must still be authorized for the exact resource and operation.","correct_example":"Reject a valid token whose audience identifies another service.","invalid_example":"Accept any valid token issued by a trusted authority regardless of its intended audience.","problem":"Credentials are accepted outside their intended authorization context.","failure_mode":"Cross-service credential reuse creates unintended authority.","cause":"Missing audience or scope validation.","solution":"Validate issuer, audience, expiry, integrity, scope, and relevant delegation semantics.","alternative":"Mutual authentication plus service-local authorization.","tradeoff":"Detailed validation improves isolation but requires consistent claim and policy semantics.","constraint":"The issuer and audience trust relationships must be explicitly configured.","edge_case":"Multiple audiences or delegated chains require precise interpretation.","security_implication":"Audience validation limits credential replay across service boundaries.","performance_implication":"Additional claim and signature validation consumes CPU and may require key-management lookups.","testing_guidance":"Test valid credentials with wrong audience, insufficient scope, expired validity, and altered claims.","verification_guidance":"Attempt cross-service credential reuse and verify deterministic rejection.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["token exchange or scoped credentials"],"prerequisites":["audience model","scope model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B84-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B84-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Token audience and scope validation","publisher":"General identity and authorization architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B85-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Secrets Management","concept":"Centralized Secrets Management","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does centralized secrets management solve?","answer":"Centralized secrets management provides controlled storage, retrieval, rotation, access policy, and auditing for sensitive credentials and cryptographic material.","explanation":"Secrets include credentials, API keys, private keys, tokens, and other sensitive values. A dedicated secrets mechanism can reduce hard-coded or widely copied credentials and support lifecycle controls.","principle":"Sensitive credentials should have an explicit lifecycle and access boundary rather than being treated as ordinary application configuration.","correct_example":"Allow an application workload to retrieve only the secrets required for its declared function from a controlled secret store.","invalid_example":"Commit long-lived production credentials to source control or package them into a public application artifact.","problem":"Secrets become broadly exposed and difficult to rotate.","failure_mode":"Credential compromise persists across deployments and systems.","cause":"Uncontrolled secret distribution and lifecycle.","solution":"Centralize secret storage or use an equivalent secure lifecycle mechanism with least-privilege retrieval.","alternative":"Platform-native secret injection with equivalent access controls.","tradeoff":"Centralized management improves lifecycle control but introduces a critical infrastructure dependency.","constraint":"Secret-store availability, recovery, access control, and key management must be addressed.","edge_case":"Bootstrap credentials may be required to obtain later secrets and therefore need separate protection.","security_implication":"Secret stores are high-value security targets and require strong authentication, authorization, encryption, audit, and recovery controls.","performance_implication":"Secret retrieval can add startup or request latency; caching must respect rotation semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized retrieval, rotation, expiry, secret-store failure, and application restart.","verification_guidance":"Verify that workloads can access only their authorized secrets and that secret values are not unnecessarily exposed in logs or artifacts.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["secret-management mechanism"],"prerequisites":["secret inventory","identity model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B85-0002","D14-AP-B71-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B82-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Secrets management architecture","publisher":"General secure-systems architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B85-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Secrets Management","concept":"Secret Rotation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an architecture define for secret rotation?","answer":"It should define secret ownership, rotation trigger, overlap period where required, consumer update behavior, revocation semantics, failure handling, and verification of successful replacement.","explanation":"Rotation is not merely generating a new value. Consumers must transition safely and old credentials must eventually lose authority according to policy.","principle":"Credential lifecycle must include issuance, distribution, replacement, revocation, and verification.","correct_example":"Issue a replacement credential, update consumers within a bounded compatibility window, verify usage of the new credential, then revoke the old credential.","invalid_example":"Rotate a credential immediately without checking whether all authorized consumers can obtain the replacement.","problem":"Credential rotation causes avoidable service outages or leaves old credentials active indefinitely.","failure_mode":"Consumers fail after rotation or compromised credentials remain usable.","cause":"No coordinated lifecycle semantics.","solution":"Define rotation protocol and verify consumer adoption before revocation when overlap is safe.","alternative":"Short-lived credentials that reduce dependence on manual rotation.","tradeoff":"Frequent rotation reduces credential lifetime but increases lifecycle and operational complexity.","constraint":"Some credentials cannot support seamless overlap and require coordinated downtime or migration.","edge_case":"A compromised credential may require immediate revocation without waiting for normal migration.","security_implication":"Rotation limits credential lifetime and supports compromise response.","performance_implication":"Frequent credential retrieval or refresh may add infrastructure load.","testing_guidance":"Test normal rotation, failed consumer update, emergency revocation, and recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify that old credentials lose authorization according to policy and new credentials function correctly.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["secret-management mechanism","identity system"],"prerequisites":["credential lifecycle"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B85-0001","D14-AP-B89-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B85-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Secret rotation lifecycle","publisher":"General credential-management architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B86-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Service-to-Service Security","concept":"Mutual Service Authentication","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does mutual service authentication solve?","answer":"Mutual service authentication allows both communicating services to establish authenticated identities before exchanging protected traffic.","explanation":"Mutual authentication can prevent a service from accepting traffic from an unauthenticated or unexpected peer. Authentication alone does not establish authorization for every operation.","principle":"Both sides of a protected service relationship should authenticate according to the trust model before relying on peer identity.","correct_example":"Service A validates Service B's authenticated workload identity before accepting a privileged service-to-service request.","invalid_example":"Trust any caller presenting an encrypted connection without validating peer identity.","problem":"A service cannot reliably determine the identity of its peer.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized services can impersonate trusted callers.","cause":"Encryption without authenticated peer identity.","solution":"Use authenticated service identities and validate them at the relevant boundary.","alternative":"One-way server authentication with application-level caller identity.","tradeoff":"Mutual authentication improves peer assurance but adds certificate or credential lifecycle complexity.","constraint":"Identity provisioning, rotation, revocation, and trust roots must be managed.","edge_case":"Legacy services may not support mutual authentication and require a controlled compatibility boundary.","security_implication":"Peer authentication reduces impersonation and unauthorized service access.","performance_implication":"Handshake and cryptographic processing add connection overhead, often mitigated through connection reuse.","testing_guidance":"Test valid peers, unknown peers, expired credentials, revoked identities, and trust-root changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that only authorized peer identities can establish protected service relationships.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["service identity","cryptographic transport or equivalent"],"prerequisites":["workload identity"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B86-0002","D14-AP-B82-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B82-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Mutual service authentication","publisher":"General secure service-to-service architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B86-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Service-to-Service Security","concept":"Authentication Authorization Separation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must service authentication and authorization remain distinct concepts?","answer":"Authentication establishes or verifies who or what a principal is, while authorization determines whether that principal is permitted to perform a specific operation on a specific resource.","explanation":"A successfully authenticated service may still have no permission for a requested resource or action. Treating authentication as authorization creates broad implicit trust.","principle":"Identity proof does not automatically grant permission.","correct_example":"Authenticate Service A and then evaluate whether Service A may invoke the requested administrative operation.","invalid_example":"Allow every authenticated internal service to access every API endpoint.","problem":"Identity verification is incorrectly treated as permission.","failure_mode":"Authenticated but unauthorized workloads gain access.","cause":"Missing authorization policy after authentication.","solution":"Perform explicit authorization based on resource, action, identity, and relevant context.","alternative":"Predefined service allowlists for narrow systems.","tradeoff":"Explicit authorization increases policy complexity but reduces implicit trust.","constraint":"Authorization policies must remain consistent with identity lifecycle.","edge_case":"A service may have different permissions for different resources or operations.","security_implication":"Separating authentication and authorization limits privilege escalation.","performance_implication":"Authorization evaluation adds processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test authenticated principals with allowed and denied operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that successful authentication alone never grants unauthorized resource access.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity","authorization"],"prerequisites":["service identity"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B86-0001","D14-AP-B83-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B86-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Authentication and authorization separation","publisher":"General access-control architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B87-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant Isolation Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does tenant isolation address in multi-tenant architecture?","answer":"Tenant isolation prevents one tenant's data, authorization scope, resource usage, or operational actions from improperly affecting or exposing another tenant.","explanation":"Isolation may be implemented at data, application, compute, network, identity, or infrastructure layers. The appropriate boundary depends on risk, workload, regulatory requirements, and platform capabilities.","principle":"Tenant identity and ownership must remain explicit across every resource boundary containing tenant-specific state.","correct_example":"Carry a verified tenant context into authorization and data-access decisions and enforce it at the protected resource boundary.","invalid_example":"Trust a tenant identifier supplied by a client without binding it to authenticated and authorized tenant context.","problem":"Shared resources can expose cross-tenant data or permit unauthorized operations.","failure_mode":"Tenant A accesses or modifies resources belonging to Tenant B.","cause":"Missing or inconsistently enforced tenant context.","solution":"Bind tenant context to authenticated identity and enforce isolation at relevant resource boundaries.","alternative":"Physically separate infrastructure per tenant.","tradeoff":"Logical isolation improves density and cost efficiency but requires rigorous enforcement and testing.","constraint":"Every path capable of accessing tenant data must preserve tenant isolation semantics.","edge_case":"Administrative or support operations may intentionally cross tenant boundaries and require separate privileged authorization.","security_implication":"Tenant isolation is a critical authorization and data-protection boundary.","performance_implication":"Per-tenant filtering and policy checks can add query or request overhead.","testing_guidance":"Perform cross-tenant access tests across APIs, databases, caches, queues, logs, files, and background jobs.","verification_guidance":"Verify that tenant context cannot be forged, omitted, or replaced during request processing.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["tenant identity","authorization","data ownership"],"prerequisites":["multi-tenant model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B87-0002","D14-AP-B81-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B81-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Multi-tenant isolation architecture","publisher":"General multi-tenant security principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B87-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Cross-Tenant Isolation Verification","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should tenant isolation be tested architecturally?","answer":"Tests should attempt unauthorized cross-tenant access through every relevant interface and state path, including direct APIs, background processing, caches, storage, messaging, and administrative workflows.","explanation":"Tenant isolation failures frequently occur outside the primary request path. A comprehensive test model must cover synchronous and asynchronous paths and derived data.","principle":"Tenant isolation must be verified at every path that can read, modify, infer, or expose tenant-specific state.","correct_example":"Execute a request authenticated for Tenant A that attempts to access Tenant B's resource and verify denial at the protected boundary.","invalid_example":"Test only the primary API endpoint while assuming background jobs and cache layers preserve tenant boundaries automatically.","problem":"Cross-tenant leakage remains undetected in secondary paths.","failure_mode":"Tenant-specific information becomes visible through caches, reports, queues, logs, or asynchronous workers.","cause":"Incomplete isolation test surface.","solution":"Enumerate all tenant-bearing data flows and test each relevant boundary.","alternative":"Formal isolation proof for systems with suitable models.","tradeoff":"Broad testing increases effort but provides stronger coverage of multi-tenant failure modes.","constraint":"Tests must avoid using real sensitive tenant data where synthetic fixtures are sufficient.","edge_case":"Aggregated analytics may intentionally combine tenants and require separate authorization semantics.","security_implication":"Isolation tests should include privilege escalation and identifier manipulation attempts.","performance_implication":"Large tenant datasets may be needed to expose partitioning or query-planning defects.","testing_guidance":"Use positive and negative tenant-access cases across all storage and processing paths.","verification_guidance":"Record evidence showing that unauthorized tenant crossings are rejected or correctly isolated.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["tenant isolation boundary"],"prerequisites":["tenant data-flow inventory"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B87-0001","D14-AP-B90-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B87-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Cross-tenant isolation verification","publisher":"Derived from tenant-isolation architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B88-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Data Classification","concept":"Data Classification Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What architectural purpose does data classification provide?","answer":"Data classification assigns meaningful protection requirements to information so architecture and controls can vary according to sensitivity, confidentiality, integrity, availability, or regulatory characteristics.","explanation":"Classification should drive access, storage, transmission, retention, logging, encryption, monitoring, and deletion requirements where applicable. Labels without enforceable consequences provide limited architectural value.","principle":"Security controls should be proportional to the defined sensitivity and handling requirements of the data.","correct_example":"Classify sensitive customer data and enforce stricter access, storage, transmission, and logging controls for that class.","invalid_example":"Mark data as sensitive without changing any access or handling behavior.","problem":"Different data sensitivities receive identical protection despite different risks.","failure_mode":"Sensitive data is exposed through ordinary application or infrastructure paths.","cause":"Classification is descriptive but not connected to policy enforcement.","solution":"Map classification levels to explicit handling and authorization policies.","alternative":"Resource-specific security policies without formal classification labels.","tradeoff":"Classification improves systematic policy application but requires governance and consistent labeling.","constraint":"Classification criteria must be understandable, stable, and applicable across data lifecycle stages.","edge_case":"A dataset may contain mixed-sensitivity fields requiring field-level or record-level controls.","security_implication":"Classification can drive encryption, access, monitoring, retention, and disclosure controls.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained classification controls may increase processing and storage overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test data movement between classification zones and verify policy changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that classified data receives the controls defined for its classification.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["data governance","authorization"],"prerequisites":["data inventory"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B88-0002","D14-AP-B87-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B87-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Data classification architecture","publisher":"General information-security architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B88-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Data Classification","concept":"Classification-Aware Data Flow","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should data classification affect architecture across data flows?","answer":"Classification should travel with data or remain derivable through authoritative metadata so downstream components can apply the required handling policy.","explanation":"Data may pass through databases, queues, caches, logs, analytics systems, backups, and external integrations. Losing classification context can cause downstream controls to be applied incorrectly.","principle":"Protection requirements must remain associated with sensitive information throughout its lifecycle.","correct_example":"Attach or derive classification metadata when publishing sensitive records to a queue so consumers can apply the required handling policy.","invalid_example":"Classify data only in the original database while downstream systems treat exported records as ordinary data.","problem":"Security requirements are lost at system boundaries.","failure_mode":"Sensitive information enters lower-protection systems without appropriate controls.","cause":"Classification metadata is not propagated or enforced.","solution":"Define classification propagation, transformation, inheritance, and downgrade rules.","alternative":"Dedicated storage and processing zones for each sensitivity class.","tradeoff":"Metadata propagation improves flexibility but requires reliable semantics across systems.","constraint":"Classification metadata must itself be protected against unauthorized modification.","edge_case":"Derived datasets may have sensitivity different from source data and require explicit reclassification.","security_implication":"Classification propagation reduces accidental disclosure across system boundaries.","performance_implication":"Metadata propagation and policy evaluation add processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Trace classified data through storage, messaging, caching, analytics, exports, and deletion paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify that downstream systems retain or correctly recompute the required classification.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["data classification","data lineage"],"prerequisites":["data-flow inventory"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B88-0001","D14-AP-B89-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B88-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Classification-aware data flow","publisher":"Derived from data classification and lifecycle principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B89-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Audit Architecture","concept":"Security Audit Trail","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does a security audit architecture solve?","answer":"A security audit architecture creates attributable records of security-relevant events so access, administrative actions, policy decisions, and significant changes can be investigated and verified.","explanation":"Audit architecture should define event scope, actor identity, target resource, action, result, timestamp semantics, correlation context, integrity protection, retention, and access controls. Logging everything indiscriminately is not equivalent to useful auditing.","principle":"Security-relevant decisions and actions should produce trustworthy evidence appropriate to their risk.","correct_example":"Record who performed a privileged operation, what resource was targeted, what authorization context applied, the result, and sufficient correlation information for investigation.","invalid_example":"Store an unstructured message such as 'admin action occurred' without actor, target, result, or correlation context.","problem":"Security events cannot be reliably reconstructed or attributed.","failure_mode":"Investigators cannot determine what happened or whether an action was authorized.","cause":"Incomplete, mutable, inaccessible, or unauditable event records.","solution":"Define an audit event schema and protect its integrity, access, retention, and lifecycle.","alternative":"Application-specific event logs without centralized audit governance.","tradeoff":"Detailed audit records improve traceability but increase storage, processing, privacy, and operational costs.","constraint":"Audit data itself may contain sensitive information and requires access control.","edge_case":"Clock differences can complicate event ordering across distributed components.","security_implication":"Audit records should resist unauthorized modification and deletion according to the required threat model.","performance_implication":"Synchronous audit writes can affect request latency; asynchronous approaches introduce durability and ordering tradeoffs.","testing_guidance":"Test audit generation, failure handling, identity attribution, ordering, retention, and unauthorized modification attempts.","verification_guidance":"Verify that defined privileged and security-relevant actions produce complete attributable records.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity","event storage"],"prerequisites":["security event model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B89-0002","D14-AP-B90-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Security audit architecture","publisher":"General security and audit architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B89-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Audit Architecture","concept":"Audit Integrity and Correlation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes distributed security audit records useful for investigation?","answer":"Audit records should preserve attributable identity, resource and action context, consistent event-time semantics, correlation identifiers, and appropriate integrity protections.","explanation":"Distributed systems produce events across multiple services and infrastructure layers. Correlation context allows related events to be reconstructed, while integrity protections help establish whether records were altered.","principle":"Audit evidence must preserve enough trustworthy context to reconstruct security-relevant actions across boundaries.","correct_example":"Propagate a correlation identifier and actor context through a request chain while each service records its local action and outcome.","invalid_example":"Each service emits isolated log messages with no correlation or actor information.","problem":"Related events cannot be reliably associated.","failure_mode":"Investigation produces ambiguous or incomplete timelines.","cause":"Missing correlation and attribution metadata.","solution":"Define standardized audit fields and propagate correlation context across trusted boundaries.","alternative":"Centralized event tracing with equivalent audit semantics.","tradeoff":"More metadata improves reconstruction but increases event size and privacy considerations.","constraint":"Correlation identifiers must not themselves become authorization credentials.","edge_case":"Asynchronous workflows can break simple request-scoped correlation and require explicit workflow identifiers.","security_implication":"Audit metadata must not leak secrets or sensitive credentials.","performance_implication":"Additional metadata and centralized collection consume network and storage resources.","testing_guidance":"Trace multi-service workflows and verify complete correlation across synchronous and asynchronous paths.","verification_guidance":"Reconstruct known test workflows from audit evidence and compare them with observed actions.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["security audit trail","distributed tracing or correlation mechanism"],"prerequisites":["audit event schema"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B89-0001","D14-AP-B86-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B89-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Audit integrity and distributed correlation","publisher":"Derived from distributed audit architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B90-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Integrated Security Architecture","concept":"Security Pattern Composition","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should security architecture patterns be composed into a coherent system?","answer":"Compose security patterns around explicit identity, resource, action, data, trust, policy, and audit boundaries rather than deploying independent security components without a shared authorization model.","explanation":"Zero trust, workload identity, policy enforcement, scoped credentials, secrets management, tenant isolation, data classification, and audit architecture reinforce different parts of the same security model. Their contracts must align.","principle":"Security controls should form a traceable control chain from identity to authorization to enforcement to evidence.","correct_example":"Authenticate a workload, issue or validate appropriately scoped credentials, enforce authorization at the protected resource, apply tenant/data policy, and record the security-relevant outcome.","invalid_example":"Deploy identity authentication, gateway authorization, tenant filtering, and audit logging independently with conflicting definitions of identity and resource ownership.","problem":"Security controls disagree or leave gaps between boundaries.","failure_mode":"A request passes one security layer because another layer interprets identity, tenant, or resource scope differently.","cause":"No shared security ontology and inconsistent policy semantics.","solution":"Define canonical identities, resources, actions, trust boundaries, policy semantics, enforcement points, and audit fields.","alternative":"Monolithic centralized security enforcement.","tradeoff":"Composed security architecture provides defense across multiple boundaries but increases integration complexity.","constraint":"Each security mechanism must have clear ownership and failure semantics.","edge_case":"Emergency operations may intentionally bypass ordinary paths and require stronger separate controls.","security_implication":"Composition should minimize privilege propagation and preserve traceability across boundaries.","performance_implication":"Multiple controls increase request-path work and require performance analysis under realistic workloads.","testing_guidance":"Test complete request paths including authentication, authorization, tenant boundaries, data classification, secrets, and audit generation.","verification_guidance":"Trace representative privileged and unprivileged requests through every security control and verify consistent decisions.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity","authorization","policy enforcement","secrets","tenant isolation","audit"],"prerequisites":["security ontology","threat model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B81-0001","D14-AP-B82-0002","D14-AP-B83-0001","D14-AP-B84-0001","D14-AP-B85-0001","D14-AP-B86-0001","D14-AP-B87-0001","D14-AP-B88-0001","D14-AP-B89-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Integrated security architecture pattern composition","publisher":"Derived from D14 security architecture relationships","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B90-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Integrated Security Architecture","concept":"Security Architecture Quality Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified before accepting a security architecture pattern set?","answer":"Verify identity authenticity, authorization correctness, enforcement coverage, privilege boundaries, secret lifecycle, tenant isolation, data classification handling, audit evidence, degraded-mode behavior, and security control interactions.","explanation":"Security architecture cannot be validated by confirming that individual security products or mechanisms exist. The system must demonstrate that controls protect the intended resources and remain effective across normal, degraded, and adversarial paths.","principle":"Security assurance requires verification of control effectiveness at the actual protected boundary.","correct_example":"Trace an unauthorized request from identity validation through policy enforcement and confirm that it is denied and appropriately audited.","invalid_example":"Declare the architecture secure because it uses encryption, authentication, and a gateway without testing bypass paths or authorization semantics.","problem":"Security controls exist but their combined effectiveness is unverified.","failure_mode":"An attacker or malfunctioning component bypasses one control through an alternate path.","cause":"Component-level validation without architecture-level threat and control analysis.","solution":"Use threat modeling, control mapping, negative testing, privilege-boundary tests, failure-injection, and audit verification.","alternative":"Independent security architecture review and formal analysis for high-assurance components.","tradeoff":"Comprehensive verification increases effort but improves evidence quality and reduces untested assumptions.","constraint":"Security claims must distinguish conceptual design from executed evidence.","edge_case":"Third-party or platform-managed controls may require external evidence rather than direct internal testing.","security_implication":"All critical security boundaries require explicit evidence of enforcement and failure behavior.","performance_implication":"Security controls should be evaluated for latency and resource impact without weakening required protection.","testing_guidance":"Include authentication, authorization, bypass, privilege escalation, tenant isolation, secret exposure, degraded dependency, and audit tests.","verification_guidance":"Use explicit evidence statuses such as NOT_EXECUTED, TESTED, VERIFIED, or CONFLICTED; never infer verification solely from design presence.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["security architecture pattern composition"],"prerequisites":["threat model","security requirements","verification strategy"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B90-0001","D14-AP-B70-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B90-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Security architecture quality gate","publisher":"Derived from D14 security verification principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}

