{"record_id":"D14-AP-B91-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Architecture Decision","concept":"Architecture Decision Record","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the purpose of an Architecture Decision Record?","answer":"An Architecture Decision Record captures a significant architectural decision, its context, considered alternatives, rationale, consequences, and status so future engineers can understand why the architecture has its current form.","explanation":"Architectural decisions often outlive the people who made them. Recording the decision prevents accidental reversal, repeated analysis, and misunderstanding of constraints. An ADR should document reasoning rather than merely record the chosen technology.","principle":"Important architectural decisions should preserve their decision context and consequences.","correct_example":"Record why a modular monolith was selected over microservices, including requirements, constraints, rejected alternatives, and consequences.","invalid_example":"Record only 'Use microservices' without documenting the problem, alternatives, constraints, or consequences.","problem":"Architectural intent is lost over time.","failure_mode":"Future changes repeat obsolete analysis or accidentally violate important constraints.","cause":"Architecture decisions exist only in informal conversations or undocumented implementation details.","solution":"Maintain versioned ADRs linked to relevant architecture components and requirements.","alternative":"Architecture documentation without explicit decision history.","tradeoff":"ADR maintenance adds documentation effort but improves architectural continuity.","constraint":"An ADR should represent the decision state at a particular point in time and may later be superseded.","edge_case":"A decision may be valid for one deployment context but inappropriate for another.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive architectural decisions should document threat assumptions and security consequences.","performance_implication":"Performance-related decisions should record workload assumptions and whether claims are measured or estimated.","testing_guidance":"Verify that implementation and architecture documentation remain consistent with accepted ADRs.","verification_guidance":"Check that each significant decision identifies evidence, assumptions, alternatives, and consequences.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture requirements","decision context"],"prerequisites":["architecture decision process"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B91-0002","D14-AP-B96-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Architecture Decision Records","publisher":"General architecture governance principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B91-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Architecture Decision","concept":"Decision Reversal Criteria","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When should an architecture decision be reconsidered?","answer":"An architecture decision should be reconsidered when its governing requirements, constraints, evidence, technology assumptions, threat model, workload, or operational conditions materially change.","explanation":"Architectural decisions are contextual rather than permanently optimal. Reconsideration should be evidence-driven rather than triggered solely by technology trends.","principle":"Architecture decisions remain valid only while their governing assumptions remain valid.","correct_example":"Reassess a persistence architecture after measured workload characteristics change significantly and the original scaling assumptions no longer hold.","invalid_example":"Replace an architecture solely because a newer technology has become popular.","problem":"Architecture either remains fixed after its assumptions become invalid or changes without evidence.","failure_mode":"Unnecessary migration or continued use of an unsuitable architecture.","cause":"No explicit decision-review criteria.","solution":"Record decision assumptions and define measurable conditions that justify reconsideration.","alternative":"Periodic architecture review independent of explicit triggers.","tradeoff":"Review processes consume engineering time but reduce uncontrolled architectural drift.","constraint":"Reconsideration should preserve existing evidence and migration costs as decision inputs.","edge_case":"Security or regulatory changes may require immediate reconsideration regardless of performance impact.","security_implication":"Threat-model changes can invalidate previously acceptable boundaries.","performance_implication":"Workload changes can invalidate capacity and latency assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Create regression checks for critical assumptions behind major decisions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that reconsideration is based on documented evidence rather than undocumented preference.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture decision record"],"prerequisites":["decision assumptions"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B91-0001","D14-AP-B94-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B91-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Architecture decision lifecycle","publisher":"Derived from architecture governance principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B92-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Legacy Modernization","concept":"Strangler Migration Architecture","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How does the Strangler migration pattern support legacy modernization?","answer":"The Strangler pattern incrementally replaces capabilities of a legacy system with new implementations while routing selected functionality to the new architecture and retaining the legacy system for unreplaced capabilities.","explanation":"Incremental replacement reduces the need for a single large migration. Routing, data synchronization, ownership boundaries, rollback strategy, and eventual legacy retirement must be explicitly designed.","principle":"Replace bounded capabilities incrementally while maintaining controlled coexistence until migration is verified.","correct_example":"Move one bounded business capability behind a controlled routing boundary, verify its behavior, then migrate the next capability.","invalid_example":"Build a parallel replacement without defining traffic ownership, data synchronization, rollback, or retirement criteria.","problem":"Legacy replacement has excessive migration risk and a large cutover boundary.","failure_mode":"The old and new systems diverge or neither system has clear ownership.","cause":"Migration is treated as a single technical rewrite instead of a sequence of controlled transitions.","solution":"Define capability boundaries, routing rules, data ownership, verification gates, and retirement milestones.","alternative":"Big-bang replacement.","tradeoff":"Incremental migration reduces cutover risk but creates temporary architectural complexity.","constraint":"Coexistence requires explicit ownership and consistency semantics.","edge_case":"Some legacy capabilities may be tightly coupled and unsuitable for immediate extraction.","security_implication":"Migration paths must preserve authentication, authorization, secrets, audit, and data-protection controls.","performance_implication":"Additional routing and synchronization can increase latency and infrastructure load.","testing_guidance":"Test old/new routing, parity, rollback, data reconciliation, and partial migration states.","verification_guidance":"Retire a legacy capability only after functional, security, data, and operational verification.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["routing boundary","legacy system","new implementation"],"prerequisites":["capability decomposition"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B92-0002","D14-AP-B95-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B30-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Strangler migration architecture","publisher":"General legacy modernization principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B92-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Legacy Modernization","concept":"Migration Ownership Boundary","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be established before migrating a legacy capability?","answer":"The migration must define authoritative ownership of behavior, data, interfaces, security policy, operational responsibility, and rollback authority for each transition stage.","explanation":"A capability can be technically migrated while remaining architecturally ambiguous if both systems can mutate the same state or independently implement the same business rules.","principle":"Every migrated capability should have explicit authority at each stage of coexistence.","correct_example":"Define the new service as authoritative for a migrated operation while the legacy system becomes read-only for that capability during the transition.","invalid_example":"Allow both systems to independently modify the same business state without reconciliation rules.","problem":"Dual ownership causes inconsistent state and behavior.","failure_mode":"Data divergence or conflicting business decisions occur during migration.","cause":"Migration lacks authoritative ownership semantics.","solution":"Define source-of-truth, write ownership, synchronization, and retirement conditions for each capability.","alternative":"Temporary dual-write with explicit reconciliation.","tradeoff":"Strict ownership simplifies correctness but can require additional migration coordination.","constraint":"Ownership changes must be observable and reversible where required.","edge_case":"Different capabilities may transition at different times within the same legacy application.","security_implication":"Authorization policy must follow ownership transitions and must not leave privileged legacy paths active unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"Dual-read or synchronization paths may increase latency and resource use.","testing_guidance":"Test ownership transitions, duplicate writes, stale reads, rollback, and reconciliation.","verification_guidance":"Verify exactly one authoritative write owner exists for each migrated state boundary.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["migration architecture","data ownership"],"prerequisites":["legacy capability inventory"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B92-0001","D14-AP-B51-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B92-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Migration ownership boundary","publisher":"Derived from legacy modernization and data ownership principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B93-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Architecture Conformance","concept":"Architecture Conformance Check","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is architecture conformance checking?","answer":"Architecture conformance checking determines whether an implementation still satisfies defined architectural constraints, boundaries, dependencies, and required structural properties.","explanation":"Conformance can be checked through static analysis, dependency rules, runtime observation, deployment validation, tests, or manual review. The mechanism should match the property being verified.","principle":"Architecture constraints should be expressed in forms that can be checked against implementation evidence.","correct_example":"Fail validation when a domain module directly imports infrastructure code prohibited by the architecture dependency rule.","invalid_example":"Document a dependency rule but never check whether implementation violates it.","problem":"Architecture gradually diverges from its intended structure.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized dependencies and boundary violations accumulate until the architecture becomes difficult to change.","cause":"Architecture exists as documentation without enforceable constraints.","solution":"Convert important architecture rules into automated or repeatable conformance checks where practical.","alternative":"Periodic manual architecture review.","tradeoff":"Automated checks provide repeatability but may require tooling and careful rule design.","constraint":"Conformance rules should distinguish intentional exceptions from violations.","edge_case":"Generated code or framework conventions may create dependencies that require explicitly documented exceptions.","security_implication":"Security boundaries should have enforceable conformance checks where feasible.","performance_implication":"Static and runtime conformance checks consume analysis resources but can reduce long-term architecture drift.","testing_guidance":"Include positive and negative architectural fixtures.","verification_guidance":"Record which architecture rules were checked, by what method, and with what result.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture rules","implementation graph"],"prerequisites":["defined architecture constraints"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B93-0002","D14-AP-B94-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B91-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Architecture conformance","publisher":"General architecture governance principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B93-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Architecture Conformance","concept":"Fitness Function","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is an architecture fitness function?","answer":"An architecture fitness function is an automated or repeatable evaluation that tests whether an architectural characteristic or constraint remains within an accepted boundary.","explanation":"Fitness functions can evaluate dependency direction, latency budgets, security properties, availability characteristics, resource limits, or other architecture-level properties. They should define the measured property and acceptance criteria explicitly.","principle":"Important architectural qualities should be converted into observable checks whenever practical.","correct_example":"Continuously verify that forbidden module dependencies remain absent from the dependency graph.","invalid_example":"State that the system must remain modular without defining any observable condition for modularity.","problem":"Architecture quality degrades without detection.","failure_mode":"Architectural erosion becomes visible only after substantial remediation cost.","cause":"Quality attributes are not connected to measurable or checkable conditions.","solution":"Define fitness functions tied to explicit architecture requirements and maintain them with the system.","alternative":"Periodic architecture audits.","tradeoff":"Fitness functions improve continuous feedback but can oversimplify qualities that require human judgment.","constraint":"Thresholds must reflect valid workload and system context.","edge_case":"Some architectural qualities cannot be reduced to a single metric and require composite evidence.","security_implication":"Security fitness functions can detect prohibited dependencies, insecure configurations, or boundary violations.","performance_implication":"Performance fitness functions should distinguish measured thresholds from estimates and control test-environment variability.","testing_guidance":"Run fitness functions in CI, integration environments, or scheduled architecture validation workflows as appropriate.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each fitness function has a defined property, method, threshold, evidence source, and failure interpretation.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture conformance"],"prerequisites":["measurable architecture requirement"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B93-0001","D14-AP-B97-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B93-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Architecture fitness functions","publisher":"General evolutionary architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B94-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Architecture Evaluation","concept":"Architecture Tradeoff Analysis","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should competing architecture patterns be compared?","answer":"Compare candidate architectures against explicit requirements, constraints, quality attributes, risks, dependencies, migration costs, operational implications, and evidence rather than selecting by pattern popularity.","explanation":"Architecture selection is context-dependent. A pattern may solve one constraint while introducing another. Comparison should preserve uncertainty when evidence is incomplete.","principle":"Architecture choice should be traceable to requirements and evidence.","correct_example":"Compare modular monolith and microservices using deployment independence, transaction boundaries, team topology, operational complexity, scaling needs, and security requirements.","invalid_example":"Choose microservices solely because they are considered modern.","problem":"Architecture selection becomes preference-driven.","failure_mode":"The selected architecture introduces unnecessary complexity or fails an important requirement.","cause":"No explicit decision criteria or evidence model.","solution":"Define weighted or prioritized requirements, but preserve the underlying facts and assumptions rather than hiding them in a single score.","alternative":"Architecture review using qualitative tradeoff analysis.","tradeoff":"Structured comparison improves traceability but cannot eliminate uncertainty from incomplete evidence.","constraint":"Do not treat estimated or assumed measurements as verified facts.","edge_case":"Requirements may conflict and require explicit stakeholder decisions.","security_implication":"Security requirements should be first-class architecture constraints rather than afterthoughts.","performance_implication":"Performance comparisons require workload-specific measurements or clearly labeled estimates.","testing_guidance":"Prototype or test high-risk assumptions before irreversible architecture decisions when feasible.","verification_guidance":"Trace every major selection claim to evidence, assumption, requirement, or explicit uncertainty.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["requirements","quality attributes","architecture alternatives"],"prerequisites":["architecture decision context"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B94-0002","D14-AP-B91-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B91-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Architecture tradeoff analysis","publisher":"General architecture evaluation principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B94-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Architecture Evaluation","concept":"Reversible Architecture Decision","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should reversibility influence architecture decisions?","answer":"When two alternatives satisfy requirements similarly, an architecture that preserves a practical path to change can reduce the cost of future uncertainty.","explanation":"Reversibility is contextual. Some decisions are cheap to change while others create deep data, organizational, or operational commitments. The goal is not to maximize reversibility at any cost but to recognize irreversible consequences.","principle":"Architectural decisions should account for the cost and feasibility of future change.","correct_example":"Use a stable interface boundary that allows an implementation to change without requiring every consumer to migrate simultaneously.","invalid_example":"Couple all consumers directly to implementation-specific details that make future replacement difficult.","problem":"Early architectural choices create unnecessary irreversible coupling.","failure_mode":"A later requirement change requires broad coordinated migration.","cause":"Decision analysis ignored switching and migration costs.","solution":"Identify irreversible commitments and isolate them behind stable boundaries where justified.","alternative":"Commit early when evidence strongly supports one architecture and reversal cost is acceptable.","tradeoff":"Designing for reversibility can add abstraction and short-term complexity.","constraint":"Reversibility itself must not override clear requirements or create unnecessary indirection.","edge_case":"Data migrations can make some architecture decisions substantially less reversible than interface changes.","security_implication":"Security boundary changes may require coordinated migration and should be considered high-cost decisions.","performance_implication":"Abstraction layers introduced for reversibility can add latency or complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test compatibility boundaries that enable future implementation replacement.","verification_guidance":"Document which parts of the decision are reversible, difficult to reverse, or effectively irreversible.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture decision analysis"],"prerequisites":["change-cost analysis"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B94-0001","D14-AP-B96-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B94-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Reversible architecture decisions","publisher":"General architecture decision principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B95-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Migration Strategy","concept":"Incremental Migration with Verification Gates","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should a high-risk architecture migration be controlled?","answer":"Divide the migration into bounded transitions with explicit entry conditions, verification evidence, rollback conditions, ownership boundaries, and exit criteria.","explanation":"Migration risk is reduced when each transition has a known scope and can be evaluated before additional changes are introduced. The gates should address functionality, data, security, performance, and operations as applicable.","principle":"Do not advance a migration stage until the evidence required for that stage has been obtained.","correct_example":"Migrate one capability, verify behavior and data reconciliation, observe production operation under controlled conditions, then proceed to the next capability.","invalid_example":"Migrate all capabilities first and defer verification until the end.","problem":"Failure scope becomes too large to isolate.","failure_mode":"A migration defect affects multiple capabilities and rollback becomes difficult.","cause":"No incremental verification boundaries.","solution":"Use staged migration with explicit verification and rollback criteria.","alternative":"Single coordinated cutover with extensive pre-production validation.","tradeoff":"Incremental migration takes longer and requires coexistence but reduces the scope of individual failures.","constraint":"Each stage must preserve enough compatibility to support rollback or controlled recovery.","edge_case":"Some irreversible data transformations require additional backup and recovery evidence before execution.","security_implication":"Each stage must preserve security controls and prevent temporary migration paths from weakening authorization.","performance_implication":"Migration infrastructure may temporarily increase load and should have resource budgets.","testing_guidance":"Test each stage independently and execute rollback/failure-injection scenarios before advancing.","verification_guidance":"Record stage-specific evidence and do not infer final migration success from partial completion.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["migration plan","verification gates"],"prerequisites":["migration decomposition"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B92-0001","D14-AP-B95-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B92-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Incremental architecture migration","publisher":"Derived from migration and verification principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B95-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Migration Strategy","concept":"Migration Rollback Boundary","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a migration rollback boundary?","answer":"A migration rollback boundary identifies the point and mechanism at which a migration stage can return to a previously verified state without violating data, security, or consistency requirements.","explanation":"Rollback is not always equivalent to deploying old code. Once data ownership or schema changes occur, rollback may require forward recovery or compensating migration rather than simple reversal.","principle":"Migration recovery strategy must be defined according to the state changes introduced by the migration.","correct_example":"Define whether rollback means routing traffic back, restoring a compatible data representation, or executing a compensating migration.","invalid_example":"Assume every migration can be rolled back by redeploying the previous application version.","problem":"State changes make application rollback unsafe or incomplete.","failure_mode":"The old implementation cannot correctly interpret the new state.","cause":"Rollback analysis considered code but not data and external side effects.","solution":"Define rollback or recovery semantics for code, data, external effects, and ownership.","alternative":"Forward-only recovery with verified compensating actions.","tradeoff":"Strong rollback capability can require compatibility layers and additional storage or testing.","constraint":"Irreversible operations require a different recovery strategy.","edge_case":"External side effects may not be technically reversible and require compensation.","security_implication":"Recovery paths must preserve authorization and audit semantics.","performance_implication":"Rollback infrastructure may require temporary duplicate capacity.","testing_guidance":"Execute migration failure scenarios and verify actual recovery behavior rather than assuming rollback works.","verification_guidance":"Mark rollback as verified only after the relevant recovery path has been tested.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["migration stage","state ownership","recovery strategy"],"prerequisites":["migration risk analysis"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B95-0001","D14-AP-B96-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B95-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Migration rollback boundaries","publisher":"Derived from migration failure and recovery principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B96-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Architecture Risk","concept":"Architecture Risk Register","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What should an architecture risk register capture?","answer":"An architecture risk register captures significant technical and operational uncertainties, their causes, affected boundaries, potential consequences, evidence, mitigation, owners, and review status.","explanation":"Architecture risk includes unknown workload behavior, technology maturity, migration difficulty, security assumptions, availability dependencies, organizational constraints, and irreversible commitments.","principle":"Uncertainty should be represented explicitly rather than hidden inside architecture decisions.","correct_example":"Record an unverified assumption that a dependency can sustain required load, define a validation experiment, and track the resulting evidence.","invalid_example":"Treat an untested capacity assumption as a verified architecture property.","problem":"Unknowns are mistaken for facts.","failure_mode":"Architecture fails when an unvalidated assumption proves false.","cause":"No explicit representation of architectural uncertainty.","solution":"Track assumptions, evidence status, risk impact, mitigation, and verification state.","alternative":"Architecture review documents without formal risk tracking.","tradeoff":"Risk tracking adds governance overhead but makes uncertainty visible and actionable.","constraint":"Risk status must distinguish verified evidence from estimates and assumptions.","edge_case":"A risk may remain unresolved because no practical validation method exists yet.","security_implication":"Security assumptions should be tracked as risks until verified.","performance_implication":"Performance assumptions should identify workload, environment, measurement method, and confidence.","testing_guidance":"Convert high-impact risks into targeted experiments or failure tests where practical.","verification_guidance":"Update risk status only when evidence supports the change.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture decisions","verification evidence"],"prerequisites":["risk taxonomy"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B96-0002","D14-AP-B97-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B91-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Architecture risk management","publisher":"General architecture governance principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B96-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Architecture Risk","concept":"Irreversible Decision Risk","knowledge_type":"RISK_CASE","question":"Why should irreversible architecture decisions receive additional analysis?","answer":"Irreversible decisions can impose large future migration costs, constrain later options, or create difficult-to-remove security, data, operational, or organizational dependencies.","explanation":"Examples include persistent data models, external contracts, identity structures, organization-wide protocols, and infrastructure commitments. The required analysis should be proportional to the cost of reversal.","principle":"The greater the cost of reversal, the stronger the evidence required before commitment.","correct_example":"Validate a long-lived external data contract before making it the canonical interface for many independent consumers.","invalid_example":"Make a permanent data contract decision based solely on a temporary prototype requirement.","problem":"Temporary assumptions become durable architectural constraints.","failure_mode":"Future changes require broad migration or compatibility layers.","cause":"Irreversibility was not considered during decision-making.","solution":"Identify irreversible commitments and obtain stronger evidence before adoption.","alternative":"Use a temporary compatibility boundary while requirements stabilize.","tradeoff":"Additional validation delays commitment but can reduce long-term migration cost.","constraint":"Evidence requirements should be proportional to decision impact and reversibility.","edge_case":"Business deadlines may require commitment before complete evidence exists; uncertainty should then remain explicitly recorded.","security_implication":"Identity and authorization contracts can be particularly expensive to change after widespread adoption.","performance_implication":"Persistent storage and communication contracts can constrain future optimization.","testing_guidance":"Prototype high-risk assumptions and test compatibility before broad adoption.","verification_guidance":"Verify that decision evidence is sufficient for the documented level of irreversibility.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture risk register"],"prerequisites":["decision reversibility analysis"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B94-0002","D14-AP-B96-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B96-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Irreversible architecture decision risk","publisher":"Derived from architecture risk and decision principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B97-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Threat Modeling","concept":"Architecture Threat Model","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should an architecture-level threat model examine?","answer":"An architecture threat model should identify protected assets, trust boundaries, actors, entry points, data flows, privileged operations, dependencies, failure modes, and plausible abuse paths.","explanation":"Threat modeling at architecture level focuses on structural security properties rather than only implementation vulnerabilities. It should be updated when architecture boundaries or significant assumptions change.","principle":"Security analysis should begin with the architecture's trust and data-flow structure.","correct_example":"Map identities, services, data stores, trust boundaries, administrative operations, and external interfaces before evaluating threats.","invalid_example":"Review only source-code input validation while ignoring trust boundaries and privileged architecture paths.","problem":"Structural security risks remain hidden despite local controls.","failure_mode":"An attacker exploits an architectural trust relationship that individual components assumed was safe.","cause":"Threat analysis was limited to component-level vulnerabilities.","solution":"Model assets, flows, boundaries, privileges, and abuse paths at architecture level.","alternative":"Component-specific security review.","tradeoff":"Architecture threat modeling requires analysis effort but can expose systemic weaknesses earlier.","constraint":"Threat models depend on accurate architecture and data-flow information.","edge_case":"Dynamic or generated infrastructure can make architecture inventory incomplete.","security_implication":"Threat models should explicitly cover identity compromise, privilege escalation, lateral movement, data exposure, and dependency compromise as applicable.","performance_implication":"Security controls introduced from threat analysis may affect latency and resource usage.","testing_guidance":"Turn high-priority threat scenarios into security tests or controlled simulations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that identified high-impact threats have explicit controls, accepted residual risk, or unresolved status.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture graph","data-flow model","trust boundaries"],"prerequisites":["asset inventory"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B97-0002","D14-AP-B90-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B81-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Architecture threat modeling","publisher":"General threat-modeling principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B97-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Performance Modeling","concept":"Architecture Performance Model","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What should an architecture performance model represent?","answer":"An architecture performance model represents relevant workload characteristics, resource constraints, latency paths, throughput requirements, bottlenecks, dependencies, and scaling behavior needed to evaluate performance assumptions.","explanation":"Architecture performance reasoning should be workload-specific. A diagram alone does not establish performance. Measurements, simulations, or analytical models should be identified when quantitative claims are required.","principle":"Performance claims require an explicit workload and evidence model.","correct_example":"Model request rate, payload characteristics, dependency latency, concurrency, resource limits, and target latency before assessing whether an architecture can meet a stated workload.","invalid_example":"Assume an architecture will scale because it uses asynchronous messaging without analyzing workload and bottlenecks.","problem":"Performance assumptions are disconnected from workload reality.","failure_mode":"A supposedly scalable architecture fails at an unmodeled bottleneck.","cause":"No explicit workload or dependency model.","solution":"Define workload assumptions and validate critical performance paths through measurement or appropriate modeling.","alternative":"Prototype benchmarking under representative workloads.","tradeoff":"Detailed modeling costs time but reduces uncertainty for high-impact decisions.","constraint":"Results from one environment should not automatically be treated as universal performance facts.","edge_case":"Performance may be dominated by external dependencies outside architectural control.","security_implication":"Performance optimization must not silently weaken required security controls.","performance_implication":"The model should distinguish measured, estimated, inferred, and unknown characteristics.","testing_guidance":"Use representative load, concurrency, payload, dependency, and failure conditions.","verification_guidance":"Record measurement methodology, environment, workload, observed result, and limitations.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["workload model","resource model"],"prerequisites":["performance requirements"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B97-0001","D14-AP-B98-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B94-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Architecture performance modeling","publisher":"General performance engineering principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B98-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Failure Analysis","concept":"Architecture Failure Mode Analysis","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What should architecture-level failure analysis examine?","answer":"Architecture failure analysis examines how component, dependency, network, data, identity, deployment, capacity, and control-plane failures propagate through the system.","explanation":"The goal is to understand not only individual failures but their consequences, containment boundaries, recovery paths, and degraded operating modes.","principle":"Architectural resilience depends on controlling failure propagation, not merely preventing individual failures.","correct_example":"Analyze what happens when an identity provider, database, queue, service, or network path becomes unavailable and identify affected capabilities and recovery behavior.","invalid_example":"Assume the architecture is resilient because every individual component has a health check.","problem":"Failure propagation is not understood.","failure_mode":"A localized dependency failure becomes a broad service outage or data-integrity incident.","cause":"Architecture was analyzed primarily under normal operation.","solution":"Model dependency failures, partial failures, cascading effects, degraded modes, recovery, and data consistency consequences.","alternative":"Component-specific resilience testing.","tradeoff":"Failure analysis increases design and test effort but exposes systemic risks.","constraint":"Not every failure can be prevented; containment and recovery must also be considered.","edge_case":"Simultaneous correlated failures can invalidate assumptions based on independent failure models.","security_implication":"Security-control failures require explicit analysis because fail-open behavior can create authorization bypasses.","performance_implication":"Retries and recovery can amplify load and create cascading resource exhaustion.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled failure injection for critical dependencies and verify containment and recovery.","verification_guidance":"Record expected failure behavior and compare it with observed evidence when tested.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["dependency graph","failure model"],"prerequisites":["architecture inventory"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B98-0002","D14-AP-B83-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B93-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Architecture failure analysis","publisher":"General resilience engineering principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B98-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Failure Analysis","concept":"Cascading Failure Containment","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can architecture reduce cascading failures?","answer":"Architecture can reduce cascading failures by limiting dependency coupling, controlling retries, applying timeouts, isolating resource pools, applying backpressure, and providing graceful degradation where appropriate.","explanation":"Resilience patterns must work together. For example, uncontrolled retries can amplify a dependency outage, while bulkheads and timeouts can limit resource exhaustion.","principle":"A failure should remain within a bounded impact domain whenever practical.","correct_example":"Apply bounded timeouts, limited retries, and isolated resource pools to a dependency whose failure should not exhaust the entire service.","invalid_example":"Retry indefinitely whenever a downstream service fails.","problem":"One dependency failure consumes shared resources across unrelated operations.","failure_mode":"Latency and resource exhaustion propagate through the dependency graph.","cause":"Unbounded coupling or recovery amplification.","solution":"Combine timeout, retry, bulkhead, backpressure, circuit-breaking, and degradation semantics according to workload requirements.","alternative":"Synchronous fail-fast architecture for low-tolerance dependencies.","tradeoff":"Isolation improves resilience but can reduce resource utilization or temporarily reduce functionality.","constraint":"Recovery mechanisms require explicit budgets and termination conditions.","edge_case":"A degraded response may itself violate correctness requirements for some operations.","security_implication":"Failure handling must not bypass authorization or expose sensitive fallback data.","performance_implication":"Retries and recovery can multiply load; retry budgets must be bounded.","testing_guidance":"Inject dependency failures and measure propagation, resource usage, recovery, and user-visible behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that defined failure boundaries contain the tested failure within the expected scope.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["timeouts","retry policy","bulkheads","backpressure"],"prerequisites":["failure model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B98-0001","D14-AP-B61-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B98-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Cascading failure containment","publisher":"General resilience architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B99-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Architecture Synthesis","concept":"Architecture Pattern Selection Matrix","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an architecture pattern be selected from multiple viable alternatives?","answer":"Select a pattern by tracing explicit requirements and constraints to pattern capabilities, consequences, operational costs, risks, evidence, and compatibility with existing boundaries.","explanation":"The selection process should expose why a pattern fits the problem and where it does not. A matrix can organize evidence without collapsing all architectural reasoning into an opaque score.","principle":"Pattern selection should be explainable and requirement-driven.","correct_example":"Map requirements such as independent deployment, strong transactions, workload isolation, and operational capacity against candidate architecture patterns and record unresolved assumptions.","invalid_example":"Select a pattern because it is widely used without analyzing whether its assumptions match the system.","problem":"Architecture patterns are applied outside their intended context.","failure_mode":"The resulting architecture inherits unnecessary complexity or violates critical constraints.","cause":"Pattern selection based on familiarity or trend rather than contextual analysis.","solution":"Compare problem fit, boundaries, quality attributes, operational requirements, migration cost, and evidence.","alternative":"Architecture prototyping before final commitment.","tradeoff":"Structured selection requires analysis effort but reduces unexplained architectural choices.","constraint":"Selection criteria must be traceable to actual requirements.","edge_case":"Multiple patterns may need composition, in which case interaction effects must also be analyzed.","security_implication":"Security boundaries and threat assumptions must be part of pattern selection.","performance_implication":"Performance claims require workload-specific evidence rather than generic pattern reputation.","testing_guidance":"Prototype or test the highest-risk assumptions behind the selected pattern.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the final pattern choice is traceable to requirements and documented evidence.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture alternatives","requirements"],"prerequisites":["quality attributes"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B99-0002","D14-AP-B94-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B94-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Architecture pattern selection","publisher":"Derived from D14 architecture decision principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B99-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Selection and Modernization","subtopic":"Architecture Synthesis","concept":"Pattern Interaction Risk","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can individually valid architecture patterns fail when combined?","answer":"Architecture patterns can interact in ways that create conflicting ownership, consistency, latency, failure, security, or operational semantics even when each pattern is individually valid.","explanation":"For example, asynchronous communication combined with strict synchronous consistency requirements may introduce architectural tension. Likewise, caching combined with strong authorization requirements requires explicit invalidation and identity semantics.","principle":"Pattern composition must be evaluated as a system rather than assuming independent pattern correctness.","correct_example":"Analyze how cache-aside, event-driven updates, tenant isolation, and authorization interact before using them together.","invalid_example":"Assume that combining several established patterns automatically produces a robust architecture.","problem":"Interactions between patterns introduce unexamined emergent behavior.","failure_mode":"The architecture satisfies local pattern assumptions but violates global consistency, security, or reliability requirements.","cause":"Pattern selection was performed independently for each subsystem.","solution":"Model shared state, ownership, timing, failure, security, and operational interactions between patterns.","alternative":"Use fewer patterns with clearer semantics where composition complexity is unjustified.","tradeoff":"Pattern composition can provide powerful capabilities but increases architectural reasoning burden.","constraint":"Every cross-pattern dependency should have explicit semantics.","edge_case":"A pattern may require assumptions that conflict with another pattern's operating model.","security_implication":"Security controls can be bypassed when pattern boundaries create alternate access paths.","performance_implication":"Layered patterns can multiply latency, storage, network traffic, or retry behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test integrated workflows rather than only pattern-specific components.","verification_guidance":"Verify cross-pattern invariants and failure behavior explicitly.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture pattern selection","pattern composition"],"prerequisites":["candidate pattern set"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B99-0001","D14-AP-B90-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B99-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Architecture pattern interaction risk","publisher":"Derived from architecture pattern composition principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B100-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Final Synthesis","concept":"Architecture Pattern End-to-End Decision Framework","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What end-to-end process should an architecture reasoning system use when selecting or evolving an architecture pattern?","answer":"Identify requirements and constraints, model quality attributes, establish boundaries, enumerate viable patterns, compare consequences, identify risks and irreversible commitments, model security and failure behavior, validate critical assumptions, define conformance rules, record the decision, and continuously monitor architecture fitness.","explanation":"Architecture reasoning is an evidence-driven lifecycle rather than a one-time pattern lookup. The selected architecture should remain traceable to requirements and revisable when governing assumptions change.","principle":"Architecture should be treated as an evolving set of explicit decisions, boundaries, constraints, evidence, and verification conditions.","correct_example":"For a new distributed capability, define requirements, compare modular monolith and service-based alternatives, model failure and security boundaries, test high-risk assumptions, document the decision, and establish fitness checks.","invalid_example":"Choose an architecture pattern from a catalog and consider the decision complete without validating its assumptions or consequences.","problem":"Architecture selection becomes disconnected from requirements and operational evidence.","failure_mode":"The system accumulates architectural complexity, unverified assumptions, and boundary violations.","cause":"Architecture is treated as a static diagram rather than an evolving decision system.","solution":"Use requirements, decision records, risk registers, conformance checks, fitness functions, migration gates, and continuous evidence collection.","alternative":"Periodic architecture review without continuous checks.","tradeoff":"Continuous architecture governance requires ongoing effort but improves traceability and early detection of architectural drift.","constraint":"The framework must preserve uncertainty and must not represent unexecuted validation as verified evidence.","edge_case":"Legacy systems may lack sufficient documentation and require discovery before formal architecture reasoning.","security_implication":"Security architecture must be evaluated across identity, authorization, data, trust, failure, and audit boundaries.","performance_implication":"Performance assumptions must remain tied to explicit workloads and measured or clearly qualified evidence.","testing_guidance":"Maintain architecture-level unit-like rules, integration checks, migration tests, failure-injection tests, security tests, and performance validation as applicable.","verification_guidance":"Every major architecture claim should have an evidence status and verification method.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["architecture requirements","decision records","risk register","conformance","fitness functions"],"prerequisites":["architecture reasoning model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B100-0002","D14-AP-B91-0001","D14-AP-B94-0001","D14-AP-B97-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B99-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"D14 architecture pattern end-to-end framework","publisher":"Derived from D14 architecture pattern knowledge","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B100-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Final Synthesis","concept":"Architecture Pattern Final Quality Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be true before an architecture-pattern knowledge set is considered complete?","answer":"The knowledge set should cover architecture fundamentals, structural patterns, distributed patterns, data patterns, resilience patterns, deployment patterns, security patterns, modernization, decision governance, failure analysis, testing, verification, tradeoffs, version considerations, provenance, contradictions, and retrieval relationships without unresolved structural duplication.","explanation":"Completeness is multidimensional. A dataset can contain many records while still missing important failure modes, invalid examples, verification semantics, version context, or relationships. The final gate therefore evaluates both content coverage and knowledge quality.","principle":"Dataset completeness means coverage plus correctness, traceability, relationships, verification status, and controlled uncertainty.","correct_example":"Complete the dataset only after structural validation, semantic deduplication, relationship checks, contradiction analysis, provenance review, retrieval testing, and coverage assessment.","invalid_example":"Declare completion solely because all planned batch numbers contain records.","problem":"Record count is mistaken for knowledge completeness.","failure_mode":"The dataset contains gaps, duplicates, contradictions, or unverifiable claims despite appearing complete.","cause":"No multidimensional final quality gate.","solution":"Run structural, semantic, version, provenance, retrieval, security, performance, testing, and verification audits before locking.","alternative":"Manual editorial review alone.","tradeoff":"A comprehensive final audit requires additional processing but provides stronger evidence for dataset readiness.","constraint":"Unverified or unknown knowledge must remain explicitly marked rather than silently promoted to verified status.","edge_case":"Some topics may legitimately remain UNKNOWN or NOT_APPLICABLE because authoritative evidence is unavailable or the concept does not apply.","security_implication":"Security-related records require defensive framing, boundary analysis, and verification semantics.","performance_implication":"Performance claims require evidence classification and must not be presented as measured when they are conceptual.","testing_guidance":"Run retrieval tests for exact, conceptual, debugging, code-generation, comparison, security, performance, and verification queries.","verification_guidance":"Lock only after all quality gates have documented outcomes and unresolved limitations are explicitly recorded.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["all D14 architecture pattern records"],"prerequisites":["D14 B01-B99 completion"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B100-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B100-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"D14 final architecture pattern quality gate","publisher":"Derived from D14 dataset quality requirements","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

