{"record_id":"D14-AP-B81-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Zero Trust","concept":"Zero Trust Architecture","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What architectural problem does Zero Trust address?","answer":"Zero Trust architecture treats access decisions as explicit policy decisions rather than assuming that network location or prior access automatically establishes trust.","explanation":"A zero-trust design evaluates identity, device or workload context, requested resource, action, policy, and relevant risk signals according to the system's requirements. The exact signals and enforcement mechanisms vary by environment.","principle":"Access should be explicitly authorized for the requested resource and action rather than granted solely because a requester is inside a trusted network boundary.","correct_example":"Require authenticated workload identity and resource-specific authorization before allowing a service to access another service's protected operation.","invalid_example":"Allow unrestricted access to all internal services because traffic originates from an internal network.","problem":"Network location is treated as a sufficient security boundary.","failure_mode":"Compromise of one internal component enables unauthorized lateral access.","cause":"Implicit trust based on network placement or prior authentication.","solution":"Apply explicit identity, policy, authorization, and enforcement boundaries to protected resources.","alternative":"Traditional network-perimeter security with internal segmentation.","tradeoff":"Zero-trust controls can improve access granularity but increase identity, policy, telemetry, and enforcement complexity.","constraint":"Reliable identity and policy-enforcement mechanisms are required.","edge_case":"Emergency or degraded operation may require explicitly governed fallback access.","security_implication":"The architecture should minimize implicit trust and constrain lateral movement.","performance_implication":"Additional identity and policy checks add processing and network overhead that should be measured.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized lateral access, expired credentials, policy changes, and degraded identity-provider conditions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that access decisions depend on defined authorization conditions rather than network location alone.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity","authorization","policy enforcement"],"prerequisites":["resource classification","identity model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B81-0002","D14-AP-B82-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D14-AP-B81-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Zero Trust architecture","publisher":"General security architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B81-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Zero Trust","concept":"Explicit Trust Boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should define a trust boundary in a zero-trust architecture?","answer":"A trust boundary should correspond to an explicit authorization and resource-protection boundary rather than simply a network segment.","explanation":"Network segmentation can support enforcement but does not by itself define authorization. A service, data store, tenant, privileged operation, or administrative interface may each require separate authorization semantics.","principle":"Trust boundaries should be derived from protected resources and allowed actions.","correct_example":"Treat an administrative operation as a distinct authorization boundary even when it is exposed through the same network path as ordinary operations.","invalid_example":"Assume every service inside one private network belongs to one unrestricted trust domain.","problem":"Different sensitivity levels share implicit authorization.","failure_mode":"A compromised low-privilege component gains access to higher-value resources.","cause":"Coarse trust boundaries.","solution":"Define resource-level and action-level authorization boundaries.","alternative":"Network-only segmentation.","tradeoff":"Fine-grained boundaries improve containment but increase policy complexity.","constraint":"Every protected boundary requires an enforceable policy decision point or equivalent mechanism.","edge_case":"A single request may cross multiple trust boundaries.","security_implication":"Explicit boundaries reduce lateral-movement opportunities.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained authorization can increase request-path overhead.","testing_guidance":"Attempt access across every defined boundary using identities with different privileges.","verification_guidance":"Verify that crossing a boundary requires the expected authorization decision.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["authorization policy"],"prerequisites":["zero-trust model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B81-0001","D14-AP-B83-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B81-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Explicit trust boundaries","publisher":"General zero-trust architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B82-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Identity-Centric Architecture","concept":"Identity Perimeter","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is an identity perimeter?","answer":"An identity perimeter makes authenticated and attributable identities a primary architectural boundary for controlling access to resources and operations.","explanation":"Instead of relying primarily on network location, the architecture associates requests with identities such as users, services, workloads, devices, or tenants. Authorization then evaluates the identity against resource and action policy.","principle":"Access control should be tied to an identifiable principal and its authorized scope.","correct_example":"Authorize a service request using a verifiable workload identity and a policy describing the permitted resource and operation.","invalid_example":"Permit access based only on source IP address when identity-level authorization is required.","problem":"Network identifiers do not adequately express who or what is requesting an operation.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized principals obtain access through shared network paths or compromised hosts.","cause":"Identity is not part of the authorization decision.","solution":"Establish verifiable identities and map them to explicit permissions.","alternative":"Network-based access control.","tradeoff":"Identity-centric controls provide finer granularity but require identity lifecycle and credential management.","constraint":"Identity must be reliably authenticated and appropriately scoped.","edge_case":"Identity-provider unavailability can affect access decisions.","security_implication":"Compromised identity credentials can become a direct authorization path, requiring credential protection and revocation mechanisms.","performance_implication":"Identity validation and policy lookup introduce request overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test identity issuance, expiration, revocation, impersonation resistance, and authorization mapping.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unauthorized identities cannot obtain permissions through network placement alone.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity provider","authorization system"],"prerequisites":["principal model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B82-0002","D14-AP-B81-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B81-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Identity-centric security architecture","publisher":"General security architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B82-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Identity-Centric Architecture","concept":"Workload Identity","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should service-to-service access use workload identity?","answer":"Workload identity provides a distinct, attributable identity for an application or service instance so authorization can be based on the workload rather than only on network location or shared credentials.","explanation":"Workload identities can support service-level authorization, credential rotation, audit attribution, and reduced dependence on shared static secrets. Implementation details depend on the platform and identity system.","principle":"Each security-relevant workload should have an identity appropriate to its authorization scope.","correct_example":"Authorize Service A to invoke a specific operation on Service B using a verifiable workload identity.","invalid_example":"Give every internal service the same shared credential with broad permissions.","problem":"Shared credentials prevent precise attribution and increase blast radius.","failure_mode":"Compromise of one service exposes access intended for many services.","cause":"Shared identity or overbroad service credentials.","solution":"Use distinct workload identities with least-privilege permissions and lifecycle management.","alternative":"Shared service account with tightly restricted scope.","tradeoff":"Distinct identities improve isolation and attribution but increase identity lifecycle complexity.","constraint":"Identity issuance and rotation must be operationally reliable.","edge_case":"Ephemeral workloads require automated identity provisioning and revocation.","security_implication":"Workload identity reduces shared-secret exposure when implemented correctly.","performance_implication":"Identity verification adds overhead but can be cached or optimized according to security requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test identity issuance, rotation, revocation, impersonation, and cross-service authorization.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each workload receives only the permissions associated with its declared identity.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity infrastructure"],"prerequisites":["service inventory","authorization model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B82-0001","D14-AP-B84-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B82-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Workload identity","publisher":"General cloud-native security architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B83-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Policy Enforcement Point","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a Policy Enforcement Point?","answer":"A Policy Enforcement Point is an architectural component that intercepts or controls access to a protected resource and enforces the result of an authorization policy decision.","explanation":"The enforcement point may be located at an API gateway, service boundary, data access layer, operating-system boundary, or another resource-specific control point. A policy decision mechanism and enforcement mechanism should have clearly defined responsibilities.","principle":"Authorization policy is effective only when its decision is reliably enforced at the protected resource boundary.","correct_example":"Require an authorization decision before a request reaches a protected administrative operation.","invalid_example":"Evaluate authorization in a user interface while allowing the backend resource to be accessed directly without enforcement.","problem":"Policy exists conceptually but can be bypassed through alternate access paths.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized requests reach the protected resource.","cause":"Missing or incorrectly placed enforcement point.","solution":"Place enforcement at every relevant resource boundary and protect alternate paths.","alternative":"Embedded authorization inside each resource implementation.","tradeoff":"Centralized enforcement improves consistency while distributed enforcement can provide resource-specific control.","constraint":"Every bypass path must be considered.","edge_case":"Internal service calls may bypass an external gateway unless equivalent enforcement exists internally.","security_implication":"A PEP is a security-critical component and should fail according to explicit policy when its decision service is unavailable.","performance_implication":"Enforcement adds request processing and potentially policy-lookup latency.","testing_guidance":"Test direct, indirect, malformed, and alternate-path access attempts.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every protected resource has an enforceable authorization boundary.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["policy decision mechanism"],"prerequisites":["resource authorization policy"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B83-0002","D14-AP-B81-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B81-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Policy Enforcement Point","publisher":"General access-control architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B83-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Fail-Safe Authorization Boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should a policy enforcement point behave when authorization cannot be determined?","answer":"The behavior should be explicitly defined according to the sensitivity of the protected operation; for security-sensitive access, the architecture commonly requires denying access when required authorization evidence is unavailable.","explanation":"The correct degraded behavior depends on resource criticality and business requirements. The important architectural property is that authorization failure is not silently converted into unrestricted access.","principle":"Loss of authorization evidence must not accidentally create broader authority.","correct_example":"Reject a privileged operation when the required authorization service or credential validation result is unavailable.","invalid_example":"Allow unrestricted administrative access whenever the policy service is unreachable.","problem":"Dependency failure creates an unintended authorization bypass.","failure_mode":"Protected operations become available during control-plane failure.","cause":"Undefined fail-open behavior.","solution":"Define explicit fail-open or fail-closed semantics per resource and risk class, with controlled emergency procedures where necessary.","alternative":"Cached authorization decisions with bounded validity.","tradeoff":"Fail-closed behavior protects security but can reduce availability during identity or policy-service outages.","constraint":"Emergency access must be separately controlled, auditable, and time-bounded.","edge_case":"Read-only low-risk operations may have different degraded-mode requirements than destructive administrative actions.","security_implication":"Authorization degradation must never silently expand privileges.","performance_implication":"Synchronous policy validation can affect latency and availability.","testing_guidance":"Simulate policy-service failure, stale decisions, expired credentials, and network partitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each protected operation follows its documented degraded authorization behavior.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["policy enforcement point","authorization service"],"prerequisites":["resource risk classification"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B83-0001","D14-AP-B89-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B83-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Authorization failure semantics","publisher":"General secure-access architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B84-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Token Exchange","concept":"Token Exchange","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does token exchange solve in distributed authorization?","answer":"Token exchange allows a caller to obtain a credential representing a narrower or different authorized context for a downstream resource instead of forwarding an original credential indiscriminately.","explanation":"A token exchange mechanism can reduce privilege propagation by issuing a token scoped to the intended audience, resource, action, or delegation context. Exact semantics depend on the identity protocol and authorization architecture.","principle":"Credentials should be scoped to the resource and authority required for the next operation.","correct_example":"Exchange an incoming user or service credential for a downstream token intended only for Service B.","invalid_example":"Forward a broad administrative credential through every downstream service regardless of its required permissions.","problem":"Broad credentials propagate unnecessarily across service boundaries.","failure_mode":"Compromise of an intermediate service exposes authority intended for unrelated resources.","cause":"Credential forwarding without audience or scope restriction.","solution":"Use scoped downstream credentials and explicit delegation semantics.","alternative":"End-to-end original-token propagation with strict audience validation.","tradeoff":"Token exchange improves privilege containment but introduces identity infrastructure and token lifecycle complexity.","constraint":"Delegation and subject identity semantics must remain auditable.","edge_case":"A downstream service may need both user identity and service identity for authorization.","security_implication":"Audience, scope, expiry, issuer, and delegation claims must be validated according to the trust model.","performance_implication":"Token exchange adds authorization infrastructure calls or cryptographic processing.","testing_guidance":"Test wrong audience, excessive scope, expired tokens, replay, delegation chains, and revocation behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that exchanged credentials cannot authorize operations outside their declared scope.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity provider","token validation"],"prerequisites":["delegation model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B84-0002","D14-AP-B82-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B82-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Token exchange and delegated authorization","publisher":"General identity architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B84-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Token Exchange","concept":"Audience and Scope Restriction","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be verified for a scoped downstream token?","answer":"The receiving resource should verify that the token was issued by an accepted authority, is intended for the receiving audience, remains valid, and contains sufficient but not excessive authority for the requested operation.","explanation":"Authentication of a token does not automatically establish authorization for a particular resource or operation. Audience and scope restrictions limit authority propagation.","principle":"Valid credentials must still be authorized for the exact resource and operation.","correct_example":"Reject a valid token whose audience identifies another service.","invalid_example":"Accept any valid token issued by a trusted authority regardless of its intended audience.","problem":"Credentials are accepted outside their intended authorization context.","failure_mode":"Cross-service credential reuse creates unintended authority.","cause":"Missing audience or scope validation.","solution":"Validate issuer, audience, expiry, integrity, scope, and relevant delegation semantics.","alternative":"Mutual authentication plus service-local authorization.","tradeoff":"Detailed validation improves isolation but requires consistent claim and policy semantics.","constraint":"The issuer and audience trust relationships must be explicitly configured.","edge_case":"Multiple audiences or delegated chains require precise interpretation.","security_implication":"Audience validation limits credential replay across service boundaries.","performance_implication":"Additional claim and signature validation consumes CPU and may require key-management lookups.","testing_guidance":"Test valid credentials with wrong audience, insufficient scope, expired validity, and altered claims.","verification_guidance":"Attempt cross-service credential reuse and verify deterministic rejection.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["token exchange or scoped credentials"],"prerequisites":["audience model","scope model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B84-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B84-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Token audience and scope validation","publisher":"General identity and authorization architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B85-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Secrets Management","concept":"Centralized Secrets Management","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does centralized secrets management solve?","answer":"Centralized secrets management provides controlled storage, retrieval, rotation, access policy, and auditing for sensitive credentials and cryptographic material.","explanation":"Secrets include credentials, API keys, private keys, tokens, and other sensitive values. A dedicated secrets mechanism can reduce hard-coded or widely copied credentials and support lifecycle controls.","principle":"Sensitive credentials should have an explicit lifecycle and access boundary rather than being treated as ordinary application configuration.","correct_example":"Allow an application workload to retrieve only the secrets required for its declared function from a controlled secret store.","invalid_example":"Commit long-lived production credentials to source control or package them into a public application artifact.","problem":"Secrets become broadly exposed and difficult to rotate.","failure_mode":"Credential compromise persists across deployments and systems.","cause":"Uncontrolled secret distribution and lifecycle.","solution":"Centralize secret storage or use an equivalent secure lifecycle mechanism with least-privilege retrieval.","alternative":"Platform-native secret injection with equivalent access controls.","tradeoff":"Centralized management improves lifecycle control but introduces a critical infrastructure dependency.","constraint":"Secret-store availability, recovery, access control, and key management must be addressed.","edge_case":"Bootstrap credentials may be required to obtain later secrets and therefore need separate protection.","security_implication":"Secret stores are high-value security targets and require strong authentication, authorization, encryption, audit, and recovery controls.","performance_implication":"Secret retrieval can add startup or request latency; caching must respect rotation semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized retrieval, rotation, expiry, secret-store failure, and application restart.","verification_guidance":"Verify that workloads can access only their authorized secrets and that secret values are not unnecessarily exposed in logs or artifacts.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["secret-management mechanism"],"prerequisites":["secret inventory","identity model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B85-0002","D14-AP-B71-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B82-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Secrets management architecture","publisher":"General secure-systems architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B85-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Secrets Management","concept":"Secret Rotation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an architecture define for secret rotation?","answer":"It should define secret ownership, rotation trigger, overlap period where required, consumer update behavior, revocation semantics, failure handling, and verification of successful replacement.","explanation":"Rotation is not merely generating a new value. Consumers must transition safely and old credentials must eventually lose authority according to policy.","principle":"Credential lifecycle must include issuance, distribution, replacement, revocation, and verification.","correct_example":"Issue a replacement credential, update consumers within a bounded compatibility window, verify usage of the new credential, then revoke the old credential.","invalid_example":"Rotate a credential immediately without checking whether all authorized consumers can obtain the replacement.","problem":"Credential rotation causes avoidable service outages or leaves old credentials active indefinitely.","failure_mode":"Consumers fail after rotation or compromised credentials remain usable.","cause":"No coordinated lifecycle semantics.","solution":"Define rotation protocol and verify consumer adoption before revocation when overlap is safe.","alternative":"Short-lived credentials that reduce dependence on manual rotation.","tradeoff":"Frequent rotation reduces credential lifetime but increases lifecycle and operational complexity.","constraint":"Some credentials cannot support seamless overlap and require coordinated downtime or migration.","edge_case":"A compromised credential may require immediate revocation without waiting for normal migration.","security_implication":"Rotation limits credential lifetime and supports compromise response.","performance_implication":"Frequent credential retrieval or refresh may add infrastructure load.","testing_guidance":"Test normal rotation, failed consumer update, emergency revocation, and recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify that old credentials lose authorization according to policy and new credentials function correctly.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["secret-management mechanism","identity system"],"prerequisites":["credential lifecycle"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B85-0001","D14-AP-B89-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B85-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Secret rotation lifecycle","publisher":"General credential-management architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B86-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Service-to-Service Security","concept":"Mutual Service Authentication","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does mutual service authentication solve?","answer":"Mutual service authentication allows both communicating services to establish authenticated identities before exchanging protected traffic.","explanation":"Mutual authentication can prevent a service from accepting traffic from an unauthenticated or unexpected peer. Authentication alone does not establish authorization for every operation.","principle":"Both sides of a protected service relationship should authenticate according to the trust model before relying on peer identity.","correct_example":"Service A validates Service B's authenticated workload identity before accepting a privileged service-to-service request.","invalid_example":"Trust any caller presenting an encrypted connection without validating peer identity.","problem":"A service cannot reliably determine the identity of its peer.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized services can impersonate trusted callers.","cause":"Encryption without authenticated peer identity.","solution":"Use authenticated service identities and validate them at the relevant boundary.","alternative":"One-way server authentication with application-level caller identity.","tradeoff":"Mutual authentication improves peer assurance but adds certificate or credential lifecycle complexity.","constraint":"Identity provisioning, rotation, revocation, and trust roots must be managed.","edge_case":"Legacy services may not support mutual authentication and require a controlled compatibility boundary.","security_implication":"Peer authentication reduces impersonation and unauthorized service access.","performance_implication":"Handshake and cryptographic processing add connection overhead, often mitigated through connection reuse.","testing_guidance":"Test valid peers, unknown peers, expired credentials, revoked identities, and trust-root changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that only authorized peer identities can establish protected service relationships.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["service identity","cryptographic transport or equivalent"],"prerequisites":["workload identity"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B86-0002","D14-AP-B82-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B82-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Mutual service authentication","publisher":"General secure service-to-service architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B86-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Service-to-Service Security","concept":"Authentication Authorization Separation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must service authentication and authorization remain distinct concepts?","answer":"Authentication establishes or verifies who or what a principal is, while authorization determines whether that principal is permitted to perform a specific operation on a specific resource.","explanation":"A successfully authenticated service may still have no permission for a requested resource or action. Treating authentication as authorization creates broad implicit trust.","principle":"Identity proof does not automatically grant permission.","correct_example":"Authenticate Service A and then evaluate whether Service A may invoke the requested administrative operation.","invalid_example":"Allow every authenticated internal service to access every API endpoint.","problem":"Identity verification is incorrectly treated as permission.","failure_mode":"Authenticated but unauthorized workloads gain access.","cause":"Missing authorization policy after authentication.","solution":"Perform explicit authorization based on resource, action, identity, and relevant context.","alternative":"Predefined service allowlists for narrow systems.","tradeoff":"Explicit authorization increases policy complexity but reduces implicit trust.","constraint":"Authorization policies must remain consistent with identity lifecycle.","edge_case":"A service may have different permissions for different resources or operations.","security_implication":"Separating authentication and authorization limits privilege escalation.","performance_implication":"Authorization evaluation adds processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test authenticated principals with allowed and denied operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that successful authentication alone never grants unauthorized resource access.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity","authorization"],"prerequisites":["service identity"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B86-0001","D14-AP-B83-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B86-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Authentication and authorization separation","publisher":"General access-control architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B87-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant Isolation Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does tenant isolation address in multi-tenant architecture?","answer":"Tenant isolation prevents one tenant's data, authorization scope, resource usage, or operational actions from improperly affecting or exposing another tenant.","explanation":"Isolation may be implemented at data, application, compute, network, identity, or infrastructure layers. The appropriate boundary depends on risk, workload, regulatory requirements, and platform capabilities.","principle":"Tenant identity and ownership must remain explicit across every resource boundary containing tenant-specific state.","correct_example":"Carry a verified tenant context into authorization and data-access decisions and enforce it at the protected resource boundary.","invalid_example":"Trust a tenant identifier supplied by a client without binding it to authenticated and authorized tenant context.","problem":"Shared resources can expose cross-tenant data or permit unauthorized operations.","failure_mode":"Tenant A accesses or modifies resources belonging to Tenant B.","cause":"Missing or inconsistently enforced tenant context.","solution":"Bind tenant context to authenticated identity and enforce isolation at relevant resource boundaries.","alternative":"Physically separate infrastructure per tenant.","tradeoff":"Logical isolation improves density and cost efficiency but requires rigorous enforcement and testing.","constraint":"Every path capable of accessing tenant data must preserve tenant isolation semantics.","edge_case":"Administrative or support operations may intentionally cross tenant boundaries and require separate privileged authorization.","security_implication":"Tenant isolation is a critical authorization and data-protection boundary.","performance_implication":"Per-tenant filtering and policy checks can add query or request overhead.","testing_guidance":"Perform cross-tenant access tests across APIs, databases, caches, queues, logs, files, and background jobs.","verification_guidance":"Verify that tenant context cannot be forged, omitted, or replaced during request processing.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["tenant identity","authorization","data ownership"],"prerequisites":["multi-tenant model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B87-0002","D14-AP-B81-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B81-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Multi-tenant isolation architecture","publisher":"General multi-tenant security principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B87-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Cross-Tenant Isolation Verification","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should tenant isolation be tested architecturally?","answer":"Tests should attempt unauthorized cross-tenant access through every relevant interface and state path, including direct APIs, background processing, caches, storage, messaging, and administrative workflows.","explanation":"Tenant isolation failures frequently occur outside the primary request path. A comprehensive test model must cover synchronous and asynchronous paths and derived data.","principle":"Tenant isolation must be verified at every path that can read, modify, infer, or expose tenant-specific state.","correct_example":"Execute a request authenticated for Tenant A that attempts to access Tenant B's resource and verify denial at the protected boundary.","invalid_example":"Test only the primary API endpoint while assuming background jobs and cache layers preserve tenant boundaries automatically.","problem":"Cross-tenant leakage remains undetected in secondary paths.","failure_mode":"Tenant-specific information becomes visible through caches, reports, queues, logs, or asynchronous workers.","cause":"Incomplete isolation test surface.","solution":"Enumerate all tenant-bearing data flows and test each relevant boundary.","alternative":"Formal isolation proof for systems with suitable models.","tradeoff":"Broad testing increases effort but provides stronger coverage of multi-tenant failure modes.","constraint":"Tests must avoid using real sensitive tenant data where synthetic fixtures are sufficient.","edge_case":"Aggregated analytics may intentionally combine tenants and require separate authorization semantics.","security_implication":"Isolation tests should include privilege escalation and identifier manipulation attempts.","performance_implication":"Large tenant datasets may be needed to expose partitioning or query-planning defects.","testing_guidance":"Use positive and negative tenant-access cases across all storage and processing paths.","verification_guidance":"Record evidence showing that unauthorized tenant crossings are rejected or correctly isolated.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["tenant isolation boundary"],"prerequisites":["tenant data-flow inventory"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B87-0001","D14-AP-B90-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B87-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Cross-tenant isolation verification","publisher":"Derived from tenant-isolation architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B88-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Data Classification","concept":"Data Classification Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What architectural purpose does data classification provide?","answer":"Data classification assigns meaningful protection requirements to information so architecture and controls can vary according to sensitivity, confidentiality, integrity, availability, or regulatory characteristics.","explanation":"Classification should drive access, storage, transmission, retention, logging, encryption, monitoring, and deletion requirements where applicable. Labels without enforceable consequences provide limited architectural value.","principle":"Security controls should be proportional to the defined sensitivity and handling requirements of the data.","correct_example":"Classify sensitive customer data and enforce stricter access, storage, transmission, and logging controls for that class.","invalid_example":"Mark data as sensitive without changing any access or handling behavior.","problem":"Different data sensitivities receive identical protection despite different risks.","failure_mode":"Sensitive data is exposed through ordinary application or infrastructure paths.","cause":"Classification is descriptive but not connected to policy enforcement.","solution":"Map classification levels to explicit handling and authorization policies.","alternative":"Resource-specific security policies without formal classification labels.","tradeoff":"Classification improves systematic policy application but requires governance and consistent labeling.","constraint":"Classification criteria must be understandable, stable, and applicable across data lifecycle stages.","edge_case":"A dataset may contain mixed-sensitivity fields requiring field-level or record-level controls.","security_implication":"Classification can drive encryption, access, monitoring, retention, and disclosure controls.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained classification controls may increase processing and storage overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test data movement between classification zones and verify policy changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that classified data receives the controls defined for its classification.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["data governance","authorization"],"prerequisites":["data inventory"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B88-0002","D14-AP-B87-0001"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B87-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Data classification architecture","publisher":"General information-security architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B88-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Data Classification","concept":"Classification-Aware Data Flow","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should data classification affect architecture across data flows?","answer":"Classification should travel with data or remain derivable through authoritative metadata so downstream components can apply the required handling policy.","explanation":"Data may pass through databases, queues, caches, logs, analytics systems, backups, and external integrations. Losing classification context can cause downstream controls to be applied incorrectly.","principle":"Protection requirements must remain associated with sensitive information throughout its lifecycle.","correct_example":"Attach or derive classification metadata when publishing sensitive records to a queue so consumers can apply the required handling policy.","invalid_example":"Classify data only in the original database while downstream systems treat exported records as ordinary data.","problem":"Security requirements are lost at system boundaries.","failure_mode":"Sensitive information enters lower-protection systems without appropriate controls.","cause":"Classification metadata is not propagated or enforced.","solution":"Define classification propagation, transformation, inheritance, and downgrade rules.","alternative":"Dedicated storage and processing zones for each sensitivity class.","tradeoff":"Metadata propagation improves flexibility but requires reliable semantics across systems.","constraint":"Classification metadata must itself be protected against unauthorized modification.","edge_case":"Derived datasets may have sensitivity different from source data and require explicit reclassification.","security_implication":"Classification propagation reduces accidental disclosure across system boundaries.","performance_implication":"Metadata propagation and policy evaluation add processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Trace classified data through storage, messaging, caching, analytics, exports, and deletion paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify that downstream systems retain or correctly recompute the required classification.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["data classification","data lineage"],"prerequisites":["data-flow inventory"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B88-0001","D14-AP-B89-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B88-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Classification-aware data flow","publisher":"Derived from data classification and lifecycle principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B89-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Audit Architecture","concept":"Security Audit Trail","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does a security audit architecture solve?","answer":"A security audit architecture creates attributable records of security-relevant events so access, administrative actions, policy decisions, and significant changes can be investigated and verified.","explanation":"Audit architecture should define event scope, actor identity, target resource, action, result, timestamp semantics, correlation context, integrity protection, retention, and access controls. Logging everything indiscriminately is not equivalent to useful auditing.","principle":"Security-relevant decisions and actions should produce trustworthy evidence appropriate to their risk.","correct_example":"Record who performed a privileged operation, what resource was targeted, what authorization context applied, the result, and sufficient correlation information for investigation.","invalid_example":"Store an unstructured message such as 'admin action occurred' without actor, target, result, or correlation context.","problem":"Security events cannot be reliably reconstructed or attributed.","failure_mode":"Investigators cannot determine what happened or whether an action was authorized.","cause":"Incomplete, mutable, inaccessible, or unauditable event records.","solution":"Define an audit event schema and protect its integrity, access, retention, and lifecycle.","alternative":"Application-specific event logs without centralized audit governance.","tradeoff":"Detailed audit records improve traceability but increase storage, processing, privacy, and operational costs.","constraint":"Audit data itself may contain sensitive information and requires access control.","edge_case":"Clock differences can complicate event ordering across distributed components.","security_implication":"Audit records should resist unauthorized modification and deletion according to the required threat model.","performance_implication":"Synchronous audit writes can affect request latency; asynchronous approaches introduce durability and ordering tradeoffs.","testing_guidance":"Test audit generation, failure handling, identity attribution, ordering, retention, and unauthorized modification attempts.","verification_guidance":"Verify that defined privileged and security-relevant actions produce complete attributable records.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity","event storage"],"prerequisites":["security event model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B89-0002","D14-AP-B90-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","title":"Security audit architecture","publisher":"General security and audit architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B89-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Audit Architecture","concept":"Audit Integrity and Correlation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes distributed security audit records useful for investigation?","answer":"Audit records should preserve attributable identity, resource and action context, consistent event-time semantics, correlation identifiers, and appropriate integrity protections.","explanation":"Distributed systems produce events across multiple services and infrastructure layers. Correlation context allows related events to be reconstructed, while integrity protections help establish whether records were altered.","principle":"Audit evidence must preserve enough trustworthy context to reconstruct security-relevant actions across boundaries.","correct_example":"Propagate a correlation identifier and actor context through a request chain while each service records its local action and outcome.","invalid_example":"Each service emits isolated log messages with no correlation or actor information.","problem":"Related events cannot be reliably associated.","failure_mode":"Investigation produces ambiguous or incomplete timelines.","cause":"Missing correlation and attribution metadata.","solution":"Define standardized audit fields and propagate correlation context across trusted boundaries.","alternative":"Centralized event tracing with equivalent audit semantics.","tradeoff":"More metadata improves reconstruction but increases event size and privacy considerations.","constraint":"Correlation identifiers must not themselves become authorization credentials.","edge_case":"Asynchronous workflows can break simple request-scoped correlation and require explicit workflow identifiers.","security_implication":"Audit metadata must not leak secrets or sensitive credentials.","performance_implication":"Additional metadata and centralized collection consume network and storage resources.","testing_guidance":"Trace multi-service workflows and verify complete correlation across synchronous and asynchronous paths.","verification_guidance":"Reconstruct known test workflows from audit evidence and compare them with observed actions.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["security audit trail","distributed tracing or correlation mechanism"],"prerequisites":["audit event schema"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B89-0001","D14-AP-B86-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B89-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Audit integrity and distributed correlation","publisher":"Derived from distributed audit architecture principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B90-0001","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Integrated Security Architecture","concept":"Security Pattern Composition","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should security architecture patterns be composed into a coherent system?","answer":"Compose security patterns around explicit identity, resource, action, data, trust, policy, and audit boundaries rather than deploying independent security components without a shared authorization model.","explanation":"Zero trust, workload identity, policy enforcement, scoped credentials, secrets management, tenant isolation, data classification, and audit architecture reinforce different parts of the same security model. Their contracts must align.","principle":"Security controls should form a traceable control chain from identity to authorization to enforcement to evidence.","correct_example":"Authenticate a workload, issue or validate appropriately scoped credentials, enforce authorization at the protected resource, apply tenant/data policy, and record the security-relevant outcome.","invalid_example":"Deploy identity authentication, gateway authorization, tenant filtering, and audit logging independently with conflicting definitions of identity and resource ownership.","problem":"Security controls disagree or leave gaps between boundaries.","failure_mode":"A request passes one security layer because another layer interprets identity, tenant, or resource scope differently.","cause":"No shared security ontology and inconsistent policy semantics.","solution":"Define canonical identities, resources, actions, trust boundaries, policy semantics, enforcement points, and audit fields.","alternative":"Monolithic centralized security enforcement.","tradeoff":"Composed security architecture provides defense across multiple boundaries but increases integration complexity.","constraint":"Each security mechanism must have clear ownership and failure semantics.","edge_case":"Emergency operations may intentionally bypass ordinary paths and require stronger separate controls.","security_implication":"Composition should minimize privilege propagation and preserve traceability across boundaries.","performance_implication":"Multiple controls increase request-path work and require performance analysis under realistic workloads.","testing_guidance":"Test complete request paths including authentication, authorization, tenant boundaries, data classification, secrets, and audit generation.","verification_guidance":"Trace representative privileged and unprivileged requests through every security control and verify consistent decisions.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["identity","authorization","policy enforcement","secrets","tenant isolation","audit"],"prerequisites":["security ontology","threat model"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B81-0001","D14-AP-B82-0002","D14-AP-B83-0001","D14-AP-B84-0001","D14-AP-B85-0001","D14-AP-B86-0001","D14-AP-B87-0001","D14-AP-B88-0001","D14-AP-B89-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Integrated security architecture pattern composition","publisher":"Derived from D14 security architecture relationships","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D14-AP-B90-0002","dataset_id":"D14","topic":"Security Architecture Patterns","subtopic":"Integrated Security Architecture","concept":"Security Architecture Quality Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified before accepting a security architecture pattern set?","answer":"Verify identity authenticity, authorization correctness, enforcement coverage, privilege boundaries, secret lifecycle, tenant isolation, data classification handling, audit evidence, degraded-mode behavior, and security control interactions.","explanation":"Security architecture cannot be validated by confirming that individual security products or mechanisms exist. The system must demonstrate that controls protect the intended resources and remain effective across normal, degraded, and adversarial paths.","principle":"Security assurance requires verification of control effectiveness at the actual protected boundary.","correct_example":"Trace an unauthorized request from identity validation through policy enforcement and confirm that it is denied and appropriately audited.","invalid_example":"Declare the architecture secure because it uses encryption, authentication, and a gateway without testing bypass paths or authorization semantics.","problem":"Security controls exist but their combined effectiveness is unverified.","failure_mode":"An attacker or malfunctioning component bypasses one control through an alternate path.","cause":"Component-level validation without architecture-level threat and control analysis.","solution":"Use threat modeling, control mapping, negative testing, privilege-boundary tests, failure-injection, and audit verification.","alternative":"Independent security architecture review and formal analysis for high-assurance components.","tradeoff":"Comprehensive verification increases effort but improves evidence quality and reduces untested assumptions.","constraint":"Security claims must distinguish conceptual design from executed evidence.","edge_case":"Third-party or platform-managed controls may require external evidence rather than direct internal testing.","security_implication":"All critical security boundaries require explicit evidence of enforcement and failure behavior.","performance_implication":"Security controls should be evaluated for latency and resource impact without weakening required protection.","testing_guidance":"Include authentication, authorization, bypass, privilege escalation, tenant isolation, secret exposure, degraded dependency, and audit tests.","verification_guidance":"Use explicit evidence statuses such as NOT_EXECUTED, TESTED, VERIFIED, or CONFLICTED; never infer verification solely from design presence.","language":"Architecture","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["security architecture pattern composition"],"prerequisites":["threat model","security requirements","verification strategy"],"related_records":["D14-AP-B90-0001","D14-AP-B70-0002"],"depends_on":["D14-AP-B90-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYNTHESIS","title":"Security architecture quality gate","publisher":"Derived from D14 security verification principles","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B01-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"API Contract","concept":"API Contract","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an API contract?","answer":"An API contract is an explicit definition of how clients and a service interact, including available operations, inputs, outputs, errors, constraints, and relevant behavioral semantics.","explanation":"A contract allows independently developed clients and servers to coordinate around stable observable behavior without requiring knowledge of internal implementation details.","principle":"An API should expose stable observable semantics while keeping implementation details behind the contract.","correct_example":"Define an operation with its request schema, response schema, authentication requirements, error behavior, and compatibility expectations.","invalid_example":"Document only a URL and HTTP method while leaving request fields, response structure, and error behavior unspecified.","problem":"Clients must infer behavior from implementation or accidental responses.","failure_mode":"Client integrations become brittle when implementation details change.","cause":"The API contract is incomplete or implicit.","solution":"Define observable request, response, error, security, and compatibility semantics explicitly.","alternative":"Informal documentation for highly temporary internal interfaces.","tradeoff":"Detailed contracts require maintenance but reduce ambiguity and integration risk.","constraint":"The contract must describe externally observable behavior rather than internal implementation.","edge_case":"An API may intentionally leave implementation-specific behavior unspecified; such uncertainty should be explicit.","security_implication":"Authentication, authorization, sensitive-data exposure, and security failure behavior should be part of the contract where applicable.","performance_implication":"Latency, pagination, payload limits, rate limits, and asynchronous behavior may form important contract expectations.","testing_guidance":"Generate contract tests from request, response, error, and constraint definitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify implementation behavior against the documented contract rather than only checking that endpoints exist.","language":"API Design","framework":null,"version":"HTTP semantics defined by RFC 9110; API contract tooling may vary","version_range":"Context-dependent","dependencies":["HTTP or another application protocol"],"prerequisites":["client-server interaction concepts"],"related_records":["D15-API-B01-0002","D15-API-B02-0001","D15-API-B07-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"1.3","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines HTTP's uniform request/response interface and core semantics."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B01-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"Contract Boundaries","concept":"Observable Contract Boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What should an API contract expose?","answer":"An API contract should expose the behavior that clients are permitted to depend on, including interface shape, accepted inputs, produced outputs, error semantics, relevant state transitions, and explicit constraints.","explanation":"Clients should not need to depend on private implementation details such as database tables, internal class structure, or internal service calls.","principle":"Expose stable behavior, not accidental implementation structure.","correct_example":"Expose an order resource and its supported operations without requiring clients to know the underlying database schema.","invalid_example":"Require clients to construct requests according to internal database column names that are not part of the intended domain contract.","problem":"Internal implementation leaks into external integration.","failure_mode":"Internal refactoring becomes a breaking API change.","cause":"No clear boundary between contract and implementation.","solution":"Define a stable domain-oriented interface and isolate internal implementation details.","alternative":"A deliberately implementation-oriented internal API when consumers and implementation are controlled together.","tradeoff":"Stable abstraction may require translation logic but reduces coupling.","constraint":"The boundary must still expose enough semantics for correct client behavior.","edge_case":"Internal APIs may intentionally expose lower-level details, but those details then become contract dependencies.","security_implication":"Do not expose internal identifiers, secrets, stack traces, or privileged implementation details unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"Contract abstractions may require transformation or aggregation that affects latency and payload size.","testing_guidance":"Test that clients can operate using only documented contract elements.","verification_guidance":"Review whether undocumented implementation details are required for correct client operation.","language":"API Design","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["API contract"],"prerequisites":["interface boundary concepts"],"related_records":["D15-API-B01-0001","D15-API-B02-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B01-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"1.3","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines uniform HTTP interfaces independent of implementation details."}],"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B02-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"HTTP API Model","concept":"HTTP Request Response Model","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What are the fundamental interactions in an HTTP API?","answer":"An HTTP API uses request and response messages in which a client sends a request containing method and target information and a server returns a response containing a status code and associated metadata and content as applicable.","explanation":"HTTP defines common semantics for request methods, status codes, headers, representations, and related metadata. Application APIs use these protocol semantics to expose domain operations.","principle":"Application-level API behavior should align with the semantics of the underlying HTTP protocol when HTTP is the selected interface.","correct_example":"Use GET to retrieve a representation and return an appropriate HTTP response status and representation.","invalid_example":"Use HTTP method names arbitrarily while assigning unrelated application semantics to them.","problem":"Application semantics conflict with protocol expectations.","failure_mode":"Generic HTTP tooling, clients, caches, or intermediaries behave unexpectedly.","cause":"API design ignores standardized HTTP semantics.","solution":"Map application behavior to appropriate HTTP methods, status codes, headers, and representations.","alternative":"Use RPC or another protocol when HTTP resource semantics are not an appropriate fit.","tradeoff":"Protocol-aligned design improves interoperability but requires understanding HTTP semantics.","constraint":"HTTP semantics do not automatically define application-specific domain behavior.","edge_case":"A custom API may use HTTP as transport while implementing RPC-like semantics; those semantics must still account for HTTP behavior.","security_implication":"Transport and protocol semantics must not be mistaken for authorization policy.","performance_implication":"HTTP connection management, payload size, caching, and intermediary behavior can affect performance.","testing_guidance":"Test request parsing, method behavior, status codes, headers, and representation handling.","verification_guidance":"Verify that implementation behavior conforms to both the API contract and applicable HTTP semantics.","language":"HTTP","framework":null,"version":"HTTP semantics: RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110 and compatible HTTP implementations","dependencies":["HTTP"],"prerequisites":["network request/response concepts"],"related_records":["D15-API-B02-0002","D15-API-B03-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"3.4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines HTTP messages as requests and responses and their core semantics."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B02-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"HTTP API Model","concept":"Resource and Representation Separation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the distinction between an HTTP resource and its representation?","answer":"A resource is the conceptual target identified by a URI, while a representation is information transferred to describe or manipulate the state associated with that resource.","explanation":"Separating resource identity from representation allows an API to expose different representations without changing the conceptual resource itself.","principle":"Resource identity and representation format are separate concerns.","correct_example":"A customer resource can be represented as JSON while retaining the same resource identity.","invalid_example":"Treat a JSON document format as if it were inherently the resource itself.","problem":"Clients become coupled to representation details as resource identity.","failure_mode":"Changing representation format is incorrectly treated as changing the resource model.","cause":"Resource and representation concepts are conflated.","solution":"Model resource identity independently from representation media types and schemas.","alternative":"RPC interfaces where the primary abstraction is an operation rather than a resource.","tradeoff":"Resource-oriented modeling can improve uniformity but may require careful modeling for action-heavy domains.","constraint":"A representation does not necessarily expose every internal aspect of a resource.","edge_case":"Some APIs expose operation results that are not naturally persistent resources.","security_implication":"Representation filtering must prevent unauthorized fields from being exposed.","performance_implication":"Representation size and serialization format influence bandwidth, latency, and processing cost.","testing_guidance":"Test resource identity independently from representation serialization.","verification_guidance":"Verify that representation changes do not unintentionally alter documented resource semantics.","language":"HTTP","framework":null,"version":"HTTP semantics: RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110 and compatible HTTP implementations","dependencies":["URI","HTTP representation semantics"],"prerequisites":["HTTP request/response model"],"related_records":["D15-API-B02-0001","D15-API-B04-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B02-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"3.1-3.2","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines resources, representations, and their separation in HTTP."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B03-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"HTTP Methods","concept":"HTTP Method Semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should HTTP methods be selected in an HTTP API?","answer":"Select an HTTP method according to its standardized semantics rather than using method names merely as arbitrary operation labels.","explanation":"RFC 9110 defines standardized method semantics including GET, HEAD, POST, PUT, DELETE, CONNECT, OPTIONS, and TRACE. API-specific behavior should not contradict the selected method's semantics.","principle":"Method choice communicates standardized intent and constrains expected behavior.","correct_example":"Use GET to retrieve a current representation and DELETE to request removal of representations associated with the target resource when those semantics fit the operation.","invalid_example":"Use GET for an operation that changes server state merely because GET is convenient for a client.","problem":"Method semantics are misused.","failure_mode":"Caching, retries, intermediaries, security controls, or clients make incorrect assumptions about the operation.","cause":"API operation names are mapped to HTTP methods without considering protocol semantics.","solution":"Choose methods based on standardized semantics and document application-specific behavior separately.","alternative":"Use POST for domain-specific processing when no more specific standardized method semantics fit.","tradeoff":"Strict semantic alignment improves interoperability but may require more deliberate resource modeling.","constraint":"Application behavior must not contradict required method semantics.","edge_case":"A domain operation may be difficult to model as a resource operation and may require POST-based processing.","security_implication":"Unsafe state-changing behavior must not be hidden behind methods whose semantics imply safe handling.","performance_implication":"Method semantics influence caching and intermediary behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test each operation against its declared method semantics, including repeat requests where relevant.","verification_guidance":"Verify method choice against RFC semantics and the application's documented contract.","language":"HTTP","framework":null,"version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["HTTP method semantics"],"prerequisites":["HTTP request/response model"],"related_records":["D15-API-B03-0002","D15-API-B05-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B02-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"9","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines standardized HTTP request method semantics."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B03-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"HTTP Methods","concept":"Safe and Idempotent Method Semantics","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What do safe and idempotent mean for HTTP methods?","answer":"A safe method is defined as read-only in its intended semantics, while an idempotent method is one where multiple identical requests have the same intended effect on server state as a single request, although responses may differ.","explanation":"These properties describe method semantics and affect retry and intermediary behavior. Idempotence does not mean a request cannot produce a response change or that every response is identical.","principle":"Safe and idempotent are protocol properties, not informal descriptions of whether an operation usually changes data.","correct_example":"GET is safe and idempotent according to HTTP semantics; PUT is idempotent but not safe.","invalid_example":"Call POST idempotent merely because the application happens to reject duplicate requests.","problem":"Retry behavior is designed using incorrect method assumptions.","failure_mode":"Automatic retries create duplicate effects or unsafe behavior.","cause":"Idempotency is confused with application-specific duplicate detection.","solution":"Understand protocol-level idempotence separately from application-level idempotency mechanisms.","alternative":"Use explicit idempotency keys when an application needs controlled duplicate suppression for operations whose method semantics do not provide the required guarantee.","tradeoff":"Application-level duplicate control adds state and complexity but can make retry-sensitive operations safer.","constraint":"Idempotence does not guarantee identical responses or absence of side effects outside the intended resource state.","edge_case":"A request can be idempotent with respect to intended server state while producing different response metadata or triggering logging.","security_implication":"Retry and duplicate suppression mechanisms must not permit authorization bypass or replay abuse.","performance_implication":"Idempotent operations may be safer to retry, but repeated requests can still consume resources.","testing_guidance":"Send repeated equivalent requests and verify the intended state effect and documented response behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify idempotence according to the protocol definition and application contract rather than response equality alone.","language":"HTTP","framework":null,"version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["HTTP method semantics"],"prerequisites":["HTTP methods"],"related_records":["D15-API-B03-0001","D15-API-B08-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B03-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"9.2","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines safe and idempotent HTTP method properties."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B04-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"Resource Modeling","concept":"Resource-Oriented API Modeling","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should resources be modeled in an HTTP API?","answer":"Model resources around stable domain identities and expose operations whose semantics correspond to retrieval, creation, replacement, deletion, or domain-specific processing as appropriate.","explanation":"Resource-oriented modeling provides predictable URI and method semantics while leaving domain-specific behavior explicit where a pure CRUD model is insufficient.","principle":"Resource identity should remain stable while operations express standardized or explicitly documented state transitions.","correct_example":"Model /customers/{customerId} as a customer resource and use appropriate methods for retrieval or replacement.","invalid_example":"Create arbitrary endpoint names that expose internal function names without stable resource semantics.","problem":"API structure mirrors implementation instead of domain concepts.","failure_mode":"Clients become tightly coupled to internal procedures.","cause":"No explicit resource model.","solution":"Identify domain resources, identities, relationships, and state transitions before defining endpoints.","alternative":"RPC-style operation endpoints where domain actions are primary and resource modeling would obscure the contract.","tradeoff":"Resource orientation improves consistency but does not eliminate the need for explicit domain actions.","constraint":"Resources should represent meaningful domain concepts rather than every internal object.","edge_case":"Commands such as 'approve' or 'calculate' may not map naturally to CRUD and can require explicit action semantics.","security_implication":"Resource identifiers must not bypass authorization scope or expose resources across tenants.","performance_implication":"Overly fine-grained resources can increase request count; overly broad resources can increase payload and processing cost.","testing_guidance":"Test resource identity, allowed methods, state transitions, and invalid transitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that URI and method semantics match the documented resource model.","language":"HTTP API","framework":null,"version":"HTTP semantics: RFC 9110","version_range":"Context-dependent","dependencies":["URI","HTTP methods"],"prerequisites":["domain modeling"],"related_records":["D15-API-B04-0002","D15-API-B03-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B02-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"3.1, 9","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Provides resource and method semantics used by resource-oriented APIs."}],"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B04-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"Resource Modeling","concept":"Resource Identity Stability","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should API resource identity remain stable?","answer":"Stable resource identity allows clients to reference the same conceptual resource across representations and supported lifecycle operations without depending on internal storage identifiers.","explanation":"Changing identifiers unnecessarily creates client migration cost and can break references, caches, relationships, and audit trails.","principle":"External resource identity should be stable independently of internal implementation changes.","correct_example":"Keep a documented customer identifier stable even if the underlying database is migrated.","invalid_example":"Expose a database row number as the API identifier and change it during storage migration.","problem":"External identity is coupled to internal storage.","failure_mode":"Storage changes break client references and integrations.","cause":"Internal identifiers were treated as public contract identifiers.","solution":"Define stable public resource identifiers and maintain mappings when internal identity changes.","alternative":"Use immutable internal identifiers as public IDs when their exposure is intentionally part of the contract.","tradeoff":"Stable public identity may require an additional mapping layer.","constraint":"Identity semantics must account for uniqueness, lifetime, and authorization scope.","edge_case":"Some resources may be intentionally ephemeral and have limited identity lifetime.","security_implication":"Identifiers should not be treated as authorization; access control must independently verify ownership or permission.","performance_implication":"Indirection between public and internal IDs can add lookup cost.","testing_guidance":"Test identifier persistence across updates, migrations, serialization changes, and deployment transitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that documented identifiers remain stable across supported lifecycle operations.","language":"HTTP API","framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":"Context-dependent","dependencies":["resource identity"],"prerequisites":["resource modeling"],"related_records":["D15-API-B04-0001","D15-API-B06-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B04-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","title":"Stable external resource identity","publisher":"Structured API design synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Supports separation between public resource identity and implementation storage."}],"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B05-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"HTTP Status Codes","concept":"Status Code Semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should HTTP status codes be used in an API?","answer":"Use HTTP status codes to communicate the standardized class and general result semantics of a request, while using response content or problem details for additional application-specific information when appropriate.","explanation":"RFC 9110 defines five status-code classes: informational, successful, redirection, client error, and server error. API-specific details should not replace correct HTTP status semantics.","principle":"Status codes communicate protocol-level outcome semantics; response content can provide additional domain-specific detail.","correct_example":"Return 201 when a successful request results in creation of a new resource, when the operation meets the semantics of that status.","invalid_example":"Return 200 for every outcome and encode all failures only inside a custom JSON field.","problem":"Generic HTTP clients cannot reliably interpret API outcomes.","failure_mode":"Monitoring, retries, caches, SDKs, and clients misclassify success and failure.","cause":"Status codes are treated as decorative rather than semantic.","solution":"Select status codes according to HTTP semantics and define application-specific details separately.","alternative":"Use a constrained internal API contract if the transport is not intended to expose generic HTTP semantics, while still respecting HTTP behavior where intermediaries participate.","tradeoff":"Correct status semantics improve interoperability but require careful mapping of application outcomes.","constraint":"Do not invent semantics that contradict standardized status-code meanings.","edge_case":"A single application operation can legitimately produce different status codes depending on outcome.","security_implication":"Avoid leaking sensitive authorization or internal failure information through status or error details.","performance_implication":"Clients may use status classes to decide retries or fallback behavior, affecting load.","testing_guidance":"Test representative success, client-error, and server-error conditions and verify exact status semantics.","verification_guidance":"Trace each documented status code to an HTTP-defined or explicitly justified application behavior.","language":"HTTP","framework":null,"version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["HTTP status codes"],"prerequisites":["HTTP request/response model"],"related_records":["D15-API-B05-0002","D15-API-B07-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B02-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"15","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines HTTP status-code classes and semantics."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B05-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"HTTP Status Codes","concept":"Status Code Class Interpretation","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What do the first digits of HTTP status codes indicate?","answer":"The first digit identifies the response class: 1xx informational, 2xx successful, 3xx redirection, 4xx client error, and 5xx server error.","explanation":"Clients must understand the class of an HTTP status code even when they do not recognize the specific code.","principle":"API clients should interpret unknown status codes according to their standardized class semantics.","correct_example":"Treat an unknown 4xx code as a client-error response while preserving any machine-readable problem details.","invalid_example":"Treat every unrecognized status code as success because the client does not know its exact meaning.","problem":"Clients fail to handle newly introduced or uncommon status codes safely.","failure_mode":"Unknown outcomes are incorrectly processed as successful operations.","cause":"Client logic depends on an exhaustive list of individual codes.","solution":"Implement class-aware handling and explicit handling for known codes required by the contract.","alternative":"Strictly reject undocumented status codes in controlled environments, provided this does not violate HTTP processing requirements.","tradeoff":"Class-aware handling improves forward compatibility but may provide less application-specific behavior for unknown codes.","constraint":"HTTP status codes are three-digit values within the standardized range.","edge_case":"An implementation may use internal non-HTTP status values, but those must not be confused with HTTP response status codes.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive failures must not be accidentally interpreted as successful operations.","performance_implication":"Status classification is computationally trivial; the main performance concern is downstream retry or fallback behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test known and representative unknown status codes within each class.","verification_guidance":"Verify that client logic preserves class semantics for unrecognized valid codes.","language":"HTTP","framework":null,"version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["HTTP status codes"],"prerequisites":["HTTP response model"],"related_records":["D15-API-B05-0001","D15-API-B07-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B05-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"15","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines status-code classes and client interpretation of unknown codes."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B06-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"Representations","concept":"Representation Media Type","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is the role of a media type in an API representation?","answer":"A media type identifies the format and semantics used to interpret a representation, allowing sender and recipient to agree on how content should be processed.","explanation":"Media types help distinguish representations such as JSON or other formats and participate in HTTP content negotiation and response interpretation.","principle":"Representation format should be explicitly identified rather than inferred solely from URL naming or payload appearance.","correct_example":"Return JSON with an appropriate Content-Type indicating the representation media type.","invalid_example":"Return JSON while labeling it as an unrelated media type or omitting representation metadata where the contract requires it.","problem":"Clients cannot reliably determine how to interpret response content.","failure_mode":"Parsing failures or silent misinterpretation occur.","cause":"Representation format is implicit or incorrectly declared.","solution":"Define supported media types and emit accurate representation metadata.","alternative":"A fixed single representation format with an explicit contract and consistent metadata.","tradeoff":"Supporting multiple media types increases compatibility options but also increases testing and maintenance.","constraint":"The declared media type must accurately describe the representation.","edge_case":"A response can contain no content, in which case representation semantics may not apply.","security_implication":"Content-type confusion can contribute to content interpretation and security problems in some clients.","performance_implication":"Different formats can have different serialization size and processing costs; claims should be measured for the target workload.","testing_guidance":"Test correct Content-Type values, unsupported formats, and negotiation behavior where applicable.","verification_guidance":"Verify that actual response content matches the declared media type.","language":"HTTP","framework":null,"version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["HTTP representation metadata"],"prerequisites":["representation concepts"],"related_records":["D15-API-B06-0002","D15-API-B09-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B02-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"8, 12","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines representation metadata and content negotiation semantics."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B06-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"Representations","concept":"Content Negotiation","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an API handle multiple representation formats?","answer":"An API can use HTTP content negotiation to select an acceptable representation based on client preferences and server capabilities, while explicitly defining supported media types and fallback behavior.","explanation":"Content negotiation separates the resource from a specific representation format. APIs should document supported formats and behavior when no acceptable representation can be produced.","principle":"Representation choice should be explicit, predictable, and compatible with HTTP negotiation semantics.","correct_example":"Document supported JSON and another representation format and define how the server responds when the requested representation is unavailable.","invalid_example":"Return different formats unpredictably based on undocumented client behavior.","problem":"Clients receive representations they cannot reliably parse.","failure_mode":"Integration failures or inconsistent client behavior occur.","cause":"Representation negotiation is implicit or undocumented.","solution":"Define supported media types, negotiation rules, and unsupported-format behavior.","alternative":"Support exactly one representation format where multi-format interoperability is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Multiple representations increase flexibility but multiply testing and compatibility requirements.","constraint":"Negotiation behavior must remain consistent with HTTP semantics.","edge_case":"A client may send an unsupported or overly restrictive preference.","security_implication":"Do not use content negotiation to bypass authorization or expose a more detailed representation to unauthorized clients.","performance_implication":"Content negotiation and multiple serializers add processing and maintenance cost.","testing_guidance":"Test supported, unsupported, conflicting, and absent representation preferences.","verification_guidance":"Verify that negotiation outcomes match the documented contract and HTTP semantics.","language":"HTTP","framework":null,"version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["media types","HTTP content negotiation"],"prerequisites":["representation media types"],"related_records":["D15-API-B06-0001","D15-API-B09-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B06-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"12","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines content negotiation concepts and representation selection."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B07-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"Request Validation","concept":"Contract-Level Input Validation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should API inputs be validated at the API boundary?","answer":"API inputs should be validated against the documented contract before deeper application processing so invalid structure, type, range, format, and constraint violations can be rejected predictably.","explanation":"Boundary validation reduces ambiguity and prevents malformed data from propagating into business logic or infrastructure components. Domain validation may still be required after structural validation.","principle":"Validate external data before trusting it as an internal domain value.","correct_example":"Reject a request whose required field has the wrong type or violates an explicitly documented constraint before invoking business logic.","invalid_example":"Pass arbitrary client-controlled values directly into database or infrastructure operations and rely on downstream failures.","problem":"Invalid external data reaches internal components.","failure_mode":"Unclear errors, corrupted state, security weaknesses, or unexpected runtime failures occur.","cause":"Missing or incomplete boundary validation.","solution":"Validate structure and contract constraints at the API boundary, then perform domain-specific validation in the appropriate layer.","alternative":"Schema validation delegated to a gateway or shared middleware when ownership and behavior remain explicit.","tradeoff":"Validation adds processing but usually reduces downstream complexity and failure ambiguity.","constraint":"Validation must not be confused with authorization or business-policy enforcement.","edge_case":"Some values cannot be fully validated without accessing domain state or external dependencies.","security_implication":"Boundary validation reduces injection and malformed-input risk but is not a substitute for context-specific output encoding or authorization.","performance_implication":"Complex validation can consume CPU; validation strategy should be proportional to payload size and risk.","testing_guidance":"Test missing fields, invalid types, boundary values, malformed structures, and valid representative inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify that invalid inputs fail before unsafe downstream operations and that errors follow the API contract.","language":"API Design","framework":null,"version_range":"Context-dependent","dependencies":["request schema","validation rules"],"prerequisites":["API contract"],"related_records":["D15-API-B07-0002","D15-API-B10-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B01-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"SECURE_API_DESIGN","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","title":"Boundary validation","publisher":"Structured API security and reliability synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Supports validation of untrusted external API input at system boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B07-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"Request Validation","concept":"Validation Error Contract","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should validation failures be represented by an API?","answer":"Validation failures should use an appropriate client-error status and a machine-readable error structure that identifies the relevant problem without exposing unnecessary implementation details.","explanation":"Clients need stable information to correct invalid requests. Error details should be structured and should not require parsing unstable human-readable messages.","principle":"Machine-readable validation errors should be stable enough for client handling while avoiding unnecessary internal disclosure.","correct_example":"Return a documented client-error response containing a problem type and structured information identifying invalid fields.","invalid_example":"Return a stack trace or require clients to parse arbitrary prose such as 'something went wrong'.","problem":"Clients cannot reliably determine which input violated the contract.","failure_mode":"Client correction logic becomes fragile and may expose sensitive server details.","cause":"No stable validation-error representation.","solution":"Define a structured validation-error contract using standardized problem details or another documented format.","alternative":"A domain-specific error schema when the API has requirements that problem details do not satisfy.","tradeoff":"Structured errors require schema governance but improve interoperability.","constraint":"Error responses must not expose secrets, internal paths, SQL statements, or sensitive diagnostics.","edge_case":"Multiple validation failures may occur in one request; the contract should define whether and how multiple problems are represented.","security_implication":"Error detail should avoid revealing information useful for attacking internal implementation or authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Detailed validation errors increase response size but can reduce client retries caused by ambiguity.","testing_guidance":"Test single-field, multi-field, malformed, and boundary validation failures.","verification_guidance":"Verify status, media type, problem structure, field references, and absence of prohibited sensitive information.","language":"HTTP API","version":"RFC 9457 for Problem Details","version_range":"RFC 9457+","dependencies":["request validation","HTTP status codes"],"prerequisites":["API error contract"],"related_records":["D15-API-B07-0001","D15-API-B08-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B05-0001","D15-API-B07-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"Problem Details for HTTP APIs","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9457","section":"3-4","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines machine-readable HTTP problem detail objects and guidance for error responses."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B08-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"Error Handling","concept":"Machine-Readable Problem Details","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the purpose of HTTP Problem Details?","answer":"HTTP Problem Details provides a standardized structure for carrying machine-readable information about an HTTP API problem in response content, commonly using application/problem+json.","explanation":"RFC 9457 defines problem details so APIs do not need to invent unrelated error formats for every failure. The HTTP status code remains important for general semantics, while problem details can provide additional application-specific information.","principle":"Use standardized error semantics where appropriate and keep application-specific details structured.","correct_example":"Return a 403 response with application/problem+json containing a documented problem type and relevant non-sensitive details.","invalid_example":"Return a successful 200 response containing a custom error object for every failure.","problem":"API errors are represented inconsistently across endpoints.","failure_mode":"Clients require endpoint-specific parsing and cannot reliably classify failures.","cause":"No common error representation.","solution":"Adopt RFC 9457 problem details or a deliberately documented domain-specific error contract.","alternative":"A domain-specific error representation where the application's requirements justify it.","tradeoff":"Standardized problem details improve consistency but still require API-specific problem-type governance.","constraint":"Problem details are not a substitute for correct HTTP status semantics.","edge_case":"Some responses do not need additional problem detail because the status code fully communicates the relevant semantics.","security_implication":"Problem details must avoid exposing implementation internals or sensitive information.","performance_implication":"Additional structured error content adds payload overhead but can reduce repeated client requests caused by ambiguous errors.","testing_guidance":"Test status code, Content-Type, problem type, required members, extension fields, and sensitive-data filtering.","verification_guidance":"Verify that problem responses are machine-readable and consistent with the API's documented error contract.","language":"HTTP API","version":"RFC 9457","version_range":"RFC 9457+","dependencies":["HTTP status codes","JSON or XML representation"],"prerequisites":["API error semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B08-0002","D15-API-B07-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B05-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457.html","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"Problem Details for HTTP APIs","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9457","section":"1-3","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines standardized machine-readable HTTP problem details."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B08-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"Error Handling","concept":"Error Detail Disclosure Boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What information should an API error response avoid exposing?","answer":"An API error response should avoid unnecessary internal implementation details such as stack traces, secrets, credentials, internal filesystem paths, database statements, and sensitive data not required by the contract.","explanation":"Error responses are observable by potentially untrusted clients. Detailed diagnostics should generally remain in controlled server-side telemetry while the API exposes stable client-actionable information.","principle":"Expose enough information for correct client behavior without unnecessarily revealing internal security or implementation details.","correct_example":"Return a stable problem type and sanitized diagnostic information while retaining detailed stack traces in protected server-side logs.","invalid_example":"Return a full exception traceback containing filesystem paths, database credentials, or internal service addresses.","problem":"Operational diagnostics leak through public API responses.","failure_mode":"Attackers gain information about internal architecture or secrets.","cause":"Server exception details are serialized directly into client responses.","solution":"Separate client-facing error contracts from internal diagnostic records and sanitize all externally exposed details.","alternative":"Highly controlled internal APIs may expose additional diagnostics if their trust model explicitly permits it.","tradeoff":"Less client-visible detail can make debugging harder without access to server telemetry.","constraint":"Sensitive data must not be exposed merely for debugging convenience.","edge_case":"Some APIs intentionally expose detailed domain validation errors; those still require disclosure review.","security_implication":"Error disclosure can reveal attack surface, identifiers, implementation technology, or sensitive data.","performance_implication":"Centralized diagnostic correlation may require additional logging infrastructure and processing.","testing_guidance":"Inject representative exceptions and inspect responses for prohibited information.","verification_guidance":"Verify that client responses contain only contract-approved diagnostic information.","language":"API Design","version_range":"Context-dependent","dependencies":["error handling","observability"],"prerequisites":["API error contract"],"related_records":["D15-API-B08-0001","D15-API-B10-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B08-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"SECURITY_API_ERRORS","source_type":"SECURITY_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","title":"Error disclosure boundary","publisher":"Structured defensive API security synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines separation between client-facing errors and internal diagnostics."}],"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B09-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"API Schemas","concept":"Schema Contract","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What role does a schema play in API design?","answer":"A schema defines the expected structure, types, constraints, and relationships of API request or response data so producers and consumers share an explicit data contract.","explanation":"Schemas can support documentation, validation, code generation, contract testing, and compatibility analysis. A schema describes contract structure but does not automatically prove runtime behavior.","principle":"Machine-readable schemas should express externally observable data constraints that clients and servers are expected to honor.","correct_example":"Define required and optional fields, data types, constraints, and response structures in a machine-readable API contract.","invalid_example":"Maintain a schema that differs materially from runtime behavior and assume generated clients remain correct.","problem":"API data structures are ambiguous or drift from implementation.","failure_mode":"Client generation, validation, and integration behavior diverge from the running service.","cause":"Schema is treated as documentation rather than a governed contract.","solution":"Validate schema against implementation and use contract tests to detect drift.","alternative":"Code-first API definitions with reliable generation of a machine-readable schema.","tradeoff":"Schema governance adds process but improves interoperability and automated validation.","constraint":"Schema correctness does not by itself prove business logic correctness.","edge_case":"Dynamic fields may be difficult to represent precisely and require explicit extension semantics.","security_implication":"Schemas should constrain unsafe inputs and prevent accidental exposure of sensitive fields.","performance_implication":"Large or deeply nested schemas can produce large payloads and expensive validation or serialization.","testing_guidance":"Validate request and response examples against schemas and test boundary constraints.","verification_guidance":"Compare schema-defined behavior with runtime behavior and mark unverified portions explicitly.","language":"API Design","framework":"OpenAPI is one commonly used contract description format","version":"OpenAPI has independently versioned specifications","version_range":"Use the specific OpenAPI version declared by the API contract","dependencies":["data schema","API contract"],"prerequisites":["API request/response modeling"],"related_records":["D15-API-B09-0002","D15-API-B10-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B01-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://spec.openapis.org/oas/","source_type":"SPECIFICATION","title":"OpenAPI Specification","publisher":"OpenAPI Initiative","version":"Multiple maintained specification versions","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines a machine-readable description format for HTTP APIs and maintains versioned specifications."}],"evidence_level":"VERIFIED FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B09-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"API Schemas","concept":"Schema Runtime Drift","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is API schema drift?","answer":"API schema drift occurs when the documented or machine-readable API contract diverges from the behavior actually exposed by the running service.","explanation":"Drift can occur through undocumented implementation changes, stale generated schemas, manual edits, incomplete validation, or deployment inconsistencies.","principle":"A schema is useful only when its relationship to deployed behavior is actively governed.","correct_example":"Detect that an endpoint now returns a required field with a different type than declared in the API schema.","invalid_example":"Change runtime behavior without updating or validating the corresponding contract.","problem":"Clients consume a contract that no longer represents the service.","failure_mode":"Generated clients, integrations, tests, and documentation become unreliable.","cause":"No contract drift detection.","solution":"Add schema validation, contract tests, CI checks, or runtime conformance checks as appropriate.","alternative":"Manual compatibility review for low-change internal APIs.","tradeoff":"Automated drift detection adds tooling but reduces silent integration failures.","constraint":"Runtime conformance checks must reflect the actual deployed version and configuration.","edge_case":"Conditional responses may legitimately vary within a documented union or alternative schema.","security_implication":"Schema drift can expose fields or operations that security review did not cover.","performance_implication":"Unexpected payload growth can increase network and serialization costs.","testing_guidance":"Compare representative runtime requests and responses against the declared schema.","verification_guidance":"Mark schema conformance as verified only when evidence comes from the relevant implementation and deployment context.","language":"API Design","framework":"OpenAPI","version":"OpenAPI version must be declared per contract","version_range":"Context-dependent","dependencies":["API schema","runtime implementation"],"prerequisites":["schema contract"],"related_records":["D15-API-B09-0001","D15-API-B10-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B09-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"https://spec.openapis.org/oas/","source_type":"SPECIFICATION","title":"OpenAPI Specification","publisher":"OpenAPI Initiative","version":"Versioned specification family","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Provides machine-readable API description semantics that can be used for conformance and tooling."}],"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B10-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"Contract Testing","concept":"API Contract Test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should an API contract test verify?","answer":"An API contract test should verify that requests and responses conform to the agreed interface, including method, path, headers, schema, status behavior, and documented constraints relevant to the test.","explanation":"Contract tests focus on interoperability between API producers and consumers. They do not replace business-logic, security, integration, performance, or end-to-end tests.","principle":"Test the observable contract independently from implementation details.","correct_example":"Send a documented request and verify the response status, media type, required fields, field types, and defined error behavior.","invalid_example":"Test only that the server process starts and infer that the API contract is correct.","problem":"API compatibility is assumed without direct evidence.","failure_mode":"Breaking changes reach consumers despite apparently passing internal tests.","cause":"No explicit contract verification.","solution":"Generate or maintain contract tests from the API specification and representative consumer expectations.","alternative":"Consumer-driven contract testing where multiple consumers define required interactions.","tradeoff":"Contract testing adds maintenance but can detect interface drift before integration failures spread.","constraint":"Contract tests must target the correct API version and deployment configuration.","edge_case":"Backward-compatible additions may require tests that tolerate permitted extra fields.","security_implication":"Contract tests should include security-relevant requirements where they form part of the observable contract.","performance_implication":"Contract tests usually focus on correctness rather than performance; separate performance tests remain necessary.","testing_guidance":"Include positive, negative, boundary, compatibility, and error-contract cases.","verification_guidance":"Record the contract version, test inputs, observed outputs, and limitations; do not infer universal correctness from passing contract tests.","language":"API Design","framework":null,"version_range":"Context-dependent","dependencies":["API contract","schema","test system"],"prerequisites":["machine-readable API specification"],"related_records":["D15-API-B10-0002","D15-API-B09-0001","D15-API-B07-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B09-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API_CONTRACT_TESTING","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","title":"API contract verification","publisher":"Structured API testing synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines contract-level verification boundaries and limitations."}],"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17"}
{"record_id":"D15-API-B10-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design Fundamentals","subtopic":"Contract Testing","concept":"Contract Verification Boundary","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What does passing an API contract test actually prove?","answer":"Passing a contract test provides evidence that the tested interaction conforms to the tested contract conditions in the tested environment; it does not prove universal correctness, security, performance, or behavior outside the tested scope.","explanation":"Verification must preserve its evidence boundary. A contract test can establish specific interface compatibility while leaving business rules, failure behavior, security controls, performance characteristics, and untested versions unresolved.","principle":"A verification claim must not exceed the scope of its evidence.","correct_example":"Record that the tested endpoint matched its schema and status contract for the tested cases and environment.","invalid_example":"State that the entire API is secure and correct because a small contract test suite passed.","problem":"Testing evidence is overgeneralized.","failure_mode":"Unverified requirements are incorrectly treated as satisfied.","cause":"No explicit verification boundary.","solution":"Record claim, test scope, observed result, environment, limitations, and confidence.","alternative":"Broader verification combining contract, integration, security, performance, and end-to-end evidence.","tradeoff":"Narrow claims are less impressive but more truthful and operationally useful.","constraint":"Verification status must remain tied to actual evidence.","edge_case":"A formal specification may establish stronger properties than ordinary tests, but its proof scope must still be explicit.","security_implication":"Contract conformance does not establish authorization correctness unless authorization behavior was explicitly tested or otherwise verified.","performance_implication":"Contract conformance does not establish latency or throughput requirements unless performance evidence was collected.","testing_guidance":"Store test scope and limitations alongside contract results.","verification_guidance":"Distinguish GENERATED, EXECUTED, TESTED, PASSED, VERIFIED, and REQUIREMENT SATISFIED states.","language":"API Design","version_range":"Context-dependent","dependencies":["contract tests","verification model"],"prerequisites":["API contract"],"related_records":["D15-API-B10-0001","D15-API-B09-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B10-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLES","source_type":"ENGINEERING_VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLE","title":"Evidence-bounded API verification","publisher":"Structured verification synthesis","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17","relevance":"Defines the boundary between test evidence and broader correctness claims."}],"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

