{"record_id":"D15-API-B11-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Resource Modeling","concept":"Resource-Oriented API Model","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is a resource-oriented API model?","answer":"A resource-oriented API models identifiable domain entities or conceptual resources and exposes operations on their representations through a consistent interface. Resource modeling should reflect stable domain concepts rather than implementation details.","explanation":"A resource is an abstraction identified by an API address and represented through a transferable representation. The resource model should remain understandable to clients even when internal storage or implementation changes.","principle":"Model externally meaningful domain concepts, not internal database tables or service implementation structures.","correct_example":"GET /customers/123 retrieves the representation of customer 123.","invalid_example":"GET /executeSql?table=customers&id=123 exposes persistence implementation as the primary API abstraction.","problem":"Clients become coupled to internal storage structures.","failure_mode":"Internal database changes require client-facing API changes.","cause":"The API contract mirrors implementation rather than domain semantics.","solution":"Define stable resource concepts and map them internally to persistence models.","alternative":"A command-oriented RPC interface may be appropriate when the domain operation is inherently action-oriented.","tradeoff":"Resource-oriented models improve uniformity and discoverability but can become awkward for highly procedural or workflow-heavy operations.","constraint":"Resource modeling must not imply that every domain operation is naturally CRUD.","edge_case":"A domain action may not correspond cleanly to creation, retrieval, update, or deletion of a resource.","security_implication":"Resource identifiers must not be treated as authorization grants; authorization must independently evaluate actor, resource, action, and context.","performance_implication":"Resource representations should avoid unnecessarily expensive aggregation when clients only require a subset of data.","testing_guidance":"Test resource identity, representation stability, unsupported operations, authorization, and mapping between public resources and internal models.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each public resource has a defined identity, lifecycle, representation, ownership semantics, and authorization boundary.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"HTTP/1.1 and HTTP Semantics as standardized by RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["HTTP semantics","domain modeling"],"related_records":["D15-API-B11-0002","D15-API-B12-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"3; 9","relevance":"Defines HTTP resource and request semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B11-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Resource Modeling","concept":"Stable Resource Identity","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should API resource identity be designed?","answer":"A resource identifier should provide a stable way for clients to refer to the same logical resource within the API contract. Identifier structure should not unnecessarily expose mutable implementation details.","explanation":"Stable identity allows clients to cache, reference, update, authorize, and correlate resources without depending on internal database identifiers or storage layout.","principle":"Public identity should be stable independently of internal persistence representation.","correct_example":"GET /orders/ord_01HXYZ returns the order identified by a stable public identifier.","invalid_example":"GET /orders/primary-db-row-88421 exposes a storage-specific identifier whose meaning can change during migration.","problem":"Client references become coupled to internal implementation.","failure_mode":"Database migration or sharding changes invalidate client assumptions.","cause":"Internal identifiers were exposed as the public identity contract.","solution":"Define an explicit public identifier strategy and map it to internal identifiers where required.","alternative":"Opaque identifiers can be used when exposing domain-readable identifiers would create undesirable coupling.","tradeoff":"Opaque identifiers reduce information leakage and coupling but may be less human-readable.","constraint":"Identifier uniqueness, lifecycle, and reuse semantics must be defined.","edge_case":"Deleted identifiers should not automatically be reused if clients may retain stale references.","security_implication":"Avoid identifiers that unnecessarily reveal sensitive information, sequential volume, or authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Identifier generation and lookup should remain efficient at expected scale.","testing_guidance":"Test uniqueness, persistence across updates, malformed identifiers, deleted resources, and migration behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that identifier semantics remain stable across supported storage and deployment changes.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B11-0001"],"prerequisites":["resource modeling"],"related_records":["D15-API-B12-0001","D15-API-B15-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B11-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":["D15-API-B11-0001"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"3","relevance":"Provides HTTP resource semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B12-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"HTTP Method Semantics","concept":"Method Semantics Versus Resource Naming","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should HTTP method semantics relate to resource naming?","answer":"The HTTP method supplies the primary request semantics while the target resource identifies the context on which those semantics operate. Resource names should not be overloaded with method semantics when standard HTTP methods already express the intended operation.","explanation":"HTTP defines method semantics independently from the URI. Combining verbs unnecessarily into resource names can reduce consistency with standard HTTP behavior.","principle":"Use the method for standardized operation semantics and the resource identifier for the target.","correct_example":"DELETE /users/123 requests deletion semantics for user 123.","invalid_example":"POST /deleteUser/123 uses a custom verb in the URI when standard DELETE semantics are appropriate.","problem":"Custom URI verbs create inconsistent API semantics.","failure_mode":"Clients need endpoint-specific rules instead of relying on standardized HTTP semantics.","cause":"Action semantics were encoded into URI paths instead of using the HTTP method where appropriate.","solution":"Use standardized methods when their semantics match the operation; model genuine domain commands explicitly when they do not.","alternative":"A command endpoint can be appropriate for operations that are not naturally represented by standard resource state transitions.","tradeoff":"Standard methods improve uniformity; command endpoints can express complex domain actions more directly.","constraint":"Do not force an artificial CRUD interpretation onto inherently procedural operations.","edge_case":"Operations such as password reset, payment authorization, or workflow transitions may require explicit command semantics.","security_implication":"Changing a URI method convention must not bypass authorization or CSRF protections where applicable.","performance_implication":"Method choice itself does not guarantee performance; server-side operation cost must be evaluated separately.","testing_guidance":"Verify method/resource combinations, unsupported methods, status codes, and idempotency semantics.","verification_guidance":"Compare each endpoint's declared operation with the normative semantics of its HTTP method.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["HTTP methods","resource modeling"],"related_records":["D15-API-B12-0002","D15-API-B13-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"9","relevance":"Defines HTTP method semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B12-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"HTTP Method Semantics","concept":"Safe And Idempotent Methods","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What is the distinction between safe and idempotent HTTP methods?","answer":"A safe method is defined as read-only in its intended semantics, while an idempotent method has the property that repeating the same request has the same intended effect as making it once, although responses may differ.","explanation":"Safety concerns the intended state-changing nature of the operation. Idempotency concerns the intended effect of multiple identical requests. RFC 9110 defines GET and HEAD as safe and GET, HEAD, PUT, DELETE, and OPTIONS as idempotent according to its method properties.","principle":"Do not infer idempotency merely from whether an endpoint appears to read or write data.","correct_example":"A PUT request that sets a resource representation to a specified state can be retried while preserving the intended final state.","invalid_example":"Assuming POST is idempotent merely because the server may happen to deduplicate requests.","problem":"Incorrect retry assumptions can produce duplicate effects.","failure_mode":"Clients retry non-idempotent operations after uncertain network outcomes and cause duplicate side effects.","cause":"Application behavior was confused with standardized method semantics.","solution":"Use standardized method semantics accurately and add application-level idempotency mechanisms where needed.","alternative":"Use an idempotency key or operation identifier for retry-sensitive non-idempotent operations.","tradeoff":"Additional idempotency mechanisms improve retry safety but require state, lifecycle, and expiration rules.","constraint":"Idempotency does not mean responses must be byte-for-byte identical.","edge_case":"A successful PUT followed by an identical PUT can return different metadata while still having the same intended effect.","security_implication":"Retry mechanisms must prevent replay from creating unauthorized or duplicate high-impact actions.","performance_implication":"Server-side deduplication may require persistent lookup state and therefore additional storage overhead.","testing_guidance":"Repeat identical requests and verify intended state/effect semantics rather than merely comparing response bodies.","verification_guidance":"Verify method semantics against the API contract and RFC-defined properties.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B12-0001"],"prerequisites":["HTTP methods"],"related_records":["D15-API-B12-0001","D15-API-B18-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B12-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"9.2; 9.3","relevance":"Defines safe and idempotent method properties."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B13-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"HTTP Methods","concept":"GET And Retrieval Semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should a GET API operation guarantee semantically?","answer":"GET requests a current selected representation of a target resource and is defined as safe and idempotent. A GET handler should not intentionally perform state-changing business actions as part of its defined semantics.","explanation":"GET is intended for retrieval. Servers may perform incidental processing such as logging or accounting, but those effects must not make the requested operation unsafe.","principle":"Keep retrieval semantics separate from state-changing commands.","correct_example":"GET /products/42 returns the current representation of product 42.","invalid_example":"GET /users/42/delete permanently removes the user as a side effect.","problem":"State-changing behavior attached to GET violates expected method semantics.","failure_mode":"Automated clients, crawlers, caches, or prefetchers can trigger unintended actions.","cause":"The API used GET as a generic endpoint invocation mechanism.","solution":"Use the appropriate state-changing method or explicit command operation.","alternative":"Use POST for a domain action that cannot be naturally expressed through a standard retrieval or state-transition method.","tradeoff":"Strict retrieval semantics improve interoperability and safety but may require additional endpoint modeling.","constraint":"GET does not guarantee that a response is cacheable in every circumstance; caching depends on HTTP caching semantics and response metadata.","edge_case":"A GET may trigger server-side logging or other incidental effects without violating its safe semantics.","security_implication":"Sensitive data returned by GET must still be protected through authentication, authorization, and appropriate cache controls.","performance_implication":"GET responses are often cache candidates, but caching behavior must be explicitly validated rather than assumed.","testing_guidance":"Test that GET does not mutate protected business state and that correct status codes and representations are returned.","verification_guidance":"Inspect server behavior and integration tests for unintended state mutation.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B12-0002"],"prerequisites":["HTTP method semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B13-0002","D15-API-B14-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B12-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"9.3.1","relevance":"Defines GET semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B13-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"HTTP Methods","concept":"PUT POST DELETE Semantic Distinction","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How do PUT, POST, and DELETE differ at a high level?","answer":"PUT requests creation or replacement of the target resource state according to its defined semantics and is idempotent; POST requests resource-specific processing and is not generally idempotent; DELETE requests removal of the target resource association and is idempotent according to HTTP semantics.","explanation":"The exact application behavior depends on the API contract, but the standardized method properties should remain intact.","principle":"Choose the method whose standardized semantics best match the intended operation.","correct_example":"PUT /documents/123 replaces or establishes the state identified by document 123.","invalid_example":"Using POST for every operation without defining why standardized PUT or DELETE semantics do not apply.","problem":"Uniform use of one method destroys semantic interoperability.","failure_mode":"Clients cannot safely infer retry and operation behavior.","cause":"Method selection was based solely on implementation convenience.","solution":"Define endpoint semantics first and select the HTTP method accordingly.","alternative":"Use command-style POST when the operation is not naturally modeled as a standard resource state transition.","tradeoff":"Standard methods provide stronger shared semantics; POST provides greater flexibility.","constraint":"The method semantics must be documented when application behavior is specialized.","edge_case":"A POST may create a subordinate resource, initiate processing, or trigger an action without directly mapping to CRUD.","security_implication":"Authorization must distinguish the operation represented by each method.","performance_implication":"Method choice does not itself determine server performance.","testing_guidance":"Test method-specific status codes, repeat requests, side effects, and failure behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify endpoint contracts against RFC-defined method semantics.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B12-0002"],"prerequisites":["HTTP methods"],"related_records":["D15-API-B13-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B12-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"9.3; 9.3.3; 9.3.4; 9.3.5","relevance":"Defines method semantics and idempotency."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B14-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"HTTP Status Semantics","concept":"Status Code Classes","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What do the HTTP status code classes represent?","answer":"HTTP status codes are grouped into informational 1xx, successful 2xx, redirection 3xx, client error 4xx, and server error 5xx classes.","explanation":"The first digit communicates the general category while the specific code conveys more precise semantics.","principle":"Select status codes according to standardized semantics rather than using arbitrary application meanings.","correct_example":"A successful resource creation can use 201 Created when its conditions are satisfied.","invalid_example":"Returning 200 OK for every operation regardless of whether a resource was created, accepted for asynchronous processing, or rejected.","problem":"Clients lose standardized semantic information.","failure_mode":"Client behavior becomes dependent on custom response conventions.","cause":"Status codes were treated as decorative rather than semantic protocol information.","solution":"Map API outcomes to appropriate standardized status codes and document application-specific response bodies separately.","alternative":"Custom application error codes can supplement HTTP status codes but should not replace useful HTTP semantics.","tradeoff":"Precise status mapping improves interoperability but requires consistent API governance.","constraint":"A status code must not claim an outcome that did not occur.","edge_case":"A request may be syntactically valid but rejected because of application state or authorization.","security_implication":"Error responses should avoid leaking sensitive implementation details.","performance_implication":"Status code selection has negligible computational cost but can influence client retry behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test representative success, redirect, client-error, and server-error conditions.","verification_guidance":"Verify status code mapping against RFC 9110 and the API contract.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["HTTP basics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B14-0002","D15-API-B16-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"15","relevance":"Defines HTTP status code classes and semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B14-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"HTTP Status Semantics","concept":"201 Created Versus 202 Accepted","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should an API distinguish 201 Created from 202 Accepted?","answer":"201 Created indicates that the request resulted in creation of a resource, while 202 Accepted indicates that the request has been accepted for processing but the processing may not have completed.","explanation":"The distinction communicates whether resource creation has already occurred versus whether processing is merely accepted for later completion.","principle":"Status codes should describe the actual processing state represented by the response.","correct_example":"POST /reports returns 202 Accepted when report generation has been accepted but is still running.","invalid_example":"Returning 201 Created before the resource actually exists or before the API has established that creation succeeded.","problem":"Clients receive a false representation of processing state.","failure_mode":"Clients act on resources or outcomes that do not yet exist.","cause":"Status selection was based on desired workflow rather than actual outcome.","solution":"Define explicit asynchronous processing semantics and use 202 only when the request has been accepted without completed processing.","alternative":"Return a completed resource with 201 when creation has actually occurred.","tradeoff":"202 supports asynchronous workflows but requires clients to understand completion tracking.","constraint":"The API should define how clients can determine eventual completion when using asynchronous processing.","edge_case":"Processing may later fail even though the request was accepted with 202.","security_implication":"Status responses and polling resources must preserve authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Asynchronous acceptance can decouple client latency from expensive processing but adds workflow infrastructure.","testing_guidance":"Test accepted-but-incomplete, completed, and failed asynchronous states.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the response accurately corresponds to the processing state at response time.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B14-0001"],"prerequisites":["HTTP status semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B16-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B14-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"15.3.2; 15.3.3","relevance":"Defines 201 Created and 202 Accepted."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B15-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"RPC Design","concept":"RPC API Model","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an RPC-style API model?","answer":"An RPC-style API models interactions primarily as operations or procedures invoked with defined inputs and outputs rather than primarily as manipulation of resource representations.","explanation":"RPC can express command-oriented operations naturally, especially when domain behavior is procedural, workflow-driven, or action-centric.","principle":"Use operation-oriented modeling when the domain action is more important than resource state representation.","correct_example":"POST /payments/{id}:capture invokes a domain operation that captures a payment.","invalid_example":"Forcing every complex workflow into artificial CRUD resources solely to appear REST-like.","problem":"The API becomes difficult to understand when domain actions do not map naturally to resource state transitions.","failure_mode":"Clients need convoluted sequences of resource mutations to express one domain command.","cause":"Architecture imposed a resource abstraction where a command abstraction was clearer.","solution":"Use explicit command semantics where justified and document their input, output, authorization, and failure behavior.","alternative":"A resource-oriented design can represent the resulting state transition when that model remains clear.","tradeoff":"RPC can express operations directly but may provide fewer uniform semantics than standard HTTP resource operations.","constraint":"RPC endpoints should still use HTTP and status semantics consistently when transported over HTTP.","edge_case":"A command can initiate asynchronous work and therefore require explicit acceptance and completion semantics.","security_implication":"Each operation requires explicit authorization; generic command endpoints should not become privilege escalation paths.","performance_implication":"Operation-oriented APIs can reduce awkward multi-step client interactions but may hide expensive server-side workflows.","testing_guidance":"Test command inputs, authorization, idempotency or replay behavior, state transitions, and failure outcomes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each RPC operation has a defined contract and that the operation cannot be confused with an unrelated resource mutation.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["HTTP semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B15-0002","D15-API-B11-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D15-API-B11-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API architecture practice","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"RPC versus Resource-Oriented API Modeling","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines operation-oriented API modeling concept."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B15-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"REST And RPC Comparison","concept":"REST Versus RPC Selection","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What factors should determine whether a resource-oriented or RPC-oriented API model is used?","answer":"Selection should depend on domain semantics, client interaction patterns, consistency requirements, interoperability needs, workflow complexity, and whether operations naturally map to resource representations or explicit commands.","explanation":"Neither modeling style universally fits every API. A design should preserve semantic clarity and a stable client contract rather than optimizing for terminology alone.","principle":"Choose the external abstraction that best represents the domain behavior and client contract.","correct_example":"Use resource operations for standard entity lifecycle management and explicit commands for domain-specific actions that do not map cleanly to resource CRUD.","invalid_example":"Choosing REST or RPC solely because a team considers one style universally superior.","problem":"Style becomes more important than semantic correctness.","failure_mode":"The API contains unnatural abstractions and complex client workflows.","cause":"Architecture selection was driven by ideology rather than domain and contract requirements.","solution":"Evaluate the operation model, consistency, interoperability, versioning, observability, and client needs before selecting the style.","alternative":"A hybrid API can combine resource-oriented operations with explicit command endpoints when the contract remains coherent.","tradeoff":"Hybrid designs can improve semantic fit but require stronger API governance to prevent inconsistency.","constraint":"The chosen model should be internally consistent and documented.","edge_case":"Different API surfaces within one platform may legitimately require different interaction models.","security_implication":"Authorization semantics must remain explicit regardless of API style.","performance_implication":"Performance depends more on operation design, payloads, network behavior, and implementation than on the REST/RPC label itself.","testing_guidance":"Compare workflows by number of requests, failure handling, contract clarity, and semantic correctness.","verification_guidance":"Document the decision criteria and verify representative use cases against the selected model.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B11-0001","D15-API-B15-0001"],"prerequisites":["resource modeling","RPC modeling"],"related_records":["D15-API-B15-0001","D15-API-B15-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B11-0001","D15-API-B15-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D15-API-B11-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API architecture practice","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Interaction Model Selection","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Supports comparison of resource-oriented and operation-oriented API modeling."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B16-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Error Semantics","concept":"HTTP Error Classification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should an API classify HTTP failures?","answer":"An API should distinguish failures according to HTTP semantics and application meaning, using appropriate status-code classes and a stable machine-readable error representation.","explanation":"A client needs enough structured information to determine whether it should correct a request, authenticate, authorize, retry, or escalate an unexpected server failure.","principle":"Error responses should communicate protocol-level outcome separately from detailed application diagnostics.","correct_example":"Return 400-class status with a structured validation problem when client input violates the API contract.","invalid_example":"Return HTTP 200 with an error object for all application failures.","problem":"Clients cannot rely on HTTP semantics to distinguish successful and failed operations.","failure_mode":"Generic client logic retries, accepts, or logs failures incorrectly.","cause":"Application error data replaced protocol-level status semantics.","solution":"Use appropriate HTTP status codes and a structured error schema.","alternative":"Domain-specific error codes may be included as extensions to the structured error response.","tradeoff":"Detailed error structures improve automation but require stable schema governance.","constraint":"Do not expose internal stack traces or implementation secrets as client-facing error details.","edge_case":"A request can be syntactically valid but semantically invalid for current application state.","security_implication":"Error responses must minimize information disclosure while retaining actionable client-level information.","performance_implication":"Structured error generation should remain lightweight and should not require expensive diagnostic processing in normal paths.","testing_guidance":"Test malformed requests, validation failures, authentication failures, authorization failures, conflicts, not-found cases, rate limits, and server failures.","verification_guidance":"Map every documented failure condition to an explicit status and error schema.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110; RFC 9457","version_range":"RFC 9457+","dependencies":["D15-API-B14-0001"],"prerequisites":["HTTP status codes"],"related_records":["D15-API-B16-0002","D15-API-B17-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B14-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"15","relevance":"Defines HTTP status semantics."},{"source":"RFC 9457","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"Problem Details for HTTP APIs","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9457","section":"3","relevance":"Defines machine-readable HTTP API problem details."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B16-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Problem Details","concept":"Problem Details Media Type","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the standardized problem-details pattern for HTTP APIs?","answer":"RFC 9457 defines a machine-readable problem-details representation for HTTP APIs, including a standard JSON media type of application/problem+json and fields such as type, title, status, detail, and instance, with extension members permitted.","explanation":"Problem Details provides a common envelope for communicating machine-readable error information without requiring every API to invent an unrelated error format.","principle":"Use standardized problem-detail semantics while defining application-specific extensions only where they provide stable client value.","correct_example":"A validation response uses application/problem+json and identifies the problem type plus structured extension data for affected fields.","invalid_example":"Returning a stack trace as the primary client-facing detail field.","problem":"Clients cannot reliably interpret heterogeneous error formats and may receive sensitive internals.","failure_mode":"Automated remediation becomes fragile and security exposure increases.","cause":"Error responses were designed around debugging output instead of a client contract.","solution":"Separate stable client-facing problem information from internal diagnostics.","alternative":"An API may define a domain-specific error envelope when another protocol requires it, while retaining equivalent semantic separation.","tradeoff":"Standardization improves interoperability but does not eliminate the need for domain-specific error definitions.","constraint":"The detail field should not be treated as a machine-readable code and should not expose sensitive implementation details.","edge_case":"A problem type may be generalized while extension fields identify specific validation failures.","security_implication":"Avoid leaking stack traces, SQL fragments, internal paths, credentials, or security-sensitive implementation details.","performance_implication":"Structured errors have modest serialization overhead but support more deterministic client handling.","testing_guidance":"Validate media type, required fields, extension schema, status consistency, and absence of sensitive diagnostic leakage.","verification_guidance":"Verify error payloads against RFC 9457 semantics and the API's published problem-type definitions.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9457","version_range":"RFC 9457+","dependencies":["D15-API-B16-0001"],"prerequisites":["HTTP error semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B16-0001","D15-API-B17-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B16-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9457","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"Problem Details for HTTP APIs","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9457","section":"3.1; 3.2","relevance":"Defines problem-details object and application/problem+json."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B17-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Validation","concept":"Boundary Validation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should API inputs be validated at the API boundary?","answer":"API boundary validation establishes that incoming data satisfies the syntactic and structural requirements of the contract before it enters deeper application logic.","explanation":"Early validation prevents malformed inputs from propagating into domain, persistence, or integration layers. It does not replace domain-level validation of business invariants.","principle":"Separate transport/schema validation from domain invariant validation.","correct_example":"Reject a request because a required field has the wrong type before invoking business logic, then separately enforce domain rules inside the domain layer.","invalid_example":"Assume that JSON parsing alone proves that a request is valid for the business domain.","problem":"Structurally valid data can still violate domain invariants.","failure_mode":"Invalid state reaches deeper layers and produces inconsistent failures.","cause":"Transport validation was confused with domain validation.","solution":"Use layered validation: transport/schema constraints at the boundary and domain invariants in the domain layer.","alternative":"Some simple APIs may combine layers when the domain model is intentionally minimal, but the semantic distinction should remain explicit.","tradeoff":"Layered validation improves clarity and failure localization but may introduce repeated checks.","constraint":"Validation rules should have a clear owner to prevent contradictory implementations.","edge_case":"A field can satisfy a schema type while still being invalid due to cross-field or external-state constraints.","security_implication":"Validation reduces attack surface but must not be treated as the only security control.","performance_implication":"Early rejection avoids unnecessary downstream processing; excessively expensive validation can itself become a resource risk.","testing_guidance":"Test missing fields, wrong types, boundary values, malformed encodings, cross-field constraints, and domain-invalid but schema-valid requests.","verification_guidance":"Trace each contract constraint to the layer responsible for enforcing it.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["API contracts","schema validation"],"related_records":["D15-API-B17-0002","D15-API-B18-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API engineering practice","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Boundary and Domain Validation","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines separation between transport validation and domain validation."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B17-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Schema Validation","concept":"Schema As Contract Constraint","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What role does a schema play in an API contract?","answer":"A schema specifies machine-readable structural constraints for data exchanged through an API, such as field types, required members, formats, and structural relationships that the chosen schema language can express.","explanation":"Schemas improve interoperability and enable tooling, validation, generation, and documentation. A schema does not necessarily express every semantic rule or operational guarantee of an API.","principle":"Treat the schema as one component of the API contract rather than the entire contract.","correct_example":"An API schema requires an integer customer_id and a string currency code while separate contract text defines authorization and lifecycle semantics.","invalid_example":"Assuming that a valid schema proves that the operation is authorized or that all business invariants hold.","problem":"Structural validation is mistaken for complete semantic verification.","failure_mode":"Clients receive structurally valid data that violates business or authorization rules.","cause":"The scope of schema validation was overstated.","solution":"Combine schema constraints with explicit behavioral, security, and domain contract rules.","alternative":"Use multiple complementary contract mechanisms when the chosen schema language cannot express required semantics.","tradeoff":"Richer schemas improve machine validation but can become difficult to maintain and still cannot encode every operational guarantee.","constraint":"If the specification text and schema disagree, the governing specification rules determine the contract according to the relevant specification.","edge_case":"A schema may permit values that are structurally valid but invalid under external state or authorization conditions.","security_implication":"Schema validation does not replace authentication, authorization, output encoding, or resource-level access control.","performance_implication":"Schema validation adds processing cost proportional to payload complexity and implementation.","testing_guidance":"Test valid, invalid, boundary, missing, additional, and nested data against the published schema.","verification_guidance":"Verify schema/spec consistency and test representative payloads using the intended validator.","language":"API Design","framework":"OpenAPI","version":"OpenAPI 3.x","version_range":"OpenAPI 3.0+","dependencies":["D15-API-B17-0001"],"prerequisites":["API contract","schema concepts"],"related_records":["D15-API-B18-0002","D15-API-B20-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B17-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenAPI Specification","source_type":"OPEN_STANDARD","title":"OpenAPI Specification","publisher":"OpenAPI Initiative","version":"3.x","section":"Specification and Schema Object guidance","relevance":"Defines machine-readable API descriptions and schema-based constraints."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B18-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Validation Failure Handling","concept":"Deterministic Validation Errors","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should validation failures be represented consistently?","answer":"Validation failures should use a stable HTTP status and structured machine-readable problem representation that identifies the applicable contract problem and, when appropriate, structured information about affected fields.","explanation":"Consistent validation errors allow clients to correct requests programmatically without parsing arbitrary human-readable messages.","principle":"Keep machine-readable error identifiers stable and treat human-readable detail as explanatory rather than as a parsing contract.","correct_example":"A validation problem identifies an invalid field through a documented extension member while using a stable problem type.","invalid_example":"Clients parse the English sentence in the detail field to determine which field failed.","problem":"Client behavior becomes coupled to wording and localization.","failure_mode":"Minor message changes break automated client logic.","cause":"Human-readable diagnostics were used as machine protocol fields.","solution":"Define stable error/problem types and structured field-level error data.","alternative":"A documented array of validation violations can be used as an API-specific extension to problem details.","tradeoff":"Structured violations improve client usability but increase schema and compatibility responsibilities.","constraint":"The error schema should define whether multiple violations are reported and how their locations are represented.","edge_case":"One invalid field may cause multiple dependent validation failures; deduplication or precedence rules may be needed.","security_implication":"Validation messages must not expose sensitive server-side values or internal implementation details.","performance_implication":"Reporting many violations can increase response size and processing cost.","testing_guidance":"Test deterministic error codes, field locations, multiple violations, malformed requests, and localization independence.","verification_guidance":"Verify that clients can handle validation failures without parsing free-form detail text.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9457","version_range":"RFC 9457+","dependencies":["D15-API-B16-0002","D15-API-B17-0001"],"prerequisites":["problem details","boundary validation"],"related_records":["D15-API-B18-0002","D15-API-B19-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B16-0002","D15-API-B17-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9457","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"Problem Details for HTTP APIs","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9457","section":"3","relevance":"Defines structured problem details and guidance for client-facing error information."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B18-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Contract Validation","concept":"Specification Validation Versus Runtime Validation","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between specification validation and runtime API validation?","answer":"Specification validation checks whether an API description is internally valid and consistent with its specification, while runtime validation checks actual requests and responses against the implemented contract and behavior.","explanation":"A syntactically valid API description does not prove that an implementation behaves according to it.","principle":"Treat specification correctness and implementation conformance as separate verification targets.","correct_example":"Validate an OpenAPI document and separately send representative requests to verify that the implementation matches documented schemas and status behavior.","invalid_example":"Assume an API is compliant merely because its OpenAPI file parses successfully.","problem":"Documentation correctness is confused with implementation correctness.","failure_mode":"Clients encounter undocumented runtime behavior despite a valid specification document.","cause":"Only the description artifact was validated.","solution":"Run specification linting plus contract and integration tests against the implementation.","alternative":"Consumer-driven contract testing can provide additional implementation-conformance evidence.","tradeoff":"Runtime contract testing requires maintained test scenarios but provides stronger implementation evidence.","constraint":"Tests must be versioned with the contract they verify.","edge_case":"An API gateway may transform requests or responses, so validation may need to cover both edge and service boundaries.","security_implication":"Security requirements such as authorization cannot be proven by schema validation alone.","performance_implication":"Runtime contract suites add test execution cost but can detect integration regressions.","testing_guidance":"Use specification linting, schema validation, positive/negative contract tests, and compatibility tests.","verification_guidance":"Record which contract claims were statically checked and which were runtime-tested.","language":"API Design","framework":"OpenAPI","version":"OpenAPI 3.x","version_range":"OpenAPI 3.0+","dependencies":["D15-API-B17-0002"],"prerequisites":["API specification","runtime testing"],"related_records":["D15-API-B20-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B17-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenAPI Specification","source_type":"OPEN_STANDARD","title":"OpenAPI Specification","publisher":"OpenAPI Initiative","version":"3.x","section":"Specification","relevance":"Defines the API description format whose validity is distinct from implementation behavior."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B19-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Contract Design","concept":"Contract Completeness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should a production API contract describe beyond endpoint paths?","answer":"A sufficiently complete API contract should define operation semantics, parameters, request schemas, response schemas, status codes, error behavior, authentication and authorization expectations, content types, compatibility requirements, and relevant behavioral guarantees.","explanation":"An endpoint path alone does not provide enough information for independent clients and services to implement interoperable behavior.","principle":"A contract should describe observable behavior that clients are expected to rely upon.","correct_example":"A contract specifies request structure, success and failure responses, authorization requirements, idempotency semantics, and versioning rules.","invalid_example":"Document only GET /orders/{id} without describing parameters, response structure, error cases, or authorization requirements.","problem":"Clients must infer critical behavior.","failure_mode":"Different clients implement incompatible assumptions.","cause":"The contract focused on routing rather than observable behavior.","solution":"Define the full observable interaction contract and maintain it as a versioned artifact.","alternative":"Separate specifications can describe transport, security, and domain semantics when combined through a controlled contract process.","tradeoff":"More complete contracts improve interoperability but require stronger maintenance discipline.","constraint":"Do not document guarantees that the implementation cannot reliably satisfy.","edge_case":"Some operational guarantees, such as latency objectives, may belong in separate service-level agreements rather than the functional API schema.","security_implication":"Authentication and authorization requirements must be explicit and must not be inferred solely from endpoint names.","performance_implication":"Payload limits, pagination behavior, and timeout expectations may need contract-level documentation for predictable client behavior.","testing_guidance":"Generate contract tests from documented success and failure cases.","verification_guidance":"Compare documented behavior with implementation and integration tests before publishing a contract version.","language":"API Design","framework":"OpenAPI","version":"OpenAPI 3.x","version_range":"OpenAPI 3.0+","dependencies":["D15-API-B18-0002"],"prerequisites":["API contracts","HTTP semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B19-0002","D15-API-B20-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B18-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenAPI Specification","source_type":"OPEN_STANDARD","title":"OpenAPI Specification","publisher":"OpenAPI Initiative","version":"3.x","section":"Paths, Operations, Parameters, Responses, Security","relevance":"Provides standardized constructs for describing observable API behavior."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B19-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Contract Evolution","concept":"Observable Contract Boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is an observable contract boundary?","answer":"An observable contract boundary consists of all externally visible API behavior that clients may reasonably depend on, including schemas, status codes, headers, error structures, method semantics, and documented behavioral guarantees.","explanation":"Changing any observable contract element can affect clients even when the server's internal implementation remains unchanged.","principle":"Compatibility analysis must examine observable behavior rather than only source-code signatures.","correct_example":"Changing a documented response field from required to absent is evaluated as a contract change even if the internal model remains unchanged.","invalid_example":"Treating an API change as backward compatible merely because server source code still compiles.","problem":"Internal implementation success is mistaken for client compatibility.","failure_mode":"Existing clients break after apparently harmless API changes.","cause":"Compatibility was assessed only at implementation level.","solution":"Define and test the externally observable contract boundary.","alternative":"Consumer-driven contracts can capture client-observed behavior for selected integrations.","tradeoff":"Broader observable-contract tracking increases governance effort but reduces compatibility surprises.","constraint":"Undocumented implementation details should not accidentally become relied-upon contract guarantees.","edge_case":"Clients may depend on undocumented behavior; this creates compatibility risk even if the behavior was never intended as public contract.","security_implication":"Security headers and error behavior can also be observable contract elements and require compatibility review.","performance_implication":"Payload-size and pagination behavior are observable and can affect client resource consumption.","testing_guidance":"Use compatibility tests against representative client expectations.","verification_guidance":"Review changes against the published contract and compatibility policy before release.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B19-0001"],"prerequisites":["API contract","compatibility"],"related_records":["D15-API-B20-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B19-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API compatibility engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Observable API Contract","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines the externally visible compatibility boundary."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B20-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"API Quality Gate","concept":"Foundational API Design Quality Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What minimum checks should an API pass before foundational design is considered complete?","answer":"The API should have explicit resource or operation semantics, correct HTTP method usage, appropriate status codes, defined request and response schemas, deterministic validation behavior, structured errors, documented observable contract behavior, and explicit security assumptions.","explanation":"Foundational correctness should be established before adding advanced concerns such as versioning, pagination, rate limiting, and distributed API behavior.","principle":"Verify semantic correctness before optimizing or expanding the API surface.","correct_example":"A contract review confirms method semantics, status mapping, schema constraints, validation ownership, error representation, and observable behavior before implementation expansion.","invalid_example":"Proceeding to API scaling features while method semantics and error contracts remain undefined.","problem":"Advanced infrastructure is built on an unstable contract.","failure_mode":"Later changes propagate across clients, gateways, tests, and integrations.","cause":"Foundational contract decisions were deferred.","solution":"Apply a design quality gate covering semantics, contract completeness, validation, errors, and security boundaries.","alternative":"An incremental API can publish a deliberately limited contract, provided unsupported behavior is explicitly marked and controlled.","tradeoff":"Early governance adds design effort but reduces downstream contract instability.","constraint":"A quality gate must distinguish documented design from runtime verification.","edge_case":"A prototype may intentionally lack production guarantees; its status must remain clearly identified as non-production.","security_implication":"Authentication, authorization, data exposure, and error disclosure must be reviewed before publication.","performance_implication":"Payload and interaction design should avoid known unbounded behavior even before detailed performance testing.","testing_guidance":"Create contract, negative, boundary, security, and compatibility tests for each published operation.","verification_guidance":"Record evidence separately for specification validation, runtime tests, and production observations.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B19-0001","D15-API-B16-0002"],"prerequisites":["API contracts","HTTP semantics","validation","error semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B20-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B19-0001","D15-API-B16-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API engineering quality practice","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Design Quality Gate","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines foundational API design verification dimensions."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B20-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"B01-B20 Synthesis","concept":"API Contract Failure Taxonomy","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What are the major foundational API contract failure categories?","answer":"Foundational API failures include ambiguous resource or operation semantics, incorrect HTTP method usage, misleading status codes, incomplete schemas, insufficient validation, unstable error structures, undocumented observable behavior, and unverified implementation-contract divergence.","explanation":"These failure categories represent contract-level defects that can propagate into clients, tests, integrations, and operational tooling.","principle":"Diagnose API failures by contract layer before diagnosing implementation details.","correct_example":"A client interprets 202 as completion; investigation finds that the API contract incorrectly documented an asynchronous operation as synchronous.","invalid_example":"Treat every client failure as a networking problem without checking contract semantics.","problem":"Contract defects are misclassified as implementation or transport defects.","failure_mode":"Repeated client-side workarounds accumulate around an unstable API.","cause":"No explicit contract-level failure taxonomy or verification process exists.","solution":"Classify failures by semantics, schema, validation, status, error representation, and runtime conformance.","alternative":"A domain-specific taxonomy can add business-specific failure categories while retaining the foundational categories.","tradeoff":"A formal taxonomy improves diagnosis but requires consistent terminology and ownership.","constraint":"Failure classification should be evidence-driven and should distinguish observed failures from hypothetical risks.","edge_case":"Multiple contract defects can interact, so root-cause analysis should identify the earliest contract divergence rather than only downstream symptoms.","security_implication":"Security failures must be separately classified when authorization, authentication, data exposure, or error disclosure is involved.","performance_implication":"Contract defects involving unbounded payloads or interactions can manifest as performance failures and should be analyzed separately from measured performance.","testing_guidance":"Map each failure category to at least one negative or compatibility test.","verification_guidance":"Use contract evidence, runtime observations, and test results to classify failures; do not infer execution from static documentation alone.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B20-0001"],"prerequisites":["API quality gates","contract verification"],"related_records":["D15-API-B20-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B20-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API engineering practice","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Contract Failure Taxonomy","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Provides a structured approach to foundational API contract failures."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

