{"record_id":"D15-API-B21-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Contract Versioning","concept":"API Versioning Purpose","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is API versioning used?","answer":"API versioning provides a controlled mechanism for evolving an API contract when changes cannot be introduced without unacceptable impact to existing consumers.","explanation":"Versioning is a compatibility management mechanism, not merely a numbering convention. The need for a new version depends on the compatibility policy and the nature of the contract change.","principle":"Version only when the compatibility boundary requires a distinct contract evolution path.","correct_example":"A breaking representation change is introduced under an explicitly governed new API contract version while the existing version remains supported for its documented lifecycle.","invalid_example":"Create a new version for every internal implementation change that has no observable client impact.","problem":"Unnecessary versions increase maintenance and migration complexity.","failure_mode":"Multiple nearly identical API versions accumulate without meaningful compatibility differences.","cause":"Versioning is treated as a release-labeling mechanism instead of a contract strategy.","solution":"Define explicit compatibility rules and version only externally meaningful breaking changes when required.","alternative":"Backward-compatible evolution can avoid a new version for compatible changes.","tradeoff":"Versioning can isolate breaking changes but increases testing, documentation, deployment, and support burden.","constraint":"Each supported version requires a clear lifecycle, compatibility policy, and deprecation strategy.","edge_case":"A security or regulatory change may require behavior changes that are technically incompatible even when the representation appears unchanged.","security_implication":"Deprecated versions can preserve vulnerable behavior; version lifecycle must include security support decisions.","performance_implication":"Supporting multiple versions can increase deployment size, test matrix size, and operational complexity.","testing_guidance":"Maintain compatibility tests independently for each supported contract version.","verification_guidance":"Classify proposed changes as compatible, conditionally compatible, or breaking before assigning a version.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B19-0002"],"prerequisites":["observable contract","compatibility"],"related_records":["D15-API-B21-0002","D15-API-B22-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B19-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API compatibility engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Versioning Strategy","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines versioning as controlled contract evolution."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B21-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Compatibility","concept":"Breaking Versus Compatible API Changes","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What distinguishes a breaking API change from a compatible API change?","answer":"A breaking change invalidates assumptions made by existing conforming clients, while a compatible change preserves the documented behavior those clients rely upon.","explanation":"Compatibility must be evaluated from the consumer's observable contract rather than solely from server implementation changes.","principle":"Judge compatibility against documented and intentionally supported client behavior.","correct_example":"Adding an optional response field can be compatible when clients tolerate unknown fields and the contract permits it.","invalid_example":"Removing a required response field while calling the change compatible because the server implementation remains internally functional.","problem":"Client compatibility is evaluated from the provider perspective instead of the consumer perspective.","failure_mode":"Existing clients fail after deployment of an apparently minor change.","cause":"The observable contract was not used as the compatibility boundary.","solution":"Maintain explicit compatibility rules for request fields, response fields, status codes, errors, headers, and behavioral semantics.","alternative":"Introduce a new contract version when the breaking behavior cannot be migrated safely within the existing version.","tradeoff":"Strict compatibility reduces consumer breakage but can constrain provider evolution.","constraint":"Compatibility rules must be documented and tested.","edge_case":"Adding enum values can break clients that incorrectly assume an exhaustive closed set; the contract should define extensibility expectations.","security_implication":"A technically compatible change can still alter security behavior and therefore requires security-specific compatibility review.","performance_implication":"A compatible payload extension can still affect clients that have strict size or parsing assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Run consumer compatibility tests against both existing and proposed contract behavior.","verification_guidance":"Use a documented change-classification procedure before release.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B21-0001"],"prerequisites":["API contract","observable behavior"],"related_records":["D15-API-B22-0001","D15-API-B23-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B21-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API compatibility engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Breaking and Compatible API Changes","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines consumer-oriented compatibility analysis."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B22-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Versioning Strategies","concept":"URI Versioning","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is URI-based API versioning?","answer":"URI-based versioning identifies a contract version through the request target, commonly by including a version component in the path.","explanation":"URI versioning makes the selected contract version visible in the request target and can simplify routing and operational inspection.","principle":"If URI versioning is chosen, version identifiers should represent contract versions rather than implementation releases.","correct_example":"GET /v2/customers/123 selects the documented version 2 customer contract.","invalid_example":"Increment /v2/ for an internal refactor that produces no observable contract change.","problem":"Version identifiers become coupled to implementation release frequency.","failure_mode":"Clients face unnecessary migrations between functionally equivalent versions.","cause":"The version identifier is used as a software release counter.","solution":"Define explicit contract-version criteria and lifecycle rules.","alternative":"Header-based or media-type-based version negotiation can keep version information outside the URI.","tradeoff":"URI versioning is explicit and operationally simple but makes versioning visible in resource references.","constraint":"The API must define how links, documentation, caches, gateways, and client SDKs handle multiple URI versions.","edge_case":"A resource may have stable identity while its representation varies by contract version.","security_implication":"Authorization policy must not accidentally differ between versions unless intentionally governed.","performance_implication":"Multiple URI versions can increase routing and deployment complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test routing, resource identity, response schemas, errors, and compatibility independently for each supported URI version.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the URI-selected version maps deterministically to one published contract.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B21-0001"],"prerequisites":["API versioning"],"related_records":["D15-API-B22-0002","D15-API-B23-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B21-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D15-API-B22-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API versioning engineering practice","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"URI-Based API Versioning","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes URI-based contract version selection."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B22-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Versioning Strategies","concept":"Header And Media-Type Versioning","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How can headers or media types be used to select an API contract version?","answer":"A client can communicate a desired representation or contract version through request headers or media-type parameters, allowing the request target to remain independent of the selected representation contract.","explanation":"Content negotiation mechanisms can separate resource identity from representation selection, but they require clear negotiation rules and operational tooling.","principle":"Use headers or media types only when clients, gateways, documentation, and observability can reliably preserve and expose the negotiated contract version.","correct_example":"A client uses an explicitly documented media type to request a supported representation version.","invalid_example":"Silently changing response semantics based on an undocumented custom header.","problem":"Version negotiation becomes invisible and difficult to diagnose.","failure_mode":"Clients receive unexpected representations because intermediary or application behavior is unclear.","cause":"Negotiation rules were not treated as part of the public contract.","solution":"Document accepted media types, version-selection precedence, fallback behavior, and failure semantics.","alternative":"Use URI versioning when explicit routing and operational visibility are higher priorities.","tradeoff":"Header/media-type versioning can preserve stable resource URLs but increases negotiation complexity.","constraint":"The version-selection mechanism must be deterministic and observable.","edge_case":"An intermediary may cache responses differently depending on request headers, requiring correct cache variation semantics.","security_implication":"Do not use hidden version headers to bypass security controls or select weaker authorization behavior.","performance_implication":"Negotiation can add processing and cache-key complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test explicit version requests, unsupported versions, fallback behavior, cache variation, and intermediary behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the selected representation always corresponds to the documented negotiation rules.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"HTTP Semantics","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B22-0001"],"prerequisites":["HTTP content negotiation","API versioning"],"related_records":["D15-API-B22-0001","D15-API-B23-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B22-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D15-API-B22-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"12","relevance":"Defines HTTP content negotiation semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B23-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Schema Evolution","concept":"Additive Contract Evolution","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What is additive API evolution?","answer":"Additive evolution introduces new optional capabilities or representations without invalidating existing conforming client behavior, subject to the API's explicit compatibility rules.","explanation":"Additive changes are often easier to deploy incrementally than breaking replacements, but compatibility depends on client parsing and semantic assumptions.","principle":"Add new behavior only when existing clients can safely ignore or tolerate it according to the contract.","correct_example":"Add an optional response property under a contract that permits unknown properties and where clients do not require an exhaustive response shape.","invalid_example":"Add a new required request field without a default or migration path and classify it as additive.","problem":"A nominally additive change creates a new client obligation.","failure_mode":"Existing clients begin receiving validation failures.","cause":"The provider evaluated only the presence of a new field rather than its compatibility impact.","solution":"Classify additions according to actual request/response compatibility semantics.","alternative":"Introduce the field as optional first and make it required only under a separately governed compatibility transition.","tradeoff":"Incremental evolution reduces immediate breakage but may temporarily support multiple contract states.","constraint":"Compatibility behavior must be explicitly defined for unknown fields and enum values.","edge_case":"Clients generated from strict schemas may reject additional fields even when the protocol technically permits them.","security_implication":"New optional fields can unintentionally expose sensitive information to existing consumers.","performance_implication":"Additional fields can increase payload size and processing requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test existing clients against responses containing newly added fields.","verification_guidance":"Verify additive changes against real contract assumptions, not only schema syntax.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B21-0002"],"prerequisites":["compatibility","schema evolution"],"related_records":["D15-API-B23-0002","D15-API-B24-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B21-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API compatibility engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Additive API Evolution","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines compatibility-aware additive changes."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B23-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Schema Evolution","concept":"Required Field Expansion Risk","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can adding a required request field break existing clients?","answer":"Existing clients that do not send the new required field can fail validation after the change, making the provider's previously valid request contract invalid for those clients.","explanation":"Changing the requiredness of a request property modifies the obligations of existing consumers.","principle":"Do not increase client obligations inside a compatibility-preserving contract without an explicit migration strategy.","correct_example":"Introduce a new field as optional while defining a controlled transition before requiring it under a new contract version.","invalid_example":"Immediately mark a newly introduced request field as required for all clients of an existing version.","problem":"Existing requests become invalid.","failure_mode":"Client requests receive validation errors after deployment.","cause":"Provider changed request requirements without compatibility analysis.","solution":"Use optional introduction, default semantics where appropriate, or a new contract version.","alternative":"Derive the new value server-side when a safe deterministic default exists.","tradeoff":"Defaults reduce client migration burden but can hide missing intent if the value is semantically important.","constraint":"A default must not silently change business meaning or security behavior.","edge_case":"A field may be technically optional but semantically required for certain operation modes.","security_implication":"Defaults involving permissions, identities, or security-sensitive configuration require explicit review.","performance_implication":"Server-side derivation can add computation or external lookups.","testing_guidance":"Run existing client request suites against the changed contract.","verification_guidance":"Verify that all previously valid conforming requests remain valid when compatibility is promised.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B23-0001"],"prerequisites":["request schemas","compatibility"],"related_records":["D15-API-B23-0001","D15-API-B24-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B23-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API compatibility engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Required Request Field Compatibility","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes compatibility risk from increasing client obligations."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B24-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Representation Design","concept":"Representation Shape Stability","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should API representation shapes be deliberately designed?","answer":"Representation shape is part of the client-visible contract and should remain stable enough for supported consumers to parse and interpret responses predictably.","explanation":"Fields, nesting, types, nullability, semantics, and relationships all influence client compatibility.","principle":"Treat response representations as contracts rather than direct serialization of internal objects.","correct_example":"A stable response DTO defines intentional fields and semantics independently of the internal database model.","invalid_example":"Serialize the internal ORM object directly and allow database refactors to alter the public response shape.","problem":"Internal implementation changes unintentionally become public API changes.","failure_mode":"Clients break when internal models are renamed, nested, or reorganized.","cause":"Public and internal data models were coupled.","solution":"Define explicit API representations and map internal models to them.","alternative":"A deliberately schema-driven serialization layer can enforce the same boundary.","tradeoff":"Explicit representations require mapping code but provide stronger compatibility control.","constraint":"Representation semantics must be documented, including nullability and omission rules.","edge_case":"A field may be omitted when unavailable versus explicitly represented as null; clients need deterministic semantics.","security_implication":"Explicit response models reduce accidental exposure of internal or sensitive fields.","performance_implication":"Mapping representations can add processing but can also reduce payload size by excluding irrelevant fields.","testing_guidance":"Snapshot or schema-test representative responses and verify sensitive-field exclusion.","verification_guidance":"Compare implementation responses with the published representation schema.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B17-0002"],"prerequisites":["API schemas","contract boundaries"],"related_records":["D15-API-B24-0002","D15-API-B25-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B17-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API contract engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Stable API Representations","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines deliberate representation boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B24-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Representation Semantics","concept":"Null Versus Missing Field Semantics","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must an API distinguish between a missing field and an explicit null when relevant?","answer":"A missing field can mean that a value is not supplied, not applicable, unavailable, or intentionally omitted, while an explicit null can represent a defined absence of value. The contract must specify the intended meaning.","explanation":"Ambiguous nullability and omission semantics force clients to infer meaning and can cause incorrect updates or display behavior.","principle":"Define omission and null semantics explicitly whenever they affect client behavior.","correct_example":"PATCH semantics document whether omission means no change and null means clear the field.","invalid_example":"Allow both missing and null values without defining whether they have the same effect.","problem":"Clients cannot reliably distinguish no-op, clear, unknown, and unavailable states.","failure_mode":"Updates unintentionally erase values or fail to apply intended changes.","cause":"Representation semantics were left implicit.","solution":"Document field presence, nullability, default behavior, and update semantics.","alternative":"Use explicit state fields when nullability cannot express the required domain states clearly.","tradeoff":"Explicit state modeling increases schema complexity but reduces semantic ambiguity.","constraint":"The same field should not silently change null/omission meaning between versions.","edge_case":"Partial updates often require a three-state model: omitted, explicit null, and concrete value.","security_implication":"Null or omission must not accidentally bypass authorization-required fields or validation constraints.","performance_implication":"Explicit state representation may slightly increase payload size but reduces repeated corrective requests.","testing_guidance":"Test omitted, null, empty, and concrete values independently for create, update, and patch operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that implementation behavior matches the documented field-state semantics.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B24-0001"],"prerequisites":["representation schemas","update semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B23-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B24-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API contract engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Field Presence and Nullability Semantics","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines the need for deterministic missing/null semantics."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B25-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Request Design","concept":"Request Payload Boundaries","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should API request payload boundaries be designed?","answer":"Request payloads should contain only data required to express the operation, with explicit size, type, structural, and semantic constraints where relevant.","explanation":"Unbounded or overly broad request bodies increase ambiguity, attack surface, processing cost, and compatibility risk.","principle":"Constrain request inputs to the minimum contract required for the operation.","correct_example":"A user creation request accepts only documented identity and profile fields rather than arbitrary database attributes.","invalid_example":"Accept an arbitrary JSON object and persist every supplied property without a defined schema.","problem":"The API accepts uncontrolled input and creates implicit behavior.","failure_mode":"Unexpected fields alter state, increase resource consumption, or become accidental contract surface.","cause":"Request schema boundaries were not explicitly defined.","solution":"Use explicit schemas, field constraints, size limits, and controlled extension mechanisms.","alternative":"A documented extension object can isolate intentionally extensible metadata from core fields.","tradeoff":"Strict schemas improve control but can make extensibility more deliberate and slower.","constraint":"Limits must be aligned with legitimate business payload requirements.","edge_case":"Large legitimate documents may require upload-specific APIs rather than ordinary JSON endpoints.","security_implication":"Input limits and schema validation reduce malformed-input and resource-exhaustion risks but do not replace authorization.","performance_implication":"Payload size directly affects bandwidth, parsing, memory, and processing costs.","testing_guidance":"Test minimum, maximum, oversized, malformed, nested, and unexpected-field payloads.","verification_guidance":"Verify that undocumented fields are handled according to the contract rather than accidentally persisted.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B17-0001"],"prerequisites":["request validation","schema design"],"related_records":["D15-API-B25-0002","D15-API-B26-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B17-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security and contract engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Request Boundary Design","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines constrained request boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B25-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Response Design","concept":"Minimum Sufficient Representation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the minimum sufficient representation pattern?","answer":"A response should expose the information required by supported client use cases without unnecessarily exposing unrelated internal data or creating excessive payload size.","explanation":"Public representations should be designed around client contract needs rather than mirroring complete internal entities.","principle":"Expose intentional contract data rather than defaulting to full internal object serialization.","correct_example":"An order response exposes order identity, status, totals, and documented customer-facing fields while excluding internal database metadata.","invalid_example":"Return every internal column because serialization is easier than defining a response model.","problem":"The API exposes accidental contract surface and unnecessary data.","failure_mode":"Internal changes become breaking changes and sensitive fields may leak.","cause":"Response design was delegated entirely to internal serialization.","solution":"Define explicit response schemas and field ownership.","alternative":"Use projections or documented sparse-field mechanisms when clients legitimately need variable representations.","tradeoff":"Smaller representations improve efficiency but may require additional endpoint or projection design.","constraint":"Removing an already published field can still be a breaking change.","edge_case":"Different clients may require different subsets of a large representation.","security_implication":"Data minimization reduces accidental information disclosure.","performance_implication":"Smaller responses reduce serialization, network, and client parsing costs.","testing_guidance":"Verify required fields, prohibited fields, payload size boundaries, and authorization-dependent fields.","verification_guidance":"Compare response payloads against the published schema and data-classification policy.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B24-0001"],"prerequisites":["representation design"],"related_records":["D15-API-B26-0001","D15-API-B27-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B24-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API contract and data minimization practice","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Minimum Sufficient API Representation","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines deliberate response representation boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B26-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Content Negotiation","concept":"Media Type Contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What role does a media type play in an HTTP API contract?","answer":"A media type identifies the representation format of a message and can communicate how the enclosed content should be interpreted.","explanation":"Content-Type describes the representation sent in a message, while Accept communicates representation preferences for responses.","principle":"Define representation media types explicitly and do not assume that JSON alone determines the complete representation contract.","correct_example":"A JSON request declares an appropriate Content-Type and the server validates the representation according to the documented schema.","invalid_example":"Send a JSON body without declaring or documenting its media type and require clients to infer interpretation.","problem":"Clients and intermediaries cannot reliably determine representation semantics.","failure_mode":"Parsing, negotiation, or validation behavior differs across consumers.","cause":"Representation metadata was treated as optional documentation rather than protocol information.","solution":"Define supported media types and request/response negotiation behavior.","alternative":"A single fixed media type can be used for a narrow API if its semantics are explicitly defined.","tradeoff":"Multiple media types increase flexibility but also increase testing and compatibility complexity.","constraint":"Unsupported media types should produce behavior consistent with the API's documented contract.","edge_case":"A request may use a valid media type but contain a representation that violates the API schema.","security_implication":"Content-type confusion can create parsing and security vulnerabilities; parsers should match the declared representation.","performance_implication":"Negotiation and alternate representations can increase serialization and caching complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test Content-Type, Accept, unsupported media types, malformed representations, and negotiation outcomes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that message representation and declared media type remain consistent.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["HTTP representations"],"related_records":["D15-API-B26-0002"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"8; 12","relevance":"Defines representation metadata and content negotiation."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B26-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Content Negotiation","concept":"Accept Versus Content-Type","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between Accept and Content-Type in an HTTP API?","answer":"Content-Type describes the media type of the representation carried in the message, while Accept communicates media types that the client can accept in a response.","explanation":"Confusing these headers can lead to incorrect request parsing or response negotiation.","principle":"Treat request representation metadata and response preference metadata as distinct concepts.","correct_example":"A client sends Content-Type: application/json for a JSON request and Accept: application/json when requesting a JSON response.","invalid_example":"Use Accept to declare the media type of the request body.","problem":"The server may interpret the wrong metadata as describing the request representation.","failure_mode":"Valid requests are rejected or parsed incorrectly.","cause":"Request representation and response preference were conflated.","solution":"Use each header according to its HTTP semantics and document supported media types.","alternative":"For APIs with one representation format, the distinction may be less visible but remains semantically important.","tradeoff":"Explicit negotiation improves interoperability but adds contract surface.","constraint":"The server's actual behavior must match documented supported media types.","edge_case":"A client can send one representation format while requesting a different response representation.","security_implication":"Incorrect content interpretation can create security-sensitive parsing discrepancies.","performance_implication":"Negotiation can affect serialization path and cache behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test independent combinations of request Content-Type and response Accept values.","verification_guidance":"Verify header semantics against RFC 9110 and runtime behavior.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B26-0001"],"prerequisites":["HTTP representations","content negotiation"],"related_records":["D15-API-B22-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B26-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"8.3; 12.5","relevance":"Defines Content-Type and Accept semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B27-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authentication","concept":"Authentication Boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What should authentication establish in an API?","answer":"Authentication establishes or verifies the identity or security principal associated with an API request according to the selected authentication mechanism.","explanation":"Authentication answers who or what is making the request. It does not by itself establish whether the principal is allowed to perform the requested action.","principle":"Keep identity establishment separate from authorization decisions.","correct_example":"A verified access token identifies a subject and relevant authentication context before authorization evaluates access to a resource.","invalid_example":"Treat possession of a syntactically valid token as automatic permission to access every resource.","problem":"Identity verification is conflated with authorization.","failure_mode":"Authenticated users or services receive access beyond intended permissions.","cause":"No explicit authentication-authorization boundary exists.","solution":"Authenticate the principal, then independently evaluate authorization against resource and action.","alternative":"Some infrastructure may combine these operations internally, but the semantic distinction should remain explicit.","tradeoff":"Separation improves reasoning and policy clarity but requires coordinated security context propagation.","constraint":"Authentication strength and token validity requirements depend on the chosen security protocol and threat model.","edge_case":"A request may be authenticated but lack sufficient authorization for the requested resource.","security_implication":"Authentication mechanisms must protect credentials, tokens, replay resistance, and identity binding according to their protocol.","performance_implication":"Authentication verification can add cryptographic or network overhead, especially for remote identity providers.","testing_guidance":"Test valid, invalid, expired, revoked, malformed, and missing credentials separately from authorization failures.","verification_guidance":"Verify that authentication evidence identifies the intended principal and is validated before authorization.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["API contract","security fundamentals"],"related_records":["D15-API-B27-0002","D15-API-B28-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Authentication and Authorization Boundary","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines authentication as identity establishment distinct from authorization."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B27-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authorization","concept":"Resource And Action Authorization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should authorization evaluate in an API?","answer":"Authorization should determine whether an authenticated principal is permitted to perform the requested action on the target resource within the applicable security and business context.","explanation":"Authorization should not depend solely on endpoint reachability or authentication status.","principle":"Authorize the specific action against the specific resource and relevant context.","correct_example":"The service verifies that the authenticated principal may update order 123 before applying the requested update.","invalid_example":"Allow any authenticated user to update any order because the endpoint requires authentication.","problem":"Authentication is treated as sufficient access control.","failure_mode":"Horizontal or vertical privilege escalation becomes possible.","cause":"Authorization scope is not bound to resource and action.","solution":"Evaluate principal, resource, action, tenant/context, and applicable policy before performing the operation.","alternative":"Centralized policy services can evaluate authorization while resource services enforce the returned decision.","tradeoff":"Centralized policy improves consistency but introduces policy-service dependency and failure semantics.","constraint":"Authorization decisions must fail according to explicitly defined security policy when required evidence is unavailable.","edge_case":"A resource may change ownership or policy between authorization and execution, requiring appropriate concurrency or transactional controls.","security_implication":"Authorization is a primary security boundary and should be enforced server-side rather than trusted to clients.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained authorization can require additional lookups or policy evaluation.","testing_guidance":"Test allowed, denied, cross-tenant, privilege-boundary, stale-context, and missing-policy cases.","verification_guidance":"Trace authorization evidence from authenticated identity through policy decision to protected resource action.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B27-0001"],"prerequisites":["authentication","resource modeling"],"related_records":["D15-API-B28-0001","D15-API-B28-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B27-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Resource and Action Authorization","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines resource-aware authorization semantics."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B28-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authentication And Authorization","concept":"Security Context Propagation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should authenticated identity and authorization context propagate explicitly through API layers?","answer":"Explicit security-context propagation ensures that downstream components receive the identity and authorization evidence required to make or enforce access decisions without silently substituting an unrelated caller identity.","explanation":"In multi-layer APIs, the original request identity can be lost when calls are delegated to internal services.","principle":"Preserve caller identity, delegation context, and authorization boundaries explicitly across service calls.","correct_example":"A downstream service receives verifiable caller identity or delegated credentials and evaluates its own resource authorization.","invalid_example":"A gateway authenticates the caller and every downstream service blindly trusts an internal network address.","problem":"Downstream services cannot independently establish the security context for protected resources.","failure_mode":"Internal compromise or routing errors can bypass intended authorization boundaries.","cause":"Security context was terminated at the gateway.","solution":"Propagate verifiable identity and appropriate authorization context to each protected service boundary.","alternative":"A trusted gateway can perform some centralized enforcement, but protected downstream resources still require clearly defined trust assumptions.","tradeoff":"End-to-end security context improves isolation but adds credential propagation and policy complexity.","constraint":"Delegation must preserve least privilege and prevent confused-deputy behavior.","edge_case":"A service may legitimately act on behalf of another service rather than directly as the original user.","security_implication":"Identity propagation must prevent token substitution, privilege escalation, and unauthorized delegation.","performance_implication":"Additional token validation or policy checks add processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test direct, delegated, missing-context, forged-context, expired-context, and cross-service authorization scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Trace identity and authorization evidence across each service boundary.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B27-0001","D15-API-B27-0002"],"prerequisites":["authentication","authorization","distributed services"],"related_records":["D15-API-B28-0002","D15-API-B29-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B27-0001","D15-API-B27-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security architecture","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Security Context Propagation","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines identity and authorization propagation across API boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B28-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Security Failure Handling","concept":"Authentication Versus Authorization Error Semantics","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should authentication and authorization failures be distinguished?","answer":"Authentication failure means the request lacks acceptable evidence of identity, while authorization failure means the identity is established but the requested action is not permitted under the applicable policy.","explanation":"The distinction supports correct client behavior and clearer security diagnostics without exposing unnecessary sensitive information.","principle":"Model identity establishment and access permission as separate security decisions.","correct_example":"A missing or invalid credential is handled as an authentication failure, while a valid identity lacking access to a resource is handled as an authorization failure according to the API's security contract.","invalid_example":"Return the same successful response for all security failures to hide every distinction without considering the API's documented threat model.","problem":"Clients and operators cannot distinguish credential problems from permission problems.","failure_mode":"Incorrect client remediation and poor security observability.","cause":"Security outcomes were collapsed into one undocumented category.","solution":"Define authentication and authorization failure semantics while minimizing information disclosure.","alternative":"Some security-sensitive systems may intentionally reduce externally observable distinctions while retaining internal classification.","tradeoff":"More explicit distinctions aid clients and operators but can increase information exposure if implemented carelessly.","constraint":"The external response must follow the selected security protocol and API contract.","edge_case":"A resource may intentionally return not-found semantics to avoid revealing its existence to unauthorized callers.","security_implication":"Error semantics should be reviewed for user-enumeration and resource-enumeration risks.","performance_implication":"Separating policy checks may require additional processing but improves diagnostic precision.","testing_guidance":"Test missing, malformed, expired, revoked, authenticated-but-denied, and resource-not-found security cases.","verification_guidance":"Verify both protocol semantics and information-disclosure behavior.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B27-0002"],"prerequisites":["authentication","authorization"],"related_records":["D15-API-B29-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B27-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Authentication and Authorization Failure Semantics","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines distinct identity and permission failure categories."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B29-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Idempotency Foundations","concept":"Application-Level Idempotency Key","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can an application-level idempotency key be useful?","answer":"An application-level idempotency key can allow a server to recognize retries of the same logical operation and avoid unintentionally applying a non-idempotent side effect multiple times.","explanation":"Transport-level method semantics do not automatically make every application operation retry-safe. An idempotency key can associate repeated attempts with one logical operation.","principle":"Make retry identity explicit when duplicate side effects are unacceptable and method semantics alone do not provide the required guarantee.","correct_example":"A payment-creation request includes a unique operation key that the server associates with the resulting operation and enforces according to a documented lifecycle.","invalid_example":"Generate a new random key for every network retry, causing each retry to appear to be a new operation.","problem":"Retries cannot be correlated to the original logical operation.","failure_mode":"Duplicate resources or side effects are created.","cause":"Operation identity was not preserved across retries.","solution":"Persist and validate an idempotency key with defined scope, request equivalence, retention, and replay semantics.","alternative":"Use naturally idempotent resource replacement operations where the domain semantics permit it.","tradeoff":"Idempotency state improves retry safety but introduces storage, expiration, concurrency, and replay-management requirements.","constraint":"The key must be scoped so that unrelated operations cannot collide.","edge_case":"The same key may be reused with different payloads; the API must define whether this is rejected, treated as a conflict, or otherwise handled.","security_implication":"Keys must not themselves become authorization credentials or expose sensitive operation data.","performance_implication":"Idempotency checks add lookup and storage overhead, especially for high-volume operations.","testing_guidance":"Test first request, identical retry, concurrent duplicate requests, conflicting payload reuse, expired key, and failed-operation replay.","verification_guidance":"Verify that duplicate logical operations produce the documented effect and that key lifecycle semantics are enforced.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B12-0002"],"prerequisites":["idempotency","retry semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B29-0002","D15-API-B30-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B12-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Application-Level Idempotency Keys","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines application-level retry deduplication."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B29-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Idempotency Foundations","concept":"Idempotency Key Scope And Replay Semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must an API define for idempotency keys?","answer":"An API using idempotency keys should define key scope, uniqueness expectations, request equivalence rules, retention period, concurrent-request behavior, response replay behavior, and handling of conflicting reuse.","explanation":"An idempotency key is not sufficient by itself; clients need deterministic semantics for retries and the server needs a reliable lifecycle.","principle":"An idempotency mechanism is a contract, not merely a header name.","correct_example":"The contract states that a key is unique per authenticated principal and operation scope, is retained for a documented period, and cannot be reused with a materially different request.","invalid_example":"Accept any key indefinitely without defining ownership, collision behavior, or payload equivalence.","problem":"Different clients and deployments interpret duplicate operations inconsistently.","failure_mode":"Duplicate effects or unexpected conflicts occur during retries.","cause":"Idempotency lifecycle semantics were unspecified.","solution":"Document and test the full key lifecycle and replay contract.","alternative":"Use a server-issued operation identifier when the workflow naturally exposes one.","tradeoff":"More explicit semantics improve reliability but increase contract complexity.","constraint":"Key scope must prevent cross-tenant or cross-operation collisions.","edge_case":"A request may time out after the server commits its effect but before the client receives the response; replay semantics must cover this case.","security_implication":"Idempotency records must be protected against cross-principal access and key guessing attacks.","performance_implication":"Long retention windows increase storage requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrent requests, network timeouts, response loss, key reuse, expiry, and cross-principal collisions.","verification_guidance":"Verify duplicate-effect prevention under realistic failure timing.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B29-0001"],"prerequisites":["application-level idempotency"],"related_records":["D15-API-B30-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B29-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Idempotency Key Contract Semantics","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines lifecycle and replay requirements for application-level idempotency."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B30-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"B21-B30 Synthesis","concept":"API Evolution And Security Decision Framework","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be reviewed before evolving an API contract?","answer":"Review observable compatibility, versioning impact, schema evolution, representation semantics, content negotiation, authentication and authorization behavior, idempotency requirements, security disclosure, and test coverage before approving a contract change.","explanation":"API evolution can alter both functional interoperability and security behavior even when the change appears local.","principle":"Evaluate API changes across compatibility, semantics, security, and operational consequences rather than reviewing only the changed field or endpoint.","correct_example":"A proposed field change is classified for request compatibility, response compatibility, authorization impact, payload implications, and migration requirements before release.","invalid_example":"Approve a contract change solely because automated schema validation succeeds.","problem":"Static schema validity is mistaken for complete compatibility and security validation.","failure_mode":"Clients or security controls fail after deployment.","cause":"Review scope is limited to syntax or implementation compilation.","solution":"Apply a multi-dimensional API change review and record evidence for each affected contract dimension.","alternative":"Automated compatibility tooling can enforce selected rules, supplemented by manual review for semantic and security effects.","tradeoff":"Broader review requires more effort but provides stronger change control.","constraint":"Verification status must distinguish static validation from runtime compatibility evidence.","edge_case":"A change can be structurally compatible but semantically breaking due to changed business meaning.","security_implication":"Security behavior is part of the observable contract and requires explicit review.","performance_implication":"Representation and negotiation changes can alter payload size, parsing, and cache behavior.","testing_guidance":"Run schema, contract, compatibility, negative, security, and regression tests relevant to the changed behavior.","verification_guidance":"Record claim, evidence, method, observed result, limitations, and confidence for each verification claim.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B21-0002","D15-API-B24-0001","D15-API-B29-0001"],"prerequisites":["API versioning","schema evolution","API security","idempotency"],"related_records":["D15-API-B30-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B21-0002","D15-API-B24-0001","D15-API-B29-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API engineering quality practice","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Evolution Decision Framework","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Integrates compatibility, security, representation, and reliability review."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B30-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"B21-B30 Quality Gate","concept":"API Contract Evolution Quality Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What quality gate should govern API contract evolution at this stage?","answer":"A contract evolution should not be considered verified until the change has a documented compatibility classification, updated schema and semantics, explicit versioning decision, security review, relevant idempotency analysis, and corresponding verification evidence.","explanation":"A successful specification parse or unit test alone cannot establish compatibility across independent consumers.","principle":"Do not equate artifact validity with end-to-end contract compatibility.","correct_example":"The release record identifies the changed contract elements, compatibility class, migration path, tests performed, observed results, and unresolved limitations.","invalid_example":"Mark the API change verified because the server starts successfully.","problem":"Verification is claimed without evidence for client-visible behavior.","failure_mode":"Production consumers encounter undocumented incompatibilities.","cause":"Verification criteria were implementation-centric.","solution":"Use a contract-focused release quality gate with explicit evidence categories.","alternative":"For internal experimental APIs, a reduced gate can be used if the API is clearly marked non-production and unsupported.","tradeoff":"A stronger gate increases release discipline but reduces the probability of undocumented contract breakage.","constraint":"Claims must not exceed the evidence actually collected.","edge_case":"Some compatibility claims may remain UNKNOWN until real consumer integration testing occurs.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive contract changes require explicit authorization and information-disclosure verification.","performance_implication":"Performance effects should be marked MEASURED only when actual measurements exist.","testing_guidance":"Require schema validation, contract tests, regression tests, negative tests, and security tests appropriate to the change.","verification_guidance":"Record VERIFIED only when required evidence is available; otherwise retain NOT_VERIFIED or UNKNOWN.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B30-0001"],"prerequisites":["API evolution framework","verification discipline"],"related_records":["D15-API-B30-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B30-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API verification engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Contract Evolution Quality Gate","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines evidence-based API evolution verification."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

