{"record_id":"D15-API-B31-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Error Semantics","concept":"HTTP Status Code Semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should HTTP status codes be selected for API outcomes?","answer":"An API should select a status code that accurately represents the semantics of the completed HTTP request processing outcome according to the HTTP specification and the API contract.","explanation":"Status codes communicate broad outcome classes to clients. Application-specific details should be represented separately rather than inventing status-code meanings.","principle":"Use standardized HTTP status semantics consistently and reserve application-specific information for the response representation.","correct_example":"A successfully created resource uses 201 when the request resulted in resource creation.","invalid_example":"Return 200 for every outcome and encode success or failure only inside an application JSON field.","problem":"Clients lose standardized protocol-level outcome information.","failure_mode":"Generic client libraries, intermediaries, monitoring systems, and application logic cannot reliably classify outcomes.","cause":"Application semantics replaced HTTP semantics.","solution":"Map API outcomes to appropriate standardized status codes and document any domain-specific interpretation.","alternative":"Use a small, consistent status-code vocabulary when the API does not need every available HTTP status.","tradeoff":"More precise status codes improve interoperability but require consistent interpretation.","constraint":"The selected code must remain compatible with the actual request-processing semantics.","edge_case":"A request can be accepted for asynchronous processing without the final operation being complete.","security_implication":"Error responses must avoid leaking authorization-sensitive resource information.","performance_implication":"Status-code classification has negligible computational cost but improves operational observability.","testing_guidance":"Test representative success, client-error, server-error, redirect, and asynchronous outcomes.","verification_guidance":"Compare each documented status mapping against RFC-defined semantics and observed implementation behavior.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["HTTP semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B31-0002","D15-API-B32-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"15","relevance":"Defines HTTP status-code semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B31-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Error Semantics","concept":"Success Status Specificity","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should successful API operations use status codes that reflect their actual semantics?","answer":"Specific success status codes communicate whether a resource was retrieved, created, accepted for processing, or otherwise handled according to defined HTTP semantics.","explanation":"Using one generic success code for every operation weakens protocol-level meaning and forces clients to inspect application payloads for basic outcome classification.","principle":"Preserve meaningful HTTP semantics at the protocol boundary.","correct_example":"Return 201 for a successful resource creation when the operation actually creates a resource.","invalid_example":"Return 200 for resource creation solely because the server returned a JSON response.","problem":"The protocol response does not accurately communicate the operation outcome.","failure_mode":"Clients require endpoint-specific assumptions to determine what happened.","cause":"Status selection was based on implementation convenience.","solution":"Map operation outcomes to documented HTTP semantics.","alternative":"A consistent 200 response may be valid for operations whose defined semantics are ordinary successful processing, but it should not obscure a materially different documented outcome.","tradeoff":"More specific statuses improve semantic clarity while requiring stronger API documentation.","constraint":"Status codes must reflect actual operation semantics rather than desired client behavior.","edge_case":"A successful asynchronous submission may use 202 because completion has not yet occurred.","security_implication":"Status distinctions must not expose unauthorized resource existence unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"No meaningful computational difference is inherent in status selection.","testing_guidance":"Assert status codes independently from response-body assertions.","verification_guidance":"Verify status code, response body, headers, and operation state are semantically consistent.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":["HTTP status semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B31-0001","D15-API-B32-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B31-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"15.3","relevance":"Defines successful-response status semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B32-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Error Semantics","concept":"Client Error Versus Server Error","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the semantic difference between 4xx and 5xx API responses?","answer":"4xx responses indicate that the request cannot or will not be fulfilled because of something attributable to the request, while 5xx responses indicate that the server failed to fulfill an apparently valid request.","explanation":"The distinction helps clients determine whether changing the request may resolve the problem or whether the server-side condition requires recovery or investigation.","principle":"Classify failures according to the semantics of the request and server processing rather than implementation exception names.","correct_example":"A malformed request is represented as a client-side error, while an unexpected server failure is represented as a server-side error.","invalid_example":"Return 500 for every validation failure because the server generated the validation response.","problem":"Client-correctable failures are classified as server failures.","failure_mode":"Clients retry inappropriate requests and operators receive misleading failure metrics.","cause":"Status codes are mapped to implementation exceptions rather than HTTP semantics.","solution":"Define an explicit API error classification policy.","alternative":"Domain-specific error codes can supplement, but should not replace, appropriate HTTP status semantics.","tradeoff":"Precise classification improves automation but requires careful error taxonomy design.","constraint":"Classification must reflect actual request and processing semantics.","edge_case":"A dependency failure may occur while processing an otherwise valid request; the externally visible classification depends on the API's responsibility and processing outcome.","security_implication":"Error details must not reveal internal infrastructure unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"Accurate classification improves operational alerting and retry decisions.","testing_guidance":"Test validation, authentication, authorization, not-found, conflict, rate-limit, and unexpected server-failure paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify status mapping independently of internal exception hierarchy.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B31-0001"],"prerequisites":["HTTP status classes"],"related_records":["D15-API-B32-0002","D15-API-B33-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B31-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"15.5; 15.6","relevance":"Defines client-error and server-error classes."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B32-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Error Semantics","concept":"Validation Error Contract","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What should a validation error contract communicate?","answer":"A validation error contract should identify that the request failed validation and, where safe and useful, provide structured information that allows the client to correct the invalid input.","explanation":"Validation failures are client-actionable when the API communicates which contract constraints were violated without exposing internal implementation details.","principle":"Make client-correctable validation failures structured and actionable.","correct_example":"Return a documented validation error containing machine-readable field or constraint information.","invalid_example":"Return an HTML stack trace or raw framework exception for invalid JSON input.","problem":"Clients cannot reliably determine which contract requirement failed.","failure_mode":"Clients resort to parsing unstable error strings or repeatedly submit invalid requests.","cause":"Validation behavior was not modeled as part of the API contract.","solution":"Define stable validation error structure and field-level semantics.","alternative":"Use RFC 9457 problem details with documented extensions for validation-specific information.","tradeoff":"Detailed validation improves developer experience but must avoid exposing sensitive validation internals.","constraint":"Error structure must remain version-compatible with supported clients.","edge_case":"A single input may violate multiple independent constraints.","security_implication":"Do not disclose sensitive validation rules that enable security-control probing.","performance_implication":"Detailed validation generation adds small processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test missing fields, wrong types, invalid ranges, malformed syntax, multiple simultaneous violations, and unexpected fields.","verification_guidance":"Verify error structure against the published contract rather than only checking status code.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B32-0001"],"prerequisites":["request schemas","validation"],"related_records":["D15-API-B33-0001","D15-API-B34-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B32-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9457","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"Problem Details for HTTP APIs","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9457","section":"3","relevance":"Defines structured problem-detail responses suitable for HTTP API errors."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B33-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Problem Details","concept":"RFC 9457 Problem Details","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the purpose of RFC 9457 problem details?","answer":"RFC 9457 defines a standard format for communicating machine-readable details about HTTP API problems while retaining the HTTP status code as the primary protocol-level outcome.","explanation":"The format provides common members such as type, title, detail, and instance, with support for extension members.","principle":"Use standardized problem representations when structured HTTP API error information is required.","correct_example":"An API returns application/problem+json with a documented problem type and appropriate HTTP status.","invalid_example":"Return a framework stack trace as the public error representation.","problem":"Error responses vary arbitrarily across endpoints and clients cannot process them consistently.","failure_mode":"Client error handling becomes tightly coupled to implementation-specific strings.","cause":"No common problem representation was established.","solution":"Adopt a documented problem-detail contract with stable types and extensions.","alternative":"A custom structured error format can be used when justified, but it should have equally explicit semantics.","tradeoff":"Standardized problem details improve interoperability while still requiring domain-specific type definitions.","constraint":"The error representation must not be treated as a replacement for the HTTP status code.","edge_case":"An API may need extensions for field-level validation, correlation identifiers, or domain-specific metadata.","security_implication":"Problem details must not expose implementation internals or sensitive information.","performance_implication":"Structured errors have small serialization overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test media type, required members, problem type, status consistency, extension fields, and malformed error scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each problem representation conforms to the API contract and RFC 9457 semantics.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9457","version_range":"RFC 9457+","dependencies":["D15-API-B32-0002"],"prerequisites":["HTTP errors","structured error responses"],"related_records":["D15-API-B33-0002","D15-API-B34-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B32-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9457","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"Problem Details for HTTP APIs","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9457","section":"1; 3","relevance":"Defines the standardized problem-details object and application/problem+json representation."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B33-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Problem Details","concept":"Stable Problem Type","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should API problem types have stable semantics?","answer":"A stable problem type allows clients and operators to identify the class of problem without parsing human-oriented detail text.","explanation":"Problem type identifies the semantics of the problem, while detail provides instance-specific information intended for humans or limited client assistance.","principle":"Use stable machine-readable problem types for programmatic classification.","correct_example":"Clients branch on a documented problem type rather than searching the detail string for keywords.","invalid_example":"Clients determine whether a request can be retried by matching arbitrary words inside detail.","problem":"Client behavior becomes dependent on unstable natural-language text.","failure_mode":"Minor wording changes break client error handling.","cause":"Human-readable detail was incorrectly treated as a machine contract.","solution":"Define stable problem types and document their semantics.","alternative":"A documented application error code can supplement problem type when additional classification is needed.","tradeoff":"Stable types improve compatibility but require lifecycle governance.","constraint":"Problem types should not be reused for semantically unrelated failures.","edge_case":"The same high-level type may have extension fields for endpoint-specific context.","security_implication":"Problem type information should not disclose sensitive implementation details.","performance_implication":"Machine-readable classification can reduce repeated client-side text parsing.","testing_guidance":"Verify stable type identifiers and ensure clients do not depend on detail wording.","verification_guidance":"Check that problem type semantics remain consistent across supported versions.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9457","version_range":"RFC 9457+","dependencies":["D15-API-B33-0001"],"prerequisites":["problem details"],"related_records":["D15-API-B33-0001","D15-API-B34-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B33-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9457","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"Problem Details for HTTP APIs","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9457","section":"3.1","relevance":"Defines the problem type member and its machine-readable role."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B34-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Problem Details","concept":"Safe Error Detail","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What information should API error detail expose?","answer":"Error detail should provide useful correction or diagnostic information appropriate to the client while avoiding secrets, stack traces, internal topology, credentials, or other implementation-sensitive information.","explanation":"Error responses cross a trust boundary and may be consumed by untrusted clients. RFC 9457 explicitly cautions against exposing implementation internals through detail.","principle":"Expose enough information to make the error actionable without exposing unnecessary internal state.","correct_example":"Return a documented validation problem explaining that a required field is missing without including internal SQL, file paths, or stack traces.","invalid_example":"Return a database exception including SQL statements, credentials, filesystem paths, and stack traces.","problem":"Internal diagnostic data crosses the API trust boundary.","failure_mode":"Attackers obtain implementation details or sensitive information through ordinary error handling.","cause":"Internal exceptions were serialized directly into public responses.","solution":"Map internal failures to controlled external problem representations and keep detailed diagnostics in protected observability systems.","alternative":"Expose a correlation identifier so authorized operators can locate internal diagnostics without returning them publicly.","tradeoff":"Less public detail improves security but may require stronger developer tooling and support workflows.","constraint":"Error information must follow data-classification and privacy requirements.","edge_case":"A multi-tenant API must avoid including another tenant's identifiers or context in an error response.","security_implication":"Error handling itself is part of the API security boundary.","performance_implication":"Structured error mapping can reduce expensive repeated serialization of large internal exception objects.","testing_guidance":"Test errors for secrets, stack traces, internal paths, database information, tenant identifiers, and sensitive metadata.","verification_guidance":"Inspect representative failure responses and verify sensitive information is absent.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9457","version_range":"RFC 9457+","dependencies":["D15-API-B33-0001"],"prerequisites":["problem details","security boundary"],"related_records":["D15-API-B34-0002","D15-API-B35-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B33-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9457","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"Problem Details for HTTP APIs","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9457","section":"5","relevance":"Defines security considerations for problem-detail information exposure."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B34-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Error Handling","concept":"Internal Error Correlation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can an API provide operational diagnostics without exposing internal error details?","answer":"The API can return a safe public problem representation containing a non-sensitive correlation identifier while storing detailed diagnostic information in protected server-side observability systems.","explanation":"Correlation separates client-facing error semantics from privileged operational diagnostics.","principle":"Expose a reference to diagnostics rather than the diagnostics themselves.","correct_example":"A server returns a stable request or correlation identifier and records the internal exception in protected logs or traces.","invalid_example":"Return the complete stack trace so the client can diagnose the server failure.","problem":"Public clients receive sensitive internal diagnostics.","failure_mode":"Information disclosure and inconsistent error handling occur.","cause":"Operational debugging and public error communication were coupled.","solution":"Separate public problem details from privileged logs and traces.","alternative":"For trusted internal APIs, more diagnostic detail may be permitted under an explicit security policy.","tradeoff":"Correlation requires observability infrastructure but substantially reduces public information exposure.","constraint":"Correlation identifiers must not contain secrets or encode sensitive state.","edge_case":"A request can generate multiple downstream failures; correlation should permit reconstruction of the causal chain without exposing it publicly.","security_implication":"Logs and traces themselves require access control and data protection.","performance_implication":"Correlation and tracing introduce instrumentation overhead.","testing_guidance":"Verify that correlation identifiers appear consistently and that detailed diagnostics remain inaccessible to unauthorized clients.","verification_guidance":"Trace a synthetic failure through the public response and protected diagnostic systems.","language":"API Operations","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B34-0001"],"prerequisites":["structured errors","observability"],"related_records":["D15-API-B35-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B34-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API observability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Error Correlation Across API Boundaries","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines separation of public error detail from protected diagnostics."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B35-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Error Taxonomy","concept":"Domain Error Versus Infrastructure Error","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should domain errors and infrastructure failures be distinguished internally?","answer":"Domain errors represent expected business or contract conditions, while infrastructure failures represent inability of technical components to perform required processing. Separating them supports appropriate response mapping and operational diagnosis.","explanation":"Both may ultimately produce HTTP errors, but their causes, remediation, retryability, and observability differ.","principle":"Preserve causal distinctions internally even when several conditions share the same external HTTP status class.","correct_example":"A business rule conflict is classified separately from a database connectivity failure before mapping each to the public API contract.","invalid_example":"Convert every exception into the same generic internal error category before logging or analysis.","problem":"Operational and client behavior cannot distinguish expected domain conditions from technical failures.","failure_mode":"Incorrect retries, misleading alerts, and poor root-cause analysis.","cause":"Internal error taxonomy is too coarse.","solution":"Maintain structured error categories with explicit mapping to public problem types and status codes.","alternative":"A simpler API may use fewer public categories while retaining richer internal classification.","tradeoff":"Richer taxonomy improves diagnostics but increases governance and mapping complexity.","constraint":"Internal categories must not leak directly as unstable public implementation details.","edge_case":"A dependency may return a domain-level rejection that should not automatically be classified as an infrastructure outage.","security_implication":"Internal error categories and diagnostics must remain appropriately protected.","performance_implication":"Structured classification has low overhead and can improve automated incident aggregation.","testing_guidance":"Test mapping for representative domain, dependency, timeout, validation, authorization, and unexpected failures.","verification_guidance":"Verify causal classification before external status/problem mapping.","language":"API Operations","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B32-0001","D15-API-B33-0001"],"prerequisites":["error taxonomy","problem details"],"related_records":["D15-API-B35-0002","D15-API-B36-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B32-0001","D15-API-B33-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Domain and Infrastructure Error Classification","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines causal distinction between domain and infrastructure failures."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B35-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Retry Semantics","concept":"Retryability Classification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should determine whether an API failure is retryable?","answer":"Retryability should be determined by the semantics of the failed operation, the HTTP method and response, the application's idempotency guarantees, and the known cause of the failure rather than by status code alone.","explanation":"A status code does not universally determine whether retrying is safe. Repeating an operation can create duplicate effects when the operation is not idempotent.","principle":"Retry only when the contract establishes that another attempt is safe and potentially useful.","correct_example":"A transient infrastructure failure on an idempotent operation may be retried under documented limits and backoff rules.","invalid_example":"Automatically retry every 4xx or 5xx response regardless of operation semantics.","problem":"Retries are triggered without understanding duplicate-effect risk.","failure_mode":"Duplicate side effects, retry storms, or unnecessary load occur.","cause":"Retry policy is based solely on response status.","solution":"Combine response classification with operation idempotency and explicit retry policy.","alternative":"An API can expose documented retry guidance for selected transient conditions.","tradeoff":"Conservative retries reduce duplicate effects but may reduce recovery from transient failures.","constraint":"Retry policies need bounded attempts, backoff, and cancellation semantics.","edge_case":"A timeout does not prove that the server did not complete the operation.","security_implication":"Repeated authentication or authorization failures should not be blindly retried.","performance_implication":"Unbounded retries can amplify load during outages.","testing_guidance":"Test timeout-after-commit, connection loss, transient dependency failure, non-idempotent operations, and retry exhaustion.","verification_guidance":"Verify retry behavior against explicit operation semantics and failure injection.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B29-0001"],"prerequisites":["idempotency","failure semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B36-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B29-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"9","relevance":"Defines safe and idempotent method semantics relevant to retry reasoning."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B36-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Request Processing","concept":"Request Validation Pipeline","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is a robust high-level API request validation sequence?","answer":"A robust validation pipeline can authenticate the request, parse the message according to its declared representation, validate structural schema constraints, apply authorization, validate domain semantics, execute the operation, and map the outcome to the documented response contract.","explanation":"The exact ordering can vary by architecture, but security and semantic boundaries should be explicit rather than accidentally determined by framework defaults.","principle":"Validate each contract layer explicitly and stop processing when a required prerequisite fails.","correct_example":"Malformed input is rejected before domain execution, and authorization is enforced before a protected state-changing operation is applied.","invalid_example":"Execute business logic first and validate authorization or schema constraints afterward.","problem":"Invalid or unauthorized input reaches deeper layers unnecessarily.","failure_mode":"Security bypasses, inconsistent state, or wasted processing can occur.","cause":"Validation responsibilities and ordering were undefined.","solution":"Define a request-processing pipeline with explicit security, structural, and semantic gates.","alternative":"Framework middleware can implement some gates while domain services enforce resource-specific rules.","tradeoff":"More explicit stages improve reasoning but require clear ownership to avoid duplicate validation.","constraint":"Authorization must occur before protected side effects.","edge_case":"Some endpoints require resource lookup before authorization because authorization depends on resource attributes; the lookup itself must not leak unauthorized existence.","security_implication":"Validation must not be treated as a substitute for authorization.","performance_implication":"Early rejection can reduce expensive downstream processing, but unnecessary duplicate validation adds overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test each rejection boundary and verify that later side effects do not occur after earlier-stage failure.","verification_guidance":"Trace representative requests through every validation stage and verify ordering.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B27-0002","D15-API-B32-0002"],"prerequisites":["authentication","authorization","schema validation","domain validation"],"related_records":["D15-API-B36-0002","D15-API-B37-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B27-0002","D15-API-B32-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API request-processing engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Layered API Request Validation","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines explicit request validation stages."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B36-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Request Processing","concept":"Structural Versus Semantic Validation","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between structural and semantic API validation?","answer":"Structural validation checks whether a request conforms to its data shape and basic constraints, while semantic validation checks whether the values make sense within the operation's domain rules and current state.","explanation":"A request can be structurally valid but semantically invalid.","principle":"Do not assume schema validity proves business validity.","correct_example":"A syntactically valid date range is structurally correct but can still be rejected when the end date precedes the start date or violates a domain rule.","invalid_example":"Accept every request that matches the JSON schema without checking domain invariants.","problem":"Domain-invalid requests pass schema validation.","failure_mode":"Invalid state transitions or inconsistent business state occur.","cause":"Structural validation was treated as complete validation.","solution":"Separate schema constraints from domain invariants and validate both.","alternative":"Some simple semantic constraints can be expressed in a schema, but domain rules often require contextual state.","tradeoff":"Additional semantic validation improves correctness but may require database or domain-service access.","constraint":"Semantic validation must use a consistent view of relevant state when concurrency matters.","edge_case":"A request may be valid at validation time but invalid when executed because another transaction changed the resource.","security_implication":"Authorization is a security decision and should not be replaced by semantic validation.","performance_implication":"Contextual validation may require additional reads or computation.","testing_guidance":"Use separate tests for malformed shape, valid shape with invalid semantics, and concurrent state changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify both schema conformance and domain invariant enforcement.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B36-0001"],"prerequisites":["schema validation","domain rules"],"related_records":["D15-API-B37-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B36-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API validation engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Structural and Semantic Validation","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines distinct schema and domain validation layers."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B37-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"State Validation","concept":"Time-of-Check Versus Time-of-Use","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why can validating an API resource before execution be insufficient in concurrent systems?","answer":"A resource can change between validation and the operation, causing a previously valid assumption to become invalid before the side effect is committed.","explanation":"This is a form of time-of-check versus time-of-use risk. APIs that depend on current state may require transactional constraints, optimistic concurrency, locking, or other consistency mechanisms.","principle":"Validate and commit according to the consistency guarantees required by the operation.","correct_example":"An update verifies a version or precondition and rejects the operation when the resource changed after the client's observed state.","invalid_example":"Read a resource, validate it, perform unrelated work, then overwrite it without checking whether the resource changed.","problem":"Concurrent updates can invalidate previously verified assumptions.","failure_mode":"Lost updates, inconsistent state, or unauthorized transitions occur.","cause":"Validation and state mutation are not coupled by an appropriate consistency mechanism.","solution":"Use transactional constraints, optimistic concurrency, preconditions, or domain-level conflict detection as appropriate.","alternative":"Serialized processing can avoid some races where the architecture permits it.","tradeoff":"Stronger consistency can reduce concurrency or increase coordination cost.","constraint":"The required mechanism depends on domain invariants and consistency requirements.","edge_case":"External side effects may occur outside the transaction and require compensation or workflow design.","security_implication":"Authorization decisions based on stale state can become incorrect before execution.","performance_implication":"Concurrency control can add contention, retries, or additional reads.","testing_guidance":"Use concurrent update tests and failure injection to reproduce state changes between validation and execution.","verification_guidance":"Verify that stale-state operations are detected according to the contract.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B36-0002"],"prerequisites":["concurrency","state validation"],"related_records":["D15-API-B37-0002","D15-API-B38-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B36-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Time-of-Check Versus Time-of-Use in API State Changes","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes stale validation in concurrent operations."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B37-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Concurrency Control","concept":"Conditional Request Preconditions","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can HTTP conditional requests help protect API updates from stale state?","answer":"Conditional request mechanisms can require the server to perform an operation only when a specified representation or resource precondition remains true, enabling optimistic concurrency control.","explanation":"HTTP provides conditional request semantics that can be used to prevent blind overwrites when clients operate on previously observed resource state.","principle":"Bind state-changing operations to explicit preconditions when stale updates are unacceptable.","correct_example":"A client submits a conditional update based on a previously observed validator and the server rejects the update if the current representation no longer satisfies the condition.","invalid_example":"Always overwrite the resource using the client's stale representation without evaluating whether the resource changed.","problem":"Concurrent clients overwrite each other's changes.","failure_mode":"Lost updates occur without reliable detection.","cause":"No concurrency precondition is attached to the update.","solution":"Use HTTP conditional request semantics or an equivalent domain concurrency mechanism.","alternative":"Use application-level version fields when HTTP validators do not map cleanly to domain state.","tradeoff":"Conditional updates improve consistency but require clients to handle conflict outcomes.","constraint":"The validator and comparison semantics must be correctly implemented by the server.","edge_case":"A resource may have multiple representations whose validators need carefully defined semantics.","security_implication":"Concurrency controls should not allow unauthorized clients to infer protected resource state.","performance_implication":"Precondition checks add little cost relative to the state operation but can reduce unnecessary writes.","testing_guidance":"Test unchanged resource, changed resource, missing precondition, invalid validator, and concurrent update scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify that stale updates are rejected according to the documented precondition semantics.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B37-0001"],"prerequisites":["HTTP conditional requests","concurrency control"],"related_records":["D15-API-B38-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B37-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"13","relevance":"Defines HTTP conditional request and precondition semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B38-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Contract Reliability","concept":"Atomicity Of API Operations","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should API contracts define whether a state-changing operation is atomic?","answer":"Clients need to know whether a successful operation represents a complete state transition or whether partial effects can remain after a failure.","explanation":"Without explicit atomicity semantics, clients may incorrectly retry or compensate for operations whose effects are only partially known.","principle":"Define the externally observable completion semantics of state-changing operations.","correct_example":"The API documents that a successful response represents the complete transaction while failed requests leave the specified state unchanged, where that guarantee is actually implemented.","invalid_example":"Return an error after partially applying a multi-step operation without documenting the possible partial state.","problem":"Clients cannot determine whether retry or compensation is safe.","failure_mode":"Retries create duplicates or inconsistent state.","cause":"Completion semantics were omitted from the contract.","solution":"Document atomicity, partial-failure, compensation, and retry semantics explicitly.","alternative":"Expose an asynchronous operation resource when completion cannot be represented as one atomic request.","tradeoff":"Atomic operations simplify clients but may constrain architecture and transaction scope.","constraint":"The documented guarantee must match actual implementation behavior.","edge_case":"External systems may prevent strict atomicity across all side effects.","security_implication":"Partial authorization or side effects can leave security-sensitive state inconsistent.","performance_implication":"Strong atomicity can require transactions or coordination with associated resource costs.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures at each step and verify final state against the documented atomicity guarantee.","verification_guidance":"Verify post-failure state, not merely the HTTP response.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B37-0001"],"prerequisites":["state-changing operations","failure semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B38-0002","D15-API-B39-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B37-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Atomicity and Partial-Failure Semantics","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines the need for explicit completion and partial-failure guarantees."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B38-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Asynchronous Operations","concept":"Accepted-For-Processing Contract","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What should an API define when it accepts work asynchronously?","answer":"An asynchronous API should define what acceptance means, how operation status is observed, how completion or failure is communicated, and what retry or duplicate-submission semantics apply.","explanation":"Acceptance of work does not necessarily mean that the requested operation has completed.","principle":"Separate submission acknowledgement from operation completion in both semantics and contract documentation.","correct_example":"The API returns an accepted response and exposes a documented operation-status mechanism for later completion observation.","invalid_example":"Return an accepted response and imply that the requested business state is already complete.","problem":"Clients confuse request acceptance with successful completion.","failure_mode":"Clients make premature decisions or submit duplicate operations.","cause":"Asynchronous lifecycle semantics were not modeled explicitly.","solution":"Define operation identity, status lifecycle, completion semantics, failure retrieval, and retry behavior.","alternative":"Use synchronous processing when completion reliably fits within the endpoint's expected request lifecycle.","tradeoff":"Asynchronous processing improves handling of long-running work but introduces state, polling/event, and failure-management complexity.","constraint":"Operation status must have deterministic semantics and lifecycle rules.","edge_case":"The original request can be lost by the client after acceptance; the API needs a way to recover operation identity and status.","security_implication":"Operation-status access must be authorized independently and must not leak other users' operation state.","performance_implication":"Asynchronous processing can smooth resource utilization but introduces queue/storage overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test accepted, processing, completed, failed, expired, cancelled, duplicate-submission, and status-access scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify that accepted status is not reported as completed business success.","language":"HTTP API","framework":"Generic","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B38-0001"],"prerequisites":["HTTP status semantics","long-running operations"],"related_records":["D15-API-B39-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B38-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"IETF_STANDARDS_TRACK","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","version":"RFC 9110","section":"15.3.3","relevance":"Defines the semantics of 202 Accepted."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B39-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"API Operation Lifecycle","concept":"Operation Resource","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can a long-running API operation be modeled as a separate operation resource?","answer":"A separate operation resource provides a stable identity for tracking asynchronous work independently from the original request-response exchange.","explanation":"The operation resource can expose lifecycle state without pretending that the original request remains open until completion.","principle":"Model long-running work as an explicit resource when clients need durable status and lifecycle observation.","correct_example":"A submission creates an operation identifier that clients can use to query documented processing status.","invalid_example":"Require clients to keep retrying the original state-changing request until completion is observed.","problem":"Clients cannot distinguish submission from execution and may duplicate work.","failure_mode":"Repeated submissions create duplicate operations or excessive load.","cause":"No durable operation identity exists.","solution":"Create an operation resource with explicit lifecycle and authorization semantics.","alternative":"Event notifications can complement or replace polling where the architecture supports reliable delivery.","tradeoff":"Operation resources improve recoverability but create additional state to manage.","constraint":"Operation identity and lifecycle retention must be documented.","edge_case":"An operation may complete before the client first retrieves its status.","security_implication":"Operation identifiers must not grant access without proper authorization.","performance_implication":"Persistent operation state adds storage and status-query load.","testing_guidance":"Test operation creation, status retrieval, completion, failure, expiration, unauthorized access, and duplicate requests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that operation status accurately reflects the underlying workflow state.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B38-0002"],"prerequisites":["asynchronous API semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B39-0002","D15-API-B40-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B38-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API asynchronous workflow engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Operation Resource Pattern","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines durable API operation identity for asynchronous work."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B39-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Operation Lifecycle","concept":"Explicit Operation State Machine","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should asynchronous API operation states be explicitly defined?","answer":"Explicit operation states prevent clients from inferring lifecycle meaning from undocumented response text or timing and make terminal and non-terminal conditions deterministic.","explanation":"A state machine should define valid transitions, terminal states, cancellation behavior, expiration, and failure semantics as applicable.","principle":"Treat asynchronous operation lifecycle as a contract rather than an implementation detail.","correct_example":"The contract defines states such as accepted, running, completed, failed, and cancelled with documented transitions.","invalid_example":"Return arbitrary status strings that can change without version governance.","problem":"Clients cannot reliably automate operation monitoring.","failure_mode":"Polling logic misinterprets transient states as completion or failure.","cause":"Lifecycle semantics were undocumented.","solution":"Publish an explicit operation-state model and transition rules.","alternative":"A smaller state set can be used when the domain does not require finer distinctions.","tradeoff":"More states provide precision but increase client and testing complexity.","constraint":"State transitions must correspond to actual underlying workflow semantics.","edge_case":"A failed operation may later be retried as a new operation rather than returning to an earlier state.","security_implication":"State visibility must respect resource authorization.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained states can increase storage and event volume.","testing_guidance":"Test every documented transition, invalid transition, terminal state, expiration, and concurrent observation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that observed state transitions conform to the published state machine.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B39-0001"],"prerequisites":["operation resources","state machines"],"related_records":["D15-API-B40-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B39-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API workflow engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Explicit Asynchronous Operation State Machines","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines explicit lifecycle semantics for long-running API operations."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B40-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"B31-B40 Synthesis","concept":"API Error And Operation Semantics Decision Framework","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be evaluated when designing API failure and operation semantics?","answer":"Evaluate HTTP status semantics, structured problem representation, client-versus-server error classification, security-safe detail, correlation, retryability, validation stages, concurrency behavior, atomicity, and asynchronous lifecycle semantics.","explanation":"Reliable API design requires consistency across protocol outcomes, domain behavior, security boundaries, and operational recovery.","principle":"Treat failure semantics as first-class API contract behavior.","correct_example":"A design documents status mapping, problem types, retryability, state-change guarantees, and asynchronous lifecycle before implementation is released.","invalid_example":"Define only successful responses and let framework exceptions determine failure behavior.","problem":"Failure behavior becomes accidental and inconsistent.","failure_mode":"Clients retry incorrectly, security details leak, and operational diagnosis becomes difficult.","cause":"Error handling was treated as secondary implementation logic.","solution":"Design, document, test, and verify failure semantics as part of the API contract.","alternative":"A mature API platform can provide standardized defaults while individual contracts define domain-specific behavior.","tradeoff":"Explicit failure contracts require more design work but reduce ambiguity and integration failures.","constraint":"All claimed guarantees must be backed by implementation or explicitly marked unverified.","edge_case":"A request may time out even though the server completed the operation, requiring idempotency or operation-status semantics.","security_implication":"Error and operation endpoints must preserve authentication, authorization, tenant isolation, and information-minimization controls.","performance_implication":"Retries, detailed validation, tracing, and operation tracking can add load and must be bounded.","testing_guidance":"Combine contract, negative, concurrency, retry, security, failure-injection, and asynchronous lifecycle tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify externally visible responses and resulting state independently.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B31-0001","D15-API-B33-0001","D15-API-B36-0001","D15-API-B39-0002"],"prerequisites":["HTTP errors","validation","concurrency","asynchronous operations"],"related_records":["D15-API-B40-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B31-0001","D15-API-B33-0001","D15-API-B36-0001","D15-API-B39-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability and contract engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Failure Semantics Decision Framework","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Integrates protocol, validation, retry, concurrency, and lifecycle semantics."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B40-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"B31-B40 Quality Gate","concept":"API Failure Contract Quality Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"When can an API failure contract be considered sufficiently specified?","answer":"It is sufficiently specified only when expected failure classes, HTTP status mappings, structured problem semantics, safe detail rules, retryability, state-change guarantees, concurrency behavior, and asynchronous lifecycle behavior are documented for relevant operations.","explanation":"An API contract is incomplete if clients know only the successful response shape but not what happens when validation, authorization, dependencies, concurrency, or processing fail.","principle":"Define negative behavior with the same precision as successful behavior.","correct_example":"Each state-changing operation documents validation failures, authorization outcomes, conflict behavior, retry guidance, and post-failure state semantics.","invalid_example":"Document only 200 responses and allow all other behavior to depend on framework defaults.","problem":"Clients cannot reliably build recovery and error-handling logic.","failure_mode":"Inconsistent integrations, duplicate effects, and unsafe retries emerge.","cause":"Negative paths were excluded from API contract design.","solution":"Make failure-path documentation and verification mandatory for applicable operations.","alternative":"A standardized platform contract can define common failures while endpoint contracts document deviations and domain-specific cases.","tradeoff":"Complete negative-path contracts increase documentation and testing effort.","constraint":"Unknown behavior must remain explicitly marked UNKNOWN or NOT_VERIFIED rather than being assumed.","edge_case":"Some failure outcomes depend on external systems and cannot be fully guaranteed by the API alone.","security_implication":"Failure contracts must be reviewed for information disclosure and authorization bypass risks.","performance_implication":"Failure-path instrumentation should remain bounded under high-error conditions.","testing_guidance":"Require negative, boundary, security, concurrency, retry, and failure-injection coverage before verification.","verification_guidance":"Mark the contract VERIFIED only when evidence supports the documented guarantees.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B40-0001"],"prerequisites":["API failure semantics","verification"],"related_records":["D15-API-B40-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B40-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API contract verification engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Failure Contract Quality Gate","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines evidence requirements for negative API behavior."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

