{"record_id":"D15-API-B41-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authentication","concept":"API Authentication Boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the purpose of authentication in an API?","answer":"Authentication establishes the identity or authenticated security principal associated with an API request; it does not by itself determine whether that principal is permitted to perform the requested operation.","explanation":"Authentication and authorization are separate security decisions. An authenticated request can still be unauthorized for a particular resource or action.","principle":"Establish identity separately from permission evaluation.","correct_example":"The API validates the presented credential and establishes a principal before evaluating whether that principal may access the requested resource.","invalid_example":"Treat possession of any valid credential as sufficient permission for every API operation.","problem":"Identity and permission semantics are conflated.","failure_mode":"Authenticated clients receive privileges that were never explicitly authorized.","cause":"Authentication was incorrectly treated as authorization.","solution":"Represent authenticated identity separately from authorization decisions.","alternative":"A gateway may perform authentication while resource services enforce authorization according to their own resource context.","tradeoff":"Separate enforcement improves security reasoning but requires explicit identity propagation and policy integration.","constraint":"The authenticated identity must be bound to the request context in a trustworthy manner.","edge_case":"Anonymous endpoints may intentionally have no authenticated principal.","security_implication":"Authentication failures must not disclose sensitive credential or identity information.","performance_implication":"Credential verification may involve cryptographic operations or remote identity systems.","testing_guidance":"Test missing credentials, malformed credentials, expired credentials, invalid credentials, valid credentials, and authenticated-but-unauthorized requests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that authentication establishes identity without implicitly granting unrelated permissions.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["API security fundamentals"],"related_records":["D15-API-B41-0002","D15-API-B42-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Authentication and Authorization Separation","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines authentication as identity establishment rather than permission granting."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B41-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authentication","concept":"Credential Validation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an API verify when accepting an authentication credential?","answer":"The API should validate the credential according to its credential scheme, including authenticity, validity period, issuer or authority where applicable, intended audience where applicable, and required integrity or signature properties.","explanation":"Credential validation establishes whether the presented authentication material can be trusted for the API request. Exact checks depend on the credential mechanism.","principle":"Validate credentials according to their defined security semantics rather than merely checking that a credential is present.","correct_example":"A token-based API validates required token security properties before establishing the request principal.","invalid_example":"Accept any syntactically valid token string as an authenticated identity.","problem":"Credential presence is mistaken for credential validity.","failure_mode":"Forged, expired, misissued, or incorrectly scoped credentials may be accepted.","cause":"Credential validation is incomplete.","solution":"Implement and document all required credential validation checks for the chosen authentication mechanism.","alternative":"A trusted identity gateway can perform credential validation and pass a verified identity context downstream when the trust boundary is explicit.","tradeoff":"More validation improves assurance but increases processing and operational complexity.","constraint":"Validation requirements depend on the credential format and trust model.","edge_case":"A credential may be cryptographically valid but issued for a different audience or purpose.","security_implication":"Credential validation is a security boundary and must fail safely when required evidence is unavailable.","performance_implication":"Cryptographic verification and remote key retrieval can affect latency.","testing_guidance":"Test expired, malformed, invalid-signature, wrong-audience, wrong-issuer, revoked, and valid credentials where applicable.","verification_guidance":"Verify every documented credential validation requirement against the implementation.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B41-0001"],"prerequisites":["authentication","credential semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B42-0001","D15-API-B43-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B41-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Credential Validation Requirements","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines credential validation as more than presence checking."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B42-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authorization","concept":"Authorization Decision Boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the purpose of authorization in an API?","answer":"Authorization determines whether the authenticated principal is permitted to perform a specific action on a specific resource or within a specific security context.","explanation":"Authorization is contextual. A principal may have permission for one operation or resource while being denied for another.","principle":"Evaluate authorization at the resource and action boundary where the protected decision is actually required.","correct_example":"An API checks whether the authenticated principal may update the requested tenant's invoice before applying the state change.","invalid_example":"Grant access to every resource solely because the caller successfully authenticated.","problem":"Permission decisions are detached from protected resources and actions.","failure_mode":"Privilege escalation or cross-resource access occurs.","cause":"Authorization was reduced to authentication status.","solution":"Implement explicit resource- and action-aware authorization decisions.","alternative":"Central policy services can evaluate policies while the protected resource boundary remains responsible for enforcement.","tradeoff":"Fine-grained authorization improves control but increases policy complexity.","constraint":"Authorization context must contain trustworthy identity, resource, action, and relevant environmental information.","edge_case":"Authorization may depend on current resource state or ownership, requiring state-aware evaluation.","security_implication":"Authorization is a primary security enforcement boundary.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained authorization can introduce policy evaluation and resource lookup overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test allowed, denied, cross-tenant, role-boundary, ownership, and stale-context scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every protected operation has an explicit authorization enforcement point.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B41-0001"],"prerequisites":["authentication","resource modeling"],"related_records":["D15-API-B42-0002","D15-API-B43-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B41-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API authorization engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Resource and Action Authorization","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines contextual authorization at protected resource boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B42-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authorization","concept":"Default Deny Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should security-sensitive API authorization fail closed when required authorization evidence is unavailable?","answer":"When a protected operation requires authorization evidence that cannot be established, denying the operation prevents missing or ambiguous security evidence from being interpreted as permission.","explanation":"Fail-closed behavior should be applied according to the security contract and operational requirements; any explicitly permitted degraded mode must itself be defined and controlled.","principle":"Do not convert missing authorization evidence into implicit authorization.","correct_example":"A protected API operation is denied when its required policy decision cannot be obtained and no documented safe degraded mode exists.","invalid_example":"Allow the operation whenever the authorization service is unavailable because authentication succeeded earlier.","problem":"Security dependency failure becomes accidental privilege escalation.","failure_mode":"Protected operations proceed without verified authorization.","cause":"Availability was prioritized without a defined security fallback.","solution":"Define explicit authorization failure semantics and deny when required authorization evidence is unavailable unless a controlled, documented exception exists.","alternative":"Use locally cached authorization decisions only when their freshness, scope, integrity, and failure semantics are explicitly bounded.","tradeoff":"Fail-closed behavior can reduce availability during authorization infrastructure failures.","constraint":"Any degraded authorization mode must have explicit scope and risk controls.","edge_case":"Public endpoints intentionally require no authorization decision.","security_implication":"Authorization failure semantics directly affect privilege boundaries.","performance_implication":"Remote policy checks may add latency; safe caching can reduce it when justified.","testing_guidance":"Simulate authorization-service timeout, invalid policy response, stale policy, and policy-engine unavailability.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unavailable authorization evidence produces the documented security outcome.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":["authorization","failure semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B43-0001","D15-API-B44-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B42-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Security authorization engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Fail-Safe Authorization Boundaries","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines secure handling of unavailable authorization evidence."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B43-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authorization Models","concept":"Role-Based Authorization","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How does role-based authorization represent API permissions?","answer":"Role-based authorization associates principals with roles and associates roles with permitted operations or resources.","explanation":"Roles provide a manageable abstraction for common permission sets, but role membership alone may be insufficient when authorization depends on resource attributes, ownership, or context.","principle":"Use roles to represent stable permission groupings while retaining explicit resource and action checks.","correct_example":"An administrator role permits documented administrative operations while ordinary users receive a narrower role.","invalid_example":"Grant a role unrestricted access to every resource simply because it is called administrator.","problem":"Role abstraction becomes broader than the intended authorization boundary.","failure_mode":"Excessive privileges and accidental access occur.","cause":"Roles were treated as unrestricted trust labels.","solution":"Define role permissions explicitly and combine them with resource-level constraints when required.","alternative":"Attribute- or policy-based authorization can represent contextual rules that roles cannot express cleanly.","tradeoff":"Roles are simple to reason about but can produce role explosion as rules become more contextual.","constraint":"Role assignments and permissions require lifecycle governance.","edge_case":"A principal may hold multiple roles whose combined permissions require conflict-resolution semantics.","security_implication":"Role assignment is itself a privileged security operation.","performance_implication":"Role lookup can be efficient when permissions are cached safely.","testing_guidance":"Test each role against allowed and denied operations and resources.","verification_guidance":"Compare effective permissions against the documented role matrix.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":["authorization","identity"],"related_records":["D15-API-B43-0002","D15-API-B44-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B42-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Authorization architecture","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Role-Based API Authorization","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes role-based permission abstraction."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B43-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authorization Models","concept":"Attribute-Based Authorization","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When can attribute-based authorization provide information that role-based authorization alone cannot?","answer":"Attribute-based authorization can evaluate contextual attributes of the principal, resource, action, or environment, allowing policies that depend on dynamic conditions rather than only predefined roles.","explanation":"Examples include resource ownership, tenant identity, geographic or device context, classification, workflow state, or other explicitly governed attributes.","principle":"Use contextual attributes when permission depends on more than stable role membership.","correct_example":"A policy permits access only when the authenticated principal belongs to the same tenant as the requested resource and has the required action permission.","invalid_example":"Assume a generic user role permits access to every resource of the same type.","problem":"Authorization cannot express resource-specific or contextual constraints.","failure_mode":"Users receive access outside the intended scope of their role.","cause":"The authorization model is too coarse for the domain.","solution":"Add explicit attributes or policy conditions where contextual authorization is required.","alternative":"Combine role-based permissions with resource ownership or tenant constraints.","tradeoff":"Attribute-based policies increase expressiveness but also increase policy complexity and evaluation requirements.","constraint":"Attributes used for authorization must come from trustworthy and appropriately fresh sources.","edge_case":"A resource attribute can change after a policy decision and before execution.","security_implication":"Untrusted client-provided attributes must not be accepted as authoritative security evidence.","performance_implication":"Complex policy evaluation can increase latency.","testing_guidance":"Test combinations of identity, resource, action, tenant, ownership, and environmental attributes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that security-relevant attributes are authoritative, correctly scoped, and enforced.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B43-0001"],"prerequisites":["authorization models","resource context"],"related_records":["D15-API-B44-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B43-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D15-API-B43-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Authorization architecture","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Attribute-Based API Authorization","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes contextual authorization based on trusted attributes."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B44-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authorization Enforcement","concept":"Resource-Level Authorization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should authorization be enforced at the protected resource boundary?","answer":"Authorization should be enforced where the API can reliably identify the target resource and action and before the protected operation produces its side effect.","explanation":"Checking authorization only at a user interface, gateway route, or coarse endpoint level may omit resource-specific constraints.","principle":"Enforce authorization as close as practical to the protected resource and side effect.","correct_example":"A service checks tenant ownership and action permission immediately before updating a protected record.","invalid_example":"Rely on a front-end button being hidden from users as the authorization control.","problem":"Untrusted callers can bypass presentation-layer controls.","failure_mode":"Direct API calls access resources without permission.","cause":"Authorization was implemented only at the user-interface layer.","solution":"Enforce authorization server-side at the protected resource boundary.","alternative":"A gateway can provide an additional coarse policy layer, but resource services should retain necessary resource-specific enforcement.","tradeoff":"Defense in depth adds policy checks but improves resistance to bypass.","constraint":"Every direct execution path to a protected resource must respect the authorization boundary.","edge_case":"Background jobs and internal service calls must receive equivalent authorization treatment when they act on behalf of a principal.","security_implication":"Resource-level enforcement prevents many horizontal and vertical privilege escalation paths.","performance_implication":"Resource authorization may require additional data access or policy evaluation.","testing_guidance":"Bypass client controls and call the API directly with altered resource identifiers and principals.","verification_guidance":"Trace every execution path to protected state and verify authorization enforcement exists.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":["authorization","resource boundaries"],"related_records":["D15-API-B44-0002","D15-API-B45-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B42-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Resource-Level Authorization Enforcement","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines authorization enforcement at protected resource boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B44-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authorization Enforcement","concept":"Object-Level Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What is object-level authorization in an API?","answer":"Object-level authorization verifies that the authenticated principal is permitted to access or modify the specific resource instance identified by the request.","explanation":"Endpoint-level permission may establish that a principal can use an operation type, while object-level authorization determines whether that particular resource instance is within the principal's permitted scope.","principle":"Do not treat permission to invoke an operation as permission to access every object handled by that operation.","correct_example":"A request to retrieve invoice 123 verifies that invoice 123 belongs to a tenant or account the principal is authorized to access.","invalid_example":"Allow any authenticated user to retrieve arbitrary invoice identifiers because they can call the invoice endpoint.","problem":"Resource identifiers become an access-control bypass mechanism.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized users can read or modify objects outside their scope.","cause":"Authorization was applied only to endpoint capability, not resource identity.","solution":"Bind object authorization to the authenticated principal and target resource before returning or modifying it.","alternative":"A data-access layer can enforce tenant or ownership predicates as defense in depth.","tradeoff":"Object-level checks improve isolation but may require resource lookup and policy evaluation.","constraint":"Resource identity and authorization context must be derived from trusted server-side state.","edge_case":"Not-found and unauthorized responses may need carefully designed semantics to avoid resource enumeration.","security_implication":"Object-level authorization is critical for preventing horizontal privilege escalation.","performance_implication":"Per-object authorization can increase query cost unless safely integrated with data-access constraints.","testing_guidance":"Test sequential and random object identifiers across authorized and unauthorized principals.","verification_guidance":"Verify that changing only the resource identifier cannot bypass authorization.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B44-0001"],"prerequisites":["resource-level authorization","identity"],"related_records":["D15-API-B45-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B44-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Object-Level Authorization","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines authorization against specific resource instances."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B45-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Tenant Authorization","concept":"Tenant-Bound Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should a multi-tenant API bind authorization to tenant context?","answer":"The API should derive tenant context from a trusted authenticated identity or authoritative resource relationship and enforce that the requested resource and operation belong to an authorized tenant scope.","explanation":"A tenant identifier supplied by a client must not automatically be treated as authoritative authorization evidence.","principle":"Bind tenant scope to trusted identity and server-side resource ownership rather than client-controlled routing or payload fields alone.","correct_example":"The server derives the caller's tenant from authenticated identity and verifies the requested resource belongs to that tenant before access.","invalid_example":"Trust a tenant_id JSON field supplied by the caller to determine which tenant data can be accessed.","problem":"Client-controlled tenant context becomes an authorization input without sufficient trust validation.","failure_mode":"Cross-tenant data access becomes possible.","cause":"Tenant isolation was implemented as a request parameter convention rather than an enforced security boundary.","solution":"Establish authoritative tenant context and enforce it across API, data, cache, queue, and background processing boundaries.","alternative":"A trusted gateway can inject tenant context, but downstream services must verify the trust boundary before relying on it.","tradeoff":"Strong tenant binding improves isolation but requires consistent propagation and enforcement.","constraint":"Every relevant execution path must preserve tenant context correctly.","edge_case":"Administrative principals may legitimately operate across tenants and therefore require explicitly broader authorization rules.","security_implication":"Tenant context is a security boundary and must be protected against spoofing and confused-deputy behavior.","performance_implication":"Tenant predicates can often be integrated into data queries to reduce separate authorization lookups.","testing_guidance":"Test cross-tenant reads, writes, updates, deletes, searches, exports, caches, queues, and background jobs.","verification_guidance":"Verify tenant isolation independently at each persistence and execution boundary.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B44-0002"],"prerequisites":["multi-tenancy","object-level authorization"],"related_records":["D15-API-B45-0002","D15-API-B46-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B44-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Multi-tenant API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Tenant-Bound Authorization","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines tenant isolation as an authorization boundary."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B45-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Tenant Authorization","concept":"Cross-Tenant Enumeration Resistance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should API resource lookup consider information disclosure during tenant authorization failures?","answer":"A resource lookup can reveal whether another tenant's resource exists even when access to its contents is denied, so API response semantics should be designed to avoid unnecessary cross-tenant information disclosure.","explanation":"The exact response strategy depends on the application's threat model and resource semantics. The important requirement is that authorization failure should not accidentally disclose protected existence information.","principle":"Consider resource existence as potentially sensitive information when designing unauthorized and not-found behavior.","correct_example":"A multi-tenant API applies an explicit policy for how inaccessible resource identifiers are represented to callers.","invalid_example":"Return detailed resource metadata for an object before checking whether the caller is authorized to access it.","problem":"Authorization failures disclose protected resource existence or metadata.","failure_mode":"Attackers enumerate identifiers or infer tenant activity.","cause":"Lookup and authorization semantics were designed independently.","solution":"Define and test resource-discovery and authorization response semantics together.","alternative":"Use opaque identifiers and uniform responses where appropriate, combined with actual authorization enforcement.","tradeoff":"Information minimization can make client troubleshooting less direct.","constraint":"The chosen response behavior must remain consistent with legitimate client requirements.","edge_case":"Administrative users may require explicit cross-tenant discovery privileges.","security_implication":"Enumeration resistance is part of confidentiality and tenant isolation.","performance_implication":"Uniform response strategies can sometimes require additional processing to equalize behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test sequential identifiers, random identifiers, authorized resources, unauthorized resources, and nonexistent resources.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unauthorized resource discovery does not expose unintended tenant information.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B45-0001"],"prerequisites":["tenant isolation","object authorization"],"related_records":["D15-API-B46-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B45-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API confidentiality engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Cross-Tenant Resource Enumeration Resistance","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Addresses information disclosure through authorization and resource lookup behavior."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B46-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Identity Propagation","concept":"Authenticated Principal Propagation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should authenticated identity be propagated between API components?","answer":"Authenticated identity should be propagated through a trusted, integrity-protected context whose provenance and scope are explicitly defined at each trust boundary.","explanation":"Downstream services need to distinguish verified identity context from arbitrary client-supplied identity claims.","principle":"Never treat client-controlled identity metadata as equivalent to trusted authentication context.","correct_example":"A gateway or service establishes a verified principal and passes a constrained identity context to a downstream service across a defined trust boundary.","invalid_example":"Trust an arbitrary X-User-ID header supplied directly by an external client as the authenticated identity.","problem":"Identity context can be spoofed by callers.","failure_mode":"Downstream authorization decisions operate on forged principals.","cause":"Trust boundaries and identity propagation semantics are undefined.","solution":"Define trusted identity propagation mechanisms and strip or reject untrusted identity headers at boundaries.","alternative":"Each service can independently authenticate requests when trust delegation is not appropriate.","tradeoff":"Central identity propagation can reduce repeated verification but increases trust in intermediary components.","constraint":"Delegated identity must have explicit integrity, scope, lifetime, and audience semantics.","edge_case":"A service may act on behalf of a user while also possessing its own workload identity.","security_implication":"Confused-deputy and identity-spoofing risks must be addressed explicitly.","performance_implication":"Delegated identity can reduce repeated authentication operations.","testing_guidance":"Attempt to inject or override identity headers and verify that untrusted values cannot alter the principal.","verification_guidance":"Trace principal provenance across every service boundary.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B41-0001","D15-API-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":["authentication","authorization","service boundaries"],"related_records":["D15-API-B46-0002","D15-API-B47-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B41-0001","D15-API-B42-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Authenticated Principal Propagation","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines trusted identity propagation across API service boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B46-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Identity Propagation","concept":"Confused Deputy Prevention","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can API architecture reduce confused-deputy authorization failures?","answer":"Services should distinguish the identity of the calling workload from the identity of the user or principal on whose behalf an action is performed, and authorization should use the correct authority for each operation.","explanation":"A service with broad privileges can accidentally use its own authority to perform actions that the initiating principal was not permitted to perform.","principle":"Preserve actor, caller, and delegated-principal semantics explicitly across service boundaries.","correct_example":"A service records both the calling workload identity and the delegated user identity and applies policy appropriate to the requested action.","invalid_example":"A privileged internal service accepts any user identifier and performs the requested operation using only its own broad credentials.","problem":"A privileged component becomes an unintended authorization proxy.","failure_mode":"A low-privilege caller gains access to operations through a higher-privilege service.","cause":"Delegation and workload identity were conflated.","solution":"Model caller identity, delegated identity, authority, audience, and scope explicitly.","alternative":"Use capability-limited downstream credentials for individual operations.","tradeoff":"Explicit delegation improves security reasoning but increases identity and policy complexity.","constraint":"Delegated authority must never exceed the intended authority of the initiating principal without explicit policy.","edge_case":"System-initiated operations may have no human principal and require a separate workload authorization model.","security_implication":"Confused-deputy prevention is critical for service-to-service API security.","performance_implication":"Additional identity and policy checks can increase request overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test low-privilege principals invoking high-privilege services with altered delegated identity values.","verification_guidance":"Verify that effective authority cannot be expanded merely by passing a different principal identifier.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B46-0001"],"prerequisites":["identity propagation","delegation"],"related_records":["D15-API-B47-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B46-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed authorization engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Confused Deputy Prevention in APIs","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines separation of workload authority and delegated principal authority."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B47-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authorization Contracts","concept":"Permission Scope","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should API credentials and authorization decisions use narrowly defined scopes where possible?","answer":"Narrow scopes reduce the set of operations or resources that a credential or delegated authority can access, limiting the impact of misuse or compromise.","explanation":"Scope should represent the intended authority rather than becoming a generic marker that grants broad access.","principle":"Grant only the authority required for the operation and context.","correct_example":"A credential permits a narrowly defined read operation against an explicitly supported API resource class.","invalid_example":"Issue one unrestricted credential because it simplifies integration.","problem":"Credentials have broader authority than required.","failure_mode":"Credential compromise or misuse produces unnecessarily broad impact.","cause":"Convenience was prioritized over least-privilege design.","solution":"Define explicit permission scopes and enforce them at protected resource boundaries.","alternative":"Role or attribute policies can supplement scopes where resource-specific authorization is required.","tradeoff":"Narrow scopes improve isolation but can increase credential and policy management complexity.","constraint":"Scope semantics must be stable, documented, and enforced consistently.","edge_case":"A broad administrative workflow may legitimately require multiple narrowly scoped operations.","security_implication":"Least privilege reduces blast radius of credential compromise.","performance_implication":"Scope checks are generally inexpensive but can add policy evaluation work.","testing_guidance":"Attempt operations outside each credential's declared scope.","verification_guidance":"Verify that changing scope changes effective permissions exactly as documented.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":["authorization","credential management"],"related_records":["D15-API-B47-0002","D15-API-B48-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B42-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API authorization engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Least-Privilege API Permission Scopes","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines narrow authorization scope as a security boundary."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B47-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authorization Contracts","concept":"Scope Versus Resource Authorization","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why is a credential scope not always sufficient for authorization?","answer":"A credential scope can describe permitted operation categories, but authorization may additionally depend on the specific resource, tenant, ownership, workflow state, or other contextual attributes.","explanation":"Scope is one input to authorization rather than necessarily the complete authorization decision.","principle":"Treat credential scope as constrained authority, not automatic permission for every matching resource.","correct_example":"A read scope permits reading invoices while a separate tenant and ownership policy determines which invoices are accessible.","invalid_example":"Assume a read scope grants access to every invoice in every tenant.","problem":"Broad scope is mistaken for object-level permission.","failure_mode":"Cross-resource or cross-tenant access occurs.","cause":"Credential scope was used as the sole authorization mechanism.","solution":"Combine scope evaluation with resource- and context-aware authorization.","alternative":"Highly specialized credentials can encode narrower resource constraints where operationally justified.","tradeoff":"Combining controls improves precision but increases policy complexity.","constraint":"All authorization inputs must be trusted and correctly scoped.","edge_case":"System administrators may have intentionally broad scopes but still require explicit policy enforcement.","security_implication":"Separating scope from resource authorization reduces privilege escalation risk.","performance_implication":"Additional contextual checks may require resource lookup or policy evaluation.","testing_guidance":"Test valid scope against authorized and unauthorized resources.","verification_guidance":"Verify effective access is the intersection of all applicable authorization constraints.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B47-0001","D15-API-B44-0002"],"prerequisites":["permission scopes","object-level authorization"],"related_records":["D15-API-B48-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B47-0001","D15-API-B44-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D15-API-B47-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API authorization engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Authorization Scope and Resource Context","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Distinguishes credential scope from resource-specific authorization."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B48-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Security Context","concept":"Authorization Context Integrity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What properties should authorization context have before it is trusted?","answer":"Authorization context should have known provenance, integrity protection, appropriate freshness, correct audience or scope, and a clearly defined trust boundary.","explanation":"Authorization decisions can become unsafe when their inputs are stale, spoofable, incorrectly scoped, or derived from untrusted client-controlled values.","principle":"Authorization is only as trustworthy as the security context used to make the decision.","correct_example":"A service verifies that the principal, tenant, requested action, and relevant policy attributes originate from trusted sources before evaluating authorization.","invalid_example":"Use an arbitrary request header as the authoritative tenant or role because upstream systems normally populate it.","problem":"Untrusted context enters a security decision.","failure_mode":"Authorization bypass or privilege escalation occurs.","cause":"Context provenance was not explicitly defined.","solution":"Define and verify provenance, integrity, freshness, audience, and scope for every security-relevant authorization input.","alternative":"Centralized policy infrastructure can standardize context construction and validation.","tradeoff":"Strong context validation increases implementation complexity but strengthens authorization assurance.","constraint":"Security context must be bound to the authenticated request and protected from caller-controlled override.","edge_case":"Some attributes are intentionally client-provided but must be treated as untrusted claims until validated.","security_implication":"Compromised context can invalidate otherwise correct authorization logic.","performance_implication":"Context verification may require cryptographic or policy operations.","testing_guidance":"Tamper with identity, tenant, role, scope, and resource attributes and verify authorization outcomes remain secure.","verification_guidance":"Document the source and trust level of every authorization input.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B46-0001","D15-API-B47-0002"],"prerequisites":["identity propagation","authorization context"],"related_records":["D15-API-B49-0001","D15-API-B50-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B46-0001","D15-API-B47-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security architecture","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Authorization Context Integrity","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines trust requirements for authorization inputs."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B48-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Security Context","concept":"Stale Authorization Context","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What risk occurs when an API uses stale authorization context?","answer":"Stale authorization context can produce an authorization decision based on permissions, ownership, tenant membership, or resource state that is no longer current.","explanation":"Caching authorization decisions can be safe only when the freshness requirements and invalidation semantics are compatible with the protected operation.","principle":"Authorization cache lifetime must match the security sensitivity and consistency requirements of the protected decision.","correct_example":"A high-impact permission change takes effect according to a documented bounded propagation interval, and the API does not claim immediate enforcement when it cannot provide it.","invalid_example":"Cache administrative authorization indefinitely without invalidation or expiration.","problem":"Revoked permissions remain effective after revocation.","failure_mode":"Formerly authorized principals retain access longer than intended.","cause":"Authorization freshness requirements were not defined.","solution":"Define bounded freshness, invalidation, revocation, and failover behavior for cached authorization context.","alternative":"Perform authoritative policy evaluation for high-impact operations.","tradeoff":"Fresh authorization improves security but may increase latency and dependency on policy infrastructure.","constraint":"Cache semantics must be explicit and appropriate for the operation's risk.","edge_case":"Emergency revocation may require a stronger path than normal policy propagation.","security_implication":"Stale authorization can directly create access-control violations.","performance_implication":"Shorter cache lifetimes increase policy evaluation load.","testing_guidance":"Test permission revocation during active sessions and cached authorization periods.","verification_guidance":"Measure or otherwise verify the actual propagation behavior before making freshness claims.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B48-0001"],"prerequisites":["authorization caching","revocation"],"related_records":["D15-API-B49-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B48-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API authorization engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Authorization Cache Freshness","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Addresses security consequences of stale authorization context."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B49-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authorization Observability","concept":"Authorization Decision Auditability","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What should an API record when authorization decisions require security auditability?","answer":"The audit record should capture sufficient information to reconstruct the authorization decision, such as actor or principal, action, resource, tenant or security scope where applicable, decision outcome, policy context identifiers, timestamp semantics, and correlation information, subject to data-minimization requirements.","explanation":"Audit records should support investigation without unnecessarily storing sensitive payloads or secrets.","principle":"Make security decisions attributable and reconstructable without turning audit logs into uncontrolled data copies.","correct_example":"Record that principal P attempted action A on resource R, policy decision D occurred, and request correlation C links the decision to protected operational evidence.","invalid_example":"Log complete authentication tokens, passwords, or sensitive request bodies for every authorization decision.","problem":"Security decisions cannot be reconstructed or logs contain excessive sensitive information.","failure_mode":"Incident investigation is impaired or audit data becomes a new confidentiality risk.","cause":"Audit requirements were undefined or implemented without data classification.","solution":"Define a minimal authorization audit schema and protect the audit store.","alternative":"Use centralized security audit infrastructure with standardized event fields.","tradeoff":"More audit context improves investigation but increases storage, privacy, and protection requirements.","constraint":"Audit data must follow retention, privacy, integrity, and access-control requirements.","edge_case":"A denied request may be more security-significant than a successful request and require different retention or alerting treatment.","security_implication":"Audit records themselves are security-sensitive assets.","performance_implication":"High-volume authorization logging can create significant storage and I/O load.","testing_guidance":"Verify allowed and denied decisions produce expected audit records without secrets.","verification_guidance":"Trace an authorization decision from request through audit evidence.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B48-0001"],"prerequisites":["authorization","security audit"],"related_records":["D15-API-B49-0002","D15-API-B50-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B48-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security observability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Authorization Decision Auditability","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines auditable security-decision evidence."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B49-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Authorization Observability","concept":"Security Event Data Minimization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should API security logs avoid unnecessary sensitive data?","answer":"Security logs should contain sufficient evidence for detection and investigation while minimizing secrets, credentials, personal data, and sensitive payload content that would create additional exposure if the logs were compromised.","explanation":"Logging is itself a data flow and therefore must be governed by security, privacy, and retention requirements.","principle":"Collect the minimum security-relevant evidence necessary for the intended operational purpose.","correct_example":"Record a credential type and safe identifier or fingerprint where appropriate rather than the raw credential value.","invalid_example":"Store bearer tokens and complete sensitive request payloads in general-purpose logs.","problem":"Operational logs become repositories of sensitive credentials or data.","failure_mode":"A logging compromise creates secondary exposure.","cause":"Logging was designed without data-classification analysis.","solution":"Define safe logging fields, redaction rules, retention, access control, and audit requirements.","alternative":"Store sensitive evidence in a restricted security system with stronger controls when genuinely required.","tradeoff":"More aggressive minimization can reduce direct forensic context.","constraint":"Redaction must be deterministic and applied before data reaches broad-access logs.","edge_case":"Structured payload fields may contain secrets unexpectedly even when the endpoint schema appears safe.","security_implication":"Logging pipelines must be included in the API threat model.","performance_implication":"Redaction and structured logging add processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Inject secrets and sensitive values into representative requests and verify they do not appear in unauthorized logs.","verification_guidance":"Inspect emitted logs rather than trusting logging configuration alone.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B49-0001"],"prerequisites":["security logging","data classification"],"related_records":["D15-API-B50-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B49-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security logging engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Security Event Data Minimization","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines minimum-necessary security logging and sensitive-data protection."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B50-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"B41-B50 Synthesis","concept":"Authentication Authorization Contract","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should an API security contract define for authentication and authorization?","answer":"The contract should define authentication requirements, credential validation, principal identity, authorization scope, resource-level permissions, tenant boundaries where applicable, delegation semantics, failure behavior, freshness requirements, and audit expectations.","explanation":"Security cannot be fully specified by naming an authentication mechanism. The API must define how authenticated identity becomes a constrained authorization decision for each protected operation.","principle":"Treat identity, authority, resource scope, and enforcement as explicit API contract dimensions.","correct_example":"Each protected operation documents required authentication, authorized principals, resource scope, denial semantics, and relevant audit behavior.","invalid_example":"Document only that the API requires authentication without defining what authenticated clients may actually do.","problem":"Clients and implementers cannot determine the effective security boundary.","failure_mode":"Authorization gaps, inconsistent enforcement, and privilege escalation risks emerge.","cause":"Authentication was documented without authorization semantics.","solution":"Define authentication and authorization as connected but distinct contract layers.","alternative":"A shared security platform can standardize common authentication while endpoint contracts specify resource authorization.","tradeoff":"Explicit security contracts require more design and testing but reduce ambiguous security behavior.","constraint":"Security guarantees must be supported by implementation evidence before being marked VERIFIED.","edge_case":"Some endpoints may intentionally be public, while others require different authentication or authorization policies.","security_implication":"Security contract errors can directly expose protected resources or operations.","performance_implication":"Authentication and authorization checks affect request latency and dependency availability.","testing_guidance":"Require positive, negative, cross-tenant, privilege-boundary, stale-context, credential, and audit tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify both the decision logic and actual protected-resource enforcement.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B41-0001","D15-API-B42-0001","D15-API-B44-0001","D15-API-B48-0001","D15-API-B49-0001"],"prerequisites":["authentication","authorization","security auditing"],"related_records":["D15-API-B50-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B41-0001","D15-API-B42-0001","D15-API-B44-0001","D15-API-B48-0001","D15-API-B49-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security architecture","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Authentication and Authorization Contract","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Integrates authentication, authorization, resource scope, and audit requirements."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B50-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"B41-B50 Quality Gate","concept":"API Security Enforcement Quality Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be verified before an API security design is considered complete for its documented scope?","answer":"The implementation must demonstrate that authentication is validated, authorization is enforced at protected resource boundaries, tenant and object isolation works where applicable, untrusted identity context cannot override trusted identity, failure behavior is safe, and relevant security decisions are auditable.","explanation":"A documented security policy is not equivalent to an implemented security control. Verification must establish the connection between policy and actual execution paths.","principle":"Verify security enforcement at the actual point where protected effects occur.","correct_example":"Security tests directly invoke protected endpoints and attempt unauthorized resource access, identity manipulation, cross-tenant access, and degraded authorization conditions.","invalid_example":"Mark an API secure solely because it declares an authentication middleware.","problem":"Security claims are based on configuration presence rather than effective enforcement.","failure_mode":"Bypass paths remain undetected.","cause":"Verification focused on intended architecture instead of executable behavior.","solution":"Test protected execution paths, trust boundaries, failure conditions, and audit evidence.","alternative":"Formal policy analysis can supplement runtime tests but does not by itself prove implementation wiring.","tradeoff":"Deep security verification requires additional testing effort and controlled test environments.","constraint":"Any untested or unverified security guarantee must remain explicitly marked NOT_VERIFIED.","edge_case":"Internal or background execution paths may bypass ordinary HTTP middleware and require separate authorization verification.","security_implication":"Unverified security boundaries must not be represented as proven controls.","performance_implication":"Security testing may require additional instrumentation and isolated environments.","testing_guidance":"Include authentication bypass, authorization bypass, object-level, tenant-isolation, stale-policy, identity-spoofing, and audit-integrity tests.","verification_guidance":"Require concrete evidence for each claimed security control and preserve limitations when evidence is incomplete.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B50-0001"],"prerequisites":["security contract","security testing"],"related_records":["D15-API-B50-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B50-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security verification engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Security Enforcement Quality Gate","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines evidence requirements for API authentication and authorization claims."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

