{"record_id":"D15-API-B51-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Idempotency","concept":"Idempotency Contract","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does idempotency mean in an API operation?","answer":"An operation is idempotent when repeating the same request according to its defined semantics produces the same intended server-side effect as performing it once, even though individual responses may differ.","explanation":"Idempotency concerns the intended effect of repeated requests, not necessarily identical response bytes or status codes.","principle":"Define idempotency at the operation-semantic level rather than assuming repeated requests are harmless.","correct_example":"A PUT request that replaces a resource with the same representation can be repeated without repeatedly creating additional equivalent resources.","invalid_example":"Assume every POST operation is safe to retry because the client expects the same payload.","problem":"Retry behavior is inferred without an explicit operation contract.","failure_mode":"Repeated requests create duplicate side effects.","cause":"Idempotency semantics were not designed explicitly.","solution":"Document which operations are idempotent and how repeated requests are handled.","alternative":"Use an explicit idempotency-key mechanism for non-idempotent operations that require safe retry semantics.","tradeoff":"Idempotency improves reliability but may require durable request-result state.","constraint":"The server must define the identity and scope of a repeated operation.","edge_case":"A repeated request may receive a different response while still producing no additional intended effect.","security_implication":"Idempotency mechanisms must prevent attackers from replaying privileged operations indefinitely.","performance_implication":"Persisting idempotency state introduces storage and lookup overhead.","testing_guidance":"Repeat identical requests under success, timeout, connection failure, and concurrent execution conditions.","verification_guidance":"Verify the resulting side effects rather than only comparing response payloads.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B31-0001","D15-API-B35-0002"],"prerequisites":["HTTP method semantics","failure handling"],"related_records":["D15-API-B51-0002","D15-API-B52-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B31-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"IETF HTTP semantics","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","version":"RFC 9110","section":"Idempotent Methods","relevance":"Defines HTTP idempotent method semantics and distinguishes them from arbitrary API retry behavior."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B51-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Idempotency","concept":"Idempotency Key","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is an idempotency key useful for an API?","answer":"An idempotency key is useful when a client needs to safely retry an operation whose normal semantics may create or modify state and the server needs to recognize retries of the same logical request.","explanation":"The key gives the server an application-level identifier for correlating repeated attempts of one logical operation.","principle":"Use an explicit operation identity when transport retries can otherwise produce duplicate effects.","correct_example":"A payment-creation API accepts a client-generated idempotency key and ensures repeated requests for the same key do not create multiple logical payments.","invalid_example":"Treat every request with the same endpoint and payload as automatically identical without defining operation identity.","problem":"Retries cannot be distinguished from independent operations.","failure_mode":"Duplicate side effects occur after client or network retries.","cause":"No stable operation identity exists.","solution":"Define an idempotency key contract including scope, lifetime, request equivalence, conflict behavior, and stored result semantics.","alternative":"Use a client-generated unique resource identifier when resource creation semantics support it.","tradeoff":"Idempotency storage increases infrastructure complexity but improves retry safety.","constraint":"The key must have a defined uniqueness scope and lifecycle.","edge_case":"Reusing a key with materially different request parameters should produce a defined conflict rather than silently executing a different operation.","security_implication":"Keys should not become bearer credentials or expose sensitive information.","performance_implication":"Idempotency lookups and durable records add storage operations.","testing_guidance":"Repeat requests with identical keys, altered payloads, concurrent attempts, expired keys, and failed first attempts.","verification_guidance":"Verify that one logical operation cannot produce multiple unintended side effects.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B51-0001"],"prerequisites":["idempotency","request identity"],"related_records":["D15-API-B52-0001","D15-API-B53-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B51-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","title":"Idempotency Key Pattern","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes application-level operation identity for safe retries."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B52-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Idempotency","concept":"Idempotency Key Scope","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must an API define for the scope of an idempotency key?","answer":"The API should define which client or principal owns the key namespace, which operation or endpoint it applies to, how long it remains valid, and how conflicting reuse is handled.","explanation":"A key is meaningful only within a defined identity and operation scope. Reusing a key across unrelated operations without semantics can create incorrect request deduplication.","principle":"Make idempotency identity unambiguous and bounded.","correct_example":"The server interprets an idempotency key within a defined authenticated-client and operation scope.","invalid_example":"Use a globally shared short string as an idempotency key across unrelated clients and operations.","problem":"Distinct operations collide in the idempotency namespace.","failure_mode":"A legitimate request is incorrectly treated as a duplicate.","cause":"Key scope was undefined.","solution":"Bind key uniqueness to documented principal, operation, and lifetime boundaries.","alternative":"Use a globally unique generated operation identifier when the architecture requires cross-service correlation.","tradeoff":"Narrow scope simplifies collision handling while broader scope can simplify distributed correlation.","constraint":"Scope must match the system's retry and persistence model.","edge_case":"Anonymous clients require a separate mechanism if idempotency cannot safely be bound to an authenticated principal.","security_implication":"Poor key isolation can enable cross-user request interference.","performance_implication":"Scoped indexes can reduce idempotency lookup cost.","testing_guidance":"Test key reuse across users, endpoints, tenants, and time periods.","verification_guidance":"Verify that a key from one authorization scope cannot suppress or replay another client's operation.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B51-0002"],"prerequisites":["idempotency keys","identity scope"],"related_records":["D15-API-B52-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B51-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","title":"Scoped Idempotency Keys","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines identity and operation scope for idempotency records."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B52-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Idempotency","concept":"Idempotency Conflict","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"What should happen when the same idempotency key is reused with a different logical request?","answer":"The API should apply an explicitly documented conflict policy rather than silently treating the new request as the original operation.","explanation":"A key identifies a logical operation. Reusing it with incompatible parameters creates ambiguity that should be surfaced rather than hidden.","principle":"Reject incompatible reuse of an established idempotency identity.","correct_example":"A second request with the same key but different protected parameters receives a documented conflict response.","invalid_example":"Execute the second request while retaining the first request's idempotency record.","problem":"One operation identity maps to multiple incompatible requests.","failure_mode":"Clients receive incorrect results or unexpected side effects.","cause":"Request equivalence was not validated.","solution":"Store sufficient request identity information to detect incompatible reuse and return a defined conflict.","alternative":"Require clients to generate a new key for each logical operation.","tradeoff":"Request comparison adds storage and processing requirements.","constraint":"Comparison must avoid storing unnecessary sensitive request data.","edge_case":"Semantically equivalent payloads may have different serialization but represent the same operation.","security_implication":"Conflict handling must not reveal sensitive details about another operation.","performance_implication":"Canonicalization or request fingerprinting may add computation.","testing_guidance":"Reuse keys with identical, semantically equivalent, and materially different requests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that incompatible key reuse cannot create a second unintended operation.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B52-0001"],"prerequisites":["idempotency key scope","request identity"],"related_records":["D15-API-B53-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B52-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Idempotency Conflict Handling","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines behavior for incompatible reuse of an operation identity."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B53-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Retry Semantics","concept":"Retry-Safe API Contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should determine whether an API request may be retried?","answer":"Retry eligibility should be determined by the operation's idempotency semantics, failure classification, timeout state, server-side processing state, and any explicit retry contract.","explanation":"A client timeout does not prove that the server did not process the request.","principle":"Retry based on known operation semantics and failure evidence rather than assuming transport failure means server non-execution.","correct_example":"A client retries a safely idempotent operation after a transient transport failure according to the API's documented retry policy.","invalid_example":"Retry every timed-out POST automatically because no response was received.","problem":"Client cannot distinguish unknown execution state from confirmed non-execution.","failure_mode":"Duplicate side effects occur.","cause":"Transport outcome was incorrectly treated as application outcome.","solution":"Combine operation idempotency with explicit retry and operation-status semantics.","alternative":"Use an idempotency key or operation resource for uncertain asynchronous or non-idempotent operations.","tradeoff":"Safer retries require more explicit API design.","constraint":"Retry policies must account for server processing that may continue after the client disconnects.","edge_case":"A response may be lost after the server commits the operation.","security_implication":"Repeated privileged actions can amplify abuse if replay controls are weak.","performance_implication":"Aggressive retries can create load amplification.","testing_guidance":"Inject response loss, connection resets, timeouts, and delayed responses.","verification_guidance":"Verify that retry behavior matches documented side-effect semantics.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B35-0002","D15-API-B51-0001"],"prerequisites":["retry semantics","failure classification"],"related_records":["D15-API-B53-0002","D15-API-B54-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B51-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Retry-Safe API Contracts","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines retry decisions using operation semantics and execution uncertainty."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B53-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Retry Semantics","concept":"Retry Amplification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is retry amplification in an API system?","answer":"Retry amplification occurs when clients, gateways, services, or libraries independently retry failures and collectively generate substantially more requests against an already degraded dependency.","explanation":"Multiple retry layers can multiply traffic instead of improving recovery.","principle":"Define ownership and limits for retries across the request path.","correct_example":"Only a designated layer retries a transient dependency failure while other layers propagate the failure according to their contracts.","invalid_example":"Client, gateway, service, database driver, and queue consumer all independently retry the same operation without coordination.","problem":"Failure creates increasing request load.","failure_mode":"A transient dependency failure becomes a broader availability incident.","cause":"Uncoordinated retry policies.","solution":"Bound retries, use backoff, identify the retry owner, and avoid stacking unlimited retry loops.","alternative":"Use asynchronous queues or circuit breaking for workloads that should not remain synchronously retry-bound.","tradeoff":"Fewer retries can reduce recovery attempts while protecting system stability.","constraint":"Retry budgets should be finite and observable.","edge_case":"A lower-level library may retry implicitly and must be included in retry analysis.","security_implication":"Retry storms can contribute to denial-of-service conditions.","performance_implication":"Repeated work increases latency, CPU, network, and dependency load.","testing_guidance":"Inject dependency failures while measuring request multiplication across layers.","verification_guidance":"Trace retries across the complete request path and verify bounded behavior.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B53-0001"],"prerequisites":["retry policy","distributed systems"],"related_records":["D15-API-B54-0001","D15-API-B55-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B53-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Retry Amplification","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes load multiplication caused by independent retry layers."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B54-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Retry Semantics","concept":"Exponential Backoff","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can exponential backoff be used for API retries?","answer":"Exponential backoff increases the delay between successive retry attempts, reducing immediate pressure on a recovering dependency and spreading retry traffic over time.","explanation":"Backoff does not by itself guarantee safe retries; the underlying operation must still have appropriate retry semantics.","principle":"Combine bounded retries with increasing delay for transient failure recovery.","correct_example":"A client retries a documented transient failure with bounded exponential delays and a maximum attempt or time budget.","invalid_example":"Retry continuously with zero delay until the server responds.","problem":"Retries arrive immediately and repeatedly during dependency recovery.","failure_mode":"Load increases while the dependency is already degraded.","cause":"No retry pacing or bound.","solution":"Use bounded backoff and explicit retry budgets.","alternative":"Use queue-based asynchronous processing when immediate synchronous retry is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Longer backoff reduces pressure but can increase end-to-end latency.","constraint":"Backoff limits must reflect user-facing latency requirements.","edge_case":"Synchronized clients can still retry simultaneously if they use identical schedules.","security_implication":"Backoff can reduce accidental retry floods but is not a complete abuse-control mechanism.","performance_implication":"Backoff reduces instantaneous load while increasing waiting time.","testing_guidance":"Simulate transient failures and verify bounded retry timing and attempt counts.","verification_guidance":"Measure actual retry behavior before claiming a specific backoff implementation.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B53-0002"],"prerequisites":["retry policy"],"related_records":["D15-API-B54-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B53-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","title":"Exponential Backoff","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes increasing retry delays for transient failures."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B54-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Retry Semantics","concept":"Jittered Retry","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does jitter address in distributed API retries?","answer":"Jitter introduces controlled variation into retry delays so clients that experience a common failure are less likely to retry simultaneously.","explanation":"Without variation, clients using the same deterministic backoff schedule can synchronize their retry attempts.","principle":"Desynchronize large populations of retrying clients when coordinated retry timing could create load spikes.","correct_example":"Clients apply a documented random or bounded variation to retry delays while respecting maximum retry limits.","invalid_example":"Every client waits exactly the same delay sequence before retrying a shared dependency.","problem":"Large client populations synchronize retries.","failure_mode":"Periodic retry spikes overload a recovering dependency.","cause":"Deterministic retry schedules.","solution":"Use bounded jitter in the retry strategy where appropriate.","alternative":"Centralized queueing can absorb bursts and control downstream consumption.","tradeoff":"Jitter makes exact retry timing less predictable while improving distribution of load.","constraint":"Jitter must remain bounded by the overall retry budget.","edge_case":"Small request populations may gain little from jitter.","security_implication":"Jitter can reduce accidental coordinated load but does not replace rate limiting.","performance_implication":"Distributed retries may reduce peak load while increasing variation in completion times.","testing_guidance":"Simulate many concurrent clients and inspect retry-time distribution.","verification_guidance":"Measure retry timing distribution rather than assuming jitter is effective from configuration alone.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B54-0001"],"prerequisites":["backoff","retry control"],"related_records":["D15-API-B55-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B54-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","title":"Jittered Retries","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes retry desynchronization."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B55-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Concurrent Duplicate Request Handling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What can happen when identical API operations arrive concurrently?","answer":"Concurrent identical requests can both pass preliminary checks before either commits its result, producing duplicate or conflicting side effects unless atomicity, uniqueness, locking, or idempotency mechanisms coordinate them.","explanation":"A check-then-act sequence is not automatically safe under concurrency.","principle":"Design duplicate suppression and uniqueness guarantees as atomic operations where required.","correct_example":"A database uniqueness constraint or transactional idempotency record prevents two concurrent requests from creating the same logical resource.","invalid_example":"Check whether a resource exists and then create it in separate non-atomic operations without a uniqueness constraint.","problem":"Concurrent requests observe the same precondition.","failure_mode":"Duplicate resources or conflicting updates are committed.","cause":"Race condition between validation and state mutation.","solution":"Use transactional constraints, atomic operations, or coordinated idempotency state.","alternative":"Serialize operations through a queue when the domain requires ordered processing.","tradeoff":"Stronger concurrency control can reduce parallelism.","constraint":"The consistency mechanism must cover the actual side effect boundary.","edge_case":"Distributed services may require coordination across multiple stores.","security_implication":"Race conditions can bypass authorization, quota, or uniqueness assumptions.","performance_implication":"Locks and transactional coordination can reduce throughput under contention.","testing_guidance":"Run concurrent identical requests and verify invariant preservation.","verification_guidance":"Use deterministic concurrency tests where practical and inspect persistent state afterward.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B51-0002","D15-API-B52-0001"],"prerequisites":["concurrency","state mutation"],"related_records":["D15-API-B55-0002","D15-API-B56-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B51-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Concurrent systems engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Concurrent Duplicate API Requests","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes race conditions around duplicate requests."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B55-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Optimistic Concurrency Contract","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can an API prevent lost updates when multiple clients modify the same resource?","answer":"An API can use an optimistic concurrency mechanism in which the client submits a representation version, validator, or other precondition and the server rejects the update when the resource has changed since that version was observed.","explanation":"The mechanism allows concurrent clients to detect conflicting modifications instead of silently overwriting one another.","principle":"Require the client to prove that its update is based on an acceptable resource version when lost updates are unacceptable.","correct_example":"An update includes a conditional request precondition tied to the resource's current representation version.","invalid_example":"Always overwrite the current resource regardless of whether another client modified it after the caller read it.","problem":"Concurrent updates silently overwrite one another.","failure_mode":"Data loss or unexpected state transitions occur.","cause":"No concurrency precondition was enforced.","solution":"Use conditional requests or another explicit version-check mechanism.","alternative":"Server-side serialization or pessimistic locking can provide stronger coordination when required.","tradeoff":"Optimistic concurrency improves parallelism but requires conflict handling by clients.","constraint":"The version or validator must accurately represent the state relevant to the update.","edge_case":"Some updates are commutative and may not require conflict rejection.","security_implication":"Concurrency controls can also protect security-sensitive state transitions from race conditions.","performance_implication":"Optimistic checks generally avoid long-held locks but can increase retry or conflict rates.","testing_guidance":"Perform concurrent reads and updates and verify stale updates are rejected according to the contract.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that a stale client cannot overwrite a newer protected state.","language":"API Design","framework":"HTTP","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"HTTP semantics applicable to conditional requests","dependencies":["D15-API-B37-0002"],"prerequisites":["conditional requests","concurrency"],"related_records":["D15-API-B56-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B37-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"IETF HTTP semantics","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","version":"RFC 9110","section":"Conditional Requests","relevance":"Defines HTTP conditional request mechanisms used to prevent unintended state changes."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B56-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Pagination","concept":"Pagination Contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an API pagination contract define?","answer":"A pagination contract should define page size behavior, boundaries, continuation semantics, ordering requirements, invalid cursor or page parameters, maximum limits, and what consistency guarantees apply while traversing a collection.","explanation":"Pagination is part of the API contract because clients need deterministic rules for retrieving collections safely and predictably.","principle":"Define pagination semantics independently of implementation details.","correct_example":"The API documents a maximum page size, stable ordering, and a defined continuation mechanism.","invalid_example":"Accept an arbitrary page parameter without documenting limits, ordering, or behavior when data changes.","problem":"Clients cannot reliably traverse large collections.","failure_mode":"Skipped, duplicated, or unexpectedly expensive results occur.","cause":"Pagination semantics are underspecified.","solution":"Specify bounded pagination behavior and collection ordering semantics.","alternative":"Cursor-based pagination can represent continuation state without relying solely on offsets.","tradeoff":"More robust pagination can make client implementation more complex.","constraint":"Pagination must protect server resources from unbounded collection requests.","edge_case":"Records may be inserted or deleted between page requests.","security_implication":"Pagination controls can limit resource-exhaustion and enumeration risks.","performance_implication":"Pagination prevents unbounded response and query workloads.","testing_guidance":"Test first, middle, last, empty, oversized, invalid, and concurrent-data scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify documented limits and ordering against actual responses.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B15-0001"],"prerequisites":["collection design","resource limits"],"related_records":["D15-API-B56-0002","D15-API-B57-0001"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API collection design","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Pagination Contracts","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines pagination as an explicit API contract."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B56-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Pagination","concept":"Offset Pagination","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What are the main characteristics of offset-based pagination?","answer":"Offset pagination identifies a page by an offset and limit or equivalent parameters, making it simple to understand but potentially sensitive to inserts, deletes, and increasing traversal cost on some data stores.","explanation":"Its suitability depends on collection size, ordering requirements, storage technology, and consistency expectations.","principle":"Use offset pagination when its consistency and performance characteristics fit the collection.","correct_example":"A small administrative collection uses bounded offset and limit parameters with a defined stable sort order.","invalid_example":"Use unbounded offsets over a very large mutable collection without considering query cost or result drift.","problem":"Deep or mutable pagination becomes inefficient or inconsistent.","failure_mode":"Requests become increasingly expensive or records are skipped or repeated.","cause":"Offset semantics were applied without considering data characteristics.","solution":"Bound offsets, define ordering, and evaluate whether cursor-based pagination is more appropriate.","alternative":"Cursor-based pagination can better represent continuation through large ordered datasets.","tradeoff":"Offset pagination is simple while cursor pagination often requires more complex client and server semantics.","constraint":"A deterministic ordering should be defined when page consistency matters.","edge_case":"Concurrent inserts before the current offset can shift later records.","security_implication":"Large unbounded offsets can contribute to resource-exhaustion conditions.","performance_implication":"Deep offsets may require the data store to scan or skip substantial numbers of records.","testing_guidance":"Test large offsets and concurrent insertion/deletion behavior.","verification_guidance":"Measure query behavior before making performance claims.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B56-0001"],"prerequisites":["pagination","ordering"],"related_records":["D15-API-B57-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B56-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API collection design","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Offset Pagination","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes offset-based collection traversal and its tradeoffs."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B57-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Pagination","concept":"Cursor Pagination","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can cursor-based pagination be useful?","answer":"Cursor-based pagination can be useful for large or frequently changing ordered collections because a cursor can represent a continuation position without requiring the server to skip an arbitrary number of preceding records.","explanation":"The cursor should be treated as an API contract artifact with defined ordering, scope, expiration, and invalidation semantics.","principle":"Represent collection continuation using a stable ordering position appropriate to the underlying data.","correct_example":"A collection returns a cursor tied to a documented sort order and the next request uses that cursor to continue traversal.","invalid_example":"Expose an opaque cursor while changing the underlying ordering between requests without documenting the resulting semantics.","problem":"Continuation state does not correspond to a stable collection order.","failure_mode":"Records are skipped, repeated, or returned unpredictably.","cause":"Cursor semantics are detached from ordering semantics.","solution":"Define cursor encoding, ordering, scope, lifetime, and invalidation behavior.","alternative":"Offset pagination may be simpler for small stable collections.","tradeoff":"Cursor pagination can improve traversal characteristics but is more complex to implement and debug.","constraint":"Cursors should be treated as untrusted input and validated server-side.","edge_case":"A cursor may become invalid after schema, authorization, or ordering changes.","security_implication":"Cursors should not expose sensitive internal identifiers or authorization context.","performance_implication":"Proper cursor queries can avoid deep offset traversal, but actual performance depends on the data store and query plan.","testing_guidance":"Test cursor replay, malformed cursors, expired cursors, changed data, and boundary conditions.","verification_guidance":"Benchmark only with the actual storage/query implementation; otherwise mark performance as unknown.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B56-0001"],"prerequisites":["pagination","stable ordering"],"related_records":["D15-API-B57-0002","D15-API-B58-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B56-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D15-API-B56-0002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API collection design","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","title":"Cursor Pagination","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes continuation-token pagination for ordered collections."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B57-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Pagination","concept":"Cursor Integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should an API validate cursor values instead of trusting them?","answer":"A cursor is client-controlled input and may be malformed, modified, replayed outside its intended scope, or crafted to expose internal query behavior unless the server validates its structure, integrity, scope, and lifetime.","explanation":"Opaque presentation does not automatically make a cursor trustworthy.","principle":"Treat pagination cursors as untrusted request input.","correct_example":"The server validates a signed or server-stored cursor against the requesting principal, collection, ordering, and expiration rules.","invalid_example":"Decode a client-provided cursor and directly use embedded database identifiers without validation.","problem":"Client-controlled continuation state influences protected data access.","failure_mode":"Authorization bypass, invalid queries, or data disclosure occurs.","cause":"Cursor contents were trusted implicitly.","solution":"Validate cursor integrity and bind it to appropriate request context.","alternative":"Store cursor state server-side and issue an opaque reference.","tradeoff":"Signed cursors reduce server storage while server-side state can simplify revocation and invalidation.","constraint":"Cursor design must not expose secrets or sensitive internal state.","edge_case":"A cursor generated under one authorization scope may become invalid after permissions change.","security_implication":"Cursor validation is part of collection authorization.","performance_implication":"Signature validation or state lookup adds processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Tamper with cursor contents, change principal context, replay old cursors, and submit malformed values.","verification_guidance":"Verify invalid or out-of-scope cursors cannot expand access.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B57-0001","D15-API-B45-0001"],"prerequisites":["cursor pagination","authorization"],"related_records":["D15-API-B58-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B57-0001","D15-API-B45-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Secure Pagination Cursor Handling","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines cursors as untrusted input requiring integrity and scope validation."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B58-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Filtering","concept":"Explicit Filtering Contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should API filtering parameters be designed?","answer":"Filtering parameters should have explicit names, types, supported operators, validation rules, authorization implications, and documented behavior for unsupported or invalid values.","explanation":"Generic query languages can unintentionally expose internal fields or expensive operations if their allowed semantics are not constrained.","principle":"Expose an intentional filtering language rather than directly exposing internal query syntax.","correct_example":"The API documents supported filters such as status and created_after with explicit types and allowed operators.","invalid_example":"Pass arbitrary database expressions from a query parameter directly to the persistence layer.","problem":"Client input becomes an uncontrolled query language.","failure_mode":"Unexpected data access, expensive queries, or injection vulnerabilities occur.","cause":"Filtering semantics were delegated directly to infrastructure.","solution":"Define an allowlisted filtering grammar and validate every operator and field.","alternative":"Provide dedicated endpoint parameters for a small number of common filters.","tradeoff":"A richer filter language improves flexibility but increases validation and security complexity.","constraint":"Only authorized fields and operations should be filterable.","edge_case":"A field may be safe to return but unsafe or too expensive to filter.","security_implication":"Filtering can become a data-discovery and query-injection boundary.","performance_implication":"Complex filters can cause expensive database execution.","testing_guidance":"Test unsupported fields, operators, types, nested expressions, large values, and adversarial inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the filter grammar maps only to intended query operations.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B36-0001","D15-API-B45-0001"],"prerequisites":["request validation","authorization"],"related_records":["D15-API-B58-0002","D15-API-B59-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B36-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API query design","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Explicit API Filtering Contracts","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines constrained filtering semantics and validation."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B58-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Filtering","concept":"Filter Field Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can filterable fields require authorization controls?","answer":"A field may contain sensitive information or enable inference about protected data even when the field is not directly returned in the response.","explanation":"Filtering can act as an information-discovery mechanism because the existence or count of matching records may reveal protected facts.","principle":"Treat filter capability as a potential information-access permission.","correct_example":"Sensitive fields are excluded from filtering unless the caller has an explicit permission to query them.","invalid_example":"Allow every client to filter by internal security classification or private account attributes.","problem":"Query capability exposes information outside the response schema.","failure_mode":"Callers infer protected information through query results or counts.","cause":"Filtering was treated as purely functional rather than security-sensitive.","solution":"Authorize filterable fields and operators separately where needed.","alternative":"Expose safe derived filters instead of raw sensitive attributes.","tradeoff":"Restricting filters reduces flexibility but limits information leakage.","constraint":"Filter authorization must remain consistent with resource-level authorization.","edge_case":"Even boolean filters can reveal information through result count or existence.","security_implication":"Filter semantics can contribute to inference and enumeration attacks.","performance_implication":"Restricting filters can prevent expensive unindexed queries.","testing_guidance":"Test sensitive fields through direct filters, negation, ranges, and combinations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that denied filter fields cannot indirectly reveal protected information.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B58-0001"],"prerequisites":["filtering","authorization"],"related_records":["D15-API-B59-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B58-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Authorization of Filter Operations","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes security implications of exposing filtering capability."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B59-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Sorting","concept":"Deterministic Sorting","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should API collection sorting be deterministic when pagination is involved?","answer":"A deterministic sort order reduces ambiguity when multiple records have equal sort values and helps clients traverse a collection without unexpected reordering between pages.","explanation":"A primary sort field alone may not uniquely order records. A stable tie-breaker can provide deterministic ordering when required.","principle":"Define stable ordering semantics when clients depend on repeatable collection traversal.","correct_example":"Sort by created_at and then by a unique resource identifier to establish a deterministic order.","invalid_example":"Sort only by a non-unique timestamp and assume the database will always return ties in the same order.","problem":"Records with equal sort values can move between pages.","failure_mode":"Clients observe duplicate or missing records during pagination.","cause":"Ordering was not fully deterministic.","solution":"Define a stable tie-breaker and document ordering semantics.","alternative":"Use a server-generated sequence or another stable ordering key appropriate to the domain.","tradeoff":"Additional ordering fields may increase query complexity.","constraint":"The ordering key must remain compatible with the pagination mechanism.","edge_case":"Mutable sort fields can still cause records to move between pages even with deterministic tie-breaking.","security_implication":"Sorting should not expose fields the caller is not authorized to use.","performance_implication":"Additional sort keys can affect index requirements and query cost.","testing_guidance":"Create equal sort values and traverse multiple pages while inserting and updating records.","verification_guidance":"Verify ordering behavior against the documented contract rather than relying on storage defaults.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B56-0001"],"prerequisites":["pagination","collection ordering"],"related_records":["D15-API-B59-0002","D15-API-B60-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B56-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API collection design","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Deterministic Collection Ordering","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines stable sorting requirements for predictable pagination."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B59-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Sorting","concept":"Sort Field Allowlisting","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should APIs allowlist sortable fields and directions?","answer":"Allowlisting sortable fields and directions prevents clients from selecting unsupported, sensitive, or unexpectedly expensive query operations.","explanation":"A generic sort parameter can become an uncontrolled query feature if every internal field is exposed.","principle":"Expose only intentionally supported sorting semantics.","correct_example":"The API permits sorting by documented public fields and rejects unsupported fields with a validation error.","invalid_example":"Pass any client-supplied field name directly to a database ORDER BY expression.","problem":"Internal schema details become externally queryable.","failure_mode":"Information disclosure, inefficient queries, or query manipulation can occur.","cause":"Sort input was passed through without semantic validation.","solution":"Validate sort fields and directions against an explicit allowlist.","alternative":"Offer predefined sort modes for simpler APIs.","tradeoff":"Allowlisting reduces flexibility but improves predictability and security.","constraint":"Sortable fields must be compatible with authorization and data-classification requirements.","edge_case":"A field may be safe to expose but unsuitable for sorting due to resource cost.","security_implication":"Sort capability can expose internal fields or support inference attacks.","performance_implication":"Unindexed sorting can require expensive database operations.","testing_guidance":"Test unknown fields, nested paths, injection-like values, excessive sort combinations, and unauthorized fields.","verification_guidance":"Verify that only documented sort expressions reach the persistence layer.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B59-0001"],"prerequisites":["sorting","input validation"],"related_records":["D15-API-B60-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B59-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API query security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Sortable Field Allowlisting","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines safe sorting parameter validation."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B60-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"B51-B60 Synthesis","concept":"Collection Query Contract","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a complete API collection-query contract define?","answer":"It should define pagination, ordering, filtering, sorting, limits, validation, authorization implications, consistency expectations, invalid parameter behavior, and resource protection requirements.","explanation":"Collection querying combines multiple independent controls. A robust contract must define how they interact rather than documenting each parameter in isolation.","principle":"Treat collection traversal and querying as one coherent API contract.","correct_example":"An endpoint documents allowed filters and sort fields, maximum page size, cursor semantics, deterministic ordering, authorization scope, and invalid-parameter behavior.","invalid_example":"Document page and sort parameters separately while leaving their interaction, limits, and security semantics undefined.","problem":"Clients and implementations derive inconsistent behavior from underspecified query semantics.","failure_mode":"Pagination drift, unauthorized filtering, unstable sorting, or resource exhaustion occurs.","cause":"Query features were designed independently.","solution":"Define and test the complete collection-query contract as an integrated system.","alternative":"Expose specialized endpoints when a generic query language becomes too complex or risky.","tradeoff":"A unified query contract improves flexibility but increases design and testing burden.","constraint":"All client-controlled query features must have explicit bounds.","edge_case":"Mutable data can change between requests even with deterministic ordering.","security_implication":"Filtering, sorting, and pagination collectively affect data exposure and resource consumption.","performance_implication":"Combined query parameters can produce expensive query plans and require appropriate indexes or limits.","testing_guidance":"Test combinations of filters, sorting, pagination, invalid values, authorization scopes, and large datasets.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual query behavior against the declared contract and mark unmeasured performance characteristics as UNKNOWN.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B56-0001","D15-API-B58-0001","D15-API-B59-0001"],"prerequisites":["pagination","filtering","sorting","authorization"],"related_records":["D15-API-B60-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B56-0001","D15-API-B58-0001","D15-API-B59-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API collection design","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Integrated Collection Query Contract","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Integrates pagination, filtering, sorting, authorization, and resource limits."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B60-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"B51-B60 Quality Gate","concept":"Idempotency Retry Query Quality Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be verified before API retry and collection-query semantics are considered complete?","answer":"The API must demonstrate defined idempotency and retry behavior, bounded retry amplification, concurrency safety for protected mutations, explicit pagination semantics, validated filtering and sorting, deterministic ordering where required, and authorization-aware query behavior.","explanation":"These features interact directly with reliability, correctness, security, and resource consumption.","principle":"Verify cross-feature behavior rather than validating each parameter or mechanism in isolation.","correct_example":"Integration tests exercise retries, duplicate requests, concurrent mutations, pagination, filtering, sorting, and authorization together.","invalid_example":"Unit-test the pagination parser and separately unit-test retry logic while never testing their integrated behavior under failure.","problem":"Individual components pass tests while system-level semantics remain incorrect.","failure_mode":"Production behavior violates the API contract under concurrency, retries, or large collections.","cause":"Verification is component-only.","solution":"Add integration, negative, concurrency, failure-injection, and boundary tests covering the complete contract.","alternative":"Formal contract testing can supplement integration testing where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Integrated testing requires more setup and execution time.","constraint":"Claims about performance or execution must remain NOT_VERIFIED until actual evidence exists.","edge_case":"Failure after server-side commit but before response delivery must be included in retry testing.","security_implication":"Query features must be tested as potential authorization and enumeration boundaries.","performance_implication":"Large collection and retry tests should characterize actual behavior rather than rely on theoretical assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate operations, lost responses, concurrent requests, malformed cursors, unauthorized filters, unstable ordering, and extreme page sizes.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence for each security, reliability, and performance claim; retain UNKNOWN or NOT_EXECUTED where evidence is absent.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B50-0002","D15-API-B60-0001"],"prerequisites":["API security","retry semantics","collection querying"],"related_records":["D15-API-B40-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B50-0002","D15-API-B60-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API engineering verification","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Integrated API Reliability and Query Quality Gate","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines cross-feature verification requirements for API reliability and collection querying."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

