{"record_id":"D15-API-B71-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Rate Limiting","concept":"Rate Limit Contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an API rate-limit contract define?","answer":"A rate-limit contract should define the protected scope, counting unit, applicable identity or resource boundary, enforcement behavior, response semantics, and whether limits are fixed, sliding, token-based, or otherwise defined.","explanation":"A numeric limit without a defined counting scope is ambiguous and can produce inconsistent client behavior.","principle":"Define rate limiting as an explicit contract rather than an undocumented infrastructure behavior.","correct_example":"An API documents that requests are limited per authenticated principal within a defined time policy and specifies the response behavior when the limit is exceeded.","invalid_example":"State only that the API has a rate limit without defining what is counted or for whom.","problem":"Clients cannot predict or correctly handle throttling.","failure_mode":"Legitimate traffic is unexpectedly rejected or abusive traffic bypasses the intended boundary.","cause":"Rate-limit scope and semantics are undefined.","solution":"Specify identity, resource, operation, time, counting, and rejection semantics.","alternative":"Different endpoint classes may use different rate-limit policies.","tradeoff":"More precise limits improve control but increase policy and operational complexity.","constraint":"Limits must account for the actual resource being protected.","edge_case":"A single principal may generate traffic through multiple client instances or network addresses.","security_implication":"Rate limiting can reduce abuse and resource-exhaustion risk but is not authentication or authorization.","performance_implication":"Rate-limit counters introduce state and processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test below-limit, exactly-at-limit, over-limit, concurrent, and reset-boundary behavior.","verification_guidance":"Measure actual enforcement behavior before claiming a specific rate or algorithm.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B35-0002"],"prerequisites":["resource protection","request classification"],"related_records":["D15-API-B71-0002","D15-API-B72-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B35-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Rate Limit Contracts","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines rate limiting as an explicit API behavior and resource-protection contract."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B71-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Rate Limiting","concept":"Rate Limit Identity Scope","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must an API define what identity or resource scope a rate limit applies to?","answer":"A rate limit is meaningful only when its counting boundary is defined, such as authenticated principal, tenant, API credential, source network, resource, or operation class.","explanation":"Different scopes produce different protection behavior and can either over-limit legitimate clients or fail to isolate abusive traffic.","principle":"Align the rate-limit identity boundary with the resource and abuse model being controlled.","correct_example":"A sensitive mutation is limited per authenticated principal and separately protected by a global service capacity limit.","invalid_example":"Apply one shared limit to all clients and assume it provides per-user protection.","problem":"The chosen limit does not correspond to the intended abuse boundary.","failure_mode":"One client can consume capacity intended for many clients, or legitimate clients are unfairly throttled.","cause":"Rate-limit scope was selected without threat and capacity analysis.","solution":"Define layered limits where different resources require different protection boundaries.","alternative":"Tenant-level limits can be used when tenant capacity isolation is the primary requirement.","tradeoff":"Multiple limits provide stronger isolation but increase policy complexity.","constraint":"Rate-limit identifiers must not rely solely on attacker-controlled values.","edge_case":"Unauthenticated traffic may require a different protection mechanism than authenticated traffic.","security_implication":"Incorrect identity scope can enable denial-of-service or quota bypass.","performance_implication":"Multiple counters increase state-management overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test traffic distribution across users, tenants, credentials, IP addresses, and resources.","verification_guidance":"Verify the effective scope using controlled traffic experiments.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B71-0001"],"prerequisites":["rate limiting","identity"],"related_records":["D15-API-B72-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B71-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API security engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Rate Limit Identity Boundaries","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Explains how rate-limit scope should correspond to security and capacity boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B72-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Rate Limiting","concept":"Retry-After Semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should an API communicate temporary request throttling to clients?","answer":"When the API defines a retry-after signal, it should communicate when or after what delay a client may retry according to the API's documented semantics rather than requiring clients to infer timing from arbitrary failures.","explanation":"A throttled response should give clients enough information to avoid immediate retry storms when the protocol and API contract support such guidance.","principle":"Make temporary throttling behavior explicit and machine-actionable where practical.","correct_example":"A rate-limited response includes a documented retry timing signal that clients can use with their retry policy.","invalid_example":"Return a generic server error and expect clients to retry continuously until the limit clears.","problem":"Clients cannot distinguish throttling from unrelated server failure.","failure_mode":"Clients retry aggressively and amplify load.","cause":"Throttle semantics are not communicated clearly.","solution":"Use an explicit documented throttling response and retry guidance.","alternative":"Clients may apply bounded local backoff when no retry timing is supplied.","tradeoff":"More explicit signals improve interoperability but reveal additional operational timing information.","constraint":"The API must not promise a retry time that the implementation cannot actually support.","edge_case":"Different rate-limit scopes may recover at different times.","security_implication":"Rate-limit responses should avoid exposing sensitive internal capacity information unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"Correct retry timing can reduce repeated rejected requests.","testing_guidance":"Verify response behavior at rate-limit boundaries and after recovery.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that advertised retry timing corresponds to actual enforcement semantics.","language":"API Design","framework":"HTTP","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"HTTP semantics","dependencies":["D15-API-B71-0001"],"prerequisites":["rate limiting","HTTP response semantics"],"related_records":["D15-API-B72-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B71-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"IETF HTTP semantics","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","version":"RFC 9110","section":"Retry-After","relevance":"Defines HTTP Retry-After semantics applicable to temporary service conditions."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B72-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Rate Limiting","concept":"Quota Versus Rate Limit","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does a quota differ from a rate limit?","answer":"A rate limit constrains request or resource consumption over a defined rate or interval, while a quota generally constrains an allocated amount of usage over a broader accounting period or resource boundary.","explanation":"The exact semantics are API-specific and should be documented rather than inferred from terminology alone.","principle":"Distinguish short-term traffic protection from longer-term usage allocation.","correct_example":"An API uses rate limits to protect service capacity and a separate monthly quota to control allocated usage.","invalid_example":"Treat a monthly quota and per-second rate limit as interchangeable controls.","problem":"Capacity protection and usage accounting become conflated.","failure_mode":"Clients either exhaust allocated usage or overload the service despite one control appearing satisfied.","cause":"Different resource objectives were represented by one mechanism.","solution":"Define separate rate and quota semantics where both are required.","alternative":"A unified usage policy can expose multiple dimensions explicitly.","tradeoff":"Multiple controls improve precision but increase client complexity.","constraint":"Quota accounting must define reset, rollover, and overage semantics.","edge_case":"A client may remain within a monthly quota while exceeding instantaneous service capacity.","security_implication":"Quotas can limit abuse but should not replace authorization.","performance_implication":"Quota accounting can require durable usage tracking.","testing_guidance":"Test short-term bursts, long-term consumption, reset boundaries, and concurrent usage.","verification_guidance":"Verify rate and quota counters independently.","language":"API Design","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B71-0001"],"prerequisites":["rate limiting","usage accounting"],"related_records":["D15-API-B73-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B71-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API capacity engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Rate Limits and Usage Quotas","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Distinguishes instantaneous capacity protection from allocated usage."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B73-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Concurrency Control","concept":"Concurrency Limit","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What does an API concurrency limit protect?","answer":"A concurrency limit bounds the number of operations that may be actively executing or occupying a defined resource at the same time.","explanation":"Concurrency limits differ from request-rate limits because a low request rate can still create excessive concurrent work when individual operations are long-running.","principle":"Limit simultaneous work when active operations consume bounded resources.","correct_example":"A computationally expensive API limits the number of simultaneous executions per principal or service capacity pool.","invalid_example":"Assume a low requests-per-second rate guarantees safe concurrency for operations that take a long time.","problem":"Long-running requests accumulate and exhaust resources.","failure_mode":"Worker pools, memory, connections, or downstream capacity become saturated.","cause":"Only request arrival rate was controlled.","solution":"Add concurrency limits appropriate to the protected resource.","alternative":"Queue asynchronous work and bound worker concurrency separately.","tradeoff":"Concurrency limits protect capacity but can increase request waiting or rejection.","constraint":"The limit must correspond to actual resource consumption and workload behavior.","edge_case":"A single request may fan out into multiple downstream operations and consume more concurrency than its request count suggests.","security_implication":"Concurrency controls can reduce resource-exhaustion attacks.","performance_implication":"Appropriate concurrency can stabilize throughput under contention.","testing_guidance":"Run long-lived requests until the concurrency boundary is reached and inspect behavior.","verification_guidance":"Measure actual resource occupancy before selecting production limits.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B71-0001"],"prerequisites":["resource capacity","rate limiting"],"related_records":["D15-API-B73-0002","D15-API-B74-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B71-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems capacity engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","title":"API Concurrency Limits","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines concurrency as a separate resource-protection dimension from request rate."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B73-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Concurrency Control","concept":"Per-Operation Concurrency Policy","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why might different API operations require different concurrency limits?","answer":"API operations can consume substantially different resources, so one global concurrency limit may fail to protect expensive operations or unnecessarily restrict inexpensive ones.","explanation":"Concurrency policy should reflect workload characteristics rather than endpoint count alone.","principle":"Align concurrency controls with resource consumption classes.","correct_example":"An expensive report-generation endpoint has a lower concurrency limit than a lightweight metadata endpoint.","invalid_example":"Allow identical concurrency for all endpoints regardless of execution cost.","problem":"A shared limit does not match workload cost.","failure_mode":"Expensive operations consume disproportionate capacity or cheap operations are unnecessarily throttled.","cause":"Workload heterogeneity was ignored.","solution":"Classify operations by resource cost and apply appropriate concurrency policies.","alternative":"Use a global capacity pool combined with weighted scheduling.","tradeoff":"Per-operation policies improve control but increase configuration complexity.","constraint":"Resource classes must be measurable or otherwise explicitly defined.","edge_case":"Operation cost can vary substantially based on request parameters.","security_implication":"Attackers may select expensive parameters to bypass assumptions based on endpoint-level limits.","performance_implication":"Weighted concurrency can improve resource utilization when correctly configured.","testing_guidance":"Exercise expensive and inexpensive parameter combinations under concurrent load.","verification_guidance":"Measure resource consumption across representative workloads.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B73-0001"],"prerequisites":["concurrency limits","resource classification"],"related_records":["D15-API-B74-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B73-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API capacity engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Operation-Specific Concurrency Controls","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines workload-aware concurrency control."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B74-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"API Backpressure","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is backpressure in an API system?","answer":"Backpressure is a mechanism by which a system limits, delays, rejects, or otherwise regulates incoming work when downstream processing capacity cannot safely keep up with demand.","explanation":"Backpressure prevents uncontrolled accumulation of work and makes capacity limits explicit.","principle":"Do not accept unlimited work when downstream capacity is bounded.","correct_example":"An API rejects or queues additional work once its bounded processing capacity is reached.","invalid_example":"Accept unlimited synchronous requests and allow memory or connection usage to grow until the process fails.","problem":"Demand exceeds processing capacity without a control mechanism.","failure_mode":"Queues, memory, workers, or downstream services become exhausted.","cause":"Ingress was decoupled from available capacity.","solution":"Apply bounded queues, concurrency limits, rate limits, or controlled rejection.","alternative":"Asynchronous job submission can decouple request acceptance from processing while retaining bounded queue capacity.","tradeoff":"Backpressure can reduce availability for excess traffic while improving system stability.","constraint":"The API must define what happens when capacity is exhausted.","edge_case":"Some operations can be safely dropped while others require durable acceptance.","security_implication":"Backpressure is an important defense against resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Proper backpressure can stabilize latency and resource utilization under overload.","testing_guidance":"Drive traffic beyond capacity and observe queue, rejection, latency, and recovery behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify bounded resource usage during sustained overload.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B73-0001"],"prerequisites":["capacity limits","concurrency"],"related_records":["D15-API-B74-0002","D15-API-B75-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B73-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","title":"API Backpressure","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines controlled demand regulation under bounded capacity."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B74-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"Bounded Queue","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should asynchronous API work queues usually have explicit capacity semantics?","answer":"A bounded queue makes the amount of accepted but unprocessed work explicit and prevents unbounded accumulation from exhausting memory or persistent infrastructure.","explanation":"Queueing can absorb temporary demand spikes but cannot eliminate a sustained capacity mismatch.","principle":"Use finite queue capacity and define behavior when the queue is full.","correct_example":"An asynchronous API accepts work only while queue capacity is available and returns a documented overload response when the queue is full.","invalid_example":"Allow the queue to grow indefinitely because asynchronous processing will eventually catch up.","problem":"Accepted work grows without a bounded resource limit.","failure_mode":"Storage, memory, or processing resources become exhausted.","cause":"Queue capacity was treated as unlimited.","solution":"Bound the queue and define rejection, expiration, or alternate handling semantics.","alternative":"Use admission control before enqueueing work.","tradeoff":"Bounded queues may reject work during sustained overload while protecting system stability.","constraint":"Capacity must be based on actual resource constraints.","edge_case":"A durable queue may survive process restarts but still requires bounded operational capacity.","security_implication":"Unbounded queues can be abused for resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Queue depth affects latency and should be observable.","testing_guidance":"Fill the queue and verify deterministic full-queue behavior.","verification_guidance":"Measure queue capacity and processing rate in the actual deployment before making capacity claims.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B74-0001"],"prerequisites":["asynchronous APIs","capacity management"],"related_records":["D15-API-B75-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B74-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems queue engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","title":"Bounded Work Queues","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines finite queue capacity as an overload-control mechanism."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B75-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Overload Protection","concept":"Admission Control","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is admission control in an API?","answer":"Admission control determines whether new work should be accepted based on current capacity, policy, priority, quota, or other resource-protection conditions.","explanation":"It prevents the system from accepting more work than it can safely process.","principle":"Decide whether work should enter the system before consuming scarce resources.","correct_example":"A service rejects low-priority work when capacity is exhausted while preserving capacity for protected operations.","invalid_example":"Allocate expensive resources before checking whether the request can be safely admitted.","problem":"Rejected work consumes resources before admission is decided.","failure_mode":"Overload worsens because capacity is consumed by requests that will ultimately fail.","cause":"Admission control occurs too late.","solution":"Perform lightweight admission checks before expensive processing.","alternative":"Use queue-based admission for workloads that can tolerate asynchronous completion.","tradeoff":"Early rejection protects stability but reduces accepted traffic during overload.","constraint":"Admission criteria must not bypass authorization or critical safety requirements.","edge_case":"Priority classes can create starvation if lower-priority work never receives capacity.","security_implication":"Admission control can limit abuse and protect critical security operations.","performance_implication":"Early rejection can reduce wasted computation under overload.","testing_guidance":"Test full capacity, priority competition, quota exhaustion, and dependency degradation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that expensive work is not allocated before admission is established.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B74-0001"],"prerequisites":["backpressure","capacity policy"],"related_records":["D15-API-B75-0002","D15-API-B76-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B74-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","title":"API Admission Control","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines controlled acceptance of new work under capacity constraints."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B75-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Overload Protection","concept":"Priority-Aware Admission","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the tradeoffs of priority-aware API admission control?","answer":"Priority-aware admission can preserve capacity for important operations during overload, but it introduces policy complexity and can cause starvation or unfairness if priorities are poorly designed.","explanation":"Priority should be explicit, bounded, and governed rather than inferred from arbitrary client claims.","principle":"Use priority only when business or safety requirements justify differentiated admission.","correct_example":"A documented critical-operation class receives protected capacity while lower-priority work is constrained during overload.","invalid_example":"Allow clients to self-declare the highest priority without authorization or validation.","problem":"Priority becomes an uncontrolled capacity-bypass mechanism.","failure_mode":"Attackers or noisy clients consume protected capacity.","cause":"Priority identity is not trusted or governed.","solution":"Authorize priority classification and enforce bounded resource reservations.","alternative":"Use separate capacity pools for operationally distinct workloads.","tradeoff":"Reserved capacity improves resilience for critical workloads but can reduce overall utilization.","constraint":"Priority semantics must be documented and testable.","edge_case":"Critical traffic can itself become abusive and still requires rate and authorization controls.","security_implication":"Priority is a security-relevant resource-allocation attribute when it affects protected capacity.","performance_implication":"Reserved capacity can improve tail behavior for protected workloads while reducing available shared capacity.","testing_guidance":"Test competing priority classes under sustained overload.","verification_guidance":"Verify that clients cannot escalate priority through request-controlled fields.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B75-0001","D15-API-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":["admission control","authorization"],"related_records":["D15-API-B76-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B75-0001","D15-API-B42-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API capacity governance","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Priority-Aware Admission Control","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Describes priority-based resource admission and its governance tradeoffs."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B76-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"HTTP Cache Semantics","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must API caching behavior be defined through explicit HTTP cache semantics?","answer":"Caching should follow explicit response metadata and application semantics so that intermediaries and clients can determine whether a representation may be reused and under what conditions.","explanation":"Caching is not merely a performance optimization; incorrect caching can return stale or unauthorized data.","principle":"Define cacheability and freshness as part of the response contract.","correct_example":"An API explicitly defines cache-control behavior for a response whose data is safe to reuse under the stated conditions.","invalid_example":"Cache every successful API response without considering authorization, freshness, or resource sensitivity.","problem":"Cached representations violate data freshness or access boundaries.","failure_mode":"Clients receive stale or unauthorized information.","cause":"Caching was added without API semantic analysis.","solution":"Define cache-control, validation, freshness, and privacy semantics for cacheable responses.","alternative":"Disable caching for responses where reuse is unsafe or not useful.","tradeoff":"Caching can reduce origin load but introduces freshness and invalidation complexity.","constraint":"Private or security-sensitive responses require appropriate cache controls.","edge_case":"A response may be cacheable for one audience but unsafe for a shared intermediary.","security_implication":"Shared-cache mistakes can cause cross-user or cross-tenant data exposure.","performance_implication":"Effective caching can reduce origin requests and latency, but actual gains require measurement.","testing_guidance":"Test cached and uncached requests across users, authorization contexts, and freshness boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Inspect actual cache behavior instead of assuming response headers are sufficient evidence.","language":"API Design","framework":"HTTP","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"HTTP caching semantics","dependencies":["D15-API-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":["HTTP response semantics","authorization"],"related_records":["D15-API-B76-0002","D15-API-B77-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B42-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"IETF HTTP semantics","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","version":"RFC 9110","section":"Caching","relevance":"Defines HTTP caching semantics and response metadata concepts."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B76-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"Authorization-Aware Caching","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must API caches consider authorization context?","answer":"A cached representation may contain information available to one principal but not another, so cache reuse must preserve the intended access-control boundary.","explanation":"A cache can bypass application authorization if it returns a previously authorized representation to a different caller without appropriate cache partitioning or validation.","principle":"Do not allow caching to create a broader audience than the original authorization decision permits.","correct_example":"Responses whose representation depends on authenticated identity use appropriate private caching or cache-key and policy semantics.","invalid_example":"Store a user-specific response in a shared cache without separating authorization context.","problem":"Cache reuse crosses an authorization boundary.","failure_mode":"One user receives another user's protected representation.","cause":"Cache identity did not include the relevant security context.","solution":"Use appropriate cache-control semantics, private caches, validated cache keys, or disable shared caching.","alternative":"Return non-sensitive public representations for shared caching.","tradeoff":"Restricting shared caching may reduce cache efficiency.","constraint":"Authorization-dependent responses require explicit cache analysis.","edge_case":"A response may contain a mix of public and private data and require careful representation design.","security_implication":"Authorization-aware caching is a confidentiality control.","performance_implication":"Private cache partitioning can reduce cache hit rates.","testing_guidance":"Request identical resources under different principals and inspect intermediary behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that protected representations cannot cross authorization boundaries through caching.","language":"API Security","framework":"HTTP","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"HTTP caching semantics","dependencies":["D15-API-B76-0001","D15-API-B42-0001"],"prerequisites":["authorization","HTTP caching"],"related_records":["D15-API-B77-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B76-0001","D15-API-B42-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"IETF HTTP semantics","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","version":"RFC 9110","section":"Caching","relevance":"Provides the HTTP caching model that must be combined with API authorization semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B77-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Conditional Requests","concept":"Conditional Request Validation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can conditional requests support API correctness and cache validation?","answer":"Conditional requests allow a client to provide a validator or precondition that the server evaluates against the current selected representation or resource state before returning or modifying it.","explanation":"They can support cache validation and prevent certain stale updates when used with the appropriate precondition semantics.","principle":"Use representation validators and request preconditions when clients need state-aware behavior.","correct_example":"A client submits a conditional update tied to the representation version it previously observed.","invalid_example":"Overwrite a resource without evaluating a documented client precondition when concurrent modification protection is required.","problem":"Client actions proceed using stale resource state.","failure_mode":"Lost updates or unnecessary full transfers occur.","cause":"Resource version relationship was not represented in the request contract.","solution":"Use appropriate conditional request semantics and define failure behavior.","alternative":"Application-specific version fields can provide similar semantics when HTTP validators are not suitable, but they require explicit application contracts.","tradeoff":"Conditional requests improve correctness but require clients and servers to handle precondition failures.","constraint":"Validators must accurately correspond to the representation or state they describe.","edge_case":"A representation can change while semantically equivalent data remains, depending on validator strategy.","security_implication":"Conditional requests should not reveal protected resource state beyond authorized representations.","performance_implication":"Validation can reduce unnecessary transfer or work.","testing_guidance":"Test matching, non-matching, missing, stale, and malformed preconditions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that preconditions are evaluated at the correct side-effect boundary.","language":"API Design","framework":"HTTP","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"HTTP conditional requests","dependencies":["D15-API-B37-0002"],"prerequisites":["HTTP validators","concurrency"],"related_records":["D15-API-B77-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B37-0002"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"IETF HTTP semantics","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","version":"RFC 9110","section":"Conditional Requests","relevance":"Defines HTTP validators and precondition semantics."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B77-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Conditional Requests","concept":"Precondition Failure Contract","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"What should an API do when a required conditional request precondition fails?","answer":"The API should return the status and error semantics defined for the failed precondition rather than silently applying the operation against a different resource state.","explanation":"The failure tells the client that its request assumptions no longer hold.","principle":"Do not silently ignore a required concurrency or state precondition.","correct_example":"A stale update is rejected and the client receives a documented indication that its resource version is no longer current.","invalid_example":"Ignore the stale precondition and overwrite the newer resource state.","problem":"Client and server state assumptions diverge.","failure_mode":"Lost updates or unintended state transitions occur.","cause":"Precondition failure was treated as optional.","solution":"Enforce documented preconditions before the protected mutation and provide actionable failure semantics.","alternative":"Use server-side conflict resolution when the domain explicitly supports it.","tradeoff":"Strict rejection preserves correctness but requires clients to handle conflicts.","constraint":"The API must document which preconditions are required or optional.","edge_case":"Multiple preconditions may interact and require defined evaluation order.","security_implication":"Precondition handling can protect security-sensitive state transitions from races.","performance_implication":"Conflict handling may require an additional read or client retry.","testing_guidance":"Create stale concurrent updates and verify the correct failure path.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that no protected state changes occur after a failed mandatory precondition.","language":"API Design","framework":"HTTP","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"HTTP conditional requests","dependencies":["D15-API-B77-0001"],"prerequisites":["conditional requests","concurrency"],"related_records":["D15-API-B78-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B77-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"IETF HTTP semantics","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","version":"RFC 9110","section":"Preconditions","relevance":"Defines conditional request evaluation and failure behavior."}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B78-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Traffic Shaping","concept":"Load Shedding","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is load shedding in an API?","answer":"Load shedding intentionally rejects or drops selected work when system capacity is insufficient so that the remaining workload can continue within controlled resource limits.","explanation":"Load shedding is a deliberate overload behavior rather than an unexpected failure.","principle":"Prefer controlled rejection to uncontrolled resource exhaustion when capacity is exceeded.","correct_example":"A service rejects excess low-priority requests after bounded capacity is reached while continuing protected operations.","invalid_example":"Continue accepting all traffic until memory, worker threads, or downstream connections are exhausted.","problem":"The system has no controlled overload boundary.","failure_mode":"Resource exhaustion causes broad failure instead of selective rejection.","cause":"Admission and overload policies are absent.","solution":"Define load-shedding conditions, protected workloads, and client-visible failure semantics.","alternative":"Queue work when the workload can tolerate asynchronous completion.","tradeoff":"Load shedding sacrifices some requests to preserve overall service stability.","constraint":"Shedding rules must be deterministic enough to test and must not bypass authorization.","edge_case":"A dependency may fail before local capacity is exhausted, requiring dependency-aware shedding.","security_implication":"Load shedding can mitigate resource-exhaustion attacks but must not create authorization bypasses.","performance_implication":"Rejecting excess work can preserve latency for accepted requests.","testing_guidance":"Drive the system beyond capacity and verify protected and shed workloads behave according to policy.","verification_guidance":"Measure resource behavior under overload before claiming effectiveness.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B75-0001"],"prerequisites":["admission control","backpressure"],"related_records":["D15-API-B78-0002","D15-API-B79-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B75-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","title":"API Load Shedding","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines controlled rejection under resource overload."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B78-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Traffic Shaping","concept":"Load Shedding Versus Rate Limiting","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does load shedding differ from rate limiting?","answer":"Rate limiting constrains request or usage rate according to a defined policy, while load shedding is an overload response that rejects selected work when current capacity or system health requires it.","explanation":"An API can use both: rate limiting controls expected traffic patterns while load shedding provides protection when actual capacity becomes constrained.","principle":"Use the mechanism that matches the failure or capacity condition being controlled.","correct_example":"A service enforces normal per-client rate limits and additionally sheds low-priority requests during severe downstream saturation.","invalid_example":"Assume a static rate limit alone guarantees safe behavior under every possible dependency failure.","problem":"Normal traffic policy is mistaken for dynamic capacity protection.","failure_mode":"The service becomes overloaded despite clients remaining under nominal rate limits.","cause":"Capacity and dependency behavior vary over time.","solution":"Combine static traffic controls with dynamic overload protection when justified.","alternative":"Use circuit breaking or bounded queues for specific dependency-related overload conditions.","tradeoff":"Layered controls improve resilience but increase policy complexity.","constraint":"Interactions between rate limits and shedding rules must be documented.","edge_case":"A low request rate can still overload an expensive operation.","security_implication":"Both controls can reduce abuse but neither replaces authorization.","performance_implication":"Dynamic shedding can preserve capacity under changing workloads.","testing_guidance":"Test traffic within rate limits while forcing downstream capacity degradation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that overload behavior depends on actual protected capacity rather than only request count.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B71-0001","D15-API-B78-0001"],"prerequisites":["rate limiting","load shedding"],"related_records":["D15-API-B79-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B71-0001","D15-API-B78-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D15-API-B71-0001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Rate Limiting and Load Shedding","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Distinguishes static traffic control from dynamic overload protection."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B79-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Traffic Isolation","concept":"Bulkhead Resource Isolation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can API architecture prevent one workload from exhausting resources needed by another?","answer":"Resource isolation, often called a bulkhead pattern, assigns separate or bounded resource pools, concurrency limits, or capacity budgets to workloads so that one workload's failure or surge has limited impact on others.","explanation":"Isolation can be applied at connection pools, worker pools, queues, tenants, endpoints, or other resource boundaries.","principle":"Partition scarce resources when failure containment requires workload isolation.","correct_example":"An API reserves bounded worker capacity for critical operations instead of allowing one expensive endpoint to consume the entire worker pool.","invalid_example":"Allow all endpoint classes to share an unbounded common worker pool.","problem":"One workload can consume all shared capacity.","failure_mode":"Unrelated API operations fail together.","cause":"Resource pools are not isolated.","solution":"Partition or bound resource consumption by workload class.","alternative":"Use admission control and priority-aware scheduling when separate pools are impractical.","tradeoff":"Isolation can reduce resource utilization when one pool is idle while another is full.","constraint":"Pool sizing must be based on actual workload and capacity evidence.","edge_case":"Too many small pools can create fragmentation and starvation.","security_implication":"Tenant or workload isolation can reduce cross-tenant resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Isolation can improve predictability under contention but may reduce aggregate utilization.","testing_guidance":"Saturate one workload class and verify independent workloads remain within their intended service behavior.","verification_guidance":"Measure resource usage and cross-workload impact under controlled overload.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B73-0001","D15-API-B78-0001"],"prerequisites":["resource pools","overload protection"],"related_records":["D15-API-B79-0002","D15-API-B80-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B73-0001","D15-API-B78-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","title":"Bulkhead Resource Isolation","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines workload isolation for failure containment."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B79-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Traffic Isolation","concept":"Tenant Resource Isolation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can tenant-specific resource isolation matter in a multi-tenant API?","answer":"Tenant-specific or tenant-aware resource controls can prevent one tenant's workload from consuming disproportionate shared capacity and degrading service for other tenants.","explanation":"Logical data isolation and resource isolation address different failure modes and may both be required.","principle":"Treat shared infrastructure capacity as a potential cross-tenant interference boundary.","correct_example":"A service limits expensive tenant operations so one tenant cannot consume all worker capacity available to other tenants.","invalid_example":"Assume data-level tenant authorization automatically prevents one tenant from exhausting shared compute resources.","problem":"Security isolation is mistaken for resource isolation.","failure_mode":"One tenant causes availability degradation for others.","cause":"Resource consumption was not included in tenant isolation design.","solution":"Apply tenant-aware quotas, concurrency limits, scheduling, or capacity pools where required.","alternative":"Global admission control can protect total capacity while tenant-level controls protect fairness.","tradeoff":"Tenant isolation increases operational complexity and may reduce shared-resource efficiency.","constraint":"Tenant resource policies must account for legitimate workload differences.","edge_case":"Enterprise tenants may have explicitly different service allocations.","security_implication":"Resource exhaustion can become a cross-tenant availability issue even without data disclosure.","performance_implication":"Isolation can improve predictable per-tenant performance while reducing pooling efficiency.","testing_guidance":"Generate competing workloads from multiple tenants and measure interference.","verification_guidance":"Verify resource isolation using actual load evidence rather than assuming logical isolation provides it.","language":"API Security","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B45-0001","D15-API-B79-0001"],"prerequisites":["multi-tenancy","resource isolation"],"related_records":["D15-API-B80-0001"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B45-0001","D15-API-B79-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"Multi-tenant reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"Tenant Resource Isolation","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Distinguishes tenant data authorization from tenant resource protection."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B80-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"B71-B80 Synthesis","concept":"API Traffic and Resource Protection Framework","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should rate limits, quotas, concurrency controls, backpressure, caching, and load shedding be combined?","answer":"These controls should be mapped to distinct resource and failure boundaries: rate limits regulate traffic rate, quotas regulate allocated usage, concurrency controls bound active work, backpressure controls accepted work under capacity pressure, load shedding protects the system during overload, and caching can reduce repeated origin work when cache semantics are safe.","explanation":"No single mechanism guarantees API reliability or security across all workload conditions.","principle":"Choose each control according to the resource, failure mode, and security boundary it protects.","correct_example":"An API combines per-principal rate limits, bounded concurrency, tenant-aware resource controls, explicit cache semantics, and overload rejection according to documented policies.","invalid_example":"Add one global request-per-second limit and claim that it protects all compute, memory, database, tenant, and downstream resources.","problem":"A single control is expected to solve multiple independent capacity problems.","failure_mode":"Unprotected resources become bottlenecks despite traffic appearing controlled.","cause":"Resource-specific capacity analysis was omitted.","solution":"Map each API control to a specific resource or failure mode and test their interactions.","alternative":"A centralized traffic policy can coordinate multiple controls while preserving explicit semantics for each dimension.","tradeoff":"Layered protection increases complexity but provides more precise failure containment.","constraint":"All limits and policies must be bounded, observable, and documented.","edge_case":"Controls can interact to produce unexpected rejection or starvation behavior.","security_implication":"Traffic controls must complement rather than replace authentication and authorization.","performance_implication":"Claims about latency, throughput, cache hit rate, or capacity require actual measurement.","testing_guidance":"Perform integrated overload, retry, concurrency, cache, multi-tenant, and dependency-failure tests.","verification_guidance":"Maintain explicit evidence for each claimed protection mechanism and mark unmeasured characteristics UNKNOWN.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B71-0001","D15-API-B73-0001","D15-API-B74-0001","D15-API-B76-0001","D15-API-B78-0001","D15-API-B79-0001"],"prerequisites":["rate limiting","concurrency","backpressure","caching","load shedding"],"related_records":["D15-API-B80-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B71-0001","D15-API-B73-0001","D15-API-B74-0001","D15-API-B76-0001","D15-API-B78-0001","D15-API-B79-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability engineering","source_type":"ENGINEERING_SYNTHESIS","title":"Integrated API Traffic and Resource Protection","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Integrates multiple API traffic and resource-control mechanisms."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B80-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"B71-B80 Quality Gate","concept":"API Traffic Control Quality Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be verified before API traffic and resource-protection controls are considered complete?","answer":"The API must demonstrate defined rate and quota semantics, bounded concurrency, controlled overload behavior, safe caching, authorization-aware resource isolation, and documented interactions among these controls.","explanation":"Configuration alone does not prove that traffic controls protect the intended resources under real failure and concurrency conditions.","principle":"Verify resource protection at the actual capacity and trust boundaries.","correct_example":"Integration and load tests demonstrate bounded behavior during bursts, retries, long-running requests, cache reuse, dependency failure, and multi-tenant contention.","invalid_example":"Mark resource protection complete because rate-limit middleware is installed.","problem":"Protection is inferred from configuration rather than observed behavior.","failure_mode":"Unprotected resource exhaustion remains hidden.","cause":"Testing does not exercise the real overload paths.","solution":"Use controlled failure, concurrency, overload, and security testing with explicit evidence requirements.","alternative":"Formal policy verification can supplement runtime testing but cannot replace evidence about actual resource behavior.","tradeoff":"Deep validation requires additional test infrastructure and representative workloads.","constraint":"No measured capacity, throughput, latency, or protection guarantee should be claimed without evidence.","edge_case":"Protection behavior can differ across local, proxy, distributed, and production-like environments.","security_implication":"Traffic controls must be tested for bypass and identity-boundary failures.","performance_implication":"Actual resource consumption and recovery behavior must be measured before performance claims are marked verified.","testing_guidance":"Test bursts, sustained overload, retry storms, concurrent operations, cache isolation, tenant contention, and dependency failures.","verification_guidance":"Track each claim as GENERATED, EXECUTED, TESTED, PASSED, VERIFIED, or NOT_VERIFIED according to available evidence.","language":"API Reliability","framework":"Generic","version":"Conceptual","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D15-API-B80-0001"],"prerequisites":["traffic control","API testing","verification"],"related_records":["D15-API-B60-0002"],"depends_on":["D15-API-B80-0001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":[{"source":"API reliability verification","source_type":"ENGINEERING_CONCEPT","title":"API Traffic Control Quality Gate","publisher":"General engineering knowledge","version":"N/A","section":"N/A","relevance":"Defines evidence requirements for traffic and resource-protection claims."}],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

