{"record_id":"D15-API-B81-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Authentication","concept":"Explicit Authentication Boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an API authentication boundary establish?","answer":"The API should establish authentication before protected application behavior depends on the caller identity. Authentication establishes who or what is presenting credentials; authorization determines what that authenticated principal may do.","explanation":"Authentication and authorization are separate concerns and should not be collapsed into a single ambiguous access decision.","principle":"Authenticate the principal before evaluating permissions that depend on that principal.","correct_example":"Authenticate a request, establish a principal identity, then evaluate the principal's permissions for the requested resource.","invalid_example":"Treat possession of an arbitrary request parameter such as user_id as proof of identity.","problem":"Identity is confused with client-supplied resource data.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized principal may be treated as an authenticated user.","cause":"Missing or improperly ordered authentication boundary.","solution":"Create an explicit authentication stage that produces a trusted principal context.","security_implication":"Improper authentication can expose protected resources or actions.","testing_guidance":"Test missing credentials, malformed credentials, expired credentials, invalid credentials, and authenticated requests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that protected operations cannot reach authorization-dependent business logic without an authenticated principal.","language":"HTTP","version":"HTTP semantics defined by RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":["D15-API-B31-0001"],"related_records":["D15-API-B82-0001"],"extends":["D15-API-B31-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 9110","section":"11","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B81-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Authentication","concept":"Authentication Scheme Contract","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What should an API document about its authentication mechanism?","answer":"An API should explicitly document the authentication mechanism, credential location, required transport protection, credential validation rules, failure behavior, expiration or revocation model, and applicable scope.","explanation":"HTTP provides a general authentication framework while individual authentication schemes define their own semantics and security requirements.","principle":"Authentication behavior must be explicit and interoperable rather than inferred from implementation details.","correct_example":"Document that protected requests require a specific authentication scheme and define how invalid or absent credentials are handled.","invalid_example":"Require a proprietary token format without documenting its transport, validation, expiration, or failure semantics.","problem":"Clients cannot reliably implement authentication.","failure_mode":"Interoperability failures or insecure credential handling.","cause":"Undocumented authentication contract.","solution":"Publish an explicit authentication contract and security requirements.","security_implication":"Ambiguous credential handling increases credential disclosure and unauthorized-access risk.","testing_guidance":"Test valid, invalid, expired, missing, malformed, and improperly transported credentials.","verification_guidance":"Compare implementation behavior against the published authentication contract.","language":"HTTP","version":"RFC 9110","version_range":"RFC 9110+","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D15-API-B81-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 9110","section":"11","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B82-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Authorization","concept":"Authentication Versus Authorization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What is the distinction between authentication and authorization in an API?","answer":"Authentication establishes the identity or authenticated context of the requester; authorization determines whether that principal is permitted to perform the requested operation on the target resource.","explanation":"A valid identity does not automatically imply permission to access every resource or operation.","principle":"Identity establishment and permission evaluation must remain logically distinct.","correct_example":"Authenticate principal A, then verify that A has permission to update resource R.","invalid_example":"Allow every authenticated principal to update every resource.","problem":"Authentication is incorrectly treated as universal authorization.","failure_mode":"Privilege escalation or unauthorized resource access.","cause":"Missing resource- and action-level authorization checks.","solution":"Evaluate authorization using principal, action, resource, tenant, and applicable policy context.","security_implication":"Authorization bypass can expose or modify protected data.","testing_guidance":"Test authenticated principals with allowed, denied, cross-tenant, and privilege-boundary operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify authorization is evaluated for every protected operation and resource boundary.","related_records":["D15-API-B81-0001","D15-API-B89-0001"],"extends":["D15-API-B81-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 9110","section":"11","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B82-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Authorization","concept":"Resource-Level Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should authorization include the target resource rather than only the authenticated user?","answer":"Authorization should evaluate whether the authenticated principal may perform the requested action on the specific target resource within the applicable security context.","explanation":"A principal may legitimately access some resources while being prohibited from accessing other resources of the same type.","principle":"Permission is contextual: principal plus action plus resource plus applicable scope.","correct_example":"Verify that user U may update document D before applying the update.","invalid_example":"Verify only that U has the role editor, then allow updates to any document identified by the request.","problem":"Authorization is too coarse for resource-specific access control.","failure_mode":"Horizontal privilege escalation.","cause":"Missing object-level authorization.","solution":"Perform explicit resource authorization before protected resource operations.","security_implication":"Cross-user or cross-tenant data access may result.","testing_guidance":"Attempt access to owned, unowned, nonexistent, and cross-tenant resource identifiers.","verification_guidance":"Confirm denied resource access cannot alter or disclose protected state.","related_records":["D15-API-B82-0001","D15-API-B89-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B83-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Bearer Tokens","concept":"Bearer Token Protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must bearer tokens be protected as sensitive credentials?","answer":"A bearer token can generally be used by whoever possesses it, so disclosure can enable unauthorized access to the associated protected resources.","explanation":"Bearer authentication does not inherently require the presenter to prove possession of a separate cryptographic key.","principle":"Treat bearer tokens as credentials whose possession can confer access.","correct_example":"Transmit bearer tokens over protected transport and prevent them from appearing in logs, URLs, or unintended telemetry.","invalid_example":"Place an access token in a query string when an Authorization header can be used.","problem":"Credential material becomes exposed through URLs, logs, history, or intermediaries.","failure_mode":"Token theft and replay.","cause":"Insecure token transport or handling.","solution":"Use protected transport and controlled credential handling; avoid URL-based bearer-token transmission.","security_implication":"A leaked bearer token can directly enable unauthorized access.","testing_guidance":"Inspect URLs, logs, traces, analytics, error messages, and proxy records for token leakage.","verification_guidance":"Verify credentials do not appear in unintended observability or request-target data.","related_records":["D15-API-B81-0002","D15-API-B83-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 6750","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework: Bearer Token Usage","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 6750","section":"5","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B83-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Bearer Tokens","concept":"Credential Transport Location","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should bearer credentials generally not be placed in URI query parameters?","answer":"Bearer tokens should generally be carried through protected request mechanisms such as the Authorization header rather than URI query parameters because URLs can be exposed through logs, browser history, monitoring systems, and other intermediaries.","explanation":"RFC 6750 identifies security weaknesses associated with URI-based bearer-token transmission.","principle":"Do not place reusable credentials in request locations that are broadly logged or retained.","correct_example":"Use an Authorization header for a bearer access token over TLS.","invalid_example":"GET /resource?access_token=secret-token","problem":"Credential appears in the request target.","failure_mode":"Credential disclosure through secondary systems.","cause":"Insecure credential placement.","solution":"Use an appropriate protected authentication mechanism and prevent sensitive credentials from entering URLs.","security_implication":"Leaked tokens can be replayed by an attacker.","testing_guidance":"Search access logs, tracing systems, proxy logs, browser history, and error telemetry for credentials.","verification_guidance":"Verify that generated API requests do not place bearer credentials in URLs.","related_records":["D15-API-B83-0001"],"contrasts_with":["D15-API-B84-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 6750","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework: Bearer Token Usage","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 6750","section":"2.3, 5.3","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B84-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Token Scope","concept":"Audience-Restricted Access Tokens","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should access tokens be restricted to their intended audience where the token system supports audience restriction?","answer":"Audience restriction limits the resources or relying parties for which an access token is intended, reducing the consequences of token misuse or redirection.","explanation":"A token issued for one resource server should not automatically be accepted by unrelated resource servers.","principle":"Credentials should be scoped to the narrowest valid security context.","correct_example":"Validate that a presented access token is intended for the receiving resource server.","invalid_example":"Accept any syntactically valid access token regardless of its intended audience.","problem":"Token acceptance is broader than intended.","failure_mode":"Token redirect or cross-service credential misuse.","cause":"Missing audience validation.","solution":"Define and validate an explicit token audience where supported by the authorization system.","security_implication":"A compromised token may otherwise be reused against unintended services.","testing_guidance":"Present a token intended for service A to service B and verify rejection.","verification_guidance":"Verify audience claims or equivalent token metadata are validated consistently.","related_records":["D15-API-B83-0001","D15-API-B84-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 6750","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework: Bearer Token Usage","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 6750","section":"5.2, 5.3","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B84-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Token Lifetime","concept":"Short-Lived Access Credentials","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What is the security purpose of limiting access-token lifetime?","answer":"Limiting token lifetime reduces the period during which a leaked access token can remain usable.","explanation":"Short lifetimes do not eliminate token theft but can reduce the exposure window.","principle":"Reduce credential exposure duration when operationally compatible with the authentication architecture.","correct_example":"Issue access credentials with an explicit expiration and enforce expiration at the resource server.","invalid_example":"Issue credentials with unlimited lifetime and no revocation or replacement strategy.","problem":"Credential compromise remains useful indefinitely.","failure_mode":"Extended unauthorized access after token disclosure.","cause":"Unbounded credential lifetime.","solution":"Use an explicit expiration and appropriate renewal or revocation mechanism.","security_implication":"Lifetime controls reduce but do not eliminate credential-compromise impact.","testing_guidance":"Test expired tokens, clock boundaries, renewal, and revocation behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify expired credentials are rejected consistently by protected endpoints.","related_records":["D15-API-B84-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 6750","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework: Bearer Token Usage","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 6750","section":"5.2, 5.3","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B85-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Sender Constraint","concept":"Proof-of-Possession Tokens","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does sender-constrained token authentication address?","answer":"Sender-constrained token mechanisms bind token use to a client-held key or another authenticated sender property, reducing the usefulness of a stolen token when the attacker does not possess the required proof material.","explanation":"DPoP is an application-level mechanism that binds OAuth tokens to a public/private key pair and requires proof in requests.","principle":"Where appropriate, bind reusable credentials to an additional proof of legitimate possession.","correct_example":"Use a DPoP-bound token and validate the corresponding proof before accepting the protected request.","invalid_example":"Treat possession of a copied token as sufficient when the security architecture requires sender constraint.","problem":"A copied bearer credential can be replayed by an unauthorized party.","failure_mode":"Credential replay after token theft.","cause":"Credential is not sender constrained.","solution":"Consider a sender-constrained mechanism appropriate to the deployment and validate its proof correctly.","security_implication":"Sender constraint can reduce the usefulness of stolen tokens but does not eliminate all replay or endpoint compromise risks.","testing_guidance":"Test valid proof, wrong key, expired proof, mismatched method/URI, and replayed proof.","verification_guidance":"Verify that token binding and proof validation are enforced before protected operations.","related_records":["D15-API-B83-0001","D15-API-B85-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9449","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"OAuth 2.0 Demonstrating Proof of Possession (DPoP)","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 9449","section":"1","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B85-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Sender Constraint","concept":"DPoP Proof Binding","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must an API verify when using DPoP-style sender-constrained authentication?","answer":"The verifier must validate the proof according to the applicable protocol requirements, including its relationship to the presented token, key possession, request context, and freshness constraints.","explanation":"DPoP binds OAuth tokens to a public key and uses a request proof that is bound to request information.","principle":"Sender-constrained authentication is only effective if every required binding is validated.","correct_example":"Reject a proof whose key does not match the token binding or whose request-bound claims do not match the actual request.","invalid_example":"Validate only that the proof is syntactically a JWT without validating its security bindings.","problem":"Proof is accepted without establishing sender constraint.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled or replayed proof may be accepted.","cause":"Incomplete proof validation.","solution":"Implement the complete applicable DPoP verification procedure.","security_implication":"Incomplete validation defeats the intended sender-constraining property.","testing_guidance":"Test mismatched key, URI, method, nonce/freshness, token binding, and replay scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Trace every required proof-validation step and verify failure causes rejection.","related_records":["D15-API-B85-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9449","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"OAuth 2.0 Demonstrating Proof of Possession (DPoP)","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 9449","section":"4, 8, 11","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B86-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant-Scoped Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should a multi-tenant API prevent cross-tenant access?","answer":"The API should derive tenant context from a trusted authenticated or authorization context and enforce that context consistently across resource lookup, authorization, mutation, caching, background processing, and observability.","explanation":"A tenant identifier supplied solely by an untrusted request field must not be treated as proof of tenant membership.","principle":"Tenant boundaries must be enforced by trusted security context at every relevant resource boundary.","correct_example":"Resolve tenant T from authenticated context, authorize access to resource R within T, and ensure repository queries are tenant-scoped.","invalid_example":"Accept tenant_id from the request body and query any resource associated with that value without authorization validation.","problem":"Tenant identity can be manipulated by the caller.","failure_mode":"Cross-tenant data disclosure or modification.","cause":"Untrusted tenant context or incomplete authorization propagation.","solution":"Bind tenant context to trusted identity and enforce it throughout the request and downstream execution path.","security_implication":"Tenant-isolation failure can expose multiple customers' data.","testing_guidance":"Attempt cross-tenant reads, writes, updates, deletes, cache access, and asynchronous operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify tenant context cannot be changed by ordinary client input and is preserved through all resource operations.","related_records":["D15-API-B82-0002","D15-API-B86-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B86-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant Context Propagation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must tenant context propagate to downstream API and data operations?","answer":"A tenant boundary is ineffective if the initial API layer identifies the tenant correctly but downstream services, repositories, caches, jobs, or events lose or replace that context.","explanation":"Multi-step API workflows can cross process boundaries and therefore require explicit propagation and validation of security context.","principle":"Security context must survive every trust boundary that depends on it.","correct_example":"Propagate authenticated tenant context through a validated internal request or trusted context mechanism and enforce it downstream.","invalid_example":"Perform a tenant-scoped API authorization check, then issue an unscoped database query in a downstream component.","problem":"Security enforcement exists only at the edge.","failure_mode":"Downstream authorization bypass.","cause":"Missing security-context propagation.","solution":"Define and verify tenant context propagation contracts for synchronous and asynchronous operations.","security_implication":"A downstream component can become an unintended cross-tenant access path.","testing_guidance":"Test direct and indirect access through every downstream path, including retries and asynchronous workers.","verification_guidance":"Trace tenant context from ingress through authorization, persistence, caching, messaging, and response generation.","related_records":["D15-API-B86-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B87-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Auditability","concept":"Security-Relevant API Audit Events","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What should an API audit when security-relevant actions occur?","answer":"Security-relevant API operations should produce appropriately structured audit information sufficient to establish who or what acted, what operation was attempted, what resource or scope was involved, when it occurred, and the resulting decision or outcome, subject to privacy and data-minimization requirements.","explanation":"Audit records should support investigation without unnecessarily storing credentials or sensitive payloads.","principle":"Record security-relevant decisions and outcomes without turning audit logs into credential or data-exfiltration channels.","correct_example":"Record principal reference, operation, resource reference, tenant scope, timestamp, decision, correlation identifier, and outcome while excluding raw secrets.","invalid_example":"Log the full Authorization header and sensitive request body for every operation.","problem":"Auditability is pursued by indiscriminately recording sensitive data.","failure_mode":"Audit system becomes a source of credential or data leakage.","cause":"No audit-data classification policy.","solution":"Define security audit fields, retention, access controls, redaction, and sensitive-data exclusions.","security_implication":"Audit data itself requires protection and access control.","testing_guidance":"Verify expected security events are emitted and sensitive credentials are absent.","verification_guidance":"Review audit records against the defined audit schema and security-data policy.","related_records":["D15-API-B87-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B87-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Auditability","concept":"Decision Outcome Traceability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an API make an authorization decision traceable?","answer":"A security decision should be traceable through a correlation or decision identifier that links the request, relevant authorization context, decision, and resulting operation without exposing secrets.","explanation":"Traceability supports incident investigation and debugging while preserving confidentiality boundaries.","principle":"Make important security decisions explainable through protected internal evidence rather than client-visible implementation details.","correct_example":"Associate a denied authorization decision with an internal correlation identifier and structured audit event.","invalid_example":"Return internal policy evaluation details, secret identifiers, or credential material directly to the client.","problem":"Either the decision cannot be investigated or too much internal information is exposed.","failure_mode":"Poor forensic visibility or information disclosure.","cause":"Unbalanced observability design.","solution":"Separate client-safe error information from protected internal audit evidence.","security_implication":"Detailed internal diagnostics may reveal authorization architecture to attackers.","testing_guidance":"Verify denied and successful sensitive operations generate appropriate traceable events.","verification_guidance":"Confirm an investigator can correlate request, decision, and outcome without access to credentials or unnecessary payload data.","related_records":["D15-API-B34-0002","D15-API-B87-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B88-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Credential Hygiene","concept":"Secret Redaction","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Which API data should generally be excluded or redacted from ordinary logs?","answer":"Credentials and other security-sensitive values should not be emitted into ordinary logs unless there is a specifically justified, controlled, and protected diagnostic requirement.","explanation":"Logging systems commonly have broad access, long retention, replication, and export paths, making them a high-impact secondary data store.","principle":"Observability must not weaken the security boundary of the API.","correct_example":"Redact Authorization values, session secrets, API keys, and equivalent credential material from logs.","invalid_example":"Log every request header verbatim for debugging without filtering credentials.","problem":"Sensitive credentials become available through logging infrastructure.","failure_mode":"Credential compromise through telemetry.","cause":"Unfiltered request/response logging.","solution":"Implement centralized secret-aware redaction and test it against all relevant logging paths.","security_implication":"A logging breach can become an authentication breach.","testing_guidance":"Send requests containing representative secret formats and verify they are redacted from logs, traces, and errors.","verification_guidance":"Inspect all observability pipelines, not only application logs.","related_records":["D15-API-B83-0001","D15-API-B87-0001"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 6750","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework: Bearer Token Usage","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 6750","section":"5","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B88-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Credential Hygiene","concept":"Sensitive URI Prevention","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should sensitive credentials and confidential values generally be kept out of URLs?","answer":"URLs are frequently captured by infrastructure outside the application's direct control, including access logs, browser history, monitoring, analytics, proxies, and tracing systems.","explanation":"A value placed in a URL can therefore have a much wider persistence and distribution path than a protected credential header.","principle":"Minimize the propagation surface of sensitive data.","correct_example":"Keep credentials and confidential request values in protected headers or request bodies according to the authentication and API contract.","invalid_example":"Put API keys or bearer tokens into query parameters for convenience.","problem":"Sensitive values become part of broadly observable request metadata.","failure_mode":"Unintended credential or privacy disclosure.","cause":"Incorrect transport location.","solution":"Use an appropriate protected transport mechanism and redact sensitive values from observability systems.","security_implication":"Credential leakage can lead to unauthorized access.","testing_guidance":"Inspect generated URLs and all URL-derived telemetry for secrets.","verification_guidance":"Use automated secret-pattern detection against request targets and observability outputs.","related_records":["D15-API-B83-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 6750","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework: Bearer Token Usage","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 6750","section":"2.3, 5.3","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B89-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Authorization Architecture","concept":"Centralized Policy With Distributed Enforcement","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can an API architecture keep authorization policy consistent across multiple endpoints or services?","answer":"Authorization policy can be defined through a consistent policy model while enforcement occurs at each resource boundary that has sufficient context to make the decision.","explanation":"Central policy representation does not remove the need for local enforcement at every protected operation.","principle":"Centralize policy semantics where useful, but enforce authorization at the actual resource and action boundary.","correct_example":"Use a shared authorization policy model while each service validates principal, action, resource, and tenant context before access.","invalid_example":"Assume that an upstream gateway authorization check makes downstream resource authorization unnecessary.","problem":"A single edge check is treated as a universal security guarantee.","failure_mode":"Authorization bypass through alternate or internal paths.","cause":"Policy centralization confused with enforcement centralization.","solution":"Define policy centrally where appropriate and enforce it at every relevant trust/resource boundary.","security_implication":"Distributed enforcement reduces bypass paths but requires consistent policy evaluation.","testing_guidance":"Test direct service access, alternate routes, internal calls, and cross-tenant operations.","verification_guidance":"Map every protected resource boundary and verify an authorization enforcement point exists.","related_records":["D15-API-B82-0001","D15-API-B86-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B89-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Authorization Architecture","concept":"Least-Privilege API Authorization","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does least privilege mean in API authorization?","answer":"Least privilege means granting only the permissions necessary for the principal, operation, resource, tenant, and execution context required by the legitimate task.","explanation":"Broad permissions increase the impact of credential compromise, implementation errors, and authorization defects.","principle":"Permission scope should be no broader than required by the operation.","correct_example":"Grant a service permission to update only the resource classes and tenant scopes it actually requires.","invalid_example":"Grant unrestricted administrative access because it simplifies implementation.","problem":"Permissions exceed operational requirements.","failure_mode":"Excessive blast radius after credential or authorization compromise.","cause":"Overbroad roles, scopes, or policies.","solution":"Define narrow permissions and periodically validate them against actual requirements.","security_implication":"Least privilege limits unauthorized-action scope.","testing_guidance":"Attempt operations outside the principal's required permission set.","verification_guidance":"Compare declared permissions against required operations and denied-operation tests.","related_records":["D15-API-B82-0001","D15-API-B89-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B90-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Integrated Security","concept":"End-to-End API Security Decision Framework","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What sequence should an API use to evaluate a protected request?","answer":"A defensible sequence is: establish transport/security prerequisites, authenticate the principal, validate request structure, establish trusted scope and tenant context, authorize the requested action against the target resource, enforce resource and business invariants, execute the operation, and emit appropriate audit evidence without leaking secrets.","explanation":"Security controls are most reliable when each stage has a defined input, decision, failure behavior, and verification point.","principle":"Treat API security as a layered decision pipeline rather than a single authentication check.","correct_example":"TLS prerequisite -> authentication -> structural validation -> trusted context -> authorization -> business validation -> execution -> audit/outcome.","invalid_example":"Execute the requested database operation and check authorization afterward.","problem":"Security decisions occur after protected effects.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized state change or data disclosure.","cause":"Incorrect control ordering.","solution":"Place preventive security decisions before the protected effect and verify downstream context.","security_implication":"Correct ordering reduces bypass opportunities.","testing_guidance":"Create negative tests for every stage and verify later stages cannot compensate for failed earlier controls.","verification_guidance":"Document and test the security pipeline as an explicit API contract.","related_records":["D15-API-B81-0001","D15-API-B82-0001","D15-API-B86-0001","D15-API-B87-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B90-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"Security Quality Gate","concept":"API Security Contract Quality Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a final API security quality gate verify?","answer":"The quality gate should verify documented authentication, authorization, resource isolation, tenant isolation where applicable, credential protection, secret redaction, failure semantics, auditability, transport requirements, token validation, security-sensitive edge cases, and negative security tests.","explanation":"A secure-looking endpoint is insufficient if alternate paths, downstream components, logs, caches, or asynchronous operations bypass the intended controls.","principle":"Verify the complete security boundary, not only the happy-path endpoint.","correct_example":"Require evidence for authentication, authorization, cross-tenant denial, secret redaction, token expiration, malformed credentials, and alternate-path protection before release.","invalid_example":"Declare the API secure because a valid login request succeeded.","problem":"Security verification is reduced to positive authentication testing.","failure_mode":"Unidentified authorization or credential-handling vulnerabilities.","cause":"Incomplete security test model.","solution":"Use a security-specific quality gate with positive, negative, boundary, and abuse-oriented tests.","security_implication":"Security assurance requires evidence across the complete attack surface.","testing_guidance":"Include authentication, authorization, isolation, credential leakage, replay, malformed input, and failure-path tests.","verification_guidance":"Mark each security requirement as VERIFIED only when supporting evidence exists; otherwise preserve NOT_VERIFIED or UNKNOWN.","related_records":["D15-API-B90-0001","D15-API-B87-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"HTTP Semantics","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 9110","section":"11, 13","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source":"RFC 6750","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework: Bearer Token Usage","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 6750","section":"5","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"},{"source":"RFC 9449","source_type":"STANDARD","title":"OAuth 2.0 Demonstrating Proof of Possession (DPoP)","publisher":"IETF","reference":"RFC 9449","section":"11","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED_AND_CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

