{"record_id":"D15-API-B91-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Contract Evolution","concept":"Backward-Compatible API Evolution","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What makes an API change backward compatible?","answer":"An API change is backward compatible when existing conforming clients can continue their previously supported interactions without requiring incompatible assumptions about request or response contracts.","explanation":"Compatibility depends on the client-visible contract rather than only internal implementation changes. A change can be internally simple but externally breaking.","principle":"Evaluate API compatibility from the perspective of existing clients and documented contract guarantees.","correct_example":"Adding an optional response field while preserving existing fields and their documented meanings can be compatible when clients tolerate additional fields.","invalid_example":"Changing an existing required field from integer to an incompatible object and assuming the change is safe because the endpoint path is unchanged.","problem":"An externally breaking change is classified as compatible.","failure_mode":"Existing clients fail or interpret responses incorrectly.","cause":"Compatibility assessed only from server implementation perspective.","solution":"Define explicit compatibility rules and test existing client contracts against the changed API.","alternative":"Introduce a new API version or endpoint when the required change cannot preserve the existing contract.","tradeoff":"Strict compatibility can constrain contract evolution; versioning can increase operational and documentation complexity.","edge_case":"A technically optional field may still break clients that incorrectly assume a closed response schema.","testing_guidance":"Run contract and regression tests against representative existing clients.","verification_guidance":"Compare old and new schemas, semantics, error behavior, authentication requirements, and documented guarantees.","version":"API contract versioning","version_range":"General","related_records":["D15-API-B41-0001","D15-API-B42-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B91-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Contract Evolution","concept":"Breaking Change Classification","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Which API changes should be evaluated as potentially breaking?","answer":"Changes to required request fields, field types, established meanings, authentication requirements, authorization behavior, status-code semantics, error contracts, resource identifiers, supported methods, pagination assumptions, or response structures can be breaking depending on the documented contract.","explanation":"Breaking behavior is determined by observable contract semantics, not merely by whether source code changed.","principle":"Classify changes according to client-observable behavior.","correct_example":"Treat changing a required request property from string to integer as a candidate breaking change.","invalid_example":"Assume that changing error status codes cannot affect clients because successful responses remain unchanged.","problem":"Only data-schema changes are considered when assessing compatibility.","failure_mode":"Unexpected client failures after deployment.","cause":"Incomplete compatibility model.","solution":"Maintain a change-impact matrix covering request, response, errors, security, semantics, and operational behavior.","related_records":["D15-API-B91-0001","D15-API-B40-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B92-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Versioning","concept":"API Versioning Strategy","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should an API use an explicit versioning strategy?","answer":"An API should use an explicit versioning strategy when independently deployed clients and servers need a controlled mechanism for evolving incompatible contract semantics.","explanation":"Versioning creates an explicit compatibility boundary but does not by itself make an API evolution safe.","principle":"Use version boundaries to manage incompatible contract evolution rather than hiding incompatible behavior behind the same contract.","correct_example":"Introduce a new contract version when an existing required field or semantic guarantee cannot remain compatible.","invalid_example":"Change the meaning of an existing field while retaining the same version and expecting clients to infer the change.","problem":"Incompatible semantics are silently introduced into an existing contract.","failure_mode":"Client misinterpretation or runtime failure.","cause":"No explicit compatibility boundary.","solution":"Define versioning rules, supported versions, migration paths, deprecation periods, and retirement criteria.","alternative":"Preserve the existing contract and add optional capabilities when compatibility can be maintained.","tradeoff":"Multiple versions increase maintenance and testing cost.","testing_guidance":"Test each supported version independently and test migration behavior between versions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every documented version exposes exactly its intended contract.","related_records":["D15-API-B91-0001","D15-API-B92-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B92-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Versioning","concept":"Version Lifecycle","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should an API version lifecycle define?","answer":"A version lifecycle should define introduction, active support, compatibility guarantees, deprecation notification, migration guidance, retirement conditions, and the final removal process.","explanation":"A version identifier without lifecycle governance can leave clients uncertain about support and migration requirements.","principle":"Every supported API version should have an explicit operational lifecycle.","correct_example":"Document when version V1 is supported, when V2 becomes available, how clients migrate, and what conditions permit V1 retirement.","invalid_example":"Publish V2 and immediately remove V1 without a documented migration or compatibility policy.","problem":"Clients cannot predict the stability or support horizon of a contract.","failure_mode":"Unexpected integration outages.","cause":"Versioning without lifecycle governance.","solution":"Maintain version status and migration metadata as part of API governance.","testing_guidance":"Test supported versions and verify deprecated versions retain their documented guarantees until retirement.","verification_guidance":"Compare implementation routes, schemas, documentation, and release policy against the version lifecycle.","related_records":["D15-API-B92-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B93-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Deprecation","concept":"Safe API Deprecation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should an API deprecate an existing operation?","answer":"Deprecation should identify the affected contract, communicate its status, provide migration guidance where applicable, preserve documented behavior during the supported period, monitor usage where permitted, and define an explicit retirement condition.","explanation":"Deprecation is a managed compatibility process rather than an immediate removal.","principle":"Give consumers a deterministic path from deprecated behavior to its replacement.","correct_example":"Mark an endpoint deprecated, document its replacement, define a support period, and validate the replacement against representative client behavior.","invalid_example":"Remove an endpoint solely because a newer endpoint exists without communicating the compatibility impact.","problem":"Clients are forced into an undocumented migration.","failure_mode":"Integration failures and emergency migration work.","cause":"Unmanaged API retirement.","solution":"Use a documented deprecation and migration lifecycle.","tradeoff":"Longer support periods increase maintenance cost but can reduce client migration risk.","testing_guidance":"Test both deprecated and replacement contracts during the transition period.","verification_guidance":"Verify deprecation status, documentation, replacement behavior, and retirement criteria.","related_records":["D15-API-B92-0002","D15-API-B93-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B93-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Deprecation","concept":"Migration Contract","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should an API migration guide contain?","answer":"A migration guide should identify changed operations, request and response differences, semantic differences, authentication or authorization changes, error behavior changes, examples, compatibility limitations, and verification steps.","explanation":"A migration guide should allow clients to determine whether and how their integration must change.","principle":"Migration documentation should describe observable contract differences rather than only implementation changes.","correct_example":"Document old request shape, new request shape, changed status behavior, replacement operation, and required client tests.","invalid_example":"State only that clients should migrate to V2 without documenting behavioral differences.","problem":"Clients lack actionable migration information.","failure_mode":"Incorrect or incomplete migration.","cause":"Implementation-focused documentation.","solution":"Build migration guidance from the API contract diff and client-impact analysis.","testing_guidance":"Use the migration guide to construct regression tests for old and new behavior.","verification_guidance":"Check that every documented breaking difference has a corresponding migration instruction.","related_records":["D15-API-B91-0002","D15-API-B93-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B94-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Contract Testing","concept":"Consumer-Driven Contract Testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What does consumer-driven contract testing verify?","answer":"Consumer-driven contract testing verifies that a provider continues to satisfy the interactions and response expectations that a consumer depends upon, within the defined contract.","explanation":"It provides a focused compatibility check between independently evolving API consumers and providers.","principle":"Test the provider against explicit consumer-visible expectations.","correct_example":"A consumer defines required request and response interactions and the provider test suite verifies those interactions against the implemented API.","invalid_example":"Test only that the endpoint returns some successful response without validating the fields and semantics required by consumers.","problem":"Provider compatibility is tested too broadly or too weakly to detect contract breakage.","failure_mode":"Consumer integration breaks after provider changes.","cause":"No executable consumer expectations.","solution":"Represent important consumer expectations as automated contract tests.","testing_guidance":"Include success, validation errors, authorization failures, pagination, idempotency, and version-specific behavior where relevant.","verification_guidance":"Record which consumer contracts were executed and which provider version satisfied them.","related_records":["D15-API-B91-0001","D15-API-B40-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B94-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"Contract Testing","subtopic":"Schema Validation","concept":"Specification-Based API Testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How can an API specification be used for automated testing?","answer":"An API specification can be used to generate or drive tests that compare requests, responses, status codes, schemas, parameters, security requirements, and documented constraints against the implementation.","explanation":"Specification-based testing turns parts of the API contract into executable compatibility checks.","principle":"Executable contract checks should validate both structure and relevant semantics.","correct_example":"Generate tests from an OpenAPI contract and verify that observed responses satisfy the declared response schemas and status codes.","invalid_example":"Assume specification validity because the specification file itself parses successfully.","problem":"Specification syntax is confused with implementation conformance.","failure_mode":"Undetected contract violations.","cause":"No implementation-versus-specification verification.","solution":"Run conformance tests against representative endpoints and failure paths.","testing_guidance":"Include boundary values, invalid payloads, security failures, and declared response variants.","verification_guidance":"Record actual observations separately from generated expectations.","related_records":["D15-API-B94-0001","D15-API-B36-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenAPI Specification","source_type":"SPECIFICATION","title":"OpenAPI Specification","publisher":"OpenAPI Initiative","reference":"OpenAPI Specification","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B95-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"API Testing","concept":"Negative API Test Matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Which negative cases should a mature API test suite cover?","answer":"A mature negative test matrix should cover missing and malformed authentication, authorization denial, invalid types, missing required fields, invalid semantic values, unsupported methods, malformed syntax, resource-not-found conditions, conflicts, rate limits, timeouts, dependency failures, oversized input, concurrency conflicts, and invalid state transitions as applicable.","explanation":"Negative tests verify that the API rejects invalid or unsafe interactions using deterministic and documented behavior.","principle":"Every important rejection condition should have an explicit expected contract.","correct_example":"For each protected operation, test unauthenticated, unauthorized, malformed, invalid-state, conflict, and dependency-failure scenarios where applicable.","invalid_example":"Test only valid requests and one generic server-error case.","problem":"Failure behavior remains unspecified or unverified.","failure_mode":"Inconsistent error semantics and security regressions.","cause":"Happy-path-focused testing.","solution":"Derive a negative test matrix from validation, authorization, state, dependency, and resource-control rules.","testing_guidance":"Store expected status, error type, invariant, and side-effect expectations for each negative case.","verification_guidance":"Verify rejected requests do not produce unauthorized or unintended state changes.","related_records":["D15-API-B32-0002","D15-API-B40-0002","D15-API-B90-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B95-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"API Testing","concept":"State-Invariant Testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should API tests verify state invariants in addition to HTTP responses?","answer":"An API may return an apparently correct status while producing an unintended state change, partial mutation, duplicate effect, or security violation.","explanation":"Response correctness does not necessarily establish postcondition correctness.","principle":"Verify both observable response behavior and protected system state.","correct_example":"After a failed update request, verify that the target resource remains unchanged.","invalid_example":"Assert only that a failed request returned a 4xx status without checking whether a partial mutation occurred.","problem":"Side effects are not included in verification.","failure_mode":"Silent data corruption or inconsistent state.","cause":"Response-only testing.","solution":"Define preconditions, response expectations, postconditions, and invariant checks for state-changing operations.","testing_guidance":"Use database, event, cache, and external-side-effect assertions where applicable.","verification_guidance":"Verify both positive and negative postconditions.","related_records":["D15-API-B38-0001","D15-API-B95-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B96-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Reliability Testing","concept":"Failure Injection for APIs","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should API reliability testing include controlled dependency failures?","answer":"Controlled failure injection verifies that an API behaves according to its failure contract when dependencies become unavailable, slow, malformed, overloaded, or partially successful.","explanation":"Normal-path tests cannot establish correct behavior under dependency failure.","principle":"Test failure behavior deliberately rather than assuming dependencies always behave normally.","correct_example":"Inject a downstream timeout and verify the API returns its documented failure response without leaving an invalid partial state.","invalid_example":"Assume dependency failures are handled because a timeout exception handler exists in source code.","problem":"Failure handling is inferred rather than observed.","failure_mode":"Unexpected cascading failures or inconsistent state.","cause":"No failure-injection testing.","solution":"Introduce controlled failures in integration or test environments and verify documented invariants.","testing_guidance":"Test timeout, connection failure, malformed dependency response, partial success, retry exhaustion, and cancellation.","verification_guidance":"Record injected condition, expected behavior, observed behavior, and whether state invariants remained satisfied.","related_records":["D15-API-B35-0002","D15-API-B74-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B96-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Reliability Testing","concept":"Timeout and Cancellation Verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified when an API operation times out or is cancelled?","answer":"Verification should establish that timeout or cancellation produces the documented response or terminal state, releases relevant resources, and does not create unintended duplicate or partial effects.","explanation":"Timeout is an observation about waiting duration; it does not automatically prove that downstream execution stopped.","principle":"Separate request termination from confirmation that the underlying operation stopped or completed.","correct_example":"After client timeout, verify the operation's documented state and whether downstream work continued, completed, or was cancelled.","invalid_example":"Assume the database or worker stopped because the HTTP client disconnected.","problem":"Client-level cancellation is confused with server-side operation cancellation.","failure_mode":"Duplicate execution, resource leakage, or unexpected late effects.","cause":"Missing cancellation semantics.","solution":"Define and test operation cancellation and post-timeout behavior explicitly.","testing_guidance":"Test client disconnect, server timeout, downstream timeout, and worker cancellation independently.","verification_guidance":"Inspect operation state and downstream effects after cancellation.","related_records":["D15-API-B38-0002","D15-API-B39-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B97-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Observability","concept":"API Correlation and Trace Context","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What should API observability connect across a distributed request?","answer":"API observability should provide a controlled way to correlate ingress requests with relevant downstream operations, logs, traces, security decisions, dependency calls, and resulting outcomes without exposing sensitive data.","explanation":"Correlation allows investigation of distributed behavior while preserving separation between client-safe identifiers and sensitive internal context.","principle":"Trace causally related operations without turning correlation metadata into a credential or data-leak channel.","correct_example":"Assign or propagate a non-secret correlation or trace identifier and associate it with structured internal events.","invalid_example":"Use an access token itself as the correlation identifier.","problem":"Security credentials are reused as observability identifiers.","failure_mode":"Credential leakage through telemetry.","cause":"No separation between authentication material and tracing metadata.","solution":"Use dedicated non-secret correlation identifiers.","security_implication":"Observability metadata must not become an authentication bypass or disclosure channel.","testing_guidance":"Verify correlation survives downstream calls while credentials remain redacted.","verification_guidance":"Trace representative requests across service boundaries and confirm complete but non-sensitive correlation.","related_records":["D15-API-B34-0002","D15-API-B87-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B97-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Observability","concept":"Outcome-Oriented API Metrics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Which API metrics are more useful than request count alone?","answer":"Useful API metrics can include latency distributions, status-code classes, validation failures, authorization denials, retry rates, timeout rates, saturation indicators, dependency failures, request volume, resource consumption, and operation-specific outcomes where meaningful.","explanation":"Request count alone cannot distinguish successful operation from rejection, degradation, dependency failure, or resource exhaustion.","principle":"Measure outcomes and failure modes rather than only traffic volume.","correct_example":"Track latency distribution and failure categories separately from total request volume.","invalid_example":"Report only total requests and average latency for a critical state-changing API.","problem":"Operational failures are hidden inside aggregate traffic metrics.","failure_mode":"Delayed detection of degradation or systematic failure.","cause":"Insufficient metric dimensions.","solution":"Define metrics around API contract outcomes, reliability, resource controls, and dependencies.","testing_guidance":"Generate representative success and failure conditions and verify metric classification.","verification_guidance":"Compare emitted metrics with known test scenarios.","related_records":["D15-API-B35-0002","D15-API-B80-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B98-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"API Governance","concept":"API Design Review Matrix","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What dimensions should a comprehensive API design review evaluate?","answer":"A comprehensive review should evaluate resource and operation semantics, schemas, validation, status codes, error contracts, authentication, authorization, idempotency, concurrency, pagination, rate limits, caching, observability, versioning, compatibility, security, performance, failure behavior, testing, and documentation.","explanation":"API quality is multidimensional and cannot be determined from endpoint naming or schema validity alone.","principle":"Review API contracts systematically across semantics, safety, reliability, security, and lifecycle concerns.","correct_example":"Use a repeatable checklist where each applicable dimension has evidence or an explicit not-applicable decision.","invalid_example":"Approve an API solely because OpenAPI validation succeeds.","problem":"Design review focuses on syntax rather than behavior.","failure_mode":"Undetected semantic, security, reliability, or lifecycle defects.","cause":"Incomplete review model.","solution":"Use a structured API governance matrix linked to test and verification evidence.","testing_guidance":"Require tests for every material contract decision.","verification_guidance":"Do not mark a criterion VERIFIED without supporting evidence.","related_records":["D15-API-B40-0002","D15-API-B80-0002","D15-API-B90-0002"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenAPI Specification","source_type":"SPECIFICATION","title":"OpenAPI Specification","publisher":"OpenAPI Initiative","reference":"OpenAPI Specification","retrieval_date":"2026-09-17"}],"evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BACKED_AND_CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B98-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"API Governance","concept":"API Design Decision Record","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should significant API design choices be recorded explicitly?","answer":"Significant API decisions should record the chosen contract behavior, alternatives considered, constraints, compatibility implications, security consequences, and rationale so later engineers can understand and verify the intended design.","explanation":"Explicit decisions reduce accidental contract drift and prevent repeated design debates without context.","principle":"Record important externally observable API decisions as durable architectural or contract knowledge.","correct_example":"Record why an operation uses asynchronous acceptance, which status is returned, how clients poll, and why alternative semantics were rejected.","invalid_example":"Encode the decision only in implementation code with no durable contract rationale.","problem":"Future changes lack the original contract reasoning.","failure_mode":"Unintentional semantic drift or incompatible redesign.","cause":"Undocumented design decisions.","solution":"Create structured API decision records and link them to the affected contract elements.","related_records":["D15-API-B39-0002","D15-API-B98-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B99-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Integrated Architecture","concept":"API Contract Synthesis","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should an API contract combine correctness, security, reliability, and lifecycle concerns?","answer":"A mature API contract should define resource and operation semantics, input and output schemas, validation, status and error behavior, authentication and authorization, idempotency and concurrency semantics, resource controls, observability, versioning, compatibility, and explicit verification requirements.","explanation":"These concerns interact: for example, retries depend on idempotency, authorization affects caching, asynchronous processing affects status semantics, and versioning affects test coverage.","principle":"Design the API as a coherent behavioral contract rather than a collection of endpoint definitions.","correct_example":"Define the complete lifecycle of a state-changing operation including authentication, validation, authorization, idempotency, processing, failure, status observation, and version compatibility.","invalid_example":"Define only URL paths and JSON schemas while leaving security, failure, retry, and lifecycle semantics implicit.","problem":"Contract fragments conflict or leave critical behavior undefined.","failure_mode":"Client incompatibility, security defects, or unreliable integrations.","cause":"Endpoint-centric rather than system-contract-centric design.","solution":"Perform cross-concern contract synthesis before implementation and release.","testing_guidance":"Generate integrated tests from the complete operation contract.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no critical lifecycle stage has undefined client-visible behavior.","related_records":["D15-API-B40-0002","D15-API-B80-0002","D15-API-B90-0001","D15-API-B98-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B99-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Integrated Architecture","concept":"API Contract Contradiction Detection","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should contradictions inside an API contract be detected?","answer":"Compare endpoint definitions, schemas, status codes, error contracts, security requirements, version rules, documentation, implementation behavior, and tests for conflicting statements or incompatible assumptions.","explanation":"A specification can contain individually valid components that become contradictory when combined.","principle":"Validate the API as a system of related claims rather than validating each artifact independently.","correct_example":"Detect that documentation says an operation is idempotent while implementation and tests permit repeated requests to create multiple effects.","invalid_example":"Validate only that every schema is syntactically valid.","problem":"Cross-artifact contradictions remain hidden.","failure_mode":"Clients implement behavior based on inconsistent contract information.","cause":"No semantic consistency analysis.","solution":"Run cross-artifact contradiction checks and preserve unresolved conflicts explicitly.","testing_guidance":"Compare documented expectations with observed implementation and test outcomes.","verification_guidance":"Classify each contradiction by version, scope, authority, and observed evidence before resolving it.","related_records":["D15-API-B38-0001","D15-API-B40-0002","D15-API-B98-0001"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B100-0001","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Final Verification","concept":"D15 API Design Completeness Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be covered before D15 API Design can be considered complete?","answer":"D15 should cover API semantics, REST and HTTP behavior, RPC and event interfaces where applicable, schemas, validation, errors, authentication, authorization, idempotency, pagination, rate limiting, resource control, caching, observability, versioning, compatibility, security, reliability, testing, verification, migration, and governance.","explanation":"Completion means the planned knowledge scope has been represented and quality-checked, not that every possible API technology has been exhaustively documented.","principle":"Use explicit scope coverage and quality gates before dataset lock.","correct_example":"Mark each planned capability as COVERED, PARTIALLY_COVERED, NOT_APPLICABLE, or UNKNOWN and preserve evidence for the assessment.","invalid_example":"Declare completion solely because 100 batches were generated.","problem":"Record count is mistaken for knowledge completeness.","failure_mode":"Important API concepts remain missing despite apparent completion.","cause":"No scope-based completeness audit.","solution":"Run the D15 completeness matrix and resolve or explicitly document every missing area.","testing_guidance":"Run retrieval tests against every major scope category and expected concept.","verification_guidance":"Require structural, semantic, provenance, contradiction, deduplication, version, retrieval, and quality-gate validation before LOCK.","related_records":["D15-API-B98-0001","D15-API-B99-0001","D15-API-B100-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

{"record_id":"D15-API-B100-0002","dataset_id":"D15","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Final Verification","concept":"D15 Dataset Lock Gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What conditions should be satisfied before locking D15?","answer":"D15 should be locked only after structural validation, semantic validation, version analysis, provenance review, contradiction analysis, exact and semantic deduplication, relationship integrity checks, retrieval testing, example and counterexample review, security review, performance-claim review, and completeness assessment.","explanation":"A dataset lock is a quality state, not merely an end-of-generation marker.","principle":"LOCK means the dataset passed its defined quality gates with limitations explicitly recorded.","correct_example":"Set status to LOCKED only after audit evidence shows all mandatory gates passed or explicitly records justified exceptions and unresolved UNKNOWN items.","invalid_example":"Set LOCKED immediately after B100 generation without running the dataset-wide audit.","problem":"Generation completion is confused with dataset verification.","failure_mode":"Unverified or contradictory knowledge enters downstream training and retrieval systems.","cause":"Missing final quality gate.","solution":"Perform the full D15 audit and preserve its manifest, quality metrics, unresolved limitations, and revision metadata.","testing_guidance":"Execute retrieval and structural checks before lock; preserve actual results rather than inferred success.","verification_guidance":"Use VERIFIED only for claims supported by evidence; retain NOT_VERIFIED, UNKNOWN, or CONFLICTED where evidence is insufficient.","related_records":["D15-API-B100-0001","D15-API-B98-0001","D15-API-B99-0002"],"provenance":[],"evidence_level":"CONCEPTUAL","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-17","updated_at":"2026-09-17"}

